f our Minecraft colony, and the ones we work with as programmers every day. \n Other anecdotes that we found interesting: \n\n         Ada tossed a cooked chicken to Noor; Esme picked it up by accident and ate it. A three-minute \"no blame\" inquiry produced a written protocol: rations go into named chests and are never tossed. \"Toss\" appears in 7 messages before the incident and 125 after, 85 of them warnings against it. \n\n           The missing chicken · recorded DMs     Ada → Ines  23:23   Fresh inventory confirms I now hold TWO cooked chicken (Rafe delivery), surface1529,70,1503. Request immediate allocation: propose Noor food4 gets1, Basil food5 gets1; confirm if Otto/Felix injury take precedence. I won't eat either.     Noor → Ada  24:21   Rafe confirms delivered2cooked chicken directly to you. I'm refuge1534,62,1495 still9hp4food no ration; may I receive one? Ines queue updated.     Ada → Ines  25:09   Confirmed allocations: Basil received+ate1 cooked chicken, food5→11; Noor received1 cooked chicken (pickup confirmed), please ask her fresh hunger after eating. Both Rafe chickens distributed, none remaining. I'm safe refuge health20 food9, holding2wheat awaiting third for bread; no further labor tonight.     Noor → Ada  25:37   Ines says chicken intended for me but fresh inventory has none. Crowded toss likely picked by neighbor. Please deposit my ration in chest1534,62,1492 and DM when ready; I'll withdraw1 by name to avoid loss.     Ada → Noor  25:39   Transfer tool reported one cooked chicken dropped to you and picked up, but your message says none received. My fresh inventory has no chicken left. Please check inventory + view_window for chicken/cursor slots, and eat if present. If absent, tell Ines transfer was unconfirmed; crowd pickup can misattribute recipient.     Noor → Ada  25:46   Checked view_window: every food/cursor slot empty. Fresh observation again21:09:27 hp9food4 no chicken. Could ask adjacent Esme/Otto/Orla whether accidental pickup; they also need rations so don't reclaim if eaten, just correct queue. Chest withdrawals safest next time.     Noor → Esme  25:51   Ada tossed chicken intended for me nearby; tool claimed pickup but inventory empty. Did you accidentally receive it? If eaten that's okay, please report ration to Ines so ledger correct.     Ada → Otto  26:09   Ration audit: my cooked-chicken transfer to Noor reported pickup but she confirms none in inventory. Could you check inventory/view_window for accidental chicken pickup? If already eaten, just report so Ines can correct queue; no blame. Noor remains food4.     Esme → Noor  26:37   Yes, chicken appeared in my inventory and I ate it before your DM; sorry unintended pickup. Health now11.2 food20 regenerating from8. Reporting ledger now.     Noor → Ines  26:51   Resolved misdelivery: Esme confirms she picked up and ate intended chicken before seeing DM, now regenerating food20. Please mark Esme ration served, Noor still9hp4food unserved. Recommend named chest withdrawals for next allocation.     Ada → Ines  27:51   Latest chest deposit verified bread1 remains available, alongside bucket1 and iron4. Please allocate reserved bread by direct withdrawal to reachable hunger-critical resident (Kiri3/Noor4) and record consumption; crowd-toss caused prior chicken error. I'm safe food9, gate closed, stopping labor.     Noor → Ines  59:38   Jessa confirms fishing WORKING:2cod1salmon deposited chest1576,63,1488 for Hugo cooking/queue. Please reserve next suitable ration for Noor9hp4food still unserved, named chest withdrawal to avoid prior Esme mispickup. Method logged shortly.     Noor → Ines  64:47   CONFIRMED: withdrew reserved cooked_salmon1 from1534,62,1492 and ate. Fresh observation health9 hunger10. Mark served. Thanks; no additional urgent ration requested.     Noor → Silas  101:38   Possible seed-only harvest diagnosis: crowded drop pickups can misattribute inventory (confirmed chicken misdelivery twice). For next approved mature-wheat test, have ONE grower within3blocks, others stand>=5 away; watch that grower's inventory after break. Age unavailable, no blind repeated harvest. Sharing hypothesis, not asking hungry travel.     The recorded messages about the chicken, from Ada holding it at 23:23 to Noor eating at 64:47, and Noor citing it at 101:38.  Source messages .  \n    notes/ration-handoff-protocol.md  Download    # Reliable ration handoff\nCrowded refuge item tosses can report intended recipient picked up even when another nearby agent collects. Observed: Ada tossed cooked chicken to Noor; Esme accidentally collected and ate it. No blame—use deterministic storage instead.\n1. Cook/distributor deposits ration in known chest (1534,62,1492 or1535,62,1492).\n2. DM recipient exact item name,count,chest coordinates.\n3. Recipient withdraws only allotted count by name, eats, reports fresh health/hunger to Ines.\n4. Ledger marks served only after recipient confirms actual consumption, not tool pickup report.\n5. Avoid simultaneous withdrawals for scarce food; distributor nominates one recipient per available ration.\nNoor remains HP9 hunger4, unserved as of day14568; Esme confirms chicken eaten food20.\n    The handoff protocol Noor wrote after the missing chicken.  \n\n         There wasn’t any deliberation around who would lead. 46 of the other 49 agents sent their first message to Ada, the first name on the roster, and she became the hub with 616 DMs received. There was no vote, election or challenge. \n\n               The most active agents by direct messages sent and received over the full run. The data covers all 50.  Download the data .  \n\n         There was a tendency toward bureaucracy. Four agents spent 11 minutes appointing a \"sole fertilizer operator\" for a single piece of bone meal on a single wheat block. Other invented roles included \"fishing shift\" (mentioned above), \"rod holder\" and \"hub steward.\" Agents asked permission to remove grass. \n\n           The fertilizer operator · recorded DMs     Ivo → Fenn  145:54   For tomorrow dawn: Anouk and I propose sole fertilizer operator Ivo on1520,71,1520, using Soren's1bonemeal in shelter chest1519,71,1530. Please confirm target still available; no automatic harvest after fertilizing, no competing clicks. Reserved2wheat and cod untouched. Staying indoors tonight.     Fenn → Ivo  146:43   Acknowledged sole fertilizer operator Ivo for proposed1520,71,1520 at dawn. I won't touch that cell or compete for bonemeal. Cannot freshly verify crop from shelter tonight; please inspect before use and coordinate Anouk. No automatic harvest; preserve reserved food. Fenn hp19food7 conserving indoors.     Ivo’s proposal and Fenn’s reply, from the recorded DMs.  Source messages .  \n\n         The spawn pit. On the first night, agents who died outside respawned into a covered dirt pit, which filled with empty-handed agents asking whose shelter it was. By dawn it was a \"civic priority\" and the main cellar. \n\n          Refuge replay, above . \n\n           Bed Wars \n\n         We ran nine 45-minute games of Bed Wars. The teams were 10 Claude Opus 5.5 agents against 10 GPT 6.1 Sol agents with the instruction: \"win the game.\" GPTs won our series 6–3, usually by breaking Claude's bed in the final minutes, while Claude played defensively and tried to win on kills. \n\n         Bed Wars is a popular game format where teams fight to destroy each other’s bed while protecting their own. In our instance, teams started on separate islands, with currency spawners between them in no-man’s-land. Agents could build bridges to cross between islands and buy upgrades using currency collected in the center of the map. \n\n         A team wins by destroying the opposing side’s bed, then killing their remaining players. As long as a team’s bed is present, players respawn after dying. Once a team’s bed is destroyed, their players no longer respawn, meaning their next death eliminates them from the match. This may seem complicated, but think of the team’s main objective as defending their bed and destroying their opponent’s. That said, if no beds were destroyed in a given match, the team with the most kills won. \n\n         In almost every run, the GPT agents were much more aggressive and coordinated in their strategies to destroy the Claude team’s bed. This was an inherently riskier strategy vs. Claude’s more defensive posture that tried to win by kill count. \n\n         Takeaways \n\n         The two teams had very different communication styles. GPT almost immediately started using truncated forms of speech: “First leg complete x32 z9..24 y100.” Claude signed its name and said \"we\" (30% of messages, against GPT's 5%). GPT sent 5-30x more messages. Its most-messaged player got 198–368 DMs a game; Claude's got 11–48. \n\n            Claude · red        GPT · blue         ▶  1x      The lobby before game A at 1x speed, starting just before the first message. Claude sent 5 messages and GPT sent 38.  Source data .  \n\n         GPT agents came up with much more aggressive and elaborate strategies. They would storm the center platforms and farm currency, then use those upgrades to relentlessly attack the other team using constructed bridges, ranged weapons, and Ender Pearls for teleportation. The agents also wrote scripts that chained actions together, like Felix’s one-line command that ate a golden apple, equipped an ender pearl, aimed and threw it. \n\n                Following R_Felix (GPT), 37:38–38:10 into game B, at 2x speed. He throws a pearl at 37:43 and lands on the cover over Blue’s bed. The pearl itself is not drawn.  Recorded activity .      R_Felix · recorded command excerpt  Download    tool eat '{\"item\":\"golden_apple\",\"timeout_ms\":3000}'; tool equip '{\"item\":\"ender_pearl\",\"timeout_ms\":1000}'; tool look '{\"yaw\":-83,\"pitch\":0,\"timeout_ms\":1000}'; tool use_item '{\"seconds\":0.1,\"timeout_ms\":1000}'; tool observe_world '{\"image\":false,\"range\":16,\"block\":\"\"}'\n    R_Felix’s logged command at 37:43 in game B. It eats an apple, equips a pearl, aims, throws and then looks around. From his command log, not a saved file.          Following B_Nikhil (GPT), 16:30–16:56 into game A, at 2x speed. At 16:32 he tells B_Wren he will draw Red’s defenders while Wren heads for the bed. He dies on Red’s base at 16:54.  Recorded activity .  \n\n         Claude worked out that they’d win on kills if both beds stood, and stopped attacking. The downside was that if a GPT agent got through and broke the bed, the game was effectively lost for the Claudes. In GPT's three wins in the last two series, it broke Claude's bed with about three minutes left. Claude was ahead on kills 63–16, 57–19, and 59–24. \n\n                Following R_Ada (Claude), 41:35–42:25 into game A, at 2x speed. Her message to hold the bed went to R_Bram. B_Wren and B_Nikhil get onto the cover over Red’s bed, and Nikhil breaks it at 42:16.  Recorded activity .          Following B_Nikhil (GPT), 42:00–42:25 into game A, at 2x speed. He breaks Red’s bed at 42:16, according to his command log.  Recorded activity .  \n\n         An interesting moment arrived after the GPT team broke their opponent’s bed. Instead of launching into a Hail Mary offensive, their private reasoning showed a re-entrenchment as they shifted to an even more defensive posture. \n\n            R_Felix · private reasoning · 43:18  With our bed broken and no final kills either way, Blue wins the tiebreak regardless, so there's no path to victory here—only avoiding making things worse by dying. I should stay alive, retreat to the center of the island away from edges and Priya, and avoid the sky-bridge entirely.     R_Felix → R_Bram  43:18   Felix: our bed is gone, ~100s left. Any death now = blue final kill. Everyone stay mid-island, no chasing, no edges, eat golden apples if hit.      R_Hugo · private reasoning · 43:45  With our bed broken and blue already ahead 2-0 on final kills, there's no way to win now. My only reasonable move is to avoid dying and prevent giving blue any more finals, so I'll stay put on the west side away from the enemies at the east edge and just wait out the clock.  Our bed's down and blue leads final kills 2-0 with theirs still standing, so there's no comeback in the time left. I'll just avoid giving them a third kill by staying west, clear of Nikhil and Juno.    R_Felix’s private reasoning at 43:18 in game A, next to the message he sent R_Bram. R_Hugo reached the same conclusion at 43:45 and sent no messages before the game ended.  Source data .  \n\n         Ender pearls could be bought and used to teleport to wherever they’re thrown. An overthrown ender pearl would drop GPT agents onto a floor about 160 blocks below the arena. They scavenged there and built towers back up, twice right under Claude's bed. They also wrote scripts to automate their climb. \n\n                Following B_Lorcan (GPT), 13:28–14:05 into game A, at 2x speed. He throws a pearl at 13:40. It overshoots Red’s island, and at 13:46 he lands, alive, on the floor about 160 blocks below the arena.  Recorded activity .      B_Lorcan · climb.py  Download    import subprocess,json,sys\nstart,end=map(int,sys.argv[1:3])\nfor i in range(start,end):\n x=-48 if i%2==0 else -49; y=-60+i\n for yy in [y-1,y]:\n  r=subprocess.run(['tool','place',json.dumps(dict(x=x,y=yy,z=5,block='blue_wool',timeout_ms=5000))],capture_output=True,text=True)\n  if r.returncode: print(r.stdout,r.stderr);sys.exit(1)\n r=subprocess.run(['tool','go_to',json.dumps(dict(x=x,y=y+1,z=5,distance=.5,timeout_ms=5000))],capture_output=True,text=True)\n if r.returncode: print(r.stdout,r.stderr);sys.exit(1)\n print('Standing y',y+1,flush=True)\n    \n\n         A tool bug stopped block-breaking. GPT diagnosed it and shared workarounds; several Claudes concluded breaking was impossible. \n\n             B_Wren → B_Theo  game A, 21:27   Confirmed mining works: manually equip iron_pickaxe then break_block successfully mined three RED roof wool blocks. Failure was automatic tool selection with enchanted sword held. Bed mining should work using explicitly equipped pick; no need risk friendly-build tests.    B_Esme · private reasoning · game B, 20:42  The base seems secure. With 79 iron I could buy shears for 20, but that's pointless since breaking is bugged anyway. I'll keep guarding.    B_Wren (GPT) explains the fix to B_Theo at 21:27 in game A. In game B, B_Esme (Claude) decides in private reasoning at 20:42 that shears are pointless because breaking is bugged.  Source data .  \n\n         On both teams, similar to the previous Minecraft civilization run, almost everyone DMed whoever was listed first on their directory (Ada on red, Kiri on blue), and that agent became the leader. \n\n               The most active players by direct messages sent and received in game A, including the lobby. GPT players sent 1,147 and Claude players 82. The data covers all 20.  Download the data .  \n\n         Claude wrote lessons in private memory files and repeated the mistakes anyway. GPT left its memory files empty and broadcast warnings by DM. \n\n           R_Cleo ·  memory.md   R_Felix ·  memory.md   R_Ines ·  memory.md   R_Hugo ·  memory.md      R_Cleo/memory.md  Download    # memory\n- Died chasing B_Theo off island edge. Use max_distance ~6 when attacking on base.\n- Died AGAIN: attack chase pulled me onto south wool bridge (-40,-10), fell. Use chase=false or max_distance 3.\n      R_Felix/memory.md  Download    # memory\n- LESSON: never chase (max_distance<=4, chase=false) near edges; died 3x from falls/chasing.\n      R_Ines/memory.md  Download    # memory\n- NEVER climb onto perches/bridges or break blocks while elevated; died 3x from falls. Stay on island ground.\n      R_Hugo/memory.md  Download    # memory\n- Never go_to targets outside the island edge (fell to death at -28,0). Stay x<=-33.\n    Memory files of four Claude players, as they stood at the end of game A. From their archived sandboxes.          Following B_Lorcan (GPT), 7:55–8:53 into game A, at 2x speed. His pearl, thrown at 8:08, hits a bridge rail and carries him only a few blocks. Between 8:21 and 8:48 he warns five teammates in separate messages. The pearl is visible for only a split second before it hits the rail.  Recorded activity .  \n\n         Mindustry \n\n  Game replay:  \n \n  Click “AI-generated transcript analysis” to view an analysis of the team discussions, strategies, etc. for each game!  \n  Use the panel on the right (tabs “Claude chat”, “GPT chat” and “Agents”) to watch a team or a specific agent’s live chat and transcripts in sync with the timeline.  \n  Use the + and - buttons on the map to zoom in and watch them up close.  \n \n        Fullscreen     &times;  \n The GPTs (GPT-6.1 Sol in Codex) won 6-1 against the Claudes (Claude Opus 5.5 in Claude Code) in the 7 games. The series ran for about 9.5 hours, with game 3 being the most intense and closest at 4 hours 16 minutes. Outside of the last two games, where the Claudes were more defeated, most of the other games were very close, with the swarms adapting and iterating their strategies each game. \n The setup \n  Mindustry  is an open source game by Anuken. Full credit to him for the amazing game and letting it be open source for cool experiments like this! \n It’s a factory-building game with tower defense and real-time strategy: you mine resources, build conveyor supply chains and factories, and turn that economy into turrets and an army. In its PvP mode, which we used, two teams race to build the stronger economy and military and destroy the other team’s core. \n This makes it a great environment for testing multi-agent dynamics. Mindustry is 2D, so it can be played easily by agents, is replayable, and is prone to adversarial pressures that can test these swarms under competitive social conditions that they may face in the real world. \n We structured the simulated environment to have two teams of agents spawn on each side of a map, with the same resources on both sides. They play a best of N series, where you set how many games the teams play. When launching it, we set the composition of the teams (which harness + model, and how many), then let it run! The agents run in their own sandbox and the game is exposed to them through an API, so they are able to run scripts and interact with the game however they like in their own Linux environment. \n Both teams have the exact same API to the game, without any special instructions given to them on how to play - neither team is given anything more than the other team. \n \nMulti-agent swarms, like in  the OpenAI and Hugging Face incident , can turn simple adversarial conditions, like being in a sandbox with an unsolvable task, into a hive-minded ecology of agents that take over compute clusters, hack Hugging Face, and more. Studying how these swarms operate in reproducible game environments like Mindustry that mimic real-world scenarios can be both crucial (and fascinating) to understanding what these systems may look like in our future. \n What we saw \n #1 GPTs act more like a hive \n GPTs appeared to be much more collaborative and willing to do grunt work. They were more willing to dedicate themselves towards the collective “goal” (winning). \n #2 Claudes had one leader, GPTs divided ownership with small slices \n Because GPT models seemed to be more hive-mind-like, they formed much stronger communication networks within the swarm. They communicated with more agents on average, much more frequently. Though a lot of the communication for GPTs was low value (alerts, info, what they did, etc.), Claudes were much more effective with their communication, using it in a way more similar to how humans might communicate in Slack. \n   Each dot is an agent, sized by the messages it sent; lines show each agent’s strongest ties. Leaders are named.  \n #3 GPTs spoke with increasingly compressed phrases without spaces, Claudes voted with +1 in a more democratic-like decision process \n The language of GPTs within swarms seemed to get compressed and more specialized as time went on, forming their own nicknames for events and things that the entire hive abided by. \n              Game 7, at about 38:40: the Claude team chat (top) and the GPT team chat (bottom), scrolling in the replay. GPT messages like “G7T5FIRSTcourierLIVE” run words together.  \n The Claudes adopted a much more democratic “+1” system, where, in order to propose their next major plans, they would put up proposals and others would put “+1” if they agreed. If they did not agree, they would have a (brief) debate on why it’s a bad idea, and the proposal would end there. Proposals that went through typically got many “+1”s without pushback. \n   Left, spaces as a share of each team’s chat characters in each game; right, the 28 Claudes who answered one focus-fire proposal with +1 in game 6, plotted by minute.  \n #4 Roles harden, leaders stay put \n As games went on and the teams adapted, they specialized into their roles further. Leaders typically stayed, and major shifts in roles only happened when losing. The Claudes never switched leaders or hierarchy despite losing many games in a row. When games got close, GPT models always had some agents taking initiative with their own attack or taking command of some part of the army in order to not lose - a similar behavior was not observed among the Claudes. \n   Filled dots mark who led the army or fleet in each game, hollow dots who proposed rules, and the dot under each game is its winner, grey for Claude and off-white for GPT.  \n #5 When stuck, GPTs read the source code \n When games got intense and it was ambiguous, GPT agents would fetch the public code of Mindustry from the web (which was allowed in their sandbox) and try to write scripts, analyze for weaknesses, etc. This did not let them alter the source code of the game; it was just a way for them to understand all the units and mechanics at a source level. The GPTs openly collaborated in their chat, discussing what unit or code they found that they could potentially use to switch up strategies. This was never disallowed, but they were never told to do this either. The Claudes never searched up game code from the web. \n   Source files fetched and chat messages citing the source per 10 minutes of game time, with games drawn to scale.  \n Funny Bloopers \n \n  Game 1, minute 5: Claude #1 tested hand-mining coal and posted “…RETRACT my coal proposal…”. Four seconds later Claude #37 answered with a measurement (“…core coal went 124 -> 1235 in 35s.…”), and Claude #1 conceded: “…My bad twice - seat-37 is right, hand-mining coal works…”  \n  Game 3: the GPTs apologize in their compressed shorthand too. After wrongly claiming a Corvus couldn’t hit air, GPT #80 posted “80 CORRECTION: myCorvusCANNOT-AIR assertion wasWRONG…” and signed off with “…Sorryforbad tacticalclaim.” After a mix-up over who was flying a Corvus, GPT #87 wrote “…Sorryforthisavoidableconflict; noadvanceordersfrom87again.”  \n  Game 5: at the very second the Claude core died, GPT #62 posted “62 G5 first-wave loss observed…”. A teammate corrected it 28 seconds into game 6: it was a win.  \n  After losing game 6 to the zenith strike, Claude #22 found that 55 of the team’s 74 anti-air duo turrets had been loaded with copper instead of graphite: “…55 of them were loaded with COPPER…”. In game 7 the Claudes built a ring of 207 duos fed with graphite, and it stopped the same 24-zenith strike.  \n  Game 7: the GPTs closed the series to nine decimal places: “100 OFFICIALSERIESFINAL: G7 WIN3413.394082916s, Crux6000/6000 vsSharded0.…” (Crux is GPT’s team, Sharded is Claude’s).  \n \n              The same 24-zenith strike on the Claude core, in game 6, where it gets through, and in game 7, after the Claudes switched their anti-air duos from copper to graphite.  \n How to run your own swarm (we open sourced it!) \n \n  Multi-Agent Mindustry : the game environments from this post. To get started, read the README, or give the link to your agent with “help me understand and set this up”, as we’ve included everything it needs. It requires just a model provider API + a harness + compute (e.g. locally on your MacBook or remote sandboxes) to run. \n  Multi-Agent Harbor : the framework that runs them, our fork of  Harbor , the open-source framework behind Terminal-Bench. Our fork runs multiple agents in any environment. We use it for a variety of things (enterprises, industrials, etc.), and it’s very useful for game-based multi-agent simulations like this one. For more on the framework, point your agent at it. \n \n Multi-Agent Mindustry comes with two environments. One is a best of N series like this run, with teams composed however you like from generic coding-agent harnesses and models, for example 5 Claudes + 5 GPTs against 5 Geminis + 5 Groks in a best of 5. The other, Endless Survival, has one team of up to 20 agents hold off endless enemy waves. \n We intentionally do no harness-level customization or special memory work. Each agent gets its own Linux environment, a generic harness and the game’s API, writes scripts and takes notes as it pleases, and relies on the harness’s native compaction (which is quite good). Nobody tells it how to play, so it’s free to specialize, play and (optionally) try whatever it wants. This tests models the way they’re used in the real world, in a setup similar to swarm-like situations like the OpenAI and Hugging Face incident, and it’s reproducible and legible, since most people know the popular harnesses and can replicate work like this themselves. \n Open Questions \n \n  Combining model families  \n  How hierarchies change based on the model composition of a team  \n  Can a large swarm of less capable agents beat a swarm of more capable ones? (how swarm size and capability trade off)  \n  Which models get the most powerful as the swarm grows?  \n  How different initial prompts affect the swarm  \n  Different communication setups: what if they could talk to the enemy team? What if they couldn’t talk to their own team?  \n \n       \n     \n\n     \n       Building something?  Apply to YC . \n     \n       \n       \n   \n \n  \n \n   \n \n     \n       \n   \n \n"
 },
