  "text": " \n \n\t \n\t\t \n\n\t\t \n\n\t\t \n\n\t\t \n\n\t\t \n\n\t\t \n\n\t\t \n\n\t\t \n\n\t\t \n\n\t\t \n\n\t\t \n\n\t\t \n\n\t\t \n\n\t\t \n\n\t\t \n\n\t\t \n\n\t\t \n\n\t\t \n\n\t\t   \n\t\t Daily Papers - Hugging Face \n\n\t\t \n\n\t\t \n\n\t\t \n\n\t\t \n\n\t\t \n\t\t  \n\t \n\t \n\t\t                   Hugging Face                                        Models        Datasets        Spaces        Buckets        Docs        Enterprise      Pricing                             Website                 Tasks                   HuggingChat                   Collections                   Languages                   Organizations               Community                 Blog                   Posts                   Daily Papers                   Hardware                   Learn                   Discord                   Forum                   GitHub               Solutions                 Team & Enterprise                    Hugging Face PRO                   Enterprise Support                   Inference Providers                   Inference Endpoints                   Storage Buckets                               Log In      Sign Up                                             new   Get trending papers in your email inbox once a day!   Get trending papers in your email inbox!    Subscribe             0.9 ), supporting the complementarity and reliability of our metrics. By benchmarking four families of UMMs, we show that IntReal Evaluation Suite reveals distinct strengths and weaknesses across models. Post-training experiments demonstrate the utility of IntReal-100K for improving interleaved generation and further show that training unimodal generation can also benefit interleaved generation. These findings suggest that interleaved generation can benefit from modality-specific optimization, rather than requiring joint text-image training at every stage.\",\"upvotes\":15,\"discussionId\":\"6ae407efc3b566b041f6c7ef\",\"projectPage\":\"https://yushaohan.github.io/IntReal/\",\"githubRepo\":\"https://github.com/yushaohan/IntReal\",\"githubRepoAddedBy\":\"user\",\"ai_summary\":\"IntReal introduces a real-world interleaved multimodal dataset and evaluation framework to diagnose unified model strengths and improve generation through targeted training.\",\"ai_keywords\":[\"Unified Multimodal Models\",\"interleaved generation\",\"IntReal-100K\",\"IntReal Evaluation Suite\",\"expert models\",\"Spearman's correlation\"],\"ai_summary_model\":\"thinkingmachines/Inkling-Small\",\"githubStars\":7,\"organization\":{\"_id\":\"68b28d79a176a9beb30d2049\",\"name\":\"meituan-longcat\",\"fullname\":\"LongCat\",\"avatar\":\"https://cdn-avatars.huggingface.co/v1/production/uploads/68a2a29ab9d4c5698e02c747/CDCAx7X7rXDt7xjI-DoxG.png\"}},\"publishedAt\":\"2026-10-27T20:00:00.000Z\",\"title\":\"IntReal: A Real-World Dataset and Benchmark for Multimodal Interleaved Generation\",\"summary\":\"Humans routinely consume multimodal content, where textual and visual information is naturally interleaved. Although Unified Multimodal Models (UMMs) can jointly generate text and images, multimodal interleaved generation remains largely underexplored. Progress in this area is hindered by two key limitations: (1) the lack of well-defined interleaved generation tasks and corresponding training data, and (2) the absence of fine-grained evaluation protocols that can reliably diagnose model performance across different capability dimensions. To this end, we present IntReal, comprising two complementary components: IntReal-100K, a real-world dataset of multimodal interleaved samples spanning four major categories, and the IntReal Evaluation Suite, which evaluates model performance across seven complementary dimensions using specialized expert models, enabling coarse-to-fine diagnosis. Extensive experiments validate our evaluation suite through low inter-metric redundancy (max pairwise absolute Spearman's ρleq 0.42 ), high agreement with human judgments (83.3% accuracy), and strong consistency across different expert evaluators (Spearman's ρ> 0.9 ), supporting the complementarity and reliability of our metrics. By benchmarking four families of UMMs, we show that IntReal Evaluation Suite reveals distinct strengths and weaknesses across models. Post-training experiments demonstrate the utility of IntReal-100K for improving interleaved generation and further show that training unimodal generation can also benefit interleaved generation. These findings suggest that interleaved generation can benefit from modality-specific optimization, rather than requiring joint text-image training at every stage.\",\"mediaUrls\":[\"https://cdn-uploads.huggingface.co/production/uploads/68f59ae49315a06ad9a01464/PYZEmEPPaR8PFmfG5Ct5U.mp4\"],\"thumbnail\":\"https://cdn-thumbnails.huggingface.co/social-thumbnails/papers/2610.38425.png\",\"numComments\":4,\"upvoted\":false,\"submittedBy\":{\"_id\":\"68f59ae49315a06ad9a01464\",\"avatarUrl\":\"https://cdn-avatars.huggingface.co/v1/production/uploads/68f59ae49315a06ad9a01464/welpgtUr6TY9Qa7nPBigg.jpeg\",\"fullname\":\"Sean Yu\",\"name\":\"yushaohan\",\"type\":\"user\",\"isPro\":true,\"isHf\":false,\"isHfAdmin\":false,\"isMod\":false,\"followerCount\":2,\"isUserFollowing\":false},\"organization\":{\"_id\":\"68b28d79a176a9beb30d2049\",\"name\":\"meituan-longcat\",\"fullname\":\"LongCat\",\"avatar\":\"https://cdn-avatars.huggingface.co/v1/production/uploads/68a2a29ab9d4c5698e02c747/CDCAx7X7rXDt7xjI-DoxG.png\"},\"isAuthorParticipating\":true},{\"paper\":{\"id\":\"2610.38331\",\"authors\":[{\"_id\":\"6ae43c86c3b566b041f6c932\",\"user\":{\"_id\":\"65edcdae85cb3a3386cb4c86\",\"avatarUrl\":\"https://cdn-avatars.huggingface.co/v1/production/uploads/65edcdae85cb3a3386cb4c86/6uzJO3Gy5DWNzzHpHRGNI.jpeg\",\"isPro\":false,\"fullname\":\"Jayden Teoh\",\"user\":\"JaydenTeoh\",\"type\":\"user\",\"name\":\"JaydenTeoh\"},\"name\":\"Jayden Teoh\",\"status\":\"claimed_verified\",\"statusLastChangedAt\":\"2026-10-30T11:13:25.418Z\",\"hidden\":false}],\"mediaUrls\":[\"https://cdn-uploads.huggingface.co/production/uploads/65edcdae85cb3a3386cb4c86/IRgTSu-n3hvrv1j88aH23.png\"],\"publishedAt\":\"2026-10-28T00:00:00.000Z\",\"submittedOnDailyAt\":\"2026-10-30T00:00:00.000Z\",\"title\":\"Multi-Token Prediction Done Right\",\"submittedOnDailyBy\":{\"_id\":\"65edcdae85cb3a3386cb4c86\",\"avatarUrl\":\"https://cdn-avatars.huggingface.co/v1/production/uploads/65edcdae85cb3a3386cb4c86/6uzJO3Gy5DWNzzHpHRGNI.jpeg\",\"isPro\":false,\"fullname\":\"Jayden Teoh\",\"user\":\"JaydenTeoh\",\"type\":\"user\",\"name\":\"JaydenTeoh\"},\"summary\":\"Multi-Token Prediction (MTP) is now a common ingredient in language-model pre-training, as it improves downstream performance and enables faster inference through speculative decoding. We identify a flaw in standard MTP training, in which a residual component of the MTP gradient interferes with both next-token and multi-token learning, increasingly so with scale. Specifically, the MTP cross-entropy gradient can be decomposed exactly into a KL self-distillation term that pulls each MTP head toward the model's own next-token prediction (NTP) distribution and improves speculative decoding, and a residual that has a low signal-to-noise ratio and buries the useful KL signal. We introduce the KL gradient-recovery trick: when two distributions share a linear output head, the gradient of their KL divergence can be recovered exactly by combining their cross-entropy gradients through straight-through gradient routing. Unlike minimizing the KL directly, this trick requires no vocabulary-sized logit matrices and is compatible with standard fused cross-entropy kernels. This enables Soft Multi-Token Prediction (Soft-MTP), a one-line code change to MTP that removes the noisy residual and backpropagates only the KL self-distillation signal by reusing the existing NTP and MTP cross-entropy computations. Soft-MTP supports both DeepSeek- and Meta-style MTP, adds no parameters, and incurs no measurable overhead. From 167M to 1.2B parameters, Soft-MTP improves over standard MTP with lower NTP perplexity, faster speculative decoding and higher zero-shot accuracy at every scale, lower MTP perplexity from 689M onward, and stronger math and code reasoning after fine-tuning at 1.2B. Soft-MTP also matches, if not exceeds, direct KL divergence minimization on most metrics. Soft-MTP is, in effect, a free lunch for MTP optimization. Our code can be found at https://github.com/JaydenTeoh/Soft-MTP.\",\"upvotes\":12,\"discussionId\":\"6ae43c86c3b566b041f6c933\",\"githubRepo\":\"https://github.com/JaydenTeoh/Soft-MTP\",\"githubRepoAddedBy\":\"user\",\"ai_summary\":\"Soft-MTP removes a noisy gradient residual from multi-token prediction via a straight-through KL recovery trick, improving perplexity, speculative decoding, and reasoning without added cost.\",\"ai_keywords\":[\"Multi-Token Prediction\",\"speculative decoding\",\"cross-entropy gradient\",\"KL self-distillation\",\"straight-through gradient routing\",\"Soft-MTP\",\"next-token prediction\"],\"ai_summary_model\":\"thinkingmachines/Inkling-Small\",\"githubStars\":48,\"organization\":{\"_id\":\"62cf234c216602c3f3123382\",\"name\":\"Massachusetts-Institute-of-Technology\",\"fullname\":\"Massachusetts Institute of Technology\",\"avatar\":\"https://cdn-avatars.huggingface.co/v1/production/uploads/1657742064194-62cf1fdb2822f319618d82a8.png\"}},\"publishedAt\":\"2026-10-27T20:00:00.000Z\",\"title\":\"Multi-Token Prediction Done Right\",\"summary\":\"Multi-Token Prediction (MTP) is now a common ingredient in language-model pre-training, as it improves downstream performance and enables faster inference through speculative decoding. We identify a flaw in standard MTP training, in which a residual component of the MTP gradient interferes with both next-token and multi-token learning, increasingly so with scale. Specifically, the MTP cross-entropy gradient can be decomposed exactly into a KL self-distillation term that pulls each MTP head toward the model's own next-token prediction (NTP) distribution and improves speculative decoding, and a residual that has a low signal-to-noise ratio and buries the useful KL signal. We introduce the KL gradient-recovery trick: when two distributions share a linear output head, the gradient of their KL divergence can be recovered exactly by combining their cross-entropy gradients through straight-through gradient routing. Unlike minimizing the KL directly, this trick requires no vocabulary-sized logit matrices and is compatible with standard fused cross-entropy kernels. This enables Soft Multi-Token Prediction (Soft-MTP), a one-line code change to MTP that removes the noisy residual and backpropagates only the KL self-distillation signal by reusing the existing NTP and MTP cross-entropy computations. Soft-MTP supports both DeepSeek- and Meta-style MTP, adds no parameters, and incurs no measurable overhead. From 167M to 1.2B parameters, Soft-MTP improves over standard MTP with lower NTP perplexity, faster speculative decoding and higher zero-shot accuracy at every scale, lower MTP perplexity from 689M onward, and stronger math and code reasoning after fine-tuning at 1.2B. Soft-MTP also matches, if not exceeds, direct KL divergence minimization on most metrics. Soft-MTP is, in effect, a free lunch for MTP optimization. Our code can be found at https://github.com/JaydenTeoh/Soft-MTP.\",\"mediaUrls\":[\"https://cdn-uploads.huggingface.co/production/uploads/65edcdae85cb3a3386cb4c86/IRgTSu-n3hvrv1j88aH23.png\"],\"thumbnail\":\"https://cdn-thumbnails.huggingface.co/social-thumbnails/papers/2610.38331.png\",\"numComments\":3,\"upvoted\":false,\"submittedBy\":{\"_id\":\"65edcdae85cb3a3386cb4c86\",\"avatarUrl\":\"https://cdn-avatars.huggingface.co/v1/production/uploads/65edcdae85cb3a3386cb4c86/6uzJO3Gy5DWNzzHpHRGNI.jpeg\",\"fullname\":\"Jayden Teoh\",\"name\":\"JaydenTeoh\",\"type\":\"user\",\"isPro\":false,\"isHf\":false,\"isHfAdmin\":false,\"isMod\":false,\"followerCount\":2,\"isUserFollowing\":false},\"organization\":{\"_id\":\"62cf234c216602c3f3123382\",\"name\":\"Massachusetts-Institute-of-Technology\",\"fullname\":\"Massachusetts Institute of Technology\",\"avatar\":\"https://cdn-avatars.huggingface.co/v1/production/uploads/1657742064194-62cf1fdb2822f319618d82a8.png\"},\"isAuthorParticipating\":true},{\"paper\":{\"id\":\"2610.21143\",\"authors\":[{\"_id\":\"6ae2e7d2d670fc6ea896c8eb\",\"user\":{\"_id\":\"64e9c44ef494f8b2a0622c39\",\"avatarUrl\":\"/avatars/3259a854bb2e42bd96fc04accc57b9c6.svg\",\"isPro\":false,\"fullname\":\"Enjoy\",\"user\":\"D-YZ\",\"type\":\"user\",\"name\":\"D-YZ\"},\"name\":\"Yingzhuo Deng\",\"status\":\"claimed_verified\",\"statusLastChangedAt\":\"2026-10-29T20:09:01.258Z\",\"hidden\":false},{\"_id\":\"6ae2e7d2d670fc6ea896c8ec\",\"name\":\"Chong Li\",\"hidden\":false},{\"_id\":\"6ae2e7d2d670fc6ea896c8ed\",\"name\":\"Bingxin Liu\",\"hidden\":false},{\"_id\":\"6ae2e7d2d670fc6ea896c8ee\",\"name\":\"Yi Shu\",\"hidden\":false},{\"_id\":\"6ae2e7d2d670fc6ea896c8ef\",\"name\":\"Tianyu Peng\",\"hidden\":false},{\"_id\":\"6ae2e7d2d670fc6ea896c8f0\",\"name\":\"Can Xie\",\"hidden\":false},{\"_id\":\"6ae2e7d2d670fc6ea896c8f1\",\"name\":\"Siyao Song\",\"hidden\":false},{\"_id\":\"6ae2e7d2d670fc6ea896c8f2\",\"name\":\"Shuo Ren\",\"hidden\":false},{\"_id\":\"6ae2e7d2d670fc6ea896c8f3\",\"name\":\"Jiajun Zhang\",\"hidden\":false}],\"publishedAt\":\"2026-10-15T00:00:00.000Z\",\"submittedOnDailyAt\":\"2026-10-30T00:00:00.000Z\",\"title\":\"CoSTA: Compressed Speech Token Adaptation for Efficient Spoken Dialogue Models\",\"submittedOnDailyBy\":{\"_id\":\"64e9c44ef494f8b2a0622c39\",\"avatarUrl\":\"/avatars/3259a854bb2e42bd96fc04accc57b9c6.svg\",\"isPro\":false,\"fullname\":\"Enjoy\",\"user\":\"D-YZ\",\"type\":\"user\",\"name\":\"D-YZ\"},\"summary\":\"Spoken Dialogue Models (SDMs) typically share a speech-token space for generation and synthesis, leading to long autoregressive sequences and costly inference. Lower-rate speech tokenizers shorten sequences but may introduce compression-quality trade-offs and costly replacement without breaking this coupling. To address this limitation, we propose CoSTA, a compatibility-preserving post-hoc framework that decouples generation and synthesis token spaces while retaining the original speech tokenizer and decoder. CoSTA extends the generation token space with compressed representations via mean-initialized vocabulary extension, pronunciation adaptation, and dialogue adaptation, followed by deterministic expansion back to the original speech-token space before synthesis. On bilingual speech-dialogue benchmarks, CoSTA achieves approximately 1.7times expanded speech-token generation speed, lower full-response latency, and higher throughput while preserving response quality. Analyses show more sustained interaction under limited budgets and a practical trade-off among compression, decoding efficiency, and quality. Further experiments demonstrate cross-architecture transfer and complementarity to low-rate tokenization.\",\"upvotes\":12,\"discussionId\":\"6ae2e7d3d670fc6ea896c8f4\",\"githubRepo\":\"https://github.com/yingzhuodeng/CoSTA\",\"githubRepoAddedBy\":\"user\",\"ai_summary\":\"CoSTA decouples generation and synthesis token spaces to accelerate spoken dialogue models while preserving quality and compatibility.\",\"ai_keywords\":[\"Spoken Dialogue Models\",\"speech-token space\",\"autoregressive sequences\",\"speech tokenizers\",\"CoSTA\",\"vocabulary extension\",\"pronunciation adaptation\",\"dialogue adaptation\",\"cross-architecture transfer\",\"low-rate tokenization\"],\"ai_summary_model\":\"thinkingmachines/Inkling-Small\",\"githubStars\":13,\"organization\":{\"_id\":\"640a887796aae649741a586f\",\"name\":\"CASIA\",\"fullname\":\"Chinese Academic of Science Institute of Automation\",\"avatar\":\"https://cdn-avatars.huggingface.co/v1/production/uploads/1678411888885-6388984e8a5dbe2f3dc5afee.jpeg\"}},\"publishedAt\":\"2026-10-14T20:00:00.000Z\",\"title\":\"CoSTA: Compressed Speech Token Adaptation for Efficient Spoken Dialogue Models\",\"summary\":\"Spoken Dialogue Models (SDMs) typically share a speech-token space for generation and synthesis, leading to long autoregressive sequences and costly inference. Lower-rate speech tokenizers shorten sequences but may introduce compression-quality trade-offs and costly replacement without breaking this coupling. To address this limitation, we propose CoSTA, a compatibility-preserving post-hoc framework that decouples generation and synthesis token spaces while retaining the original speech tokenizer and decoder. CoSTA extends the generation token space with compressed representations via mean-initialized vocabulary extension, pronunciation adaptation, and dialogue adaptation, followed by deterministic expansion back to the original speech-token space before synthesis. On bilingual speech-dialogue benchmarks, CoSTA achieves approximately 1.7times expanded speech-token generation speed, lower full-response latency, and higher throughput while preserving response quality. Analyses show more sustained interaction under limited budgets and a practical trade-off among compression, decoding efficiency, and quality. Further experiments demonstrate cross-architecture transfer and complementarity to low-rate tokenization.\",\"thumbnail\":\"https://cdn-thumbnails.huggingface.co/social-thumbnails/papers/2610.21143.png\",\"numComments\":2,\"upvoted\":false,\"submittedBy\":{\"_id\":\"64e9c44ef494f8b2a0622c39\",\"avatarUrl\":\"/avatars/3259a854bb2e42bd96fc04accc57b9c6.svg\",\"fullname\":\"Enjoy\",\"name\":\"D-YZ\",\"type\":\"user\",\"isPro\":false,\"isHf\":false,\"isHfAdmin\":false,\"isMod\":false,\"isUserFollowing\":false},\"organization\":{\"_id\":\"640a887796aae649741a586f\",\"name\":\"CASIA\",\"fullname\":\"Chinese Academic of Science Institute of Automation\",\"avatar\":\"https://cdn-avatars.huggingface.co/v1/production/uploads/1678411888885-6388984e8a5dbe2f3dc5afee.jpeg\"},\"isAuthorParticipating\":true},{\"paper\":{\"id\":\"2610.37786\",\"authors\":[{\"_id\":\"6ae3fbe1c3b566b041f6c78c\",\"user\":{\"_id\":\"6ac35b05d87a082f95aa51cf\",\"avatarUrl\":\"https://cdn-avatars.huggingface.co/v1/production/uploads/6ac35b05d87a082f95aa51cf/C2blEtb8hQfT_glCviPcq.png\",\"isPro\":false,\"fullname\":\"tianci xie\",\"user\":\"Mumuthanks\",\"type\":\"user\",\"name\":\"Mumuthanks\"},\"name\":\"Tianci Xie\",\"status\":\"claimed_verified\",\"statusLastChangedAt\":\"2026-10-30T08:45:05.040Z\",\"hidden\":false},{\"_id\":\"6ae3fbe1c3b566b041f6c78d\",\"name\":\"Zikun Cai\",\"hidden\":false},{\"_id\":\"6ae3fbe1c3b566b041f6c78e\",\"name\":\"Chaoyun Zhang\",\"hidden\":false},{\"_id\":\"6ae3fbe1c3b566b041f6c78f\",\"name\":\"Pinxue Guo\",\"hidden\":false},{\"_id\":\"6ae3fbe1c3b566b041f6c790\",\"name\":\"Bingxuan Li\",\"hidden\":false},{\"_id\":\"6ae3fbe1c3b566b041f6c791\",\"name\":\"Rui Zheng\",\"hidden\":false},{\"_id\":\"6ae3fbe1c3b566b041f6c792\",\"name\":\"Hang Yan\",\"hidden\":false}],\"publishedAt\":\"2026-10-27T00:00:00.000Z\",\"submittedOnDailyAt\":\"2026-10-30T00:00:00.000Z\",\"title\":\"WebAnything: From the Wild Web to Scalable Agent Environments\",\"submittedOnDailyBy\":{\"_id\":\"654dbac9938fbf1e696be8aa\",\"avatarUrl\":\"/avatars/185c57abc99caa4aedaaf342f4a65f75.svg\",\"isPro\":false,\"fullname\":\"Chaoyun Zhang\",\"user\":\"vyokky\",\"type\":\"user\",\"name\":\"vyokky\"},\"summary\":\"Web agents require diverse, executable, and resettable website environments for reproducible training and evaluation. Human-assisted mirrors demand per-site engineering, while generative construction can diverge from specified real websites. Capture-and-replay preserves observations, with interaction coverage constrained by captured content and replay capabilities.\\n  We present WebAnything, a fully automated framework that reconstructs source-grounded, reusable Web environments from real URLs without per-site manual implementation or repair after initial setup and authorization. An Explorer-Builder collects browser-observable structures, resources, and interaction outcomes; an independent verifier checks frozen candidates and guides local repair or targeted source recapture.\\n  We construct 244 website artifacts across 14 application domains and 53 categories, supporting 12,984 executed and accepted tasks. Compared with human-assisted WebHarbor, WebAnything achieves higher aggregate scores on three fidelity metrics, with 12 wins, two ties, and no losses in a blind audit. Fine-tuning Qwen3.5-9B on 5,856 interaction trajectories improves REAL success by 9.9 percentage points, Odysseys-Easy Rubric Avg and Task Avg by 6.25 and 6.32 percentage points, and WebArena-Verified Overall success by 13.33 percentage points under the fixed harness. Code: https://github.com/china-qijizhifeng/Web-Anything\",\"upvotes\":12,\"discussionId\":\"6ae3fbe2c3b566b041f6c793\",\"projectPage\":\"https://china-qijizhifeng.github.io/Web-Anything/\",\"githubRepo\":\"https://github.com/china-qijizhifeng/Web-Anything\",\"githubRepoAddedBy\":\"user\",\"ai_summary\":\"WebAnything automates the reconstruction of reusable, source-grounded web environments from real URLs, enabling scalable agent training and evaluation with verified fidelity.\",\"ai_keywords\":[\"WebAnything\",\"Explorer-Builder\",\"frozen candidates\",\"source recapture\",\"interaction trajectories\",\"Qwen3.5-9B\",\"REAL success\",\"WebArena-Verified\"],\"ai_summary_model\":\"thinkingmachines/Inkling-Small\",\"githubStars\":3},\"publishedAt\":\"2026-10-26T20:00:00.000Z\",\"title\":\"WebAnything: From the Wild Web to Scalable Agent Environments\",\"summary\":\"Web agents require diverse, executable, and resettable website environments for reproducible training and evaluation. Human-assisted mirrors demand per-site engineering, while generative construction can diverge from specified real websites. Capture-and-replay preserves observations, with interaction coverage constrained by captured content and replay capabilities.\\n  We present WebAnything, a fully automated framework that reconstructs source-grounded, reusable Web environments from real URLs without per-site manual implementation or repair after initial setup and authorization. An Explorer-Builder collects browser-observable structures, resources, and interaction outcomes; an independent verifier checks frozen candidates and guides local repair or targeted source recapture.\\n  We construct 244 website artifacts across 14 application domains and 53 categories, supporting 12,984 executed and accepted tasks. Compared with human-assisted WebHarbor, WebAnything achieves higher aggregate scores on three fidelity metrics, with 12 wins, two ties, and no losses in a blind audit. Fine-tuning Qwen3.5-9B on 5,856 interaction trajectories improves REAL success by 9.9 percentage points, Odysseys-Easy Rubric Avg and Task Avg by 6.25 and 6.32 percentage points, and WebArena-Verified Overall success by 13.33 percentage points under the fixed harness. Code: https://github.com/china-qijizhifeng/Web-Anything\",\"thumbnail\":\"https://cdn-thumbnails.huggingface.co/social-thumbnails/papers/2610.37786.png\",\"numComments\":2,\"upvoted\":false,\"submittedBy\":{\"_id\":\"654dbac9938fbf1e696be8aa\",\"avatarUrl\":\"/avatars/185c57abc99caa4aedaaf342f4a65f75.svg\",\"fullname\":\"Chaoyun Zhang\",\"name\":\"vyokky\",\"type\":\"user\",\"isPro\":false,\"isHf\":false,\"isHfAdmin\":false,\"isMod\":false,\"followerCount\":5,\"isUserFollowing\":false},\"isAuthorParticipating\":false},{\"paper\":{\"id\":\"2610.38342\",\"authors\":[{\"_id\":\"6ae438f9c3b566b041f6c915\",\"name\":\"Huangfei Jiang\",\"hidden\":false},{\"_id\":\"6ae438f9c3b566b041f6c916\",\"name\":\"Anning Hu\",\"hidden\":false},{\"_id\":\"6ae438f9c3b566b041f6c917\",\"name\":\"Kai Sheng\",\"hidden\":false},{\"_id\":\"6ae438f9c3b566b041f6c918\",\"user\":{\"_id\":\"651e2a19242e10766e61a669\",\"avatarUrl\":\"/avatars/fc5130da1e0cd22e0d8d6167ae543960.svg\",\"isPro\":false,\"fullname\":\"caiyuchen\",\"user\":\"caiyuchen\",\"type\":\"user\",\"name\":\"caiyuchen\"},\"name\":\"Yuchen Cai\",\"status\":\"claimed_verified\",\"statusLastChangedAt\":\"2026-10-30T08:45:05.140Z\",\"hidden\":false},{\"_id\":\"6ae438f9c3b566b041f6c919\",\"name\":\"Rui Ge\",\"hidden\":false},{\"_id\":\"6ae438f9c3b566b041f6c91a\",\"name\":\"Xiangyu Kong\",\"hidden\":false},{\"_id\":\"6ae438f9c3b566b041f6c91b\",\"name\":\"Jianghao Lin\",\"hidden\":false},{\"_id\":\"6ae438f9c3b566b041f6c91c\",\"name\":\"Shuzi Chen\",\"hidden\":false},{\"_id\":\"6ae438f9c3b566b041f6c91d\",\"name\":\"Jiyi Weng\",\"hidden\":false},{\"_id\":\"6ae438f9c3b566b041f6c91e\",\"name\":\"Junchi Yan\",\"hidden\":false}],\"mediaUrls\":[\"https://cdn-uploads.huggingface.co/production/uploads/651e2a19242e10766e61a669/AEAiccZLlw8PA3GgJR_hl.mp4\"],\"publishedAt\":\"2026-10-28T00:00:00.000Z\",\"submittedOnDailyAt\":\"2026-10-30T00:00:00.000Z\",\"title\":\"Investor's Last Exam: Can AI Agents Do Equity Investment Without Seeing the Future?\",\"submittedOnDailyBy\":{\"_id\":\"651e2a19242e10766e61a669\",\"avatarUrl\":\"/avatars/fc5130da1e0cd22e0d8d6167ae543960.svg\",\"isPro\":false,\"fullname\":\"caiyuchen\",\"user\":\"caiyuchen\",\"type\":\"user\",\"name\":\"caiyuchen\"},\"summary\":\"Financial agents must form forecasts and investment judgments from decision-time evidence, with predictions evaluated against subsequent outcomes. Existing benchmarks assess financial knowledge, research workflows, or forecasting, but evaluating the full research process requires a strictly frozen information set and professional evaluation criteria. We introduce Investor's Last Exam (ILE), an equity-research benchmark led by experienced buy-side investors and fund managers. Its three components are a versioned financial database spanning 3.43 million document versions and nine source categories, supplemented by structured market data, with validated timestamps governing retrieval and computation; a task taxonomy organized around professional analyst deliverables; and an expert-led framework linking scoring criteria to sources, calculations, and assumptions through financial dependency graphs. Evaluation spans 86 assignments and jointly assesses research quality, forecast accuracy, and simulated investment performance. GPT-6 Astra and Claude Opus 5.5 lead overall at 68.1 and 66.9 out of 100, yet even Astra scores only 52.5 on Forecast & Model. Our results show that even state-of-the-art models fail to translate overall research capability into predictive performance. ILE shifts the evaluation unit from isolated capabilities to the evidence--assumption--judgment chain, allowing researchers to locate where analytical advantages fail to translate into forecasts and investment judgments, and providing a traceable, diagnostic foundation for training and evaluating financial agents.\",\"upvotes\":11,\"discussionId\":\"6ae438f9c3b566b041f6c91f\",\"projectPage\":\"https://prophet-lab.cn/benchmarks/investor-last-exam-v1/\",\"githubRepo\":\"https://github.com/Prophetlab/Investor-Last-Exam\",\"githubRepoAddedBy\":\"user\",\"ai_summary\":\"Investor's Last Exam evaluates financial agents on end-to-end equity research using frozen evidence, expert criteria, and dependency graphs, revealing that top models excel at research but fail at forecasting and investment judgments.\",\"ai_keywords\":[\"financial dependency graphs\",\"versioned financial database\",\"forecast accuracy\",\"simulated investment performance\",\"evidence-assumption-judgment chain\"],\"ai_summary_model\":\"thinkingmachines/Inkling-Small\",\"githubStars\":127},\"publishedAt\":\"2026-10-27T20:00:00.000Z\",\"title\":\"Investor's Last Exam: Can AI Agents Do Equity Investment Without Seeing the Future?\",\"summary\":\"Financial agents must form forecasts and investment judgments from decision-time evidence, with predictions evaluated against subsequent outcomes. Existing benchmarks assess financial knowledge, research workflows, or forecasting, but evaluating the full research process requires a strictly frozen information set and professional evaluation criteria. We introduce Investor's Last Exam (ILE), an equity-research benchmark led by experienced buy-side investors and fund managers. Its three components are a versioned financial database spanning 3.43 million document versions and nine source categories, supplemented by structured market data, with validated timestamps governing retrieval and computation; a task taxonomy organized around professional analyst deliverables; and an expert-led framework linking scoring criteria to sources, calculations, and assumptions through financial dependency graphs. Evaluation spans 86 assignments and jointly assesses research quality, forecast accuracy, and simulated investment performance. GPT-6 Astra and Claude Opus 5.5 lead overall at 68.1 and 66.9 out of 100, yet even Astra scores only 52.5 on Forecast & Model. Our results show that even state-of-the-art models fail to translate overall research capability into predictive performance. ILE shifts the evaluation unit from isolated capabilities to the evidence--assumption--judgment chain, allowing researchers to locate where analytical advantages fail to translate into forecasts and investment judgments, and providing a traceable, diagnostic foundation for training and evaluating financial agents.\",\"mediaUrls\":[\"https://cdn-uploads.huggingface.co/production/uploads/651e2a19242e10766e61a669/AEAiccZLlw8PA3GgJR_hl.mp4\"],\"thumbnail\":\"https://cdn-thumbnails.huggingface.co/social-thumbnails/papers/2610.38342.png\",\"numComments\":4,\"upvoted\":false,\"submittedBy\":{\"_id\":\"651e2a19242e10766e61a669\",\"avatarUrl\":\"/avatars/fc5130da1e0cd22e0d8d6167ae543960.svg\",\"fullname\":\"caiyuchen\",\"name\":\"caiyuchen\",\"type\":\"user\",\"isPro\":false,\"isHf\":false,\"isHfAdmin\":false,\"isMod\":false,\"followerCount\":2,\"isUserFollowing\":false},\"isAuthorParticipating\":true},{\"paper\":{\"id\":\"2610.40454\",\"authors\":[{\"_id\":\"6ae42a08c3b566b041f6c8b9\",\"user\":{\"_id\":\"65d7266b5b5a8c20669b47eb\",\"avatarUrl\":\"https://cdn-avatars.huggingface.co/v1/production/uploads/65d7266b5b5a8c20669b47eb/09FVHlQR496hPbqL7y9iJ.jpeg\",\"isPro\":true,\"fullname\":\"Metinov Adilet\",\"user\":\"metinovadilet\",\"type\":\"user\",\"name\":\"metinovadilet\"},\"name\":\"Adilet Metinov\",\"status\":\"claimed_verified\",\"statusLastChangedAt\":\"2026-10-30T08:45:05.118Z\",\"hidden\":false},{\"_id\":\"6ae42a08c3b566b041f6c8ba\",\"name\":\"Chingiz Arziev\",\"hidden\":false}],\"mediaUrls\":[\"https://cdn-uploads.huggingface.co/production/uploads/65d7266b5b5a8c20669b47eb/5aIhrppUdzEwwudD3U0PV.png\",\"https://cdn-uploads.huggingface.co/production/uploads/65d7266b5b5a8c20669b47eb/5dvFQIX_BRFeuqzZqlkB8.mp4\"],\"publishedAt\":\"2026-10-29T00:00:00.000Z\",\"submittedOnDailyAt\":\"2026-10-30T00:00:00.000Z\",\"title\":\"Glance: The Generator Is Its Own Reranker\",\"submittedOnDailyBy\":{\"_id\":\"65d7266b5b5a8c20669b47eb\",\"avatarUrl\":\"https://cdn-avatars.huggingface.co/v1/production/uploads/65d7266b5b5a8c20669b47eb/09FVHlQR496hPbqL7y9iJ.jpeg\",\"isPro\":true,\"fullname\":\"Metinov Adilet\",\"user\":\"metinovadilet\",\"type\":\"user\",\"name\":\"metinovadilet\"},\"summary\":\"Retrieval-augmented generation commonly serves separate models for reranking and answering, duplicating model residency and passage processing. We introduce Glance, which equips the generator with a lightweight decision adapter and pointer head to score candidate passages from a shared prefill. Short relevance probes branch from the cached context, producing one score per passage without separately prefilling each query-passage pair. A single resident 9B backbone supports generation, general decisions and reranking, with approximately 0.1 GiB of additional memory per decision skill. After 39 minutes of MS MARCO fine-tuning, Glance achieves nDCG@10 of 0.741 on SciFact and 0.345 on NFCorpus, compared with 0.721 and 0.326 for bge-reranker-v2-m3. On 300 HotpotQA questions, its selected context improves answer F1 by 0.045 over cross-encoder-selected context under the same base generator (paired bootstrap 95% CI [0.014, 0.076]). In a scaling study, reranking quality keeps rising up to 200 candidates (43k tokens) scored from one shared prefill, where it exceeds the cross-encoder by 0.058 nDCG@10, and a probe-only activated adapter lets generation continue from the original passage cache. These initial results demonstrate generator-based reranking with one resident backbone; evaluation currently covers two retrieval datasets, one QA dataset and one backbone family.\",\"upvotes\":11,\"discussionId\":\"6ae42a08c3b566b041f6c8bb\",\"projectPage\":\"https://huggingface.co/airun-ai/GlanceRAG-MiMo-9B-Reranker\",\"ai_summary\":\"Glance integrates reranking and generation into a single backbone using lightweight adapters and pointer heads to score passages from shared context, reducing memory overhead while improving retrieval and answer quality.\",\"ai_keywords\":[\"retrieval-augmented generation\",\"reranking\",\"pointer head\",\"decision adapter\",\"shared prefill\",\"cross-encoder\",\"nDCG@10\",\"answer F1\"],\"ai_summary_model\":\"thinkingmachines/Inkling-Small\",\"organization\":{\"_id\":\"6a8834621e913a13bd4fc845\",\"name\":\"airun-ai\",\"fullname\":\"Airun Intelligence Labs \",\"avatar\":\"https://cdn-avatars.huggingface.co/v1/production/uploads/6a88335b7c2e089c5406d59a/aMXfNClvs-4s3E-xoGCJ7.png\"}},\"publishedAt\":\"2026-10-28T20:00:00.000Z\",\"title\":\"Glance: The Generator Is Its Own Reranker\",\"summary\":\"Retrieval-augmented generation commonly serves separate models for reranking and answering, duplicating model residency and passage processing. We introduce Glance, which equips the generator with a lightweight decision adapter and pointer head to score candidate passages from a shared prefill. Short relevance probes branch from the cached context, producing one score per passage without separately prefilling each query-passage pair. A single resident 9B backbone supports generation, general decisions and reranking, with approximately 0.1 GiB of additional memory per decision skill. After 39 minutes of MS MARCO fine-tuning, Glance achieves nDCG@10 of 0.741 on SciFact and 0.345 on NFCorpus, compared with 0.721 and 0.326 for bge-reranker-v2-m3. On 300 HotpotQA questions, its selected context improves answer F1 by 0.045 over cross-encoder-selected context under the same base generator (paired bootstrap 95% CI [0.014, 0.076]). In a scaling study, reranking quality keeps rising up to 200 candidates (43k tokens) scored from one shared prefill, where it exceeds the cross-encoder by 0.058 nDCG@10, and a probe-only activated adapter lets generation continue from the original passage cache. These initial results demonstrate generator-based reranking with one resident backbone; evaluation currently covers two retrieval datasets, one QA dataset and one backbone family.\",\"mediaUrls\":[\"https://cdn-uploads.huggingface.co/production/uploads/65d7266b5b5a8c20669b47eb/5aIhrppUdzEwwudD3U0PV.png\",\"https://cdn-uploads.huggingface.co/production/uploads/65d7266b5b5a8c20669b47eb/5dvFQIX_BRFeuqzZqlkB8.mp4\"],\"thumbnail\":\"https://cdn-thumbnails.huggingface.co/social-thumbnails/papers/2610.40454.png\",\"numComments\":2,\"upvoted\":false,\"submittedBy\":{\"_id\":\"65d7266b5b5a8c20669b47eb\",\"avatarUrl\":\"https://cdn-avatars.huggingface.co/v1/production/uploads/65d7266b5b5a8c20669b47eb/09FVHlQR496hPbqL7y9iJ.jpeg\",\"fullname\":\"Metinov Adilet\",\"name\":\"metinovadilet\",\"type\":\"user\",\"isPro\":true,\"isHf\":false,\"isHfAdmin\":false,\"isMod\":false,\"followerCount\":10,\"isUserFollowing\":false},\"organization\":{\"_id\":\"6a8834621e913a13bd4fc845\",\"name\":\"airun-ai\",\"fullname\":\"Airun Intelligence Labs \",\"avatar\":\"https://cdn-avatars.huggingface.co/v1/production/uploads/6a88335b7c2e089c5406d59a/aMXfNClvs-4s3E-xoGCJ7.png\"},\"isAuthorParticipating\":true},{\"paper\":{\"id\":\"2610.38678\",\"authors\":[{\"_id\":\"6ae41054c3b566b041f6c823\",\"name\":\"Lu Wang\",\"hidden\":false},{\"_id\":\"6ae41054c3b566b041f6c824\",\"name\":\"Zhuoran Jin\",\"hidden\":false},{\"_id\":\"6ae41054c3b566b041f6c825\",\"name\":\"Longxiang Wang\",\"hidden\":false},{\"_id\":\"6ae41054c3b566b041f6c826\",\"name\":\"Junhong Chen\",\"hidden\":false},{\"_id\":\"6ae41054c3b566b041f6c827\",\"name\":\"Yupu Hao\",\"hidden\":false},{\"_id\":\"6ae41054c3b566b041f6c828\",\"name\":\"Yao Xu\",\"hidden\":false},{\"_id\":\"6ae41054c3b566b041f6c829\",\"name\":\"Daojian Zeng\",\"hidden\":false},{\"_id\":\"6ae41054c3b566b041f6c82a\",\"name\":\"Yubo Chen\",\"hidden\":false},{\"_id\":\"6ae41054c3b566b041f6c82b\",\"name\":\"Kang Liu\",\"hidden\":false},{\"_id\":\"6ae41054c3b566b041f6c82c\",\"name\":\"Guang Liu\",\"hidden\":false},{\"_id\":\"6ae41054c3b566b041f6c82d\",\"name\":\"Jun Zhao\",\"hidden\":false}],\"publishedAt\":\"2026-10-28T00:00:00.000Z\",\"submittedOnDailyAt\":\"2026-10-30T00:00:00.000Z\",\"title\":\"AskStream: When and What to Ask Proactively under Uncertainty in Streaming Video Understanding\",\"submittedOnDailyBy\":{\"_id\":\"67d15f29bacfd19231a6e178\",\"avatarUrl\":\"https://cdn-avatars.huggingface.co/v1/production/uploads/67d15f29bacfd19231a6e178/nbm1rAJgnH-QdEZS2tF8Z.png\",\"isPro\":false,\"fullname\":\"Lu  Wang\",\"user\":\"wanglucs\",\"type\":\"user\",\"name\":\"wanglucs\"},\"summary\":\"Recent advances in multimodal large language models have increased interest in interaction paradigms for video understanding. The evolution from offline to streaming and proactive interaction has gradually shifted models from passive responders toward more proactive agents that can decide when to answer. However, existing proactive paradigms typically assume that the information required to answer a query is complete, leaving models to only wait or answer. In the real world, however, information is often incomplete due to uncertainty from either the video or the user. In proactive paradigms, such uncertainty can cause the model to wait forever or generate highly hallucinatory responses. We therefore argue that proactive interaction should be redefined with a third action: ask, allowing models to actively ask for missing information. To study when and what models should ask before answering, we introduce AskStream, the first multi-turn benchmark spanning video and user uncertainty for streaming video understanding, with four task categories and eight subtasks. It contains 438 videos with 2,714 manually annotated frame-level time windows across three types: wait, ask, and answer. Across both multiple-choice and open-ended settings, even the strongest model attains only 52.18% on our proposed Ask-F1 metric, highlighting substantial limitations in proactive information seeking under uncertainty.\",\"upvotes\":10,\"discussionId\":\"6ae41054c3b566b041f6c82e\",\"projectPage\":\"https://askstream.top/\",\"githubRepo\":\"https://github.com/wanglu-cs/AskStream\",\"githubRepoAddedBy\":\"user\",\"ai_summary\":\"Proactive video understanding should include asking for missing information under uncertainty, and the AskStream benchmark reveals current models struggle with this decision.\",\"ai_keywords\":[\"multimodal large language models\",\"streaming video understanding\",\"proactive interaction\",\"AskStream\",\"Ask-F1 metric\",\"video uncertainty\",\"user uncertainty\"],\"ai_summary_model\":\"thinkingmachines/Inkling-Small\",\"githubStars\":12,\"organization\":{\"_id\":\"640a887796aae649741a586f\",\"name\":\"CASIA\",\"fullname\":\"Chinese Academic of Science Institute of Automation\",\"avatar\":\"https://cdn-avatars.huggingface.co/v1/production/uploads/1678411888885-6388984e8a5dbe2f3dc5afee.jpeg\"}},\"publishedAt\":\"2026-10-27T20:00:00.000Z\",\"title\":\"AskStream: When and What to Ask Proactively under Uncertainty in Streaming Video Understanding\",\"summary\":\"Recent advances in multimodal large language models have increased interest in interaction paradigms for video understanding. The evolution from offline to streaming and proactive interaction has gradually shifted models from passive responders toward more proactive agents that can decide when to answer. However, existing proactive paradigms typically assume that the information required to answer a query is complete, leaving models to only wait or answer. In the real world, however, information is often incomplete due to uncertainty from either the video or the user. In proactive paradigms, such uncertainty can cause the model to wait forever or generate highly hallucinatory responses. We therefore argue that proactive interaction should be redefined with a third action: ask, allowing models to actively ask for missing information. To study when and what models should ask before answering, we introduce AskStream, the first multi-turn benchmark spanning video and user uncertainty for streaming video understanding, with four task categories and eight subtasks. It contains 438 videos with 2,714 manually annotated frame-level time windows across three types: wait, ask, and answer. Across both multiple-choice and open-ended settings, even the strongest model attains only 52.18% on our proposed Ask-F1 metric, highlighting substantial limitations in proactive information seeking under uncertainty.\",\"thumbnail\":\"https://cdn-thumbnails.huggingface.co/social-thumbnails/papers/2610.38678.png\",\"numComments\":4,\"upvoted\":false,\"submittedBy\":{\"_id\":\"67d15f29bacfd19231a6e178\",\"avatarUrl\":\"https://cdn-avatars.huggingface.co/v1/production/uploads/67d15f29bacfd19231a6e178/nbm1rAJgnH-QdEZS2tF8Z.png\",\"fullname\":\"Lu  Wang\",\"name\":\"wanglucs\",\"type\":\"user\",\"isPro\":false,\"isHf\":false,\"isHfAdmin\":false,\"isMod\":false,\"followerCount\":1,\"isUserFollowing\":false},\"organization\":{\"_id\":\"640a887796aae649741a586f\",\"name\":\"CASIA\",\"fullname\":\"Chinese Academic of Science Institute of Automation\",\"avatar\":\"https://cdn-avatars.huggingface.co/v1/production/uploads/1678411888885-6388984e8a5dbe2f3dc5afee.jpeg\"},\"isAuthorParticipating\":false},{\"paper\":{\"id\":\"2610.39664\",\"authors\":[{\"_id\":\"6ae40e7ec3b566b041f6c818\",\"name\":\"Haiyang Xu\",\"hidden\":false},{\"_id\":\"6ae40e7ec3b566b041f6c819\",\"name\":\"Tianyu Wang\",\"hidden\":false},{\"_id\":\"6ae40e7ec3b566b041f6c81a\",\"name\":\"Jialu Li\",\"hidden\":false},{\"_id\":\"6ae40e7ec3b566b041f6c81b\",\"name\":\"Jiteng Mu\",\"hidden\":false},{\"_id\":\"6ae40e7ec3b566b041f6c81c\",\"name\":\"Yuanjun Xiong\",\"hidden\":false},{\"_id\":\"6ae40e7ec3b566b041f6c81d\",\"name\":\"Jianming Zhang\",\"hidden\":false},{\"_id\":\"6ae40e7ec3b566b041f6c81e\",\"name\":\"Zhuowen Tu\",\"hidden\":false},{\"_id\":\"6ae40e7ec3b566b041f6c81f\",\"name\":\"Mingze Xu\",\"hidden\":false}],\"mediaUrls\":[\"https://cdn-uploads.huggingface.co/production/uploads/632a994b4ee2960462ecbf7e/TxvbXSdulQPILjAQkPAnY.png\"],\"publishedAt\":\"2026-10-29T00:00:00.000Z\",\"submittedOnDailyAt\":\"2026-10-30T00:00:00.000Z\",\"title\":\"EditAgent: Rethinking Agentic Image Editing\",\"submittedOnDailyBy\":{\"_id\":\"632a994b4ee2960462ecbf7e\",\"avatarUrl\":\"/avatars/5b462ddc343e035c4d147b521180691b.svg\",\"isPro\":true,\"fullname\":\"Haiyang Xu\",\"user\":\"xuhaiyang3110\",\"type\":\"user\",\"name\":\"xuhaiyang3110\"},\"summary\":\"Agentic image editors decompose complex requests into steps, but when a step fails, they mostly retry with the same narrow set of tools and a judge that scores each output by eye directly. We argue that such agents are bounded by two spaces: the action space of what the agent can do, and the observation space of what its judge can see. On our expert-annotated benchmark, a general-purpose editor yields no usable result in over 40% of the cases, and no judge is reliable across edit types. We present EditAgent, which expands both spaces. It routes each step to general editors and specialized methods, and attaches to each candidate a verification record. With these records, off-the-shelf VLM judges outperform finetuned reward models. Candidates are generated in parallel and judged in one listwise call: experts prefer this to sequential refinement in 78.5% of decided comparisons, at no more than half the judge calls. EditAgent improves two open-weight editors on three benchmarks and serves as a data engine for training stronger editors.\",\"upvotes\":10,\"discussionId\":\"6ae40e7ec3b566b041f6c820\",\"ai_summary\":\"EditAgent broadens agentic image editing by routing steps to diverse editors and using verification records for listwise judging, improving reliability and efficiency.\",\"ai_keywords\":[\"agentic image editors\",\"action space\",\"observation space\",\"VLM judges\",\"verification records\",\"listwise judging\",\"reward models\",\"open-weight editors\"],\"ai_summary_model\":\"thinkingmachines/Inkling-Small\",\"organization\":{\"_id\":\"61e5d14f77496de0a6d95c6b\",\"name\":\"adobe\",\"fullname\":\"Adobe\",\"avatar\":\"https://cdn-avatars.huggingface.co/v1/production/uploads/1645217431826-61e35e517ac6b6d06cfa8081.png\"}},\"publishedAt\":\"2026-10-28T20:00:00.000Z\",\"title\":\"EditAgent: Rethinking Agentic Image Editing\",\"summary\":\"Agentic image editors decompose complex requests into steps, but when a step fails, they mostly retry with the same narrow set of tools and a judge that scores each output by eye directly. We argue that such agents are bounded by two spaces: the action space of what the agent can do, and the observation space of what its judge can see. On our expert-annotated benchmark, a general-purpose editor yields no usable result in over 40% of the cases, and no judge is reliable across edit types. We present EditAgent, which expands both spaces. It routes each step to general editors and specialized methods, and attaches to each candidate a verification record. With these records, off-the-shelf VLM judges outperform finetuned reward models. Candidates are generated in parallel and judged in one listwise call: experts prefer this to sequential refinement in 78.5% of decided comparisons, at no more than half the judge calls. EditAgent improves two open-weight editors on three benchmarks and serves as a data engine for training stronger editors.\",\"mediaUrls\":[\"https://cdn-uploads.huggingface.co/production/uploads/632a994b4ee2960462ecbf7e/TxvbXSdulQPILjAQkPAnY.png\"],\"thumbnail\":\"https://cdn-thumbnails.huggingface.co/social-thumbnails/papers/2610.39664.png\",\"numComments\":2,\"upvoted\":false,\"submittedBy\":{\"_id\":\"632a994b4ee2960462ecbf7e\",\"avatarUrl\":\"/avatars/5b462ddc343e035c4d147b521180691b.svg\",\"fullname\":\"Haiyang Xu\",\"name\":\"xuhaiyang3110\",\"type\":\"user\",\"isPro\":true,\"isHf\":false,\"isHfAdmin\":false,\"isMod\":false,\"followerCount\":2,\"isUserFollowing\":false},\"organization\":{\"_id\":\"61e5d14f77496de0a6d95c6b\",\"name\":\"adobe\",\"fullname\":\"Adobe\",\"avatar\":\"https://cdn-avatars.huggingface.co/v1/production/uploads/1645217431826-61e35e517ac6b6d06cfa8081.png\"},\"isAuthorParticipating\":false},{\"paper\":{\"id\":\"2610.39264\",\"authors\":[{\"_id\":\"6ae43845c3b566b041f6c90d\",\"name\":\"Peitong Duan\",\"hidden\":false},{\"_id\":\"6ae43845c3b566b041f6c90e\",\"name\":\"Khashayar Gatmiry\",\"hidden\":false},{\"_id\":\"6ae43845c3b566b041f6c90f\",\"name\":\"Jeffrey Nichols\",\"hidden\":false},{\"_id\":\"6ae43845c3b566b041f6c910\",\"name\":\"Samy Bengio\",\"hidden\":false},{\"_id\":\"6ae43845c3b566b041f6c911\",\"name\":\"Emmanuel Abbe\",\"hidden\":false}],\"publishedAt\":\"2026-10-29T00:00:00.000Z\",\"submittedOnDailyAt\":\"2026-10-30T00:00:00.000Z\",\"title\":\"Online Dense Reinforcement Learning with Planning-Grounding Agent for UI Navigation on Live IOS Apps\",\"submittedOnDailyBy\":{\"_id\":\"66807cae46fbc5d4417478ea\",\"avatarUrl\":\"/avatars/a745100b85cdb8402e7e37341b8c9e84.svg\",\"isPro\":false,\"fullname\":\"Peitong Duan\",\"user\":\"peitongd\",\"type\":\"user\",\"name\":\"peitongd\"},\"summary\":\"While frontier vision-language models demonstrate strong capabilities in user interface (UI) navigation, their high inference costs prohibit everyday use. Conversely, smaller models trained via supervised fine-tuning (SFT) or offline reinforcement learning often memorize static trajectories, leading to brittle execution and an inability to recover from errors. To address these limitations, we propose a novel online reinforcement learning framework that trains UI navigation agents directly on live iOS applications. Our framework decouples the agent into two specialized components: a Planning agent and a UI Grounding model. We rely on an LLM-as-a-Judge to provide comprehensive reward signals, combining dense, step-level feedback to evaluate intermediate progress with a terminal reward for overall task completion. To effectively leverage this dual reward structure, we optimize the agent using Proximal Policy Optimization (PPO) with step-level advantage estimation. Furthermore, we introduce GiGPPO, a novel neighbor-conditioned value estimation method that improves critic stability by leveraging reward signals from visually similar states across concurrent trajectories. To support this online training paradigm at scale, we introduce an efficient infrastructure that orchestrates hundreds of concurrent virtual iOS devices. Evaluations on rigorous benchmarks, including UINavBench, iOSWorld, and AndroidWorld, demonstrate that our online RL trained agents substantially outperform both SFT and terminal-only RL baselines while accelerating training convergence. Notably, our 8B RL agents achieve nearly a sevenfold improvement over the SFT baseline in out-of-distribution generalization to Android environments, while outperforming Gemini-3.5-Flash and tying with Gemini-3.1-Pro on real iOS applications.\",\"upvotes\":9,\"discussionId\":\"6ae43845c3b566b041f6c912\",\"ai_summary\":\"An online reinforcement learning framework trains UI navigation agents on live mobile apps using a planning agent, UI grounding model, and a novel neighbor-conditioned value estimation method to improve robustness and reduce inference costs.\",\"ai_keywords\":[\"online reinforcement learning\",\"UI navigation\",\"Planning agent\",\"UI Grounding model\",\"LLM-as-a-Judge\",\"Proximal Policy Optimization\",\"step-level advantage estimation\",\"GiGPPO\",\"neighbor-conditioned value estimation\",\"concurrent virtual iOS devices\"],\"ai_summary_model\":\"thinkingmachines/Inkling-Small\",\"organization\":{\"_id\":\"628cbd99ef14f971b69948ab\",\"name\":\"apple\",\"fullname\":\"Apple\",\"avatar\":\"https://cdn-avatars.huggingface.co/v1/production/uploads/1653390727490-5dd96eb166059660ed1ee413.jpeg\"}},\"publishedAt\":\"2026-10-28T20:00:00.000Z\",\"title\":\"Online Dense Reinforcement Learning with Planning-Grounding Agent for UI Navigation on Live IOS Apps\",\"summary\":\"While frontier vision-language models demonstrate strong capabilities in user interface (UI) navigation, their high inference costs prohibit everyday use. Conversely, smaller models trained via supervised fine-tuning (SFT) or offline reinforcement learning often memorize static trajectories, leading to brittle execution and an inability to recover from errors. To address these limitations, we propose a novel online reinforcement learning framework that trains UI navigation agents directly on live iOS applications. Our framework decouples the agent into two specialized components: a Planning agent and a UI Grounding model. We rely on an LLM-as-a-Judge to provide comprehensive reward signals, combining dense, step-level feedback to evaluate intermediate progress with a terminal reward for overall task completion. To effectively leverage this dual reward structure, we optimize the agent using Proximal Policy Optimization (PPO) with step-level advantage estimation. Furthermore, we introduce GiGPPO, a novel neighbor-conditioned value estimation method that improves critic stability by leveraging reward signals from visually similar states across concurrent trajectories. To support this online training paradigm at scale, we introduce an efficient infrastructure that orchestrates hundreds of concurrent virtual iOS devices. Evaluations on rigorous benchmarks, including UINavBench, iOSWorld, and AndroidWorld, demonstrate that our online RL trained agents substantially outperform both SFT and terminal-only RL baselines while accelerating training convergence. Notably, our 8B RL agents achieve nearly a sevenfold improvement over the SFT baseline in out-of-distribution generalization to Android environments, while outperforming Gemini-3.5-Flash and tying with Gemini-3.1-Pro on real iOS applications.\",\"thumbnail\":\"https://cdn-thumbnails.huggingface.co/social-thumbnails/papers/2610.39264.png\",\"numComments\":2,\"upvoted\":false,\"submittedBy\":{\"_id\":\"66807cae46fbc5d4417478ea\",\"avatarUrl\":\"/avatars/a745100b85cdb8402e7e37341b8c9e84.svg\",\"fullname\":\"Peitong Duan\",\"name\":\"peitongd\",\"type\":\"user\",\"isPro\":false,\"isHf\":false,\"isHfAdmin\":false,\"isMod\":false,\"followerCount\":1,\"isUserFollowing\":false},\"organization\":{\"_id\":\"628cbd99ef14f971b69948ab\",\"name\":\"apple\",\"fullname\":\"Apple\",\"avatar\":\"https://cdn-avatars.huggingface.co/v1/production/uploads/1653390727490-5dd96eb166059660ed1ee413.jpeg\"},\"isAuthorParticipating\":false},{\"paper\":{\"id\":\"2610.40564\",\"authors\":[{\"_id\":\"6ae4b5b8ac2aed10b8c1c1a8\",\"user\":{\"_id\":\"6611c797a0af69dc7dd8278b\",\"avatarUrl\":\"/avatars/f4c1e834056a95286ddc22a9cb47d163.svg\",\"isPro\":false,\"fullname\":\"Yuda Song\",\"user\":\"sfanm\",\"type\":\"user\",\"name\":\"sfanm\"},\"name\":\"Yuda Song\",\"status\":\"claimed_verified\",\"statusLastChangedAt\":\"2026-10-30T16:08:39.840Z\",\"hidden\":false},{\"_id\":\"6ae4b5b8ac2aed10b8c1c1a9\",\"user\":{\"_id\":\"66f61744c6cef3d86ee4fe46\",\"avatarUrl\":\"/avatars/6301a37b8d9c5f16fe9d27602af6835f.svg\",\"isPro\":false,\"fullname\":\"Alvin\",\"user\":\"AZH04\",\"type\":\"user\",\"name\":\"AZH04\"},\"name\":\"Alvin Zhang\",\"status\":\"claimed_verified\",\"statusLastChangedAt\":\"2026-10-30T16:08:37.424Z\",\"hidden\":false},{\"_id\":\"6ae4b5b8ac2aed10b8c1c1aa\",\"name\":\"Fahim Tajwar\",\"hidden\":false},{\"_id\":\"6ae4b5b8ac2aed10b8c1c1ab\",\"name\":\"Daman Arora\",\"hidden\":false},{\"_id\":\"6ae4b5b8ac2aed10b8c1c1ac\",\"name\":\"Jeff Schneider\",\"hidden\":false},{\"_id\":\"6ae4b5b8ac2aed10b8c1c1ad\",\"name\":\"Daniel Khashabi\",\"hidden\":false},{\"_id\":\"6ae4b5b8ac2aed10b8c1c1ae\",\"name\":\"Ruslan Salakhutdinov\",\"hidden\":false},{\"_id\":\"6ae4b5b8ac2aed10b8c1c1af\",\"name\":\"J. Andrew Bagnell\",\"hidden\":false},{\"_id\":\"6ae4b5b8ac2aed10b8c1c1b0\",\"name\":\"Aarti Singh\",\"hidden\":false},{\"_id\":\"6ae4b5b8ac2aed10b8c1c1b1\",\"name\":\"Andrea Zanette\",\"hidden\":false}],\"publishedAt\":\"2026-10-29T00:00:00.000Z\",\"submittedOnDailyAt\":\"2026-10-30T00:00:00.000Z\",\"title\":\"Training Foundation Models with a Single Algorithm\",\"submittedOnDailyBy\":{\"_id\":\"66f61744c6cef3d86ee4fe46\",\"avatarUrl\":\"/avatars/6301a37b8d9c5f16fe9d27602af6835f.svg\",\"isPro\":false,\"fullname\":\"Alvin\",\"user\":\"AZH04\",\"type\":\"user\",\"name\":\"AZH04\"},\"summary\":\"Foundation models are typically trained in stages: pretraining, midtraining, supervised fine-tuning (SFT), and reinforcement learning (RL), with changes in both data and learning algorithms. We show that this algorithmic separation is not fundamental. By deriving suitable RL objectives as maximum-likelihood fitting to model-generated target distributions, we place all of these stages under a single likelihood-based training algorithm, unified training. This perspective turns the foundation-model training lifecycle into a problem of choosing which data to train on, in what proportions, and when, with conventional staged training as a special case. We instantiate this framework in a unified training system that mixes static data and RL generations within a single trainer. Across controlled and real-world experiments, unified training improves the accuracy-compute tradeoff and task coverage while preserving broader capabilities, and provides a stronger starting point for continual learning. This work lays the groundwork for a unified science of foundation-model training, connecting mathematical principles, training infrastructure, and the development of model capabilities.\",\"upvotes\":8,\"discussionId\":\"6ae4b5b9ac2aed10b8c1c1b2\",\"projectPage\":\"https://yudasong.github.io/unified-training/\",\"githubRepo\":\"https://github.com/yudasong/unified-training\",\"githubRepoAddedBy\":\"user\",\"ai_summary\":\"Foundation-model training stages can be unified under a single likelihood-based algorithm that blends static data and reinforcement learning generations, improving efficiency and capability retention.\",\"ai_keywords\":[\"maximum-likelihood fitting\",\"RL objectives\",\"unified training\",\"foundation models\",\"reinforcement learning\",\"continual learning\"],\"ai_summary_model\":\"thinkingmachines/Inkling-Small\",\"githubStars\":17,\"organization\":{\"_id\":\"691d9a1012cc4d473e1c862f\",\"name\":\"CarnegieMellonU\",\"fullname\":\"Carnegie Mellon University\",\"avatar\":\"https://cdn-avatars.huggingface.co/v1/production/uploads/68e396f2b5bb631e9b2fac9a/6I146aJvxxlRCEbYFFAeQ.png\"}},\"publishedAt\":\"2026-10-28T20:00:00.000Z\",\"title\":\"Training Foundation Models with a Single Algorithm\",\"summary\":\"Foundation models are typically trained in stages: pretraining, midtraining, supervised fine-tuning (SFT), and reinforcement learning (RL), with changes in both data and learning algorithms. We show that this algorithmic separation is not fundamental. By deriving suitable RL objectives as maximum-likelihood fitting to model-generated target distributions, we place all of these stages under a single likelihood-based training algorithm, unified training. This perspective turns the foundation-model training lifecycle into a problem of choosing which data to train on, in what proportions, and when, with conventional staged training as a special case. We instantiate this framework in a unified training system that mixes static data and RL generations within a single trainer. Across controlled and real-world experiments, unified training improves the accuracy-compute tradeoff and task coverage while preserving broader capabilities, and provides a stronger starting point for continual learning. This work lays the groundwork for a unified science of foundation-model training, connecting mathematical principles, training infrastructure, and the development of model capabilities.\",\"thumbnail\":\"https://cdn-thumbnails.huggingface.co/social-thumbnails/papers/2610.40564.png\",\"numComments\":3,\"upvoted\":false,\"submittedBy\":{\"_id\":\"66f61744c6cef3d86ee4fe46\",\"avatarUrl\":\"/avatars/6301a37b8d9c5f16fe9d27602af6835f.svg\",\"fullname\":\"Alvin\",\"name\":\"AZH04\",\"type\":\"user\",\"isPro\":false,\"isHf\":false,\"isHfAdmin\":false,\"isMod\":false,\"followerCount\":1,\"isUserFollowing\":false},\"organization\":{\"_id\":\"691d9a1012cc4d473e1c862f\",\"name\":\"CarnegieMellonU\",\"fullname\":\"Carnegie Mellon University\",\"avatar\":\"https://cdn-avatars.huggingface.co/v1/production/uploads/68e396f2b5bb631e9b2fac9a/6I146aJvxxlRCEbYFFAeQ.png\"},\"isAuthorParticipating\":true},{\"paper\":{\"id\":\"2610.40453\",\"authors\":[{\"_id\":\"6ae433e1c3b566b041f6c8fc\",\"user\":{\"_id\":\"652f93640b13cc0739961f37\",\"avatarUrl\":\"https://cdn-avatars.huggingface.co/v1/production/uploads/652f93640b13cc0739961f37/FDdWv7RKUyrro8rxFmiRE.png\",\"isPro\":false,\"fullname\":\"YueGong\",\"user\":\"Arthuringy\",\"type\":\"user\",\"name\":\"Arthuringy\"},\"name\":\"Yue Gong\",\"status\":\"claimed_verified\",\"statusLastChangedAt\":\"2026-10-30T08:45:05.133Z\",\"hidden\":false},{\"_id\":\"6ae433e1c3b566b041f6c8fd\",\"name\":\"Le Zhang\",\"hidden\":false},{\"_id\":\"6ae433e1c3b566b041f6c8fe\",\"name\":\"Rui Chen\",\"hidden\":false},{\"_id\":\"6ae433e1c3b566b041f6c8ff\",\"name\":\"Song Chang\",\"hidden\":false},{\"_id\":\"6ae433e1c3b566b041f6c900\",\"name\":\"Xiaochao Qu\",\"hidden\":false},{\"_id\":\"6ae433e1c3b566b041f6c901\",\"name\":\"Luoqi Liu\",\"hidden\":false},{\"_id\":\"6ae433e1c3b566b041f6c902\",\"name\":\"Ting Liu\",\"hidden\":false},{\"_id\":\"6ae433e1c3b566b041f6c903\",\"name\":\"Yebin Liu\",\"hidden\":false}],\"publishedAt\":\"2026-10-29T00:00:00.000Z\",\"submittedOnDailyAt\":\"2026-10-30T00:00:00.000Z\",\"title\":\"PhysInsert: Physically Plausible Object Insertion with Geometric Priors and Bidirectional Object-Scene Interactions\",\"submittedOnDailyBy\":{\"_id\":\"66fd43e80cde4879f9aeca01\",\"avatarUrl\":\"/avatars/7fc9590d78efe421ad742c42a43c7618.svg\",\"isPro\":false,\"fullname\":\"Hongyu Li\",\"user\":\"appletea2333\",\"type\":\"user\",\"name\":\"appletea2333\"},\"summary\":\"We present PhysInsert, a framework for physically plausible reference-based object insertion that jointly adapts objects and their surrounding scenes. Existing methods often rely on masked inputs, generation-based pairs, or training pairs with limited viewpoint variation, leaving pose and illumination adaptation and bidirectional object-scene interactions insufficiently constrained. We construct the Insert World dataset with strictly aligned Blender-rendered counterfactual pairs and object references across viewpoints and lighting conditions. Building on these data, Geometry-Guided Adaptation couples a VGGT geometry stream with image and text streams under target-scene feature supervision to guide spatially consistent insertion. Complementary 2D Object-Scene Spatial Supervision jointly supervises object occupancy and environmental changes while grounding spatial instructions in object regions through image-text attention. We introduce PhysInsert-Bench with dedicated cross-environment and cross-view cases requiring illumination and pose adaptation beyond appearance copying. Experiments on PhysInsert-Bench and DreamBooth demonstrate improved image fidelity; a user study further shows more accurate placement and improved lighting integration, perspective, and pose plausibility.\",\"upvotes\":8,\"discussionId\":\"6ae433e2c3b566b041f6c904\",\"projectPage\":\"https://arthuring.github.io/PhysInsert/\",\"ai_summary\":\"PhysInsert enables physically plausible object insertion by jointly adapting objects and scenes using geometry-guided adaptation and spatial supervision on aligned counterfactual data.\",\"ai_keywords\":[\"PhysInsert\",\"Geometry-Guided Adaptation\",\"VGGT\",\"cross-attention\",\"object-scene spatial supervision\",\"counterfactual pairs\",\"illumination adaptation\",\"pose adaptation\"],\"ai_summary_model\":\"thinkingmachines/Inkling-Small\"},\"publishedAt\":\"2026-10-28T20:00:00.000Z\",\"title\":\"PhysInsert: Physically Plausible Object Insertion with Geometric Priors and Bidirectional Object-Scene Interactions\",\"summary\":\"We present PhysInsert, a framework for physically plausible reference-based object insertion that jointly adapts objects and their surrounding scenes. Existing methods often rely on masked inputs, generation-based pairs, or training pairs with limited viewpoint variation, leaving pose and illumination adaptation and bidirectional object-scene interactions insufficiently constrained. We construct the Insert World dataset with strictly aligned Blender-rendered counterfactual pairs and object references across viewpoints and lighting conditions. Building on these data, Geometry-Guided Adaptation couples a VGGT geometry stream with image and text streams under target-scene feature supervision to guide spatially consistent insertion. Complementary 2D Object-Scene Spatial Supervision jointly supervises object occupancy and environmental changes while grounding spatial instructions in object regions through image-text attention. We introduce PhysInsert-Bench with dedicated cross-environment and cross-view cases requiring illumination and pose adaptation beyond appearance copying. Experiments on PhysInsert-Bench and DreamBooth demonstrate improved image fidelity; a user study further shows more accurate placement and improved lighting integration, perspective, and pose plausibility.\",\"thumbnail\":\"https://cdn-thumbnails.huggingface.co/social-thumbnails/papers/2610.40453.png\",\"numComments\":2,\"upvoted\":false,\"submittedBy\":{\"_id\":\"66fd43e80cde4879f9aeca01\",\"avatarUrl\":\"/avatars/7fc9590d78efe421ad742c42a43c7618.svg\",\"fullname\":\"Hongyu Li\",\"name\":\"appletea2333\",\"type\":\"user\",\"isPro\":false,\"isHf\":false,\"isHfAdmin\":false,\"isMod\":false,\"followerCount\":7,\"isUserFollowing\":false},\"isAuthorParticipating\":false},{\"paper\":{\"id\":\"2610.39287\",\"authors\":[{\"_id\":\"6ae45611c3b566b041f6ca32\",\"name\":\"Junli Liu\",\"hidden\":false},{\"_id\":\"6ae45611c3b566b041f6ca33\",\"name\":\"Zhongrui You\",\"hidden\":false},{\"_id\":\"6ae45611c3b566b041f6ca34\",\"name\":\"Chenhui Li\",\"hidden\":false},{\"_id\":\"6ae45611c3b566b041f6ca35\",\"name\":\"Zhen Li\",\"hidden\":false},{\"_id\":\"6ae45611c3b566b041f6ca36\",\"name\":\"Yuyang Tian\",\"hidden\":false},{\"_id\":\"6ae45611c3b566b041f6ca37\",\"name\":\"Qizhen Chen\",\"hidden\":false},{\"_id\":\"6ae45611c3b566b041f6ca38\",\"name\":\"Delin Qu\",\"hidden\":false},{\"_id\":\"6ae45611c3b566b041f6ca39\",\"name\":\"Haoming Song\",\"hidden\":false},{\"_id\":\"6ae45611c3b566b041f6ca3a\",\"name\":\"Dong Wang\",\"hidden\":false},{\"_id\":\"6ae45611c3b566b041f6ca3b\",\"name\":\"Xuelong Li\",\"hidden\":false},{\"_id\":\"6ae45611c3b566b041f6ca3c\",\"name\":\"Zhigang Wang\",\"hidden\":false},{\"_id\":\"6ae45611c3b566b041f6ca3d\",\"name\":\"Bin Zhao\",\"hidden\":false}],\"publishedAt\":\"2026-10-29T00:00:00.000Z\",\"submittedOnDailyAt\":\"2026-10-30T00:00:00.000Z\",\"title\":\"OpenFlyV2: An onboard vision-language-action model for aerial perception and navigation\",\"submittedOnDailyBy\":{\"_id\":\"656850a60e4b5ff9d552e825\",\"avatarUrl\":\"/avatars/691e86538943ee32015f23da63fd68e8.svg\",\"isPro\":false,\"fullname\":\"Junli Liu\",\"user\":\"Ideallll\",\"type\":\"user\",\"name\":\"Ideallll\"},\"summary\":\"Human cognition flexibly draws on a shared perceptual foundation to understand environments, attend to referenced entities, reason about spatial relationships, and act. Endowing aerial robots with an analogous functional organization remains challenging, as existing systems are often specialized for individual capabilities, dependent on offboard computation, or unable to sustain closed-loop operation on resource-constrained platforms. We present OpenFlyV2, a unified onboard vision-language-action architecture that uses a shared vision-language representation to support semantic understanding, language-grounded perception, spatial reasoning, and continuous aerial control within a single model. A compact action expert converts the shared multimodal context into short-horizon flight waypoints, while motion-aware temporal memory and action priors reduce the computational cost of closed-loop execution. We construct , a simulation-based dataset comprising approximately 70,000 navigation trajectories and 60,000 vision-language instruction--response pairs. OpenFlyV2 generates action chunks, each comprising 12 waypoints, entirely onboard at 10 Hz using an NVIDIA Jetson Orin NX. It transfers zero-shot from simulation to physical quadrotor flight, achieving a 48\\\\% navigation success rate across four real-world environments without real-world aerial training data. Extensive results demonstrate that broad vision-language competence and continuous closed-loop flight can coexist within a single model on a resource-constrained aerial platform. Code will be made publicly available at https://github.com/Ideal-ljl/AeroBrain.\",\"upvotes\":5,\"discussionId\":\"6ae45612c3b566b041f6ca3e\",\"projectPage\":\"https://ideal-ljl.github.io/AeroBrain/\",\"githubRepo\":\"https://github.com/Ideal-ljl/AeroBrain\",\"githubRepoAddedBy\":\"user\",\"ai_summary\":\"OpenFlyV2 is a unified onboard vision-language-action model that enables real-time aerial navigation and reasoning on resource-constrained hardware using shared multimodal representations.\",\"ai_keywords\":[\"vision-language-action\",\"vision-language representation\",\"multimodal context\",\"action expert\",\"temporal memory\",\"action priors\",\"closed-loop execution\",\"zero-shot sim-to-real transfer\"],\"ai_summary_model\":\"thinkingmachines/Inkling-Small\",\"githubStars\":3,\"organization\":{\"_id\":\"66bada488c4119f92e6e8b81\",\"name\":\"IPEC-COMMUNITY\",\"fullname\":\"IPEC at Shanghai AI Laboratory\",\"avatar\":\"https://cdn-avatars.huggingface.co/v1/production/uploads/66bad9725b3a757a7414fb83/m9eXMGXJj7VwhQYGJUmYb.png\"}},\"publishedAt\":\"2026-10-28T20:00:00.000Z\",\"title\":\"OpenFlyV2: An onboard vision-language-action model for aerial perception and navigation\",\"summary\":\"Human cognition flexibly draws on a shared perceptual foundation to understand environments, attend to referenced entities, reason about spatial relationships, and act. Endowing aerial robots with an analogous functional organization remains challenging, as existing systems are often specialized for individual capabilities, dependent on offboard computation, or unable to sustain closed-loop operation on resource-constrained platforms. We present OpenFlyV2, a unified onboard vision-language-action architecture that uses a shared vision-language representation to support semantic understanding, language-grounded perception, spatial reasoning, and continuous aerial control within a single model. A compact action expert converts the shared multimodal context into short-horizon flight waypoints, while motion-aware temporal memory and action priors reduce the computational cost of closed-loop execution. We construct , a simulation-based dataset comprising approximately 70,000 navigation trajectories and 60,000 vision-language instruction--response pairs. OpenFlyV2 generates action chunks, each comprising 12 waypoints, entirely onboard at 10 Hz using an NVIDIA Jetson Orin NX. It transfers zero-shot from simulation to physical quadrotor flight, achieving a 48\\\\% navigation success rate across four real-world environments without real-world aerial training data. Extensive results demonstrate that broad vision-language competence and continuous closed-loop flight can coexist within a single model on a resource-constrained aerial platform. Code will be made publicly available at https://github.com/Ideal-ljl/AeroBrain.\",\"thumbnail\":\"https://cdn-thumbnails.huggingface.co/social-thumbnails/papers/2610.39287.png\",\"numComments\":2,\"upvoted\":false,\"submittedBy\":{\"_id\":\"656850a60e4b5ff9d552e825\",\"avatarUrl\":\"/avatars/691e86538943ee32015f23da63fd68e8.svg\",\"fullname\":\"Junli Liu\",\"name\":\"Ideallll\",\"type\":\"user\",\"isPro\":false,\"isHf\":false,\"isHfAdmin\":false,\"isMod\":false,\"followerCount\":2,\"isUserFollowing\":false},\"organization\":{\"_id\":\"66bada488c4119f92e6e8b81\",\"name\":\"IPEC-COMMUNITY\",\"fullname\":\"IPEC at Shanghai AI Laboratory\",\"avatar\":\"https://cdn-avatars.huggingface.co/v1/production/uploads/66bad9725b3a757a7414fb83/m9eXMGXJj7VwhQYGJUmYb.png\"},\"isAuthorParticipating\":false},{\"paper\":{\"id\":\"2610.39663\",\"authors\":[{\"_id\":\"6ae450a5c3b566b041f6ca15\",\"name\":\"Bin Xu\",\"hidden\":false},{\"_id\":\"6ae450a5c3b566b041f6ca16\",\"name\":\"Mo Zhang\",\"hidden\":false},{\"_id\":\"6ae450a5c3b566b041f6ca17\",\"name\":\"Zhengfeiyang Zhang\",\"hidden\":false},{\"_id\":\"6ae450a5c3b566b041f6ca18\",\"name\":\"Xinfeng Yang\",\"hidden\":false},{\"_id\":\"6ae450a5c3b566b041f6ca19\",\"name\":\"Yixing Ma\",\"hidden\":false},{\"_id\":\"6ae450a5c3b566b041f6ca1a\",\"name\":\"Wei Wang\",\"hidden\":false},{\"_id\":\"6ae450a5c3b566b041f6ca1b\",\"name\":\"HeXin Wang\",\"hidden\":false},{\"_id\":\"6ae450a5c3b566b041f6ca1c\",\"name\":\"Pengfei Zhou\",\"hidden\":false},{\"_id\":\"6ae450a5c3b566b041f6ca1d\",\"name\":\"Wangbo Zhao\",\"hidden\":false}],\"publishedAt\":\"2026-10-29T00:00:00.000Z\",\"submittedOnDailyAt\":\"2026-10-30T00:00:00.000Z\",\"title\":\"AB-Attention: Accurate Block Attention for Accelerating Video Diffusion Transformers\",\"submittedOnDailyBy\":{\"_id\":\"69551af665df8d6e64034222\",\"avatarUrl\":\"/avatars/02c23ea68bd440751189a523622f5b20.svg\",\"isPro\":false,\"fullname\":\"Xubin\",\"user\":\"xubinknight\",\"type\":\"user\",\"name\":\"xubinknight\"},\"summary\":\"Video diffusion transformers generate high-quality videos, but the quadratic cost of 3D full attention dominates their inference. Dynamic block-sparse attention reduces this cost by computing exact attention only on key/value blocks selected from current activations. However, its fidelity degrades under aggressive sparsity: blocks are scored from coarse summaries or formed by costly global clustering, pruned blocks vanish from the softmax, and whole tiles are kept or dropped even when only a few of their keys matter. To address these limitations, we propose Accurate Block Attention (AB-Attention), a training-free method that accurately approximates dense attention at high sparsity. Its Intra-Block Clustering Selector (IBCS) estimates block importance faithfully yet cheaply by clustering queries only within each query block, without permuting tokens. Sampling with Inclusion-Probability Weighting (SIP) then samples blocks in proportion to these estimates and reweights them by inverse inclusion probability, so that one pass estimates dense attention rather than truncating it. Finally, its kernel gathers key/value blocks much smaller than a tile into full tensor-core tiles, refining selection at no extra tensor-core cost. On Wan2.1 and HunyuanVideo, AB-Attention skips 89--92\\\\% of attention, whereas the baselines compute 1.8--3.6times as much, yet it still improves PSNR by up to 4.4~dB over the strongest baseline and achieves up to 2.23times end-to-end speedup. Our code is available at https://github.com/InfRec-Cardinal/AB-Attention.\",\"upvotes\":5,\"discussionId\":\"6ae450a5c3b566b041f6ca1e\",\"projectPage\":\"https://infrec-cardinal.github.io/AB-Attention/\",\"githubRepo\":\"https://github.com/InfRec-Cardinal/AB-Attention\",\"githubRepoAddedBy\":\"user\",\"ai_summary\":\"AB-Attention approximates dense video diffusion attention with high sparsity via intra-block clustering, weighted sampling, and fine-grained kernel gathering, improving fidelity and speed.\",\"ai_keywords\":[\"video diffusion transformers\",\"3D full attention\",\"dynamic block-sparse attention\",\"AB-Attention\",\"Intra-Block Clustering Selector\",\"Inclusion-Probability Weighting\",\"tensor-core tiles\"],\"ai_summary_model\":\"thinkingmachines/Inkling-Small\",\"githubStars\":24},\"publishedAt\":\"2026-10-28T20:00:00.000Z\",\"title\":\"AB-Attention: Accurate Block Attention for Accelerating Video Diffusion Transformers\",\"summary\":\"Video diffusion transformers generate high-quality videos, but the quadratic cost of 3D full attention dominates their inference. Dynamic block-sparse attention reduces this cost by computing exact attention only on key/value blocks selected from current activations. However, its fidelity degrades under aggressive sparsity: blocks are scored from coarse summaries or formed by costly global clustering, pruned blocks vanish from the softmax, and whole tiles are kept or dropped even when only a few of their keys matter. To address these limitations, we propose Accurate Block Attention (AB-Attention), a training-free method that accurately approximates dense attention at high sparsity. Its Intra-Block Clustering Selector (IBCS) estimates block importance faithfully yet cheaply by clustering queries only within each query block, without permuting tokens. Sampling with Inclusion-Probability Weighting (SIP) then samples blocks in proportion to these estimates and reweights them by inverse inclusion probability, so that one pass estimates dense attention rather than truncating it. Finally, its kernel gathers key/value blocks much smaller than a tile into full tensor-core tiles, refining selection at no extra tensor-core cost. On Wan2.1 and HunyuanVideo, AB-Attention skips 89--92\\\\% of attention, whereas the baselines compute 1.8--3.6times as much, yet it still improves PSNR by up to 4.4~dB over the strongest baseline and achieves up to 2.23times end-to-end speedup. Our code is available at https://github.com/InfRec-Cardinal/AB-Attention.\",\"thumbnail\":\"https://cdn-thumbnails.huggingface.co/social-thumbnails/papers/2610.39663.png\",\"numComments\":2,\"upvoted\":false,\"submittedBy\":{\"_id\":\"69551af665df8d6e64034222\",\"avatarUrl\":\"/avatars/02c23ea68bd440751189a523622f5b20.svg\",\"fullname\":\"Xubin\",\"name\":\"xubinknight\",\"type\":\"user\",\"isPro\":false,\"isHf\":false,\"isHfAdmin\":false,\"isMod\":false,\"followerCount\":1,\"isUserFollowing\":false},\"isAuthorParticipating\":false},{\"paper\":{\"id\":\"2610.40362\",\"authors\":[{\"_id\":\"6ae428cec3b566b041f6c8a8\",\"name\":\"Qifan Yu\",\"hidden\":false},{\"_id\":\"6ae428cec3b566b041f6c8a9\",\"name\":\"Xinyu Ma\",\"hidden\":false},{\"_id\":\"6ae428cec3b566b041f6c8aa\",\"name\":\"Xingyan Bin\",\"hidden\":false},{\"_id\":\"6ae428cec3b566b041f6c8ab\",\"name\":\"Di He\",\"hidden\":false}],\"publishedAt\":\"2026-10-29T00:00:00.000Z\",\"submittedOnDailyAt\":\"2026-10-30T00:00:00.000Z\",\"title\":\"Your LR Schedule May Not Be Principled: Effective Learning Rate Scheduling in Weight-Normalized Training\",\"submittedOnDailyBy\":{\"_id\":\"650b03e64e6a363198b8af6b\",\"avatarUrl\":\"/avatars/365b0900bdce90a42a5483f72329cfa5.svg\",\"isPro\":false,\"fullname\":\"Qifan Yu\",\"user\":\"CauchyLovesU\",\"type\":\"user\",\"name\":\"CauchyLovesU\"},\"summary\":\"Weight-normalized (WN) training is emerging as a compelling alternative to conventional weight decay (WD), with recent studies reporting substantial pretraining loss gains. We show that these gains are driven primarily by the induced effective learning rate (ELR) trajectory rather than by normalization alone. Matching ELR trajectories largely collapses the training loss dynamics of WN and WD. Schedule design should therefore target ELR rather than the nominal learning rate (LR) alone. Building on this principle, we propose AIR (Affine Inverse-square-Root decay), which combines a theoretically derived 1/t decay with an affine adjustment to satisfy prescribed peak and terminal LRs over a finite training horizon. We further identify late-stage ELR scheduling as a key source of its gains, because a shared late schedule attenuates the effect of earlier LR choices. Experiments on 128 B-token Mixture-of-Experts pretraining demonstrate that AIR outperforms cosine and other candidate schedules in training loss, downstream performance, and hyperparameter robustness, reducing terminal training loss by approximately 0.01 relative to cosine at matched cumulative LR. Applying the same ELR trajectory to WD reproduces the corresponding training dynamics, showing that the benefits extend beyond fixed-norm training and establishing ELR as the principled scheduling object.\",\"upvotes\":5,\"discussionId\":\"6ae428cec3b566b041f6c8ac\",\"ai_summary\":\"Effective learning rate trajectories primarily drive weight-normalized training gains, motivating an affine inverse-square-root schedule that improves large-scale pretraining loss and robustness.\",\"ai_keywords\":[\"weight-normalized training\",\"weight decay\",\"effective learning rate\",\"affine inverse-square-root decay\",\"AIR\",\"Mixture-of-Experts\",\"cosine schedule\",\"hyperparameter robustness\"],\"ai_summary_model\":\"thinkingmachines/Inkling-Small\",\"organization\":{\"_id\":\"67d1140985ea0644e2f14b99\",\"name\":\"ByteDance-Seed\",\"fullname\":\"ByteDance Seed\",\"avatar\":\"https://cdn-avatars.huggingface.co/v1/production/uploads/6535c9e88bde2fae19b6fb25/flkDUqd_YEuFsjeNET3r-.png\"}},\"publishedAt\":\"2026-10-28T20:00:00.000Z\",\"title\":\"Your LR Schedule May Not Be Principled: Effective Learning Rate Scheduling in Weight-Normalized Training\",\"summary\":\"Weight-normalized (WN) training is emerging as a compelling alternative to conventional weight decay (WD), with recent studies reporting substantial pretraining loss gains. We show that these gains are driven primarily by the induced effective learning rate (ELR) trajectory rather than by normalization alone. Matching ELR trajectories largely collapses the training loss dynamics of WN and WD. Schedule design should therefore target ELR rather than the nominal learning rate (LR) alone. Building on this principle, we propose AIR (Affine Inverse-square-Root decay), which combines a theoretically derived 1/t decay with an affine adjustment to satisfy prescribed peak and terminal LRs over a finite training horizon. We further identify late-stage ELR scheduling as a key source of its gains, because a shared late schedule attenuates the effect of earlier LR choices. Experiments on 128 B-token Mixture-of-Experts pretraining demonstrate that AIR outperforms cosine and other candidate schedules in training loss, downstream performance, and hyperparameter robustness, reducing terminal training loss by approximately 0.01 relative to cosine at matched cumulative LR. Applying the same ELR trajectory to WD reproduces the corresponding training dynamics, showing that the benefits extend beyond fixed-norm training and establishing ELR as the principled scheduling object.\",\"thumbnail\":\"https://cdn-thumbnails.huggingface.co/social-thumbnails/papers/2610.40362.png\",\"numComments\":2,\"upvoted\":false,\"submittedBy\":{\"_id\":\"650b03e64e6a363198b8af6b\",\"avatarUrl\":\"/avatars/365b0900bdce90a42a5483f72329cfa5.svg\",\"fullname\":\"Qifan Yu\",\"name\":\"CauchyLovesU\",\"type\":\"user\",\"isPro\":false,\"isHf\":false,\"isHfAdmin\":false,\"isMod\":false,\"followerCount\":2,\"isUserFollowing\":false},\"organization\":{\"_id\":\"67d1140985ea0644e2f14b99\",\"name\":\"ByteDance-Seed\",\"fullname\":\"ByteDance Seed\",\"avatar\":\"https://cdn-avatars.huggingface.co/v1/production/uploads/6535c9e88bde2fae19b6fb25/flkDUqd_YEuFsjeNET3r-.png\"},\"isAuthorParticipating\":false},{\"paper\":{\"id\":\"2609.05881\",\"authors\":[{\"_id\":\"6ae16583a5eb0647c29072d7\",\"user\":{\"_id\":\"6a98fc48a1cd458a22800a3f\",\"avatarUrl\":\"/avatars/a7d861498cb5d773fa5fd7c89a8c48ac.svg\",\"isPro\":false,\"fullname\":\"Aditi patodiya\",\"user\":\"aditiPatodiya\",\"type\":\"user\",\"name\":\"aditiPatodiya\"},\"name\":\"Aditi Patodiya\",\"status\":\"claimed_verified\",\"statusLastChangedAt\":\"2026-10-28T08:45:05.449Z\",\"hidden\":false}],\"mediaUrls\":[\"https://cdn-uploads.huggingface.co/production/uploads/6a98fc48a1cd458a22800a3f/mdJcewSrPRFUFNfFL_2De.png\"],\"publishedAt\":\"2026-10-25T00:00:00.000Z\",\"submittedOnDailyAt\":\"2026-10-30T00:00:00.000Z\",\"title\":\"Broken on Arrival: Non-Functional Quantized LLM Artifacts in Public Model Registries and How to Catch Them\",\"submittedOnDailyBy\":{\"_id\":\"6a98fc48a1cd458a22800a3f\",\"avatarUrl\":\"/avatars/a7d861498cb5d773fa5fd7c89a8c48ac.svg\",\"isPro\":false,\"fullname\":\"Aditi patodiya\",\"user\":\"aditiPatodiya\",\"type\":\"user\",\"name\":\"aditiPatodiya\"},\"summary\":\"Developers download quantized large language models (LLMs) from public registries to run them locally. None of the registries we examined documented functional testing of their files. We tested all 305 eligible code-capable GGUF tags (the llama.cpp format) across 15 model lines in the official Ollama library, plus 22 popular Hugging Face community files. A 15-task code screen identified suspects, which we retested on all 164 HumanEval+ tasks. Seven official tags (2.3%) solved at most six of them. All were low-bit quantizations of models with at most four billion parameters. We rebuilt five suspect files from the original weights. All five registry files matched our uncalibrated rebuilds tensor for tensor, so none was corrupted. Exact reproducibility did not imply function. With the weights, toolchain and tensor mix held fixed, adding an importance matrix raised three Qwen2.5-Coder-3B builds from zero to 76-113 solved tasks. The same uncalibrated recipe left the smaller Qwen2.5-Coder-1.5B functional, showing that the recipe alone does not predict failure. None of 66 sampled tags that passed the screen was non-functional. We release the audit data and the quantcheck tool and recommend screening low-bit tags before publication, then calibrating or withholding failures.\",\"upvotes\":4,\"discussionId\":\"6ae16584a5eb0647c29072d8\",\"projectPage\":\"https://huggingface.co/datasets/aditiPatodiya/quantcheck-audit\",\"githubRepo\":\"https://github.com/aditi-p31/quantcheck\",\"githubRepoAddedBy\":\"user\",\"ai_summary\":\"Quantized small language model files in public registries can be non-functional due to uncalibrated quantization recipes, and screening plus calibration is recommended before release.\",\"ai_keywords\":[\"quantized large language models\",\"GGUF\",\"llama.cpp\",\"Ollama\",\"Hugging Face\",\"HumanEval+\",\"low-bit quantization\",\"importance matrix\",\"Qwen2.5-Coder\",\"tensor\"],\"ai_summary_model\":\"thinkingmachines/Inkling-Small\",\"githubStars\":0},\"publishedAt\":\"2026-10-24T20:00:00.000Z\",\"title\":\"Broken on Arrival: Non-Functional Quantized LLM Artifacts in Public Model Registries and How to Catch Them\",\"summary\":\"Developers download quantized large language models (LLMs) from public registries to run them locally. None of the registries we examined documented functional testing of their files. We tested all 305 eligible code-capable GGUF tags (the llama.cpp format) across 15 model lines in the official Ollama library, plus 22 popular Hugging Face community files. A 15-task code screen identified suspects, which we retested on all 164 HumanEval+ tasks. Seven official tags (2.3%) solved at most six of them. All were low-bit quantizations of models with at most four billion parameters. We rebuilt five suspect files from the original weights. All five registry files matched our uncalibrated rebuilds tensor for tensor, so none was corrupted. Exact reproducibility did not imply function. With the weights, toolchain and tensor mix held fixed, adding an importance matrix raised three Qwen2.5-Coder-3B builds from zero to 76-113 solved tasks. The same uncalibrated recipe left the smaller Qwen2.5-Coder-1.5B functional, showing that the recipe alone does not predict failure. None of 66 sampled tags that passed the screen was non-functional. We release the audit data and the quantcheck tool and recommend screening low-bit tags before publication, then calibrating or withholding failures.\",\"mediaUrls\":[\"https://cdn-uploads.huggingface.co/production/uploads/6a98fc48a1cd458a22800a3f/mdJcewSrPRFUFNfFL_2De.png\"],\"thumbnail\":\"https://cdn-thumbnails.huggingface.co/social-thumbnails/papers/2609.05881.png\",\"numComments\":3,\"upvoted\":false,\"submittedBy\":{\"_id\":\"6a98fc48a1cd458a22800a3f\",\"avatarUrl\":\"/avatars/a7d861498cb5d773fa5fd7c89a8c48ac.svg\",\"fullname\":\"Aditi patodiya\",\"name\":\"aditiPatodiya\",\"type\":\"user\",\"isPro\":false,\"isHf\":false,\"isHfAdmin\":false,\"isMod\":false,\"isUserFollowing\":false},\"isAuthorParticipating\":true},{\"paper\":{\"id\":\"2610.38174\",\"authors\":[{\"_id\":\"6ae4a548ac2aed10b8c1c15b\",\"name\":\"Debargha Ganguly\",\"hidden\":false},{\"_id\":\"6ae4a548ac2aed10b8c1c15c\",\"name\":\"Divya Raghunathan\",\"hidden\":false},{\"_id\":\"6ae4a548ac2aed10b8c1c15d\",\"name\":\"Dejan Jovanović\",\"hidden\":false},{\"_id\":\"6ae4a548ac2aed10b8c1c15e\",\"name\":\"Vikash Singh\",\"hidden\":false},{\"_id\":\"6ae4a548ac2aed10b8c1c15f\",\"name\":\"Soonho Kong\",\"hidden\":false},{\"_id\":\"6ae4a548ac2aed10b8c1c160\",\"name\":\"Aaron Tomb\",\"hidden\":false},{\"_id\":\"6ae4a548ac2aed10b8c1c161\",\"name\":\"Aman Goel\",\"hidden\":false},{\"_id\":\"6ae4a548ac2aed10b8c1c162\",\"name\":\"Ferhat Erata\",\"hidden\":false},{\"_id\":\"6ae4a548ac2aed10b8c1c163\",\"name\":\"Sam Bayless\",\"hidden\":false}],\"publishedAt\":\"2026-10-28T00:00:00.000Z\",\"submittedOnDailyAt\":\"2026-10-30T00:00:00.000Z\",\"title\":\"Scaling Lean Theorem Proving for Real-World Code Verification\",\"submittedOnDailyBy\":{\"_id\":\"61deb0a302496c6d78da4ade\",\"avatarUrl\":\"/avatars/4930b46a1f5655a2fcf2f1172bcadca4.svg\",\"isPro\":false,\"fullname\":\"Debargha Ganguly\",\"user\":\"Debargha\",\"type\":\"user\",\"name\":\"Debargha\"},\"summary\":\"We present EasyProver, a Lean proving system built from recursive agents with isolated contexts, asynchronous search, parallel tactic probing, and an elastic elaboration service. Using an off-the-shelf frontier model with no additional training, it proves 669/672 PutnamBench problems and all six IMO 2026 formalizations and resolves 597/600 NTP4VC verification conditions and all 189 VERINA tasks by proof or refutation. Because these benchmarks are close to saturation, we also introduce Aegis, which converts competitive programming problems into much harder verification tasks over real implementations. The pipeline writes safe Rust solutions, translates them into Lean with Aeneas, generates specifications, and labels each frozen statement with a kernel-checked proof or refutation. It produces 2,045 differentially validated Rust-Lean program pairs and 11,533 candidate specifications. Unlike hand-written benchmarks, these tasks carry implementation-level obligations from fixed-width arithmetic, explicit failure, generated loops, casts, and functionalized mutation. On a stratified sample of 2,000 statements, EasyProver proves 1,054 (52.7%) within a 30-minute budget per statement. The same tasks can also be used to benchmark other provers and as rewards for reinforcement learning.\",\"upvotes\":4,\"discussionId\":\"6ae4a548ac2aed10b8c1c164\",\"ai_summary\":\"EasyProver is a Lean proving system using recursive agents and asynchronous search that solves nearly all Putnam and IMO benchmark problems, while Aegis generates harder verification tasks from competitive programming to benchmark provers and support reinforcement learning.\",\"ai_keywords\":[\"reinforcement learning\"],\"ai_summary_model\":\"thinkingmachines/Inkling-Small\",\"organization\":{\"_id\":\"65424e525bb41e82fba69276\",\"name\":\"amazonwebservices\",\"fullname\":\"Amazon Web Services (AWS)\",\"avatar\":\"https://cdn-avatars.huggingface.co/v1/production/uploads/65424dd6afa1020a9c31759e/wt7-MpCfO2O8pVG8mISJS.png\"}},\"publishedAt\":\"2026-10-27T20:00:00.000Z\",\"title\":\"Scaling Lean Theorem Proving for Real-World Code Verification\",\"summary\":\"We present EasyProver, a Lean proving system built from recursive agents with isolated contexts, asynchronous search, parallel tactic probing, and an elastic elaboration service. Using an off-the-shelf frontier model with no additional training, it proves 669/672 PutnamBench problems and all six IMO 2026 formalizations and resolves 597/600 NTP4VC verification conditions and all 189 VERINA tasks by proof or refutation. Because these benchmarks are close to saturation, we also introduce Aegis, which converts competitive programming problems into much harder verification tasks over real implementations. The pipeline writes safe Rust solutions, translates them into Lean with Aeneas, generates specifications, and labels each frozen statement with a kernel-checked proof or refutation. It produces 2,045 differentially validated Rust-Lean program pairs and 11,533 candidate specifications. Unlike hand-written benchmarks, these tasks carry implementation-level obligations from fixed-width arithmetic, explicit failure, generated loops, casts, and functionalized mutation. On a stratified sample of 2,000 statements, EasyProver proves 1,054 (52.7%) within a 30-minute budget per statement. The same tasks can also be used to benchmark other provers and as rewards for reinforcement learning.\",\"thumbnail\":\"https://cdn-thumbnails.huggingface.co/social-thumbnails/papers/2610.38174.png\",\"numComments\":2,\"upvoted\":false,\"submittedBy\":{\"_id\":\"61deb0a302496c6d78da4ade\",\"avatarUrl\":\"/avatars/4930b46a1f5655a2fcf2f1172bcadca4.svg\",\"fullname\":\"Debargha Ganguly\",\"name\":\"Debargha\",\"type\":\"user\",\"isPro\":false,\"isHf\":false,\"isHfAdmin\":false,\"isMod\":false,\"followerCount\":3,\"isUserFollowing\":false},\"organization\":{\"_id\":\"65424e525bb41e82fba69276\",\"name\":\"amazonwebservices\",\"fullname\":\"Amazon Web Services (AWS)\",\"avatar\":\"https://cdn-avatars.huggingface.co/v1/production/uploads/65424dd6afa1020a9c31759e/wt7-MpCfO2O8pVG8mISJS.png\"},\"isAuthorParticipating\":false},{\"paper\":{\"id\":\"2610.36420\",\"authors\":[{\"_id\":\"6ae141cea5eb0647c2907080\",\"user\":{\"_id\":\"636ab44cc95145940bff5eff\",\"avatarUrl\":\"https://cdn-avatars.huggingface.co/v1/production/uploads/no-auth/aQ_GtJTsFxzV6S3WjZmKq.png\",\"isPro\":false,\"fullname\":\"Truman Hickok\",\"user\":\"5hadytru\",\"type\":\"user\",\"name\":\"5hadytru\"},\"name\":\"Truman Hickok\",\"status\":\"claimed_verified\",\"statusLastChangedAt\":\"2026-10-28T00:45:04.738Z\",\"hidden\":false}],\"publishedAt\":\"2026-10-27T00:00:00.000Z\",\"submittedOnDailyAt\":\"2026-10-30T00:00:00.000Z\",\"title\":\"SO-101 Bench: Accessible Spatial Manipulation\",\"submittedOnDailyBy\":{\"_id\":\"636ab44cc95145940bff5eff\",\"avatarUrl\":\"https://cdn-avatars.huggingface.co/v1/production/uploads/no-auth/aQ_GtJTsFxzV6S3WjZmKq.png\",\"isPro\":false,\"fullname\":\"Truman Hickok\",\"user\":\"5hadytru\",\"type\":\"user\",\"name\":\"5hadytru\"},\"summary\":\"Low-cost arms such as the SO-101 have become the most common entry point to robot learning, yet no released policy performs spatially constrained, language-conditioned tasks on them out of the box, and existing SO-101 benchmarks run only on physical hardware with simple tasks and few held-out objects. We introduce SO-101 Bench, a simulated benchmark for language-conditioned spatial manipulation built on an Isaac Lab digital twin of a real SO-101 setup. It has four tasks scored as five families, 138 objects in seen, unseen-instance and unseen-class splits, 916 evaluation episodes with subsets for held-out appearances, rotate-to-fit planning and occlusion memory, and 3,629 simulated demonstrations on which every learned policy is fine-tuned under an equal compute budget. The twin ranks policies as the real robot does: five policies trained without real data, four VLAs and an LLM agent, keep their exact ranking when the same weights run on the physical arm in an unseen environment with unseen objects (Spearman rho = 1.00, MMRV = 0.000), although simulation overestimates success. Across nine VLAs and world-action models, the best learned policies score near 61%, with spatial placement, unseen object classes and rotate-to-fit planning remaining open. An LLM agent that writes code and hands grasping to a learned policy scores 83.2%, and an ablation shows that this lead comes from the choice of harness and the choice of language model.\",\"upvotes\":4,\"discussionId\":\"6ae141cfa5eb0647c2907081\",\"projectPage\":\"https://so101-bench.github.io/\",\"githubRepo\":\"https://github.com/5hadytru/so101_bench\",\"githubRepoAddedBy\":\"user\",\"ai_summary\":\"A simulated benchmark for language-conditioned spatial manipulation on low-cost robot arms reveals that hybrid LLM agents outperform learned vision-language-action policies, with ranking consistency between simulation and real hardware.\",\"ai_keywords\":[\"Isaac Lab\",\"digital twin\",\"VLAs\",\"vision-language-action models\",\"world-action models\",\"LLM agent\",\"spatial manipulation\",\"rotate-to-fit planning\",\"occlusion memory\",\"SO-101 Bench\"],\"ai_summary_model\":\"thinkingmachines/Inkling-Small\",\"githubStars\":41},\"publishedAt\":\"2026-10-26T20:00:00.000Z\",\"title\":\"SO-101 Bench: Accessible Spatial Manipulation\",\"summary\":\"Low-cost arms such as the SO-101 have become the most common entry point to robot learning, yet no released policy performs spatially constrained, language-conditioned tasks on them out of the box, and existing SO-101 benchmarks run only on physical hardware with simple tasks and few held-out objects. We introduce SO-101 Bench, a simulated benchmark for language-conditioned spatial manipulation built on an Isaac Lab digital twin of a real SO-101 setup. It has four tasks scored as five families, 138 objects in seen, unseen-instance and unseen-class splits, 916 evaluation episodes with subsets for held-out appearances, rotate-to-fit planning and occlusion memory, and 3,629 simulated demonstrations on which every learned policy is fine-tuned under an equal compute budget. The twin ranks policies as the real robot does: five policies trained without real data, four VLAs and an LLM agent, keep their exact ranking when the same weights run on the physical arm in an unseen environment with unseen objects (Spearman rho = 1.00, MMRV = 0.000), although simulation overestimates success. Across nine VLAs and world-action models, the best learned policies score near 61%, with spatial placement, unseen object classes and rotate-to-fit planning remaining open. An LLM agent that writes code and hands grasping to a learned policy scores 83.2%, and an ablation shows that this lead comes from the choice of harness and the choice of language model.\",\"thumbnail\":\"https://cdn-thumbnails.huggingface.co/social-thumbnails/papers/2610.36420.png\",\"numComments\":3,\"upvoted\":false,\"submittedBy\":{\"_id\":\"636ab44cc95145940bff5eff\",\"avatarUrl\":\"https://cdn-avatars.huggingface.co/v1/production/uploads/no-auth/aQ_GtJTsFxzV6S3WjZmKq.png\",\"fullname\":\"Truman Hickok\",\"name\":\"5hadytru\",\"type\":\"user\",\"isPro\":false,\"isHf\":false,\"isHfAdmin\":false,\"isMod\":false,\"followerCount\":6,\"isUserFollowing\":false},\"isAuthorParticipating\":true},{\"paper\":{\"id\":\"2610.34298\",\"authors\":[{\"_id\":\"6ae420b9c3b566b041f6c876\",\"user\":{\"_id\":\"673c593615bf70b248c1a487\",\"avatarUrl\":\"/avatars/8beee7deac9dba7cc8dfdd8cef7fe483.svg\",\"isPro\":false,\"fullname\":\"zhenning shi\",\"user\":\"sznnzs\",\"type\":\"user\",\"name\":\"sznnzs\"},\"name\":\"Zhenning Shi\",\"status\":\"claimed_verified\",\"statusLastChangedAt\":\"2026-10-30T08:45:05.095Z\",\"hidden\":false},{\"_id\":\"6ae420b9c3b566b041f6c877\",\"name\":\"Liang Pang\",\"hidden\":false},{\"_id\":\"6ae420b9c3b566b041f6c878\",\"name\":\"Yongqi Li\",\"hidden\":false}],\"publishedAt\":\"2026-10-25T00:00:00.000Z\",\"submittedOnDailyAt\":\"2026-10-30T00:00:00.000Z\",\"title\":\"Matched Capabilities, Different Behaviors: A Controlled Study of MCP and CLI Interfaces for Language Agents\",\"submittedOnDailyBy\":{\"_id\":\"673c593615bf70b248c1a487\",\"avatarUrl\":\"/avatars/8beee7deac9dba7cc8dfdd8cef7fe483.svg\",\"isPro\":false,\"fullname\":\"zhenning shi\",\"user\":\"sznnzs\",\"type\":\"user\",\"name\":\"sznnzs\"},\"summary\":\"Language agents increasingly act through external tools and interactive environments, making the interface between the model and its executable capabilities part of the decision process. Model Context Protocol (MCP) and command-line interfaces (CLI) can expose the same semantic operations through markedly different model-facing representations. We study whether these representational differences change agent behavior when executable capabilities are matched. We introduce Matched Interface Comparison and Analysis (MICA), which renders MCP and CLI from a shared semantic action contract and routes decoded actions through the same runtime. Across six models and three interactive environments, matched interfaces produce substantial differences in task performance and interaction cost. These effects extend beyond surface validity to semantic action selection, failure pathways, and recovery. Matched-state probes localize sensitivity to capability presentation, realization, and feedback. Paired natural trajectories reveal structured divergence in 94.3% of 2,004 episodes and show that early differences propagate through subsequent interaction. This analysis identifies interface realization as a direct intervention boundary. We therefore introduce Decoupled Action Realization and Targeted Repair (DART), which preserves validated semantic commitments while repairing interface-specific realization. DART improves benchmark score in all six evaluated settings by up to 9.0 points and reduces MCP token cost in all three environments by up to 27.1k tokens per task. Together, these results establish interface representation as part of the closed-loop decision environment shaping agent behavior.\",\"upvotes\":3,\"discussionId\":\"6ae420b9c3b566b041f6c879\",\"projectPage\":\"https://modalitydance.github.io/MICA/\",\"githubRepo\":\"https://github.com/ModalityDance/MICA\",\"githubRepoAddedBy\":\"user\",\"ai_summary\":\"Matched interface representations significantly alter agent behavior despite identical executable capabilities, and targeted repair of interface-specific realization improves performance while reducing cost.\",\"ai_keywords\":[\"Model Context Protocol\",\"CLI\",\"MICA\",\"matched interface comparison\",\"semantic action contract\",\"interaction cost\",\"matched-state probes\",\"DART\",\"decoupled action realization\",\"targeted repair\",\"closed-loop decision environment\"],\"ai_summary_model\":\"thinkingmachines/Inkling-Small\",\"githubStars\":0,\"organization\":{\"_id\":\"69396d0f6ef210a3d45ac4b7\",\"name\":\"ModalityDance\",\"fullname\":\"ModalityDance\",\"avatar\":\"https://cdn-avatars.huggingface.co/v1/production/uploads/653a111eee5888edef9182cf/7BPn5_PnfH27PkAaLQnxW.png\"}},\"publishedAt\":\"2026-10-24T20:00:00.000Z\",\"title\":\"Matched Capabilities, Different Behaviors: A Controlled Study of MCP and CLI Interfaces for Language Agents\",\"summary\":\"Language agents increasingly act through external tools and interactive environments, making the interface between the model and its executable capabilities part of the decision process. Model Context Protocol (MCP) and command-line interfaces (CLI) can expose the same semantic operations through markedly different model-facing representations. We study whether these representational differences change agent behavior when executable capabilities are matched. We introduce Matched Interface Comparison and Analysis (MICA), which renders MCP and CLI from a shared semantic action contract and routes decoded actions through the same runtime. Across six models and three interactive environments, matched interfaces produce substantial differences in task performance and interaction cost. These effects extend beyond surface validity to semantic action selection, failure pathways, and recovery. Matched-state probes localize sensitivity to capability presentation, realization, and feedback. Paired natural trajectories reveal structured divergence in 94.3% of 2,004 episodes and show that early differences propagate through subsequent interaction. This analysis identifies interface realization as a direct intervention boundary. We therefore introduce Decoupled Action Realization and Targeted Repair (DART), which preserves validated semantic commitments while repairing interface-specific realization. DART improves benchmark score in all six evaluated settings by up to 9.0 points and reduces MCP token cost in all three environments by up to 27.1k tokens per task. Together, these results establish interface representation as part of the closed-loop decision environment shaping agent behavior.\",\"thumbnail\":\"https://cdn-thumbnails.huggingface.co/social-thumbnails/papers/2610.34298.png\",\"numComments\":2,\"upvoted\":false,\"submittedBy\":{\"_id\":\"673c593615bf70b248c1a487\",\"avatarUrl\":\"/avatars/8beee7deac9dba7cc8dfdd8cef7fe483.svg\",\"fullname\":\"zhenning shi\",\"name\":\"sznnzs\",\"type\":\"user\",\"isPro\":false,\"isHf\":false,\"isHfAdmin\":false,\"isMod\":false,\"isUserFollowing\":false},\"organization\":{\"_id\":\"69396d0f6ef210a3d45ac4b7\",\"name\":\"ModalityDance\",\"fullname\":\"ModalityDance\",\"avatar\":\"https://cdn-avatars.huggingface.co/v1/production/uploads/653a111eee5888edef9182cf/7BPn5_PnfH27PkAaLQnxW.png\"},\"isAuthorParticipating\":true},{\"paper\":{\"id\":\"2610.23656\",\"authors\":[{\"_id\":\"6ad74cc8774b2a8cb5c7dee8\",\"user\":{\"_id\":\"68b95480c11731b80a01ca96\",\"avatarUrl\":\"https://cdn-avatars.huggingface.co/v1/production/uploads/noauth/EOdkKRJgrR1ikcLmvqMMd.jpeg\",\"isPro\":false,\"fullname\":\"Xiaohang Yu\",\"user\":\"xhyumiracle\",\"type\":\"user\",\"name\":\"xhyumiracle\"},\"name\":\"Xiaohang Yu\",\"status\":\"claimed_verified\",\"statusLastChangedAt\":\"2026-10-30T16:09:42.454Z\",\"hidden\":false},{\"_id\":\"6ad74cc8774b2a8cb5c7dee9\",\"name\":\"Hejia Geng\",\"hidden\":false},{\"_id\":\"6ad74cc8774b2a8cb5c7deea\",\"name\":\"William Knottenbelt\",\"hidden\":false}],\"publishedAt\":\"2026-10-16T00:00:00.000Z\",\"submittedOnDailyAt\":\"2026-10-30T00:00:00.000Z\",\"title\":\"ABF-Bench: A Verifiable Benchmark for Agentic Blockchain Forensics\",\"submittedOnDailyBy\":{\"_id\":\"68b95480c11731b80a01ca96\",\"avatarUrl\":\"https://cdn-avatars.huggingface.co/v1/production/uploads/noauth/EOdkKRJgrR1ikcLmvqMMd.jpeg\",\"isPro\":false,\"fullname\":\"Xiaohang Yu\",\"user\":\"xhyumiracle\",\"type\":\"user\",\"name\":\"xhyumiracle\"},\"summary\":\"Blockchain forensics is adversarial and open-ended: money launderers split, swap, and bridge funds, cut their trail at mixers or custodial services, and change tactics from one incident to the next. Investigators must keep adapting their tracing in response. Yet the immutability of public ledgers preserves every recorded movement in public view, making much of the investigation independently verifiable. We introduce ABF-Bench, an interactive benchmark that makes evidence-backed fund tracing a verifiable agent task, in which every claimed movement must cite ledger records the agent retrieved. A case-to-task pipeline builds well-posed tasks across ledgers: conservative rules halt tracing wherever the records leave a continuation unresolved, and each task's reference graph is the answer those rules determine. Built from real laundering activity, the tasks combine multi-hop transfers, DeFi exchanges, cross-chain round trips, custodial and mixer deposits, and address poisoning. We also propose the ABF agent harness, built around a working memory, and evaluate it alongside general-purpose agents and fixed-order tracers. The harness reaches 75% edge recall, against 47% for Codex with the same model and 10% for Claude Code, which loses recall to overconfidence: it cites records it left unretrieved. Compared with fixed-order tracers, the harness acquires evidence faster than the best of them early in the budget and recognizes undisclosed custodial services that they walk past (89% vs. 0%), with 98% fidelity. However, agents tend to stop early and lose recall on larger tasks, which holds their final recall below the best tracer. Beyond exposing these gaps, ABF-Bench opens blockchain forensics to LLM agents as a verifiable domain, where the verdicts that score agents also offer potential training signals.\",\"upvotes\":3,\"discussionId\":\"6ad74cc8774b2a8cb5c7deeb\",\"ai_summary\":\"ABF-Bench introduces an interactive, evidence-backed benchmark for blockchain fund tracing that evaluates adaptive agents against fixed tracers, revealing gaps in recall and overconfidence while enabling verifiable LLM agent training.\",\"ai_keywords\":[\"ABF-Bench\",\"blockchain forensics\",\"fund tracing\",\"multi-hop transfers\",\"DeFi exchanges\",\"cross-chain round trips\",\"custodial services\",\"mixer deposits\",\"address poisoning\",\"working memory\",\"edge recall\",\"LLM agents\"],\"ai_summary_model\":\"thinkingmachines/Inkling-Small\",\"organization\":{\"_id\":\"650987fc2feb9570c5137ac2\",\"name\":\"ImperialCollegeLondon\",\"fullname\":\"Imperial College London\",\"avatar\":\"https://cdn-avatars.huggingface.co/v1/production/uploads/630ca0817dacb93b33506ce7/u6ceSXXV6ldtt0qZOOMJw.jpeg\"}},\"publishedAt\":\"2026-10-15T20:00:00.000Z\",\"title\":\"ABF-Bench: A Verifiable Benchmark for Agentic Blockchain Forensics\",\"summary\":\"Blockchain forensics is adversarial and open-ended: money launderers split, swap, and bridge funds, cut their trail at mixers or custodial services, and change tactics from one incident to the next. Investigators must keep adapting their tracing in response. Yet the immutability of public ledgers preserves every recorded movement in public view, making much of the investigation independently verifiable. We introduce ABF-Bench, an interactive benchmark that makes evidence-backed fund tracing a verifiable agent task, in which every claimed movement must cite ledger records the agent retrieved. A case-to-task pipeline builds well-posed tasks across ledgers: conservative rules halt tracing wherever the records leave a continuation unresolved, and each task's reference graph is the answer those rules determine. Built from real laundering activity, the tasks combine multi-hop transfers, DeFi exchanges, cross-chain round trips, custodial and mixer deposits, and address poisoning. We also propose the ABF agent harness, built around a working memory, and evaluate it alongside general-purpose agents and fixed-order tracers. The harness reaches 75% edge recall, against 47% for Codex with the same model and 10% for Claude Code, which loses recall to overconfidence: it cites records it left unretrieved. Compared with fixed-order tracers, the harness acquires evidence faster than the best of them early in the budget and recognizes undisclosed custodial services that they walk past (89% vs. 0%), with 98% fidelity. However, agents tend to stop early and lose recall on larger tasks, which holds their final recall below the best tracer. Beyond exposing these gaps, ABF-Bench opens blockchain forensics to LLM agents as a verifiable domain, where the verdicts that score agents also offer potential training signals.\",\"thumbnail\":\"https://cdn-thumbnails.huggingface.co/social-thumbnails/papers/2610.23656.png\",\"numComments\":2,\"upvoted\":false,\"submittedBy\":{\"_id\":\"68b95480c11731b80a01ca96\",\"avatarUrl\":\"https://cdn-avatars.huggingface.co/v1/production/uploads/noauth/EOdkKRJgrR1ikcLmvqMMd.jpeg\",\"fullname\":\"Xiaohang Yu\",\"name\":\"xhyumiracle\",\"type\":\"user\",\"isPro\":false,\"isHf\":false,\"isHfAdmin\":false,\"isMod\":false,\"isUserFollowing\":false},\"organization\":{\"_id\":\"650987fc2feb9570c5137ac2\",\"name\":\"ImperialCollegeLondon\",\"fullname\":\"Imperial College London\",\"avatar\":\"https://cdn-avatars.huggingface.co/v1/production/uploads/630ca0817dacb93b33506ce7/u6ceSXXV6ldtt0qZOOMJw.jpeg\"},\"isAuthorParticipating\":true},{\"paper\":{\"id\":\"2610.25276\",\"authors\":[{\"_id\":\"6ae47c8bf221e5ccb6d5bb90\",\"user\":{\"_id\":\"64b55f62e6b0c53f97e64034\",\"avatarUrl\":\"https://cdn-avatars.huggingface.co/v1/production/uploads/64b55f62e6b0c53f97e64034/TsQKf50BUI7MO8slbR6m6.png\",\"isPro\":true,\"fullname\":\"LAN YUSHI\",\"user\":\"yslan\",\"type\":\"user\",\"name\":\"yslan\"},\"name\":\"Yushi Lan\",\"status\":\"claimed_verified\",\"statusLastChangedAt\":\"2026-10-30T16:09:15.245Z\",\"hidden\":false},{\"_id\":\"6ae47c8bf221e5ccb6d5bb91\",\"name\":\"Zeren Jiang\",\"hidden\":false},{\"_id\":\"6ae47c8bf221e5ccb6d5bb92\",\"name\":\"Koichi Namekata\",\"hidden\":false},{\"_id\":\"6ae47c8bf221e5ccb6d5bb93\",\"name\":\"Xingang Pan\",\"hidden\":false},{\"_id\":\"6ae47c8bf221e5ccb6d5bb94\",\"name\":\"Chuanxia Zheng\",\"hidden\":false},{\"_id\":\"6ae47c8bf221e5ccb6d5bb95\",\"name\":\"Andrea Vedaldi\",\"hidden\":false}],\"mediaUrls\":[\"https://cdn-uploads.huggingface.co/production/uploads/64b55f62e6b0c53f97e64034/NMSCgFOLsJMuOF8TaKlQ2.mp4\"],\"publishedAt\":\"2026-10-18T00:00:00.000Z\",\"submittedOnDailyAt\":\"2026-10-30T00:00:00.000Z\",\"title\":\"Holo4D: Holistic 4D Reconstruction as Geometric Control for Video Diffusion\",\"submittedOnDailyBy\":{\"_id\":\"64b55f62e6b0c53f97e64034\",\"avatarUrl\":\"https://cdn-avatars.huggingface.co/v1/production/uploads/64b55f62e6b0c53f97e64034/TsQKf50BUI7MO8slbR6m6.png\",\"isPro\":true,\"fullname\":\"LAN YUSHI\",\"user\":\"yslan\",\"type\":\"user\",\"name\":\"yslan\"},\"summary\":\"Synthesizing a monocular video from a new camera trajectory requires preserving the observed scene geometry and motion while completing regions that become newly visible. Feed-forward 4D reconstructors recover the structure of the observed scene, whereas video diffusion models provide a generative prior for completing unseen content. We introduce Holo4D, a video reshooting framework that uses reconstructed 3D trajectories as a holistic source-view 4D scaffold. We project these trajectories into the requested views and encode their geometry and source appearance to guide a pretrained video diffusion model. Together with the source video and target cameras, the scaffold grounds generation in reconstructed observations while leaving unsupported regions to the generative prior. Experiments demonstrate the value of trajectory-based conditioning for camera control and novel-view synthesis. The framework accommodates different 4D reconstructors without retraining the video generator, allowing it to benefit from future advances in source reconstruction. More details can be found in our project page.\",\"upvotes\":3,\"discussionId\":\"6ae47c8cf221e5ccb6d5bb96\",\"projectPage\":\"https://nirvanalan.github.io/projects/holo4d/\",\"githubRepo\":\"https://github.com/NIRVANALAN/Holo4D\",\"githubRepoAddedBy\":\"user\",\"ai_summary\":\"Holo4D guides video diffusion with reconstructed 3D trajectory scaffolds to synthesize novel-view videos while preserving geometry and motion.\",\"ai_keywords\":[\"4D reconstructors\",\"video diffusion models\",\"3D trajectories\",\"4D scaffold\",\"trajectory-based conditioning\",\"novel-view synthesis\"],\"ai_summary_model\":\"thinkingmachines/Inkling-Small\",\"githubStars\":19,\"organization\":{\"_id\":\"627bbc28fbab61b048eba8b6\",\"name\":\"Oxford\",\"fullname\":\"University of Oxford\",\"avatar\":\"https://cdn-avatars.huggingface.co/v1/production/uploads/68e396f2b5bb631e9b2fac9a/u0ey2LfYu6uG6iu8m_kH7.png\"}},\"publishedAt\":\"2026-10-17T20:00:00.000Z\",\"title\":\"Holo4D: Holistic 4D Reconstruction as Geometric Control for Video Diffusion\",\"summary\":\"Synthesizing a monocular video from a new camera trajectory requires preserving the observed scene geometry and motion while completing regions that become newly visible. Feed-forward 4D reconstructors recover the structure of the observed scene, whereas video diffusion models provide a generative prior for completing unseen content. We introduce Holo4D, a video reshooting framework that uses reconstructed 3D trajectories as a holistic source-view 4D scaffold. We project these trajectories into the requested views and encode their geometry and source appearance to guide a pretrained video diffusion model. Together with the source video and target cameras, the scaffold grounds generation in reconstructed observations while leaving unsupported regions to the generative prior. Experiments demonstrate the value of trajectory-based conditioning for camera control and novel-view synthesis. The framework accommodates different 4D reconstructors without retraining the video generator, allowing it to benefit from future advances in source reconstruction. More details can be found in our project page.\",\"mediaUrls\":[\"https://cdn-uploads.huggingface.co/production/uploads/64b55f62e6b0c53f97e64034/NMSCgFOLsJMuOF8TaKlQ2.mp4\"],\"thumbnail\":\"https://cdn-thumbnails.huggingface.co/social-thumbnails/papers/2610.25276.png\",\"numComments\":2,\"upvoted\":false,\"submittedBy\":{\"_id\":\"64b55f62e6b0c53f97e64034\",\"avatarUrl\":\"https://cdn-avatars.huggingface.co/v1/production/uploads/64b55f62e6b0c53f97e64034/TsQKf50BUI7MO8slbR6m6.png\",\"fullname\":\"LAN YUSHI\",\"name\":\"yslan\",\"type\":\"user\",\"isPro\":true,\"isHf\":false,\"isHfAdmin\":false,\"isMod\":false,\"followerCount\":33,\"isUserFollowing\":false},\"organization\":{\"_id\":\"627bbc28fbab61b048eba8b6\",\"name\":\"Oxford\",\"fullname\":\"University of Oxford\",\"avatar\":\"https://cdn-avatars.huggingface.co/v1/production/uploads/68e396f2b5bb631e9b2fac9a/u0ey2LfYu6uG6iu8m_kH7.png\"},\"isAuthorParticipating\":true},{\"paper\":{\"id\":\"2610.38293\",\"authors\":[{\"_id\":\"6ae503c3ac2aed10b8c1c2f2\",\"name\":\"Yanbin Yin\",\"hidden\":false},{\"_id\":\"6ae503c3ac2aed10b8c1c2f3\",\"name\":\"Chuyu Xu\",\"hidden\":false},{\"_id\":\"6ae503c3ac2aed10b8c1c2f4\",\"name\":\"Liqin Ye\",\"hidden\":false},{\"_id\":\"6ae503c3ac2aed10b8c1c2f5\",\"name\":\"Jing Peng\",\"hidden\":false},{\"_id\":\"6ae503c3ac2aed10b8c1c2f6\",\"name\":\"Chao Zhang\",\"hidden\":false}],\"publishedAt\":\"2026-10-28T05:24:11.000Z\",\"submittedOnDailyAt\":\"2026-10-30T00:00:00.000Z\",\"title\":\"EPSBench: Benchmarking LLM Agents for Earnings Forecasting\",\"submittedOnDailyBy\":{\"_id\":\"65bc5265f525e2008ebdded0\",\"avatarUrl\":\"https://cdn-avatars.huggingface.co/v1/production/uploads/65bc5265f525e2008ebdded0/DJESzOpHD47N3VLAfXfvp.jpeg\",\"isPro\":true,\"fullname\":\"Liqin Ye\",\"user\":\"liqinye\",\"type\":\"user\",\"name\":\"liqinye\"},\"summary\":\"Quarterly earnings forecasting requires an agent to connect financial evidence to the income-statement line items that produce a company's reported earnings. We introduce EPSBench, a benchmark for studying this ability across 149 firm-quarters from 86 US companies. Agents read a fixed collection of filings, earnings-call transcripts, and news, then estimate company-specific income-statement drivers with cited evidence. A shared accounting calculator converts these estimates into GAAP diluted earnings per share (EPS), providing a common basis for comparing forecast assumptions. We evaluate six models under a common harness, assessing point forecast error relative to date-matched analyst consensus and checking whether their prediction intervals achieve the stated coverage. Three models show no detectable paired difference in point forecast error from consensus, and three have higher error. Across all six models, nominal 80% intervals contain reported EPS in only 37--70% of runs that return an interval, and most misses fall above the upper bound because the forecasts run low. The driver records trace most of the error gap between models to operating-income estimates.\",\"upvotes\":2,\"discussionId\":\"6ae503c3ac2aed10b8c1c2f7\",\"githubRepo\":\"https://github.com/JadeXu16/epsbench\",\"githubRepoAddedBy\":\"user\",\"ai_summary\":\"The study introduces EPSBench to evaluate AI agents in forecasting quarterly earnings by linking financial evidence to income-statement drivers and comparing model predictions to analyst consensus.\",\"ai_keywords\":[\"EPSBench\",\"earnings forecasting\",\"income-statement drivers\",\"GAAP diluted earnings per share\",\"prediction intervals\",\"operating-income estimates\"],\"ai_summary_model\":\"thinkingmachines/Inkling-Small\",\"githubStars\":0,\"organization\":{\"_id\":\"64155eaa95fb6f824b237c3d\",\"name\":\"GeorgiaTech\",\"fullname\":\"Georgia Institute of Technology\",\"avatar\":\"https://cdn-avatars.huggingface.co/v1/production/uploads/64155e8abe60230f2f40b03a/3i-AL3LrNkaTarSKnaGy8.png\"}},\"publishedAt\":\"2026-10-28T01:24:11.000Z\",\"title\":\"EPSBench: Benchmarking LLM Agents for Earnings Forecasting\",\"summary\":\"Quarterly earnings forecasting requires an agent to connect financial evidence to the income-statement line items that produce a company's reported earnings. We introduce EPSBench, a benchmark for studying this ability across 149 firm-quarters from 86 US companies. Agents read a fixed collection of filings, earnings-call transcripts, and news, then estimate company-specific income-statement drivers with cited evidence. A shared accounting calculator converts these estimates into GAAP diluted earnings per share (EPS), providing a common basis for comparing forecast assumptions. We evaluate six models under a common harness, assessing point forecast error relative to date-matched analyst consensus and checking whether their prediction intervals achieve the stated coverage. Three models show no detectable paired difference in point forecast error from consensus, and three have higher error. Across all six models, nominal 80% intervals contain reported EPS in only 37--70% of runs that return an interval, and most misses fall above the upper bound because the forecasts run low. The driver records trace most of the error gap between models to operating-income estimates.\",\"thumbnail\":\"https://cdn-thumbnails.huggingface.co/social-thumbnails/papers/2610.38293.png\",\"numComments\":3,\"upvoted\":false,\"submittedBy\":{\"_id\":\"65bc5265f525e2008ebdded0\",\"avatarUrl\":\"https://cdn-avatars.huggingface.co/v1/production/uploads/65bc5265f525e2008ebdded0/DJESzOpHD47N3VLAfXfvp.jpeg\",\"fullname\":\"Liqin Ye\",\"name\":\"liqinye\",\"type\":\"user\",\"isPro\":true,\"isHf\":false,\"isHfAdmin\":false,\"isMod\":false,\"followerCount\":1,\"isUserFollowing\":false},\"organization\":{\"_id\":\"64155eaa95fb6f824b237c3d\",\"name\":\"GeorgiaTech\",\"fullname\":\"Georgia Institute of Technology\",\"avatar\":\"https://cdn-avatars.huggingface.co/v1/production/uploads/64155e8abe60230f2f40b03a/3i-AL3LrNkaTarSKnaGy8.png\"},\"isAuthorParticipating\":false},{\"paper\":{\"id\":\"2610.38681\",\"authors\":[{\"_id\":\"6ae4efddac2aed10b8c1c29b\",\"name\":\"Yecheng Wu\",\"hidden\":false},{\"_id\":\"6ae4efddac2aed10b8c1c29c\",\"name\":\"Song Han\",\"hidden\":false},{\"_id\":\"6ae4efddac2aed10b8c1c29d\",\"name\":\"Han Cai\",\"hidden\":false}],\"publishedAt\":\"2026-10-28T00:00:00.000Z\",\"submittedOnDailyAt\":\"2026-10-30T00:00:00.000Z\",\"title\":\"Weaken to Strengthen: Weakening-Induced On-Policy Self-Distillation for Large Reasoning Models\",\"submittedOnDailyBy\":{\"_id\":\"6579569562d3ac18171cf9cb\",\"avatarUrl\":\"/avatars/230db859ac89537c8c0e85cd17dc295a.svg\",\"isPro\":false,\"fullname\":\"Yecheng Wu\",\"user\":\"gbcfchc\",\"type\":\"user\",\"name\":\"gbcfchc\"},\"summary\":\"Self-distillation offers a way to improve reasoning models when a suitable external teacher is unavailable. However, common approaches rely on privileged information to construct a stronger self-teacher, limiting their use when only unlabeled prompts are available. In this paper, we introduce Weakening-Induced On-Policy Self-Distillation (WISD), which instead constructs a weaker counterpart of the original model and uses the contrast between the two as a training signal without requiring privileged information. We explore two ways to construct the weaker model: low-bit quantization and magnitude pruning. We evaluate WISD across diverse architectures and scales on mathematical and coding reasoning tasks. Across these benchmarks, quantization and pruning improve average accuracy over the original models by 4.0 and 5.5 percentage points, respectively, achieving state-of-the-art performance even without privileged information. Notably, pruning raises Qwen3.5-4B's accuracy from 57.7% to 70.5% on HMMT 2025 and from 39.1% to 56.7% on LiveCodeBench v6. Quantization raises Qwen3.6-35B-A3B's accuracy from 68.5% to 74.6% and from 61.8% to 69.5% on the same benchmarks, respectively. These results establish WISD as a novel and effective self-distillation framework for large reasoning models.\",\"upvotes\":2,\"discussionId\":\"6ae4efddac2aed10b8c1c29e\",\"projectPage\":\"https://jet-ai-projects.github.io/WISD/\",\"githubRepo\":\"https://github.com/jet-ai-projects/WISD\",\"githubRepoAddedBy\":\"user\",\"ai_summary\":\"WISD improves reasoning models by contrasting them with weaker quantized or pruned versions to generate training signals without privileged data.\",\"ai_keywords\":[\"self-distillation\",\"on-policy self-distillation\",\"low-bit quantization\",\"magnitude pruning\",\"reasoning models\"],\"ai_summary_model\":\"thinkingmachines/Inkling-Small\",\"githubStars\":0,\"organization\":{\"_id\":\"68a572de321cc978fe804cbf\",\"name\":\"jet-ai\",\"fullname\":\"Jet-AI\",\"avatar\":\"https://cdn-avatars.huggingface.co/v1/production/uploads/650e2b14c945dfc9386a7e28/45y6yKSdl4y_SCoACH9Ia.png\"}},\"publishedAt\":\"2026-10-27T20:00:00.000Z\",\"title\":\"Weaken to Strengthen: Weakening-Induced On-Policy Self-Distillation for Large Reasoning Models\",\"summary\":\"Self-distillation offers a way to improve reasoning models when a suitable external teacher is unavailable. However, common approaches rely on privileged information to construct a stronger self-teacher, limiting their use when only unlabeled prompts are available. In this paper, we introduce Weakening-Induced On-Policy Self-Distillation (WISD), which instead constructs a weaker counterpart of the original model and uses the contrast between the two as a training signal without requiring privileged information. We explore two ways to construct the weaker model: low-bit quantization and magnitude pruning. We evaluate WISD across diverse architectures and scales on mathematical and coding reasoning tasks. Across these benchmarks, quantization and pruning improve average accuracy over the original models by 4.0 and 5.5 percentage points, respectively, achieving state-of-the-art performance even without privileged information. Notably, pruning raises Qwen3.5-4B's accuracy from 57.7% to 70.5% on HMMT 2025 and from 39.1% to 56.7% on LiveCodeBench v6. Quantization raises Qwen3.6-35B-A3B's accuracy from 68.5% to 74.6% and from 61.8% to 69.5% on the same benchmarks, respectively. These results establish WISD as a novel and effective self-distillation framework for large reasoning models.\",\"thumbnail\":\"https://cdn-thumbnails.huggingface.co/social-thumbnails/papers/2610.38681.png\",\"numComments\":2,\"upvoted\":false,\"submittedBy\":{\"_id\":\"6579569562d3ac18171cf9cb\",\"avatarUrl\":\"/avatars/230db859ac89537c8c0e85cd17dc295a.svg\",\"fullname\":\"Yecheng Wu\",\"name\":\"gbcfchc\",\"type\":\"user\",\"isPro\":false,\"isHf\":false,\"isHfAdmin\":false,\"isMod\":false,\"followerCount\":6,\"isUserFollowing\":false},\"organization\":{\"_id\":\"68a572de321cc978fe804cbf\",\"name\":\"jet-ai\",\"fullname\":\"Jet-AI\",\"avatar\":\"https://cdn-avatars.huggingface.co/v1/production/uploads/650e2b14c945dfc9386a7e28/45y6yKSdl4y_SCoACH9Ia.png\"},\"isAuthorParticipating\":false},{\"paper\":{\"id\":\"2610.39293\",\"authors\":[{\"_id\":\"6ae4ce97ac2aed10b8c1c271\",\"user\":{\"_id\":\"62bb84f82ada492aa5775709\",\"avatarUrl\":\"https://cdn-avatars.huggingface.co/v1/production/uploads/no-auth/ltN5gU-f9NojwgrQq2G-s.png\",\"isPro\":false,\"fullname\":\"Rishav Pramanik\",\"user\":\"rishavpramanik\",\"type\":\"user\",\"name\":\"rishavpramanik\"},\"name\":\"Rishav Pramanik\",\"status\":\"claimed_verified\",\"statusLastChangedAt\":\"2026-10-31T00:45:04.562Z\",\"hidden\":false},{\"_id\":\"6ae4ce97ac2aed10b8c1c272\",\"name\":\"Marco Pedersoli\",\"hidden\":false},{\"_id\":\"6ae4ce97ac2aed10b8c1c273\",\"name\":\"Zhaozheng Yin\",\"hidden\":false}],\"publishedAt\":\"2026-10-29T00:00:00.000Z\",\"submittedOnDailyAt\":\"2026-10-30T00:00:00.000Z\",\"title\":\"Unlocking Any-Order Generation in Pretrained Autoregressive Image Models\",\"submittedOnDailyBy\":{\"_id\":\"62bb84f82ada492aa5775709\",\"avatarUrl\":\"https://cdn-avatars.huggingface.co/v1/production/uploads/no-auth/ltN5gU-f9NojwgrQq2G-s.png\",\"isPro\":false,\"fullname\":\"Rishav Pramanik\",\"user\":\"rishavpramanik\",\"type\":\"user\",\"name\":\"rishavpramanik\"},\"summary\":\"Autoregressive (AR) image generators are powerful in principle but inflexible in practice: training with a fixed raster-scan order hardcodes a single factorization of the image distribution into the model, leaving it unable to inpaint, outpaint, or edit at inference time. Existing solutions, including the modern discrete-diffusion family, train from scratch on a mixture of token orderings, paying roughly 3x the compute of standard raster-scan training; discrete diffusion further sacrifices compatibility with the AR inference ecosystem (KV caching, vLLM) by construction. We argue that this cost is unnecessary. A pretrained raster-scan AR model has already learned the joint distribution over image tokens; what it lacks is not knowledge but the ability to express that knowledge through non-sequential conditional pathways. We propose a recipe for adapting pretrained raster-scan models into any-order generators using two components: future-aware positional embeddings that resolve a target-position identifiability problem at negligible overhead, and an ordering curriculum that traces a stable optimization path from the pretrained solution to a good any-order basin. Applied to four base models across two architectures and two scales (LlamaGen-L/XL, RAR-L/XL), the recipe substantially outperforms MaskGIT on ImageNet inpainting (FID 6.21 vs. 10.44) and outpainting, is competitive with MAR-L, trades a 1.2-1.9 increase in class-conditional FID for this flexibility, and costs 13-33% additional compute beyond pretraining, while leaving the standard AR inference stack untouched. Inpainting, outpainting, and editing, our results suggest, need not be added to autoregressive image models. They can be unlocked from the ones that exist. Code is available at https://github.com/Ordered-AR/unlockedadaptation.\",\"upvotes\":2,\"discussionId\":\"6ae4ce97ac2aed10b8c1c274\",\"githubRepo\":\"https://github.com/Ordered-AR/unlockedadaptation\",\"githubRepoAddedBy\":\"user\",\"ai_summary\":\"Pretrained raster-scan autoregressive image models can be adapted into flexible any-order generators via future-aware positional embeddings and an ordering curriculum, enabling inpainting and outpainting with minimal extra compute.\",\"ai_keywords\":[\"autoregressive image generators\",\"discrete diffusion\",\"future-aware positional embeddings\",\"ordering curriculum\",\"KV caching\",\"vLLM\",\"MaskGIT\",\"MAR\",\"LlamaGen\",\"RAR\"],\"ai_summary_model\":\"thinkingmachines/Inkling-Small\",\"githubStars\":0},\"publishedAt\":\"2026-10-28T20:00:00.000Z\",\"title\":\"Unlocking Any-Order Generation in Pretrained Autoregressive Image Models\",\"summary\":\"Autoregressive (AR) image generators are powerful in principle but inflexible in practice: training with a fixed raster-scan order hardcodes a single factorization of the image distribution into the model, leaving it unable to inpaint, outpaint, or edit at inference time. Existing solutions, including the modern discrete-diffusion family, train from scratch on a mixture of token orderings, paying roughly 3x the compute of standard raster-scan training; discrete diffusion further sacrifices compatibility with the AR inference ecosystem (KV caching, vLLM) by construction. We argue that this cost is unnecessary. A pretrained raster-scan AR model has already learned the joint distribution over image tokens; what it lacks is not knowledge but the ability to express that knowledge through non-sequential conditional pathways. We propose a recipe for adapting pretrained raster-scan models into any-order generators using two components: future-aware positional embeddings that resolve a target-position identifiability problem at negligible overhead, and an ordering curriculum that traces a stable optimization path from the pretrained solution to a good any-order basin. Applied to four base models across two architectures and two scales (LlamaGen-L/XL, RAR-L/XL), the recipe substantially outperforms MaskGIT on ImageNet inpainting (FID 6.21 vs. 10.44) and outpainting, is competitive with MAR-L, trades a 1.2-1.9 increase in class-conditional FID for this flexibility, and costs 13-33% additional compute beyond pretraining, while leaving the standard AR inference stack untouched. Inpainting, outpainting, and editing, our results suggest, need not be added to autoregressive image models. They can be unlocked from the ones that exist. Code is available at https://github.com/Ordered-AR/unlockedadaptation.\",\"thumbnail\":\"https://cdn-thumbnails.huggingface.co/social-thumbnails/papers/2610.39293.png\",\"numComments\":2,\"upvoted\":false,\"submittedBy\":{\"_id\":\"62bb84f82ada492aa5775709\",\"avatarUrl\":\"https://cdn-avatars.huggingface.co/v1/production/uploads/no-auth/ltN5gU-f9NojwgrQq2G-s.png\",\"fullname\":\"Rishav Pramanik\",\"name\":\"rishavpramanik\",\"type\":\"user\",\"isPro\":false,\"isHf\":false,\"isHfAdmin\":false,\"isMod\":false,\"isUserFollowing\":false},\"isAuthorParticipating\":true},{\"paper\":{\"id\":\"2610.37695\",\"authors\":[{\"_id\":\"6ae3f479c3b566b041f6c75e\",\"user\":{\"_id\":\"69b0cc94e29a0b14bf239920\",\"avatarUrl\":\"https://cdn-avatars.huggingface.co/v1/production/uploads/69b0cc94e29a0b14bf239920/tHl5ZcGulvKtm8axVw5ja.png\",\"isPro\":false,\"fullname\":\"Bo Zou\",\"user\":\"ZouBo\",\"type\":\"user\",\"name\":\"ZouBo\"},\"name\":\"Bo Zou\",\"status\":\"claimed_verified\",\"statusLastChangedAt\":\"2026-10-30T08:45:05.025Z\",\"hidden\":false},{\"_id\":\"6ae3f479c3b566b041f6c75f\",\"name\":\"Chao Xu\",\"hidden\":false}],\"mediaUrls\":[\"https://cdn-uploads.huggingface.co/production/uploads/69b0cc94e29a0b14bf239920/xMUoGXNFKs6P50CwW_UT-.mp4\"],\"publishedAt\":\"2026-10-24T00:00:00.000Z\",\"submittedOnDailyAt\":\"2026-10-30T00:00:00.000Z\",\"title\":\"After One-Click Video: The Inflow of Service-Type Content, the Failure of the Recommendation Pool Mechanism, and the BoLe System\",\"submittedOnDailyBy\":{\"_id\":\"69b0cc94e29a0b14bf239920\",\"avatarUrl\":\"https://cdn-avatars.huggingface.co/v1/production/uploads/69b0cc94e29a0b14bf239920/tHl5ZcGulvKtm8axVw5ja.png\",\"isPro\":false,\"fullname\":\"Bo Zou\",\"user\":\"ZouBo\",\"type\":\"user\",\"name\":\"ZouBo\"},\"summary\":\"In September 2026, a new large language model was released. Soon after, a new way of making explainer videos became very popular: people asked the model to write a program, and the program produced the video. \\\"One-click video\\\" content then appeared in large amounts on many platforms. On the surface, this is a rise in creative power at the level of the individual. We argue that, at a deeper level, it changes the structure of the content industry. This judgment rests on two observations. First, this wave of content is service-type content. People watch it to meet a need, not to enjoy the content itself. The other kind is work-type content. AI makes both kinds faster to produce. But anyone can come in and make service-type content, while work-type content is limited by the number of people who have something to say. So the ratio between the two slowly becomes unbalanced. Second, free platforms choose content through layers of trial viewing, and this uses up user attention. The total amount of attention is fixed. The unbalanced ratio also dilutes good content. A simplified mathematical model shows that, as long as these conditions hold, the recommendation pool mechanism will fail once new content grows past a certain multiple. Based on this, we discuss one possible way out. Machines and entry rules judge whether service-type content passes the bar. Trusted people judge work-type content. We call this the BoLe system. At the end, we briefly imagine how the two kinds of content may develop from here.\",\"upvotes\":2,\"discussionId\":\"6ae3f47ac3b566b041f6c760\",\"projectPage\":\"https://huggingface.co/spaces/BreadStudio/recommendation-pool\",\"ai_summary\":\"AI-generated video tools shift content production toward service-type material, unbalancing the industry and overwhelming recommendation systems, prompting a dual-judgment BoLe framework to protect work-type content.\",\"ai_keywords\":[\"large language model\",\"recommendation pool mechanism\",\"BoLe system\"],\"ai_summary_model\":\"thinkingmachines/Inkling-Small\",\"organization\":{\"_id\":\"69b0d40a64a392f94806a497\",\"name\":\"BreadStudio\",\"fullname\":\"Bread Studio\",\"avatar\":\"https://cdn-avatars.huggingface.co/v1/production/uploads/69b0cc94e29a0b14bf239920/4X5kmuAPqevfVXdfZH0wX.png\"}},\"publishedAt\":\"2026-10-23T20:00:00.000Z\",\"title\":\"After One-Click Video: The Inflow of Service-Type Content, the Failure of the Recommendation Pool Mechanism, and the BoLe System\",\"summary\":\"In September 2026, a new large language model was released. Soon after, a new way of making explainer videos became very popular: people asked the model to write a program, and the program produced the video. \\\"One-click video\\\" content then appeared in large amounts on many platforms. On the surface, this is a rise in creative power at the level of the individual. We argue that, at a deeper level, it changes the structure of the content industry. This judgment rests on two observations. First, this wave of content is service-type content. People watch it to meet a need, not to enjoy the content itself. The other kind is work-type content. AI makes both kinds faster to produce. But anyone can come in and make service-type content, while work-type content is limited by the number of people who have something to say. So the ratio between the two slowly becomes unbalanced. Second, free platforms choose content through layers of trial viewing, and this uses up user attention. The total amount of attention is fixed. The unbalanced ratio also dilutes good content. A simplified mathematical model shows that, as long as these conditions hold, the recommendation pool mechanism will fail once new content grows past a certain multiple. Based on this, we discuss one possible way out. Machines and entry rules judge whether service-type content passes the bar. Trusted people judge work-type content. We call this the BoLe system. At the end, we briefly imagine how the two kinds of content may develop from here.\",\"mediaUrls\":[\"https://cdn-uploads.huggingface.co/production/uploads/69b0cc94e29a0b14bf239920/xMUoGXNFKs6P50CwW_UT-.mp4\"],\"thumbnail\":\"https://cdn-thumbnails.huggingface.co/social-thumbnails/papers/2610.37695.png\",\"numComments\":2,\"upvoted\":false,\"submittedBy\":{\"_id\":\"69b0cc94e29a0b14bf239920\",\"avatarUrl\":\"https://cdn-avatars.huggingface.co/v1/production/uploads/69b0cc94e29a0b14bf239920/tHl5ZcGulvKtm8axVw5ja.png\",\"fullname\":\"Bo Zou\",\"name\":\"ZouBo\",\"type\":\"user\",\"isPro\":false,\"isHf\":false,\"isHfAdmin\":false,\"isMod\":false,\"followerCount\":5,\"isUserFollowing\":false},\"organization\":{\"_id\":\"69b0d40a64a392f94806a497\",\"name\":\"BreadStudio\",\"fullname\":\"Bread Studio\",\"avatar\":\"https://cdn-avatars.huggingface.co/v1/production/uploads/69b0cc94e29a0b14bf239920/4X5kmuAPqevfVXdfZH0wX.png\"},\"isAuthorParticipating\":true},{\"paper\":{\"id\":\"2610.39423\",\"authors\":[{\"_id\":\"6ae41582c3b566b041f6c847\",\"name\":\"Hina Otake\",\"hidden\":false},{\"_id\":\"6ae41582c3b566b041f6c848\",\"user\":{\"_id\":\"630780e8b052e6916db45022\",\"avatarUrl\":\"/avatars/e69a96cfa04cd9933a2237ad5bc4ea9a.svg\",\"isPro\":false,\"fullname\":\"Shinei Arakawa\",\"user\":\"ShineiArakawa\",\"type\":\"user\",\"name\":\"ShineiArakawa\"},\"name\":\"Shinei Arakawa\",\"status\":\"claimed_verified\",\"statusLastChangedAt\":\"2026-10-30T08:45:05.083Z\",\"hidden\":false},{\"_id\":\"6ae41582c3b566b041f6c849\",\"name\":\"Keitaro Tanaka\",\"hidden\":false},{\"_id\":\"6ae41582c3b566b041f6c84a\",\"name\":\"Yoshihiro Fukuhara\",\"hidden\":false},{\"_id\":\"6ae41582c3b566b041f6c84b\",\"name\":\"Hirokatsu Kataoka\",\"hidden\":false},{\"_id\":\"6ae41582c3b566b041f6c84c\",\"name\":\"Shigeo Morishima\",\"hidden\":false}],\"publishedAt\":\"2026-10-29T00:00:00.000Z\",\"submittedOnDailyAt\":\"2026-10-30T00:00:00.000Z\",\"title\":\"FDSML: Formula-Driven Supervised Metric Learning with Parameter-Aware Triplet Loss\",\"submittedOnDailyBy\":{\"_id\":\"630780e8b052e6916db45022\",\"avatarUrl\":\"/avatars/e69a96cfa04cd9933a2237ad5bc4ea9a.svg\",\"isPro\":false,\"fullname\":\"Shinei Arakawa\",\"user\":\"ShineiArakawa\",\"type\":\"user\",\"name\":\"ShineiArakawa\"},\"summary\":\"Formula-Driven Supervised Learning (FDSL) enables vision pre-training without real images, but existing methods cast pre-training as classification on discretized labels, discarding continuity in the generative process. We propose Formula-Driven Supervised Metric Learning (FDSML), which derives relational supervision from image-generation parameters beyond discrete class labels. FDSML combines FineFractalDB, our refined fractal dataset with higher intra-class variation and more stable structures, with a Parameter-Aware Triplet (PA-Triplet) Loss that assigns positives and negatives according to distances in the underlying Iterated Function System parameter space. This objective trains embeddings to respect parameter-derived relative similarity orderings. Experiments show that FDSML improves transferability to image retrieval while remaining competitive on downstream classification and improving robustness under several corruption settings. On ResNet-50, Recall@1 improves from 65.6% to 74.0% on Stanford Online Products and from 56.2% to 78.4% on Stanford Cars over FDSL pre-training. These results suggest that parameter-derived relational supervision provides a stronger synthetic pre-training signal than label-based classification for transfer beyond recognition.\",\"upvotes\":1,\"discussionId\":\"6ae41582c3b566b041f6c84d\",\"projectPage\":\"https://shineiarakawa.github.io/fdsml/\",\"githubRepo\":\"https://github.com/ShineiArakawa/fdsml\",\"githubRepoAddedBy\":\"user\",\"ai_summary\":\"Formula-driven supervised metric learning uses continuous generative parameters to train embeddings with parameter-aware triplet loss, improving synthetic pre-training for image retrieval and robustness.\",\"ai_keywords\":[\"Formula-Driven Supervised Learning\",\"FineFractalDB\",\"Parameter-Aware Triplet Loss\",\"Iterated Function System\",\"metric learning\",\"synthetic pre-training\",\"image retrieval\"],\"ai_summary_model\":\"thinkingmachines/Inkling-Small\",\"githubStars\":0},\"publishedAt\":\"2026-10-28T20:00:00.000Z\",\"title\":\"FDSML: Formula-Driven Supervised Metric Learning with Parameter-Aware Triplet Loss\",\"summary\":\"Formula-Driven Supervised Learning (FDSL) enables vision pre-training without real images, but existing methods cast pre-training as classification on discretized labels, discarding continuity in the generative process. We propose Formula-Driven Supervised Metric Learning (FDSML), which derives relational supervision from image-generation parameters beyond discrete class labels. FDSML combines FineFractalDB, our refined fractal dataset with higher intra-class variation and more stable structures, with a Parameter-Aware Triplet (PA-Triplet) Loss that assigns positives and negatives according to distances in the underlying Iterated Function System parameter space. This objective trains embeddings to respect parameter-derived relative similarity orderings. Experiments show that FDSML improves transferability to image retrieval while remaining competitive on downstream classification and improving robustness under several corruption settings. On ResNet-50, Recall@1 improves from 65.6% to 74.0% on Stanford Online Products and from 56.2% to 78.4% on Stanford Cars over FDSL pre-training. These results suggest that parameter-derived relational supervision provides a stronger synthetic pre-training signal than label-based classification for transfer beyond recognition.\",\"thumbnail\":\"https://cdn-thumbnails.huggingface.co/social-thumbnails/papers/2610.39423.png\",\"numComments\":2,\"upvoted\":false,\"submittedBy\":{\"_id\":\"630780e8b052e6916db45022\",\"avatarUrl\":\"/avatars/e69a96cfa04cd9933a2237ad5bc4ea9a.svg\",\"fullname\":\"Shinei Arakawa\",\"name\":\"ShineiArakawa\",\"type\":\"user\",\"isPro\":false,\"isHf\":false,\"isHfAdmin\":false,\"isMod\":false,\"isUserFollowing\":false},\"isAuthorParticipating\":true}],\"prevDate\":\"2026-10-29\",\"publisher\":{\"_id\":\"60f1abe7544c2adfd699860c\",\"avatarUrl\":\"https://cdn-avatars.huggingface.co/v1/production/uploads/1674929746905-60f1abe7544c2adfd699860c.jpeg\",\"isPro\":false,\"fullname\":\"AK\",\"user\":\"akhaliq\",\"type\":\"user\"},\"periodType\":\"day\",\"query\":{},\"isTrending\":false}\">     Daily Papers       by   AK   and the research community                                                       Daily       Weekly       Monthly                       Oct 30                           Submitted by         hyeon9698                       98                            Look Only When Needed: Certificate-Guided Visual Routing for Efficient Diffusion VLMs           KAIST AI           12           3                                    Submitted by         shenyunhang                       67                            Youtu-Parsing-Omni: One Encoder, One Schema, All Modalities           Tencent               3                          Submitted by         RandallBalestriero                       58                            Proving and Improving the Platonic Representation Hypothesis with JEPAs           galilai-group           36           4                                    Submitted by         zhshj0110                       58                            A-RSI: Adaptive Task Refresh for Zero-Data Multimodal Self-Improvement           Xiaomi Research               4                                    Submitted by         wlsgur1018                       38                            L3G-World: Real-Time Infinite World Generation with Latent 3D Gaussian Memory           KAIST AI           25           5                                    Submitted by         Gokottaw434                       36                            Read, Allocate, Route: Role-Coupled Tokenization for Pixel-Space Generative Transformers                                      ·  5 autho