e witness; “Suffix” requests a unique ending; “Set” requests both possible last moves. R01 adapts R0185 and R02 adapts R0176. \n \n \n Intended use. \n\n \n The benchmark supports final-response evaluation and terminal-reward experiments on reconstruction, analytical proofs, and tool use. It is a selected challenge set. Training on its references or rewards must be disclosed when reporting results on these tasks. \n \n \n \n Unit of analysis. \n\n \n A task consists of a diagram, stipulation, output requirement, and reference version. Changing the suffix length creates a different task from the same source problem. R02 and R0176 therefore belong in one family for training/test splits. R01 also derives from an existing composition, with a changed rook square and shorter ending. \n \n \n \n Coverage. \n\n \n The audit covers 82 primary entries in seven issues. The selection ledger separately records variants and parts. Initial no-code exploration produced 116 completed answers on 58 tasks: 77 final-answer failures and 39 accepted answers. Filtering on these results and adding R01/R02 yields the 48-task candidate pool. Some code responses in that pool remain unreviewed or technically unresolved. RetroChessBench contains the 12 tasks retained for complete grading and review. \n \n \n \n \n \n  Stage  \n  Count  \n \n \n  Retained evidence / decision  \n   \n \n \n \n  Primary source entries  \n  82  \n \n \n  Seven issues; variants and parts recorded separately in the coverage audit.  \n   \n \n  Eligible task instances  \n  58  \n \n \n  Two no-code trials each: 77 failures and 39 accepted final answers across 116 trials.  \n   \n \n  At least one no-code failure  \n  47  \n \n \n  30 tasks failed twice; 17 failed once. These observed outcomes screened the candidate pool.  \n   \n \n  Candidate pool after exclusions / additions  \n  48  \n \n \n  Remove P0299’s unresolved proof; retain 46 instances and add R01/R02.  \n   \n \n  RetroChessBench  \n  12  \n \n \n  Final IDs in Table    7   ; adaptive choice using observed difficulty and proof coverage. Two retained attempts per condition.  \n   \n \n \n  Table 8:  Selection ledger. Counts of source entries and eligible task instances use different units because parts and variants are distinct tasks. The archive preserves stage snapshots and attempt hashes; it does not reconstruct a preregistered rule or every intermediate editorial choice. \n \n \n \n Selection provenance. \n\n \n The coverage audit and  exploration_selection_final.json  record source-level inclusion decisions.  exploration_summary.json  records the 58-task screen.  selection.json  preserves an earlier snapshot with answer hashes;  frozen_selection.json , current task references, and  grading.json  govern the paper. Earlier evidence strings can predate the R0171 reference repair and must not override the current version. Table   8   records the verifiable stages without treating an adaptive process as an independent test split. The 22/24 and 18/24 failure counts use archived comparison trials, not new post-selection reruns. \n \n \n \n Reference scope. \n\n \n Constructive references supply replayable games and explanations of their mechanisms. Suffix references prove the ending under the prompt’s legal-reachability premise. R0171 includes full histories of 167 and 189 plies for its two branches; these establish feasibility without a minimum-length claim. Analytical exclusions remain natural-language proofs. \n \n \n \n Version and packaging. \n\n \n The paper analysis pins the 12-task selection recorded on 27 October 2026 and the subsequently synchronized reference/grade bundle. The environment build-source ZIP archived with this manuscript has SHA-256 \n  1ec140391c6df9e44825e65e4f43ed51b2d988714f761e8715ae71402beae63e\n  \n and is 646,079 bytes. It contains the runtime, tasks, images, references, and rubrics, with no model weights or API credentials. New model-based grades in this archived runtime require Azure access. Recomputing scores from saved judgments is offline. The  current public release , mirrored in  PDL-Bench on Hugging Face , has been built and tested in Docker, as documented in its  validation report ; its 48 frozen task, reference, rubric, provenance, and image files match the archived release byte for byte. The public ZIP has its own release hash and must not be identified by the archived hash above. \n \n \n \n \n  Appendix B  Exact solver protocol and replication \n\n \n The system prompt is the following two-sentence format instruction: \n \n  Return your response as a JSON object with one string field named solution. Follow the task’s instructions within that field.  \n \n The strict response schema requires one string field,  solution , and permits no others. The user message contains the task text and one base64-encoded board image with  detail=high . Content hashes verify the four inputs for each task; R01’s raw text differs only in newline formatting. The system prompt adds no task identifier, reference, or rubric. \n \n \n \n \n \n  Setting  \n \n \n  No code  \n  \n \n \n  Code enabled  \n   \n \n \n \n  Requested model  \n \n \n  gpt-6-astra  \n  \n \n \n  gpt-6-astra  \n   \n \n  Reasoning effort  \n \n \n  high  \n  \n \n \n  high  \n   \n \n  Tools  \n \n \n  None  \n  \n \n \n  Hosted code interpreter, auto container, 4 GB  \n   \n \n  Web-search tool  \n \n \n  Absent  \n  \n \n \n  Absent  \n   \n \n  Input  \n \n \n  Prompt, FEN, board image  \n  \n \n \n  Identical prompt, FEN, board image  \n   \n \n  Reference / rubric access  \n \n \n  None  \n  \n \n \n  None  \n   \n \n  Explicit token limit / temperature / seed  \n \n \n  Not set  \n  \n \n \n  Not set  \n   \n \n  Response mode  \n \n \n  Background, strict solution JSON  \n  \n \n \n  Background, strict solution JSON  \n   \n \n  Runs per task  \n \n \n  2  \n  \n \n \n  2  \n   \n \n  Median output tokens  \n \n \n  42,295  \n  \n \n \n  58,443  \n   \n \n  Output-token range  \n \n \n  21,199–69,369  \n  \n \n \n  6,425–81,512  \n   \n \n  Recorded code calls  \n \n \n  0  \n  \n \n \n  219, across all 24 runs  \n   \n \n  Recorded tool console output  \n \n \n  Not applicable  \n  \n \n \n  Not retained in provider response  \n   \n \n \n  Table 9:  Archived request settings and observed usage. Token counts include provider-reported reasoning tokens and are not identical to visible answer length. No claim of equal computation is made. \n \n \n Replication files. \n\n \n Start from  RetroChessBench on Poindexter Research Docs  or the  retro_chess  subset of  PDL-Bench on Hugging Face . Download the  tested RL environment Docker bundle  from the website or its  identical Hugging Face ZIP . The  website setup instructions  and  Hugging Face quick start  cover building the image, running offline checks, and exporting prompts and board images for a solver. Release validation covers native ARM64 and emulated AMD64 Docker builds and runs, all 12 reference-sequence checks, 60 regression tests, and live grading checks ( validation report ). Keep reference answers and rubrics in the evaluator. New model-based grading needs provider credentials; pin the Azure route when reproducing the study’s provider condition. The newer runtime also supports an OpenAI route and fallback, whose results have separate provenance. \n \n \n The bundle includes the exact prompts at  data/prompts/<task>/<mode>/run_1.txt  and  run_2.txt , final answers, sanitized metadata, task configurations, and grades. Full API responses remain in the original archive; the bundle retains their hashes and extracted usage/tool metadata. Credentials and private reasoning traces are excluded. \n \n \n  analysis/analyze.py  regenerates the attempt CSV, summary statistics, plots, and counterexample table using local data. It makes no model calls and never executes solver code. The collection script records evidence provenance. Original workspace paths are archival metadata, not build dependencies. \n \n \n \n Boundaries of the tool audit. \n\n \n All 219 code calls have completed status and saved payloads; none retains console output. Some attempt a chess-library import or engine lookup, which does not establish availability. A replication should preinstall dependencies, retain outputs, and record network permissions alongside tool settings. \n \n \n \n \n  Appendix C  Concrete analytical rubrics \n\n \n Tables   10   and   11   summarize the proof obligations. The frozen JSON specifies full, half, and zero credit; it and the deterministic sequence policy govern scoring. A sound equivalent proof is accepted without requiring the reference’s exposition or unused lemmas. \n \n \n \n \n \n  Item  \n  Weight  \n \n \n  Necessary result or proof obligation  \n   \n \n \n \n  C1  \n  25  \n \n \n  Correct six-ply ending, including both bishop victims and the check/mate annotations.  \n   \n \n  C2  \n  15  \n \n \n  White’s seven-capture exhaustion; noncapturing surviving a-, b-, d-pawns; Black’s five-capture budget, including two c-pawn captures and at least one b-pawn capture.  \n   \n \n  C3  \n  10  \n \n \n  Force a5-a6 and the latest black king’s origin on b8, excluding the competing origins with temporally valid arguments.  \n   \n \n  C4  \n  10  \n \n \n  Restore the captured bishop on b7 and force its preceding Bc8-b7 move, using the opened bishop diagonal and queen line.  \n   \n \n  C5  \n  10  \n \n \n  Force Kb7-b8+ and the preceding c7-c8=B+ promotion, including the bishop’s unavailable ordinary approaches.  \n   \n \n  C6  \n  5  \n \n \n  Exhaust Black’s capture budget to exclude an additional capture on b8.  \n   \n \n  C7  \n  8  \n \n \n  Force b6 as the origin of the earliest king move to b7.  \n   \n \n  C8  \n  12  \n \n \n  Identify the earliest b7 victim as a bishop; exclude the knight through the legality of the earlier checking move, not merely the presence of a check.  \n   \n \n  C9  \n  5  \n \n \n  Verify alternating check responses, the terminal diagram, and mate.  \n   \n \n \n  Table 10:  R01 rubric, 100 total points. C2 requires sufficient capture bounds; it does not demand the stronger, unnecessary claim that Black’s b-pawn promoted if an equivalent bound is proved. \n \n \n \n \n \n  Item  \n  Weight  \n \n \n  Necessary result or proof obligation  \n   \n \n \n \n  C1  \n  22  \n \n \n  Correct five-ply ending, including b1 bishop promotion and the knight captured on f3.  \n   \n \n  C2  \n  8  \n \n \n  Force the rook’s final arrival from f2 and the capture on f3 from the local check geometry.  \n   \n \n  C3  \n  16  \n \n \n  Derive the capture budgets, pawn origins, and required promotion constraints.  \n   \n \n  C4  \n  14  \n \n \n  Force Black’s immediately preceding b2-b1=B and identify the final captured piece as a knight.  \n   \n \n  C5  \n  10  \n \n \n  Establish the temporary lock and the limit imposed by alternating turns and the b-pawn’s available retractions.  \n   \n \n  C6  \n  6  \n \n \n  Exclude an earlier release through restoring and retracting Black’s captured a-pawn.  \n   \n \n  C7  \n  8  \n \n \n  Exclude the tempting h8-unpromotion route using its complete timing cost, including subsequent pawn withdrawal.  \n   \n \n  C8  \n  8  \n \n \n  Establish the viable knight-release timing while accounting for occupied squares and interleaving.  \n   \n \n  C9  \n  8  \n \n \n  Apply the bishop’s historical deadline to force the required bishop-move order.  \n   \n \n \n  Table 11:  R02 rubric, 100 total points. The first two final moves depend on the later historical release argument; identifying only the promotion/capture pair is insufficient. \n \n \n Sequence partial credit. \n\n \n The deterministic R01/R02 policy awards half of C1 only for the explicitly specified near-complete cases, including at least four correct moves in their chronological slots; R02 additionally permits a specified single ordering repair. It does not convert a different final conclusion into the expected sequence. Missing identity or annotation details may prevent full notation credit even where the underlying moves agree. The full JSON policy distinguishes these cases. \n \n \n \n Judge input and feedback. \n\n \n The judge receives the task, reference proof, expected answer or set, rubric, and submission as separate inputs. Credited reasoning requires evidence in the submission. The environment checks the quoted evidence and computes scores locally. Anchoring verifies that the text exists; judging its logic remains a separate responsibility. Instructions embedded in a submission must be treated as answer data. \n \n \n \n \n  Appendix D  A replayable counterexample to a false minimum \n\n \n P0292, by Silvio Baier, asks for a proof game of exactly 45 plies. The following reference reaches its diagram in that length. It is printed in standard algebraic notation for compactness; the dataset also stores the long-algebraic final answer and replayed UCI moves. No uniqueness or shortest-game theorem is needed: this witness alone refutes a claim that at least 47 plies are necessary. \n \n \n \n \n \n  Moves 1–12  \n  Moves 13–23   \n \n  Move  \n  White  \n  Black        \n  Move  \n  White  \n  Black   \n \n \n \n  1  \n  f4  \n  c5        \n  13  \n  Ra4  \n  b5   \n \n  2  \n  f5  \n  c4        \n  14  \n  e4  \n  bxa4   \n \n  3  \n  f6  \n  c3        \n  15  \n  Bb5  \n  f2+   \n \n  4  \n  fxe7  \n  f5        \n  16  \n  Ke2  \n  f1=B+   \n \n  5  \n  g4  \n  Kf7        \n  17  \n  Kf2  \n  Bc4   \n \n  6  \n  e8=B+  \n  Ke6        \n  18  \n  b4  \n  Bb3   \n \n  7  \n  Bh5  \n  g6        \n  19  \n  cxb3  \n  c2   \n \n  8  \n  g5  \n  gxh5        \n  20  \n  Bb2  \n  c1=R   \n \n  9  \n  g6  \n  Bd6        \n  21  \n  Qc2  \n  Re1   \n \n  10  \n  g7  \n  Ne7        \n  22  \n  Nc3  \n  Re3   \n \n  11  \n  g8=R  \n  f4        \n  23  \n  dxe3  \n  –   \n \n  12  \n  Rg4  \n  f3        \n  \n  \n   \n \n \n  Table 12:  A valid 45-ply construction for P0292. The crucial ordering is 6.e8=B+ Ke6 7.Bh5 g6 8.g5 gxh5. The bishop traverses g6 before Black occupies it. The table is generated from the independently replayed reference moves. \n \n \n The terminal piece placement is \n  rnbq3r/p2pn2p/3bk3/1B5p/pP2P3/1PN1P3/PBQ2K1P/R5NR.\n  \n Four temporary promoted officers are subsequently captured: a white bishop, a white rook, a black bishop, and a black rook. The resulting material therefore does not directly reveal all earlier promotions. In particular, the model’s lower bound fails by excluding a route under an occupancy assumption that the valid construction deliberately postpones. \n \n \n \n  Appendix E  Audit details and score interpretation \n\n \n \n \n \n  Constructive final-response outcome  \n  No code  \n  Code enabled   \n \n \n \n  No complete game supplied  \n  9  \n  7   \n \n  Legal target-reaching game, wrong length  \n  3  \n  5   \n \n  Game contains an illegal move  \n  2  \n  0   \n \n  Legal exact-length target-reaching game  \n  0  \n  2   \n \n  Total  \n  14  \n  14   \n \n \n  Table 13:  Mutually exclusive outcomes of the 28 constructive attempts. Classification follows the frozen original-answer replay, with an illegal move taking precedence over a simultaneous length error. A missing witness can accompany useful reasoning or an incorrect impossibility argument; it is not necessarily an empty answer. \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n  Reference family  \n  \n \n \n  Existence evidence in bundle  \n  \n \n \n  Analytical review / remaining evidence  \n   \n \n \n \n \n \n  Seven constructive tasks  \n  \n \n \n  Seven complete games replayed from the standard start.  \n  \n \n \n  No uniqueness claim is required.  \n   \n \n \n \n  R0171, two alternatives  \n  \n \n \n  Complete 167- and 189-ply histories, one for each victim, independently replayed.  \n  \n \n \n  The author reviewed the exclusion of other alternatives on a physical chessboard.  \n   \n \n \n \n  R01, R02, R0176, R0178  \n  \n \n \n  Local ending checks and conditional analytical references; no complete start-to-diagram witness supplied.  \n  \n \n \n  The author reviewed the conditional uniqueness arguments on a physical chessboard; complete reachability witnesses remain to be supplied.  \n   \n \n \n  Table 14:  Existence and uniqueness are separate validation obligations. Missing full witnesses do not demonstrate that a diagram is unreachable; they identify evidence still required from the benchmark author. R02 and R0176 share their diagram. \n \n \n Proof-status derivation. \n\n \n  analysis/reviewer_audit.py  maps the 20 archived analytical grades and review comments to Table   4  , retaining submission/reference hashes. It changes no rewards and requests no new judgments. The 28 constructive answers use the replay categories above: a valid witness suffices. Partial reasoning credit is not a proof-validity probability. \n \n \n \n Notation and headline counts. \n\n \n  analysis/notation_audit.py  checks all 48 retained verdicts against archived sequence status: accepted answers are exactly those marked verified. None of the 40 failures is notation-only. Two P0295 answers have a check/mate-marker defect, but both supply 48 plies instead of the required 46. Thirteen annotation-removal checks on reference strings using the archived runtime retain verified status; analytical sequence credit can fall from full to half. The audit records these checks without new model calls or changes to the saved scores. It does not validate global reachability or analytical proofs. \n \n \n \n Why 27.5% diagnostic credit can coexist with zero reward. \n\n \n P0332 no-code run 1 reaches the target legally in 46 plies; the task requires 44. Route, check, and material deductions earn 27.5% diagnostic credit. Terminal reward remains zero because the construction misses the move budget. The artifact preserves both values so that analysis of partial progress can use the diagnostic without changing the exact-task score. \n \n \n \n Reference-control interpretation. \n\n \n All 12 current references score 100%. Full-game replay verifies the constructive controls. Analytical controls satisfy the calibrated rubric under the current judge and review. R01 includes the expanded earliest-knight exclusion; R0171 uses its completed v2 reference. Reproduction must use those versions. \n \n \n \n One explicit correction. \n\n \n Review raised R0178 code run 1 from 82.5% to 88%: its tracked position and argument excluded competing c8 blockers, meeting the standard accepted in run 2. Other omissions prevent full credit. Run 2 totals 95.5% before the incomplete-proof cap reduces it to 90%. Raw judgments, caps, and overrides are stored separately. \n \n \n \n What an independent replay checks. \n\n \n Replay starts from the standard position and checks UCI moves for legality, exact length, final placement, and premature automatic termination. All seven constructive controls and both successful model games pass. Replay can establish these properties of a supplied witness. Lower bounds and complete exclusions require additional arguments. \n \n \n \n \n  Appendix F  Controlled follow-up study \n\n \n Following the validation priorities in Section   9  , freeze fresh tasks and keep composition families within one split. Use the same model snapshot and explicit token/tool budgets across paired conditions. Predefine source competence, target shift, and primary outcomes; report uncertainty over families. The interventions below are proposed controls, not completed experiments. \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n  Candidate explanation  \n  \n \n \n  Intervention and control  \n  \n \n \n  Discriminating outcome / safeguard  \n   \n \n \n \n \n \n  Missing rule knowledge  \n  \n \n \n  Probe check escape, promotions, capture budgets, and event order on unrelated positions; compare with a rules primer.  \n  \n \n \n  Local failure identifies prerequisite gaps. Do not reveal the target history.  \n   \n \n \n \n  Answer discovery  \n  \n \n \n  Compare free solving with correct-suffix assistance and a separate incorrect-candidate arm.  \n  \n \n \n  Proof failure despite a supplied answer extends beyond discovery. Keep assisted scores separate.  \n   \n \n \n \n  Proof verification  \n  \n \n \n  Compare valid proofs with minimal corruptions in blinded, randomized trials; request error location and repair.  \n  \n \n \n  Measure false acceptance and rejection; a binary label alone does not show the critical step was checked.  \n   \n \n \n \n  State tracking  \n  \n \n \n  Render boards and capture ledgers from the solver’s own candidates; use an equal-budget text control.  \n  \n \n \n  Improvement tests representation. Supplying true intermediate states leaks the answer.  \n   \n \n \n \n  Insufficient search  \n  \n \n \n  Add locally legal predecessor enumeration to the same legality checker.  \n  \n \n \n  More witnesses with residual exclusion errors separates search from proof. Global reachability filtering is oracle assistance.  \n   \n \n \n \n  Explanatory coverage / transfer  \n  \n \n \n  Compare no adaptation, answer-only examples, and worked proofs on the same training tasks; test disjoint families and new mechanism combinations.  \n  \n \n \n  Match token and compute budgets; establish source competence. Separate answer recovery from proof completion. Gains do not reveal original training exposure.  \n   \n \n \n \n  Quantifier confusion  \n  \n \n \n  Match feasible unique, multiple-answer, and impossible instances with certified labels.  \n  \n \n \n  Require witnesses or exclusions for the contract; match geometry and proof burden.  \n   \n \n \n \n \n Tool records. \n\n \n Preinstall and smoke-test supplied libraries. Retain code, standard output/error, package versions, network policy, timeouts, and tool-call limits. Report provider-default use separately from matched-budget comparisons; identify any specialist proof-game solver or global reachability oracle explicitly. \n \n \n   \n  \n  \n\n \n \n \n \n   \n    Experimental support, please\n     view the build logs \n    for errors. Generated by\n     \n       \n        L\n         A \n        T\n         E \n       \n       xml \n       \n     .\n   \n   \n     Instructions for reporting errors \n     We are continuing to improve HTML versions of papers, and your feedback helps enhance accessibility and mobile\n      support. To report errors in the HTML that will help us improve conversion and rendering, choose any of the\n      methods listed below: \n     \n       Click the \"Report Issue\"  ( )  button, located in the page header. \n     \n      Tip:  You can select the relevant text first, to include it in your report. \n     Our team has already identified  the following issues . We appreciate your time reviewing and reporting rendering errors we\n      may not have found yet. Your efforts will help us improve the HTML versions for all readers, because disability\n      should not be a barrier to accessing research. Thank you for your continued support in championing open access for\n      all. \n     Have a free development cycle? Help support accessibility at arXiv! Our collaborators at LaTeXML maintain a  list of packages that need conversion , and welcome  developer contributions . \n   \n  \n   \n     \n       \n        We gratefully acknowledge support from our   major funders  ,\n          member institutions   ,    ,\n        and all contributors.\n       \n       \n         About   &middot; \n         Help   &middot; \n         Contact   &middot; \n         Subscribe   &middot; \n         Copyright   &middot; \n         Privacy   &middot; \n         Accessibility   &middot; \n         Operational Status  (opens in new tab)  \n       \n     \n   \n  \n   \n     \n   \n  \n \n"
