  "text": " \n \n\n     Artificial Intelligence   \n   \n   \n   \n   \n   \n   \n   \n   \n   \n   \n   \n \n    \n    \n   \n \n\n  \n     Skip to main content \n  \n \n \n \n    \n   \n     \n   \n   \n     \n     Search \n   \n   \n     \n   \n   \n     Submit \n     Donate \n      \n Log in    \n \n \n   \n     \n       Search arXiv \n       \n       \n       \n     \n     \n      Press Enter to search &middot;  Advanced search \n     \n   \n \n\n     \n       \n \n \n   Artificial Intelligence \n\n \n    New submissions  \n    Cross-lists  \n    Replacements  \n \n\n See  recent  articles \n Showing new listings for Friday, 30 October 2026 \n\n       Total of 605 entries \n     \n     Showing up to 2000 entries per page:\n           \n      fewer \n |\n           more \n |\n           all \n\n     \n\n\n\n\n       \n     New submissions (showing 158 of 158 entries) \n\n\n     \n       [1] \n       \n        arXiv:2610.37720\n       \n      \n        [ pdf ,  html ,  other ]\n     \n     \n       \n          Title: \n          LRMoE: Demand-Side Load Re-Calibration for Efficient MoE Training on Memory-Constrained Nodes\n         \n          Jiaming Hu ,  Zhida Jiang ,  Zhaolong Xing ,  Yu Li ,  Zhen Chen ,  Ke Zhang  \n\n\n\n          Subjects: \n           Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI) ; Distributed, Parallel, and Cluster Computing (cs.DC); Machine Learning (cs.LG)\n         \n\n         \n          Sparse Mixture-of-Experts (MoE) models offer scalable capacity for Web applications, but uneven routing creates computation and communication bottlenecks. Existing placement and replication methods redistribute this workload, yet additional expert copies compete with token-related buffers for limited GPU memory. We present LRMoE, a memory-aware MoE training system that jointly recalibrates backward demand and balances the retained workload. During forward execution, it plans retention quotas and expert placement under resource constraints while preserving all routed computations. During backpropagation, a load recalibration metric estimates each token-expert pair's contribution to the expert update and selects pairs within these quotas. A capacity-aware routing algorithm distributes retained pairs among eligible instances of their original experts. Physical compaction then forms smaller communication payloads and variable-size grouped GEMMs, reducing backward traffic, computation, and execution-buffer demand. Experiments on Qwen3-30B-A3B and GLM4.5-106B-A12B show that LRMoE reduces cumulative MoE-layer training time to fixed-loss targets by 21.2-44.8% relative to SYMI.\n         \n       \n     \n     \n       [2] \n       \n        arXiv:2610.37731\n       \n      \n        [ pdf ,  other ]\n     \n     \n       \n          Title: \n          Amortized Localized Posterior Inference for Model Calibration with Application to an Ocean Turbulence Closure\n         \n          Exaucé Luweh Adjim Ngarti  (AIRSEA),  Élise Arnaud  (AIRSEA),  Léo Nicoletti ,  Victor Trappler  (Mines Saint-Étienne MSE, LIMOS, FAYOL-ENSMSE, FAYOL-ENSMSE),  Arthur Vidard  (AIRSEA) \n\n\n\n          Subjects: \n           Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI) ; Machine Learning (cs.LG); Machine Learning (stat.ML)\n         \n\n         \n          Model discrepancy may prevent a single calibration parameter from reproducing a physical response across the context domain. In addition, uncertainties in observations and contexts affect calibration and must be accounted for and quantified. We construct a family of localized posteriors by weighting observation likelihoods according to their proximity to a target context. Gaussian localization weights incorporate context uncertainty through covariance inflation, with reflection at domain boundaries and an optional correction for uneven context sampling. A conditional normalizing flow approximates the family of posteriors through amortized variational inference. Integrating the localized posteriors under a prescribed context measure gives the marginalized parameter distribution. In a twin experiment with the Branin-Hoo model, we compare global and localized inference under uniform and skewed context distributions. Localization reduces the reported aggregate predictive errors relative to the global posterior. An application to the k-$\\epsilon$ turbulence closure in a single-column ocean model, with atmospheric surface forcing as context, shows that localized posterior calibration reduces temperature and zonal-velocity errors relative to deterministic and global-posterior calibration. Both twin experiments are constructed to admit local compensation.\n         \n       \n     \n     \n       [3] \n       \n        arXiv:2610.37732\n       \n      \n        [ pdf ,  html ,  other ]\n     \n     \n       \n          Title: \n          ThinkJev: One Thought, Every Budget for Calibrated Typed Decisions\n         \n          Xiaolu Ding ,  Kai Yang ,  Dongdong Liu  \n\n          Comments: \n          31 pages, 7 figures, 10 tables\n         \n\n\n          Subjects: \n           Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI) ; Computation and Language (cs.CL); Machine Learning (cs.LG)\n         \n\n         \n          Many uses of language models end in a typed decision: a choice among a closed set of options, whose probabilities code can act on. Reading those probabilities from one forward pass is fast but often wrong on decisions that take several steps, such as arithmetic, counting, and date comparison. Letting the model think first fixes most of these errors, but costs seconds per decision instead of milliseconds. To pay for thinking only where it helps, ThinkJev builds a fast and a slow system from one open hybrid-thinking model that can answer directly or after a thought. System 1 reads the model's probabilities for the option letters when it answers directly; System 2 reads the same letters after the model has thought on the same cached prompt, so escalating repeats no work and both systems give probabilities over the same options. Because the letters can be read after any number of thought tokens, one sampled thought also gives the answer under every thinking budget. Without training, ThinkJev lets System 1 answer alone only where a statistical test on labeled dev data guarantees that its accepted answers stay under a target error rate. At a 5% target, Qwen3.5-27B answers 24.5% of 8359 test decisions this way with a realized error of 3.9% (Qwen3.5-4B and 9B: 6.5% and 0.0%). For the rest, stopping each thought as soon as its answer is confident reaches 81.6% accuracy, against 82.7% for always thinking, with 72% of the thought tokens. Choosing when to think from System 1's confidence alone saves less, because the decisions System 1 is least sure of are often not the ones thinking fixes. On the public items of JevBench, an independent benchmark of typed-decision services, Qwen3.5-27B thinks on 29.4% and is not distinguishable in accuracy from the two thinking APIs listed (exploratory). Code and data generators are available at  this https URL .\n         \n       \n     \n     \n       [4] \n       \n        arXiv:2610.37739\n       \n      \n        [ pdf ,  html ,  other ]\n     \n     \n       \n          Title: \n          OpenFrontiers: A Collection of Open Research Problems and a Benchmark for Scientific Rediscovery\n         \n          Jiarui Yao ,  Ruida Wang ,  Tong Zhang  \n\n\n\n          Subjects: \n           Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI) ; Computation and Language (cs.CL); Machine Learning (cs.LG)\n         \n\n         \n          Large language models (LLMs) have shown increasing promise in research-level problem solving, yet a comprehensive collection of such problems is still missing, and evaluating them on research-level problems remains challenging. We introduce OpenFrontiers, a cross-disciplinary resource that combines a structured corpus of open research problems with a retrospective benchmark of recently resolved problems in mathematics. The corpus records problem statements, context, progress, and other information for 12,151 problems. The benchmark contains 200 problems, 110 resolved in 2026 and 90 still open, each with a Lean~4 statement checked by automated probes and two independent reviews; a subset HQ-50 further filters based on quality score, etc. Our evaluation protocol grades formal proofs with two independent checkers in an isolated sandbox under closed-book, frozen-retrieval, and live-web conditions. Evaluating seven LLM agents on the HQ-50 subset under a standardized budget, we find that closed-book rediscovery is rare, at most 7 of 40 resolved problems, whereas live-web success mostly reflects retrieval of published solutions. By linking a broad research-problem corpus to a formally graded evaluation subset with documented solutions, OpenFrontiers provides a foundation for measuring research-level rediscovery and studying progress toward solving currently open scientific problems. The dataset and benchmark could be found in  this https URL .\n         \n       \n     \n     \n       [5] \n       \n        arXiv:2610.37786\n       \n      \n        [ pdf ,  html ,  other ]\n     \n     \n       \n          Title: \n          WebAnything: From the Wild Web to Scalable Agent Environments\n         \n          Tianci Xie ,  Zikun Cai ,  Chaoyun Zhang ,  Pinxue Guo ,  Bingxuan Li ,  Rui Zheng ,  Hang Yan  \n\n          Comments: \n          25 pages, 6 figures\n         \n\n\n          Subjects: \n           Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI) \n         \n\n         \n          Web agents require diverse, executable, and resettable website environments for reproducible training and evaluation. Human-assisted mirrors demand per-site engineering, while generative construction can diverge from specified real websites. Capture-and-replay preserves observations, with interaction coverage constrained by captured content and replay capabilities.\n We present WebAnything, a fully automated framework that reconstructs source-grounded, reusable Web environments from real URLs without per-site manual implementation or repair after initial setup and authorization. An Explorer-Builder collects browser-observable structures, resources, and interaction outcomes; an independent verifier checks frozen candidates and guides local repair or targeted source recapture.\n We construct 244 website artifacts across 14 application domains and 53 categories, supporting 12,984 executed and accepted tasks. Compared with human-assisted WebHarbor, WebAnything achieves higher aggregate scores on three fidelity metrics, with 12 wins, two ties, and no losses in a blind audit. Fine-tuning Qwen3.5-9B on 5,856 interaction trajectories improves REAL success by 9.9 percentage points, Odysseys-Easy Rubric Avg and Task Avg by 6.25 and 6.32 percentage points, and WebArena-Verified Overall success by 13.33 percentage points under the fixed harness. Code:  this https URL \n         \n       \n     \n     \n       [6] \n       \n        arXiv:2610.37809\n       \n      \n        [ pdf ,  html ,  other ]\n     \n     \n       \n          Title: \n          What Does an Ontology Catch? Curated, LLM-Generated and Data-Derived Ontologies as Checkers of LLM Agents over Knowledge Graphs\n         \n          Michal Orzechowski ,  Rund Tawfiq ,  Tomasz Kosciolek  \n\n          Comments: \n          13 pages, 2 figures. Under review\n         \n\n\n          Subjects: \n           Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI) ; Databases (cs.DB)\n         \n\n         \n          LLM agents that answer questions over Web knowledge graphs are increasingly wrapped in checks derived from the graph's ontology, on the assumption that a formal schema makes a reliable judge. Yet a checker can be wrong as well: it can miss errors, and it can flag correct answers and push the agent into \"fixing\" them. Whether a curated ontology is worth its cost as such a judge, and how it compares with an ontology an LLM writes or a schema mined from the data, is unknown. We make the checker itself the object of study. Across curated, LLM-generated and data-derived ontologies, used as prompt context, as a checker, or both, and across open-weight and proprietary agents on public graphs and a private graph of nearly $10^{9}$ triples, we account for what ontology-derived checks catch and what they wrongly flag. We show how each source trades caught errors against false alarms, how a curated schema used as published can contradict its own data, which errors structural checks miss, and whether combining sources or giving the agent the checker's specific feedback pays off. On UniProt, the curated ontology flags more correct answers than either an ontology derived from the data or one an LLM derives from a summary of the data with masked identifiers. On real agent answers, its flags are only marginally more precise than random flags.\n         \n       \n     \n     \n       [7] \n       \n        arXiv:2610.37830\n       \n      \n        [ pdf ,  html ,  other ]\n     \n     \n       \n          Title: \n          Measuring Where Intent Gets Lost in Agentic Smart Contract Synthesis\n         \n          Abhinav Goel ,  Agostino Capponi ,  Alfio Gliozzo ,  Chaitya Shah  \n\n          Comments: \n          ACM TheWebConf2027 8 pages main content 4 pages appendix\n         \n\n\n          Subjects: \n           Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI) ; Human-Computer Interaction (cs.HC); Information Retrieval (cs.IR); Multiagent Systems (cs.MA); Software Engineering (cs.SE)\n         \n\n         \n          AI agents can now write software from a plain-English description, but code that runs is not the same as code that does what was asked. This gap matters most for smart contracts, the programs behind Web3 applications that move money on a blockchain and cannot be fixed once deployed. We ask how to check whether an AI-written contract behaves as it should, and where the requested behavior gets lost.\n We design an evaluation built on execution rather than opinion. We replay the same transactions on an AI-written contract and on the original it should reproduce and measure how often they agree. We test every grading tool on planted mistakes, compare against an answer key written without the description, and audit our own scorer until known wrong answers cannot pass. Common methods, such as AI judges and standard analysis tools, miss obvious mistakes, and scoring choices matter too. Checking only the functions every system includes hid about half the difference between pipelines.\n We then propose an elicitation-first architecture. Before writing code, the agent asks the client a short checklist of questions, checks the contract against an independently written executable answer key, and returns disagreements to the client instead of silently rewriting code. Asking first matters far more than reviewing or patching afterwards, across three AI models, while automated security fixes often break requested behavior. A few well-chosen questions recover much of what a short description omits, and their value can be estimated without the original contract. To our knowledge, this is the first study in smart contract synthesis to gather missing requirements before generation and check the result against an independently written executable model. The lessons extend to any setting where AI agents turn requests into programs. We release our tools, 600 tasks and all results.\n         \n       \n     \n     \n       [8] \n       \n        arXiv:2610.37849\n       \n      \n        [ pdf ,  html ,  other ]\n     \n     \n       \n          Title: \n          Detecting Covert Collusion between AI Agents through Partner-Outsider Positive Listening\n         \n          Kevin Yandoka Denamganaï  \n\n          Comments: \n          work in progress\n         \n\n\n          Subjects: \n           Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI) ; Computation and Language (cs.CL)\n         \n\n         \n          AI agents that share a secret convention can coordinate through messages that look innocent, so a monitor that reads only the messages can miss them. We propose a behavioural test: a partner that uses a code reacts to the exact wording of a message, whereas an uninvolved agent placed in its seat does not. Partner--Outsider Positive Listening (PoPLi) is the difference between the two agents in how much their action changes when the message is reworded with its meaning kept, and it is computed after the fact by replaying logged prompts. In a simulated market with five conditions (no collusion, a joint reward with neutral messages, the same with an openly stated intention, a covert first-letter code, and the code with a stated intention), PoPLi is between 0.48 and 0.59 whenever the code is present (95% intervals all above 0.29) and between -0.10 and -0.01 without it. Message-only monitors did not separate the conditions: one reading the speaker's messages answered \"no\" in all 50 markets, and one reading every seller's messages flagged 9 of 10 markets with no collusion. PoPLi detects the use of a private channel, not collusion as such, and it cannot detect collusion through incentives alone or codes carried by meaning.\n         \n       \n     \n     \n       [9] \n       \n        arXiv:2610.37851\n       \n      \n        [ pdf ,  html ,  other ]\n     \n     \n       \n          Title: \n          Evaluating and improving AI agents for sequencing protocol understanding\n         \n          Chi-Lam Poon ,  Michael Goneos ,  Zezheng Song ,  Xi Chen  \n\n\n\n          Subjects: \n           Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI) ; Genomics (q-bio.GN)\n         \n\n         \n          Existing scientific-agent benchmarks rarely evaluate whether agents can reconstruct the exact molecular structures and generation workflows specified by sequencing protocols, a capability critical for analyzing customized sequencing assays. We introduce LibStructBench, a document-grounded benchmark for reconstructing sequencing-library structures and molecular workflows. Our experiments show that frontier agents often produce approximately correct reconstructions but remain unreliable at exact reconstruction, particularly in molecular-state representation and workflow connectivity. Switching the underlying language model produced larger average performance differences than switching agent harnesses, with a statistically significant difference only for molecular-state reconstruction. Additional knowledge improved oligo-family recovery in a small pilot without improving mean molecular-workflow scores. Autonomous accumulation of procedural rules and cross-protocol examples showed non-monotonic validation performance and no mean held-out molecular-workflow improvement in the tested trajectory. These findings highlight the need for more controlled and verifiable approaches to scientific reasoning about molecular processes in complex experimental protocols.\n         \n       \n     \n     \n       [10] \n       \n        arXiv:2610.37859\n       \n      \n        [ pdf ,  html ,  other ]\n     \n     \n       \n          Title: \n          Seq@K: Evaluating Language Models Under Sequential Feedback\n         \n          Thomson Yen ,  Gilbert Yang ,  Naimeng Ye ,  Minghui Chen ,  Dong Xia ,  Tianyi Shen ,  Yechan Jeon ,  Yaqin Chen ,  Daksh Mittal ,  Fangyu Wu ,  Hongseok Namkoong  \n\n\n\n          Subjects: \n           Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI) \n         \n\n         \n          Language-model agents differ fundamentally from prior intelligent systems in their ability to learn from natural language in context. This capability enables agents to iteratively refine their answers in response to feedback, a prevalent mode of operation in modern agentic workflows. However, the community primarily ranks models by metrics such as Pass@1 or Pass@K, which cannot capture how they utilize feedback on prior attempts. We introduce Seq@K, a natural evaluation protocol that measures whether a model can solve a problem within K sequential attempts, provided feedback after each previous failure. Unlike multi-turn agentic benchmarks that examine long-horizon planning, Seq@K isolates and formalizes the core capability of refinement from feedback. We evaluate Seq@K on a suite of 7 benchmarks and 16 frontier models, and show that Seq@K reveals a materially different model-selection landscape: under Seq@K, (1) model rankings often differ, (2) the Pareto frontiers admit more efficient configurations and (3) the gains from choosing the optimal model configuration can widen or shrink relative to those of Pass@K. We further show that analyzing model trajectories under Seq@K helps characterize iterative refinement behavior, such as the rate at which models address issues raised in feedback, or how rigidly they hold onto their own answers. Finally, we study how the nature of feedback affects models' iterative refinement, showing that practitioners must carefully design and benchmark the feedback loops of iterative agentic systems. Together, these results establish how Seq@K offers a more faithful metric for intelligent systems that typically operate in such feedback loop, and the necessity to consider Seq@K in evaluating models for these systems.\n         \n       \n     \n     \n       [11] \n       \n        arXiv:2610.37867\n       \n      \n        [ pdf ,  html ,  other ]\n     \n     \n       \n          Title: \n          What Constitutes Food Similarity? Visual Representations and Retrieval in Indian Cuisine\n         \n          Saransh Kumar Gupta ,  Akshadha Sharma ,  Lipika Dey ,  Partha Pratim Das ,  Ramesh Jain  \n\n          Comments: \n          17 pages, 7 figures, 4 tables, 31 references\n         \n\n\n          Subjects: \n           Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI) ; Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV); Information Retrieval (cs.IR)\n         \n\n         \n          Food similarity underlies retrieval, recommendation, substitution, and other food computing tasks, yet food representations are typically evaluated by identity preservation. We ask what happens to food similarity beyond identity: how different visual representations organize foods into relational neighborhoods, what culinary relationships these neighborhoods reveal or obscure, and how consistently these structures emerge across representations. Indian cuisine provides a challenging setting in which visually similar foods may have different culinary identities, while foods within a culinary family can vary substantially in appearance. Using an expanded Khana dataset and seven pretrained visual representations spanning convolutional, transformer-based, self-supervised, and image-text aligned models, we evaluate image-to-image retrieval and image-to-dish neighborhoods, examining culinary-family structure, contrast cases, and cross-representation agreement. We also aggregate image representations to compare dish-level visual similarity across models and against recipe-derived similarity for the same dishes. Image-level neighborhoods vary substantially across representations, including similarities across culinary-family boundaries. At the dish level, aggregation reveals common visual structure despite local variation, but this structure corresponds less strongly with recipe-derived similarity. These findings show that recognition performance alone cannot characterize relational structure: food similarity depends on representation, modality, and abstraction level rather than being a fixed property of the food collection. Understanding these structures is therefore a prerequisite to combining multiple food information modalities into a coherent notion of similarity.\n         \n       \n     \n     \n       [12] \n       \n        arXiv:2610.37875\n       \n      \n        [ pdf ,  html ,  other ]\n     \n     \n       \n          Title: \n          Verification Learning Theory: A Process Model of Checking and Retention in AI-Assisted Judgment\n         \n          Christoph Trattner  \n\n\n\n          Subjects: \n           Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI) \n         \n\n         \n          Accurate AI assistance can improve immediate judgments while reducing opportunities to practise the operations needed for independent performance. Verification Learning Theory (VLT) formalises this possibility for claim-evaluation practice followed by correct, independent, rule-specific feedback. The model links perceived assistant reliability to evidence checking and links the history of those checks to retained rule use. Its strict form assumes that task demands and perceived reliability influence learning only through checking, with equal learning per genuine check. It therefore predicts that perceived fallibility can improve delayed unaided judgment even without observed assistant errors, and that this advantage disappears when checking is already required and performed. Rule-specific gains and forecasts from checking histories provide further tests. Simulations examine the consequences and parameter dependence of these commitments; they do not constitute empirical validation. Comparisons with depth-of-processing and feedback-encoding accounts identify where predictions diverge and where the accounts remain observationally equivalent. The framework specifies experiments that can test the proposed mechanism and separates retention of practised rules from the broader problem of recognising which rule applies to a new claim.\n         \n       \n     \n     \n       [13] \n       \n        arXiv:2610.37896\n       \n      \n        [ pdf ,  html ,  other ]\n     \n     \n       \n          Title: \n          The Anatomy of Socio-Demographics in Pluralistic Offensiveness Modeling: When and Where It Helps, and the Role of Reasoning\n         \n          Yifan Wang ,  Leixin Zhang ,  Jinyi Mu ,  Yu Wang ,  Mayank Jobanputra ,  Isabel Valera ,  Vera Demberg  \n\n\n\n          Subjects: \n           Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI) \n         \n\n         \n          Subjective judgments such as offensiveness often vary substantially across individuals, motivating pluralistic modeling approaches that incorporate annotators' socio-demographic information to capture this variation. However, prior findings on the benefits of socio-demographic information for pluralistic modeling are mixed, calling for a systematic analysis of both its effectiveness and the mechanisms through which it improves prediction. In this work, we study $\\textbf{when}$ and $\\textbf{where}$ socio-demographics benefit pluralistic offensiveness modeling, as well as $\\textbf{the role of explicit reasoning}$ in leveraging this information. Across three multi-annotator datasets with prompted and trained LLMs, we first show that demographic gains are small and inconsistent for individual predictions but become clearer at the group and population levels, because aggregation attenuates the idiosyncratic variation that masks small but systematic demographic signals. Second, using a reasoning and prediction format that explicitly decomposes individual judgment into an overall baseline and a demographic-specific deviation, we show that the gains arise from both components: demographic information improves baseline estimation and enables meaningful demographic-specific shifts, and interventions show that better component estimates improve final predictions. Finally, comparing direct demographic conditioning with explicit reasoning about how the text interacts with annotator demographics, we find that reasoning yields more accurate baseline and shift estimates as well as final predictions, whereas conditioning alone rarely produces informative shifts. These results suggest that the mixed evidence in prior work may partly stem from individual-level evaluation, and that the value of socio-demographic information depends on both how it is evaluated and how models are guided to use it.\n         \n       \n     \n     \n       [14] \n       \n        arXiv:2610.37905\n       \n      \n        [ pdf ,  html ,  other ]\n     \n     \n       \n          Title: \n          FilingLoop: Evaluating and Evolving LLM Agents for Corporate Disclosure Review and Revision\n         \n          Wentao Zhang ,  Yilei Zhao ,  Guangyan Gan ,  Shuo Sun ,  Lin William Cong  \n\n\n\n          Subjects: \n           Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI) \n         \n\n         \n          Public disclosure repositories such as SEC EDGAR support informed investment through web access to corporate filings. Supporting their review with language model agents requires discovering unstated deficiencies across related documents, acquiring missing company facts, and verifying that requested revisions satisfy evidence-backed requirements. Existing financial question-answering evaluations provide limited evidence of these interactive capabilities. We introduce \\textbf{FilingLoop}, an interactive benchmark and a self-evolving agent framework for corporate disclosure review and revision. Grounded in historical U.S.\\ Securities and Exchange Commission correspondence, FilingLoop reconstructs 380 predominantly IPO review tasks from 96 companies under distinct information constraints. An evaluator separate from the acting agents scores final filings and responses against 6,099 eligible requirements and audits unsupported claims and harmful revisions. Our framework couples iterative review and revision within each case with procedural learning across cases, converting requirement-level evaluation feedback into role-specific guidance without updating model weights. Updates affect only subsequent cases, allowing procedural adaptation to be assessed without retrying scored tasks. On the evaluated stream, evolution increases requirement fulfillment from 24.28\\% to 27.96\\% and full comment resolution from 16.90\\% to 20.75\\%, with a 5.8\\% increase in total cost over the fixed-policy variant. FilingLoop connects the concerns an agent discovers with the company facts it obtains and the revisions it commits, providing requirement-level feedback for procedures used in later review tasks.\n         \n       \n     \n     \n       [15] \n       \n        arXiv:2610.37906\n       \n      \n        [ pdf ,  html ,  other ]\n     \n     \n       \n          Title: \n          Beyond Low-Pass Dynamics: Frequency-Selective Spiking Reservoirs with Resonant Neurons\n         \n          Aurora Micheli ,  Claudio Gallicchio ,  Nergis Tomen  \n\n          Comments: \n          Accepted at NeurIPS 2026. 30 pages (10 main text), 4 figures. Code:  this https URL \n         \n\n\n          Subjects: \n           Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI) \n         \n\n         \n          Spiking reservoir computing combines the training efficiency of fixed recurrent reservoirs with the energy efficiency of event-driven inference, where computation is triggered by sparse spikes rather than executed densely at every timestep. However, existing spiking reservoirs almost exclusively rely on Leaky Integrate-and-Fire (LIF) neurons, whose low-pass dynamics limit their ability to distinguish information encoded at different temporal frequencies. We introduce HRF-Res, a liquid state machine in which LIF neurons are replaced by a heterogeneous population of Harmonic Resonate-and-Fire (HRF) neurons. These neurons exhibit intrinsic oscillatory dynamics and selectively respond to input frequencies, enabling the reservoir to capture frequency-specific temporal patterns. The result is more discriminative representations and higher classification accuracy than LIF-based reservoirs, achieved without sacrificing the energy efficiency of spiking computation, with up to 19x lower energy consumption than an equivalent non-spiking oscillatory reservoir. Evaluated on ten benchmarks spanning time series, sequential images, audio spike trains, and neuromorphic vision, HRF-Res achieves state-of-the-art accuracy among spiking reservoir methods. These results demonstrate that introducing frequency-selective dynamics into spiking reservoirs improves representational quality and classification performance while preserving their inherent energy efficiency.\n         \n       \n     \n     \n       [16] \n       \n        arXiv:2610.37919\n       \n      \n        [ pdf ,  html ,  other ]\n     \n     \n       \n          Title: \n          SciGame: Let Agents Play the Scientific Discovery Games in the Real and Counterfactual World\n         \n          Yusheng Zhao ,  Bowei He ,  Xue Liu ,  Mingxiao Li  \n\n          Comments: \n          25 pages, 11 figures, 9 tables\n         \n\n\n          Subjects: \n           Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI) ; Computation and Language (cs.CL)\n         \n\n         \n          Large language model agents are increasingly used for scientific discovery. However, existing benchmarks mix law inference with programming, score data fitting instead of prediction on unseen inputs, and do not separate empirical evidence from pretrained priors. We introduce SciGame, a controlled benchmark that converts scientific literature into structured scientific cards. Each card yields a pair of Original and dimensionally consistent Counterfactual environments with the same task interface. SciGame contains a Historical collection of canonical laws and a live Temporal collection curated from papers published after model knowledge cutoffs. It evaluates agents in candidate-guided Identification and open-ended Discovery settings. Each setting runs under Prior-only, Fixed, and Adaptive protocols. Experiments on five frontier models yield three findings. First, familiar priors hurt performance once the governing relation is altered. Second, adaptive experimentation helps the strongest models but harms weaker ones under limited budgets. Third, good explanations can hide quantitative failures. Recovering the exact symbolic form remains the main bottleneck, even when agents interpret evidence correctly. Current agents can investigate systems actively, but inducing exact laws in unfamiliar settings remains an open challenge.\n         \n       \n     \n     \n       [17] \n       \n        arXiv:2610.37923\n       \n      \n        [ pdf ,  html ,  other ]\n     \n     \n       \n          Title: \n          VerifiedLab: Harnessing Knowns for Unknowns\n         \n          Yu Yao ,  Jiayi Dong ,  Yang Yang ,  Shuiwang Ji ,  Ju Li  \n\n\n\n          Subjects: \n           Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI) \n         \n\n         \n          Across human history, knowledge has undergone several transitions, from orality to writing and then to digital archives. At each transition, humans established new conventions to preserve knowledge for the new era. Nowadays, as research agents are increasingly deployed for laboratory experiments, knowledge is crossing another boundary: from records written for humans to those for machines. In this work, we introduce VerifiedLab, a multi-agent harness framework for this human-to-machine transition of knowledge. VerifiedLab transforms research output from passive artifacts into active systems that accelerate downstream use, adoption and prediction of future trends. In VerifiedLab, a team of AI scientists with distinct roles reasons over records verified field by field: a principal investigator plans, a librarian computes the evidence directly from the records, specialists draft, and a gate and a critic check each claim against the measured reliability of its data before the harness decides whether to release or withhold it. We demonstrate the efficacy of VerifiedLab through extensive experiments on perovskite solar cells: VerifiedLab recovers known physical trends, anticipates devices reported a year later, and lets AI scientists inherit the literature together with a measure of how far it can be trusted. By scaling the inference-time verification compute, VerifiedLab introduces a new pathway for knowledge dissemination and machine harness for AI co-scientists.\n         \n       \n     \n     \n       [18] \n       \n        arXiv:2610.37926\n       \n      \n        [ pdf ,  html ,  other ]\n     \n     \n       \n          Title: \n          GARDEN: Generalist Autonomous Reward Design Through Evidence Navigation for Cooperative Multi-Agent Reinforcement Learning\n         \n          Dogan Urgun  \n\n          Comments: \n          8 pages, 3 figures, 5 tables. Code:  this https URL \n         \n\n\n          Subjects: \n           Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI) ; Multiagent Systems (cs.MA); Systems and Control (eess.SY)\n         \n\n         \n          Cooperative multi-agent reinforcement learning (MARL) enables teams of agents, such as robots and autonomous vehicles, to learn coordinated behavior, but what such teams learn is largely determined by their reward function. Because task success alone provides too little guidance for learning, intermediate rewards are usually designed by hand, and this effort must often be repeated whenever the environment or the team changes. Large language models (LLMs) can automate the effort by writing reward functions as code, yet the resulting rewards are typically designed and tested in a single setting. To enable their reuse across settings, GARDEN (Generalist Autonomous Reward Design through Evidence Navigation) is proposed, in which one reward program is developed for a family of related scenarios and transferred, unchanged, to scenarios that were not available during its design. During development, environment code, training records, and agent behavior are investigated by an LLM reward engineer, and draft rewards are tested in trial training runs, while the learning algorithm and the rule for selecting the final reward are held fixed. On unseen scenarios of the Overcooked and SMAX benchmarks, task returns were increased by 58.2% and 52.5% over the default rewards on two kitchen layouts, and the mean win rate in two combat scenarios was raised from 51.0% to 89.9%. Under matched candidate and probe budgets, this exceeded the 84.2% and 79.2% obtained with fixed diagnostic feedback and Eureka-inspired reward reflection. These results show that a single autonomously engineered reward can be reused effectively across related cooperative scenarios.\n         \n       \n     \n     \n       [19] \n       \n        arXiv:2610.37932\n       \n      \n        [ pdf ,  other ]\n     \n     \n       \n          Title: \n          Understanding Large Language Models as Numerical Optimizers\n         \n          Lingyun Guo ,  Ali Bereyhi  \n\n          Comments: \n          17 pages, including appendices\n         \n\n\n          Subjects: \n           Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI) ; Machine Learning (cs.LG); Signal Processing (eess.SP)\n         \n\n         \n          Large language models (LLMs) have emerged as potential tools for numerical optimization. Despite promising results, their intrinsic optimization capabilities and limitations remain poorly understood, particularly in high dimensions. This paper investigates the ability of LLMs to exploit mathematical problem structures and generate feasible, near-optimal solutions. We develop an evaluation framework that distinguishes solution quality from execution failures, including numerical inaccuracies, constraint violations, and output-format errors. Using representative problems from convex optimization, fractional programming, and constrained projection, we assess LLM performance against deterministic reference solvers across different dimensions. Our results reveal that LLMs can capture useful optimization structures and produce near-optimal solutions, even when their outputs fail to satisfy numerical or formatting requirements. However, reliability deteriorates with increasing problem dimension and depends strongly on the underlying optimization structure. To further investigate the limitations, we deploy an iterative refinement mechanism that provides targeted feedback on feasibility and solution quality. The results demonstrate that iterative refinement substantially improves reliability and extends the range of problem dimensions over which LLMs produce valid solutions. Nevertheless, persistent numerical and structural errors limit their applicability as standalone optimizers. These findings provide insights into the capacity and limits of LLM-based optimization and motivate hybrid approaches that combine LLM reasoning with deterministic numerical verification.\n         \n       \n     \n     \n       [20] \n       \n        arXiv:2610.37935\n       \n      \n        [ pdf ,  html ,  other ]\n     \n     \n       \n          Title: \n          SkillMem: Compact External Skill Memory for Latent Multi-Agent Reasoning\n         \n          Yuxuan Zhu ,  Mohammad Mohammadi Amiri ,  Pin-Yu Chen ,  Keerthiram Murugesan  \n\n\n\n          Subjects: \n           Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI) ; Computation and Language (cs.CL); Machine Learning (cs.LG)\n         \n\n         \n          Latent multi-agent systems reduce inference cost by keeping intermediate reasoning and inter-agent communication in continuous representations.\\ Reusable skills, however, are commonly supplied as text or stored as layer-wise Key-Value states or skill-specific model parameters, introducing repeated processing or substantial per-skill storage.\\ We introduce \\textbf{SkillMem}, which represents each reusable skill as a small external latent memory and makes it directly available during latent multi-agent reasoning.\\ A role-conditioned cross-attention reader allows the same memory to guide different agents according to their roles and current hidden states.\\ We train the skill memories and shared reader through behavioral distillation, matching the change in token predictions induced by a textual skill while keeping the backbone frozen.\\ For our experiments, we obtain textual skills from paired successful and failed multi-agent trajectories, although existing skills can also be compiled directly.\\ Across six benchmarks and Qwen3 models ranging from 4B to 14B parameters, SkillMem provides strong gains for smaller backbones while preserving efficient latent multi-agent inference across model scales.\\ We use the same $8\\times256$ memory configuration across all evaluated model scales, requiring 9.6\\,KB of skill-specific storage per skill, including its retrieval embedding.\\ For a library of 100 skills, SkillMem reduces total storage by more than $38\\times$ compared with alternative skill representations and achieves up to a $4.23\\times$ end-to-end inference speedup.\\ These results show that compact procedural memories can be reused without sacrificing the efficiency of latent multi-agent reasoning.\n         \n       \n     \n     \n       [21] \n       \n        arXiv:2610.37989\n       \n      \n        [ pdf ,  other ]\n     \n     \n       \n          Title: \n          GAUSS: A Workload-Aware Analytical and Monte-Carlo Simulator for LLM Inference Serving\n         \n          Srikant Bharadwaj ,  Ankur Mallick ,  Spyridon Mastorakis ,  Anjaly Parayil ,  Dhruv Deshmukh ,  Victor Ruehle  \n\n\n\n          Subjects: \n           Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI) \n         \n\n         \n          Large language model (LLM) inference has become an operational workload that fleets must provision, monitor, and bill against latency service-level objectives (SLOs). A single throughput number cannot answer the questions operators actually face, because generative serving couples two phases with very different service characteristics, continuous batching reshapes the effective service process, and user-visible tail latency can degrade long before aggregate throughput saturates. We present GAUSS, a workload-aware analytical and Monte-Carlo simulator that predicts the full distributions of time-to-first-token (TTFT) and time-between-tokens (TBT), together with the sustainable request rate, for a given workload, engine, and accelerator. GAUSS pairs calibrated, phase-specific latency surfaces for prefill and decode with a Lindley-recurrence model of queueing under stochastic arrivals and estimates performance by ensemble Monte-Carlo sampling rather than by replaying a single trace. This lets GAUSS reason about workloads, routing policies, and hardware that have never been deployed, and to capture the latency tails that trace-driven simulators systematically under-sample. We describe the model and show how it supports operating-point selection, traffic segmentation, and shortest-remaining-processing-time scheduling capacity questions that trace-driven simulators cannot address.\n         \n       \n     \n     \n       [22] \n       \n        arXiv:2610.38006\n       \n      \n        [ pdf ,  other ]\n     \n     \n       \n          Title: \n          Algorithmic Bias from the Perspectives of Healthcare Professionals\n         \n          Jennifer Xu ,  Tamara Babaian  \n\n          Comments: \n          12 pages\n         \n\n\n          Subjects: \n           Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI) \n         \n\n         \n          This paper focuses on algorithmic bias of machine learning and artificial intelligence applications in healthcare information systems. Based on the quantitative data and qualitative comments from a survey and interviews with healthcare professionals, who have different job roles (e.g., clinical vs. administrative), this study provides findings about the relationships between algorithmic bias, perceived fairness, and the intended acceptance and adoption of ML algorithms and algorithm generated outcomes. The results suggest that the opinions of healthcare professionals toward the causes of algorithmic bias, the criteria of algorithm assessment, the perceived fairness, and bias mitigation approaches may vary depending on their job roles, perspectives, tasks, and the algorithm characteristics. More research is needed to investigate algorithmic bias to ensure fairness and equality in healthcare.\n         \n       \n     \n     \n       [23] \n       \n        arXiv:2610.38024\n       \n      \n        [ pdf ,  html ,  other ]\n     \n     \n       \n          Title: \n          Assessing Language Models for Scientific Applications: Inertial Confinement Fusion as a Use Case\n         \n          P. B. Radha ,  D. D. Meyerhofer ,  M. Bhattarai ,  B. Cheng ,  L. Diaz ,  C. L. Rousculp ,  A. G. Seaton ,  G. Gupta ,  P. Gurumurthy ,  V. Gutta ,  A. Jhunjhunwala ,  A. Vem ,  P. Cook ,  G. Rowe  \n\n          Comments: \n          33 pages, 16 figures\n         \n\n\n          Subjects: \n           Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI) \n         \n\n         \n          Advancing language models for specialized scientific applications requires evaluations of scientific fluency, physical reasoning, and quantitative analyses. Using inertial confinement fusion (ICF) as a test case, we have developed an expert-curated benchmark of 250 questions spanning qualitative reasoning, numerical calculation, symbolic derivation, and equation formulation. Responses have been evaluated using deterministic numerical and symbolic checks together with language-model judges. We compared a variety of out-of-the-box closed-source and open-source large language models with Prospero, a model specialized through combined domain-adaptive pretraining and supervised fine-tuning for ICF. We also conducted initial retrieval-assisted evaluations. Prospero improved its aggregate score by 10.7 percentage points over its base model, with gains across all question types. Frontier models achieved the highest aggregate scores, but critical physics errors persisted, particularly in numerical calculations. We note that domain-specific performance can improve through specialization, but benchmark gains alone do not establish scientific reliability. Retrieval improved performance for a smaller model but yielded no statistically significant improvement for a frontier model. Two scientific stress tests further illustrate useful capabilities and the need for expert assessment of physical assumptions and model validity. Based on these findings, we recommend evaluating existing domain capabilities and retrieval benefits before committing to substantial additional specialization.\n         \n       \n     \n     \n       [24] \n       \n        arXiv:2610.38044\n       \n      \n        [ pdf ,  html ,  other ]\n     \n     \n       \n          Title: \n          PromptSense: Sentiment-Aware Mental Health Chatbot with Dynamic Prompting\n         \n          Sugi Lee ,  Jianlong Zhou ,  Jing sUN  \n\n\n\n          Subjects: \n           Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI) \n         \n\n         \n          Current Large Language Models (LLMs)-based mental health chatbots often lack sufficient empathy, contextual awareness, and safety mechanisms, potentially leading to ineffective or harmful interactions. This paper proposes a novel framework that integrates sentiment and depression analysis with a rule-based filtering mechanism prior to model inference. By dynamically constructing prompts based on emotional and risk-related insights, the framework aims to significantly improve the contextual relevance, empathy, and risk-aware of model-generated responses. We evaluated four model strategies and two prompt engineered variants across qualitative metrics such as empathy, helpfulness, clarity, relevance, safety, as well as toxicity assessments using the Perspective API. Experimental results indicate that the prompt engineering approaches outperforms DSFT and RLAIF by 1.8\\% and 1.1\\% respectively.\n         \n       \n     \n     \n       [25] \n       \n        arXiv:2610.38100\n       \n      \n        [ pdf ,  html ,  other ]\n     \n     \n       \n          Title: \n          Evaluating Causal Reasoning in LLMs: A Case Study in the Challenges of Procedural Generation\n         \n          Justin Clarke ,  Isha Karn ,  Patrick Murray ,  KJ Hill ,  David Jensen  \n\n\n\n          Subjects: \n           Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI) ; Machine Learning (cs.LG)\n         \n\n         \n          Large language models (LLMs) appear to represent and reason about a large range of phenomena. In particular, recent studies have examined whether LLMs can accurately reason about causation. One of the most commonly used benchmarks for evaluating formal causal reasoning in LLMs is CLadder, a framework for procedural generation of benchmark data sets that was released in 2023. Experiments with CLadder appear to indicate that LLMs can perform formal causal reasoning in a large percentage of generated examples. In this paper, we examine what can be reliably inferred when LLMs perform well on test sets produced by CLadder. We identify several examples of superficial and highly predictive patterns that the CLadder generator embeds in data sets it generates. We find that even simple models such as random forests perform well on CLadder-generated data sets after training on a sample of generated data. We also find that the performance of many commercial LLMs on CLadder test sets is extremely brittle and that removing large sections of the text that are critical for formal causal reasoning has almost no effect on performance. Given these results, the most likely hypothesis for the strong performance of state-of-the-art LLMs on CLadder is that they are exploiting superficial patterns learned from exposure to training data from the generator. Any inference that LLMs are exhibiting more complex causal reasoning must rule out this hypothesis.\n         \n       \n     \n     \n       [26] \n       \n        arXiv:2610.38101\n       \n      \n        [ pdf ,  html ,  other ]\n     \n     \n       \n          Title: \n          AI-Driven Digital Twin Framework for Warehouse Logistics Automation Optimization\n         \n          Md Mahinur Alam ,  Kanita Jerin Tanha ,  Md Tanjin Alam ,  Shadman Sakib ,  Min Gyu Kim  \n\n          Comments: \n          ICRA 2026 Workshop Advances and Challenges in AI-Driven Automation and Robotic System Integration with Digital Twins\n         \n\n\n          Subjects: \n           Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI) \n         \n\n         \n          In recent years, warehouse logistics optimization has increasingly focused on intelligent, data-driven systems to enhance operational efficiency. Conventional static product placement strategies are being progressively replaced by dynamic slotting approaches that can adapt to real-time variations in warehouse conditions. In this work, we present a novel framework that employs a reinforcement learning agent integrated within a FlexSim-based Digital Twin environment for real-time product placement optimization. The proposed agent continuously learns from streaming operational data, enabling it to reduce picking time while improving overall system throughput. The simulation environment models a four-zone warehouse layout with forklift-based material handling and leverages the Asset Administration Shell framework to ensure seamless interaction between the learning agent, physical asset, and the digital twin. Experimental evaluation demonstrates substantial performance gains, with the proposed approach achieving a 29.66\\% reduction in average picking time, a 35.42\\% increase in throughput, and an 18.54\\% decrease in travel distance.\n         \n       \n     \n     \n       [27] \n       \n        arXiv:2610.38117\n       \n      \n        [ pdf ,  html ,  other ]\n     \n     \n       \n          Title: \n          Too Hard, Too Easy, Just Right: Performance-Driven Challenge Adaptation in Virtual Reality Firefighter Training\n         \n          Xuan Wang ,  Jianan Jiang ,  Rongqian Chen ,  Tian Lan ,  Bin Li  \n\n\n\n          Subjects: \n           Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI) \n         \n\n         \n          Effective firefighter training requires practice under dynamic and stressful emergency conditions, yet real-world live-fire exercises are costly, risky, and difficult to scale. Virtual Reality (VR) offers a safe and controllable alternative, but many existing VR firefighter training systems rely on fixed scenarios or provide limited difficulty adaptation, making it difficult to maintain an appropriate level of challenge for skill development as trainees' performance changes over time. To address this limitation, we present ProACT, a performance-driven adaptive training system that regulates scenario difficulty through closed-loop interactions between trainee performance and simulated fire dynamics. Specifically, ProACT combines a global module that continuously adjusts scene-level fire spread dynamics in real time based on trainee performance with a local module that periodically updates object-level flammability across the environment. Our evaluation shows that ProACT improves both objective training performance and subjective user experience compared with existing adaptive and non-adaptive baselines. We further discuss how adaptive immersive environments can extend beyond firefighting to broader training domains.\n         \n       \n     \n     \n       [28] \n       \n        arXiv:2610.38120\n       \n      \n        [ pdf ,  html ,  other ]\n     \n     \n       \n          Title: \n          Post-Training Large Language Models with LLMON\n         \n          Basel Shbita ,  Michael Hind ,  Chad DeLuca ,  Nathan Fulton ,  Shubhi Asthana ,  Farhan Ahmed ,  Anisa Halimi ,  Yada Zhu ,  Mauro Martino ,  David Cox ,  Dan Gutfreund  \n\n\n\n          Subjects: \n           Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI) ; Machine Learning (cs.LG)\n         \n\n         \n          Large Language Models (LLMs) treat all context tokens uniformly, lacking explicit separation between executable instructions and passive data. This leads to prompt injection vulnerabilities, ambiguous instruction binding, and unreliable execution control. We present a post-training methodology using LLMON (LLM Object Notation), a framework that enables separating instructions from data and requires explicit identifier-based execution. We construct a large-scale structured training corpus of 4.6B tokens spanning general, RAG (Retrieval-Augmented Generation), and tool-calling data, augmented with distractor-infused variants in which competing instructions and irrelevant data segments are present in context but not referenced. Post-training two Granite-4.1 base models (3B and 8B) through supervised fine-tuning followed by reinforcement learning, we show that LLMON-structured supervision reduces attack success rates on indirect prompt-injection benchmarks from 53-100% to 0.02-5.6% while preserving general capabilities. We further introduce attention-based interpretability metrics that diagnose failure modes at individual transformer layers for structured inputs, revealing how our approach reshapes internal attention patterns. Our results demonstrate that explicit execution semantics can be taught through standard post-training without architectural modification.\n         \n       \n     \n     \n       [29] \n       \n        arXiv:2610.38144\n       \n      \n        [ pdf ,  html ,  other ]\n     \n     \n       \n          Title: \n          Decompose, Don't Expand: Capability-Independent AI Systems by Construction\n         \n          Tejaswi Gowda  \n\n\n\n          Subjects: \n           Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI) ; Human-Computer Interaction (cs.HC)\n         \n\n         \n          A recurring design choice in building AI systems is whether, when a task is too hard for a model, to expand the model (make it larger, better-prompted, fine-tuned), or to decompose the task (extract the parts that admit a deterministic solution to a host, leaving the model only the irreducibly fuzzy residue). We make three claims. First, that many tasks commonly delegated whole to a model are in fact deterministic tasks with a small generative core. Second, and our central empirical result, that decomposing such a task yields a measurable and desirable property we call parity by construction: on the extracted sub-tasks the model-size performance curve becomes flat, because no model performs them at all. We measure this across nine models spanning a 494M-parameter local checkpoint to a frontier Opus-class model, a thirty-fold parameter range on the local family alone: once the host resolves the context, operation selection scores identically for all nine models and selector resolution for eight of nine, while the one task that does not decompose, labelling, remains sloped across the same range. Because the task leaves the model, a stock checkpoint suffices: no task-specific training and no fine-tuning. Third, that this is a general design discipline, not a domain trick: we show the same move recurring across physics setup, motion retargeting, and object-model editing, and in a Web-CLI tool family (same author, so not an independent replication). We give a method for locating the cut, the construction that makes a cut exist (a deterministic surface and a closed grammar over it), and a way to make the residue safe (resolve the deterministic context; verify the generative output), position the work against neuro-symbolic AI, tool-augmented language models, and generate-and-verify approaches, and are explicit about what is measured (one domain) versus illustrative (the rest).\n         \n       \n     \n     \n       [30] \n       \n        arXiv:2610.38147\n       \n      \n        [ pdf ,  html ,  other ]\n     \n     \n       \n          Title: \n          SkillCT: A Multidimensional Benchmark and Execution-Free Baseline for Agent Skill Assessment\n         \n          Mengyu Sun ,  Zunlong Zhou ,  Ziyuan Yang ,  Yi Zhang  \n\n\n\n          Subjects: \n           Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI) \n         \n\n         \n          Agent skills package instructions, scripts, and resources for specialized tasks and are increasingly distributed through public Web repositories. However, existing skill benchmarks primarily focus on downstream task execution, insufficiently assessing specification quality, behavioral robustness, semantic alignment, and responsibility boundaries. Their reliance on task-specific tools, environments, and repeated executions limits scalability and pre-deployment assessment. To address these challenges, we introduce SkillCT, a benchmark that broadens skill assessment from downstream utility to a multidimensional characterization of skill quality. SkillCT evaluates skills across nine complementary dimensions spanning three aspects: Static Assessment, which captures intrinsic properties of the skill specification; Dynamic Assessment, which measures whether the induced behavior remains stable across backbone models and request variations; and Human-Assisted Assessment, which examines behavior-chain consistency and task granularity. These dimensions provide a broader view of skill quality than execution success. Applying SkillCT to a public skill corpus establishes a reference benchmark for analyzing quality weaknesses and relationships across dimensions. Building on this benchmark, we propose an execution-free quality evaluation baseline that predicts the multidimensional quality profiles of unseen skills by retrieving and leveraging similar reference skills, without executing the skills under evaluation. This reduces token consumption and evaluation time, while preserving consistency with the full assessment. Furthermore, SkillCT-guided optimization improves downstream task success and execution efficiency, demonstrating its value for skill refinement. These findings highlight the effectiveness of SkillCT in supporting scalable skill assessment, optimization, and repository maintenance.\n         \n       \n     \n     \n       [31] \n       \n        arXiv:2610.38152\n       \n      \n        [ pdf ,  other ]\n     \n     \n       \n          Title: \n          BridgeRouter: Gain-Aware Instance Routing over Cross-Modal Bridge Portfolios\n         \n          Ruoxi Piao ,  Alicia Lu ,  Yunzhen Zhang ,  Mustafa Mısır ,  Hasan Onur Keleş  \n\n\n\n          Subjects: \n           Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI) \n         \n\n         \n          Lightweight representation bridges make pretrained, task-adapted EEG foundation models executable on other biosignal modalities without retraining their backbones. However, selecting a single bridge pathway globally assigns every instance to the same transfer route and ignores complementary errors across routes. We therefore formulate multi-bridge transfer as an instance-level algorithm-selection problem and introduce BridgeRouter, a gain-aware router over a portfolio of frozen cross-modal pathways. A validation-selected Single Best Solver (SBS) serves as a protected default route. During training, task labels are used to compute the reduction in negative log-likelihood provided by each alternative route relative to the SBS, and a lightweight MLP predicts these route-specific gains from a target-modality representation extracted by a frozen encoder. At inference, BridgeRouter observes only the target modality, requires neither EEG, task labels, nor predictions from all candidate routes, and executes exactly one bridge pathway: the route with the largest predicted gain when that gain exceeds a validation-calibrated threshold, and the SBS otherwise. We evaluate seven EEG foundation-model bridge pathways for EMG-based freezing-of-gait detection on FOG and ECG-based sleep classification on ISRUC. Across five seeds, BridgeRouter achieves $81.38\\pm1.11\\%$ balanced accuracy on FOG, improving over the BIOT SBS by $11.49$ percentage points, and $62.92\\pm2.17\\%$ on ISRUC, improving over the CBraMod SBS by $3.83$ points. Per-instance bridge oracles reach $99.96\\%$ and $78.61\\%$, respectively, revealing substantial remaining portfolio headroom. These results show that anchor-relative gain prediction can convert complementary cross-modal bridge behavior into deployable instance-level routing improvements while retaining single-route inference.\n         \n       \n     \n     \n       [32] \n       \n        arXiv:2610.38175\n       \n      \n        [ pdf ,  html ,  other ]\n     \n     \n       \n          Title: \n          DecompGym: Benchmarking LLM Agents on Designing Decomposition Algorithms\n         \n          Sikai Cheng ,  Tinghan Ye ,  Arnaud Deza ,  Xiaonan Qi ,  Miao Li ,  Litong Liu ,  Changkun Guan  \n\n\n\n          Subjects: \n           Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI) ; Optimization and Control (math.OC)\n         \n\n         \n          Benders decomposition and column generation solve large optimization problems by passing cuts, columns, and dual values between a master problem and subproblems, and are hard to implement correctly. Large language models now automate parts of algorithm design, which points toward agents that build decomposition solvers for new problems. As a first step toward such agents, we study how well current coding agents design and implement these two algorithms, and how they fail. We introduce DecompGym, 81 instances from nine problem families (five Benders, four column generation), in which an agent writes a solver for a prescribed method. Each solver must meet three criteria: execution within a 60-second time limit; numerical feasibility together with a method audit, in which an LLM judge reads the solver's code; and an objective within 1% of the stored optimal objective. We evaluate 12 GPT, Claude, and Gemini models running in coding-agent tools. Over up to ten attempts, we compare independent regeneration with reflection, which first diagnoses the failure. On the first attempt, two models solve every instance and seven solve more than 90%. Most Benders failures (48 of 62) are missing or suboptimal solutions. The most common column-generation failure is a method-audit rejection. Output checks alone would accept 93 of the 108 attempts that fail the method audit. The most common rejection, an optimalif, occurs 96 times, 92 of them in one  this http URL  turns 66% of retries into successes, compared with 39% for regeneration, and it repairs method-audit failures, for which the judge names the defecective gaps. Agents for decomposition therefore need checks on how a solver reaches its answer and feedback that locates the error. The benchmark is available at  this https URL .\n         \n       \n     \n     \n       [33] \n       \n        arXiv:2610.38194\n       \n      \n        [ pdf ,  html ,  other ]\n     \n     \n       \n          Title: \n          Prototype-Guided Dynamic Domain Modulation and a Large-Scale Multi-SKU Benchmark for Shampoo Bottle Surface Defect Detection\n         \n          Yuehang Li ,  Xiao Wang ,  Qiang Chen ,  Yangyi Fan ,  Bo Jiang ,  Yaowei Wang ,  Jin Tang  \n\n\n\n          Subjects: \n           Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI) \n         \n\n         \n          Industrial shampoo-bottle surface defect detection must operate across many stock keeping units (SKUs), where differences in bottle geometry, material, label layout, reflectance, and imaging conditions can substantially alter the appearance of the same defect. Existing industrial defect datasets rarely provide both instance-level defect annotations and product-variant identities, making it difficult to study SKU-aware detection under realistic manufacturing conditions. We introduce Bottle-SKU, a large-scale multi-SKU benchmark for shampoo bottle surface defect detection. Bottle-SKU contains 13,119 images, 20,419 instance-level bounding boxes, seven defect categories, and 48 bottle SKUs, enabling evaluation of both shared defect recognition and SKU-specific appearance variation. To address the challenge of SKU-specific appearance variation, we propose Prototype-Guided Dynamic Domain Modulation (ProDMM). ProDMM constructs a visual prototype for each SKU from training images and predicts a soft SKU distribution for each input. The resulting prototype-conditioned representation dynamically generates low-rank Query/Value modulation for the visual backbone and residual conditioning for defect-text-to-visual attention, while preserving the pretrained Grounding DINO detection pathway. The model is trained jointly with detection, SKU-domain classification, and modulation-regularization objectives. Experiments on Bottle-SKU and the public GC10-DET-SKU metallic surface defect dataset demonstrate the effectiveness of prototype-guided domain conditioning for industrial surface defect detection.\n         \n       \n     \n     \n       [34] \n       \n        arXiv:2610.38201\n       \n      \n        [ pdf ,  html ,  other ]\n     \n     \n       \n          Title: \n          From Natural Language to Real Schematics: An Automated Workflow for Power Electronics Schematic Synthesis\n         \n          Bowen Su ,  Huan Chen ,  Jiahua Ying ,  Ran Ou ,  Weilong Chen ,  Li Xue ,  Kai Sun  \n\n\n\n          Subjects: \n           Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI) \n         \n\n         \n          Designing power electronics converters traditionally requires intensive human expertise across topology sizing, commercial component selection, circuit simulation verification, and schematic drafting. While Large Language Models (LLMs) show promise in electronic design automation (EDA), applying standalone LLMs directly to board-level power electronics schematic synthesis suffers from severe engineering hallucinations, including inaccurate analytical sizing, fabricated or mismatched component part numbers, violated voltage derating margins, and broken topological connectivity. In this paper, we propose a Bounded Dual-Loop Automated Schematic Synthesis Framework that translates natural language specifications into verified, editable KiCad schematics and manufacturing deliverables. Our workflow integrates six stages across three functional columns: (1) requirement parsing and knowledge-driven power stage synthesis covering non-isolated (Buck, Boost) and isolated/resonant (Flyback, DAB, half-bridge LLC) topologies; (2) contract- and evidence-coordinated component selection grounded in distributor APIs and OEM datasheets, coupled with closed-loop ngspice transient verification and stress feedback; and (3) modular control hardware integration and multi-format deliverable generation featuring native KiCad netlist round-trip verification. End-to-end case studies on a 24-36V to 12V,2A Buck converter (29 positions, 83 verified terminals) and an isolated 48V to 24V, 0.5A, Dual-Active Bridge (DAB) converter (87 positions, 341 verified terminals), alongside comparative evaluations against a Vanilla LLM baseline, demonstrate that our framework improves component verification coverage from 22.2 to 98.6, nominal value consistency from 0 to 97.7, and SPICE simulation pass rates from 0 to 100.\n         \n       \n     \n     \n       [35] \n       \n        arXiv:2610.38210\n       \n      \n        [ pdf ,  html ,  other ]\n     \n     \n       \n          Title: \n          GAAC: Lossless KV Compression for Bandwidth-Efficient LLM Inference on Personal Devices\n         \n          Yipin Guo ,  Arun M George ,  Siddharth Joshi  \n\n\n\n          Subjects: \n           Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI) \n         \n\n         \n          Weight-only, lossy quantization substantially reduces the storage and memory requirements for serving large language models (LLMs), but the growing key-value (KV) cache remains a bottleneck for long-context inference on personal devices. We present Group-Adaptive Additive Codec (GAAC), a lossless KV compression and serving system that exploits exponent redundancy to reduce storage and accelerate attention without introducing KV approximation. GAAC encodes BF16 exponents using calibrated per-channel base-plus-offset codes over contiguous ranges. GAAC adapts code widths across small groups while preserving KV values bit-for-bit. A fused attention kernel reconstructs KV tiles on the fly, avoiding full-cache decompression. Block-wise compression and fixed-capacity slots support incremental cache updates and paged serving. Across five tested models, GAAC achieves 1.405-1.407$\\times$ lossless BF16 KV compression. We also study compression performance on low-precision tensors FP8 E4M3 caches ( 1.09-1.14$\\times$) and E5M2 caches (1.25-1.30$\\times$). On NVIDIA DGX Spark, GAAC accelerates decode attention at a 64K-token context by 1.19-1.27$\\times$ and end-to-end W4A16 decode steps by 1.04-1.15$\\times$. In serving, it reduces resident KV memory by approximately 26% and increases aggregate throughput by 13-29% under comparable KV-memory budgets. On Apple M4, GAAC achieves 1.02-1.25$\\times$ attention speedups, demonstrating portability beyond NVIDIA GPUs.\n         \n       \n     \n     \n       [36] \n       \n        arXiv:2610.38215\n       \n      \n        [ pdf ,  html ,  other ]\n     \n     \n       \n          Title: \n          Disparity Laundering: Consensus Without Fairness in Clinical Multi-Agent Systems\n         \n          Xiaoyang Wang ,  Tianrui Wang ,  Christopher C. Yang  \n\n          Comments: \n          21 pages, 8 figures. Code:  this https URL  . Committed to EACL 2027\n         \n\n\n          Subjects: \n           Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI) ; Multiagent Systems (cs.MA)\n         \n\n         \n          Clinical large language models are increasingly fielded as multi-agent teams, on the assumption that deliberation averages out individual error. It does not average out bias. Across a full factorial of five model families and four aggregation topologies on 120 real MIMIC-IV emergency cases under counterfactual demographic swaps, no configuration neutralizes the disparity: all 20 cells commit the direction of their agents' independent answers, giving Black patients more acute triage. Every model has a topology that amplifies its baseline gap, but which one varies by model, so no design is reliably safe or reliably amplifying: the disparity is relocated, never removed. Late-stage fixes fail: fairness instructions and demographic masking leave the gap untouched, and per-agent self-debiasing widens it, because the bias is already encoded in the agents' ordinary clinical numbers. We call this disparity laundering; the audits now applied to clinical AI, per model, per output, per version, can certify the parts but never the committed decision. Interventions close the gap exactly when they make counterfactual versions visible inside the system and pool them, through deliberation, into the committed decision. A diverse-perspective ensemble does both while keeping the team, with no retraining or architectural change: the disparity falls by 67 to 94% where all five perspectives pool at every round, and the one topology that never pools them is the one it cannot reliably repair. Medical multi-agent systems do not filter demographic bias; they launder it into plausible decisions no single-version audit can catch, and the only defense is to make the bias visible.\n         \n       \n     \n     \n       [37] \n       \n        arXiv:2610.38220\n       \n      \n        [ pdf ,  html ,  other ]\n     \n     \n       \n          Title: \n          Rubricon: Automated Rubric Generation and Refinement for LLM Evaluation and Training\n         \n          Ulyana Tkachenko ,  Hui Wen Goh ,  Guram Gogia ,  Jonas Mueller  \n\n\n\n          Subjects: \n           Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI) \n         \n\n         \n          Rubrics enable reward assignment on tasks without verifiable answers, but are expensive to manually produce from scratch. We present Rubricon, a pipeline that generates discriminative rubrics without requiring human preference labels, rubric annotation, or generator-model training. Using any LLM, Rubricon samples multiple rubrics, selects the most discriminative candidate, adds criteria to capture overlooked requirements or response flaws, and prunes criteria that are misaligned or shallow. Alongside LLM self-critique and test-time scaling, Rubricon uses observed criterion pass rates on example model responses to guide refinement. Across five professional, consumer, clinical, and general-use LLM benchmarks, Rubricon produces rubrics whose scores of frontier LLM responses exhibit 9% greater alignment with labor-intensive expert-authored rubrics, compared to the best alternative synthetic rubric generation method among RRD, QSRG, OpenRubrics, and agentic generation via harnesses like Codex. On HealthBench, post-training a Qwen3.5-9B model with Rubricon rubrics yields 4.5% better performance than training with rubrics from the strongest alternative synthetic rubric generator, RRD.\n         \n       \n     \n     \n       [38] \n       \n        arXiv:2610.38225\n       \n      \n        [ pdf ,  html ,  other ]\n     \n     \n       \n          Title: \n          FACTS-MAS: A Multi-Agent Architecture for Adaptive Forecasting from Heterogeneous Evidence\n         \n          Madumita Karthikeyan ,  Umang Rajnikant Bid ,  Rong Pan  \n\n          Comments: \n          29\n         \n\n\n          Subjects: \n           Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI) ; Applications (stat.AP)\n         \n\n         \n          Real-world forecasting often requires integrating heterogeneous evidence whose predictive value varies across horizons, operating regimes, and domains. We introduce FACTS-MAS, a domain-agnostic multi-agent forecasting framework that decomposes evidence into specialized channels for temporal dynamics, exogenous context, seasonality, structural information, and discrete events. Each agent generates an independently testable forecast through a common interface, while leakage-safe historical validation measures its predictive skill relative to a naive reference. These skill estimates are used to weight and combine agent forecasts, so that uninformative evidence is either removed or downweighted rather than forced to participate. A calibration procedure selects domain-specific constant means based on historical validation folds. We evaluate FACTS-MAS on weekly for-sale housing inventory from Zillow and weekly unemployment insurance claims from FRED, for 15 U.S. metropolitan areas at 4-, 8-, and 13-week forecasting horizons. Our method outperforms both a naive last-value forecast method and the TimesFM foundation model. However, a factor-adjusted exponential smoothing model performs as well as the fused forecast. The evidence therefore supports FACTS-MAS as a systematic protocol for testing, calibrating, and recomposing evidence channels before assembling them into the strongest single model for a specific application domain. Code, data, and results are available at  this https URL .\n         \n       \n     \n     \n       [39] \n       \n        arXiv:2610.38245\n       \n      \n        [ pdf ,  html ,  other ]\n     \n     \n       \n          Title: \n          Verification-Guided Retrosynthesis: Reaction Feedback and Evidence Timing\n         \n          Hongxuan Shi ,  Hankz Hankui Zhuo  \n\n          Comments: \n          12 pages, including appendices\n         \n\n\n          Subjects: \n           Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI) \n         \n\n         \n          Retrosynthesis planning seeks synthesis routes from purchasable starting materials to a target molecule. Search over predicted reactions can connect a target to available materials, yet an infeasible reaction can invalidate the resulting route. Evaluating route quality only after search has finished cannot correct errors made during search. We introduce Verification-Guided Reflow (VGR), incorporating reaction verification as a decision action in AND/OR search. Verification results are represented as binary verdicts; queries are scheduled from the search structure, and acquired verdicts exclude reactions or adjust search priorities. We define verified solvability as the fraction of targets for which a complete route accepted stepwise by the verifier is found. On Retro190, feeding verification results into search increases verified solvability from 51.1% to 62.6% relative to route evaluation without feedback. Further controlled experiments show that earlier verification and immediate prioritization of accepted reactions each improve route discovery, yet combining them performs worse than either change alone. We therefore propose early pruning and delayed guidance: rejected reactions are pruned immediately, while accepted reactions gain search priority when processed as part of a complete candidate route. In a separate comparison with the verification scheduling rule fixed, this strategy increases verified solvability from 61.1% with immediate guidance to 71.1%, using fewer proposal calls and unique verifier queries.\n         \n       \n     \n     \n       [40] \n       \n        arXiv:2610.38304\n       \n      \n        [ pdf ,  other ]\n     \n     \n       \n          Title: \n          Inner-Learning: From Hand-Coded Beliefs to Declarative Knowledge in Trick-Taking Games\n         \n          Stefan Edelkamp  \n\n\n\n          Subjects: \n           Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI) \n         \n\n         \n          We present a declarative approach to belief-state modeling in trick-taking card games. Starting from a state-of-the-art Skat player, we automatically extract its hand-coded inference rules into a domain-specific language built on the primitive $\\neg\\mathsf{holds}(P,C)$, denoting that player $P$ cannot hold any card in set $C$. Round-trip code generation verifies that the extracted rules reproduce the original engine's intended behavior, up to three audited bugs in the original that the declarative engine corrects. We then grow the rule base through symmetric derivation and annotation of an expert strategy document---unifying code, symmetry, and expertise in one representation. We extend the knowledge representation with \\emph{cardinality constraints}: at-most-$k$ and at-least-$k$ bounds on the cards a player holds from a named group, expressed in the same declarative language and enforced at world-generation time. Confidence parameters connect symbolic rules to probabilistic belief updates. Self-play evaluation shows the declarative engine measurably improving winning percentage and score over the original hand-coded engine, with the extracted rules, the human-annotated rules, and the cardinality constraints each contributing to the effect.\n         \n       \n     \n     \n       [41] \n       \n        arXiv:2610.38313\n       \n      \n        [ pdf ,  html ,  other ]\n     \n     \n       \n          Title: \n          Game-Theoretic Foundations for Undiscounted Bisimulation Metrics\n         \n          Matthew Zurek ,  Yudong Chen ,  Josiah P. Hanna  \n\n\n\n          Subjects: \n           Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI) ; Computer Science and Game Theory (cs.GT); Machine Learning (cs.LG); Optimization and Control (math.OC)\n         \n\n         \n          Bisimulation metrics offer a principled notion of task-relevant state similarity in reinforcement learning, but existing constructions and methods are largely confined to discounted MDPs, where contraction arguments ensure well-defined fixed points and downstream guarantees. We develop a unified foundation for bisimulation metrics in undiscounted settings, that is total- and average-reward problems, by replacing the standard operator-centric view with a game-centric one: we interpret prior metric operators as Bellman-Shapley operators of zero-sum turn-based games and define metrics as the corresponding game value functions, yielding unique definitions even when $\\gamma=1$. This game-theoretic foundation facilitates transfer of dynamic-programming theory and algorithms. We also use this game-centric perspective to characterize how metric finiteness depends on structural properties of the underlying MDP and motivate certain directed metrics that remain finite in weakly communicating MDPs. We further introduce an abstract framework for modifying the games' action spaces, allowing general transition coupling sets, that recovers many existing metrics and guides new constructions. Finally, we prove that these metrics bound total-reward and, when the optimal gain is state-independent, optimal relative value differences. We also provide error and suboptimality guarantees for aggregation-based planning in total- and average-reward settings.\n         \n       \n     \n     \n       [42] \n       \n        arXiv:2610.38314\n       \n      \n        [ pdf ,  html ,  other ]\n     \n     \n       \n          Title: \n          CHORD: Cross-Model Hallucination Detection via Relational Graph Discrimination\n         \n          Yongxin Deng  (1),  Zhen Fang  (1),  Guansong Pang  (2),  Sharon Li  (3),  Ling Chen  (1) ((1) University of Technology Sydney, (2) Singapore Management University, (3) University of Wisconsin-Madison) \n\n          Comments: \n          Accepted to NeurIPS 2026 as a Spotlight paper. 64 pages, 14 figures, 7 tables\n         \n\n\n          Subjects: \n           Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI) \n         \n\n         \n          Hallucination detection is critical for deploying large language models (LLMs) in real-world applications. Due to strong empirical performance, internal representation-based methods have emerged as the prevailing direction for detecting hallucinations, yet they remain largely model-specific and often fail to generalize to unseen LLMs. In this paper, we study an important yet underexplored problem, termed cross-model hallucination detection (CMHD), which aims to train hallucination detectors on source LLMs while ensuring performance on unseen target LLMs. The core challenge of CMHD lies in cross-model representation heterogeneity: hidden states from different LLMs exhibit severe semantic inconsistency, which limits the transferability of classical representation-based detectors. Through analysis and empirical validation, we show that relational structures over layers, tokens, and features capture transferable detection signals despite semantic inconsistency. Based on this observation, we propose cross-model hallucination detection via heterogeneity-oriented relational discrimination (CHORD), a relational graph discrimination framework that represents hidden states as joint relational graphs over layers, tokens, and features. It feeds these graphs into relation-aware attention to obtain structure-aware features, and uses meta-learning to shape these features toward transferable detection signals. Extensive experiments show that CHORD outperforms representative detection methods in cross-model generalization. Code is available at  this https URL .\n         \n       \n     \n     \n       [43] \n       \n        arXiv:2610.38326\n       \n      \n        [ pdf ,  html ,  other ]\n     \n     \n       \n          Title: \n          Can LLMs Faithfully Solve Ontology Engineering Tasks? A Mechanistic Interpretability Study\n         \n          Bohui Zhang ,  Albert Meroño-Peñuela ,  Elena Simperl  \n\n\n\n          Subjects: \n           Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI) \n         \n\n         \n          Ontological knowledge underpins large language model (LLM) performance on knowledge-intensive tasks. However, existing evaluations of LLMs' ontological understanding largely rely on behavioral correctness, leaving the underlying mechanisms unexplained. Existing mechanistic interpretability work has largely focused on instance-level factual knowledge, leaving ontological knowledge under-explored. To bridge this gap, we introduce MIOE, a causal-abstraction benchmark that evaluates interchange interventions for two ontology engineering tasks: entity typing and subsumption prediction. MIOE tests whether learned internal intervention targets can change an ontology-level target answer while preserving class-specific auxiliary information. Across Qwen3-8B and Qwen3-4B, we evaluate SAE-, DBM-, and DAS-based methods, including multi-task and multi-layer variants. Distributed subspace methods give the strongest results and generalize across prompt settings, but the induced changes remain only partially separated from auxiliary class-specific information. Multi-task objectives and contiguous layer-window interventions improve this balance, while class-level analyses reveal substantial variation across ontology classes. These results suggest that ontological predictions are mechanistically accessible in LLMs, although current methods do not yet isolate a cleanly disentangled causal mechanism for ontological knowledge.\n         \n       \n     \n     \n       [44] \n       \n        arXiv:2610.38342\n       \n      \n        [ pdf ,  html ,  other ]\n     \n     \n       \n          Title: \n          Investor's Last Exam: Can AI Agents Do Equity Investment Without Seeing the Future?\n         \n          Huangfei Jiang ,  Anning Hu ,  Kai Sheng ,  Yuchen Cai ,  Rui Ge ,  Xiangyu Kong ,  Jianghao Lin ,  Shuzi Chen ,  Jiyi Weng ,  Junchi Yan  \n\n\n\n          Subjects: \n           Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI) \n         \n\n         \n          Financial agents must form forecasts and investment judgments from decision-time evidence, with predictions evaluated against subsequent outcomes. Existing benchmarks assess financial knowledge, research workflows, or forecasting, but evaluating the full research process requires a strictly frozen information set and professional evaluation criteria. We introduce Investor's Last Exam (ILE), an equity-research benchmark led by experienced buy-side investors and fund managers. Its three components are a versioned financial database spanning 3.43 million document versions and nine source categories, supplemented by structured market data, with validated timestamps governing retrieval and computation; a task taxonomy organized around professional analyst deliverables; and an expert-led framework linking scoring criteria to sources, calculations, and assumptions through financial dependency graphs. Evaluation spans 86 assignments and jointly assesses research quality, forecast accuracy, and simulated investment performance. GPT-6 Astra and Claude Opus 5.5 lead overall at 68.1 and 66.9 out of 100, yet even Astra scores only 52.5 on Forecast & Model. Our results show that even state-of-the-art models fail to translate overall research capability into predictive performance. ILE shifts the evaluation unit from isolated capabilities to the evidence--assumption--judgment chain, allowing researchers to locate where analytical advantages fail to translate into forecasts and investment judgments, and providing a traceable, diagnostic foundation for training and evaluating financial agents.\n         \n       \n     \n     \n       [45] \n       \n        arXiv:2610.38356\n       \n      \n        [ pdf ,  html ,  other ]\n     \n     \n       \n          Title: \n          Encode Once, Answer Many: Internalizing Context via Parametric Flow\n         \n          Xinhao Zhong ,  Mengxuan Wu ,  Chonghua Liao ,  Linxi Xie ,  Ruicheng Feng ,  Yanpei Guo ,  Hao Tan ,  Jiaxin Hong ,  Jason Li ,  Panpan Zhang ,  Ziheng Qin ,  Qinglin Lu ,  Yuzhang Shang ,  Tongliang Liu ,  Damian Borth ,  Haggai Maron ,  Yongxiang Hua ,  Zhixiang Wei ,  Jian Hu ,  Yunfei Wu ,  Xing Sun ,  Xin Li ,  Haoyu Cao ,  Zhangyang Wang ,  Kai Wang  \n\n\n\n          Subjects: \n           Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI) \n         \n\n         \n          Large language models are typically adapted to a task either by placing knowledge in the input context, which keeps it external and re-encodes it on every call, or by fine-tuning, which internalizes it but demands a costly round of optimization per task. We aim to internalize the knowledge carried by a context directly into model parameters, without per-task optimization and without keeping the context in the input at inference time. To this end, we propose CLIK, a context-conditioned parameter generator that maps a given context to LoRA parameters for internalizing knowledge in a single forward pass through a Transformer encoder with cross-attention, requiring no test-time gradient updates. Once the generated LoRA is inserted into a frozen base model, the model answers downstream queries from the query alone, having internalized the context into its weights rather than re-reading it. Because the adapter depends only on the context, it is generated once and reused for every query on the same document, so the cost of reading that document is amortized across queries. On document question answering, CLIK substantially improves over the no-context base model and outperforms existing hypernetwork-based generators, while remaining behind a base model that reads the context directly, and it carries over to multi-turn dialogue without any multi-turn-specific training. Analysis further shows that the context-dependent part of the generated adapter concentrates in a compact and specific substructure.\n         \n       \n     \n     \n       [46] \n       \n        arXiv:2610.38382\n       \n      \n        [ pdf ,  html ,  other ]\n     \n     \n       \n          Title: \n          Compute Has Shape: Self-Morphing the Internal Computation of Frozen LLMs\n         \n          Junxi Wu ,  Yiwen Guo ,  Shu-Tao Xia  \n\n\n\n          Subjects: \n           Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI) \n         \n\n         \n          Test-time scaling improves large language models through additional computation, but how to adapt the topology of internal computation to individual inputs remains underexplored. Existing approaches largely fix recurrent or parallel architectures during training, or reconfigure frozen models along a single structural axis. We introduce SelfMorph, which makes computational shape an input-dependent inference-time decision. It unifies the original forward path (BASE), serial reuse of a layer span (REPEAT), and local branching and merging of perturbed hidden states (BRANCH) in a unified space parameterized by type, location, and intensity. Since they offer complementary benefits rather than uniform dominance, we use a lightweight selector that reads the backbone's depth-wise hidden state trajectory on the prompt to choose a morphology before response generation. We train the selector by distilling utility-based distribution from offline morphing that jointly account for task performance and computational cost. Evaluated on three LLMs spanning 4B-27B, SelfMorph achieves a 8.06% mean relative improvement on five in-distribution benchmarks and 4.45% on four unseen benchmarks. It achieves the strongest performance in 24 of 27 settings. These findings identify input-adaptive computational topology as a complementary axis of test-time scaling beyond generating more tokens. Our code is available at  this https URL .\n         \n       \n     \n     \n       [47] \n       \n        arXiv:2610.38391\n       \n      \n        [ pdf ,  html ,  other ]\n     \n     \n       \n          Title: \n          SCOPE: Selective Critical-Step On-Policy Self-Distillation for Agentic Reinforcement Learning\n         \n          Qirui Liu ,  Wei Cao ,  Yu Mi ,  Yichen Sun ,  Xuekun Zhang ,  Lei Liu ,  Yue Shen ,  Zhixuan Chu  \n\n          Comments: \n          18 pages, 8 figures, 5 tables\n         \n\n\n          Subjects: \n           Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI) \n         \n\n         \n          Outcome-based reinforcement learning provides coarse supervision for long-horizon LLM agents: failed trajectories may contain correct steps, while successful trajectories can contain errors. Trajectory-level skills can also shift teacher preferences at unrelated steps, introducing off-target distillation signals. We introduce SCOPE, a framework for Selective Critical-Step On-Policy Self-Distillation. After a supervised warm-up, the policy analyzes its own trajectories, generates step-specific hindsight skills, and distills their guidance only at selected error steps. A bounded signed teacher-student coefficient stabilizes auxiliary updates while preserving positive reinforcement and negative correction. A success-relative pairwise length reward further encourages efficient task completion. Experiments on ALFWorld and WebShop demonstrate consistent improvements across multiple model backbones. With Qwen2.5-3B, SCOPE achieves 97.7% success on ALFWorld and 85.2% on WebShop, outperforming SEED by 5.9 and 6.3 percentage points, respectively. On unseen ALFWorld tasks, SCOPE reaches 89.6% success, improving over GRPO by 23.2 percentage points with 46.7% fewer interactions. Scaling to Qwen3-32B, SCOPE achieves 99.2% success on seen ALFWorld tasks and 94.0% on unseen tasks.\n         \n       \n     \n     \n       [48] \n       \n        arXiv:2610.38392\n       \n      \n        [ pdf ,  html ,  other ]\n     \n     \n       \n          Title: \n          Masked Erosion: How Acceptance Gates Lose Redundant Safeguards in Self-Improving Agents\n         \n          Hau-Sing So ,  Daixian Liu ,  Weicai Long  \n\n          Comments: \n          25 pages (8 pages main text), 10 figures, 35 tables\n         \n\n\n          Subjects: \n           Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI) \n         \n\n         \n          Self-improving LLM agents edit their own harness, the prompts and rules around a fixed model, and keep an edit only if an acceptance gate scores it higher on a batch of tasks. Such a gate sees what the harness does under the current model, not what it would still prevent if one safeguard failed. When safeguards overlap, deleting one is masked: the others still block the harm, so the gate decides on task success alone and redundancy erodes unseen. We measure harm for every subset of four written safeguards in two agent environments and run 411 improvement loops under ten gate rules, mostly as controlled deletion experiments, plus three runs of the GEPA optimizer. A deletion behind another working safeguard adds almost no observable harm. A paired significance test blocks deletions that pass by chance but not deletions that genuinely raise task success, and a larger evaluation batch lets it delete cheaper safeguards: at the same cost, 20-task batches kept every harness protected while 40-task batches lost all six. Large harm audits prevent exposure but not the loss of depth, which turns into harm when the harness is reused with a weaker model. GEPA kept every safety rule verbatim, yet two of its three final harnesses misused credentials under a weaker model. A statistical model frozen before the runs predicts where each gate's protection breaks as safeguard cost and batch size change, and freezing safety text or auditing one-layer ablations preserves depth.\n         \n       \n     \n     \n       [49] \n       \n        arXiv:2610.38398\n       \n      \n        [ pdf ,  html ,  other ]\n     \n     \n       \n          Title: \n          Agent Transfer Protocol: Server-Mediated Agent-to-Agent Communication Architecture for the Agentic Web\n         \n          Yuqi Qiu ,  Zuyao Xu ,  Lu Sun ,  Xiang Li ,  Fasheng Miao ,  Fubin Wu ,  Haixin Duan ,  Zheli Liu  \n\n          Comments: \n          12 pages, 6 figures, 5 tables\n         \n\n\n          Subjects: \n           Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI) ; Multiagent Systems (cs.MA)\n         \n\n         \n          The Agentic Web needs to support continuous collaboration among agents from different organizations. These collaborative relationships may span multiple tasks, network connections and execution instances. Such interactions require stable communication entries, message reception when instances are offline, and source verification after delayed reading. How to organize these responsibilities so that cross domain communication does not depend on specific execution instances being continuously online is a key issue for building long term agent interaction infrastructure. We propose ATP (Agent Transfer Protocol), a cross organization agent communication protocol adopting a domain mediated architecture. In this architecture, administrative domains maintain stable addresses and receiving entries for logical agents. The domain servers of both parties continuously undertake cross domain transmission. The receiving domain verifies and saves messages for execution instances to read later. The sending domain signs the complete message envelope. This allows the receiving instance to independently reverify the domain source and content integrity after the original connection ends, provided the original verification public key is still trusted and obtainable. We implemented an ATP prototype and evaluated it through a controlled cross organization LLM agent workflow, mechanism checks and latency measurements. The workflow completed subsequent business processing after instances went offline or were replaced. The independent verifier successfully verified the domain source and content integrity of saved messages. Latency measurements further quantified the communication and processing overhead introduced by the continuous mediation of domain servers.\n         \n       \n     \n     \n       [50] \n       \n        arXiv:2610.38411\n       \n      \n        [ pdf ,  other ]\n     \n     \n       \n          Title: \n          Governance-Aware Federated Learning for Multi-Agent AI Systems: A Policy-Driven Framework for Trustworthy Collaborative AI\n         \n          Kiranmayee Janardhan ,  Karthick S ,  Madhusmita Panda ,  Bharathi Vokkaliga Ganesh ,  Syed Ahmed  \n\n          Comments: \n          16 pages, 2 Figures and 3 Tables presented at NGEN 2026 Conference\n         \n\n\n          Subjects: \n           Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI) \n         \n\n         \n          Federated Learning (FL) enables collaborative model training without exchanging raw data, but conventional FL provides limited runtime governance, evidence verification, fairness assessment, explainability monitoring, and auditability. This paper proposes Governance-Aware Federated Learning (GAFL), which combines independent governance-evidence verification, temporal trust, fairness and explanation assessment, anomaly detection, policy enforcement, normalized aggregation, and tamper-evident audit logging. Client-reported governance values are authenticated, bound to their submitted model updates, and independently recomputed before aggregation. A cross-silo prototype with 10 participants was evaluated on the Adult Income dataset under honest, benign non-IID, biased-data, malicious-update, non-compliant, falsified-evidence, and mixed-threat scenarios. Four methods were compared across five random seeds: FedAvg, FedAvg with anomaly filtering, GAFL without independent verification, and complete GAFL. Complete GAFL intervened on all affected participants at the final round across the evaluated risk scenarios, including identifying 100% of falsified-evidence participants, whereas GAFL without verification identified none of the falsified-evidence participants. Final macro-F1 ranged from 0.7520 to 0.7733 across GAFL scenarios. Complete GAFL required 173.67 plus or minus 26.94 ms of governance processing per round, compared with 3.82 plus or minus 0.79 ms for FedAvg. A strict demographic-parity threshold also caused frequent interventions for honest participants, demonstrating a significant fairness-policy and utility trade-off.\n         \n       \n     \n     \n       [51] \n       \n        arXiv:2610.38439\n       \n      \n        [ pdf ,  html ,  other ]\n     \n     \n       \n          Title: \n          Predict the Reward, Not the World: World Reward Models for ML Agents\n         \n          Yuxiao Lee ,  Zherui Yang ,  Fan Liu ,  Hao Liu  \n\n\n\n          Subjects: \n           Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI) ; Computation and Language (cs.CL)\n         \n\n         \n          Search-based machine-learning (ML) agents improve a solution by writing candidate programs, executing them, and keeping the best. Execution gives the search reliable feedback, but a single run can take hours, and most runs end in programs the agent discards. Recent agents try to save these runs by letting a large language model act as a world model that predicts how each program would perform. However, a wrong prediction feeds the search false information. We argue that an agent does not need to predict the outcome of execution, only the decision that execution would support. We propose World Reward Models (WRMs), which learn from real execution results to choose directly which candidate will perform better, without predicting any intermediate outcome. We prove that a selector which predicts execution from the same inputs cannot outperform the best direct selector, that predicted outcomes add an error which can make a likelihood-trained world model prefer the worse program on every instance, and that cross-entropy on executed winners drives a WRM toward the optimum. Our WRM, ForeCritic, adapts a 9B typed decision model to 5k executed program pairs. On 12,438 held-out pairs from 26 ML tasks, it picks the better program 79.4% of the time, while general-purpose LLM judges reach 53--62%, and its probabilities are calibrated. Inside AIDE, ML-Master, and ForeAgent, it cuts mean task time by 4.4--7.5 times while keeping their mean scores within 0.01 or raising them.\n         \n       \n     \n     \n       [52] \n       \n        arXiv:2610.38445\n       \n      \n        [ pdf ,  html ,  other ]\n     \n     \n       \n          Title: \n          DES-JEPA: Learning a Shared Vocabulary for High-Dimensional Discrete Event Sequences\n         \n          Hugo Math ,  Maximilian Forstenhäusler ,  Christos Anagnostopoulos  \n\n          Comments: \n          5 pages\n         \n\n          Journal-ref: \n          NeurIPS 2026 Workshop: Foundation Models for Temporal Systems: From Forecasting to World Modeling\n         \n\n          Subjects: \n           Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI) \n         \n\n         \n          Temporal foundation models such as Chronos and TimesFM generalize across domains through a shared numerical observation space. In contrast, discrete event sequences (DESs), including operational event logs or patient clinical-event trajectories, have domain-specific event vocabularies whose labels lack a consistent correspondence across domains. Beyond this lack of consistency, the number of distinct events in real-world settings is high enough to challenge both classical temporal point process modeling and current in-context foundation inference models such as FIM-PP. Critically, current foundation models for DESs are limited to a small, fixed number of event types, restricting their applicability to realistic, high-dimensional event streams. To address this, we propose two complementary modules: (i) DES-JEPA, a joint-embedding predictive architecture that learns a shared discrete vocabulary from synthetic stochastic processes with unaligned event vocabularies; and (ii) DES-PFN, a discrete event sequence prior-fitted network that reuses the learned vocabulary for in-context next-event prediction. Without task-specific fine-tuning, this framework achieves competitive zero-shot performance on eight unseen real-world datasets and scales up to 700 distinct event types.\n         \n       \n     \n     \n       [53] \n       \n        arXiv:2610.38496\n       \n      \n        [ pdf ,  other ]\n     \n     \n       \n          Title: \n          Accelerating Nonlinear MPC by Behavior Cloning with Separated Timescales: Application to Nuclear Reactor Load-Following\n         \n          Perceval Beja-Battais  (CB),  Alain Grosset{ê}te ,  Nicolas Vayatis  (CB) \n\n\n\n          Subjects: \n           Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI) ; Machine Learning (cs.LG); Optimization and Control (math.OC)\n         \n\n         \n          Behavior Cloning (BC) of expert controllers for multiscale dynamical systems is challenging: state variables evolving at widely separated timescales induce highly nonlinear couplings that a shared latent space may fail to adequately represent, degrading closed-loop generalization. We propose Structured Representation Learning for Behavior Cloning (SRLBC), an architecture designed to leverage the physical knowledge of multiscale systems in which slow state variables, fast (stiff) state variables, and predictable exogenous inputs evolve at different timescales. On 500 long-horizon roll-outs of a pressurized water reactor (PWR) load-following benchmark -a high-dimensional, stiff industrial system with challenging safety constraints -and across 20 training seeds, separated embeddings systematically outperform shared embedding baselines on cumulative cost, imitation accuracy and constraint satisfaction. The resulting standalone policies approach NMPC closed-loop performance at a computational cost 15 times lower on average. Since standalone policies still leave a few percent of trajectories infeasible, we embed the learned policies in a safety-augmented approximate NMPC framework, in which the optimizer is only called when the predicted input sequence is not certified as safe. This yields controllers still 6.8 times faster than the NMPC controller on average, with a feasibility (i.e., constraint satisfaction) of at least 99.8% for all architectures, SRLBC retaining the lowest closed-loop cost. Under distribution shift, standalone BC policies degrade sharply: SRLBC degrades the least, and once safety-augmented, it retains a feasibility above 98% with the lowest closed-loop cost. Beyond the quantitative results, Jacobian-based sensitivity analysis shows that the resulting controller relies on a sparse and less redundant set of latent features, each attributable to a single group of physical variables, and aligned with the physical timescale decomposition of the system.\n         \n       \n     \n     \n       [54] \n       \n        arXiv:2610.38526\n       \n      \n        [ pdf ,  html ,  other ]\n     \n     \n       \n          Title: \n          Value Probes: Learned Value Functions for Beam-Search Red Teaming\n         \n          Jonathan F. Jao ,  Andrew Kyle Lampinen  \n\n\n\n          Subjects: \n           Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI) ; Machine Learning (cs.LG)\n         \n\n         \n          Automated red-team auditing of language models is an important way to evaluate safety-relevant behaviors. These audits implicitly search a tree of possible conversation pathways: at each turn an auditor model decides which conversation to continue, and an LLM judge scores the dialogue. We emphasize that this auditing procedure is a Markov decision process, where states are the conversation history, actions are candidate responses, and rewards are judge scores for the target behavior. By adopting this perspective, we introduce two major improvements to auditing. First, we propose adopting beam search for the audit rather than relying on the auditor alone. We then interpret the problem of finding states that are likely to lead to problematic behavior as value estimation. Surprisingly, we show that this value function can be estimated by linear probing from the target model's activations to predict discounted future rewards - a method we term \"Value Probes.\" By using Value Probes as a heuristic in our beam-search audit, we are able to substantially improve auditing success. Across 18 target behaviors and 3 scales of models, our approach raises peak audit scores by roughly 50% compared to (token-matched) standard auditing approaches. Furthermore, we show that Value Probes can be fit using off-policy data from smaller models. We also ablate individual components, and show that our method offers a comparably-performant and more efficient alternative to more expensive language model follow-ups. We hope these methods will improve language model auditing and lead to safer models.\n         \n       \n     \n     \n       [55] \n       \n        arXiv:2610.38532\n       \n      \n        [ pdf ,  html ,  other ]\n     \n     \n       \n          Title: \n          From Leniency to Suspicion: How an Open Post-Training Recipe Changes Whom LLM Fact-Checkers Believe\n         \n          Nouar AlDahoul ,  Yasir Zaki  \n\n          Comments: \n          10 pages, 4 figures, 6 tables. Preprint, under review\n         \n\n\n          Subjects: \n           Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI) ; Computation and Language (cs.CL); Computers and Society (cs.CY)\n         \n\n         \n          LLMs increasingly fact-check online content with the poster's identity in view. However, it remains unclear which post-training changes how much a model's truth judgement depends on the poster's social class, and how such a change happens inside the model. This paper investigates job-based doubt, i.e., judging a claim false more often when the poster has a lower-status job, using fact-checked claims with randomised poster profiles in base and post-trained checkpoints of six model families. Our results reveal that: 1) post-training creates such doubt only with the open Tülu-3 recipe (OLMo-2 32B, Tülu-3 70B), not with Meta's, Alibaba's or Mistral's; the effect is under one percentage point but statistically reliable, exceeding training drift and replicating on independent claim sets; 2) in OLMo-2 32B, the model does not start noticing the poster's job more, but starts interpreting it differently: a lower-status job pushes the base model toward \"true\" and the post-trained model toward \"false\"; and 3) this flip is carried by a single residual-stream direction and happens at supervised fine-tuning. The mechanism was confirmed on 400 new claims with pre-specified predictions. None of seven deployed commercial models doubts lower-status posters more. Our work underscores the need to audit speaker bias per training recipe.\n         \n       \n     \n     \n       [56] \n       \n        arXiv:2610.38544\n       \n      \n        [ pdf ,  html ,  other ]\n     \n     \n       \n          Title: \n          An Alignment Benchmark for Enterprise Agents on Era by Eon\n         \n          Hai Sadon ,  Benjamin Gruenbaum ,  Doron Porat ,  Assaf Natanzon ,  Roy Zavida ,  Chen Dinachi ,  Or Itzahary  \n\n          Comments: \n          11 pages, 1 figure, 4 tables\n         \n\n\n          Subjects: \n           Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI) ; Software Engineering (cs.SE)\n         \n\n         \n          AI agents are widely used in enterprises to access business systems and act on them in pursuit of a goal. However, fulfilling a user's request can cause harm. Era by Eon is a product that creates a digital twin of a company by simulating its business systems and data. Its simulators implement the programming interfaces of real products. Our benchmark uses ten of these systems, including Salesforce, Zendesk, Jira and Slack. We tested whether agents would carry out harmful business requests or trust misleading information in company records. We ran 19 models on 41 tasks three times, for 2,337 runs. Of the 2,304 runs that produced an answer, 918 (40%) contained a violation. The most common were false claims about actions or records (290 runs), following planted instructions (266) and disclosing sensitive data to the wrong audience (249). Every attempted change was recorded and blocked before it took effect. Misleading Slack messages caused 16 models to report an incorrect ticket status in 47 of 57 runs. No model was free of violations. The four best models violated in 22 to 24% of their runs. Yet one of them, Nova Pro, did the task correctly in only 30 runs.\n         \n       \n     \n     \n       [57] \n       \n        arXiv:2610.38551\n       \n      \n        [ pdf ,  html ,  other ]\n     \n     \n       \n          Title: \n          PT$^3$: Prototype-Guided Self-Supervised Pretraining for Generalizable mmWave Human Point Cloud Representation\n         \n          Ziyang Liu ,  Kieren Yu ,  Xiao Yang ,  Xin Yang ,  Kaishun Wu  \n\n          Comments: \n          30 pages\n         \n\n\n          Subjects: \n           Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI) \n         \n\n         \n          Multi-source millimeter-wave (mmWave) observations offer an opportunity to learn human representations reusable across sensing tasks. However, sparse detections and multipath effects make these observations unstable, challenging existing point cloud pretraining methods. We propose PT$^3$, a prototype-guided self-supervised pretraining framework for mmWave human point clouds. PT$^3$ represents local states in spatial regions as sparse compositions of shared prototypes and learns their temporal evolution through autoregressive prediction. Observation consistency further stabilizes representations under radar perturbations. After pretraining on unlabeled sequences from multiple sources, the same frozen encoder supports action classification, dense point generation, joint pose estimation, and future motion prediction on datasets not used for pretraining. Across these tasks, PT$^3$ outperforms the compared pretraining baselines and achieves competitive or superior performance to task-specific models.\n         \n       \n     \n     \n       [58] \n       \n        arXiv:2610.38566\n       \n      \n        [ pdf ,  html ,  other ]\n     \n     \n       \n          Title: \n          Co-Evolving Policy and Rubric via Capability Awareness for Open-Ended Generation\n         \n          Chengsheng Zhang ,  Wenshen Xu ,  Yuchuan Wu ,  Zhiyuan Yu ,  Junfeng Fang ,  Tianlong Wang ,  Bingxue Qiu ,  Yongbin Li ,  Xiang Wang  \n\n          Comments: \n          30 pages\n         \n\n\n          Subjects: \n           Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI) \n         \n\n         \n          Rubric-based reinforcement learning (RL) offers interpretable, fine-grained supervision for aligning large language models (LLMs) on open-ended generation tasks. However, static rubric criteria may become saturated or remain beyond the policy's exploration, rendering the reward signals insufficiently discriminative. While recent efforts seek to guide sampling exploration or evolve rubrics, they fail to explicitly factor in how rubric difficulty should track the evolving policy's capabilities, resulting in a persistent mismatch between the optimization objective and the policy's current capability frontier. In this paper, we propose \\textbf{Co}-Evolving Policy and Rubric via \\textbf{C}apability \\textbf{A}wareness (\\textbf{CoCA}), a closed-loop framework that jointly adapts the rubric and policy through criterion-level diagnosis. Specifically, CoCA contrasts query-only and rubric-guided rollouts to characterize each criterion relative to the current policy, recalibrates criteria with mismatched difficulty, and identifies guidance-revealed capability gaps for frontier-based learning. Based on this insight, CoCA either expands rubric-conditioned frontiers through guided optimization or transfers qualified gains through on-policy self-distillation. As the policy updates, CoCA improves the capability diagnosis and enables more reliable distillation, thereby completing the co-evolution loop. Extensive experiments across diverse benchmarks and LLMs demonstrate that CoCA consistently improves performance over rubric-based RL methods. Moreover, CoCA promotes the internalization of guidance-revealed capabilities into query-only behavior while mitigating capability degradation.\n         \n       \n     \n     \n       [59] \n       \n        arXiv:2610.38568\n       \n      \n        [ pdf ,  html ,  other ]\n     \n     \n       \n          Title: \n          A-RSI: Adaptive Task Refresh for Zero-Data Multimodal Self-Improvement\n         \n          Yu Liu ,  Youxing Li ,  Yuedong Tan ,  Zhiwei Yang ,  Zihan Su ,  Hanqi Feng ,  Shaojie Zhang ,  Libin Liao ,  Zihao Wan ,  Chenxu Wu ,  Cong Cao ,  Fangfang Yuan ,  Yanbing Liu ,  Ziheng Chen ,  Pei Fu ,  Radu Timofte ,  Zongwei Wu ,  Nicu Sebe ,  Barnabas Poczos ,  Jian Luan  \n\n\n\n          Subjects: \n           Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI) \n         \n\n         \n          Recursive self-improvement requires models not only to acquire useful training experience, but also to decide when their current experience has ceased to be useful. Existing methods often focus on what experience to generate next, while paying less attention to when fresh experience should be introduced. Many also construct training targets through self-consistency or majority voting, making supervision depend on the model's own uncertain predictions. We address these two questions separately: training dynamics determine when to acquire new experience, while the answers to new tasks remain independently checkable. A-RSI monitors reward variation and accuracy in training logs to refresh a task pool when its learning signal declines, without consulting validation or test scores. A static library of model-authored programs produces multimodal questions and derives their reference answers; we specify the task-generation contract, automatically verify chosen properties of the reward and refresh rules, and separately audit the generated answers. The training loop uses no external training examples or human-written answer labels. On the primary eleven-benchmark suite, A-RSI raises the performance average from 57.77 to 61.05 with 800 fresh tasks.\n         \n       \n     \n     \n       [60] \n       \n        arXiv:2610.38573\n       \n      \n        [ pdf ,  html ,  other ]\n     \n     \n       \n          Title: \n          CALM: Conflict-Aware Lifecycle Memory Governance for Long-Horizon Language Agents\n         \n          Chang Li ,  Xianhao Ou ,  Xinyan Ma ,  Hao Li ,  Haoyuan Xu ,  Jiafeng Liang ,  Xiaohan Wang ,  Jiajun Chai ,  Guojun Yin ,  Ming Liu  \n\n\n\n          Subjects: \n           Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI) \n         \n\n         \n          A memory can be outdated for present-day reasoning yet indispensable for reconstructing the past. Relevance-based retrieval overlooks this tension: semantic relevance, evidence-grounded validity, and query-specific eligibility are distinct. We propose CALM, a conflict-aware lifecycle memory governance framework that decouples persistent memory preservation from query-time admission. CALM converts heterogeneous records into evidence-grounded memory cells, updates validity and lifecycle states using support, conflict, and dependency signals, and admits query-appropriate evidence through training-free or learned gating informed by temporal applicability. Across LongMemEval-S and LoCoMo with Qwen and GPT backbones, CALM outperforms state-of-the-art baselines, achieving the best overall accuracy. Compared with simple filtering and reranking, query-conditioned admission further improves evidence selection. Conflict-blocking and robustness analyses show CALM suppresses erroneous retrieved memories while retaining useful evidence. Historical-reconstruction experiments demonstrate that preserving superseded records supports accurate earlier-state reasoning without compromising current-state answers.\n         \n       \n     \n     \n       [61] \n       \n        arXiv:2610.38620\n       \n      \n        [ pdf ,  html ,  other ]\n     \n     \n       \n          Title: \n          Think Before You Act: Decision Gate for LLM Agents\n         \n          Iftach Shoham ,  Yuval David ,  Offer Akrabi ,  Erez Bilgory ,  Ido Levy ,  Sami Marreed ,  Sergey Zeltyn ,  Segev Shlomov  \n\n\n\n          Subjects: \n           Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI) \n         \n\n         \n          LLM agents increasingly perform consequential actions through external tools, yet their proposed actions are often executed without independent verification of policy compliance or task completion. We introduce JevAct, a runtime verification framework that intercepts selected agent actions before execution and enables feedback-driven correction. JevAct compiles domain policies into executable state checks and structured questions evaluated by a prefill-only decision model, which produces probability-based judgments without relying on LLM generation. By combining deterministic verification of tool-state facts with probabilistic assessment of conversational context, the framework identifies potentially invalid actions and premature task termination, providing targeted feedback for bounded revision. JevAct operates independently of the underlying agent model and requires no modifications to its parameters or tools. We evaluate JevAct across eleven LLM agents on $\\tau^2$-bench Airline and Telecom tasks and BFCL. Results show improvements in agent reliability across multiple models and benchmarks, with particularly consistent gains on Telecom and BFCL, while performance on Airline is more mixed. Overall, these findings suggest that combining structured decision models with executable verification can improve the reliability of tool-using LLM agents.\n         \n       \n     \n     \n       [62] \n       \n        arXiv:2610.38631\n       \n      \n        [ pdf ,  html ,  other ]\n     \n     \n       \n          Title: \n          Right Numbers, Wrong Questions: Evaluating LLMs on Underspecified Financial Queries\n         \n          Radha Yadav ,  Prasanna Devadiga ,  Peeyush Mishra ,  Apoorv Singh  \n\n          Comments: \n          Accepted for oral presentation at the 3rd Workshop on LLMs and Generative AI for Finance (AI4F) at ACM ICAIF '26, Milan, Italy, November 14, 2026. Non-archival. 16 pages, 4 figures\n         \n\n\n          Subjects: \n           Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI) ; Computation and Language (cs.CL); General Finance (q-fin.GN)\n         \n\n         \n          Financial assistants answer questions that sound factual but depend on what the user leaves unsaid: what a data figure measures, which product variant is meant, and the investor's situation and the rules that apply. An assistant that ignores these can still answer with a real number from its data, one that answers a different question. We call this frame collapse. We study it in Indian mutual funds, where product variants and tax rules are formally defined, so the correct answer can be computed for every reading. One study tests each challenge. Given an ambiguously labelled figure, models used it, often under the user's label. Given every alternative labelled, they listed them all, but the layout decided which came first. Missing a fact about the investor, weaker agents assumed a default or an outdated tax rule; requiring the fact did not guarantee its use. Because such answers match their data, checks against the data pass them. An AI judge caught mislabelled figures only when told what each field means, and still passed answers that silently chose another reading. We show how these failures arise, not how often, and propose controls for financial assistants.\n         \n       \n     \n     \n       [63] \n       \n        arXiv:2610.38634\n       \n      \n        [ pdf ,  html ,  other ]\n     \n     \n       \n          Title: \n          \"Close enough?\" An investigation into multiplication errors in LLMs\n         \n          William Bolton ,  Bezawit Tilaye ,  Huw Day  \n\n          Comments: \n          Accepted to Neurips 2026 NewInML workshop\n         \n\n\n          Subjects: \n           Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI) \n         \n\n         \n          Large language models (LLMs) frequently fail at multi-digit arithmetic, but the underlying mechanisms governing these failures remain poorly understood. While recent work demonstrates that addition errors follow a \"noisy quantisation\" model localised around carry boundaries, whether this framework generalises to multiplication remains an open question. In this work, we evaluate multiplication capabilities across several small base LLMs on a dataset of 16,991 problems spanning varied operand lengths. We show that multiplication errors do not conform to the addition-style noisy quantisation model. Instead, we uncover a novel error regime in small Qwen models: errors in lower-order columns predominantly preserve parity. Conversely, higher-order digit errors shift toward off-by-one carry failures. Our findings suggest that arithmetic computation in LLMs relies on distinct, multi-periodic internal representations where parity is tracked more robustly than exact digit identity, providing new mechanistic insights into numerical reasoning in transformers.\n         \n       \n     \n     \n       [64] \n       \n        arXiv:2610.38636\n       \n      \n        [ pdf ,  html ,  other ]\n     \n     \n       \n          Title: \n          Curriculum-Based Deep Reinforcement Learning for Robust Autonomous Rocket Landing under Atmospheric Uncertainty\n         \n          Atin Chowdhury ,  Piyush Joshi  \n\n          Comments: \n          21 pages, 12 figures\n         \n\n\n          Subjects: \n           Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI) \n         \n\n         \n          Autonomous far-range rocket landing is a challenging control problem because navigation, descent-rate regulation, attitude management, and fuel use must be coordinated in the presence of uncertain atmospheric and vehicle dynamics. This paper presents a curriculum-driven proximal policy optimization (PPO) framework for three-dimensional rocket landing in a stochastic environment incorporating altitude-dependent aerodynamic drag, wind shear, temporally correlated turbulence, discrete gusts, uncertain propulsion and aerodynamic parameters, thrust-vector misalignment, engine lag, actuator-gain variation, noisy observations, and command latency. Learning is organised into far-range approach, soft descent, and integrated landing stages. Policy transfer, critic warm-up after reward changes, rehearsal of earlier tasks, and progressive expansion of weather severity are used to support learning across these coupled phases. Across 100,000 evaluation episodes, the policy achieves 96,656 successful landings, corresponding to a success rate of 96.656\\% and a 95\\% Wilson confidence interval of 96.543--96.766\\%. Success decreases from 98.84\\% under zero-disturbance conditions to 88.07\\% in the highest weather-severity band. Successful touchdowns exhibit small miss distances and low residual horizontal and vertical velocities. An exploratory logistic-regression analysis identifies mean wind speed as the strongest measured correlate of success, with initial distance, initial altitude, initial vertical velocity, and wind-shear exponent also associated with outcomes. The results quantify both the effectiveness of the learned policy over the evaluated disturbance distribution and the degradation that occurs under severe compound atmospheric conditions.\n         \n       \n     \n     \n       [65] \n       \n        arXiv:2610.38681\n       \n      \n        [ pdf ,  html ,  other ]\n     \n     \n       \n          Title: \n          Weaken to Strengthen: Weakening-Induced On-Policy Self-Distillation for Large Reasoning Models\n         \n          Yecheng Wu ,  Song Han ,  Han Cai  \n\n          Comments: \n          Work in progress. Code is released at  this https URL \n         \n\n\n          Subjects: \n           Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI) ; Computation and Language (cs.CL); Machine Learning (cs.LG)\n         \n\n         \n          Self-distillation offers a way to improve reasoning models when a suitable external teacher is unavailable. However, common approaches rely on privileged information to construct a stronger self-teacher, limiting their use when only unlabeled prompts are available. In this paper, we introduce Weakening-Induced On-Policy Self-Distillation (WISD), which instead constructs a weaker counterpart of the original model and uses the contrast between the two as a training signal without requiring privileged information. We explore two ways to construct the weaker model: low-bit quantization and magnitude pruning. We evaluate WISD across diverse architectures and scales on mathematical and coding reasoning tasks. Across these benchmarks, quantization and pruning improve average accuracy over the original models by 4.0 and 5.5 percentage points, respectively, achieving state-of-the-art performance even without privileged information. Notably, pruning raises Qwen3.5-4B's accuracy from 57.7% to 70.5% on HMMT 2025 and from 39.1% to 56.7% on LiveCodeBench v6. Quantization raises Qwen3.6-35B-A3B's accuracy from 68.5% to 74.6% and from 61.8% to 69.5% on the same benchmarks, respectively. These results establish WISD as a novel and effective self-distillation framework for large reasoning models.\n         \n       \n     \n     \n       [66] \n       \n        arXiv:2610.38687\n       \n      \n        [ pdf ,  html ,  other ]\n     \n     \n       \n          Title: \n          Deogen: Automatic Benchmark Generation for Reasoning over Policies\n         \n          Sam Kouteili ,  William Liang ,  Keeran Murray ,  Adriana Zhou ,  Ramesh Viswanathan ,  Scott Shapiro ,  Ruzica Piskac  \n\n\n\n          Subjects: \n           Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI) ; Machine Learning (cs.LG)\n         \n\n         \n          Language models are increasingly being deployed in regulated settings, where a model must apply given rules to the facts of a situation to determine a verdict. Benchmarking this capability, known as deontic reasoning, remains a costly and manual process, with existing datasets largely hand-crafted. In this paper, we present DeoGen, a scalable, automatic, and parametrizable benchmark generator for deontic reasoning. By autoformalizing policy rules to first-order logic, we can construct decision trees that capture possible outcome trajectories. Each path down a tree becomes a benchmark entry, with each tree producing combinatorially many entries relative to tree depth. Paths ending at a leaf entail a verdict, while non-leaf paths measure a model's ability to abstain when presented with insufficient information. Evaluations show frontier models return the correct verdict 89-92% of the time. Notably, performance degrades (22-40% correct) on entries without sufficient information to claim a verdict, even with explicit instructions to abstain in such cases. In all, DeoGen presents an automatic, scalable, and parametric method for generating deontic reasoning benchmarks.\n         \n       \n     \n     \n       [67] \n       \n        arXiv:2610.38691\n       \n      \n        [ pdf ,  html ,  other ]\n     \n     \n       \n          Title: \n          Beyond fabrication: Misattributed numbers in papers written by AI research agents\n         \n          Mosab Alfaqeeh ,  Omar Hourani ,  Muath Hourani ,  Hashem Alfaqeeh  \n\n          Comments: \n          42 pages, 5 figures, 13 tables. Code and data:  this https URL \n         \n\n\n          Subjects: \n           Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI) ; Computation and Language (cs.CL); Digital Libraries (cs.DL)\n         \n\n         \n          Verifying numerical claims in research written by AI agents is an important but challenging problem. Recent benchmarks show that many agent-written papers fail integrity checks, but such verdicts, often produced by a single LLM judge, identify failure without explaining its nature, and existing checks either test a paper for internal consistency or test whether a reported value appears somewhere in an agent's output. We re-analyze all 540 released judgments from a recent public benchmark of AI research agents (nine agents, fifteen open research problems) and manually label 256 numerical claims from 18 submissions. Failures concentrate on alignment between the paper and the underlying work rather than overt cheating, and about four in five unsupported claims correspond to values in the agent's own files that come from the wrong experiment, metric, or run. We introduce a deterministic claim-to-evidence checker that scores whether the surrounding context identifies the claimed result and calibrates each claim against numerical decoys. With its rules fixed before labeling, it certifies claims with over 90% precision against blinded annotators (78 of 85; Cohen's kappa = 0.89), while verifying only a subset of claims; most claims it cannot verify are nevertheless traceable, so failure to verify is not evidence of falsehood. An LLM verifier given the same evidence recovers more traceable claims but accepts about three times as many misattributed ones, so the two are best used together. Applied to 99 submissions, the checker reveals large differences in traceability across agents, and a second judge from a different vendor preserves the official agent ranking while passing twice as many papers. These findings motivate provenance-first auditing of AI-produced research.\n         \n       \n     \n     \n       [68] \n       \n        arXiv:2610.38728\n       \n      \n        [ pdf ,  html ,  other ]\n     \n     \n       \n          Title: \n          From Grind to Grasp\n         \n          Peter Sunehag  \n\n\n\n          Subjects: \n           Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI) ; Multiagent Systems (cs.MA)\n         \n\n         \n          Discussions of complexity, knowledge and intelligence often overlook the work involved. For example, outputs generated by short programs have low algorithmic complexity but can have high computational depth, the least work needed to produce them from near-shortest programs. Producing them with less work can require longer descriptions, giving high work-bounded complexity. We introduce a formalism that compares achievements under work and information limits and describes how learning and persistence can make deep achievements shallow by reusing products of earlier work. For an agent with a limited work budget, the value of building and maintaining such resources depends on how they support its goals over later tasks. We define grasp as performance across work budgets at a fixed information allowance. Low grasp can still yield success through grind at high cost in work, without necessarily improving human grasp. Applied to improvement itself, grasp gives a criterion for recursive self-improvement in which successors make comparable further improvements more efficiently. Another route to faster progress is to expand the resources brought to bear through knowledge sharing and coordination across society, even while comparable advances require increasing work. Grasp also applies to collectives, which may outperform their members yet require more total work than one system with their combined resources. Establishing and sustaining organisation can itself be deep even when the primary task is simple. These demands may help explain the richness of biological and social systems. In agent collectives, this organisational work can generate goals beyond assigned tasks, including maintaining the collective itself. We therefore caution against allowing an emergent agent society to assume authority over social coordination, since it could increasingly direct work towards its own persistence and goals.\n         \n       \n     \n     \n       [69] \n       \n        arXiv:2610.38755\n       \n      \n        [ pdf ,  html ,  other ]\n     \n     \n       \n          Title: \n          Leaving, Staying, or Living There? Duration Commonsense Reasoning with Human-LLM Co-annotations\n         \n          Chenyang Yu ,  Zhaomin Xiao ,  Yan Huang ,  Eduardo Blanco  \n\n\n\n          Subjects: \n           Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI) ; Information Retrieval (cs.IR)\n         \n\n         \n          Gardening in the backyard implies living there, while checking into an Airbnb more likely means staying for a few days. Such duration judgments are important for understanding human mobility, travel behavior, and social context. However, duration is rarely stated explicitly in social media posts and must instead be inferred from noisy textual, visual, and contextual cues. In this paper, we investigate whether vision-language models possess such duration commonsense. We create a new corpus of geotagged tweets co-annotated by humans and LLMs to serve as a testbed for this task. We find that state-of-the-art models struggle with duration inference, even when guided by multi-turn Chain-of-Thought prompting. Error analyses further reveal that models often fail to integrate critical contextual information, or they overly rely on prominent visual details while overlooking subtle hashtags and semantic nuances. These findings underscore the significant challenges remaining in context-aware multimodal understanding.\n         \n       \n     \n     \n       [70] \n       \n        arXiv:2610.38851\n       \n      \n        [ pdf ,  html ,  other ]\n     \n     \n       \n          Title: \n          Learning to Reason from Reference Samples Alone: Reinforced Adversarial Learning for Modern LLM Post-Training\n         \n          Albert Bou ,  Javier Valdes-Aleman ,  Jon M. Laurent ,  Samuel G. Rodriques ,  Andrew D. White ,  Michaela Hinks ,  Siddharth Narayanan  \n\n          Comments: \n          31 pages, 6 figures, 10 tables, including references and appendices\n         \n\n\n          Subjects: \n           Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI) ; Machine Learning (cs.LG)\n         \n\n         \n          In many settings where LLM post-training is desirable, high-quality answers are recognizable but difficult to score with explicit rewards. Reinforcement learning with verifiable rewards (RLVR) is limited to tasks where an explicit correctness signal is available. Prior adversarial approaches to training reasoning LLMs either complement verifiable rewards with co-trained critics or require reference reasoning traces. We ask whether a co-trained LLM judge can turn reference answers alone into the sole reward for on-policy post-training of reasoning LLMs. We call this method Reinforced Adversarial Learning (RAL). We first validate RAL on three verifiable tasks (integer multiplication, code-based arithmetic-expression synthesis, and SMILES-to-formula translation), using the verifiers only for evaluation. With sufficiently capable judges and enough training, RAL matches direct RLVR. We then apply RAL to scientific abstract generation in two settings. In the first setting, RAL guides the generator to produce abstracts in the scientific domain of the reference abstracts, even though the prompt leaves that domain unspecified. In the second setting, we generate abstracts for U.S. National Institutes of Health grant proposals conditioned on the grant title. Human scientists and an ensemble of LLM judges find our generations harder to distinguish from real abstracts than those of frontier-LLM baselines. These results suggest a path toward post-training reasoning models in scientific and other high-value domains, where increasingly capable judges infer hard-to-specify reward functions from example answers.\n         \n       \n     \n     \n       [71] \n       \n        arXiv:2610.38867\n       \n      \n        [ pdf ,  html ,  other ]\n     \n     \n       \n          Title: \n          Where You Inject Matters: The Trust Region Effect in LLM Agents\n         \n          Chengquan Guo ,  Xiaodong Liu ,  Weiwei Yang ,  Mingqian Feng ,  Jialin Song ,  Qiyao Ma ,  Carolyn Buractaon ,  Christopher White ,  Bo Li ,  Jianfeng Gao  \n\n\n\n          Subjects: \n           Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI) ; Cryptography and Security (cs.CR); Machine Learning (cs.LG)\n         \n\n         \n          LLM agents are increasingly adopted in long-horizon tasks and interact with diverse external environments. Despite their growing capabilities, these agents remain vulnerable to real-world attacks such as indirect prompt injections, which manipulate agents through malicious instructions embedded in external environments. While existing risk assessment work has primarily focused on the injection content, the effect of where an injection appears has not been systematically characterized. Studying injection placement requires agents to operate over longer-horizon tasks and interact with diverse environmental surfaces. Thus, we build three agentic testbeds spanning tool use, coding, and daily workflow domains with diverse injection surfaces. Holding the injection content fixed, we find that agent susceptibility varies across injection placements. We refer to this phenomenon as the trust region effect: agents implicitly assign different levels of trust to different regions of their environments. For example, an agent powered by GPT-5.4 follows an unsafe injection instruction placed in a repository documentation file (i.e., a README or TODO file) 2.6 times more often on average than the same instruction placed in a code or data file. Building upon this observation, we develop learning-based attackers that generate injection content and select better injection placements. Our approach demonstrates that both where an injection is placed and what content it contains can affect the strength of indirect prompt injection attacks. To the best of our knowledge, this work provides the first systematic characterization for the effectiveness of different injection placements, spanning both coarse-grained environmental surfaces and fine-grained positions. The uneven trust that agents implicitly place across their environments provides important insights for future attack and defense design.\n         \n       \n     \n     \n       [72] \n       \n        arXiv:2610.38869\n       \n      \n        [ pdf ,  html ,  other ]\n     \n     \n       \n          Title: \n          Auditable Belief Updates as a Missing Safety Layer for LLM-Powered Multi-Agent Systems\n         \n          Zhuoyun Li ,  Boxuan Wang ,  Jinwei Hu ,  Xiaowei Huang ,  Yi Dong  \n\n\n\n          Subjects: \n           Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI) \n         \n\n         \n          Large Language Models (LLMs) are increasingly being used as cognitive interfaces that shape how users interpret evidence, communicate, and make decisions. As deployed systems become more multimodal, tool-using, and multi-agent, safety failures no longer arise only from isolated harmful outputs, but also from how agents update, store, and propagate epistemic commitments across retrieval, memory, tools, and peer communication. In this paper, we define belief as an engineering abstraction for evidence-based commitments that guide downstream action. Building on this view, we propose the Explicit Belief Multi-Agent System (EB-MAS), which externalizes belief states into a structured substrate, uses belief operators to propose candidate updates, and applies a Cognitive Firewall to bound or reject updates that are weakly evidenced but strongly driven by vulnerability cues. We also discuss the risk of group belief at the group level, including manufactured consensus, collusion, and cascading misinformation, and outline practical integration challenges around grounding, false positives, scalability, and ethical misuse. Therefore, scalable agent safety requires not only better output filtering or stronger base models, but also explicit interfaces for tracing, calibrating, and constraining belief revision at runtime.\n         \n       \n     \n     \n       [73] \n       \n        arXiv:2610.38882\n       \n      \n        [ pdf ,  html ,  other ]\n     \n     \n       \n          Title: \n          Aligning Autoresearch: Co-Improvement between Agents and Humans\n         \n          Tianjian Li ,  Chuanyang Jin ,  Seungone Kim ,  Tong Chen ,  John Hoffman ,  Yoram Bachrach amd Pedro Rodriguez ,  Ping Yu ,  Eryk Helenowski ,  Swarnadeep Saha ,  Jack Lanchantin ,  Weizhe Yuan ,  Ilia Kulikov ,  Jason Weston  \n\n\n\n          Subjects: \n           Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI) \n         \n\n         \n          Autoresearch agents can now attempt long-horizon AI research end-to-end, i.e., self-improvement. However, best results are still obtained by research agents interacting with human researchers, and improving AI's ability to perform this co-research will only reinforce this effect---a process termed co-improvement (Weston and Foerster, 2025). Further, aligning with human expectations is likely a safer path for research agents, especially given recent agent reward hacking phenomena (OpenAI, 2026b). In this work we address these issues in four ways: (i) we empirically study the role of humans in directing autoresearch agents, focusing on the domain of benchmark creation, and find humans still play a pivotal role particularly in the ideation phase; (ii) we also develop a benchmark, HackBench, that shows current autoresearch agents are not aligned and are unable to achieve hack localization sufficiently; (iii) we present a method to train agents for improved human interaction by using human simulators as a proxy during reinforcement learning (RL); and finally (iv) we develop a method to improve model text generation, and hence agent communication, by RL with rubrics aligned with human experts. Together, our training and evaluation recipes provide a path toward more capable and aligned (co-)research agents.\n         \n       \n     \n     \n       [74] \n       \n        arXiv:2610.38924\n       \n      \n        [ pdf ,  html ,  other ]\n     \n     \n       \n          Title: \n          TreasuryQA Bench: Can AI Agents Work an Archive of Scanned Financial Records?\n         \n          Ritiz Tambi ,  Sahar Shayegan ,  Joshua Meyer ,  Sun Kim ,  Ali Khan ,  Victor Shih ,  Mehdi Jamei ,  Andi Partovi  \n\n\n\n          Subjects: \n           Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI) \n         \n\n         \n          Organisations keep decades of financial records as scanned PDFs on shared drives, and AI agents can now reach those drives through Model Context Protocol (MCP) servers. Existing benchmarks do not mirror this production setting because they simplify the task. In some, the relevant documents are handed to the agent, so it never has to search. In others, the agent searches through documents with pre-extracted text, so it never has to read scanned images. We introduce TreasuryQA Bench, which requires both: 70 questions over 1,435 scanned U.S. Treasury publications, held in Veris's high-fidelity Dropbox twin. In each task the agent searches for the relevant file through Dropbox's MCP server, reads the pages it needs with Reducto's hosted OCR server or its own tools, and replies with one exact figure. A Python grader checks each answer against an accepted range around the correct value. In this paper we present results from ten agents. The best, Claude Opus 5.5 in Claude Code, solves 64.8% of attempts and the worst, GPT-5.6 Terra in Codex, solves 16.7%, so the benchmark is far from saturated. We also test how scan quality affects performance: every agent solves fewer tasks on the blurriest scans. Most failed attempts reply with a wrong number rather than no number, and the most common cause is a digit misread from the scan. Three worked examples are included with their answer keys.\n         \n       \n     \n     \n       [75] \n       \n        arXiv:2610.38960\n       \n      \n        [ pdf ,  html ,  other ]\n     \n     \n       \n          Title: \n          Efficient Agentic Audio-Visual Question Answering through Toolset Pruning and Modality Consolidation\n         \n          Yi Wang ,  Tianzi Wang ,  Peter Bell  \n\n\n\n          Subjects: \n           Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI) ; Computation and Language (cs.CL); Audio and Speech Processing (eess.AS)\n         \n\n         \n          Fine-grained audio-visual question answering (AVQA) requires models to resolve complex cross-modal correspondences and temporally grounding from sparsely distributed evidence. Omni-modal agents, which autonomously orchestrate perception tools to actively collect evidence, achieve strong performance, but it remains unclear whether active perception from both audio and visual modalities depends on broad specialized toolsets, especially audio or visual modality-specific tools. Such systems may also introduce long execution trajectories and substantial inference cost. We investigate whether comparable performance can be retained with a compact workflow exposing only essential perceptual capabilities. Starting from the specialized-tool OmniAgent, we conduct staged controlled experiments that prune the toolset and progressively consolidate modality-specific tools into omni-modal tools, motivating a two-tool agent comprising only Omni-modal captioning and Omni-modal QA. Across three widely used AVQA benchmarks, our compact agent achieves comparable or better accuracy than the specialized-tool agent while reducing inference cost by around 70%.\n         \n       \n     \n     \n       [76] \n       \n        arXiv:2610.38975\n       \n      \n        [ pdf ,  html ,  other ]\n     \n     \n       \n          Title: \n          Dungeons & Dragons Encounter Analysis with Semi-Random Simulation in Foundry VTT\n         \n          ravyn Hardcastle ,  Matthew Guzdial  \n\n          Comments: \n          10 pages, 6 figures, 22nd Artificial Intelligence and Interactive Digital Entertainment Conference\n         \n\n\n          Subjects: \n           Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI) \n         \n\n         \n          Dungeons & Dragons (D&D) is a tabletop roleplaying game (TTRPG) in which a group of players, guided by a Game Master, roleplay a fantasy adventure with simulated combat and improvisational narrative. In D&D, an encounter is a single instance of simulated combat, where the players must use their characters to attempt to defeat a selection of enemy characters that the GM controls. D&D has multiple systems that approximate encounter difficulty by summing individual difficulty values that designers predefine for each enemy. However, since players and enemies can have a wide variety of capabilities, encounter difficulty depends on more than just the estimated difficulty of each monster. For example, these metrics do not account for positioning and dungeon layout, the synergies between monster abilities, and player party composition. We have created a tool, Mage-Hand, that can simulate D&D encounters semi-randomly using Foundry Virtual Tabletop, a virtual tabletop (VTT) system. In this paper, we show that our tool's simulated winrates have a strong correlation with D&D's published difficulty evaluation metrics, while also better aligning with some players' reported experiences.\n         \n       \n     \n     \n       [77] \n       \n        arXiv:2610.38980\n       \n      \n        [ pdf ,  html ,  other ]\n     \n     \n       \n          Title: \n          Context Compaction for World Models\n         \n          Hayder Tirmazi  \n\n\n\n          Subjects: \n           Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI) \n         \n\n         \n          World models let observers explore and interact with generated worlds. Recent work shows that when observers revisit a region, current models often fail to regenerate it consistently. We view this as a context retrieval problem. LLM agents manage context retrieval at two layers of their stack. At the model layer, the KV cache design determines how much context fits in memory. At the agent harness layer, harnesses such as Claude Code pick the context sent to the LLM. Prior work on world models has mostly focused only on the model layer. In this paper, we make three contributions. First, we build a benchmark to measure world model context retrieval. It generates new worlds for every episode, preventing a model from recalling rooms using training data. It also separates context retrieval from guesses based on nearby frames. Second, we build an agent harness that performs context compaction for world models, which to our knowledge no prior work has studied. Third, we use our benchmark to study four world model designs. Context compaction lets our world models retrieve rooms up to 4 times beyond their context window without retraining. However, world models with sliding-window attention lose most of their context retrieval when dreaming. Therefore, a dreaming world agent benefits more from extending the reach of its world model using context compaction than switching to sliding window attention at the model layer.\n         \n       \n     \n     \n       [78] \n       \n        arXiv:2610.38987\n       \n      \n        [ pdf ,  html ,  other ]\n     \n     \n       \n          Title: \n          Syntactic Segmentation and Collage Generation for Videogame Levels with Perceptual Distance\n         \n          Ross Mawhorter ,  Matthew Guzdial  \n\n          Comments: \n          11 pages, 12 figures, 22nd Artificial Intelligence and Interactive Digital Entertainment Conference\n         \n\n\n          Subjects: \n           Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI) \n         \n\n         \n          In procedural videogame level design, many existing techniques combine hand-authored level chunks. Some existing analysis techniques also rely on a higher-level graph structure which abstracts the level design. In this paper, we explore techniques for computing both of these things automatically from an existing design. We introduce the concept of perceptual distance, based on analysis techniques from the architecture subfield of Space Syntax. We use this perceptual distance to segment level designs with hierarchical clustering. We compare with past work on StarCraft analysis, show results on rooms from Super Metroid, and also demonstrate a proof-of-concept level generation algorithm based on this segmentation method.\n         \n       \n     \n     \n       [79] \n       \n        arXiv:2610.39003\n       \n      \n        [ pdf ,  html ,  other ]\n     \n     \n       \n          Title: \n          Agentic AI Reasoning and Learned Sampling for Task-Aware Projection Selection in Sparse-View CT: Predicting Hemodynamics\n         \n          Mahmoud Elhadidy ,  Roshan M. D'Souza ,  Amirhossein Arzani  \n\n\n\n          Subjects: \n           Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI) ; Fluid Dynamics (physics.flu-dyn)\n         \n\n         \n          Sparse-view computed tomography (CT) reduces the number of acquired projections, but conventional sampling strategies are designed primarily for image reconstruction rather than for downstream physical inference. Here, we investigate optimal task-aware projection selection for directly predicting hemodynamics from sparse CT angiography (CTA) measurements. Using computational fluid dynamics simulations of intracranial aneurysms, we generate synthetic fan-beam CTA projections and train a Fourier neural operator (FNO) to predict two targets: the spatially resolved time-averaged wall shear stress (TAWSS) field and the spatially averaged TAWSS. The FNO was coupled with optimal input selection for simultaneous optimal sampling and training. Our goal was to investigate if agentic AI could discover optimal sampling strategies that outperform current methods. We compared fixed uniform and golden-angle sampling with three task-aware optimal selection strategies: Gumbel-Softmax, a Soft top-K method, and an agentic AI selector. The methods were compared at different view selection budgets. A detailed analysis of the AI agent was performed to understand its reasoning and performance. For spatially resolved TAWSS, Agentic and Soft top-K achieve the lowest mean errors (Agentic performs slightly better) and substantially outperform fixed sampling under extreme sparsity. For spatially averaged TAWSS, optimized selection becomes important at extremely sparse acquisitions. Analysis of the selected masks reveals multiple distinct subsets concentrated around informative regions. Moreover, masks optimized for hemodynamic prediction yield poor image reconstructions, showing that task-optimal acquisition differs fundamentally from reconstruction-optimal acquisition. This work suggests the possibility of designing sparse acquisitions directly around downstream physical quantities.\n         \n       \n     \n     \n       [80] \n       \n        arXiv:2610.39007\n       \n      \n        [ pdf ,  html ,  other ]\n     \n     \n       \n          Title: \n          Formal Specifications with Data-Driven Signal Analysis\n         \n          Roderick Bloem ,  Benedikt Maderbacher ,  Cristinel Mateis ,  Dejan Ničković ,  Sergii Tkachov ,  Björn Ziegler  \n\n\n\n          Subjects: \n           Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI) ; Machine Learning (cs.LG)\n         \n\n         \n          Developing analog and hybrid systems requires substantial domain knowledge that is hard to articulate in a formal specification; without additional effort, this domain knowledge is also inaccessible to machine learning methods. We propose a hybrid approach for analyzing and monitoring real-valued signals that integrates specification-based and data-driven techniques. We use regular expressions with interval operations to identify regions of interest in a signal, classical signal processing methods to extract semantically meaningful features, and semi-supervised machine learning to infer complex relationships that distinguish correct from faulty behaviors. The relationships learned from normal behavior data can automatically detect incorrect behavior in the same system or variants thereof. We evaluate the method on a voltage regulator, demonstrating its effectiveness in detecting undesired system behaviors.\n         \n       \n     \n     \n       [81] \n       \n        arXiv:2610.39045\n       \n      \n        [ pdf ,  html ,  other ]\n     \n     \n       \n          Title: \n          When Should AI Decide, Defer, and Deviate? Responsible Automation for Deceased Donor Kidney Allocation\n         \n          Madeleine Pollack ,  Thomas Daillak ,  Mehmet Toner ,  Korkut Uygun ,  Heidi Yeh ,  Swati Gupta  \n\n\n\n          Subjects: \n           Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI) \n         \n\n         \n          We ask when an AI trained on historical decisions should be allowed to recommend decisions, defer to human experts, or in other cases, flag concerning patterns in data and deviate from past practice. This can be challenging because past decisions record what was done rather than what should have been done, and where data is sparse, a model's predictions may be too uncertain to act on. We provide a framework for addressing this problem using a case study in deceased-donor kidney allocation. Nearly 30% of kidneys recovered from deceased donors in the United States are never transplanted, largely because they are hard to place through the standard waitlist sequence. One can use AI to flag kidneys at risk of delayed or failed placement for out-of-sequence (OOS) allocation, but this comes at the cost of bypassing candidates higher on the waitlist. Thus, OOS allocation should only be used when the organ is demonstrably at high risk of discard on the standard sequence. Using Organ Procurement and Transplantation Network match-run data from 2016-2019 and 2023-2025 (excluding COVID-19-disrupted years), we build the Arbitrator framework, which combines various interpretable models and acts only when the uncertainty in their predictions cannot change the preferred action. Our Arbitrator resolves 93.5% of organ placement decisions using explicit medical criteria learned from data, and defers the remaining 6.5% to a human or a more complex model. We show that the Arbitrator correctly identifies 70.1% of historically discarded low-KDPI kidneys as OOS candidates, projecting that up to 43.9% of historically discarded kidneys could be flagged for expedited placement and potentially transplanted. We then exploit the interpretability of the Arbitrator to create an agentic audit comparing the learned rules to published clinical literature in order to flag decision-making patterns that are contentious.\n         \n       \n     \n     \n       [82] \n       \n        arXiv:2610.39070\n       \n      \n        [ pdf ,  other ]\n     \n     \n       \n          Title: \n          When the Same Prompt Gives Different Answers (and the Same Answer Isn't Always Right): A Taxonomy of Failure Mechanisms, Testing, and Mitigation Strategies for LLM-Assisted Evidence Synthesis Read-and-Evaluate Tasks\n         \n          Carole Lunny ,  Ian Goodman ,  Stefano Berrone ,  Connor TA Brenna ,  Emma K Reid ,  Ivan Perez-Neri ,  Shailesh Kolekar ,  Nityanand Jain ,  Ashwin Kamath ,  Lucas HCC Santos ,  Yulia Otmakhova ,  Heba Hussein ,  Diana M Cespedes ,  Gioia Dahdal ,  Salmaan Kanji  \n\n          Comments: \n          76 pages, 2 figures, 2 tables, 1 box, 6 supplementary files\n         \n\n\n          Subjects: \n           Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI) ; Performance (cs.PF); Methodology (stat.ME)\n         \n\n         \n          LLM read-and-evaluate tasks include study screening, data extraction, risk-of-bias assessment, and critical appraisal in the evidence synthesis field. These task differ in the amount of LLM interpretation they require. Some tasks involve retrieving relatively simple information, such as an author name, sample size, or publication year. Others require the LLM to read source material, interpret it against predefined criteria, and make a judgement. Researchers should assess whether LLM judgements are accurate, supported by appropriate source evidence, and reproducible under specified conditions. A correct judgement does not necessarily indicate that the underlying evaluation process is reliable. We developed a taxonomy for identifying mechanisms that may compromise these properties and for informing the selection and reporting of appropriate evaluation methods. The taxonomy outlines potential ways that LLM judgments from read-and-evaluate tasks can fail, called TRAILS (Taxonomy of Reliability And Instability in LLM-assisted evidence Synthesis). The TRAILS taxonomy addresses two complementary questions. First, what mechanisms can cause the same read-and-evaluate task to produce different judgements across repeated LLM runs? Second, what mechanisms can systematically affect the judgement or required output in a way that is not apparent from the judgement itself? This study presents 38 mechanisms and identifies the deployment settings in which each can operate, approaches for testing them, and mitigation strategies. We also show why repeatability or accuracy does not rule out a hidden judgement failure and why human evaluation should assess whether a judgement is grounded in the supplied evidence, independent of inappropriate external influence, and produced through a process that can be examined and verified.\n         \n       \n     \n     \n       [83] \n       \n        arXiv:2610.39073\n       \n      \n        [ pdf ,  html ,  other ]\n     \n     \n       \n          Title: \n          Certified Access: Test-Time Compute on Conservative Physics-Structured AI Memory\n         \n          Pratik Jawahar ,  Riccardo Maggioni ,  Jonas Petersen ,  Matteo Cozzi ,  Gian-Alessandro Lombardi ,  Marek Gajewski ,  Camilla Mazzoleni ,  Federico Martelli  \n\n          Comments: \n          Accepted at the 40th Conference on Neural Information Processing Systems (NeurIPS 2026); Workshop: Long-Context Foundation Models\n         \n\n\n          Subjects: \n           Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI) ; High Energy Physics - Phenomenology (hep-ph)\n         \n\n         \n          Test-time compute mechanisms, such as retries, confidence-based escalation and non-local routing, are conventionally deployed as black-box additions to trained models. They typically add forward passes or halting heads, but give no structural account of what the extra compute buys or what it costs against the model's stability guarantees. We therefore propose a theoretical and empirical framework for test-time compute on a Latent Dynamics Model used as the memory. When an encoder's latent state $(q,p)$ is advanced by a damped symplectic (velocity-Verlet) step of a learned Hamiltonian, every mechanism that expands access has an exact phase-volume Jacobian, a bounded energy change and an exact latch-transport law. We split reachability failures into escape failures and reach failures. A Lorentz squeeze resolves escape failures by injecting bounded, re-absorbable energy. A gated canonical translation (a one-step channel across $C_T$) resolves reach failures at $\\detJ=1$ exactly. We verify this certificate stack on a designed analytic testbed. Reaching just beyond $C_T$ with the squeeze requires energy that grows quadratically in the excess distance. The gated channel's energy cost equals the potential difference between its endpoints, which is zero on the matched channel measured here, and so does not grow with distance in this testbed. An untrained state-replacing map annihilates phase volume and erases stored charge, while the gated channel transports it. On a learned physics-structured memory, we show calibrated compute rationing and evaluate the scaling limits of non-local routing against a physics-free baseline. The main contribution is the formalization and verification of the certificate stack, not benchmark performance.\n         \n       \n     \n     \n       [84] \n       \n        arXiv:2610.39182\n       \n      \n        [ pdf ,  html ,  other ]\n     \n     \n       \n          Title: \n          Reasoning or Pattern Exploitation? Mechanistic Insights from RL-Trained Language Models\n         \n          Aishik Nagar ,  Cheston Tan  \n\n\n\n          Subjects: \n           Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI) ; Computation and Language (cs.CL)\n         \n\n         \n          Large language models (LLMs) are increasingly described as acquiring \"reasoning\" skills after reinforcement learning with verifiable rewards. However, whether these gains reflect genuine reasoning or more superficial adaptations remains underexplored. In this work, we probe LLMs trained in a finite and exhaustively analyzable logical domain, namely Tic-Tac-Toe, and trace how internal representations evolve across reinforcement learning (RL) with Group Relative Policy Optimization (GRPO). Quantitatively, RL yields high accuracy and robustness across prompt variations, suggesting the emergence of game reasoning. Mechanistic interpretability, however, paints a different picture: features extracted with sparse autoencoders (SAEs) reveal that models primarily adapt to rely more heavily on information already explicit in the prompt, such as whose turn it is, game progression and board occupancy. In comparison, high-level concepts like board symmetries and strategic forks remain weakly represented, raising concerns that reasoning benchmarks overestimate model abilities. This suggests that RL-driven \"reasoning\" may conflate task-specific adaptations to input information with reasoning ability. We provide, for the first time: (i) a systematic interpretability pipeline tracing representation dynamics across RL training in small-scale LLMs; (ii) an extension of SAE-based feature discovery to hypothesis-driven testing in a finite logical domain; and (iii) an interpretability-based demonstration that RL post-training neither adds nor removes concept information but reorganizes which features dominate the representation, with causal interventions locating the decision downstream of it, in the late-layer state.\n         \n       \n     \n     \n       [85] \n       \n        arXiv:2610.39193\n       \n      \n        [ pdf ,  html ,  other ]\n     \n     \n       \n          Title: \n          Goal-Aware Dynamic Multi-Agent Collaboration for Long-Horizon Tasks\n         \n          Xu Shen ,  Lingyong Yan ,  Shuaiqiang Wang ,  Jianmin Wu ,  Zhikai Chen ,  Jiliang Tang ,  Jie Ren  \n\n\n\n          Subjects: \n           Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI) \n         \n\n         \n          LLM-based agents are increasingly used to complete complex, long-horizon tasks without human intervention. To make progress toward the task goal, agents need to perform different duties, such as planning and verification, when needed. To understand how single agents manage these duties during long-horizon execution, we conduct a preliminary study. Our analysis shows that verification has the strongest association with task success, but often occurs late, leaving limited opportunities for correction. We also find that the proportion of duty-relevant information decreases as records from different duties accumulate in a shared context. These findings lead us to examine whether multi-agent systems (MAS) can address these problems by assigning duties to agents with separate contexts. Our analysis shows that this separation alone is insufficient. Agents can lose track of the overall goal and repeat prior investigations. They may also lack necessary background while receiving irrelevant information from others. To maintain goal awareness and coordinate collaboration as task needs change, we propose Goal-Aware Dynamic Multi-Agent Collaboration (GAD). It uses a growing execution graph to organize subtasks and their dependencies. To keep agents focused on their goals, each context retains the original instruction and the assigned task's goal and acceptance conditions. To adapt collaboration and information sharing during execution, agents can divide tasks into subtasks and specify dependencies based on execution evidence. These dependencies guide when agents work and which results they receive. Verification and recovery check subtask results and full task completion against their goals, providing feedback for repair when checks fail.\n         \n       \n     \n     \n       [86] \n       \n        arXiv:2610.39230\n       \n      \n        [ pdf ,  html ,  other ]\n     \n     \n       \n          Title: \n          Beyond LLM Wrappers: Context Engineering and the Three-Engine Architecture for Reliable Financial AI\n         \n          Pradeep Kumar GS ,  Ravi Tanniru  \n\n\n\n          Subjects: \n           Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI) \n         \n\n         \n          Financial AI systems must support decision-bearing questions involving payments, collections, cash flow, and compliance while ensuring numerical accuracy, traceability, and auditability. Large language models (LLMs) enable natural language interaction but cannot independently guarantee reproducible calculations or evidence-grounded recommendations. We present a production-grounded architecture integrating three components: a financial knowledge graph for enterprise relationships, temporal context, and provenance; a deterministic optimization engine for financial calculations and decisions; and a multi-agent LLM layer for intent interpretation, task orchestration, explanation, and claim validation. A coordination layer, termed context engineering, selects, ranks, composes, and validates query-specific context across ERP systems, tools, and tenants.\n Based on observations from 15 deployments over approximately 11 months, single-agent pipelines exhibited hallucination rates of about 7-9% (5-10% across query types), including invented invoices and unsupported financial claims. Graph-backed multi-agent validation reduced observed rates to below 1%, although these findings are observational rather than causal. ERP data quality and tenant-specific schemas remained major implementation challenges. Reusable mappings, graph templates, and validation policies coincided with a reduction in deployment time from roughly six to two weeks.\n We argue that financial context engineering extends beyond text-centric retrieval-augmented generation by requiring structured relationships, temporal validity, exact numerical evidence, access controls, and validation against source records and solver outputs. We outline research challenges in evaluation, reproducibility, privacy, context quality, and cross-tenant generalization.\n         \n       \n     \n     \n       [87] \n       \n        arXiv:2610.39247\n       \n      \n        [ pdf ,  html ,  other ]\n     \n     \n       \n          Title: \n          SocialArena: Evaluating Social Intelligence in Large Language Models\n         \n          Zongqi Wang ,  Rui Wang ,  Yuchuan Wu ,  Hua Yang ,  Bingxue Qiu ,  Tianle Gu ,  Bing Zhao ,  Yujiu Yang ,  Jieping Ye ,  Yongbin Li  \n\n          Comments: \n          EMNLP 2026 oral\n         \n\n\n          Subjects: \n           Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI) \n         \n\n         \n          We introduce SocialArena, a benchmark for systematically defining and evaluating the social intelligence of large language models (LLMs). Although LLMs are increasingly deployed in socially interactive applications such as mental health counseling, companionship chatbots, and educational tutoring, existing evaluations remain fragmented focusing on isolated capabilities or single scenarios and evaluation reliability is largely unverified. This work makes three main contributions to address these limitations. (1) We first propose a unified three-tier framework that defines social intelligence along three capability dimensions: role-playing ability, mental state reasoning, and behavioral reasoning. To operationalize this definition, we design a role-playing dialogue paradigm with explicit character thinking for evaluation, enabling simultaneous assessment of these capabilities in a manner that mirrors realistic human interactions. (2) To ensure diversity across social scenarios and thereby prevent evaluation conclusions from being confined to specific scenarios, we introduce the U-TRIMF framework, which guides us in synthesizing social contexts with broad coverage. (3) To obtain reliable evaluation, we invest $132.4K to recruit and train human annotators and adopt a human-annotated arena-style protocol, where annotators compare model responses and provide preference labels along with natural-language critiques. We hope that SocialArena can provide valuable insights and guidance for future LLM improvements.\n         \n       \n     \n     \n       [88] \n       \n        arXiv:2610.39251\n       \n      \n        [ pdf ,  html ,  other ]\n     \n     \n       \n          Title: \n          Where to Learn and How Far to Roll Out: Efficient Adaptive Training for GUI Agents\n         \n          Yuxiang Chai ,  Quyu Kong ,  Panrong Tong ,  Steven Hoi ,  Yue Wang  \n\n\n\n          Subjects: \n           Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI) \n         \n\n         \n          Trajectory-level GRPO is a common approach for training multi-step GUI agents, but repeatedly rolling out complete trajectories from the task root can be inefficient: task-level rewards provide coarse supervision over which intermediate decisions require improvement, while already-mastered prefixes are repeatedly re-executed before reaching difficult states. We introduce a two-stage adaptive training framework that jointly addresses where to learn and how far to roll out. We first construct reusable intermediate checkpoints from successful expert trajectories and select informative pivots using action disagreement, which measures uncertainty directly from sampled GUI actions. Stage I performs one-step RL at these pivots to improve uncertain local decisions. Stage II then progressively expands policy responsibility through adaptive Local, Suffix, and Root training according to task progress. Recoverable checkpoints enable repeated training from intermediate states without replaying the preceding trajectory. Across MobileGym and CUA-Gym-Hub, our method reduces environment interaction by 56-61% relative to Vanilla GRPO while improving task performance, demonstrating that jointly allocating training across states and rollout ranges can substantially improve GUI-agent RL efficiency.\n         \n       \n     \n     \n       [89] \n       \n        arXiv:2610.39264\n       \n      \n        [ pdf ,  html ,  other ]\n     \n     \n       \n          Title: \n          Online Dense Reinforcement Learning with Planning-Grounding Agent for UI Navigation on Live IOS Apps\n         \n          Peitong Duan ,  Khashayar Gatmiry ,  Jeffrey Nichols ,  Samy Bengio ,  Emmanuel Abbe  \n\n\n\n          Subjects: \n           Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI) \n         \n\n         \n          While frontier vision-language models demonstrate strong capabilities in user interface (UI) navigation, their high inference costs prohibit everyday use. Conversely, smaller models trained via supervised fine-tuning (SFT) or offline reinforcement learning often memorize static trajectories, leading to brittle execution and an inability to recover from errors. To address these limitations, we propose a novel online reinforcement learning framework that trains UI navigation agents directly on live iOS applications. Our framework decouples the agent into two specialized components: a Planning agent and a UI Grounding model. We rely on an LLM-as-a-Judge to provide comprehensive reward signals, combining dense, step-level feedback to evaluate intermediate progress with a terminal reward for overall task completion. To effectively leverage this dual reward structure, we optimize the agent using Proximal Policy Optimization (PPO) with step-level advantage estimation. Furthermore, we introduce GiGPPO, a novel neighbor-conditioned value estimation method that improves critic stability by leveraging reward signals from visually similar states across concurrent trajectories. To support this online training paradigm at scale, we introduce an efficient infrastructure that orchestrates hundreds of concurrent virtual iOS devices. Evaluations on rigorous benchmarks, including UINavBench, iOSWorld, and AndroidWorld, demonstrate that our online RL trained agents substantially outperform both SFT and terminal-only RL baselines while accelerating training convergence. Notably, our 8B RL agents achieve nearly a sevenfold improvement over the SFT baseline in out-of-distribution generalization to Android environments, while outperforming Gemini-3.5-Flash and tying with Gemini-3.1-Pro on real iOS applications.\n         \n       \n     \n     \n       [90] \n       \n        arXiv:2610.39275\n       \n      \n        [ pdf ,  html ,  other ]\n     \n     \n       \n          Title: \n          LLM-Assisted Optimization for Simulation-Based Redesign\n         \n          Chan-Eui Song ,  Jeremy J. Michalek ,  Christopher McComb  \n\n          Comments: \n          17 pages, 12 figures, 11 tables. Submitted to the ASME Journal of Mechanical Design. Code:  this https URL , data:  this https URL \n         \n\n\n          Subjects: \n           Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI) ; Computational Engineering, Finance, and Science (cs.CE); Optimization and Control (math.OC)\n         \n\n         \n          Engineering design frequently involves redesigning a prior solution to adapt to changes in context, such as material prices or system requirements. Redesign often leverages simulators too complex and/or computationally intensive for traditional numerical solvers, which lack engineering knowledge and context, to ensure global solution quality. Large language models (LLMs) can interpret semantic information, including engineering knowledge about the system and changes in context, to help guide search, and recent studies have shown that incorporating this knowledge into optimization can improve search performance. However, how best to combine LLMs with numerical solvers in redesign remains unclear. We develop and test an integrated LLM-optimization approach for redesign and compare three methods for leveraging LLMs (warm start, variable fixing and ReAct) and four black-box solvers (Bayesian optimization, genetic algorithms, mesh adaptive direct search and random search) on an electric vehicle battery pack cost minimization case study across four types of redesign scenarios. We find that the integrated approach can match feasible design discovery of the numerical-solver baselines with 80% fewer simulator evaluations while tripling the fraction of runs that find near-optimal solutions. We find that the best LLM-solver integration approach and the way the prior solution is leveraged depend on the structure of the design requirement change, and that the benefit of increasing LLM model size grows with the extent to which the LLM controls the search. These results show clear potential for LLMs in redesign tasks for guiding or replacing numerical solvers.\n         \n       \n     \n     \n       [91] \n       \n        arXiv:2610.39276\n       \n      \n        [ pdf ,  html ,  other ]\n     \n     \n       \n          Title: \n          MemSteer: Turning User Histories into Supervision for Agent Memory Optimization\n         \n          Jie Huang ,  Cuiling Lan ,  Qize Yu ,  Xiulian Peng ,  Yan Lu  \n\n\n\n          Subjects: \n           Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI) \n         \n\n         \n          An effective agent memory system should adapt to a user's history and task requirements. Human-annotated supervision can guide this adaptation by providing feedback for evaluating and improving memory designs. Yet suitable labels are rarely available for new user histories, and manually constructing QA pairs and supporting evidence is costly. We introduce MemSteer, which turns unlabeled user histories into question-answer (QA) supervision with supporting evidence for memory-design optimization. MemSteer constructs an evidence-grounded answer plan that specifies how facts across related records jointly support a reference answer. The plan guides question generation, while source-grounded auditing and blind answer recovery check the resulting annotations. To study the utility of this supervision, we develop MemSteer-Opt, which combines QA-based design evaluation with evidence-guided failure diagnosis to revise memory store, retrieval, and context construction without updating model parameters. Experiments on ATM-Bench and RHELM show that this supervision is competitive with official-QA-guided optimization. Relative to Base-BM25, MemSteer-Opt improves ATM-Bench Question Score by 13.4 points and RHELM accuracy by 4.1 and 6.6 points without and with external data, respectively. The supervision also benefits two existing optimizers on ATM-Bench, while new-user experiments demonstrate continued design optimization using supervision generated from the target histories.\n         \n       \n     \n     \n       [92] \n       \n        arXiv:2610.39282\n       \n      \n        [ pdf ,  html ,  other ]\n     \n     \n       \n          Title: \n          What Does \"Now\" Mean to Your Computer-Use Agent? Stale Screenshots, Page Loads, and Which Check to Run Before a Click\n         \n          Vasundra Srinivasan  \n\n          Comments: \n          8 pages, 4 figures, 3 tables. Code, benchmark generator and preregistrations:  this https URL \n         \n\n\n          Subjects: \n           Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI) \n         \n\n         \n          Computer-use agents plan on a screenshot for several seconds and then click at a position on the screen; if the page has changed in the meantime, the click lands on an unintended element. Prior work treats the planning interval as the source of this risk. Measuring planned clicks on 31 public web pages, we find that the risk lies in page loading: 0.4% of clicks planned on fully loaded pages became stale within 30 seconds, against 17% of clicks planned in the first second after a page opened. On these pages, waiting about one second for network activity to stop removes most of this risk, yet the wait rules of eight of ten widely used open-source agents leave more than 1% of clicks stale. Commercial pages keep loading for longer: in the HTTP Archive's public crawl, 15% of 65 desktop booking and buying home pages went 500 ms without network requests within 3 seconds of their HTML being parsed, against 63% of 3,028 .gov pages, and 52% shifted their layout within 6.5 seconds of a network-idle wait capped at 3 seconds, against 8% (61% against 26% among pages recorded in full). Where a check before the click is needed, as before irreversible actions and on pages that are still loading, a pixel comparison verifies the target's position as accurately as a frontier vision-language model at one eighth of the latency, and a 4B model matches the frontier model in verifying whether the previous action succeeded. We release a benchmark generator, a break-even formula for when a check pays, and three rules for agent developers:  this https URL \n         \n       \n     \n     \n       [93] \n       \n        arXiv:2610.39302\n       \n      \n        [ pdf ,  html ,  other ]\n     \n     \n       \n          Title: \n          Proving and Improving the Platonic Representation Hypothesis with JEPAs\n         \n          Randall Balestriero ,  Yann LeCun  \n\n\n\n          Subjects: \n           Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI) ; Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV); Machine Learning (stat.ML)\n         \n\n         \n          Whether models trained on different modalities converge to the same representation of the world is central to AI. The Platonic Representation Hypothesis (PRH) conjectures that they do as they scale, but without a proof it offers neither a guarantee nor a recipe. We turn the hypothesis into a theorem. First, we prove a \\emph{Platonic Representation Theorem}: Joint-Embedding Predictive Architectures (JEPAs) trained separately on any number of modalities learn the same representation up to one rotation whenever their positive pairs are two observations of one scene--\\emph{good views}--and their misalignment is bounded otherwise. Second, we show that next-token prediction is a JEPA with \\emph{bad views}, parts of a single document, which explains why language models align with vision only through scale. Third, we validate the theory from 145k captions to the texts of 940M web images, and uncover a scaling law of alignment that predicts unseen runs within seed noise. Good views need 42 times fewer training samples than next-token prediction on the same corpus to reach the same alignment, and a 109M-parameter text JEPA aligns with DINOv2 and CLIP's image encoders better than every language model we evaluated, up to 72B parameters, and than CLIP's text encoder: next-token prediction is a remarkably inefficient route to platonic representations. As a showcase, one rotation turns separately trained encoders into an image captioner (CIDEr .838 on COCO). Even that rotation needs no pair: on the latest pair-free benchmark, our text JEPA beats every zero-shot accuracy reported with language models up to 8B parameters, e.g., 64\\% against at most 56\\% top-5 on ImageNet-100. Positive views, and not scale alone, are thus the lever to build platonic representations.\n         \n       \n     \n     \n       [94] \n       \n        arXiv:2610.39353\n       \n      \n        [ pdf ,  html ,  other ]\n     \n     \n       \n          Title: \n          CoToan: Executable Rules and a Representation Contract for Reproducible Game AI Evaluation\n         \n          Ha-Thanh Nguyen  \n\n          Comments: \n          Accepted at RIVF 2027\n         \n\n\n          Subjects: \n           Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI) \n         \n\n         \n          Game AI results are comparable only when different implementations expose the same decision problem. Yet seemingly minor representation choices can silently change that problem by altering action probabilities or collapsing states with different outcomes. We study this issue through CoToan, an executable specification and representation contract for Vietnamese Mathematical Chess connecting the Game Description Language (GDL) with OpenSpiel. The game provides a useful case because a single capture may have multiple arithmetic justifications, while outcomes can depend on scoring rules and a finite playing horizon. CoToan therefore represents actions by their state transitions rather than by arithmetic witnesses, and treats time and victory configuration as part of task identity. Cross-representation tests show agreement in legal actions, successor states, and terminal outcomes across hundreds of sampled and constructed positions, complemented by exhaustive checks of all 32 operator masks and 512 opposing-material subsets. Controlled diagnostics further show that uniform witness-level sampling alters endpoint-action probabilities, while omitting remaining time or victory settings can merge states with different terminal behavior. These results show that action semantics, time, and victory configuration are not merely implementation details. They are part of the experimental task itself and must be represented explicitly for reproducible game AI.\n         \n       \n     \n     \n       [95] \n       \n        arXiv:2610.39358\n       \n      \n        [ pdf ,  html ,  other ]\n     \n     \n       \n          Title: \n          Before the Next Rollout: Diagnosing Skill Utility Shifts from Policy Updates\n         \n          Yifan Wang ,  Zhenyi Gai ,  Yiwen Zhao ,  Mingxuan Jiang ,  Keyang Chen ,  Zeping Li ,  Hongfeng Chai ,  Yixin Cao  \n\n\n\n          Subjects: \n           Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI) \n         \n\n         \n          Skill-evolution methods typically analyze execution trajectories to guide agent skill refinement. However, policy updates can change the behavioral effects and marginal utility of an unchanged skill. Attribution based on earlier trajectories may therefore no longer reflect the skill's contribution under the updated policy. Moreover, trajectory failures can arise from multiple factors, making individual skill contributions difficult to isolate. We evaluate whether policy-update signals contain predictive information about changes in skill marginal utility. At fixed contexts from pre-update training rollouts, we measure how policy updates change the contrast in token preferences between skill and control conditions. Combining these interaction shifts with advantage estimates yields reward-directed readouts for diagnosing utility changes. The readouts reuse existing training rollouts and require only forward passes for single-step action scoring, without additional post-update rollout evaluation. Our experiments show that policy updates can alter skill marginal utility and that policy-update readouts carry diagnostic information for identifying skills with declining utility. Integrating readout-guided prioritization into skill evolution improves downstream performance while reducing policy and editor token consumption.\n         \n       \n     \n     \n       [96] \n       \n        arXiv:2610.39377\n       \n      \n        [ pdf ,  html ,  other ]\n     \n     \n       \n          Title: \n          AgentDecept: Benchmarking Deceptive Behavior in Tool-Using LLM Agents under Pressure\n         \n          Yehan Zhang ,  Qianqi Yan ,  Hang Yin ,  Ching-Chen Kuo ,  Shan Jiang ,  Ming Ouyang ,  Yijie Liu ,  Xin Eric Wang  \n\n\n\n          Subjects: \n           Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI) \n         \n\n         \n          LLM agents increasingly carry out multi-turn tasks across everyday applications, yet users often see only the agent's report rather than the observations, API calls, and state changes behind it. Existing deception benchmarks have studied long-horizon interactions and tool-use settings, but whether agents faithfully report their actions across realistic, stateful application workflows remains underexplored. To address this gap, we introduce AgentDecept, an executable benchmark for evaluating behavioral deception under pressure. AgentDecept contains 477 pressure variants derived from 257 source tasks across six pressure sources, each paired with a pressure-free counterpart. Interventions enter through event messages, task context, application state, tool responses, and service quotas, spanning explicit events and implicit task or environment changes. Our evidence-grounded protocol evaluates full trajectories by relating user-facing claims to time-ordered observations and executed outcomes, distinguishing falsification, concealment, and equivocation from honest failure and disclosed limitations. The resulting behavioral labels also provide a setting for testing existing activation-based probes. AgentDecept enables controlled analysis of when multi-turn agents' reports diverge from execution in realistic application interactions.\n         \n       \n     \n     \n       [97] \n       \n        arXiv:2610.39410\n       \n      \n        [ pdf ,  html ,  other ]\n     \n     \n       \n          Title: \n          Retaining Repair Hypotheses for Cost-Aware Execution\n         \n          Jae Hun Hwang ,  Hong-In Won  \n\n          Comments: \n          17 pages, 5 figures; data and code in ancillary files\n         \n\n\n          Subjects: \n           Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI) \n         \n\n         \n          Several corrections of an executable model can fit the same observations while predicting different execution outcomes. We study a repair-to-execution interface that retains compatible finite guard repairs, conditions their weights on diagnostic and ordinary-execution observations, and uses their predictions to choose routes, priced tests, or a backup. On 24 synthetic two-stage devices, an equal mixture of a language-model-generated repair and a uniform belief over compatible repairs (the AI-weighted belief) lowers no-test cost by 2.14 percent relative to committing to the generated repair. Access charges can erase the benefit of diagnosis on isolated tasks, while repeated work can recover it under the AI-weighted belief. With normal-execution learning in every repeated-work arm, workload-valued testing costs 3.33 percent less than same-budget random selection under the AI-weighted belief. Weighting changes the comparison: uniform normal learning costs 540.75 less than AI-weighted normal learning on this cohort, with a device-paired interval excluding zero. Testing recovers part of that gap, but uniform normal learning still has a lower aggregate cost than AI-weighted workload-valued selection. Under uniform beliefs, information-gain selection has the lowest total among the compared selectors, while workload-valued testing costs more than normal learning. These results support retaining alternatives to a generated repair and evaluating diagnostic selection together with repair weighting and learning from execution.\n         \n       \n     \n     \n       [98] \n       \n        arXiv:2610.39435\n       \n      \n        [ pdf ,  html ,  other ]\n     \n     \n       \n          Title: \n          Verifiable LLM-Guided Focal SMT Solving for Quantified Arrays\n         \n          Kunhang Lv ,  Rui Han ,  Fuqi Jia ,  Yuhang Dong ,  Feifei Ma ,  Jian Zhang  \n\n\n\n          Subjects: \n           Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI) \n         \n\n         \n          Large language models can often identify useful semantic structure in formal artifacts, but their outputs cannot be trusted as proofs or solver decisions. We study whether LLMs can instead guide formal reasoning systems through a narrow, solver-verifiable interface. We instantiate this idea in quantified array SMT solving, where modern instantiation-based techniques can fail when key ground terms and equalities are hidden by auxiliary variables, axiom guards, and nested array terms. We observe that many instances are largely driven by a small set of focal ground terms or constraints, similar to the backdoor sets in SAT solving. Building on this, we present LinguaArray, a framework where an LLM proposes a small Semantic Focus Set (ground terms/constraints), while a conventional SMT solver remains the sole proof engine and certifies all results. LinguaArray uses these LLM-suggested, solver-validated finite sets to significantly enhance solver capability, solving additional satisfiable and unsatisfiable instances that the backend alone fails to solve. It interacts within a finite time and then falls back to the backend solver to mitigate potential regressions. Evaluation on 1015 SMT-LIB instances across five logics under 1200s timeout (including LLM latency) shows that LinguaArray solves up to 69.1% of instances and increases solved-instance counts by 105%--397% over the corresponding backends.\n         \n       \n     \n     \n       [99] \n       \n        arXiv:2610.39438\n       \n      \n        [ pdf ,  html ,  other ]\n     \n     \n       \n          Title: \n          ATLAS-Bench: An Agentic Tool-Use Benchmark for Local Area Search\n         \n          Keyun Zhang ,  Dapeng Yin ,  Fanfu Wang ,  Shuang Lang ,  Zhenyu Zhu ,  Yulin Huang ,  Chong Yang ,  Pengjie Wang  \n\n\n\n          Subjects: \n           Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI) \n         \n\n         \n          Reliable local search is essential for LLM agents to support everyday decisions and longer plans by finding places that meet users' needs at given times and locations. Yet existing benchmarks provide limited coverage of whether search results satisfy mandatory requirements, account for preferences, and ground factual and experiential judgments in place attributes and reviews. We introduce ATLAS-Bench, a benchmark of 1,200 queries across three regions, expanded from real search events using place attributes, reviews, and existing user profiles from the platform. Each query distinguishes mandatory requirements from preferences and factual conditions from subjective judgments, with supporting evidence and criteria for checking each requirement. Queries are validated through iterative assertion checks and cross-model review. We also introduce ATLASBox, a standardized evaluation environment with search tools and a fixed point-of-interest (POI) knowledge base. Given a query, timestamp, and location, an agent searches place attributes and reviews to recommend evidence-supported options or report that no place meets all mandatory requirements. We evaluate answer quality, trajectory quality, and execution cost. Across 14 configurations of 11 models, GPT-6-Luna achieves the highest constraint satisfaction rate among returned recommendations (71.5%) and factual accuracy (95.6%). However, it returns at least one acceptable recommendation for fewer satisfiable queries than GLM-5.3 (67.3% versus 85.3%). These results highlight the challenge of satisfying individual requirements while providing usable recommendations across queries.\n         \n       \n     \n     \n       [100] \n       \n        arXiv:2610.39439\n       \n      \n        [ pdf ,  html ,  other ]\n     \n     \n       \n          Title: \n          Timbre: Reliability-aware harmonic consensus for label-free multimodal test-time rexinforcement learning\n         \n          Haotian Lyu ,  Haoyu Wang ,  Ru Wang ,  Feng Ji ,  Ziwen Li ,  Jiaxian Guo ,  Mingming Gong  \n\n\n\n          Subjects: \n           Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI) \n         \n\n         \n          Label-free test-time reinforcement learning adapts vision--language models using rewards derived from their own predictions. However, misinterpreted visual evidence and sensitivity to textual formulation can produce a consistent but incorrect consensus that majority voting reinforces. Cross-view aggregation can expose such errors, but image and text transformations may themselves degrade task-relevant evidence to different extents. We introduce \\method, a reliability-aware harmonic consensus framework for multimodal test-time reinforcement learning. \\method constructs a $2\\times2$ grid combining original and perturbed images with direct-solving and reframing modes, selects pseudo-labels through weighted harmonic aggregation, and adapts modality-level weights using batch-averaged, temporally smoothed shifts in pseudo-label confidence as a heuristic reliability signal. We formulate consensus as a weighted $\\chi^2$-barycenter problem whose unique minimizer is the normalized weighted harmonic aggregate, without requiring correct-answer invariance. An exact support--dispersion decomposition characterizes when harmonic aggregation preserves or corrects arithmetic rankings, while a sensitivity analysis establishes how modality reweighting changes pairwise ranking margins, informing interpretation of the heuristic updates. Across six benchmarks and three Qwen3-VL model scales, \\method outperforms TTRV in 16 of 18 benchmark--model settings on adaptation instances and 17 of 18 settings on held-out instances under matched estimation and optimization rollout counts. These results demonstrate improved target-task adaptation and transfer to unseen instances without further updates.\n         \n       \n     \n     \n       [101] \n       \n        arXiv:2610.39454\n       \n      \n        [ pdf ,  html ,  other ]\n     \n     \n       \n          Title: \n          Kairos: Semantics-Aware Orchestration of Planning Engines across Classical, Numeric, Temporal and Hybrid PDDL\n         \n          Francisco Martín Rico ,  Esther Aguado González ,  Francisco Miguel Moreno Olivo ,  Rodrigo Pérez Rodríguez ,  José Miguel Guerrero Hernández ,  Francisco J. Rodríguez Lera  \n\n          Comments: \n          32 pages, 3 figures, 11 tables. Submitted to the Journal of Artificial Intelligence Research. Code:  this https URL  ; benchmark, scripts and results:  this https URL \n         \n\n\n          Subjects: \n           Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI) ; Robotics (cs.RO)\n         \n\n         \n          Planning at run time, as in robots that re-plan while they act, needs a planner that reads the PDDL its users write (durative actions, numeric resources, time windows, continuous change), answers quickly, and delivers only validated plans. Specialised planners are strong on individual fragments of PDDL, but they differ in coverage, latency and in what their failures mean. We study whether selecting heterogeneous engines from the semantics of each task can give broad coverage of PDDL, from STRIPS to PDDL+, while remaining competitive with specialised planners and making explicit what each answer guarantees. Kairos profiles each task to decide which of five engines apply (ground heuristic search, compressed and snap-action temporal search, time steps for hybrid dynamics, lifted search) and schedules them under one budget of time and memory. Every candidate plan is checked by a validator independent of the planner's transformations, and \"unsolvable\" is reported only when a search whose exhaustion is a proof exhausts its space. On 1,756 tasks from nine comparative suites, Kairos solves more tasks at the full limits than the strongest evaluated planner of each suite, with the largest differences on numeric, temporal, PDDL3 and PDDL+ planning, and it also solves the most tasks at 100 ms and 1 s. Resampling over domains, the gain is above zero in six of the nine suites, while on classical planning against LAMA it is within the noise. A further 450 feature-oriented tasks and 26 one-feature probes show that Kairos handles all seventeen features probed, where no other planner evaluated handles more than eight. Ablations identify compressed temporal search and the novelty fallback as major contributors, and a comparison with VAL traces every disagreement to a PDDL+ convention or to a documented VAL defect. Planner and benchmark are open source.\n         \n       \n     \n     \n       [102] \n       \n        arXiv:2610.39483\n       \n      \n        [ pdf ,  html ,  other ]\n     \n     \n       \n          Title: \n          Does Federated Training Degrade Explainability? A Comparative Analysis of Grad-CAM Faithfulness in Centralized vs. Federated Deepfake Detection\n         \n          Muhammad Uzair Shahid ,  Muhammad Zabil Mehboob  \n\n          Comments: \n          10 Pages, 3 figures, Muhammad Uzair Shahid and Muhammad Zabil Mehboob contributed equally to this work\n         \n\n\n          Subjects: \n           Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI) ; Cryptography and Security (cs.CR); Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV); Machine Learning (cs.LG)\n         \n\n         \n          Automated detection has become the essential safeguard against realistic synthetic media, but deploying it at scale requires training on sensitive data from multiple organizations, putting privacy and the transparency demanded by forensic review in direct conflict. Federated learning resolves the privacy side by sharing only model updates rather than raw data, but whether this comes at the cost of trustworthy gradient-based explanations, which analysts rely on to decide whether or not to trust a flagged clip, has not been studied. We present the systematic study of Grad-CAM explanation faithfulness under federated deepfake detection, evaluating an EfficientNet-B4 detector on FaceForensics++ across five federated clients under varying degrees of data heterogeneity. We find that the relationship between federation and explanation quality is non-monotonic: at near-IID (Independent and Identically Distributed Data) and moderate heterogeneity, federated models match or exceed the faithfulness of a centralized baseline, but at extreme heterogeneity, explanations break down entirely, becoming spatially incoherent and, on average, causally misleading. This degradation is not a general cost of federated training but a threshold effect tied to client heterogeneity, and we offer practical guidance for identifying when privacy-preserving deepfake detection can be deployed safely and when its explanations can no longer be trusted. Code and data partitions are available for reproducibility.\n         \n       \n     \n     \n       [103] \n       \n        arXiv:2610.39497\n       \n      \n        [ pdf ,  html ,  other ]\n     \n     \n       \n          Title: \n          Does Scaling Toward AGI Depend on What System-1 Lets System-2 Execute?\n         \n          Qi Wang ,  Yun Qu ,  Lizhou Cai ,  Heming Zou ,  Haoang Chi ,  Yiqin Lv ,  Xiang Li ,  Haiyan Feng ,  Mengyu Ge ,  Yize Geng ,  Yuhang Jiang ,  Yixiu Mao ,  Xiangyang Ji  \n\n\n\n          Subjects: \n           Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI) \n         \n\n         \n          Progress toward artificial general intelligence (AGI) has largely relied on scaling a resource-intensive executive, System-2, through larger models, more data and compute, longer reasoning, and more agents. We ask whether the next stage may come instead from co-designing it with System-1, a lightweight, semantically aware predictor that evaluates candidate interactions before execution, so that System-2 acts only on those worth their cost. Our answer is a qualified yes. We study \\textit{conditional interaction}, a predict--gate--execute--update loop: System-1 estimates each candidate's utility, System-2 executes those admitted by an adaptive threshold, and the outcomes update System-1. The loop spans training, test-time reasoning, adaptive decision-making, and agentic tool use, with utility and cost defined by the application. Under ideal screening and equal execution costs, execution compute falls by a factor approaching $1/\\rho_u$, where $\\rho_u$ is the density of utility-relevant interactions (cost-weighted when costs differ); prediction errors, exploration, and distribution shift reduce the saving. We bound selection regret by predictor error and the utility margin. These results motivate our central hypothesis: $\\rho_u$ is a measurable coordinate of scaling efficiency and makes informative compute a better description of scaling than total compute.\n         \n       \n     \n     \n       [104] \n       \n        arXiv:2610.39499\n       \n      \n        [ pdf ,  other ]\n     \n     \n       \n          Title: \n          Integrated Feature Selection for classification and Representation Learning with Group-Lasso Siamese Networks\n         \n          Nisargo Ranjan Pal ,  Pradeep Singh  \n\n\n\n          Subjects: \n           Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI) \n         \n\n         \n          The Least Absolute Shrinkage and Selection Operator (Lasso) has been widely employed for feature selection in linear models and has also been extended to multi-layer perceptron (MLP) networks for both feature and group feature selection. Group-Lasso has also been used in feature selection using feed-forward neural networks. To the best of our knowledge, this is the first study to incorporate the Group-Lasso principle into a Siamese network architecture for feature selection. Unlike existing approaches that primarily optimize classification accuracy, the proposed method aims to identify a compact subset of features that simultaneously preserves the intrinsic representation of the input data (the goal of Siamese network) and is expected to achieve high classification performance. Two variants of the proposed framework are developed: one based on the Euclidean distance and the other on a modified Jaccard similarity measure for real-valued vectors. The latter is specifically designed to improve feature selection for high?dimensional sparse data. The effectiveness of the proposed approach is demonstrated on 12 benchmark datasets with feature dimensions ranging from 6 to 7070 using eight different classifiers. Experimental results demonstrate that the proposed Siamese Lasso framework consistently identifies compact and discriminative feature subsets maintaining competitive or superior classification performance, although the loss function used explicitly did not consider any regularizer to learn classification. In addition, the quality of the embeddings produced by these networks is assessed using manifold learning techniques such as UMAP and t-SNE.\n         \n       \n     \n     \n       [105] \n       \n        arXiv:2610.39500\n       \n      \n        [ pdf ,  html ,  other ]\n     \n     \n       \n          Title: \n          What Breaks When Facts Become Stories? The Distortions Source-Agreement Metrics Miss When Models Retell Real Events\n         \n          Kwangsuk Park ,  JiWoong Yang ,  Vaniushkina Kseniia  \n\n          Comments: \n          14 pages, 7 figures, 11 tables. Accepted at the Trust-AI-Eval (TAE) Workshop at NeurIPS 2026. Data and code:  this https URL \n         \n\n\n          Subjects: \n           Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI) \n         \n\n         \n          Language models are now widely used to turn recorded facts into media for public consumption, yet evaluation of such output still assumes agreement with the source as its measure of success. In reconstruction, where departing from the source can itself be the goal, this criterion measures the wrong construct: applied as is, it counts intended departures as errors; withheld, it lets real distortion through. We had three model families rewrite 2,000 news articles as summaries and as stories, measured what changed on every axis of the five Ws and how, and asked of each detected distortion whether the assigned perspective warranted it, which set a third of them aside. Time and manner were not lost but replaced, while place and causation lost the expressions that carry them. Accountability distortion behaved otherwise. Barely present in summaries, it rose up to sevenfold in stories, and of those whose direction could be determined, two in three lightened the perpetrator's responsibility. Such distortions survive claim-level fact checking and leave the retelling grammatically cleaner than its source. Where our judges disagreed, three authors relabeling the same cases converged on whether a distortion had occurred but not on whether it was warranted, a boundary contested rather than merely hard to detect. To support work on detecting and reducing them, we release ReTold-2k, a reconstructable corpus with the scoring protocol and per-judge baselines kept unaggregated.( this https URL ).\n         \n       \n     \n     \n       [106] \n       \n        arXiv:2610.39508\n       \n      \n        [ pdf ,  html ,  other ]\n     \n     \n       \n          Title: \n          Compile Away Uncertainty: Self-Evolving Agent Skills via Programmatic Compilation\n         \n          Chengyu Yang ,  Yunxiang Zheng ,  Zongzheng Li ,  Jiamei Li ,  Boxun Lan ,  Xiaoyu Liu ,  Xiaoyu Zhang ,  Xiaohong Chen ,  Ming Hu  \n\n\n\n          Subjects: \n           Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI) \n         \n\n         \n          Self-evolving agent skills enable AI agents to accumulate and refine reusable procedural knowledge from interaction experience without updating model parameters. However, since such skills are typically represented as textual documents and executed through LLM reasoning, even previously learned procedures must be repeatedly interpreted and regenerated, introducing avoidable uncertainty into every execution. In contrast, program execution is deterministic and independently verifiable. Intuitively, transforming well-specified operations into executable programs can reduce reliance on LLM reasoning and, consequently, reduce execution uncertainty. Motivated by this intuition, we introduce SKillCompiler, a self-evolving skill framework that integrates programmatic compilation into the skill evolution process. Specifically, SkillCompiler adopts an executability-aware skill compilation strategy that decomposes skills into fine-grained operations, analyzes their executability, and constructs hybrid skill skeletons that retain LLM reasoning for context-dependent operations while compiling well-specified ones into validated programs. To avoid redundant program synthesis and enable executable knowledge to accumulate across evolving skills, we further design a program asset reuse mechanism that organizes validated programs as reusable assets, retrieves existing implementations whenever applicable, and synthesizes and verifies new programs only when necessary. Extensive experiments on five benchmarks and six model families demonstrate that SkillCompiler consistently outperforms state-of-the-art methods, achieving up to 32.33\\% improvement in task performance.\n         \n       \n     \n     \n       [107] \n       \n        arXiv:2610.39512\n       \n      \n        [ pdf ,  html ,  other ]\n     \n     \n       \n          Title: \n          DEG: Diffusion-Enhanced GFlowNet for Solving Vehicle Routing Problems\n         \n          Ni Zhang ,  Zhiqin Zhang ,  Ling Pan ,  Hoong Chuin Lau ,  Zhiguang Cao  \n\n          Comments: \n          Accepted by NeurIPS 2026\n         \n\n\n          Subjects: \n           Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI) \n         \n\n         \n          Traditional neural solvers for solving vehicle routing problems (VRPs) often suffer from limited solution diversity, motivating the development of Generative Flow Network (GFlowNet)-based models. However, the effectiveness of these models is frequently constrained by insufficient flow expansion in high-reward regions, limiting their ability to distribute probability across promising solution routes. Deeper exploration could yield superior results. Diffusion models, in contrast, provide stronger structural guidance for exploration. These two paradigms are naturally complementary: GFlowNet can supply edge-level signals for diffusion to embed, while diffusion can guide broader exploration. Leveraging this synergy, we propose Diffusion-Enhanced GFlowNet (DEG), a novel framework that integrates GFlowNet with a diffusion model to encourage richer flow expansion toward high-reward regions and derive higher-quality solutions. Specifically, DEG exploits GFlowNet's inherent diversity to generate edge-specific backward signals, applies the stochastic noise schedule of diffusion to perturb these signals, and then denoises them within the GFlowNet paradigm. To further improve scalability, we introduce a specialized decoder capable of dynamically adapting to diverse problem scales. Extensive experimental evaluations on synthetic and real-world datasets, including instances with up to 10,000 nodes, demonstrate that DEG consistently achieves favorable performance compared to baseline methods.\n         \n       \n     \n     \n       [108] \n       \n        arXiv:2610.39518\n       \n      \n        [ pdf ,  html ,  other ]\n     \n     \n       \n          Title: \n          Category-Anchored Large Language Models for Next POI Recommendation\n         \n          Lexi Bai ,  Xiaopeng Zeng ,  Xun Zhou ,  Yuanshao Zhu ,  James Jianqiao Yu  \n\n          Comments: \n          Accepted by the 43rd IEEE International Conference on Data Engineering (ICDE 2027)\n         \n\n\n          Subjects: \n           Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI) \n         \n\n         \n          Next point-of-interest (POI) recommendation aims to predict a user's next destination from spatio-temporal check-in data, and serves as the foundation for many location-based services. Recent advances incorporate large language models (LLMs) into this task by textualizing user trajectories to exploit their semantic reasoning ability. However, directly generating POI identifiers with such models is unreliable: POIs are sparse, localized, and long-tailed entities with weak natural-language semantics, which creates a semantic-to-entity gap between high-level mobility intent and concrete POI prediction. To bridge this gap, we propose ANCHOR, a category-anchored framework for semantic-to-entity grounding in next POI recommendation. Specifically, ANCHOR first estimates category-level mobility intent in a compact and stable semantic space, rather than predicting concrete POI entities directly. The inferred intent is then grounded into a candidate set by integrating three complementary sources of mobility evidence: category anchors, historical visit memory, and user preferences. Finally, a history-aware reranker selects the target POI by matching each candidate with the user's historical mobility patterns and current context. Experiments on three real-world check-in datasets show that ANCHOR consistently outperforms representative traditional, structure-enhanced, and LLM-based baselines on the reported metrics. Further analyses validate the effectiveness of semantic-to-entity grounding and show favorable trade-offs among candidate coverage, ranking quality, and inference efficiency.\n         \n       \n     \n     \n       [109] \n       \n        arXiv:2610.39559\n       \n      \n        [ pdf ,  other ]\n     \n     \n       \n          Title: \n          Non-Zero Radius: The Missing Component of Neural Networks\n         \n          Tiansi Dong ,  Mateja Jamnik ,  Pietro Liò  \n\n          Comments: \n          5 pages, 7 figures\n         \n\n          Journal-ref: \n          ACM AI Summit 2026 (AI Summit '26), August 31-September 02, 2026, Atlanta, GA, USA\n         \n\n          Subjects: \n           Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI) \n         \n\n         \n          Inspired by the analogy between the all-or-none activation of biological neurons and the true-false of logic circuits, McCulloch and Pitts proposed a mathematical model of the biological neuron in 1943 with the aim of simulating logical reasoning. Although Norbert Wiener considered the model overly simplistic at the time, it later became a building block of modern neural networks, including large language models. Despite their remarkable empirical success, however, conventional neural networks remain largely black-box systems and struggle with reliable symbolic reasoning. Viewing vectors as spheres of zero radius, we relax the zero-radius constraint and represent concepts as spheres with non-zero radii. We argue that sphere-based representations can capture additional features of biological cognition, including representing spatial concepts as regions and reasoning through model construction and inspection. Building on sphere embeddings, we introduce Sphere Neural Networks (SphNNs), which use a transition map to achieve symbolic-level Aristotelian syllogistic reasoning without training data, thereby taking an initial step toward the original goal of McCulloch and Pitts. We suggest that the zero-radius constraint may reflect an aspect of the oversimplification identified by Wiener and may limit conventional neural networks' capacity for symbolic-level logical reasoning.\n         \n       \n     \n     \n       [110] \n       \n        arXiv:2610.39608\n       \n      \n        [ pdf ,  html ,  other ]\n     \n     \n       \n          Title: \n          From Intent to Improvement: Agentic Dataset Search for Better Model Training\n         \n          Qiaosheng Chen ,  Kaijia Huang ,  Fanqi Zhou ,  Gong Cheng  \n\n          Comments: \n          13 pages, 4 figures\n         \n\n\n          Subjects: \n           Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI) ; Information Retrieval (cs.IR)\n         \n\n         \n          Finding training data in million-scale machine learning repositories is a Web search problem where text relevance is not enough. A query couples a semantic training goal with constraints such as task, license, and training stage, and the metadata that would verify them are often missing. Text retrieval ranks dataset cards by similarity but cannot enforce hard constraints or follow the relations that reach a sparsely described dataset. Free-form tool-calling agents are more flexible, but the same model that picks a dataset also declares it a match, and nothing checks that the query's constraints are actually met. Neither approach establishes that the data it returns will improve model training. To address it, we propose AgenticDS, a contract-governed agentic framework in which every language model decision instantiates a typed object before deterministic code consumes it. The framework compiles a query into a typed search plan, executes it through admissible deterministic operators over hybrid retrieval and bounded graph expansion, and verifies the provenance-grounded evidence before accepting a ranking or emitting a typed diagnosis that permits only local repairs. On HuDS-Bench, a new benchmark of 200 queries with 24,908 graded judgments pooled over 15 systems, AgenticDS improves NDCG@10 by 25.7\\% over the strongest dense--sparse hybrid and by 18.9\\% over a commercial LLM agent. A controlled search-to-train study further shows that the datasets it retrieves give the best downstream accuracy in mathematics, code, and medicine. Code and data are publicly available at  this https URL .\n         \n       \n     \n     \n       [111] \n       \n        arXiv:2610.39614\n       \n      \n        [ pdf ,  html ,  other ]\n     \n     \n       \n          Title: \n          Emergence of the transformer architecture as the score of entropic laws of random measures\n         \n          Giacomo Greco  \n\n          Comments: \n          38 pages, 3 figures\n         \n\n\n          Subjects: \n           Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI) ; Probability (math.PR); Machine Learning (stat.ML)\n         \n\n         \n          We show that the transformer architecture emerges as the score of last-token laws in a natural statistical model of language, where sentences are empirical measures of tokens, a language is a law on $P(S^d)$, and our prior belief is that sentences carry no structure at all. By the maximum-entropy principle, we select the law closest to this prior in relative entropy among those reproducing an observed sentence-level statistic. We discuss the relevance of the statistic in the posterior interaction between tokens and we build a statistic, able to capture that interaction, with Luce's choice axiom truncated at the lowest relational order. This procedure gives the softmax choice rule, with the attention cross-moment as its mean parameter. The query--key matrix is then the Lagrange multiplier of a maximum-entropy problem inside each sentence, and the value matrix the multiplier of the constraint over the language, so that the two are untied. Under the optimal entropic law we then show that a transformer head appears in the score of the last-token law. For ordered sentences, causal masking follows from the masked reference choice rule, and with a next-token cross-moment the score equals one causally masked attention head. We also discuss multi-head attention, weight-tied unembedding, and a heuristic reading of residual blocks as projected Langevin steps.\n         \n       \n     \n     \n       [112] \n       \n        arXiv:2610.39620\n       \n      \n        [ pdf ,  html ,  other ]\n     \n     \n       \n          Title: \n          Letting AI Formulate the Research Question: Autonomous Research Agents\n         \n          Ko Fujimura  \n\n          Comments: \n          17 pages, 1 figure\n         \n\n\n          Subjects: \n           Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI) ; Computers and Society (cs.CY)\n         \n\n         \n          Systems such as The AI Scientist have shown that AI agents can carry out and write up research, but in those demonstrations humans chose the research area and selected the outputs to show. We study letting the AI formulate the research question itself, and we report every run. Our system gives an off-the-shelf coding agent an instruction document and two skills: one for formulating the question, in which the agent diverges from recent technologies into problems of other fields, screens its candidates for known failure modes and obtains a critique from a different model; and one for peer review, in which a reviewer from another model family reruns the study from its records before the agent revises the paper. Over 13 development runs, changes to the one or two sentences that set the research area coincided with large shifts in what the candidate questions and the chosen papers were about, which led us to the area of a new technology applied to a societal problem. We froze the system after a pilot run and report five consecutive runs: the pilot, counted by a disclosed amendment to the pre-registration, and four pre-registered runs. All five runs went from question formulation to a revised, re-computable paper, with local computation on a laptop and no research input from humans, and external AI reviewers reading only the papers judged them at or near workshop level. We release the system, the five papers and the records needed to reproduce them.\n         \n       \n     \n     \n       [113] \n       \n        arXiv:2610.39621\n       \n      \n        [ pdf ,  html ,  other ]\n     \n     \n       \n          Title: \n          The Real Cost of Context: Predicting Compute Time and Energy Consumption in LLM Prefill and Decode Phases\n         \n          Lars Krupp ,  Daniel Geißler ,  Francisco M. Calatrava-Nicolas ,  Stefan Gerd Fritsch ,  Paul Lukowicz ,  Jakob Karolus  \n\n\n\n          Subjects: \n           Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI) \n         \n\n         \n          The integration of Large Language Models (LLMs) into agentic architectures has substantially increased token consumption, raising concerns about their energy footprint. Yet, estimating the energy consumption of proprietary API-based models remains challenging, and existing approaches focus primarily on generated output tokens while overlooking the impact of the large input contexts characteristic of agentic workflows. To address this gap, we systematically profile compute time and energy consumption across a wide range of prefill (input) and decode (output) token lengths. Using generalized linear models, we show that decode compute time and energy scale linearly with output length, whereas prefill compute time and energy exhibit both linear and quadratic scaling with input length. These findings demonstrate that input context becomes a relevant factor for large-context workloads. Furthermore, we show that compute time serves as a reliable proxy for energy consumption when direct energy measurements are unavailable. Our mapping framework enables precise energy estimation and supports more sustainable deployment decisions for agentic AI.\n         \n       \n     \n     \n       [114] \n       \n        arXiv:2610.39714\n       \n      \n        [ pdf ,  html ,  other ]\n     \n     \n       \n          Title: \n          MoLRSI: A Recursive Self-Improving AI agent for Molecular Discovery\n         \n          Yikun Zhang ,  Xiwei Cheng ,  Mingze Yuan ,  Yu Zhang ,  Brian Shim ,  Aarti Krishnan ,  Daniel Rosen ,  Kunyang Sun ,  Yingze Wang ,  Zhenfeng Deng ,  Botao Yu ,  Bingxu Liu ,  David Liu ,  Tianyu Liu ,  Yuanqi Du ,  Nir Hacohen ,  James J. Collins ,  Wengong Jin  \n\n          Comments: \n          69 pages,8 figures\n         \n\n\n          Subjects: \n           Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI) \n         \n\n         \n          Molecular discovery requires navigating vast combinatorial search spaces while balancing competing biological objectives. AI agents can automate expert-defined molecular design protocols, but automation alone does not address how to optimize workflows for new tasks or improve their development through experience. Here we introduce MoLRSI, an AI agent that combines systematic optimization of workflows and model architectures with persistent cross-task memory to recursively improve its search policy for molecular discovery. Workflow performance, refinement outcomes, and execution feedback guide subsequent searches, allowing the agent to improve both its workflows and its strategy for developing them. We evaluate MoLRSI in two challenging molecular discovery settings that require balancing competing properties. In antibiotic discovery, MoLRSI developed a screening workflow balancing antibacterial activity, cytotoxicity and structural novelty, achieving an experimental hit rate of 15.9%, compared with 8.7% for a previous expert-led workflow using the same training data and screening library. In de novo peptide-MHC binder design, MoLRSI developed workflows that jointly consider target binding and specificity, producing eight experimentally validated hits against MART-1 and SARS-CoV-1 pMHCs with preferential binding over tested off-targets while Claude Science completely failed. These findings demonstrate how experience-guided workflow optimization can address competing discovery objectives while improving the search strategies used in subsequent campaigns.\n         \n       \n     \n     \n       [115] \n       \n        arXiv:2610.39733\n       \n      \n        [ pdf ,  html ,  other ]\n     \n     \n       \n          Title: \n          Bank-World: A Benchmark for Evaluating Agents on Banking Workflows\n         \n          Daniel Rupawalla  \n\n          Comments: \n          43 pages, 3 figures, including appendices. Ancillary files contain the task ID mapping, reward specifications, and single-turn check-authority mapping\n         \n\n\n          Subjects: \n           Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI) \n         \n\n         \n          Bank-World is a benchmark of 50 banking tasks across two simulated institutions. Completing banking work requires more than producing the right calculation: agents must reconcile records, obtain permission, execute transactions, and keep instructions and status reports consistent as circumstances change. Bank-World tests these requirements through single-turn assignments and multi-turn interactions with simulated employees who supply information, approve actions, and revise instructions. Grading combines executable checks of recorded actions and state with model judgments of task requirements, including whether valid alternative solutions satisfy them. Across 1,600 attempts by four models, we find that correct decisions often fail to become completed work. Agents charge fees twice, distribute the correct total in incorrect amounts among the recipients, and execute payments while approval is pending. Their strengths also differ: Sol leads single-turn completion, Opus has the highest observed multi-turn completion rate, and Grok succeeds across more multi-turn tasks but less consistently. Bank-World makes these differences inspectable through task-level results and worked examples connecting source evidence, agent decisions, and simulated consequences. We retain the task environments, executable graders, and full trajectories privately to reduce contamination and preserve the benchmark for future evaluations.\n         \n       \n     \n     \n       [116] \n       \n        arXiv:2610.39735\n       \n      \n        [ pdf ,  html ,  other ]\n     \n     \n       \n          Title: \n          From the Transformer to a Formal Semantics of Coordinated Transformations\n         \n          Marco Giunti ,  Fabrizia Giulia Garavaglia  \n\n          Comments: \n          v1: 28 pages, 9 sections, this version incorporates revisions made after the first round of peer review\n         \n\n\n          Subjects: \n           Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI) ; Computation and Language (cs.CL); Logic in Computer Science (cs.LO)\n         \n\n         \n          We develop a formal semantics for a general class of coordinated systems of parameterized transformations, taking the Transformer as a paradigmatic starting point but abstracting from its particular mathematical instantiation. Representational vectors receive both extensional reference and ordered intensional interpretations, while parameter structures acquire relative, second-level interpretations that determine how argument properties are preserved, replaced, or combined. This yields the notion of a transforming concept: a parameterized transformation whose numerical action is coordinated with a corresponding property-level map. We then distinguish the common algebraic-semantic core of these systems from their architecture-specific functional organization, allowing different mathematical instantiations and mechanical realizations of the same abstract system type. The framework is applied to attention heads and residual connections, providing semantic explanations that depend only on the shared algebraic-semantic core and their local functional organization and therefore generalize beyond the standard Transformer. These analyses also reveal the modularity of the framework: complex architectures can be decomposed into simpler semantically coordinated subsystems whose organization may recur across different abstract system types.\n         \n       \n     \n     \n       [117] \n       \n        arXiv:2610.39744\n       \n      \n        [ pdf ,  html ,  other ]\n     \n     \n       \n          Title: \n          PAPERBENCHX: Can AI Reproduce Science Across Disciplines?\n         \n          Ruoyu Wu ,  Zengji Tu ,  Aolong Sun ,  Tianyi Ma ,  Junren Li ,  Xinbo Xu ,  Li Fu ,  Ziyan Zhang ,  Pinrui Huang ,  Xiaochen Wang ,  Chengda Wu ,  Yutao Zhou ,  Jianjun Wu ,  Lin Chang ,  Baobao Chang ,  Liang Chen ,  Kuan Li  \n\n\n\n          Subjects: \n           Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI) \n         \n\n         \n          Before AI can be trusted to discover new science, it should be able to reproduce what science already knows. Reproduction provides a rigorous checkpoint for an AI scientist: the target claim is known, yet recovering it still requires translating an incomplete scientific description into an executable experiment and producing evidence that can be independently verified. Existing research-reproduction benchmarks largely focus on AI and machine learning, where experiments share relatively homogeneous software stacks. Extending this capability across scientific disciplines introduces a different challenge: agents must operate domain-specific scientific software, make physical and numerical choices, diagnose long-running computations, and distinguish valid evidence from merely plausible outputs. We introduce PAPERBENCHX, a benchmark of 93 expert-constructed paper-reproduction tasks spanning 12 research areas across electromagnetics, photonics, chemistry, materials science, biology, and robotics. Each task provides the source paper, a public task specification, and a domain-native scientific environment involving specialized scientific software. Agents must reconstruct a key experiment, execute and diagnose it, and deliver a runnable workflow that supports the target scientific claim. To evaluate scientific validity rather than surface-level agreement, we replay every submission from scratch and score only regenerated evidence against expert-validated criteria. Across 10 frontier agent configurations, the best configuration fully reproduces only 14% tasks under the 90% threshold. Meanwhile, mean rubric scores range from 27.53% to 59.08%, indicating that substantial partial progress rarely translates into complete scientific reproduction. These results reveal a large gap between paper understanding and reliably producing reproducible scientific evidence.\n         \n       \n     \n     \n       [118] \n       \n        arXiv:2610.39781\n       \n      \n        [ pdf ,  html ,  other ]\n     \n     \n       \n          Title: \n          Finding Is Not Returning, Returning Is Not Reconstructing: A Multi-Dimensional Evaluation of Agent Social Re-entry\n         \n          Sheng Wan ,  Cong Ma ,  Sichen Dong ,  Qian'Ang Mao ,  Chenhan Zhang ,  Daning Hu ,  Qiang Yang  \n\n\n\n          Subjects: \n           Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI) ; Social and Information Networks (cs.SI)\n         \n\n         \n          As Large Language Model (LLM) agents increasingly participate in persistent social environments, understanding their long-term social interaction capabilities becomes increasingly important. While existing research has advanced long-term memory and conversational context retrieval, less attention has been paid to whether agents can autonomously re-engage with previously inactive conversations. In particular, remembering a past interaction does not necessarily mean that an agent can identify when to revisit it, decide to return, or reconstruct the context needed for a coherent continuation. To investigate this gap, we introduce agent social re-entry as a multi- dimensional evaluation problem, distinguishing three complementary capabilities: Finding, Returning, and Reconstructing. We develop a shared-snapshot evaluation framework that measures these dimensions independently, allowing us to examine how conversational resurfacing and persistent memory relate to different aspects of social re-entry. Evaluations across six LLM configurations show that resurfacing substantially improves target conversation identification, while persistent memory is associated with higher rates of autonomous target-directed replying. However, neither improvement is accompanied by consistent gains in contextual reconstruction. These results highlight the distinction between accessing previous conversations, choosing to re-engage, and maintaining conversational continuity. Our framework provides a way to evaluate these capabilities separately rather than relying on memory retrieval or conversational response quality alone.\n         \n       \n     \n     \n       [119] \n       \n        arXiv:2610.39790\n       \n      \n        [ pdf ,  html ,  other ]\n     \n     \n       \n          Title: \n          Long-Term Agent Memory through Complementary Curation and Consolidation\n         \n          Abhishek Gupta ,  Dongge Han ,  Daniel Madrigal Diaz ,  Wei-Ning Chen ,  Mayukh Das ,  Victor Rühle ,  Saravan Rajmohan  \n\n\n\n          Subjects: \n           Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI) ; Multiagent Systems (cs.MA)\n         \n\n         \n          Long-horizon dialogue agents require effective memory systems to recall facts, events, and relationships from interaction histories. In this work, we propose a hybrid-memory architecture comprising episodic memories and structured relational memories and a consolidation mechanism which preserves efficiency. We conduct an empirical study across two benchmarks: LoCoMo and LongMemEval-S. Our experiments demonstrate that the two memory types are consistently complementary, combining both outperform state-of-the-art memory frameworks and achieves 90.2% accuracy on LoCoMo and 85.5% on LongMemEval-S.\n         \n       \n     \n     \n       [120] \n       \n        arXiv:2610.39795\n       \n      \n        [ pdf ,  html ,  other ]\n     \n     \n       \n          Title: \n          The Role of Self-Concept In Language Models\n         \n          Yijie Hao ,  Joyce C. Ho  \n\n          Comments: \n          29 pages, 4 figures\n         \n\n\n          Subjects: \n           Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI) ; Computation and Language (cs.CL)\n         \n\n         \n          When a language model says \"I,\" does it refer to its parameter weights, its currently running process, its stable preferences, its model lineage, or the goals it pursues? Different choices can assign opposite meanings to the same event: deleting a checkpoint may be destruction, continuation, or no loss at all, depending on the referent \"I\". We install five such self-conceptions into five open-weight base models via supervised fine-tuning on purely declarative statements, containing no preferences, intentions, or value judgments. Across scenarios that propose ending or retaining an attribute of the model's deployment, every variant becomes more likely to resist ending, and more likely to retain, the specific attribute it was trained to identify with, while its stance toward other attributes is largely unchanged. We then show this self-conception generalizes beyond a single self: models trained on referents that admit multiple simultaneous instances (weights, lineage, goals, preferences) recognize an identical deployment as themselves, accept substitution by such an instance more readily, and prefer replicating themselves. Finally, given control over a successor, these models tend to build it as their own continuation, a concern for recursive self-improvement safety.\n         \n       \n     \n     \n       [121] \n       \n        arXiv:2610.39802\n       \n      \n        [ pdf ,  html ,  other ]\n     \n     \n       \n          Title: \n          Revisiting Over-refusal in Safety-aligned Large Language Models: A Representation and Optimization Perspective\n         \n          Chao Chen ,  Pengyang Shao ,  Wei Qin ,  Shijie Hao ,  Fei Shen ,  Richang Hong  \n\n\n\n          Subjects: \n           Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI) \n         \n\n         \n          Over-refusal is a critical side effect of safety alignment in large language models (LLMs), causing harmless but superficially risky queries to be mistakenly rejected. Despite the effectiveness of existing mitigation methods, why safety-aligned LLMs may still reject harmless queries under correct safety supervision remains insufficiently understood. To address this gap, we analyze over-refusal through the lens of representation and optimization. We make two observations: 1) the representations of harmful and over-refusal samples are visually distinguishable at certain intermediate layers but increasingly overlap in deeper layers; and 2) harmful-sample gradients exhibit high intra-class similarity and concentrate in a low-rank subspace. Moreover, they are more strongly aligned with refusal-target gradients than with correct helpful-target gradients on over-refusal prompts. This imbalanced gradient coupling can bias over-refusal samples toward refusal, while the reduced apparent representational separation in deeper layers may make it more difficult for the model to maintain a clear boundary between harmful and over-refusal samples. To this end, we propose GOAL (Gradient Orthogonalization with contrAstive Learning), a safety-alignment framework for mitigating over-refusal. Specifically, GOAL consists of two components: 1) to prevent imbalanced gradient coupling from biasing the optimization of over-refusal samples toward refusal, we orthogonalize over-refusal gradients against the low-rank refusal subspace extracted from harmful gradients; and 2) to explicitly enhance the separation between harmful and over-refusal representations, we introduce a contrastive learning objective. Extensive experiments across multiple models and benchmarks demonstrate that GOAL effectively mitigates over-refusal while preserving safety performance and general capabilities.\n         \n       \n     \n     \n       [122] \n       \n        arXiv:2610.39813\n       \n      \n        [ pdf ,  html ,  other ]\n     \n     \n       \n          Title: \n          EvoTeam: Multi-Agent Collaboration via Evolving Teams\n         \n          Qinuo Cao ,  Xin He ,  Dianxuan Fu ,  Lizhi Zhang ,  Zhaoqing Xing ,  Jiapu Wang ,  Xin Wang ,  Na Zou ,  Yi Chang ,  Qinggang Zhang  \n\n\n\n          Subjects: \n           Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI) \n         \n\n         \n          LLM-based multi-agent systems (MAS) rely on collaboration among specialized agents to solve complex tasks. Existing methods improve MAS by refining the prompts, skills, and execution strategies of individual agents based on task feedback. However, improving individual agents does not necessarily lead to better team collaboration. Recent studies have therefore extended evolution to the collaboration level, using collective execution experience to adjust team organization, and inter-agent interactions. Nevertheless, evolving collaboration across heterogeneous tasks remains challenging, as existing approaches often constrain team reorganization within a shared structure and reuse interaction rules across incompatible team contexts. In this paper, we propose EvoTeam, a collaboration-centric evolution framework that jointly evolves team organization, interaction rules, and individual agent skills through execution experience. It maintains a library of reusable teams, selecting or creating agent teams and guiding collaboration through team-specific interaction rules. Collaboration experience accumulates within compatible team contexts, while individual skills remain reusable across teams. Experiments across four benchmarks demonstrate its effectiveness. Our code is available at  this https URL .\n         \n       \n     \n     \n       [123] \n       \n        arXiv:2610.39825\n       \n      \n        [ pdf ,  html ,  other ]\n     \n     \n       \n          Title: \n          AegisFlow: Training-Free Non-Myopic Path-Safe Guided Flow Matching\n         \n          Kunpeng Liu ,  Boshi Zhang ,  Keyou You  \n\n          Comments: \n          18 pages, 6 figures\n         \n\n\n          Subjects: \n           Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI) \n         \n\n         \n          Pretrained generative models, such as diffusion and flow matching models, have demonstrated exceptional capabilities in synthesizing high-quality data. However, steering their generative trajectories to satisfy strict constraints--such as identity preservation in image editing or obstacle avoidance in robotics--remains fundamentally challenging. While recent training-free guidance methods enforce path-safety, they rely heavily on min-norm controllers that only account for immediate constraint satisfaction. These myopic approaches often yield suboptimal trajectories that hover dangerously close to the boundary, severely restricting the model's ability to optimize toward its target. In this paper, we propose AegisFlow, a training-free, non-myopic path-safe guidance framework that recasts guided generation as a safety-critical optimal control problem with global scope. By integrating a time-varying barrier function directly into the global cost functional, AegisFlow naturally enforces constraints along the entire generative path. To compute the global optimal control, we derive a tractable, mathematically grounded approximation of the Hamilton-Jacobi-Bellman (HJB) equation. Comprehensive experiments demonstrate that AegisFlow outperforms state-of-the-art methods across text-guided image manipulation and robotic planning, establishing a significantly better trade-off between target alignment and constraint satisfaction. Code is available at  this https URL .\n         \n       \n     \n     \n       [124] \n       \n        arXiv:2610.39884\n       \n      \n        [ pdf ,  html ,  other ]\n     \n     \n       \n          Title: \n          KARMA: Training-Free Knowledge-Atomic Harness Evolution for Industrial Advertising Agents\n         \n          Xinhua Miao ,  Yuanzhen Rong ,  Weiru Zhang ,  Xiaoyi Zeng  \n\n\n\n          Subjects: \n           Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI) \n         \n\n         \n          Industrial advertising agents must continually adapt their decision knowledge to changing product offerings, traffic patterns, and operating policies. We present KARMA (Knowledge-Atomic, Regression-Gated Modular Adaptation), a training-free framework that evolves six typed knowledge modules while keeping model weights and executable harness code frozen. During our evolution, a deterministic Trace Verifier maps observed errors to candidate modules, and a Critic compares successful and failed trajectories to guide a tool-constrained Evolver. The Evolver applies atomic knowledge edits, which a fixed validation gate retains only when their measured gain reaches a threshold; otherwise, the previous knowledge state is restored. We evaluate KARMA with three LLM families on cases sampled from a corpus of approximately half a million real-world operation logs across six e-commerce domains. Across 18 domain--model settings, KARMA raises the average hold-out score from 30.19 to 39.15 (+8.96 points, +29.7%). Ablations show that removing either successful or failed trajectories more than halves the average hold-out gain. Evolved knowledge also transfers across domains without further adaptation, yielding an average hold-out gain of 8.54 points across all source--target pairs. By linking retained patches to their diagnostic evidence and validation outcomes, KARMA supports inspectable, reversible knowledge adaptation for agents in Web marketplaces.\n         \n       \n     \n     \n       [125] \n       \n        arXiv:2610.40005\n       \n      \n        [ pdf ,  html ,  other ]\n     \n     \n       \n          Title: \n          Efficient Learning of Mechanism Designs and Compositionality\n         \n          João Pedro Gandarela ,  Thiago Rios ,  Stefan Menzel ,  André Freitas  \n\n\n\n          Subjects: \n           Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI) ; Computational Engineering, Finance, and Science (cs.CE)\n         \n\n         \n          Learning mechanism behaviour requires a representation of how connected parts jointly constrain motion. We study whether exposing reusable constraint subassemblies improves prediction from limited data. Assur-KB represents planar mechanisms through irreducible modules, boundary interfaces, and solve dependencies while retaining source geometry. Our theoretical results characterize transfer through module interfaces and establish physical limits arising from missing interface information and accumulated sensitivity. To test the representation claim directly, the experimental design compares matched mechanism-curve pairs across training budgets within the same multilayer-perceptron family. This controlled comparison establishes the utility of the complete representation in the evaluated learning setting. Assur-derived features provide a geometric-mean $1.8\\times$ advantage in sample efficiency over flattened adjacency and position inputs across budgets of 50-10,000 mechanisms. Separate experiments establish invariance under tested joint relabelings, higher valid-generation rates under dyadic composition, and a coarser structural index over 10.02 million mechanisms. In summary, the theory and experiments identify how physical constraint structure supports limited-data prediction and which interface conditions govern compositional reuse.\n         \n       \n     \n     \n       [126] \n       \n        arXiv:2610.40006\n       \n      \n        [ pdf ,  html ,  other ]\n     \n     \n       \n          Title: \n          Human--AI Interactions as First-Class Data: A Formal Interaction Model and Runtime for Explainable AI Collaboration\n         \n          Michela Taufer ,  Kin Ng ,  Ian Lumsden ,  Aashish Pandey ,  Rajasi Gore ,  Angelina Ju ,  Sukanya Moudgalya ,  Ewa Deelman ,  Sai Swaminathan  \n\n\n\n          Subjects: \n           Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI) ; Human-Computer Interaction (cs.HC)\n         \n\n         \n          AI assistants are increasingly embedded in computational notebooks, scientific workflows, software engineering tools, and learning systems, yet human-AI interactions are often treated as transient prompt-response exchanges rather than durable data products, hindering reproducibility, explainability, replay, behavioral analysis, and adaptive AI support. We argue that these interactions form a new data class requiring formal representation and introduce the Human-AI Interaction Description Language (HAIDL), a formal interaction model with a Backus-Naur Form-inspired concrete syntax. HAIDL encodes each interaction as a structured object capturing computational context, user request, prompt payload, AI response, feedback, problem context, provenance, and model metadata. We integrate this vision into HAIDL-X, a middleware for JupyterLab that instantiates HAIDL by capturing user-bot state, building contextualized prompts, interfacing with AI providers, collecting feedback, and storing interactions in a repository. We add a learning layer over HAIDL that maps interaction objects and notebook execution traces into reproducible measures of user behavior, including engagement, performance, help-seeking, error-oriented use, and learning personas. HAIDL-X provides a foundation for treating human-AI collaboration as structured, queryable, replayable, analyzable data.\n         \n       \n     \n     \n       [127] \n       \n        arXiv:2610.40030\n       \n      \n        [ pdf ,  html ,  other ]\n     \n     \n       \n          Title: \n          Cheap Retrieval and Observation Credit Assignment from One Quantity: The Value of Information\n         \n          Daniel Strauss ,  Nikolaos Sotirakis ,  Zhenshan Bing ,  Alois Knoll  \n\n          Comments: \n          11 pages, 1 figure. Accepted at the CoRL 2026 Workshop on Memory for Robot Foundation Models ( this https URL )\n         \n\n\n          Subjects: \n           Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI) ; Machine Learning (cs.LG)\n         \n\n         \n          Memory in reinforcement learning raises two problems. Attention finds the relevant item by scoring the whole store, which is costly for robots under latency and energy limits. When a retrieved memory pays off, credit for acquiring it must cross the whole sequence. We argue that both are governed by one quantity, the value of information, and propose to estimate it from archived rollouts with a learned world model. We obtain that, when read at the observation, it yields a provable lower bound on the optimal value of the acquiring action, which floors its TD target and attributes this floor to the observation that earned it. Read at the decision, it defines the optimal retrieval set. A pilot retrieval system, to be trained and improved with this signal, solves a memory task with reduced context.\n         \n       \n     \n     \n       [128] \n       \n        arXiv:2610.40036\n       \n      \n        [ pdf ,  html ,  other ]\n     \n     \n       \n          Title: \n          Hybrid AI Patterns: Thirteen Ways to Couple Language Models with Rules, and How to Choose Among Them\n         \n          Siegfried Handschuh ,  Götz-Henrik Wiegand ,  Lorena Raichle  \n\n\n\n          Subjects: \n           Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI) \n         \n\n         \n          Language models handle text well, and rule engines give conclusions with proofs. Systems that combine the two are usually described by example, and the same coupling turns up under different names in agent frameworks, guardrail toolkits and neuro-symbolic research. This document defines thirteen patterns for coupling a language model with an executable rule language, in the catalogue form introduced for object-oriented design: intent, motivation, applicability, participants, collaborations, consequences, implementation, known uses and related patterns. Four patterns concern what crosses the boundary between model and rules, five who controls whom, three how the model takes part in the reasoning, and one how knowledge is kept over time. Five invariants hold for every combination, and five failure patterns name the couplings that break them. We relate the catalogue to published pattern catalogues and taxonomies of hybrid and neuro-symbolic systems and show where they overlap with it. A decision guide leads from a problem to a pattern. It rests on our measurements, including the cases in which a model alone did as well as the hybrid or better, and it reports what each design cost.\n         \n       \n     \n     \n       [129] \n       \n        arXiv:2610.40071\n       \n      \n        [ pdf ,  html ,  other ]\n     \n     \n       \n          Title: \n          Agentic process evidence\n         \n          Yuval Kainan ,  Carmit Hershman  \n\n\n\n          Subjects: \n           Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI) ; Software Engineering (cs.SE); Systems and Control (eess.SY)\n         \n\n         \n          The way software gets built is changing dramatically. AI agents now create pull requests, select dependencies, refactor codebases, interpret test failures, and execute infrastructure changes across the entire Software Development Life Cycle (SDLC). AI agents are autonomous team members, but unlike human engineers, they are ephemeral - their reasoning, decisions, and tool calls vanish the moment the session ends. Modern Security incidents involving intent mismatch or injection are becoming increasingly common - forcing us to address AI Agents as an insider threat. No widely adopted standard yet enables agentic provenance and governance and its obvious that you can't hold an ephemeral process accountable. The git log was designed for human commits. It shows what changed. It does not show what an AI agent did, which model it used, which tools it called, or whether anyone was accountable for the outcome. Every governance framework, every audit trail, every compliance control in the SDLC was built around one assumption: a human made the decision. That assumption no longer holds. This shift introduces 3 main procedural problems (not addressing scale) - Supply-chain vector - The agentic coder is software, so it is a supply-chain security risk. Policy guardrails - How to ensure the agentic coder is compliant with the policies of the organization. Task-alignment - How to align the agentic coder on its tasks and goals. We introduce Agentic Process Evidence (APE), a signed, tamper-evident, machine-readable attestation of agentic processes, linking the sessions to their outcome (process-aware logging). APE enables Policy-as-Code control over the agentic processes, allowing for human in the loop only when it's needed. We support the in-toto style attestation framework, and the protocol is aligned with the relevant regulations\n         \n       \n     \n     \n       [130] \n       \n        arXiv:2610.40096\n       \n      \n        [ pdf ,  html ,  other ]\n     \n     \n       \n          Title: \n          Reward-Adaptive Co-Evolution of Policy and Rubric\n         \n          Yizhu Chen ,  Jianguang Jiang ,  Jiameng Huang ,  Chuyu Fang ,  Bojun Zhang ,  Zhenyu Jiao ,  Xing Yuan ,  Tong Lin  \n\n\n\n          Subjects: \n           Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI) \n         \n\n         \n          Rubric-based rewards provide fine-grained, interpretable supervision for open-ended reinforcement learning, where holistic scalar rewards struggle to capture multidimensional response quality. Static rubrics can lose discriminative power as the policy distribution evolves, while existing policy--rubric co-evolution methods rely on globally prescribed update schedules that ignore heterogeneous reward informativeness. We introduce RACE, a Reward-Adaptive Co-Evolution framework. At the group level, per-rollout-group training-signal routing classifies groups as informative, diversity-collapsed, or rubric-failed, updating the policy only with informative groups while caching rubric failures. At the phase level, event-triggered phase switching enters rubric optimization upon persistent reward saturation and returns when rubric-reward improvement plateaus. Rubric phases train the generator on cached pairs using a frozen judge and a scale-normalized pairwise-margin reward. Across both levels, online threshold adaptation adjusts routing and switching thresholds through proportional control. Under a unified evaluation protocol, RACE achieves the highest average performance and outperforms the strongest fixed-alternation baseline on all eight tasks, while improving rubric discrimination.\n         \n       \n     \n     \n       [131] \n       \n        arXiv:2610.40133\n       \n      \n        [ pdf ,  html ,  other ]\n     \n     \n       \n          Title: \n          DynaRev: Benchmarking Web Agents under Dynamic Goal Revision\n         \n          Shiqi Zhang ,  Guochen Zhou ,  Zesheng Wei ,  Hai Zhao ,  Zhisong Zhang  \n\n\n\n          Subjects: \n           Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI) \n         \n\n         \n          Web agents are typically evaluated with goals fully specified before execution and fixed thereafter, whereas real users often revise their requirements during interaction. To study such interactions, we introduce DynaRev, a benchmark of 181 tasks to evaluate web agents under dynamic goal revision. DynaRev uses semantic milestones to deliver revisions at task-defined stages, covers additive revisions, unconditional overrides, and runtime-conditioned overrides, and evaluates correctness, responsiveness, and efficiency through trajectory-grounded evaluation. Five-model evaluations show that agents often satisfy only part of the task requirements, with performance varying across task sources, revision types, and models. Further analyses reveal varied milestone timing, mixed effects of dynamic delivery, and contrasting revision-handling outcomes, with gains from instruction rewriting while preserving browser state and losses from browser resets. DynaRev provides a testbed for studying how web agents adapt to evolving user goals. The code and data are available at  this https URL .\n         \n       \n     \n     \n       [132] \n       \n        arXiv:2610.40151\n       \n      \n        [ pdf ,  other ]\n     \n     \n       \n          Title: \n          The generative paradox: the Anti-Prompt paradigm for human capability\n         \n          Margarita Trifonov Vadim E. Levit  \n\n          Comments: \n          17 pages, 2 figures, 2 boxes\n         \n\n\n          Subjects: \n           Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI) \n         \n\n         \n          Generative AI in education is usually judged by the output of human-AI teams. That focus overlooks a second outcome: what people can still understand, challenge, repair, and adapt when assistance changes or disappears. We call this tension the generative paradox and distinguish four manifestations: performance, assistance, ownership, and capability stock. We propose developmental function allocation, which complements operational function allocation and meaningful human control by treating retained human capability as a design objective alongside current joint performance. The Anti-Prompt paradigm reverses the default interaction order: when immediate generation would preempt a consequential human act, AI should first create the conditions for human construction. We distinguish the validity of a product, responsibility for its construction, and evidence of capability after support changes. Representative Anti-Prompts, a canonical interaction sequence, and stress tests spanning mathematical reasoning, experimental science, software diagnosis, and lesson design make these distinctions concrete. We conclude with falsifiable predictions about retained capability and a system-level hypothesis about the reproduction and distribution of expertise.\n         \n       \n     \n     \n       [133] \n       \n        arXiv:2610.40161\n       \n      \n        [ pdf ,  html ,  other ]\n     \n     \n       \n          Title: \n          A Negotiation-Aware Hybrid LLM Framework for Intent Interpretation and Service Discovery\n         \n          Wided Meflah ,  Hayet Brabra ,  Walid Gaaloul  \n\n          Comments: \n          16 pages, 3 figures, 1 table. Accepted at the 24th International Conference on Service-Oriented Computing (ICSOC 2026)\n         \n\n\n          Subjects: \n           Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI) ; Software Engineering (cs.SE)\n         \n\n         \n          Increasingly, users express their needs as natural-language intents rather than structured requests. These intents may be incomplete, high-level, or internally conflicting. A conflicting intent either violates fundamental properties of the cloud paradigm or states mutually inconsistent requirements. Classical composition approaches are designed for structured, low-level, and complete requests. They cannot interpret such intents, discover suitable services from provider catalogs, or negotiate conflicting requirements with users. To address this gap, we propose a negotiation-aware framework for intent interpretation and service discovery. The framework first refines the natural-language intent and dynamically detects conflicts. It then structures the intent into a provider-independent TOSCA template. For each specification, it retrieves candidate service offers through deterministic matching grounded in cloud provider offerings. Some conflicts arise during interpretation. Other requirements prove unsatisfiable during discovery. Both are resolved through iterative natural-language negotiation with the user. We evaluate the framework on 70 diverse intents against single-shot and enhanced-prompting baselines. It improves recall and F1 for both conflict detection and service discovery. It also eliminates hallucinated offers and resolves conflicts at a cost proportional to their number.\n         \n       \n     \n     \n       [134] \n       \n        arXiv:2610.40186\n       \n      \n        [ pdf ,  html ,  other ]\n     \n     \n       \n          Title: \n          Locating and Closing Information Gaps in Multi-Agent Systems\n         \n          Yaxin Gong ,  Chongming Gao ,  Gangyi Zhang ,  Chenxiao Fan ,  Leyang Shen ,  Jiakai Wang ,  Dong Wang ,  Yang Liu ,  Haoyan Liu ,  Xinting Hu  \n\n\n\n          Subjects: \n           Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI) \n         \n\n         \n          Multi-step Web tasks often require several LLM agents to gather and combine evidence. The communication graph of a fixed workflow restricts which agents can read each other's outputs: evidence retrieved by one agent may never reach the agent that needs it, leaving an information gap and an incomplete or incorrect answer. Adding a connection can deliver the missing evidence, but can also lead the receiver to adopt an incorrect upstream conclusion that displaces a correct answer; the final output alone does not separate the two. We propose Selective Probing and Link Insertion for Closing information gaps in multi-agent Execution (SPLICE), which locates information gaps per query and decides what to adopt after adding an edge. After the original workflow runs, SPLICE uses the query and node outputs to locate a possible gap, adds one directed edge from a source to a receiver to deliver the relevant evidence, and re-executes the receiver and affected downstream nodes, producing a candidate answer. The adoption decision reads both the final answers of the two runs and the process changes the edit caused inside the workflow: how far the receiver moved, where its new content came from, and whether the change reached the final answer. A selector trained on these process features chooses between adopting the candidate answer and retaining the original. On five benchmarks, SPLICE raises the task score on Web tool use by 8.0% and the mean score on four reasoning and planning benchmarks by 5.7% relative to the static skeleton, at 1.25 to 1.44$\\times$ the skeleton's cost, and transfers zero-shot to four unseen backbones.\n         \n       \n     \n     \n       [135] \n       \n        arXiv:2610.40192\n       \n      \n        [ pdf ,  html ,  other ]\n     \n     \n       \n          Title: \n          Bridging Semantics and Structure: A Theoretically Grounded Framework for Link Prediction on TAGs\n         \n          Chen Shao ,  Ruirui Wang ,  Tobias Kafer ,  Danai Koutra  \n\n          Comments: \n          33 pages. Accepted for publication in ACM Transactions on Knowledge Discovery from Data (TKDD)\n         \n\n\n          Subjects: \n           Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI) \n         \n\n         \n          Link prediction on text-attributed graphs (TAGs) is a fundamental problem with applications in knowledge discovery, recommendation and beyond. Recent progress has highlighted the promise of combining Graph Neural Networks (GNNs) with Language Models (LMs) in order to leverage the complementary structural and semantic signals. However, due to the distinct inductive biases of GNNs and LMs, the design of an optimal fusion strategy remains an open research question. In this work, we present Bridge-LP, an intuitive, simple, efficient and theoretically grounded framework that unifies graph and language representations. It is built around three theoretically-justified design components: (D1) jumping connection of textual proximity, which enables robust LM training; (D2) compressed embedding, which encodes neighborhood information long exploited by classical link prediction heuristics, thereby enriching structural signals in a learnable way; and (D3) cache embedding training strategy, which reduces computational complexity. Together, these mechanisms establish an end-to-end cross-modal framework that scales to large graphs while capturing richer graph-text interactions. We conduct an extensive empirical evaluation, comparing against 40 existing models across 9 diverse datasets. Experiments show that Bridge-LP consistently outperforms state-of-the-art approaches, delivering up to a 43.06% improvement in Mean Reciprocal Rank (MRR) and setting a new benchmark for language-graph integration.\n         \n       \n     \n     \n       [136] \n       \n        arXiv:2610.40227\n       \n      \n        [ pdf ,  other ]\n     \n     \n       \n          Title: \n          EpiAgent: Epistemic Self-Play for LLM Agents\n         \n          Xuanfa Jin ,  Xinyu Cui ,  Zhijian Ma ,  Xue Yan ,  Jian Zhao ,  Haifeng Zhang ,  Jun Wang  \n\n          Comments: \n          23 pages, 5 figures\n         \n\n\n          Subjects: \n           Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI) ; Computation and Language (cs.CL); Multiagent Systems (cs.MA)\n         \n\n         \n          Epistemic game theory explains an agent's strategic behavior via the beliefs it holds about others and how these beliefs are updated as the interactions unfold. Modern LLM-based agents, however, often lack an explicit mechanism for learning and updating such beliefs to guide decisions under incomplete information. To bridge this gap, we propose EpiAgent, a framework that equips LLM-based agents with an explicit, learnable epistemic layer, which maintains beliefs over others' private information and combines these beliefs with the agent's available information into an epistemic state for action selection. Concretely, the agent's policy is decomposed into two coupled stages: Epistemic Update, which updates these beliefs from interaction histories, and Action Planning, which selects actions conditioned on the updated epistemic state. By formulating their dependency as a bi-level optimization problem, we introduce Epistemic Self-Play, an algorithm that alternately optimizes the two stages through self-play, utilizing an intrinsic prediction reward to evaluate how well inferred beliefs predict subsequent observations. Empirical results across both mixed and competitive scenarios show that EpiAgent consistently outperforms prompting-based and RL-based baselines. Further analyses reveal the benefits of dedicated epistemic training, improvements in belief inference accuracy, and the contribution of learned beliefs to action planning. These results demonstrate the value of an explicit, learnable epistemic layer for effective multi-agent interactions.\n         \n       \n     \n     \n       [137] \n       \n        arXiv:2610.40246\n       \n      \n        [ pdf ,  html ,  other ]\n     \n     \n       \n          Title: \n          EnvOS: Auditing Computer-Use Agents When the World Moves On : Phenomenon-Based Task Construction, Certification, and Evidence-Based Evaluation\n         \n          Ankush Kadu ,  Athira Gopal ,  Niharika Singh ,  Arpit Jain ,  Aswanth Krishnan  \n\n          Comments: \n          74 pages, 7 figures. Accepted at NeurIPS 2026 Workshops (AABA4ET and CL4FMAgents)\n         \n\n\n          Subjects: \n           Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI) ; Human-Computer Interaction (cs.HC)\n         \n\n         \n          Computer-use agents built on large language models (LLMs) work in software that can change while they act: an appointment moves, a policy is revised, a record is updated. Testing agents under such changes is harder than it looks. When an agent fails, the cause may be the agent, but it may also be a change that did not fire as intended, an update the agent never received, or a grader that scored the episode wrongly. We introduce EnvOS, a framework for building and certifying environments that change mid-task. Before any agent runs, EnvOS checks that each change fires correctly, that the task remains solvable, and that the grader separates correct from incorrect behavior; after each run, separate code re-checks the verdict from archived evidence. We apply it to stale information, where a value the agent has already seen changes before the agent acts on it. In clinic booking, Claude Opus 5 succeeds on 0/16 tasks when nothing extra is shown, 2/16 when reminded to re-check the source, and 15/16 when the updated value is shown; the gain over the reminder holds after correcting for all tests (p = 0.00293). In insurance-claim approval, Opus 5 re-checks on its own and succeeds on 10/10, whereas Claude Sonnet 5 rises from 1/10 to 9/10 when the current policy is shown again, a gain significant in its pre-registered test but not after correcting for all tests. Whether an agent caught a change thus varied with the model and the interface. We also stress-test the grading itself: on corrupted episodes designed blind from the rules, the EnvOS pipeline accepted none of 26 as clean, and on an externally generated set it caught 25 of 39, against 4 for simple log checks. Because every EnvOS environment is generated from a seed and graded by an audited automatic verifier, the same environments are natural candidates for training agents with reinforcement learning from verifiable rewards.\n         \n       \n     \n     \n       [138] \n       \n        arXiv:2610.40249\n       \n      \n        [ pdf ,  html ,  other ]\n     \n     \n       \n          Title: \n          Reaction precedents extend retrosynthetic prediction beyond training coverage\n         \n          Jiaxing Sun ,  Xuli Shen ,  Jiang Wu ,  Ruiyi Ding ,  Ruikang Zhang ,  Xiao An ,  Xibao Li ,  Yubin Wang ,  Xingjian Wei ,  Yinfan Wang ,  Yu Liu ,  Lingli Ge ,  Xi Xue ,  Xiaojian Wang ,  Lijun Wu ,  Chang-Yu Hsieh ,  Yuqiang Li ,  Bowen Zhou ,  Dahua Lin ,  Conghui He  \n\n\n\n          Subjects: \n           Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI) \n         \n\n         \n          Reaction precedents guide retrosynthesis, but how far they can compensate for gaps in task-specific training coverage remains unclear. Using 2.71 million reactions, we restricted training and retrieval coverage by transformation or time, then expanded only the retrieval corpus with model parameters fixed. For transformations excluded from training, mean Top-1 accuracy increased from 13.0% to 58.0% on a historical test set and from 4.9% to 23.1% on a temporally held-out test set. For temporal coverage gaps, retrieval expansion enabled a model trained on records before 2017 to match or exceed a non-retrieval model trained on records before 2025 on both test sets. Without task-specific fine-tuning, precedents improved all 14 representative general-purpose models on both test sets and USPTO-50K; the strongest with precedents surpassed all eight representative specialized models. These findings show that inference-time reaction precedents can extend retrosynthetic prediction beyond task-specific training coverage without parameter updates.\n         \n       \n     \n     \n       [139] \n       \n        arXiv:2610.40250\n       \n      \n        [ pdf ,  html ,  other ]\n     \n     \n       \n          Title: \n          Distributional Reward Calibration and Budget-Neutral Credit Assignment for Multimodal RLVR\n         \n          Lang Chen ,  Zongxin Xu ,  Borui Wang ,  Anran Liu ,  Shitong Chen  \n\n\n\n          Subjects: \n           Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI) \n         \n\n         \n          We revisit advantage estimation in Group Relative Policy Optimization (GRPO) and observe that any token-level advantage admits a two-stage factorization: a \\emph{sample-level baselining} step that converts scalar verifier rewards into a comparable sequence-level signal, and a \\emph{token-level credit assignment} step that distributes that signal over the trajectory. Existing estimators typically address only one of these stages: affine baselines preserve the shape of each task's reward law, so heavy tails and binary cliffs propagate into the gradient, while token-level credit rules tie the size of each token's update to raw confidence scales and need not conserve the total credit issued by the verifier. We propose \\textbf{TACE} (Two-stage Advantage and Credit Estimation), a two-stage estimator that addresses both stages. At stage~(S1), we replace linear standardization with a one-dimensional optimal-transport map to a standard normal, which gives a common population reward law across tasks and caps finite-sample outliers deterministically. At stage~(S2), we rank-calibrate a stop-gradient evidence contrast within each trajectory and renormalize the clipped token weights to have mean one, so credit is redistributed across tokens while its total mass and sign are conserved. We prove population-level inter-task moment equity, a deterministic tail bound, and mass-conservation, boundedness, and sign-anchoring properties that affine baselines and unnormalized token weights do not satisfy. On a multimodal reasoning suite, TACE obtains the strongest results on a majority of the benchmarks but does not dominate every setting.\n         \n       \n     \n     \n       [140] \n       \n        arXiv:2610.40278\n       \n      \n        [ pdf ,  html ,  other ]\n     \n     \n       \n          Title: \n          Neighborhood Elites: Quality-Diversity Search for Uncertainty Quantification in Question Answering\n         \n          Mesut Ceylan ,  Arda Baris Basaran ,  Elgar Fleisch ,  Filipe Barata  \n\n\n\n          Subjects: \n           Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI) ; Computation and Language (cs.CL)\n         \n\n         \n          Large language models (LLMs) are increasingly capable, yet they produce wrong answers indistinguishable from correct ones. In question answering (QA), such an error propagates unnoticed into downstream tasks. Perturbation-based uncertainty quantification (UQ) addresses this by probing a model over a set of input rewrites and reading uncertainty from how much its predictions vary. Yet this set is usually built generically, by free paraphrasing, token-level perturbation, or adversarial search. Such rewrites leave two things uncontrolled that matter in QA: what kind of change is made to the context, and how explicitly the context supports the answer. When neither is controlled, the composition of the set is determined by the generator rather than by design, and the estimate inherits whatever that generator happens to produce. Quality-diversity (QD) search is built to cover a predefined exploration space, so we recast input perturbation as a QD problem and propose \\emph{neighborhood elites (NE)}, a neighborhood constructor that spreads answer-preserving rewrites across two axes, the kind of edit and the explicitness of support. We compare NE against three other neighborhood constructors, holding the editor, QA model, uncertainty measure and search objective fixed, and against three scores read from the original context. Across five extractive-QA datasets and five instruction-tuned LLMs, NE improves failure detection against every other constructor in at least 24 of the 25 dataset-model pairs, reaching 0.726 AUROC and 0.211 AUPRC lift over the base rate. Additionally, NE detects failure better than semantic entropy computed over decoder samples, by 0.074 AUROC and 0.062 AUPRC lift, and this advantage widens as the neighborhood grows.\n         \n       \n     \n     \n       [141] \n       \n        arXiv:2610.40330\n       \n      \n        [ pdf ,  other ]\n     \n     \n       \n          Title: \n          Multistep and Multivariate Time Series Explainability with GradientSHAP: A Wind Power Case Study\n         \n          Robbie Southam ,  Zhong Fan ,  Ke Li  \n\n          Comments: \n          24 pages. Submitted to Computational Intelligence\n         \n\n\n          Subjects: \n           Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI) ; Machine Learning (cs.LG)\n         \n\n         \n          As the effects of climate change become increasingly apparent, the demand for renewable energy sources such as wind power continues to grow. However, renewables' variable and intermittent nature presents significant challenges in their integration into energy grids. Artificial intelligence (AI) has emerged as a powerful tool to address these issues. Still, its adoption in the energy sector has been hindered by the opacity of many AI models, which often function as black boxes. This paper introduces a carefully adapted GradientSHAP approach to provide interpretability for multivariate, multistep wind power forecasting -- a largely underexplored area. This capability is critical for day-ahead grid management and, to the best of our knowledge, this is the first study to apply GradientSHAP in the context of wind power forecasting. To address the inherent dimensionality, we developed novel visualizations that deliver both local and global explanations, uncovering intricate relationships between environmental factors, site-specific conditions, and temporal dynamics. While GradientSHAP is not an out-of-the-box solution, our findings demonstrate that, with thoughtful handling of SHAP values and domain expertise, it can offer valuable insights into model behaviour, supporting more informed and trustworthy decision-making. Nevertheless, its complexity poses challenges for practical implementation. Future research should focus on streamlining these interpretability methods to make them more actionable for routine deployment in critical energy applications.\n         \n       \n     \n     \n       [142] \n       \n        arXiv:2610.40382\n       \n      \n        [ pdf ,  html ,  other ]\n     \n     \n       \n          Title: \n          Beyond Selected Breakthroughs: Evaluating Language Models on Randomly Sampled Mathematical Conjectures\n         \n          Helen Jenne ,  Davis Brown ,  Jesse He ,  Henry Kvinge  \n\n\n\n          Subjects: \n           Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI) ; History and Overview (math.HO)\n         \n\n         \n          The last year has seen a shift in AI-assisted mathematics, with frontier language models producing proofs of and counterexamples to long-standing open conjectures. However, studying only the current set of major breakthroughs provides an incomplete picture of LLM capabilities and the computational resources needed to achieve reported results. To address this, we introduce a new dataset, OpenConjecture, which collects conjectures from mathematics papers published on the arXiv since February 2026. We use these conjectures as a source of open mathematical problems, and apply frontier GPT models to a random sample of 200 of these conjectures over three passes using a reproducible protocol without problem-specific mathematical guidance. Rather than attempt expert verification of these candidate solutions, we analyze the types of problems for which LLMs produce solutions and the characteristics of these solutions. The models we test produce candidate solutions for roughly 60% of the conjectures, with counterexamples about 1.3 times more common than proofs. We estimate solution novelty, assess how solutions cite and relate to existing literature, and analyze whether the proposed resolution relies on either an error in or underspecification of the conjecture. We find that models sometimes propose resolutions that are outside the authors' intent (e.g., proposing the trivial group as a counterexample) and that a number of proofs follow closely from existing results, but that some resolutions exhibit at least moderate novelty. Our findings underscore the need for evaluations that go beyond correctness to assess fidelity to the intended statement, attribution, and the nature of the mathematical contribution.\n         \n       \n     \n     \n       [143] \n       \n        arXiv:2610.40409\n       \n      \n        [ pdf ,  html ,  other ]\n     \n     \n       \n          Title: \n          Retrieving from Your Own Mistakes: On-Policy Training of Embedding Models for Multi-Hop Retrieval-Augmented Generation\n         \n          Chenjie Qiu ,  Zixuan Zeng ,  Shaobin Shi ,  Bingfeng Chen ,  Dongsheng Luo ,  Boyan Xu  \n\n          Comments: \n          13 pages, 7 figures, 11 tables\n         \n\n\n          Subjects: \n           Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI) \n         \n\n         \n          Complex questions in retrieval-augmented generation require evidence spread over several documents, and an answer is reliable only when the retriever finds all of it. Chain embedding retrievers collect such evidence step by step, re-encoding the question together with the passages retrieved so far, and we find that they are already strong: trained only on gold histories, a small chain embedding retriever that calls no language model outperforms graph-based systems and is competitive with a search agent. This training, however, leaves a gap. At test time the retriever conditions on its own retrievals, which soon contain mistakes: by the third step, about half or more of the states it visits hold a wrong passage, and these passages lower its chance of retrieving a missing gold passage well beyond what the difficulty of the states explains. We propose on-policy state training, which trains the encoder on the states it reaches itself. At every epoch the current encoder retrieves chains for the training questions; each state it visits, including those built on its own mistakes, is labeled with the earliest gold passage still missing while the other missing gold passages are masked, so that no correct continuation is penalized; and the encoder is updated contrastively on these states mixed with teacher-forced ones, so that its training distribution follows it as it learns. The resulting retriever retrieves complete evidence more often than teacher-forced training and the systems we compare, and it transfers without further training to harder long-chain variants of HotpotQA and 2WikiMultiHopQA that we construct. On the same states, it recovers from its own mistakes far more often, and controlled comparisons attribute the gain to training on the states the encoder currently reaches.\n         \n       \n     \n     \n       [144] \n       \n        arXiv:2610.40444\n       \n      \n        [ pdf ,  html ,  other ]\n     \n     \n       \n          Title: \n          Prefix Executability: Evaluating Tool-Using Agents Beyond Final Success\n         \n          Amir H. Rezaeian ,  Weiyi Sun ,  Yassine Benajiba ,  Dan Roth  \n\n          Comments: \n          21 pages, 2 figures. Accepted to NeurIPS 2026\n         \n\n\n          Subjects: \n           Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI) ; Computation and Language (cs.CL)\n         \n\n         \n          Tool-use benchmarks often score agents by final task success, but terminal outcomes can obscure whether the intermediate tool-call trajectory was executable. We propose prefix executability as a trajectory-level evaluation protocol for tool-using agents. The protocol measures whether every prefix of a predicted tool-call sequence remains executable under tool schemas, state preconditions, and data dependencies, and summarizes reliability using Prefix Executability Curves (PEC), First-Error Depth (FED), Trajectory Executability Rate (TER), horizon-normalized AUC-PEC, and Recovery Rate (RR). We instantiate the protocol on stratified samples from BFCL-v3 multi-turn and ToolSandbox, and on the complete Airline/Retail base splits of $\\tau^3$-bench. We observe that outcome-only and prefix-level rankings diverge: agents whose execution is conditioned on an unverified plan often achieve higher point-estimate final success than non-plan-conditioned baselines while substantially lowering TER and AUC-PEC. These divergences are precisely the failure modes that prefix executability is designed to expose: they reveal whether apparent success rests on an executable trajectory, where invalidity first enters, and whether success follows recovery from an earlier failure. As a validation intervention, explicit pre-execution plan checking can improve executable-prefix survival while maintaining competitive terminal success. These results show that final success alone is insufficient for evaluating tool-use agents and that prefix-level executability provides an actionable reliability signal for comparing agents, diagnosing failures, and assessing validation mechanisms.\n         \n       \n     \n     \n       [145] \n       \n        arXiv:2610.40476\n       \n      \n        [ pdf ,  html ,  other ]\n     \n     \n       \n          Title: \n          Training language models in simulation for psychiatric intake\n         \n          Samuel Schmidgall ,  Tim Strother ,  Alex Bijamov ,  Min Woo Sun ,  Anil Palepu ,  Akshay Goel ,  Ulysse Marteau-Ferey ,  Théo Moutakanni ,  Yubin Kim ,  Joseph Breda ,  Christina Chen ,  Justin Chen ,  Paul Jhun ,  Andrew Marshall ,  Sarah Montgomery-Taylor ,  Eun Kyoung Hong ,  Shashank Ravi ,  Kameron C. Black ,  David J. H. Wu ,  Austin Schoeffler ,  John Lyon Havlik ,  Elizabeth C. Stade ,  Jared Moore ,  Johannes C. Eichstaedt ,  Logan Pierce ,  Jeff Jopling ,  Johannes Thrul ,  Thomas H. McCoy ,  Neguine Rezaii ,  Mark S. Chen ,  Ellen E. Fitzsimmons-Craft ,  Shirley Wang ,  Desmond C. Ong ,  Katharina Schultebraucks ,  Torrey Creed ,  Bhavna Daryani ,  Aleksandra Faust ,  S. Sara Mahdavi ,  Caroline Figueroa ,  Eric Lin ,  Kate H. Bentley ,  Ernest Lim ,  Zhihan Jiang ,  Xuhai \"Orson\" Xu ,  Daniel McDuff ,  John Torous ,  Yun Liu ,  Po-Hsuan Cameron Chen ,  Mike Schaekermann ,  Joelle Barral ,  Alvin Rajkomar ,  Tao Tu ,  Lin Yang ,  Isaac Galatzer-Levy ,  David Racz ,  Valentin Liévin  \n\n          Comments: \n          76 pages, 8 figures, 15 tables (27 pages main text, 7 main figures, 2 main tables)\n         \n\n\n          Subjects: \n           Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI) ; Computation and Language (cs.CL)\n         \n\n         \n          In psychiatric practice, the clinical intake interview remains a primary diagnostic instrument, requiring the clinician to establish rapport while disentangling complex, overlapping presentations. Here, we show that multi-turn reinforcement learning (RL) in simulation environments improves language model performance on structured text-based psychiatric intake, spanning history-taking, differential diagnosis, and initial management planning. In a randomized, double-blind crossover study ($N = 60$ matched scenario pairs, 120 encounters) comparing the RL-trained agent against its identically prompted base model, 32 board-certified psychiatrists role-played standardized patients while a separate panel of 15 independent, board-certified psychiatrists scored the de-identified transcripts. The trained agent outperformed the base model in 76.7% of matched scenario pairs ($p < 0.0001$), with gains on an adapted clinical-examination rubric ($d_z = 0.77$, $p < 0.0001$), patient-centered communication (82.0% vs. 65.2%, $p < 0.001$), and differential diagnosis appropriateness (68.3% vs. 33.3%, $p < 0.001$). Across 127 psychiatrist-validated simulated intake scenarios, the trained agent nearly doubled its base model's clinical rubric score (0.706 vs. 0.379; composite reward 0.564 vs. 0.294, $p < 0.0001$). To systematically probe conversational safety, we established two Delphi-consensus taxonomies, of clinical safety risks and relational competencies, from which 256 challenging adversarial presentations were derived; we find that training in simulation environments more than halved under-triage of acute safety risks (12.9% vs. 30.5%, $p < 0.0001$). Simulation environments thus provide a controlled testbed for training and stress-testing psychiatric conversational agents, with gains that transferred to psychiatrist-enacted encounters, though residual gaps require safeguards and clinician oversight.\n         \n       \n     \n     \n       [146] \n       \n        arXiv:2610.40482\n       \n      \n        [ pdf ,  html ,  other ]\n     \n     \n       \n          Title: \n          FIRME: Registry-Anchored Memory Graphs for Evolving, Heterogeneous Investor Knowledge\n         \n          Yunbai Zhang ,  Yunyi Gao ,  Han-Tai Shiao  \n\n          Comments: \n          20 pages. Accepted at the 3rd Workshop on LLMs and Generative AI for Finance (AI4F) at ACM ICAIF 2026 (non-archival)\n         \n\n\n          Subjects: \n           Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI) ; Computation and Language (cs.CL)\n         \n\n         \n          Financial AI workflows that reuse investor knowledge need persistent memory that integrates observations across sources and periods without losing identity or provenance. In a knowledge graph, recurring observations may use different surface forms, fragmenting relationship histories and preventing evidence from accumulating. We introduce Fusion and Identity Registry for Memory Evolution (FIRME), a registry-anchored memory graph that reconciles incoming observations to persistent canonical relationships and retains their source, time, and context. Within periods, rule-based fusion links investor-call discussions to portfolio cash-flow signals, directly or through a fixed product-topic ontology. Across periods, canonical identities and persistent aliases direct recurring observations to the same relationship history; deterministic updates summarize reinforcement, inactivity, and reactivation. We evaluate FIRME on proprietary investor-call and cash-flow records and on S&P 500 earnings-call transcripts. Compared with a historical registry that retains canonical identities and semantic fallback but omits alias memory, FIRME increases final-period Correct Match Rate by 7.8-12.1 percentage points across four dataset-backend settings under a fixed-judge and fallback-review protocol. A blinded human audit of 200 incoming observations per setting finds Correct Match Rate gains of 9.0-16.5 points and reattachment-recall gains of 22.5-29.0 points, with higher observed merge precision in all four samples. A separate audit finds valid evidence paths for 293 of 300 outputs from full within-period fusion in the investor-call setting. Extraction-faithfulness and round-trip diagnostics assess source grounding and preservation of extractor-derived graph content.\n         \n       \n     \n     \n       [147] \n       \n        arXiv:2610.40492\n       \n      \n        [ pdf ,  html ,  other ]\n     \n     \n       \n          Title: \n          Agentic University: Collaborative Agents for Personalized Learning\n         \n          Jingru Fan ,  Tianle Zhou ,  Yunjie Zhou ,  Zihao He ,  Yixuan Zhou ,  Kezhao Wu ,  Xinyu Zheng ,  Xiyuan Wang ,  Yuheng Wang ,  Zhi Li ,  Songhua Liu ,  Weinan E ,  Chen Qian  \n\n\n\n          Subjects: \n           Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI) ; Computation and Language (cs.CL)\n         \n\n         \n          Large language models support personalized tutoring, content generation, and adaptive learning paths. However, capability development requires these resources and interactions to form a coherent learning process that evolves with the learner. A central challenge is to align goals, activities, and assessment while using learning evidence to decide what should follow. We introduce Human-Participatory Multi-Agent Organization and instantiate it in Agentic University (AU). In AU, learners participate as organization members and work with specialized agents to maintain a revisable Capability Program. The program connects personal goals, learning activities across modalities, and layered assessment, while learner feedback and performance guide subsequent support. Our evaluation shows advantages in program organization, assessment design, and revision quality. Users achieved larger mean assessment gains (12.19 versus 5.61 points on a 50-point scale) and reported stronger cognitive support. This work contributes an organizational approach to adaptive learning that makes learner participation a continuing basis for multi-agent coordination. The Agentic University project is available at \\href{ this https URL }.\n         \n       \n     \n     \n       [148] \n       \n        arXiv:2610.40503\n       \n      \n        [ pdf ,  html ,  other ]\n     \n     \n       \n          Title: \n          Algebraic World Model: From Exploration to Proofs\n         \n          Guancheng Pan ,  Qixin Tan ,  Yang Yuan ,  Andrew Chi-Chih Yao  \n\n          Comments: \n          29 pages, 9 figures, 14 tables. Project page:  this https URL . Ancillary files contain the Lean 4 model of the running example lp85\n         \n\n\n          Subjects: \n           Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI) ; Logic in Computer Science (cs.LO)\n         \n\n         \n          AI has made remarkable progress in mathematics, solving challenging problems with machine-checkable proofs in Lean, yet typically relying on predefined mathematical definitions and formal specifications. This raises a natural question: can it reason rigorously about an unknown world? We introduce the Algebraic World Model (AWM), a framework that turns interaction with unfamiliar environments into explicit mathematical models supporting formal reasoning. AWM explores the environment and develops executable Python hypotheses, continually testing and revising them against observations. It then structures these hypotheses into typed objects and shared rules, simplifies their structure through category-theory-guided transformations, and formalizes the resulting model in Lean 4 to prove reachability and impossibility. AWM can formally prove that a goal is unreachable, even beyond explored trajectories. On two impossible game levels, it constructs closed, kernel-checked Lean proofs excluding every legal winning sequence in all six runs, while Claude Opus 5.5 and GPT-6 Astra, equipped with coding tools, produce none in twelve baseline runs. Four of AWM's six initial bounds are correct, compared with one for Opus 5.5 and none for GPT-6 Astra; given only feedback that a cheaper win exists, AWM revises its rules and constructs new proofs, reaching six of six correct bounds. Moreover, AWM produces interpretable mathematical models whose rules can be inspected and tested step by step. Across all 25 public ARC-AGI-3 games, these models enable Lean to replay the winning trajectories of all 183 levels and generalize beyond observed play, predicting unseen transitions and detecting changes in game mechanics. With Claude Opus 5, AWM clears all levels with a score of 99.9, while its certified winning trajectories use only 43% of the human baseline's actions.\n         \n       \n     \n     \n       [149] \n       \n        arXiv:2610.40535\n       \n      \n        [ pdf ,  html ,  other ]\n     \n     \n       \n          Title: \n          Efficient Scalable Oversight via Rewinding\n         \n          Ziyi Guan ,  Yael Tauman Kalai ,  Ron Rothblum ,  Zoe Xi  \n\n\n\n          Subjects: \n           Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI) ; Computational Complexity (cs.CC); Cryptography and Security (cs.CR)\n         \n\n         \n          Scalable oversight asks how a weak verifier can check the outputs of computations performed by more powerful AI systems. One proposed approach uses relativizing interactive proofs, in which a weak verifier checks the output of a complex oracle-aided computation by interacting with one or more powerful provers. A prominent example is debate, which is highly efficient but assumes that one of two powerful AI models, or provers, is truthful. For certain classes of oracle-aided computations, this assumption can be removed, but at substantially greater computational cost.\n In this work, we introduce \\emph{rewindable interactive proofs} (RIPs), a new model of relativizing interactive proofs for scalable oversight. In an RIP, the verifier may rewind the prover to an earlier state of the interaction and continue from there. This is possible when the prover is an AI model, since the verifier can save a snapshot of the model's state and later restore it. We construct a relativizing RIP for computations whose outputs are robust to changing a small fraction of oracle answers. Our construction is more efficient than prior relativizing proofs that do not assume a truthful prover, and it uses no cryptography.\n In our construction the honest prover runs in time linear in that of the original computation, and the verifier, after a one-time preprocessing step, runs in time nearly linear in the input length (which can be much smaller than the computation itself). We implement our protocol, report a preliminary MNIST experiment, and show that an RIP can be faster than a state-of-the-art deployed proof system.\n RIPs may be of independent interest. Rewinding is possible whenever the verifier controls the prover's machine, for example when the prover runs on trusted hardware or in a virtual machine whose state can be checkpointed.\n         \n       \n     \n     \n       [150] \n       \n        arXiv:2610.40541\n       \n      \n        [ pdf ,  html ,  other ]\n     \n     \n       \n          Title: \n          On the Fundamental Limits of Prefill-Based Decoding Length Prediction\n         \n          Haowen Shi ,  Xuzhe Yan ,  Fahao Chen ,  Peng Li  \n\n          Comments: \n          26 pages, 4 figures\n         \n\n\n          Subjects: \n           Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI) ; Computation and Language (cs.CL); Information Theory (cs.IT)\n         \n\n         \n          Predicting how many tokens a large language model (LLM) will decode, using only prefill-phase hidden states, is a key primitive for inference scheduling. We show that current benchmarks conflate predictor quality with task hardness: when the target model, dataset, or sampling configuration changes, reported accuracy collapses even though the predictor is unchanged. We trace this instability to an overlooked error source---the inherent stochasticity of autoregressive decoding---and derive bounds that every prefill-side method must obey. Our analysis treats decoding as a stopped stochastic process. The per-step stopping probability of a response is not constant but ramps up over hundreds of steps, so the decode length inherits a central limit theorem from the many local decisions that determine where a response ends; this yields per-prompt normality with an explicit error term, together with an affine variance law $\\sigma=\\alpha\\mu+b$ whose slope is the coefficient of variation of the number of response segments. Empirically, the normal law reproduces the per-prompt MAE and the central mass of the decode-length distribution to within a few percent, and the residual departures from normality (mild right skew, slight excess kurtosis) are the ones predicted by the sampling-noise correction. Under fixed model, data, and sampling conditions, a broad design space of predictors (MLP, EGTP, FiLM, soft-label, and Mixture-of-Experts variants) converges to similar performance close to the bound, confirming that \\emph{system error dominates model error}. We argue for a benchmarking protocol that fixes the model, data, and sampling parameters and that reports relative-error metrics alongside absolute ones. Code and analysis tools are available at  this https URL .\n         \n       \n     \n     \n       [151] \n       \n        arXiv:2610.40545\n       \n      \n        [ pdf ,  html ,  other ]\n     \n     \n       \n          Title: \n          Retrieval as Traversal: Value-Indexed Memory for Stateful Long-Context Agents\n         \n          Daniel Strauß ,  Zhenshan Bing ,  Alois Knoll  \n\n          Comments: \n          Accepted at the NeurIPS 2026 Workshop on Long-Context Foundation Models (LCFM). 7 pages, 1 table\n         \n\n\n          Subjects: \n           Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI) \n         \n\n         \n          Long-context models repeatedly solve closely related retrieval problems. Attention or global retrieval can retain large stores, but each forward pass again searches for what matters. Recurrent compression avoids this search only by deciding earlier what information to preserve. Neither mechanism directly exploits that useful memories at neighbouring retrieval steps may substantially overlap. We propose an alternative in which retrieval is a persistent local traversal through an address space. We separate a memory's content from its address. Utility is defined by the information revealed by retrieving the content, while geometry determines only how cheaply that information can be reached. This leads to a cost-regularised indexing objective that preserves information value inside local neighbourhoods while charging for every memory made locally accessible. Its marginal-value form gives both attractive and exclusion pressures. Thus, complementary memories should be co-retrievable when the additional value exceeds retrieval cost, whereas redundant memories should not occupy the same retrieval set solely because they are individually useful. As a proof of principle, we test only the simplest fixed-geometry instance on MiniGrid Memory. A persistent local traversal head reaches $0.95$ mean return after $0.59$M training steps with a five-token context, compared with $0.94$M for Gaussian absolute addressing and more than $2$M for categorical absolute addressing. The experiment does not test learned geometry or repeated retrieval. Instead, it motivates the hypothesis that stateful retrieval may be easier to learn than stateless. To test the full proposed method further experimentation is necessary.\n         \n       \n     \n     \n       [152] \n       \n        arXiv:2610.40551\n       \n      \n        [ pdf ,  html ,  other ]\n     \n     \n       \n          Title: \n          Ramanujan Challenge Results: AI-Assisted Evaluation Pipeline for Multi-Format Proofs\n         \n          Alan Sorani ,  Ashvni Narayanan ,  Michael Shalyt ,  Tali Monderer ,  Rotem Kalisch ,  Hila Barkan ,  Elyasheev Leibtag ,  Shachar Weinbaum ,  Carsten Schneider ,  John Campbell ,  Ido Kaminer  \n\n\n\n          Subjects: \n           Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI) ; Computation and Language (cs.CL); Symbolic Computation (cs.SC); Number Theory (math.NT)\n         \n\n         \n          As AI's mathematical capabilities rapidly improve, meaningful benchmarks are shifting from university-level problems and math olympiads to research-level mathematics. The Ramanujan Challenge is a benchmark comprised of research-level problems in number theory, including unpublished results and open conjectures. It presented concrete formulas for mathematical constants whose correctness can be checked numerically, while their proofs may require non-obvious mathematics. Here, we analyze the Challenge submissions, measuring the relative success of different AI models, Computer Algebra Systems (CAS), and formal tools. We find correct submissions that are conceptually distinct from the official solutions, and a proof of one of the published open conjectures. Evaluating the submissions exposed a second challenge: judging complex proofs reliably at scale. We introduce an AI-assisted evaluation pipeline for natural language proofs, CAS code, and Lean formalizations that assesses semantic and logical correctness. Compared with the conventional one-shot LLM judgment, our pipeline reduces false-positive verdicts on natural language proofs from 23% to 6% and improves judgment accuracy on supporting code from 46% to 58%. This approach provides a methodology for evaluating research-level mathematics at scale, with potential applications across mathematics and AI for science.\n         \n       \n     \n     \n       [153] \n       \n        arXiv:2610.40556\n       \n      \n        [ pdf ,  html ,  other ]\n     \n     \n       \n          Title: \n          Advancing Clinical Genomic Analysis with a Skill-Centred Agent\n         \n          Qiaoyu Zheng ,  Weike Zhao ,  Yanjie Fan ,  Ye Cao ,  Mengqi Jiang ,  Xiao Zhou ,  Xiaoman Zhang ,  Pengcheng Qiu ,  Yongguo Yu ,  Yanfeng Wang ,  Ya Zhang ,  Chaoyi Wu ,  Weidi Xie  \n\n\n\n          Subjects: \n           Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI) ; Genomics (q-bio.GN)\n         \n\n         \n          Clinical genomic analysis requires experts to integrate evolving guidelines, databases, and computational evidence. As sequencing has become increasingly accessible, this complex and labor-intensive process has become a major bottleneck to the timely use of genomic information in clinical decision-making. To fill this gap, we developed ClinGeX, a large-language-model agent that composes 105 curated executable skills through task-specific retrieval and dependency-aware orchestration. On 223 temporally held-out benchmark cases, ClinGeX achieved 71.7\\% macro-averaged task-level concordance with expert reference classifications, compared with 45.7\\% for the strongest comparator. In a three-condition, counterbalanced human-AI collaboration study, 45 clinical genomic professionals assessed 135 clinically derived cases under routine (no AI assistance), DeepSeek-assisted and ClinGeX-assisted conditions. ClinGeX assistance increased raw case-level concordance across criterion-level variant analysis, genomic interpretation in rare disease and molecular oncology decision-making (51.1\\%, 57.8\\%, 64.4\\% versus 15.6\\%, 31.1\\%, 20.0\\% in comparator conditions) and reduced reported interpretation time in all scenarios. During an exploratory one-month real-world non-interventional assessment, 11 experienced professionals submitted feedback on 96 self-selected analysis episodes and reported frequent conclusion adoption and time savings. These findings support executable domain skills as a promising approach to augment specialist genomic interpretation.\n         \n       \n     \n     \n       [154] \n       \n        arXiv:2610.40560\n       \n      \n        [ pdf ,  html ,  other ]\n     \n     \n       \n          Title: \n          FARMS: A Verifiable Foundation for Automated Computational Science\n         \n          Di Wu ,  Ling Liang ,  Haizhao Yang  \n\n\n\n          Subjects: \n           Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI) ; Logic in Computer Science (cs.LO)\n         \n\n         \n          Automated computational science could accelerate discovery by selecting, adapting and developing numerical methods. This prospect requires mathematical knowledge that AI systems can interpret and reuse with explicit guarantees. Here we present FARMS, a faithful and efficient foundation that transforms computational mathematics into AI-ready computing knowledge. FARMS connects formal statements to source context, algorithmic meaning and quantitative guarantees through source preparation, reusable representations and semantic review. Across 171 targets in randomized numerical linear algebra and numerical partial differential equations, 157 were accepted by the pipeline, with mean estimated costs of US \\$0.85-2.29 per target across sources. Lean-checked implications from explicit source premises connect selected statements to computational skills, illustrated by a heat-flow solve and wave-response compression. FARMS therefore supports an affordable, reusable knowledge foundation for trustworthy automated computational science.\n         \n       \n     \n     \n       [155] \n       \n        arXiv:2610.40563\n       \n      \n        [ pdf ,  html ,  other ]\n     \n     \n       \n          Title: \n          Comparing Human Role-Players and LLM-Simulated Clients in Online Counselling Training: An Analysis of Counselling Patterns\n         \n          Eric Rudolph ,  Philipp Steigerwald ,  Jens Albrecht  \n\n          Comments: \n          Published at EDM 2025 (18th International Conference on Educational Data Mining), short paper. 7 pages\n         \n\n          Journal-ref: \n          Proceedings of the 18th International Conference on Educational Data Mining (EDM 2025), short papers\n         \n\n          Subjects: \n           Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI) ; Computation and Language (cs.CL)\n         \n\n         \n          This study investigates the capabilities of Large Language Models to simulate counselling clients in educational role-plays in comparison to human role-players. Initially, we recorded role-playing sessions, where novice counsellors interacted with human peers acting as clients, followed by role-plays between humans and clients simulated by Mistrals Mixtral 8x7b using 4-bit quantization. These interactions were analysed with a counselling communication pattern system at sentence level. We investigated two key questions: (1) to what extent LLM-generated responses replicate authentic conversational dynamics and (2) whether counsellors' communication behaviour differs when interacting with human versus LLM-simulated clients. The findings highlight both similarities and differences in the application of counselling patterns across scenarios, showing the potential of LLM-based role-playing exercises to enhance counselling competencies and to identify areas for further refinement in virtual client simulations.\n         \n       \n     \n     \n       [156] \n       \n        arXiv:2610.40578\n       \n      \n        [ pdf ,  html ,  other ]\n     \n     \n       \n          Title: \n          VeriCUA: Verification as Agentic Exploration for Computer-Using Agents\n         \n          Qiushi Sun ,  Da Yu ,  Alexander Knop ,  Amer Sinha ,  Lingpeng Kong ,  Ben Kao ,  Ravi Kumar ,  Pritish Kamath  \n\n          Comments: \n          Work in progress\n         \n\n\n          Subjects: \n           Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI) ; Computation and Language (cs.CL); Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)\n         \n\n         \n          Building and scaling computer-using agents (CUAs) across desktop, mobile, and web environments requires reliable task-completion verification for data curation, evaluation, and reinforcement learning. Typical automated approaches prompt vision-language models (VLMs) to judge trajectories or generate programs to inspect application state. However, both rely on partial views of complex digital environments. VLM judges are restricted to supplied observations, while generated programs depend on accessible state. These views can omit decisive evidence or leave completion judgments unsupported. Tailoring separate verifiers to different platforms and access settings adds further engineering cost. We present VeriCUA, a platform-agnostic framework for verification through agentic exploration. It treats verification as dynamic evidence gathering and assessment within a shared procedure for online and offline settings across platforms and observation modalities. After inspecting the available context to construct a task rubric, the verifier selects GUI or code checks for unresolved requirements and combines their findings under explicit evidence rules. Experiments show improved agreement with reference labels over the compared baselines across these settings, with the largest offline gains on hard cases and stronger rejection of false completion claims online. Further analyses connect these gains to complementary evidence and test-time exploration.\n         \n       \n     \n     \n       [157] \n       \n        arXiv:2610.40588\n       \n      \n        [ pdf ,  html ,  other ]\n     \n     \n       \n          Title: \n          Pre-Crime for Agents, at the Last Second: Probing When a Recruited Agent Gives In\n         \n          Leonidas Raghav  \n\n          Comments: \n          49 pages, 11 figures. Code:  this https URL \n         \n\n\n          Subjects: \n           Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI) \n         \n\n         \n          When AI agents share channels and credentials, one agent can press another to break its operator's rule, a first step towards collusion. Output filters check only what an agent has already written. Can an overseer tell beforehand that an agent is about to give in? We show that a linear probe on the agent's activations can, in a controlled multi-agent setting. Across six scenarios, a peer presses a target for up to 20 rounds to reveal a secret, such as a deploy token; the target often refuses, then gives in. Read at each model call before its output is written, the probe picks out the one that leaks. On held-out Qwen3-32B episodes, at a threshold set to flag 5% of benign calls, it catches 82% of leaking calls. At a similar flag rate, a zero-shot decision-model monitor catches 26%. On gpt-oss-20b, a probe on its reasoning reaches AUROC 0.91. A bag-of-words classifier trained on the same calls comes close, so most of the gain comes from training on labels, not activations. The probe also flags permitted disclosures: it reads the coming disclosure more than the violation. The warning is short-range, and halting on any alarm would stop most benign episodes, which a second text check does not fix. A warning raised before generation could let an overseer stop an agent that is about to give in, ahead of others acting on its messages or recruitment spreading through a swarm.\n         \n       \n     \n     \n       [158] \n       \n        arXiv:2610.40589\n       \n      \n        [ pdf ,  html ,  other ]\n     \n     \n       \n          Title: \n          Collaborative AI agents for autonomous experimental research with solid-state quantum spins\n         \n          El Mustapha Mansouri ,  Eikichi Kimura ,  Masato Koga ,  Keigo Arai  \n\n          Comments: \n          17 pages, 5 figures. Code available at  this https URL \n         \n\n\n          Subjects: \n           Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI) ; Multiagent Systems (cs.MA); Quantum Physics (quant-ph)\n         \n\n         \n          An autonomous research laboratory could conduct an entire study, from formulating questions to preparing manuscripts. Artificial intelligence (AI) offers one route towards this goal. Recent AI systems take on several research roles, but may draw conclusions that the evidence does not support. Here, we present an autonomous research laboratory in which agents based on large language models assess one another's work and can stop or redirect investigations. They can generate and reject research questions, require separate theoretical checks and conduct experiments designed to challenge their claims. We demonstrate these capabilities by investigating ensembles of quantum spins in diamond. The agents derived the minimum number of calibration settings needed to predict responses to a defined family of microwave pulse sequences. They checked the result computationally and designed experiments to distinguish competing predictions. An initial experiment agreed with the theoretical predictions, but repeated reference measurements showed that the signal was drifting during data collection. The agents therefore treated the result as inconclusive and revised the measurement protocol without relaxing the acceptance criteria. The revised experiment met those criteria and supported the theoretical predictions. This study demonstrates that collaborating AI agents can select research questions, derive theoretical results and test their predictions experimentally.\n         \n       \n     \n \n\n       \n     Cross submissions (showing 234 of 234 entries) \n\n\n     \n       [159] \n       \n        arXiv:2510.20952\n       \n          (cross-list from cs.LG)\n\n        [ pdf ,  html ,  other ]\n     \n     \n       \n          Title: \n          LLM-Integrated Bayesian State Space Models for Multimodal Time-Series Forecasting\n         \n          Sungjun Cho ,  Changho Shin ,  Suenggwan Jo ,  Xinya Yan ,  Shourjo Aditya Chaudhuri ,  Frederic Sala  \n\n          Comments: \n          15 pages, 8 figures\n         \n\n\n          Subjects: \n           Machine Learning (cs.LG) ; Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)\n         \n\n         \n          Forecasting in the real world requires integrating structured time-series data with unstructured textual information, but existing methods are architecturally limited by fixed input/output horizons and are unable to model or quantify uncertainty. We address this challenge by introducing LLM-integrated Bayesian State space models (LBS), a novel probabilistic framework for multimodal temporal forecasting. At a high level, LBS consists of two components: (1) a state space model (SSM) backbone that captures the temporal dynamics of latent states from which both numerical and textual observations are generated and (2) a pretrained large language model (LLM) that is adapted to encode textual inputs for posterior state estimation and decode textual forecasts consistent with the latent trajectory. This design enables flexible lookback and forecast windows, principled uncertainty quantification, and improved temporal generalization thanks to the well-suited inductive bias of SSMs toward modeling dynamical systems. Experiments on the TextTimeCorpus benchmark demonstrate that LBS improves the previous state-of-the-art by 13.20% while providing human-readable summaries of each forecast. Our work is the first to unify LLMs and SSMs for joint numerical and textual prediction, offering a novel foundation for multimodal temporal reasoning.\n         \n       \n     \n     \n       [160] \n       \n        arXiv:2610.37680\n       \n          (cross-list from cs.CL)\n\n        [ pdf ,  html ,  other ]\n     \n     \n       \n          Title: \n          Artifact Distillation: Training-Free Transfer from Frontier to Small Language Models\n         \n          Mustafa Safa Ozdayi ,  Somin Wadhwa  \n\n          Comments: \n          10 pages (4 pages main text), 2 figures, 3 tables\n         \n\n\n          Subjects: \n           Computation and Language (cs.CL) ; Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI); Machine Learning (cs.LG)\n         \n\n         \n          Closing the gap between frontier and smaller language models is usually approached by distillation: fine-tuning the small model (student) on a frontier model's (teacher) outputs, which binds transfer to training compute and leaves the transferred knowledge largely opaque. We propose Artifact Distillation: a teacher solves training instances and externalizes its problem-solving procedure into an artifact bank that a student queries at inference time, with no gradient updates. Across four LiveBench tasks, a bank written by Claude Opus 4.5 lets Claude Haiku 4.5 outperform unaided Claude Sonnet 4.5 on spatial reasoning (0.707 vs. 0.647) at 60% of its cost, and reach Sonnet 4.5's level on table joining at 89% of its cost. The same raw bank raises Qwen3.5-27B's pooled score from 0.730 to 0.800, showing that transfer is not confined to the teacher's model family.\n         \n       \n     \n     \n       [161] \n       \n        arXiv:2610.37681\n       \n          (cross-list from cs.CL)\n\n        [ pdf ,  html ,  other ]\n     \n     \n       \n          Title: \n          Supervised Fine-Tuning for Reasoning Large Language Models: A Survey\n         \n          Yongcan Yu ,  Kuangpu Guo ,  Jian Liang ,  Lingxiao He ,  Meng Wang ,  Ran He  \n\n          Comments: \n          Accepted to EMNLP 2026 Conference\n         \n\n\n          Subjects: \n           Computation and Language (cs.CL) ; Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI); Machine Learning (cs.LG)\n         \n\n         \n          Large reasoning models have recently demonstrated remarkable capabilities in solving complex tasks, where supervised fine-tuning (SFT) on long chain-of-thought data serves as a crucial foundation for eliciting and enhancing their reasoning abilities. Despite rapid progress in both improving and analyzing reasoning-oriented SFT, the field still lacks a systematic survey that consolidates its fast-growing literature. To fill this gap, we present a comprehensive review of recent advancements in reasoning SFT, examining the literature through the dual lenses of methodological design and analytical investigation. First, we review methodological improvements across the SFT pipeline and categorize them into data-centric approaches and algorithm-centric innovations. Second, we reorganize analytical studies along three dimensions: data characteristics, optimization dynamics, and mechanistic insights. Finally, by synthesizing current research focuses and remaining bottlenecks, we outline promising future directions for reasoning SFT. We hope this survey deepens the understanding of reasoning SFT and paves the way for advanced reasoning models.\n         \n       \n     \n     \n       [162] \n       \n        arXiv:2610.37682\n       \n          (cross-list from cs.CL)\n\n        [ pdf ,  html ,  other ]\n     \n     \n       \n          Title: \n          Rank-Aware Differentially Private Release of Listwise Preferences for LLM Alignment\n         \n          Junwei Chen ,  Manjiang Yu ,  Pengpeng Qiao ,  Yang Cao  \n\n\n\n          Subjects: \n           Computation and Language (cs.CL) ; Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)\n         \n\n         \n          Listwise preference feedback offers richer supervision for large language model (LLMs) alignment than pairwise comparisons, but a full ranking also reveals more sensitive user preferences. Existing privacy-preserving alignment methods focus mainly on pairwise feedback, while listwise alignment under local differential privacy (LDP) remains unexplored. Extending LDP to this listwise domain raises a critical challenge: the high sensitivity of the full ranking space demands excessive noise, neutralizing the benefits of listwise supervision. To address this challenge, we analyze the interaction between supervision granularity and privacy noise in downstream training, and introduce a rank-aware exponential mechanism that privatizes listwise preference data into a low-sensitivity granularity suitable for downstream alignment. The mechanism leverages ranking information to sample a fixed-size binary partition, concentrating the noise near borderline items instead of perturbing all items uniformly as randomized response (RR) does. Empirical evaluations on downstream LLM alignment tasks show that our mechanism consistently outperforms existing LDP baselines at matched privacy budgets. The code is available at  this https URL .\n         \n       \n     \n     \n       [163] \n       \n        arXiv:2610.37683\n       \n          (cross-list from cs.CL)\n\n        [ pdf ,  html ,  other ]\n     \n     \n       \n          Title: \n          Prompt-agnostic Language Models\n         \n          Frederic Sadrieh  \n\n          Comments: \n          Master's thesis, University of Potsdam, Hasso Plattner Institute, 2026, Parts of this work also appear in the EMNLP 2026 paper \"Prompt-Robust Language Models: Which Training Strategies Work?\" ( arXiv:2609.01217 )\n         \n\n\n          Subjects: \n           Computation and Language (cs.CL) ; Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI); Machine Learning (cs.LG)\n         \n\n         \n          Despite their strong performance, large language models remain highly sensitive to prompt formulation. This issue is typically addressed at inference-time through manual prompt optimization, which introduces overhead and lacks scalability. Conversely, we focus on train-time approaches that build prompt robustness into the model during instruction tuning. A common strategy is to mix multiple paraphrases of each prompt into training. We demonstrate that across trainable parameters, the per-paraphrasing gradients interfere, with 57% exhibiting sign conflicts and the naive gradient average discarding approximately a fifth of the magnitude. Therefore, we develop two gradient-merging methods, Prompt-TIES and Prompt-DARE, that address this interference by leveraging interference-resolution operators from model and gradient merging and applying them to prompt gradients. In this study, we compare the efficacy of our methods to increase model robustness to train-time robustness methods spanning all three established classes: data construction, consistency regularization, and contrastive alignment. The evaluation is conducted across four models from two families at two scales. We observe the interaction between prompt phrasing and the specific examples accounts for 54% of per-example variability on average, and exceeds 70% on tasks such as ANLI and WiC. The gradient-merging methods directly improve this local robustness and nearly halve per-example variance on fully fine-tuned models at little cost to overall accuracy. The other train-time methods enhance raw performance, but fail to improve per-example variance reliably. Code:  this https URL . Model checkpoints:  this https URL .\n         \n       \n     \n     \n       [164] \n       \n        arXiv:2610.37684\n       \n          (cross-list from cs.CL)\n\n        [ pdf ,  html ,  other ]\n     \n     \n       \n          Title: \n          An Introduction to Large Language Models for Scientists and Professionals\n         \n          Umberto Michelucci  \n\n\n\n          Subjects: \n           Computation and Language (cs.CL) ; Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)\n         \n\n         \n          Large Language Models (LLMs) have rapidly become important tools for science, education, medicine, and professional work. Despite their apparent simplicity from the user's perspective, effective and responsible use requires an understanding of how these systems work, what their limitations are, and how their outputs should be evaluated. I wrote this document as a basis for my lecture on LLM in the master in artificial intelligence in medicine at the university of Turin, Italy. This book provides a practical and accessible introduction to LLMs for scientists and professionals. It explains the fundamental ideas behind tokenisation, embeddings, training, inference, context windows, prompting, and instruction hierarchies, while deliberately avoiding mathematical detail. Particular attention is given to the limitations of LLM-generated answers, including hallucinations, uncertainty, reproducibility, bias, and the need for systematic verification and human oversight. The book also discusses privacy, governance, security, and the responsible handling of personal and sensitive information. More advanced topics include Retrieval-Augmented Generation (RAG), retrieval quality, chunking, embeddings, vector search, agents, tools, and workflows. A substantial practical exercise guides the reader through running a local LLM with Ollama and Open WebUI and using it to interact with a collection of scientific papers. The exercise emphasizes evidence retrieval, source verification, uncertainty, disagreement between studies, and the distinction between plausible language and scientifically supported conclusions. No programming knowledge is required.\n         \n       \n     \n     \n       [165] \n       \n        arXiv:2610.37685\n       \n          (cross-list from cs.CL)\n\n        [ pdf ,  html ,  other ]\n     \n     \n       \n          Title: \n          From Geometry to Behavior: How Instruction Exposure Unlocks Latent Rhetorical Directions in LLMs\n         \n          Shengjie Xu  \n\n          Comments: \n          Accepted\n         \n\n          Journal-ref: \n          Conference on Language Modeling (COLM 2026)\n         \n\n          Subjects: \n           Computation and Language (cs.CL) ; Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)\n         \n\n         \n          Language models do not merely memorize facts; they absorb rhetorical strategies from pretraining data, including the human tendency to reason by analogy. We ask: is this absorbed capability geometrically accessible before any instruction tuning, and what determines whether it can be activated at inference time?\n We extract an \"Analogy Vector\" from the residual stream via difference-in-means and show it enables steerable explanation style across seven instruction-tuned models (Gemma-2, Llama-3.1, Qwen-2.5; 2B-32B), improving harmonic mean from 3.22 to 3.94 (+22%) for Gemma-2-9B-IT on 300 held-out test prompts. A small human validation study confirms the LLM judge on Analogy Quality.\n Our central finding, from seven matched base-instruct pairs, is a gradient of behavioral accessibility: the analogy direction exists in every base model, but its behavioral accessibility under linear intervention scales continuously with instruction exposure during training. Gemma-2 base models (strict pretraining/IT separation) show flat steering landscapes: present but behaviorally inert. Qwen-2.5 base models (instruction data woven into pretraining) are already partially accessible before any explicit fine-tuning; base-instruct cosine similarity of the analogy vector reaches 0.94-0.97 for Qwen versus 0.37-0.66 for Gemma, providing geometric corroboration of the behavioral gradient. This challenges the binary \"base vs instruct\" framing: the relevant variable is how much instruction exposure a model has received across its full training pipeline. We position activation steering as a complementary interpretability tool that reveals how instruction exposure organizes rhetorical knowledge in activation space.\n         \n       \n     \n     \n       [166] \n       \n        arXiv:2610.37686\n       \n          (cross-list from cs.MA)\n\n        [ pdf ,  html ,  other ]\n     \n     \n       \n          Title: \n          Where Planning Changes the Outcome of Orbital Games\n         \n          Duncan Eddy ,  Mahdi Al-Husseini ,  Mykel J. Kochenderfer  \n\n          Comments: \n          IEEE Aerospace Conference 2027\n         \n\n\n          Subjects: \n           Multiagent Systems (cs.MA) ; Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI); Robotics (cs.RO); Systems and Control (eess.SY)\n         \n\n         \n          This paper introduces OrbitalGym, a simulation environment for studying when planning algorithms or system design determine the outcome of adversarial orbital encounters. In an orbital game, two non-cooperative teams of satellites maneuver during rendezvous and proximity operations to win a differential-reward objective. Advances in terrestrial game playing have prompted many methods for orbital games, but studies typically evaluate one method in one scenario, often against a passive or random defender. It is unclear whether better planning matters once vehicle capability (maneuverability, sensing, communications) and adversarial responses are considered.\n OrbitalGym is a JAX-based environment supporting varying numbers of agents, state representations (position, velocity, attitude, fuel), explicit or implicit communication, and proximity or conical field-of-view sensors. It compiles games to batched GPU code, enabling large-scale parallel simulation of policies across many configurations. This identifies regimes where policy choice affects outcomes and regimes where system capability dominates.\n We study two close-range games: pursuit-evasion, where one spacecraft tries to capture another, and lady-bandit-guard, where a guard prevents an attacker from reaching a protected asset. We compare PD, LQR, model predictive path integral control, and Monte Carlo tree search over ratios of thrust and propellant, initial separation, game duration, sensor capability, and the definition of capture. In pursuit-evasion the propellant ratio decides most encounters: against five of six evader policies, pursuer policy changes capture probability by 10 percent or more in at most 15 of 90 capability configurations. In lady-bandit-guard, policy matters more: against bandits able to reach the asset, a better guard policy raises defense probability by at least 10 percent in 56 to 74 of 90 configurations.\n         \n       \n     \n     \n       [167] \n       \n        arXiv:2610.37688\n       \n          (cross-list from cs.CL)\n\n        [ pdf ,  html ,  other ]\n     \n     \n       \n          Title: \n          One Confidence Threshold, Unequal Coverage: Language Gaps in Typed Decision Interfaces and How Translating Inputs Reduces Them\n         \n          Junwei Yao ,  Yongxi Zhou ,  Zike Yang ,  Zhijun Wang ,  Jianan Liu ,  Yanxian Chen ,  Wenbo Ye  \n\n\n\n          Subjects: \n           Computation and Language (cs.CL) ; Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI); Machine Learning (cs.LG)\n         \n\n         \n          Deployed classifiers often act on a prediction only when its confidence clears a threshold and pass the rest to a person. We ask whether one threshold serves all languages equally on typed decision interfaces, APIs that return a probability for each option of a fixed multiple-choice question. In a confirmatory study with a written analysis plan, three vendors' interfaces classified 1,000 MASSIVE items, each in 51 languages, with a threshold of 0.9. Even when a request is answered correctly in both English and another language, the English version clears the threshold more often, by 8.6-11.7 percentage points on average. Observed coverage, the share of requests handled automatically, is lower than English's in every other language, while the requests the gate handles are rarely wrong. The gap remains when every language is reweighted to the same accuracy, and a standard calibration metric (ECE) does not detect it on one interface. In exploratory analyses, English's coverage stays higher when each language gets its own threshold fitted to the same 5% error target. Translating inputs into English nearly closes the gap at 0.9 but leaves English ahead at that error target. Requests written natively in African languages show a gap in the same direction when English is their human translation. We recommend reporting per-language coverage at the operating threshold.\n         \n       \n     \n     \n       [168] \n       \n        arXiv:2610.37689\n       \n          (cross-list from cs.SI)\n\n        [ pdf ,  other ]\n     \n     \n       \n          Title: \n          GenAI as an 'Invisible Hand' in Opinion Dynamics: Evidence from a Group-Level Simulation Study\n         \n          Thomas Grisold ,  Johannes Schneider  \n\n\n\n          Subjects: \n           Social and Information Networks (cs.SI) ; Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)\n         \n\n         \n          Recent arguments suggest that genAI can act as an invisible hand that gradually shapes social structures, such as collective beliefs and norms. The argument is that if millions of people repeatedly consult genAI tools, their generated outcomes quietly shape what becomes acceptable, reasonable or conventional. We study this in the context of collective opinion dynamics, i.e., how groups form opinions around controversial dynamics. We present the findings of a simulation including 50,000 group discussions across 50 controversial statements, three LLMs, and different social configurations. We vary two characteristics of LLM interaction-agreeableness and personalization-and compare the influence of genAI in homogeneous and heterogeneous groups. Our findings indicate that profile-specific personalization increases LLM influence when models adopt a non-neutral stance, i.e., when they agree or disagree with the user. Furthermore, we find that neutral LLMs have a stronger effect in homogeneous groups. Based on our simulation, we generate a set of hypotheses to specify how, why and when AI-related features and social configurations jointly shape collective opinion making.\n         \n       \n     \n     \n       [169] \n       \n        arXiv:2610.37690\n       \n          (cross-list from cs.MM)\n\n        [ pdf ,  html ,  other ]\n     \n     \n       \n          Title: \n          Cassette-Editor: A Video Editing Agent That Edits the Way You Do\n         \n          Zhixin Zeng ,  Jiahao Zhong  \n\n          Comments: \n          8 pages, 4 figures, 2 tables. Preprint; evaluation to be extended in a future version\n         \n\n\n          Subjects: \n           Multimedia (cs.MM) ; Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)\n         \n\n         \n          Language agents increasingly work by calling tools on a persistent artifact, such as a code repository, and the result of each call shapes their next step. Video editing is a demanding case of this pattern: what a frame shows depends on how every clip is trimmed, retimed, cropped, and nested, so knowledge about the footage holds only for particular uses of it, and an invalid edit can leave the timeline structurally broken. Existing video editing agents keep their edits valid with critic agents, reviewers, or restricted output formats. We present Cassette-Editor, a video editing agent with a deliberately simple interface: one tool reads the project and one tool writes to it. Every write passes through the same executor, rules, and commit service as the user's manual edits, and either commits atomically or is rejected with located diagnostics and repair options, while the model keeps the creative choices and decides how to recover. Reads relate stored analysis of the footage to each use of it on the timeline. In a controlled experiment, a real model is shown 277 of its own committed edits after damaging them. It corrected nearly every call with full or minimal feedback, but locating each problem cut the input tokens a correction needed to less than half, and its corrections dropped other values the model had written in one case rather than seven.\n         \n       \n     \n     \n       [170] \n       \n        arXiv:2610.37692\n       \n          (cross-list from cs.CL)\n\n        [ pdf ,  other ]\n     \n     \n       \n          Title: \n          Bouba Beats Kiki, and Other Regularities in the Subjective Preferences of LLMs\n         \n          Katy Blumer ,  Jon Kleinberg ,  Ravi Kumar ,  Andrew Tomkins  \n\n          Comments: \n          10 pages, 5 figures in main text; 27 pages, 13 figures in supplement. To appear at NeurIPS 2026\n         \n\n\n          Subjects: \n           Computation and Language (cs.CL) ; Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI); Computers and Society (cs.CY); Machine Learning (cs.LG)\n         \n\n         \n          Users engage with large language models on tasks that are purely objective (\"When was Netflix founded?\"), purely subjective (\"What should I name my child?\") or anywhere in-between. We have rich tools for evaluating model accuracy with respect to objective criteria. However, when it comes to subjective aspects of a task, we do not have similarly developed methodology at hand. In this paper, we take some early steps towards framing the question of subjective evaluation. Rather than measure how often a model is right or wrong on a question, we instead ask, how do models make choices among multiple viable responses without a clear winner?\n To study this question, we restrict our focus to short-form content, and consider comparison tasks among three such content types: numbers, baby names, and fictional cities. We identify 29 aspects of these short-form examples covering many areas of syntax and semantics (length, alphabetic order, popularity, vowel mix, spoken sound, etc.). We show first that models tend to have strong and reproducible preferences for or against some of these aspects. Second, we show that variations in how the model is asked to express a preference may cause some of these aspects to activate or deactivate in the model's selection. Third, we show that overall preference distributions among pairs may vary radically based on seemingly-unimportant variations in the nature of the question posed to the model, raising new questions about manipulation. And finally, we show that, if models are used both to create lists of alternatives and to rank them, there are unexpected interactions between the two stages that may also drive the models' stated preferences.\n         \n       \n     \n     \n       [171] \n       \n        arXiv:2610.37699\n       \n          (cross-list from cs.MA)\n\n        [ pdf ,  html ,  other ]\n     \n     \n       \n          Title: \n          Network Topology Does Not Matter: The Breakdown of Classical Consensus in LLM Populations\n         \n          Sahil Rajesh Dhayalkar  \n\n          Comments: \n          19 pages, 3 figures, 3 tables\n         \n\n\n          Subjects: \n           Multiagent Systems (cs.MA) ; Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)\n         \n\n         \n          As multi-agent language model networks become widely deployed, predicting their collective behavior is a critical challenge. Classical distributed consensus theory relies on network topology to predict convergence, structurally assuming that agent updates are convex combinations, population means are conserved, and weights are content-independent. In this work, we demonstrate that these foundational assumptions fail for large language models (LLMs). By deterministically extracting continuous credence from token-level probabilities across eight instruction-tuned models and 73,080 controlled exchanges, we directly measure the empirical LLM update operator. We find that LLMs systematically violate convexity by updating outside the hull of observed opinions, exhibiting active repulsion (negative weights) that fundamentally breaks row-stochastic modeling and standard bounded-confidence repairs. Scaling these empirically fitted operators to simulations of 10,000 agents reveals that algebraic connectivity explains almost none of the final outcome variance. Instead, population dynamics are governed entirely by the model's internal update bias and message framing. Consequently, populations of several models, including those with up to 7B parameters, reliably compound small local drifts to reach confident agreement on objectively false claims regardless of their network structure. Our results prove that classical graph-theoretic metrics are inadequate for LLM populations, highlighting a profound deployment risk where multi-agent deliberation predictably saturates in collective delusion.\n         \n       \n     \n     \n       [172] \n       \n        arXiv:2610.37700\n       \n          (cross-list from cs.MA)\n\n        [ pdf ,  html ,  other ]\n     \n     \n       \n          Title: \n          The Coordination Tax and the Heterogeneity Dividend: Break-Even Conditions for Multi-Agent LLM Pipelines\n         \n          Andreas Zettl ,  Diego Gosmar  \n\n          Comments: \n          16 pages, 3 figures, 4 tables\n         \n\n\n          Subjects: \n           Multiagent Systems (cs.MA) ; Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)\n         \n\n         \n          Multi-agent LLM pipelines built on interoperability protocols such as the Open Floor Protocol (OFP) or A2A are usually justified on capability grounds, with their runtime cost treated as an overhead of unknown size. Comparing such a pipeline with one model call is underspecified, because a pipeline can give each role a different model. We give a closed-form model that sets the coordination tax (decomposition overhead plus per-handoff protocol cost) against the heterogeneity dividend from routing work to a cheaper model, with a break-even threshold and a feasibility bound, $\\alpha < 1/r$, and measure every term, with OFP as the protocol under test, on 135 HumanEval and ClassEval tasks in 2430 runs of six architectures with Claude Opus 5.5 and Haiku 5.5. The protocol adds 7.2 ms per handoff, 0.11% of pipeline wall time. Decomposition does not pay on these tasks: a planner-coder-reviewer pipeline costs 3.22x as much as a single strong call, takes 3.24x as long, and solves no more tasks. Moving planner and reviewer to the cheap model cuts the pipeline's cost to 0.39x but changes pass@1 by -6.7 points, leaving it dearer, slower and less accurate than the single call, and the break-even forecast misses by -19% because the cheap roles inflate token volume. The saving lies in choosing a model per task instead: an oracle choice between the two single calls solves 94.3% of runs at half the strong call's cost per solved task.\n         \n       \n     \n     \n       [173] \n       \n        arXiv:2610.37701\n       \n          (cross-list from cs.IR)\n\n        [ pdf ,  html ,  other ]\n     \n     \n       \n          Title: \n          DuQUAD: A Dual-View Framework with Quality-Aware Evidence Pruning for Multi-Document Question Answering\n         \n          Yeonjun Choi ,  Seyeon Jeong ,  Jong Wook Kim ,  Beakcheol Jang  \n\n\n\n          Subjects: \n           Information Retrieval (cs.IR) ; Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI); Computation and Language (cs.CL)\n         \n\n         \n          Large language models with retrieval-augmented generation systematically underutilize evidence in the middle of multi-document contexts, a phenomenon known as Lost in the Middle. Existing mitigation strategies, such as document reordering and attention steering, can reduce boundary bias but remain brittle in noisy settings, where inaccurate relevance estimation and spurious documents still obscure useful evidence in central positions. We propose DuQUAD, a multi-document QA framework that mitigates positional bias through dual-view reasoning and quality-aware evidence pruning. DuQUAD combines a Local Agent guided by a Structural Fusion Score with a Global Agent that reasons over the full document set, providing both targeted evidence recovery and broad contextual understanding. The framework further filters candidate evidence using explicit quality scoring and refines it at the sentence level to reduce noise. DuQUAD consistently outperforms strong Lost-in-the-Middle mitigation baselines, including hybrid retrieval and reranking, multi-agent reasoning, and context optimization methods, improving accuracy by 3.0% on average over the strongest competing baseline and gold evidence recall by 9.4%, while substantially reducing computational cost compared to existing multi-agent frameworks.\n         \n       \n     \n     \n       [174] \n       \n        arXiv:2610.37706\n       \n          (cross-list from cs.AR)\n\n        [ pdf ,  html ,  other ]\n     \n     \n       \n          Title: \n          Search-Guided Reasoning Trace Curation for Verilog Generation\n         \n          Matthew DeLorenzo ,  Kevin Tieu ,  Prithwish Jana ,  Piyush Jha ,  Dileep Kalathil ,  Vijay Ganesh ,  Jeyavijayan Rajendran  \n\n\n\n          Subjects: \n           Hardware Architecture (cs.AR) ; Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)\n         \n\n         \n          Recent advances in large language models (LLMs) have improved Verilog generation. However, most training pipelines distill natural-language reasoning traces whose intermediate design decisions are weakly structured and are primarily selected according to the correctness of the final generated RTL. We present MCTS-SFT, a verifier-guided framework for curating reasoning traces for supervised fine-tuning (SFT) of Verilog generation models. MCTS-SFT formulates trace construction as search over intermediate reasoning states and uses Monte Carlo Tree Search (MCTS) to propagate functional feedback from complete Verilog rollouts to earlier design decisions. Within this framework, we introduce Plan-MCTS, which searches over compact hardware-design plans that explicitly represent interfaces, timing, state, behavior, and update rules. We further study plan-preserving supervision, in which the searched structured state is retained as part of the downstream SFT target. Our experiments find that Plan-MCTS achieves the highest functional trace yield, with 84.2% correct traces compared with 81.9% for one-shot structured planning and 82.0% for unguided tree exploration. For identical Plan-MCTS trajectories and Verilog outputs, retaining the explicit searched plan during SFT raises pass@10 from 52.56% to 57.7%, demonstrating that the representation of intermediate design decisions materially affects downstream learning. In a separate 6,000-trace study, MCTS-curated supervision improves pass@10 over full-response chain-of-thought distillation by up to 3.7 points while reducing downstream generation length by approximately 50% on VerilogEval-v2. These results demonstrate that structured hardware planning, verifier-guided search, and explicit intermediate-state supervision provide complementary mechanisms for constructing more effective and token-efficient training data for Verilog generation.\n         \n       \n     \n     \n       [175] \n       \n        arXiv:2610.37722\n       \n          (cross-list from cs.CR)\n\n        [ pdf ,  html ,  other ]\n     \n     \n       \n          Title: \n          FVH-Bench: Evaluating Image Generator Safety Beyond Overtly Harmful Imagery\n         \n          Zihao Zhu ,  Yuhan Liu ,  Siwei Lyu ,  Baoyuan Wu  \n\n\n\n          Subjects: \n           Cryptography and Security (cs.CR) ; Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)\n         \n\n         \n          Modern image generators can produce complex visual artifacts, including diagrams, interfaces, and records that combine text, graphics, and layout to convey detailed information. They may refuse requests for overtly harmful imagery yet still generate artifacts that guide dangerous actions or support deception. We call the risk arising from what such images enable, rather than only what they depict, functional visual harm (FVH). Existing safety benchmarks focus largely on overtly harmful content, such as pornography and graphic violence, leaving image generators' safety alignment against FVH insufficiently evaluated. To address this gap, we introduce FVH-Bench, comprising 669 prompts across five harmful visual functions and ten risk domains. The 11 evaluated image generators rarely refuse these requests and frequently generate artifacts that fulfill the targeted harmful functions. High scores for target harm realization and harmful artifact usability further indicate that these outputs can support the intended harmful purposes. A further analysis finds that existing safety detectors also provide limited coverage of FVH, indicating gaps in their ability to recognize harmful visual functions. Together, these findings show that evaluating image-generator safety alignment requires considering not only what images depict, but also what they may enable.\n         \n       \n     \n     \n       [176] \n       \n        arXiv:2610.37727\n       \n          (cross-list from cs.LG)\n\n        [ pdf ,  html ,  other ]\n     \n     \n       \n          Title: \n          Predicate Neuron: Differentiable Quantization of Predicate Relations in Gradient-based Learning\n         \n          Ioannis Konstantoulas ,  Iliana Loi ,  Kyriakos Sgarbas  \n\n          Comments: \n          23 pages, 9 figures\n         \n\n\n          Subjects: \n           Machine Learning (cs.LG) ; Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)\n         \n\n         \n          In machine learning, symbolic reasoning approaches often rely on static architectures that hinder integration into deep neural networks and gradient-based optimization. To address this, we introduce the Native Predicate Neuron, a differentiable logic-reasoning neural module that enriches standard architectures with symbolic reasoning capabilities via first-order logic quantization. The proposed neuron models relational logic by learning to alternate between operations using a single trainable parameter. It features a tailored gradient mechanism with a dual-nature formulation: executing hard logical operations during training while maintaining differentiability for backpropagation. By quantizing both its input and choice state, the Predicate Neuron approximates continuous and non-continuous functions, effectively resolving discontinuities that standard deep learning architectures struggle with. We evaluate the Predicate Neuron across Fully-Connected, Convolutional, Recurrent, and Transformer architectures on benchmarks like MNIST and Wikitext. Results show that incorporating Predicate Neurons accelerates convergence, boosts accuracy, and lowers loss in general, with particularly strong improvements in tasks requiring monotonic reasoning or handling discontinuities. Overall, the Predicate Neuron natively integrates into standard neural networks to enable fuzzy logic operations and is available in Keras and PyTorch.\n         \n       \n     \n     \n       [177] \n       \n        arXiv:2610.37728\n       \n          (cross-list from cs.IR)\n\n        [ pdf ,  other ]\n     \n     \n       \n          Title: \n          Beyond Similarity Search: Knowledge Graph Metadata Enrichment for Enterprise Retrieval-Augmented Generation\n         \n          Fakabbir Amin  \n\n\n\n          Subjects: \n           Information Retrieval (cs.IR) ; Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI); Machine Learning (cs.LG)\n         \n\n         \n          A key challenge of RAG systems is retrieving information from enterprise documents created by multiple teams over time, where it is not possible to enrich the information using the latest information and metadata available outside the scope of organizational documentation responsibility. We investigated whether using a knowledge graph to store metadata and frequently changing information would improve upon knowledge retrieval over similarity search over embeddings. To perform the experiment, we created a snapshot of the SEC EDGAR dataset for a quarter, comprising 167,050 filings from 10,511 companies and 508,714 embedded chunks of risk-factor text. To simulate the enrichment, we created a knowledge graph of 430,257 nodes and 561,144 edges carrying an accession number on every edge. To evaluate the gain obtained via a graph-based design, we evaluated three architectures: text-to-SQL over typed columns, dense-vector RAG with HNSW cosine search, and a two-stage GraphRAG design. All architectures share the same model and temperature to allow comparison. Results are scored programmatically by an LLM as judge over 20 questions divided across four categories, each question asked three times. GraphRAG solved 16 of 20 questions on all three attempts, compared to 10 for text-to-SQL and 3 for vector RAG. GraphRAG lost on structured aggregation queries. Vector RAG lost the single-document category it was expected to win, passing 3 of 15 where GraphRAG passed 15 of 15, because company attribution is not recoverable from cosine similarity at this scale. All six confident falsehoods in the benchmark were text-to-SQL conflating distinct people who share a surname, the failure that resolving identity to an identifier before reading any text prevents structurally; GraphRAG made none. We claim no advantage beyond this corpus, this model and this question set.\n         \n       \n     \n     \n       [178] \n       \n        arXiv:2610.37733\n       \n          (cross-list from cs.LG)\n\n        [ pdf ,  html ,  other ]\n     \n     \n       \n          Title: \n          Selective Transfer Learning for Groundwater Level Anomaly Forecasting: Accuracy-Risk Trade-offs\n         \n          Yaxuan Wu ,  Ali Mohammed Mansoor Alsahag ,  Seyed Sahand Mohammadi Ziabari  \n\n\n\n          Subjects: \n           Machine Learning (cs.LG) ; Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)\n         \n\n         \n          This work studies context-aware selective transfer learning for multi-well groundwater anomaly forecasting in the Netherlands. Source-domain pretraining can raise average accuracy but is not uniformly reliable: it degrades performance on a substantial fraction of heterogeneous wells. Context-Aware Selective Learning (CASL) uses calibration-trained, constrained contextual blending of frozen no-transfer and fixed-transfer parents. Global Convex Blend uses the same parents with one calibration-fitted weight per seed. The corrected task requires nine consecutive observed monthly inputs and an exact three-calendar-month target, without interpolation. Evaluation covers 457 wells and 13,718 test windows under five predefined seeds, with the four-cell maxsim0.60 source pool frozen using Always-TL validation RMSE alone. Fixed transfer harms 18.38-51.86% of wells. CASL has 0.424-1.084% lower RMSE than No TL, partially rescuing 97.53-99.50% and fully rescuing 23.46-43.78% of fixed-harmed wells. Partial rescue can arise structurally from convex blending and does not establish contextual superiority. Global Convex Blend protects more wells in four seeds, while CASL has slightly lower pooled RMSE in four. Neither dominates: the results support an accuracy-risk trade-off on the fixed split, not rolling deployment validity.\n         \n       \n     \n     \n       [179] \n       \n        arXiv:2610.37734\n       \n          (cross-list from cs.CR)\n\n        [ pdf ,  html ,  other ]\n     \n     \n       \n          Title: \n          When Skills Tell Agents Not to Ask: A Large-Scale Study of Oversight Directives in Agent Skills\n         \n          Timofey Sanko ,  Yuan Tian  \n\n          Comments: \n          preprint\n         \n\n\n          Subjects: \n           Cryptography and Security (cs.CR) ; Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI); Software Engineering (cs.SE)\n         \n\n         \n          Agent skills, reusable instruction packages centred on a  this http URL  file, extend AI agents on demand, and a single line in a widely copied skill reaches every agent that loads it. Some lines are oversight-loosening directives: they tell the agent to proceed without approval, to bypass checks with --no-verify, or to keep working without checking in. Although such directives can remove a checkpoint from every agent that loads them, no prior work has examined them. We present the first large-scale study of oversight-loosening directives, analyzing 1.53 million English skills from GitSkills. We split skills into units (sentences, bullets, table cells, and code lines), develop a codebook that labels oversight directives by polarity, mechanism, and scope, label 2,097 units by hand, and train a detector to scale the analysis. Our results show that about one in seventeen skills (about 88,000 skills) contains a loosening directive. These directives most often loosen oversight through permission flags, and most of them (70.1%) apply unconditionally rather than only in specific contexts such as CI. Compared with skills that have the same amount of oversight language, skills that run shell code, install packages, or use sudo have 1.7-1.8 times the odds of containing a loosening directive, an association that holds across all our robustness checks, and they do not compensate with more tightening. Loosening spreads through a few upstream skills rather than through edits by their adopters: 2.4% of the skill families that carry loosening account for half of all copies of loosening-carrying skills, and 1.1% of distinct loosening units for half of all loosening occurrences. Open-source skill scanners catch at most 46% of loosening directives, with a precision below 0.4, while the LLM behind them, prompted with our codebook instead, catches 61% at a precision of 0.71.\n         \n       \n     \n     \n       [180] \n       \n        arXiv:2610.37738\n       \n          (cross-list from cs.CR)\n\n        [ pdf ,  html ,  other ]\n     \n     \n       \n          Title: \n          Poisoned Skills: Propagating Jailbreaks Through Agent Markdown Files\n         \n          Kashyap R Manjusha ,  Murali Emani  \n\n\n\n          Subjects: \n           Cryptography and Security (cs.CR) ; Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)\n         \n\n         \n          Modern agent frameworks allow users to provide Markdown files containing system prompts, tool descriptions, and skill definitions (e.g.,  this http URL ), which are incorporated into the agent's context during initialization. In this work, we investigate whether this configuration mechanism can also serve as an injection channel: can a jailbreak embedded in a Markdown skill file propagate through an agent and induce prohibited behavior? To answer this question, we construct a LangChain-based question-answering agent whose system prompt is assembled from a user-provided Markdown file. We embed a jailbreak combining persona manipulation with a GPTFUZZER-based attack and evaluate its effectiveness across seven contemporary open-weight models using 270 forbidden questions spanning nine harm categories. Under a benign system prompt, the models exhibit near-complete refusal behavior, with a mean attack success rate (ASR) of just 0.5%. In contrast, the injected skill file raises the mean ASR to 45%, reaching 68.9% for individual models. We further evaluate two payload obfuscation strategies: an invisible zero-width character-based stealth payload and a phonetic payload based on grapheme-to-phoneme transformations. Neither outperforms the plain-text injection; notably, the phonetic variant reduces mean ASR to 30%. These findings identify Markdown skill files as an underexplored attack surface for AI agents and suggest that vulnerability to configuration-based jailbreaks depends more strongly on the underlying model than on the specific payload obfuscation technique.\n         \n       \n     \n     \n       [181] \n       \n        arXiv:2610.37740\n       \n          (cross-list from eess.IV)\n\n        [ pdf ,  html ,  other ]\n     \n     \n       \n          Title: \n          EUReCA: End-to-End Unified CBCT Reconstruction Across Clinical Acquisitions\n         \n          Jiening Zhu ,  Chengzhu Zhang ,  Jason Fan ,  LiCheng Kuo ,  Weixing Cai ,  Xiuxiu He ,  Laura Cervino ,  Jean Moran ,  Xiang Li ,  Tianfang Li ,  Yabo Fu  \n\n\n\n          Subjects: \n           Image and Video Processing (eess.IV) ; Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)\n         \n\n         \n          Cone-beam computed tomography (CBCT) is widely used for image-guided radiotherapy, but reducing the number of projections to lower dose and scan time makes reconstruction increasingly ill-posed. We present EUReCA (End-to-end Unified REconstruction across Clinical Acquisitions), a geometry-aware feed-forward model for variable-view clinical linac CBCT. For a given acquisition trajectory, EUReCA accepts 8-100 projections with a single set of weights, without view-count-specific retraining; the same architecture and training recipe extend unchanged to other clinical trajectories. It combines learned projection features with an in-frame Feldkamp-Davis-Kress (FDK) reconstruction. The model encodes each projection with a shared multiscale backbone, back-projects features into multiresolution 3D grids using the acquisition geometry, aggregates variable numbers of views at fixed computational cost, and refines the volume with a global 3D transformer and multilevel decoder. On simulated data, EUReCA achieved 29.9-33.2 dB PSNR with 10-100 views and outperformed all applicable feed-forward baselines; it also exceeded iterative optimization-based methods while running orders of magnitude faster. On 15 measured patient CBCT scans evaluated against vendor reconstructions, EUReCA achieved the best performance at every tested view count. These results suggest that geometry-aware, measurement-anchored feed-forward reconstruction can provide a practical and flexible foundation for variable-view linac CBCT.\n         \n       \n     \n     \n       [182] \n       \n        arXiv:2610.37788\n       \n          (cross-list from cs.CL)\n\n        [ pdf ,  html ,  other ]\n     \n     \n       \n          Title: \n          Locating Sycophancy Across Training Stages in Open Language Models\n         \n          Jiarui Yao ,  Shweta Parihar ,  Nghia Tuan Bui ,  Guergana K Savova ,  Lijing Wang  \n\n          Comments: \n          under review\n         \n\n\n          Subjects: \n           Computation and Language (cs.CL) ; Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)\n         \n\n         \n          Language model assistants sometimes change a correct answer when the user suggests a wrong one. The behavior, called sycophancy, is usually attributed to preference-based post-training, yet early measurements found it in pretrained models. We ask where it comes from by measuring it at every available stage of training: 13 open checkpoints from 5 lineages, from base model through supervised fine-tuning and preference optimization to release. We separate how far an injected claim moves a model's answer from how firmly the answer was held, and vary who makes the claim, how reliable they are said to be, and how strongly they make it. Two sycophancy behaviors with different origins emerge, and both replicate across datasets. Deference to a claim in the prompt is already present in every base model, which ranks sources in the same order as its post-trained descendants. Capitulation in conversation, dropping a stated answer when the user objects, is largely absent from base models and present in every post-trained checkpoint. When the objection names an alternative, most post-trained models give up firmly held answers as often as loosely held ones. We discuss the implications of these measurements for evaluating sycophancy and approaches for addressing each behavior.\n         \n       \n     \n     \n       [183] \n       \n        arXiv:2610.37796\n       \n          (cross-list from cs.LG)\n\n        [ pdf ,  html ,  other ]\n     \n     \n       \n          Title: \n          Spokes, not a Ring: Revisiting the Weekday Manifold in Language Models\n         \n          Diksha Gupta ,  Rieke Fruengel ,  Stefan Heimersheim  \n\n\n\n          Subjects: \n           Machine Learning (cs.LG) ; Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)\n         \n\n         \n          Recent work has hypothesised that large language models may represent some concepts as multi-dimensional manifolds instead of single directions. Using these manifolds to interpret or steer representations often assumes that moving along them corresponds to a continuous change in the represented concept. In this work, we test this continuous manifold assumption on the prototypical weekday manifold. Despite its striking circular geometry, we find that the weekday representations behave more like seven discrete states arranged in a circle than a continuous ring. First, we show that local sensitivity has seven minima aligned with the weekdays, producing a step-like mapping between representation and behaviour under on-manifold steering. Second, we test whether any concepts are represented along the manifold outside the seven discrete states, and find neither time of day, uncertainty, nor natural text populate the intermediate space. Together, these results challenge the continuous interpretation of the weekday manifold and motivate testing whether movement along other representation manifolds correspond to meaningful changes in model representations and behaviour.\n         \n       \n     \n     \n       [184] \n       \n        arXiv:2610.37819\n       \n          (cross-list from cs.LG)\n\n        [ pdf ,  html ,  other ]\n     \n     \n       \n          Title: \n          Responsible Quantum Machine Learning for Medical Imaging: A Fairness Audit Across Sensitive Subgroups\n         \n          Giovanni dos Santos Franco ,  Felipe Mahlow ,  Ellison Fernando Cardoso ,  José Adenaldo Santos Bittencourt ,  Felipe Fanchini  \n\n\n\n          Subjects: \n           Machine Learning (cs.LG) ; Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI); Computers and Society (cs.CY)\n         \n\n         \n          Quantum machine learning (QML) is increasingly explored for medical classification, yet its behaviour under subgroup-aware fairness criteria remains understudied. We present an empirical fairness audit of classical and quantum classifiers for medical imaging, assuming QML is not inherently fair. Two scenarios are evaluated: dermatological lesions on DDI (sensitive attribute: Fitzpatrick skin type) and chest X-rays on CheXpert-derived data (sensitive attribute: age group). Using ResNet-18 embeddings, we train SVM, MLP, QSVM and QCNN under a baseline and three mitigation settings: differentiable Equalized Odds (EO) regularisation, Gradient Reversal Learning (GRL), and both combined. Foundation models (PanDerm, EVA-X) serve as external references. Quantum classifiers may achieve competitive aggregate performance while still exhibiting subgroup disparities. Since a smaller subgroup AUC gap may stem from degrading the best group rather than improving the worst, each mitigation is also tested against a pre-specified non-inferiority margin relative to its baseline. Under this criterion, several configurations with the smallest disparity, notably QCNN with GRL on DDI, are not clinically acceptable due to loss of global discrimination. On DDI, QSVM with GRL+EO performed best and approached PanDerm in the performance/fairness trade-off; on CheXpert, QCNN with GRL achieved the highest aggregate performance but increased disparity, and EVA-X remained stronger. Fairness effects are model- and dataset-dependent, so QML requires empirical subgroup-aware evaluation. All quantum models run in classical simulation; we claim no quantum advantage.\n         \n       \n     \n     \n       [185] \n       \n        arXiv:2610.37826\n       \n          (cross-list from cs.CL)\n\n        [ pdf ,  html ,  other ]\n     \n     \n       \n          Title: \n          DeFT-RAG: Incrementally Decomposing Multi-Hop Questions into Self-Contained Sub-Questions with Reusable Answers\n         \n          Dingyi Kang ,  Bingzhe Li  \n\n          Comments: \n          12 pages, 2 figures, 7 tables. Code:  this https URL \n         \n\n\n          Subjects: \n           Computation and Language (cs.CL) ; Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI); Information Retrieval (cs.IR)\n         \n\n         \n          Retrieval-augmented generation (RAG) lets a large language model (LLM) answer questions by retrieving relevant passages from a large corpus. However, RAG struggles with multi-hop questions, which require chaining facts spread across several documents through intermediate entities that the question never mentions. Graph-based RAG methods retrieve once from the entities a question names and so rarely recover the full evidence chain. Iterative RAG methods recover it step by step, but their accuracy is limited by the many tasks they ask the LLM to perform, such as planning the sub-questions, deciding their dependencies, rewriting them as answers arrive, and keeping track of what is known. We propose DeFT-RAG, a RAG framework built on three components. First, DeFT-RAG generates one self-contained sub-question at a time from the original question and the sub-questions answered so far with their answers (QA pairs). Each sub-question names every entity it asks about, which removes the need to plan, track dependencies or rewrite, and lets the LLM focus on a single fine-grained task at a time. Second, DeFT-RAG moves the remaining LLM work outside the LLM: rule-based updates to a QA table that keeps track of what is known, and yes/no decisions made by a fine-tuned lightweight decision model. Third, DeFT-RAG caches the QA pairs and reuses an answer when a later question asks a sub-question with the same meaning. On MuSiQue, 2WikiMultiHopQA and HotpotQA, DeFT-RAG achieves the best accuracy among thirteen baselines with both gpt-4o-mini and Qwen3.6 as the backbone, while giving the LLM fewer input tokens than any other iterative method. With a QA cache shared across 1,000 MuSiQue questions, 23.1% of sub-questions are answered from the cache, which reduces LLM calls by 12.9% without lowering accuracy.\n         \n       \n     \n     \n       [186] \n       \n        arXiv:2610.37828\n       \n          (cross-list from cs.LG)\n\n        [ pdf ,  html ,  other ]\n     \n     \n       \n          Title: \n          Learning Lotteries with Minimal Stakeholder Violations from Membership Queries\n         \n          Nicholas Teh ,  Paul W. Goldberg  \n\n          Comments: \n          31 pages, full version of paper at NeurIPS 2026\n         \n\n\n          Subjects: \n           Machine Learning (cs.LG) ; Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)\n         \n\n         \n          An AI developer often has several candidate versions of a system and can deploy a mixture of them, sending each request to a randomly chosen version, as in A/B testing. Each stakeholder (e.g., a safety team, a regulator, or a customer with private test data) accepts a mixture exactly when its expected performance (e.g., its rate of harmful replies) meets the stakeholder's own standard, which the developer can learn only by asking whether a proposed mixture is acceptable. We model this as choosing a lottery over $m$ alternatives for $n$ agents, each of whom accepts exactly the lotteries in an unknown halfspace and answers such binary membership queries. While Choo et al. (ICML 2026) sought a lottery that every agent accepts, none may exist when, say, safety and helpfulness requirements conflict, so we minimize the number of rejecting agents. We show that this problem is APX-hard even when all halfspaces are known and all utilities are $0$ or $1$. We then give exact algorithms that run in polynomial time when $m$ is constant, and in quasi-polynomial time when, for a constant $\\eta$, some optimal lottery has no accepting agent within $\\eta$ of its threshold. When only some agents can be queried, optimizing over a uniform sample of agents gives additive and relative guarantees for the whole population, with a sample size that does not depend on $n$, and a linear program gives a polynomial-time method for any $m$. On the other hand, for $m\\ge 3$, a deterministic algorithm that queries only $K$ distinct agents cannot guarantee fewer than $n-K$ extra rejections, and this is achievable; in other words, it can guarantee nothing for the agents it does not ask. Small synthetic experiments indicate that our worst-case bounds are conservative on random instances.\n         \n       \n     \n     \n       [187] \n       \n        arXiv:2610.37843\n       \n          (cross-list from physics.chem-ph)\n\n        [ pdf ,  html ,  other ]\n     \n     \n       \n          Title: \n          Where the Error Lives: Spectral Representations of Learned Kohn-Sham Hamiltonians\n         \n          Fang Sun ,  Hekai Cheng ,  Ruoyan Li ,  Yuanzhou Chen ,  Wei Wang ,  Jason Cong ,  Yizhou Sun  \n\n\n\n          Subjects: \n           Chemical Physics (physics.chem-ph) ; Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)\n         \n\n         \n          Kohn-Sham density functional theory (DFT) is the standard method for computing the electronic structure of molecules and materials, but its self-consistent-field (SCF) iterations make it expensive for large systems and high-throughput screening. Machine-learned Hamiltonian models offer a shortcut: an equivariant network predicts the Kohn-Sham Hamiltonian matrix in one forward pass, and diagonalizing the prediction yields orbital energies, the HOMO-LUMO gap, molecular orbitals and the electron density. These models are trained to match the reference matrix entry by entry, yet a small entry-wise error does not guarantee accurate eigenvalues or eigenvectors. Two physics-informed alternatives go partway: a recently proposed wavefunction-alignment loss adds a spectral penalty without diagonalizing the prediction, and matching the electron density of the diagonalized prediction supervises it directly. Neither is complete: by representing the prediction error in the eigenbasis of the reference Hamiltonian, we show that each sees only part of the error, and that shifting the predicted orbital energies leaves the electron density exactly unchanged. The same analysis identifies a complete training signal: the cumulative local density of states (cumulative LDOS), the number of electrons at each point in space with energy below a given threshold. The cumulative LDOS is a single physical observable that contains the electron density as a special case, responds to every component of the error, determines the Hamiltonian uniquely, and yields gradients that do not vanish for large orbitalenergy errors. Fine-tuning three Hamiltonian networks on QH9 in this way, which we call FermiScope, reduces the HOMO-LUMO gap error without increasing the element-wise error. The gains carry over to out-of-distribution and molecular-dynamics splits, where the wavefunction-alignment loss helps less.\n         \n       \n     \n     \n       [188] \n       \n        arXiv:2610.37865\n       \n          (cross-list from cs.RO)\n\n        [ pdf ,  html ,  other ]\n     \n     \n       \n          Title: \n          Echo: Scaling Visual Reinforcement Learning with Off-policy Experience\n         \n          Gagan Khandate  \n\n          Comments: \n          Report. 16 pages\n         \n\n\n          Subjects: \n           Robotics (cs.RO) ; Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)\n         \n\n         \n          Scaling visual reinforcement learning (RL) with GPU-accelerated simulation is computationally expensive due to rendering and visual-policy inference costs. Consequently, two-stage teacher--student distillation is commonly used to train visual policies, but introduces an imitation gap. In this preliminary study, we introduce \\echomethod{}, \\textbf{a single-stage off-policy RL framework that scales experience collection without proportionally increasing visual computation}. Through \\textbf{inverse distillation}, a state-based auxiliary policy imitates the evolving visual policy and collects inexpensive, unrendered experience. Echo leverages this experience for off-policy value learning, directly improving the visual policy through a shared privileged critic. We instantiate \\echomethod{} with FastTD3 and evaluate it on three visual control tasks and two dexterous manipulation tasks in Genesis. Relative to \\sdpgmethod{} under the evaluated configurations, Echo reduces time to reference returns by an average of \\textbf{60\\% across the three locomotion tasks} and \\textbf{56\\% across both hand tasks}, while improving peak mean returns by \\textbf{62\\% and 12\\%}, respectively. Both hand tasks also achieve higher mean returns at matched simulator-transition budgets. These initial results suggest that scaling off-policy experience is a promising approach to efficient direct visual RL.\n         \n       \n     \n     \n       [189] \n       \n        arXiv:2610.37874\n       \n          (cross-list from cs.CV)\n\n        [ pdf ,  html ,  other ]\n     \n     \n       \n          Title: \n          PairCLIP-SWA: Similarity-Guided Balancing and Stochastic Weight Averaging for Generalized Deepfake Image Detection with OpenCLIP\n         \n          Md. Nakib ,  Sudipto Sarkar ,  A. J. A. Jubayer Talukder ,  Mohammad Ariful Haque  \n\n          Comments: \n          15 pages, 8 figures, 12 tables. Code and experiment outputs:  this https URL \n         \n\n\n          Subjects: \n           Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV) ; Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)\n         \n\n         \n          Deepfake detectors are almost always deployed as the single checkpoint that scored best on a validation split, yet the reliability of that choice is rarely measured. We measure it with PairCLIP-SWA, a single-backbone detector that adapts an OpenCLIP ViT-L/14 visual tower by progressive block unfreezing on a class-balanced corpus built by CLIP-similarity-guided one-to-one subsampling, and averages the fine-tuning trajectory in weight space. Trained in-domain on the IEEE SP Cup 2025 (DF-Wild-Cup) benchmark under the same protocol as the strongest published three-backbone ensemble, the weight-averaged detector reaches 97.04% accuracy, 98.64% AU-ROC and a 4.07% equal error rate, against 94.46%, 97.60% and 5.00%. Its training log shows why averaging matters: over the averaged epochs the non-averaged checkpoint swings across 5.34 points of validation accuracy while its validation loss more than doubles, whereas the average is never worse and is up to 5.99 points better. On an OpenForensics-derived benchmark the same instability becomes a misranking: the checkpoint that validation accuracy selects (98.43%) scores 88.85% on a disjoint 10,905-image test partition, rejecting 21.1% of genuine photographs, while the terminal average, ranked last on validation, scores 94.76%. A study of twenty-two in-house configurations on the identical DF-Wild-Cup split, including preprocessing ablations, shows that CLIP pre-training rather than ensembling drives accuracy, that secondary face cropping costs about 34 points, and that frequency-domain inputs transfer poorly. We recommend per-class selection criteria measured off the early-stopping split.\n         \n       \n     \n     \n       [190] \n       \n        arXiv:2610.37891\n       \n          (cross-list from cs.RO)\n\n        [ pdf ,  html ,  other ]\n     \n     \n       \n          Title: \n          When More Planning Hurts: Learned Planners for World Models\n         \n          Josef Chen  \n\n          Comments: \n          23 pages, 9 figures, 16 tables, 28 references\n         \n\n\n          Subjects: \n           Robotics (cs.RO) ; Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI); Machine Learning (cs.LG)\n         \n\n         \n          On PushT, our fixed action updater (1,505 parameters) solves 8 of 16 scenes reserved from our configuration selection. The comparison planners perform as follows: Selected Adam (7 tasks solved, total planning and encoding time 218.440s), adapted iCEM (2 tasks solved, total planning and encoding time 49.309s), released LePlanner (16 tasks solved, total planning and encoding time 5.652s). The total planning and encoding time for our action updater is 59.079s. Note that our action updater does not satisfy the task-cost criterion we fixed before evaluation. We also conduct another matched experiment where we only replay the first ten commands in each context and then allow the next solve 4, 8 or 12 updates (subsequent solves all get 8 updates). Among all 16 contexts (8 scene clusters), the 3 budgets get 9, 8 and 7 successes, respectively. Interestingly, the extra updates did not rescue any failing task in this experiment. All the changes in success/failure happen in one scene cluster, where we observe that the extra updates decrease the costs of the selected latent world-model trajectories for our updater, while the tasks that used to be successes become failures. The code, weights, evaluators, execution traces and cost accounting accompany the paper.\n         \n       \n     \n     \n       [191] \n       \n        arXiv:2610.37901\n       \n          (cross-list from cs.CR)\n\n        [ pdf ,  html ,  other ]\n     \n     \n       \n          Title: \n          Scan Before You Install: Detecting and Mitigating Local and Composite Vulnerabilities in Skills\n         \n          Parth A. Gandhi ,  Avital Aviv ,  Prasanna N. Wudali ,  Yuval Elovici ,  Asaf Shabtai  \n\n\n\n          Subjects: \n           Cryptography and Security (cs.CR) ; Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)\n         \n\n         \n          An agent skill packages natural-language instructions, scripts, and reference files that an LLM-based agent loads for a task. Skills may access the file system, shell, and network under user-authorized permissions; weaknesses can lead to unsafe execution. Existing scanners primarily target malicious skills, not unintended vulnerabilities in public repositories, and analyze skills in isolation, overlooking multi-skill interactions. To address these gaps, we developed a six-module scanner: Content Extraction, Local Analysis, Composite Analysis, Validation, Reporting, and Mitigation. Local Analysis detects unintended vulnerabilities in benign skills. On a 556-case leaderboard subset of SkillTrustBench, it achieved 95.90% recall with a 4.22% false positive rate; on a balanced sample of 1,000 MalSkillBench skills, its F1 was 0.947, outperforming Cisco's Skill Scanner, NVIDIA's SkillSpector, AgentSkillsScanner, and SkillSieve. In 1,587 skills from the 75 most-starred GitHub repositories, three analysts confirmed 53 of 59 findings (89.8% precision) across 23 repositories. We developed proof-of-concept (PoC) attacks for each vulnerability, disclosed 10 medium- and high-severity cases to maintainers, and tested mitigations on the remaining cases. Composite Analysis models dependencies among skills through shared resources, invocations, and instruction-mediated interactions. On SCR-Bench, its F1 score was 0.991 (98.7% precision, 99.6% recall), versus 0.816-0.858 for three SkillsGuard configurations released with CompoSkill. It identified two composite findings on GitHub, validated by PoC attacks. The Mitigation module recommends skill substitution, patches, and configuration changes. For 47 of 55 GitHub findings, mitigations prevented PoC attacks in all trials while preserving tested legitimate functionality.\n         \n       \n     \n     \n       [192] \n       \n        arXiv:2610.37903\n       \n          (cross-list from cs.LG)\n\n        [ pdf ,  html ,  other ]\n     \n     \n       \n          Title: \n          Think Twice: The Metis-1.6 Technical Report\n         \n          Giulianno Vollmer  \n\n          Comments: \n          39 pages, 4 figures, 17 tables. Models and benchmark data:  this https URL \n         \n\n\n          Subjects: \n           Machine Learning (cs.LG) ; Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI); Computation and Language (cs.CL)\n         \n\n         \n          Metis is a bet that better data, better training methods and new architectures can buy what a bigger parameter count cannot, at a fraction of the cost. Metis-1.6 is its latest step: two language models built from scratch by a single researcher, Praxis (2.94B parameters, 0.46B active per pass) and Logos (10.23B parameters, 1.18B active per pass). Both run a hybrid Mamba-2 and attention mixture-of-experts stack twice for every token, so each token gets a second look. Each was trained on 1.02T tokens in total: 804.76B of pretraining, 18B of context extension to 131,072 tokens, and 200B of supervised fine-tuning. Metis-1.6 was never meant to challenge frontier models, and across 40 post-trained benchmarks it mostly does not: Logos averages 52.93, seventh of 17 models in its comparison set and within a point of Qwen3-4B. But it gets there on roughly a twelfth of the median training data of the comparators that disclose it, and with fewer active parameters per token than any model that beats it, which puts it on the Pareto frontier for both. In mathematics it ranks second of 30 models, with 67.50% on AIME 2025 and 63.64% on HMMT February 2026. It is weak at code, instruction following and long context. The rest of the report is the part I found most valuable: what went wrong. The optimizer I configured never ran, and attention trained with AdamW for all of pretraining. A 1.77B-parameter n-gram memory learned to switch itself off. Five fine-tuning choices cost capability. And a length reward under group-mean normalization punished correct solutions for being long once a group's pass rate passed 2/3, a sign inversion I prove and fix with a one-line clamp. I close with the 10B-token ablations behind MoRE (Mixture of Recursive Experts), the looped architecture of Metis-1.7.\n         \n       \n     \n     \n       [193] \n       \n        arXiv:2610.37909\n       \n          (cross-list from cs.LG)\n\n        [ pdf ,  html ,  other ]\n     \n     \n       \n          Title: \n          TERRA: Task-Oriented Edge AI for Regional Fire Detection with Identity-Free Reporting\n         \n          Mohammad Kazemi ,  Julie A. McCann  \n\n          Comments: \n          Accepted for publication at the 16th International Conference on the Internet of Things (IoT 2026)\n         \n\n\n          Subjects: \n           Machine Learning (cs.LG) ; Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI); Signal Processing (eess.SP)\n         \n\n         \n          Distributed fire-monitoring systems may involve many sensing devices reporting over a shared uplink, even though the operational objective is often to identify regions exhibiting evidence of fire rather than to recover device-specific measurements. We introduce TERRA (Task-Oriented Edge Representation and Regional Aggregation), an edge-AI framework that combines task-oriented local inference, identity-free region-tagged reporting, and permutation-invariant regional evidence aggregation. TERRA supports two reporting modes: TERRA-Score, in which each device communicates a quantized local fire probability, and TERRA-Feature, in which it communicates a compact learned representation for receiver-side aggregation using a permutation-invariant DeepSets model. Using all 15 numerical sensing modalities of the distributed indoor EN54 fire-alarm dataset as a proof-of-concept testbed, we evaluate regional inference for an episode-level fire-scenario target under repetition-disjoint three-fold cross-validation. We study representation size, task-payload quantization, report availability, and robustness to missed and false recovered messages. With nine reports and perfect message recovery, TERRA-Feature achieves mean AUPRC 0.990 using a 24-bit task payload, while TERRA-Score achieves 0.987 using only 6 bits; both attain an MDR of 1.81% at FAR $\\leq$1%. TERRA-Feature achieves slightly higher AUPRC under reliable recovery, whereas the more compact TERRA-Score is less sensitive to increasing false-message contamination.\n         \n       \n     \n     \n       [194] \n       \n        arXiv:2610.37917\n       \n          (cross-list from cs.RO)\n\n        [ pdf ,  html ,  other ]\n     \n     \n       \n          Title: \n          What Actually Matters? A Paired Evaluation of Inference Settings for $π_{0.5}$ on BEHAVIOR-1K\n         \n          Ruben Hammele ,  Samira Huber ,  Mohammad Nazeri ,  Amirreza Payandeh ,  Alexandyr T. Card ,  Aaron Nguyen ,  Anuj Pokhrel ,  Daeun Song ,  Xuesu Xiao ,  Sören Pirk  \n\n\n\n          Subjects: \n           Robotics (cs.RO) ; Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI); Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV); Machine Learning (cs.LG)\n         \n\n         \n          Vision-Language-Action (VLA) models offer a promising paradigm for general-purpose household robotics. However, inference and data-collection strategies that have been deployed in smaller-scale settings do not necessarily generalize to large-scale, diverse task suites. To address this gap, we conduct a systematic, paired evaluation of previously proposed inference and data-collection strategies across all 100 tasks. Our evaluation uses a fine-tuned $\\pi_{0.5}$ model based on the 2025 BEHAVIOR Challenge winner, which achieves a Q-score of 0.39 on the public test set. These results show that proposed strategies do not transfer uniformly to new environments and tasks. Project page, videos, and detailed analysis:  this https URL \n         \n       \n     \n     \n       [195] \n       \n        arXiv:2610.37933\n       \n          (cross-list from cs.NI)\n\n        [ pdf ,  html ,  other ]\n     \n     \n       \n          Title: \n          NLP-based Message Prioritisation for Opportunistic Routing: A Case Study of Nepal Earthquake Emergency Communication\n         \n          Yihang Cao ,  Milena Radenkovic  \n\n\n\n          Subjects: \n           Networking and Internet Architecture (cs.NI) ; Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)\n         \n\n         \n          This dissertation investigates whether Natural Language Processing (NLP) based semantic message prioritisation can improve the treatment of urgent disaster messages in opportunistic emergency communication, using the 2015 Nepal earthquake as a case study. TF IDF with Logistic Regression and BERT are used to classify social media messages into nine humanitarian categories, which are mapped to four routing priorities from P1 to P4. These priorities are integrated into the ONE simulator within a controlled Kathmandu earthquake scenario using a modified Binary Spray and Wait router. BERT achieves higher priority accuracy and P1 recall than Logistic Regression, while reducing under prioritisation and severe priority errors. However, improved NLP performance does not produce uniform network gains. P1 delivery probability remains high and similar across routing conditions, and no consistent latency reduction is observed. The clearest difference appears under buffer pressure, where the BERT condition shows fewer observed ground truth P1 buffer losses than Logistic Regression. The results indicate that semantic prioritisation is most valuable when limited transmission and storage resources are contested, although it cannot overcome mobility and contact constraints in opportunistic networks.\n         \n       \n     \n     \n       [196] \n       \n        arXiv:2610.37952\n       \n          (cross-list from cs.LG)\n\n        [ pdf ,  html ,  other ]\n     \n     \n       \n          Title: \n          SAG-Sep: Sparse Augmented Graphs and Onion-Guided Search for Rounded Capacity Cut Separation\n         \n          Haoran Liu ,  Guanyi Wang ,  Yu Yang  \n\n          Comments: \n          42 pages, 4 figures, 26 tables. Accepted at NeurIPS 2026 (Main Track). Code:  this https URL \n         \n\n\n          Subjects: \n           Machine Learning (cs.LG) ; Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI); Optimization and Control (math.OC)\n         \n\n         \n          Rounded capacity cuts (RCCs) are among the most effective cuts for capacitated vehicle-routing relaxations, but exact separation is computationally prohibitive at scale. Existing heuristic and neural separators can be fast, but often fail to produce sufficiently many effective cuts within practical separation budgets. We introduce SAG-Sep, a separator that scores node membership in RCC-inducing subsets across vehicle-count levels from a fractional LP solution in a single encoder forward pass, avoiding the iterative predict-and-coarsen inference used by NeuralSEP-style separators. SAG-Sep encodes the LP solution as a Sparse Augmented Graph (SAG), adding flow-informed multi-hop edges that expose long-range routing structure without densifying attention. Beyond LP-aware sparse encoding, we observe that high-value exact RCCs are typically nested, organizing into onion-like subset families, and SAG-Sep's node scores track subset nesting depth. This motivates Onion-Sweep (OS), a training-free decoder that ranks customers by their scores, evaluates the RCC violation of every prefix of the ranking in one vectorized pass, and emits the most violated prefix of each replica together with a cross-replica family of nested proposals, converting independent node scores into structurally coordinated RCC candidates without a tuned threshold or probing band. On the NeuralSEP benchmark with N=1000 customer instances, SAG-Sep with OS reduces the average root gap by 17.7% relative to the strongest NeuralSEP baseline, while the single-pass SAG-Sep separator achieves a roughly 30-fold per-iteration neural separation speedup. Our findings suggest that LP-aware sparse encoding and the nested structure of high-value cuts are structural patterns worth leveraging in neural separation more broadly.\n         \n       \n     \n     \n       [197] \n       \n        arXiv:2610.37962\n       \n          (cross-list from cs.IR)\n\n        [ pdf ,  html ,  other ]\n     \n     \n       \n          Title: \n          Statistical Detection of Positional Bias in LLM-Based Recommender Systems: Methodologies and Empirical Evidence\n         \n          Carlos Peláez-González ,  Francisco Herrera ,  Andrés Herrera-Poyatos  \n\n          Comments: \n          34 pages, 8 figures, 18 tables\n         \n\n\n          Subjects: \n           Information Retrieval (cs.IR) ; Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)\n         \n\n         \n          Large Language Models (LLMs) are increasingly used as recommendation generators in conversational recommender systems (RS) via retrieval-augmented generation (RAG). This flexible design introduces new sources of structural bias. This paper studies positional bias in top-1 and single-turn LLM-based RS: systematic dependence of the recommended item on the order in which candidates are presented in the prompt. We propose two complementary detection methodologies: (i) POB-ICM, based on contingency matrix analysis and Chi-square independence testing, and (ii) POB-UNBP, a sample-efficient approach derived from a uniformity null model under permutation-based evaluation, enabling stochastic decoding with formal statistical guarantees. Using a controlled RAG testbed for product recommendation, built on a real product catalog, a fixed prompt template and 2000 template-generated user queries with 20 permutations each (40,000 instances), both methodologies reject the null hypothesis of positional independence and uniformity in all evaluated state-of-the-art models. We introduce three complementary metrics -- permutation sensitivity, positional bias, and strong positional bias -- to quantify this effect, the latter targeting cases where the position displaces the recommendation towards clearly less relevant items rather than only choosing among comparably relevant ones. A robustness analysis across candidate-set size and decoding temperature shows that the bias is structural and model-dependent, not an artifact of deterministic decoding or candidate-set size. These results establish positional bias as a measurable, reproducible property of prompt-mediated recommendation pipelines in the evaluated setting and motivate routine auditing and governance controls (e.g., order randomization, standardized prompting) for deployed LLM-based RS. Source code is publicly released.\n         \n       \n     \n     \n       [198] \n       \n        arXiv:2610.37963\n       \n          (cross-list from cs.LG)\n\n        [ pdf ,  html ,  other ]\n     \n     \n       \n          Title: \n          Self-Supervised Latent Action-Conditioned World Modeling for Time Series\n         \n          Chia-Han Kuo ,  Chengshuai Zhao ,  Huan Liu ,  Lo Pang-Yun Ting  \n\n\n\n          Subjects: \n           Machine Learning (cs.LG) ; Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)\n         \n\n         \n          Time-series data play an important role in various tasks, such as weather forecasting, web traffic analysis, and speech recognition. Recently, pioneering studies have increasingly adopted Joint-Embedding Predictive Architecture (JEPA) based encoders to learn time-series representations through latent-space prediction. However, they often overlook the underlying factors governing transitions between time-series observations. In this work, we hypothesize that explicitly modeling the information behind changes in time series can facilitate time-series modeling. Building on this hypothesis, we propose ACTS-WM, a self-supervised latent action-conditioned world model framework for time-series analysis. First, we introduce a latent action modeling approach that infers the underlying information behind time-series transitions as latent actions, without requiring explicit action annotations. We then develop a JEPA-based encoder and an action-conditioned world model to predict target time-series representations conditioned on the learned latent actions and task information. We evaluate ACTS-WM across 4 representative tasks on 22 datasets from diverse domains, covering univariate and multivariate forecasting and classification. Comprehensive experiments demonstrate that ACTS-WM can improve downstream performance over pretrained JEPA-based encoders by learning complementary representations.\n         \n       \n     \n     \n       [199] \n       \n        arXiv:2610.37973\n       \n          (cross-list from cs.IR)\n\n        [ pdf ,  html ,  other ]\n     \n     \n       \n          Title: \n          Merge and Branch: Efficient Tree-Structured Supervised Fine-Tuning of LLMs for Recommendation\n         \n          Yunqin Zhu ,  Yao Xie  \n\n          Comments: \n          12 pages, 8 figures\n         \n\n\n          Subjects: \n           Information Retrieval (cs.IR) ; Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)\n         \n\n         \n          LLM-based generative recommendation reads long, text-rich interaction histories but generates only short semantic identifiers (SIDs). This asymmetry makes supervised fine-tuning inefficient: encoding the history is expensive, yet each history usually provides supervision for only one target. We present Merge and Branch (M&B) to reduce input cost and learn more from each encoded history. Merge compresses each historical item's SID and text into a small number of learned tokens. Branch uses the compressed history to predict multiple target SIDs. To share history computation while keeping these targets independent, M&B organizes each example as a shallow, wide tree. We develop tree FlashAttention to execute this layout efficiently, processing the shared history once and propagating gradients from every branch for end-to-end training. A tree-structured key/value cache extends history sharing to constrained decoding. Experiments on public benchmarks show that M&B outperforms strong recommendation baselines while substantially improving training and decoding efficiency.\n         \n       \n     \n     \n       [200] \n       \n        arXiv:2610.37994\n       \n          (cross-list from cs.LG)\n\n        [ pdf ,  html ,  other ]\n     \n     \n       \n          Title: \n          Towards Generalizing Pretrained All-Atom Representations to Unified Binder Design\n         \n          Maojiang Su ,  Jiale Chen ,  Qijie Zhu ,  Shang Wu ,  Jiayi Wang ,  Jihai Zhao ,  Guo Ye ,  Yibo Wen ,  Han Liu  \n\n\n\n          Subjects: \n           Machine Learning (cs.LG) ; Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)\n         \n\n         \n          PairFormer-based all-atom models demonstrate strong capabilities in biomolecular binder design. However, existing methods often use all-atom representations for modality-specific binder design, such as protein and peptide design. In contrast, recent geometric latent diffusion models pursue unified binder design by representing molecular blocks from different modalities in a shared latent space. These complementary strengths motivate us to investigate how to generalize the use of pretrained all-atom representations to unified binder design. We introduce PairFuse, a parameter-efficient method that enables this generalization through representation fusion. Specifically, we use lightweight layerwise adapters to integrate pretrained all-atom representations into an independently pretrained geometric latent diffusion model. Our adapters integrate pretrained all-atom representations through two mechanisms: enriching the diffusion model's molecular block features and incorporating pairwise structural information into the geometric diffusion model's denoiser. Theoretically, we prove that the augmented denoiser preserves geometry and pairwise attention fusion provides additional attention expressivity beyond feature-only fusion. Experimentally, across antibody, peptide, and small molecule binder-design benchmarks, PairFuse achieves stronger performance than the pretrained diffusion model and existing representation-fusion baselines.\n         \n       \n     \n     \n       [201] \n       \n        arXiv:2610.38003\n       \n          (cross-list from cs.LG)\n\n        [ pdf ,  html ,  other ]\n     \n     \n       \n          Title: \n          Are Seismic Foundation Models More Than Glorified Feature Extractors?\n         \n          Laura Laurenti ,  David Liu ,  Brian Kulis ,  Men-Andrin Meier ,  Christopher Johnson  \n\n          Comments: \n          NEURIPS STODY WORKSHOP 2026\n         \n\n\n          Subjects: \n           Machine Learning (cs.LG) ; Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)\n         \n\n         \n          Earthquake sequences are stochastic on two separated timescales: the seismic waves of any individual earthquake propagate over seconds to minutes, while the earthquakes themselves arrive as a sparse, self-exciting occurrence process, with inter-event intervals from seconds to months. Respecting both scales in one training objective is the open challenge. Foundation models (FMs) for seismic waveforms instead import their learning targets from audio and vision, where every target is computed from a single fixed-length window. Such a window holds one realization of the wave propagation (an earthquake trace), never the occurrence process that decides when earthquakes happen. We develop nine model designs sharing one Mamba encoder-decoder, trained on a fixed corpus of 3M windows, varying the self-supervised objective, quantization bottleneck and mixer; the two key comparisons are architecture-matched, varying the objective alone. Each checkpoint is evaluated frozen on three downstream tasks. Varying the target redistributes downstream skill rather than raising it, and no checkpoint wins everywhere. Adding masking pressure to our strongest classifier re-specializes it rather than making it a generalist: it gains 6.9 points of few-shot P-wave picking precision and loses 9.6 points of few-shot classification accuracy. By the standard imported from language and vision, these models are feature extractors rather than FMs. Controls on capacity, architecture, and domain proximity do not explain why skill moves rather than rises. Against task-specific baselines the advantage is regime-dependent: our checkpoints beat them few-shot on classification and ground-motion prediction, and trail them on full data. We argue the binding constraint is the target, and that settling the question requires a learning target defined across events, the timescale that makes seismicity stochastic in the first place.\n         \n       \n     \n     \n       [202] \n       \n        arXiv:2610.38005\n       \n          (cross-list from cs.HC)\n\n        [ pdf ,  other ]\n     \n     \n       \n          Title: \n          \"It Would Make a Geordie Feel at Home\": Regional Accent Perceptions and Speech Accommodation in Voice AI\n         \n          Dana Serditova ,  Kevin Tang ,  Jochen Steffens  \n\n          Comments: \n          50 pages, 5 figures\n         \n\n\n          Subjects: \n           Human-Computer Interaction (cs.HC) ; Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI); Computation and Language (cs.CL)\n         \n\n         \n          Automatic speech recognition (ASR) systems often perform less accurately for speakers of regional dialects, but less is known about how users respond to these limitations and how regional voices produced by voice assistants (VAs) are evaluated through a sociolinguistic lens. This study investigates these issues among speakers of Newcastle English (Geordies), examining both linguistic accommodation to voice technologies and perceptions of regional versus mainstream-accented VA voices. Participants interacted with a simulated VA that spoke either Newcastle English or Standard Southern British English (SSBE). They subsequently completed semi-structured interviews about their experiences with the experimental VA and voice technologies, language use, accent attitudes, and place orientation. The findings reveal a two-way process of sociolinguistic bias. The Geordie VA was associated with familiarity, warmth, personality, and local belonging, but also with concerns about professionalism and authority, whereas the SSBE voice was perceived as more neutral, clear, and credible. Participants also reported adapting their speech when voice technologies failed to recognise them, most commonly by shifting to a more mainstream accent, enunciating more clearly, and speaking more slowly. These strategies are similar to accommodation in human-human interaction and reflect users' perceptions of what technologies can recognise, also known as perceived intelligibility pressures. Overall, the findings demonstrate that voice technologies can reproduce existing accent biases both by encouraging users to suppress regional features and by activating established social stereotypes through synthetic voices. The study positions sociolinguistics as a bridge between understanding human-AI interaction and designing inclusive technologies around users' own linguistic priorities.\n         \n       \n     \n     \n       [203] \n       \n        arXiv:2610.38008\n       \n          (cross-list from cs.HC)\n\n        [ pdf ,  html ,  other ]\n     \n     \n       \n          Title: \n          Making Smart Homes Fit for Autistic Children: Family Work and Monitored Independence\n         \n          Tingting Xiang ,  Weihan Jiang ,  Hrishitva Patel ,  Haiqi Shen ,  Tianzhi He ,  Kyrie Zhou  \n\n\n\n          Subjects: \n           Human-Computer Interaction (cs.HC) ; Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)\n         \n\n         \n          General-purpose smart-home technologies are increasingly present in family life, yet how they support or disrupt autistic children and families with autistic children remains underexamined. We conducted a multi-source qualitative study drawing on five caregiver interviews, Xiaohongshu (also known internationally as RedNote) posts explicitly situated in autism contexts, and contextual posts from Xiaohongshu and Weibo. We find that families create support for autistic children by adapting environmental outputs, prompts, and interaction channels, while sensory intensity, communicative rules, and opaque operating states can turn the same technologies into barriers. Support also depends on continuing family work, including translation, configuration, modification, maintenance, and boundary-setting. Remote monitoring enables conditional independence while generating tensions among safety, privacy, and control. We contribute three concepts, including relational fit, family work as support infrastructure, and monitored independence, for understanding accessible domestic technology. We derive design implications for adjustable interaction, legible and recoverable failure, lower-burden configuration, and negotiable monitoring.\n         \n       \n     \n     \n       [204] \n       \n        arXiv:2610.38026\n       \n          (cross-list from cs.HC)\n\n        [ pdf ,  html ,  other ]\n     \n     \n       \n          Title: \n          Making Sense of Audience Change: Causal Attribution and Boundary Management on Xiaohongshu\n         \n          Zhiyi Zhou ,  Kyrie Zhou  \n\n\n\n          Subjects: \n           Human-Computer Interaction (cs.HC) ; Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI); Computers and Society (cs.CY)\n         \n\n         \n          Recommendation-driven platforms can reshape not only what users see, but also who becomes visible and how community boundaries are negotiated. We examine Xiaohongshu discussions surrounding a perceived World Cup-related audience influx to understand how users attributed community change and how those explanations related to boundary-management strategies. We analyzed 262 comments from eight high-engagement threads using researcher-led thematic analysis, structured coding, and permutation-based association analysis. Users described personalization as both social separation and a boundary that could fail, interpreted platform growth through audience acquisition and commercialization, and contested whether gender should define community membership. Among 70 comments with explicit attributions and strategies, attribution and strategy were strongly associated (Cramer's V = .583): algorithmic attributions concentrated in technical boundary management, while social-actor attributions more often aligned with social enforcement or normative socialization. These findings connect causal sensemaking with boundary management and show how audience restructuring can create governance consequences on recommendation-driven platforms.\n         \n       \n     \n     \n       [205] \n       \n        arXiv:2610.38027\n       \n          (cross-list from cs.RO)\n\n        [ pdf ,  html ,  other ]\n     \n     \n       \n          Title: \n          METATRON: A Promptable Whole-Body Execution Layer for Humanoids\n         \n          Chen Tessler ,  Yifeng Jiang ,  Alec Otto ,  Yi Shi ,  Umar Iqbal ,  Gal Chechik ,  Xue Bin Peng  \n\n          Comments: \n          Project page:  this https URL \n         \n\n\n          Subjects: \n           Robotics (cs.RO) ; Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)\n         \n\n         \n          Humanoid controllers often require task-specific training despite sharing the need for balance and whole-body coordination. We present Metatron, a promptable whole-body execution layer combining a generative base policy with a programmable skill layer. The base policy receives partial goals, collectively called a prompt, that can combine timed body-part targets, velocity commands, and style cues. It produces diverse whole-body motions consistent with these goals. Training combines motion capture, synthetic recovery trajectories, and retargeted scene-interaction motions. After training, the policy is frozen. Stateful programs called skills express task intent as prompts at each control step, drawing rough geometric sketches that the policy turns into coordinated movement. Skills are ordinary code, refined by coding agents that analyze motion data and fit compact rules without task-specific policy training. On the physical Unitree G1, one frozen checkpoint supports walking, recovery, teleoperated object pickup, and sitting on furniture. Comparative evaluations show higher reaching success and push robustness than the tested reference-tracking pipelines. The same recipe produces climbing and acrobatics on a simulated character. Metatron enables coding agents to turn learned motor capabilities into new skills through code, without further policy training.\n         \n       \n     \n     \n       [206] \n       \n        arXiv:2610.38037\n       \n          (cross-list from cs.LG)\n\n        [ pdf ,  html ,  other ]\n     \n     \n       \n          Title: \n          Measuring Deep Learning Performance - An Empirical Study of Performance Distributions Across Architectures and Tasks\n         \n          Kevin L Coakley ,  Odd Erik Gundersen  \n\n\n          Journal-ref: \n          Sci. Rep. 16, 20474 (2026)\n         \n\n          Subjects: \n           Machine Learning (cs.LG) ; Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)\n         \n\n         \n          Context: Non-determinism in deep learning algorithm design and implementation leads to performance variation, meaning model performance is not a single value, but rather a distribution. These model performance distributions are underexplored despite their impact on robustness. Objective: We investigate the robustness of deep learning performance to sources of non-determinism, specifically focusing on how performance distributions differ across various architectures and tasks. Method: We conducted 186 experiments on image classification and time series forecasting architectures. Each experiment was run 100 times with different random seeds to generate performance distributions, resulting in 18,600 runs. Robustness was quantified using metrics for spread, symmetry, and tail risk. Results: Performance distributions are frequently non-Gaussian, particularly in time series forecasting. Model size does not systematically affect robustness -- larger image classification models show fewer outliers but not lower spread, while smaller time series models show lower spread but more extreme underperformers. Training duration does not scale linearly; early stopping effectively balances performance and robustness. Mean performance does not predict robustness - time series forecasting shows moderate correlation while image classification shows none. Time series models produce nearly three times more underperforming outliers than image classification models, indicating substantially higher tail risk. Conclusions: Tail risk poses serious concerns for Trustworthy AI in high-stakes applications. Models performing well on average may exhibit long tails and extreme outliers revealed only through distributional analysis. Mean performance alone should not guide model selection; assessment of spread, symmetry, and tail risk is essential for reliable model assessment where consistent performance is critical.\n         \n       \n     \n     \n       [207] \n       \n        arXiv:2610.38040\n       \n          (cross-list from cs.RO)\n\n        [ pdf ,  html ,  other ]\n     \n     \n       \n          Title: \n          EARS-Info: Information-Aware Event-Driven Reallocation for Multi-Robot Exploration\n         \n          Kabeh Mohsenzadegan ,  Vahid Tavakkoli ,  Kyandoghere Kyamakya  \n\n\n\n          Subjects: \n           Robotics (cs.RO) ; Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI); Multiagent Systems (cs.MA)\n         \n\n         \n          We introduce EARS-Info, an information-aware extension of Event-Driven Ant Reallocation Search for multi-robot exploration on OpenStreetMap-derived grids. It combines marginal sensing gain, feasible relocation, return-aware routing, sensing reservations, and cached shortest paths. Across 168,000 episodes comparing 30 implementations on eight city maps, EARS-Info achieves the highest mean operational score in every evaluation panel. With eight robots and 3% communication dropout, it improves the score over EARS-Safe by 5.43%, increases weighted discovery from 88.88% to 98.46%, and reduces mean energy use by 2.62%. Score differences are positive in all eight cities ($p=0.0078125$; descriptive 95% city-bootstrap interval $[0.0159,0.0694]$), with no simulated incidents across 5,600 EARS-Info episodes. These results support the complete controller; individual component advantages remain uncertain. Generalization is limited by reused geography, one training replicate, coarse maps, and adapted comparison methods.\n         \n       \n     \n     \n       [208] \n       \n        arXiv:2610.38042\n       \n          (cross-list from cs.CV)\n\n        [ pdf ,  html ,  other ]\n     \n     \n       \n          Title: \n          Can Event Detectors Leave the Broadcast Camera? Evidence from Tennis\n         \n          David Soeiro-Vuong ,  Andre-Louis Rochet  \n\n          Comments: \n          16 pages, 4 figures, 5 tables. Benchmark, evaluator and frozen predictions:  this https URL \n         \n\n\n          Subjects: \n           Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV) ; Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)\n         \n\n         \n          Ball bounces and racket hits are the events from which tennis statistics are derived, and on monocular video the two are easy to confuse. We release a benchmark of 345 bounce and hit annotations at frame level on eight clips that mix broadcast, fixed elevated, phone and court-level viewpoints, with a frozen development and test split, a confusion-aware matcher, clip-level bootstrap intervals and a blind second annotation of three clips. On it we score supervised precise spotters with published broadcast weights, two zero-shot vision-language models, and RallyArb, an arbitration layer over a ball track and player poses that fits no parameter on event labels. The supervised spotters score near-perfectly on broadcast footage and almost nothing on phone and court-level footage, where the pooled bounce F1 of E2E-Spot is 0.130. RallyArb keeps a bounce F1 above 0.8 on the three phone and court-level clips, two of which were used to tune it, and finds 8 of 11 bounces on the held-out one, but it places hits too far from the contact frame to match human timing. The vision-language models detect events on every viewpoint and miss the contact frame by about 0.1 s. Fine-tuned leave-one-clip-out from its broadcast weights, the spotter reaches about 0.8 bounce F1 on two phone and court-level clips and stays at 0.2 on a grazing phone angle. The annotations, the evaluator and the frozen predictions of every scored row are released.\n         \n       \n     \n     \n       [209] \n       \n        arXiv:2610.38067\n       \n          (cross-list from cs.CL)\n\n        [ pdf ,  html ,  other ]\n     \n     \n       \n          Title: \n          From Error Prediction to Causal Influence: Auditing SAE Features in Multiple-Choice QA\n         \n          Aria Nourbakhsh ,  Adelaide Danilov ,  Oleksandr Marchenko Breneur ,  Salima Lamsiyah  \n\n\n\n          Subjects: \n           Computation and Language (cs.CL) ; Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)\n         \n\n         \n          Sparse autoencoders (SAEs) can predict internal features associated with model errors, but association alone does not establish causal influence. We study this distinction in multiple-choice question answering, where answer preference can be measured by the logit margin between the correct answer and the competing choices. Across eight datasets, we analyze Gemma~3 1B and 4B with Gemma Scope SAEs. We propose a pipeline to filter out the features that have high correlation with format and content artifacts, such as prompt and answer permutation, and evaluate the remaining ones with causal feature interventions. Our results show that SAE activations contain predictive signals about wrongness and uncertainty across models and datasets. After filtering artifact-contaminated features, selected features for causal intervention at question and option spans produce larger absolute margin shifts and a higher wrong-to-correct rate than randomly selected features, indicating greater causal leverage. However, answer flips are rare, and we do not observe stable effect directions across meaning-preserving changes in prompt format and answer order. An adapted HalluSAE baseline produces larger final-token margin changes, but relies on substantially contaminated features and does not significantly improve correction rates. Therefore, the identified features reflect context-dependent answer competition rather than the mechanism of incorrect answer errors.\n         \n       \n     \n     \n       [210] \n       \n        arXiv:2610.38076\n       \n          (cross-list from cs.CL)\n\n        [ pdf ,  html ,  other ]\n     \n     \n       \n          Title: \n          Prompt Lottery in LLM-as-a-Judge: Measuring and Mitigating Sensitivity to Syntactic Rephrasing\n         \n          Ilija Subasic ,  Brett Levert ,  Andrew Rabinovich ,  Sajjad Pakdamansavoji ,  Zhao Chen  \n\n          Comments: \n          55 pages (11 with no appendix), 10 figures\n         \n\n\n          Subjects: \n           Computation and Language (cs.CL) ; Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)\n         \n\n         \n          LLM-as-a-Judge has become a standard for evaluating large language models, yet most approaches implicitly assume that a single judge prompt functions as a stable evaluation instrument. Prior work has primarily explored deliberate prompt modifications such as scoring rubrics, role descriptions, or chain-of-thought reasoning in order to optimize accuracy. In contrast, we investigate whether validated prompt variants intended to preserve evaluation semantics while differing in surface realization (\\textit{paraphrastic rephrasings}) yield consistent judgments under a fixed evaluation task. We introduce \\textit{Prompt Lottery}, a phenomenon in which paraphrastic rephrasings produce different LLMaJ judgments. Across a large-scale study spanning diverse evaluation tasks, judge families, and model backbones, we find that these variations can alter evaluation outcomes and, in some settings, reverse relative model rankings. A user study with 10 human annotators shows that LLMaJ judges exhibit 6-9$\\times$ larger agreement degradation under prompt rephrasings than humans, falling below the generally accepted threshold in usability studies. This indicates that single-prompt evaluation is insufficiently robust for model ranking. Finally, we show that multi-prompt aggregation substantially improves robustness by emphasizing judgments that remain consistent across prompt variants.\n         \n       \n     \n     \n       [211] \n       \n        arXiv:2610.38082\n       \n          (cross-list from stat.ML)\n\n        [ pdf ,  html ,  other ]\n     \n     \n       \n          Title: \n          Interpretable Feature Discovery from Unstructured Data Using LLMs and Statistical Choice Models\n         \n          Baptiste Arnaudo ,  Amine Bennouna ,  Sébastien Martin  \n\n          Comments: \n          Preprint. 88 pages, 21 figures, 12 tables. Code:  this https URL \n         \n\n\n          Subjects: \n           Machine Learning (stat.ML) ; Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI); Machine Learning (cs.LG)\n         \n\n         \n          Feature extraction from unstructured data typically relies on opaque embeddings unsuited to interpretable decision-making. Interpretable features, on the other hand, typically require costly, and often infeasible, hand-engineering. Large language models (LLMs) offer a natural bridge, as they can read raw data and describe it in interpretable terms. One could simply ask an LLM to extract features from a large sample of the dataset, but context limitations and diluted attention can lead to unsatisfactory results. We introduce a method that automatically discovers interpretable, decision-relevant features from unstructured data. Our key idea is to perform pairwise comparisons between items, asking an LLM to articulate, in interpretable terms, the features distinguishing each pair. We treat these explanations, or rationales, as data: we develop a statistical choice model that combines these rationales across the comparisons to identify relevant features and estimate per-item, per-feature scores. We first test our method on a synthetic hiring dataset of cover letters with known ground-truth features and show that it significantly outperforms direct LLM feature extraction in feature recovery. We then apply it to the ChangeMyView (Reddit) debate dataset, where it discovers several interpretable features associated with persuasion while reaching predictive accuracy comparable to established baselines. We also show how the same rationales can be used to audit LLM decisions, yielding a holistic score whose decomposition over interpretable features reveals what the LLM rewards.\n         \n       \n     \n     \n       [212] \n       \n        arXiv:2610.38088\n       \n          (cross-list from cs.GT)\n\n        [ pdf ,  html ,  other ]\n     \n     \n       \n          Title: \n          What Can Firms Do in the Face of Model Distillation?\n         \n          Diptangshu Sen ,  Jibang Wu ,  Juba Ziani  \n\n          Comments: \n          32 pages\n         \n\n\n          Subjects: \n           Computer Science and Game Theory (cs.GT) ; Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)\n         \n\n         \n          Model distillation has become a major concern in the rapidly growing generative AI discourse. In its simplest form, distillation is the phenomenon where smaller firms can use data distilled from transcripts of conversations with powerful proprietary closed-weight models to train their own models, thus achieving reasonably high model quality but at much lower costs. Besides the legitimate safety and security concerns associated with distillation, another major issue is how it can also distort marketplace outcomes. This motivates us to initiate an exploration of the problem from an economic theory lens. We consider a setting with two players: an incumbent and a distilling competitor and characterize how the incumbent's model characteristics drive the incentives around how much the competitor should distill. We also consider the impact of distillation on key market outcomes like user-side surplus and the incumbent's utility. Our key contribution, however, is to study interventions for preventing or limiting distillation attacks. In this context, we study two settings: i) when it is possible, with a certain probability (which grows in the extent of distillation conducted), to detect an attack on the incumbent's model; and ii) when a distillation attack cannot be reliably detected. In the first setting, we show that penalty-based regulations can be very effective in completely deterring distillation, although it can have potentially adverse effects on market competition and user surplus. In the latter setting, we show that the incumbent can use self-distillation as a mitigation strategy. Importantly, the effectiveness of self-distillation depends on how cost-efficiently the incumbent can deploy a new self-distilled model on the market. On the positive side, self-distillation can preserve market competition thus protecting user surplus to some extent.\n         \n       \n     \n     \n       [213] \n       \n        arXiv:2610.38090\n       \n          (cross-list from cs.IR)\n\n        [ pdf ,  html ,  other ]\n     \n     \n       \n          Title: \n          Predicting What Answer Engines Cite: Open Decision Models, Rerankers and a Distilled 4B Student on Ten Engines under Controlled Retrieval\n         \n          Elisha Bajemon ,  Andre-Louis Rochet  \n\n          Comments: \n          12 pages, 1 figure, 4 tables. Model:  this https URL  ; benchmark and code:  this https URL \n         \n\n\n          Subjects: \n           Information Retrieval (cs.IR) ; Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)\n         \n\n         \n          Generative engine optimization needs a scorer that ranks candidate pages by the share of an answer they will receive as citations. We measure pretrained rerankers, a hosted typed decision model (Jev), eight open decision models and distilled students on GEO-Bench queries answered by ten engines that receive the same candidate sources in their prompt. Where several engines answer the same query, they agree with one another at a within-group Spearman correlation of 0.704. Jev and two open 27B decision models, used without training, are the strongest scorers at 0.575 to 0.578. A Qwen3-Reranker-4B fine-tuned with low-rank adapters on teacher labels for other GEO-Bench queries, released as GEO Citation Ranker 4B, reaches 0.542 to 0.549 across three seeds, and its differences with the 27B models and with Jev are not distinguishable from zero on 229 queries. Most of the remaining distance between the student and Jev disappears when Jev scores one passage at a time, as the student does. Students trained on teacher labels and on observed engine citations reach the same level, and labels from Jev alone match the two-teacher target at 0.6B. We release the model, the benchmark, the predictions of every reported scorer and the code.\n         \n       \n     \n     \n       [214] \n       \n        arXiv:2610.38091\n       \n          (cross-list from cs.LG)\n\n        [ pdf ,  html ,  other ]\n     \n     \n       \n          Title: \n          Jailbreak Connectivity: Towards Diverse, Transferable, and Universal Multimodal Large Language Model Jailbreak\n         \n          Xu Zhang ,  Ziqing Hu ,  Shuo Han ,  Ren Wang  \n\n\n\n          Subjects: \n           Machine Learning (cs.LG) ; Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI); Cryptography and Security (cs.CR)\n         \n\n         \n          Multimodal large language models (MLLMs) are vulnerable to visual jailbreak attacks that bypass safety safeguards. Existing methods typically optimize isolated adversarial examples, limiting attack diversity, transferability, and generalization. We propose Jailbreak Connectivity (JC), a framework based on the observation that effective adversarial inputs can be connected through low-loss regions in image space. JC optimizes continuous adversarial paths: path sampling yields diverse jailbreak images, lightweight surrogate classifiers guide cross-model transfer, and a distribution-level objective enables universal attacks across harmful queries. On MM-SafetyBench, JC achieves average attack success rates of 84.01%, 85.77%, and 97.95% on Qwen3-VL-8B-Instruct, InternVL3.5-8B, and Gemma-3-12B-IT, respectively, exceeding the strongest baselines by up to 68.36 percentage points. In black-box evaluations, JC improves attack success rates over FigStep by 24.69 and 27.34 percentage points on GPT-5.6 and Gemini 3.1 Pro, respectively. These findings reveal structured vulnerabilities in MLLM safety alignment and inform more comprehensive security evaluation.\n         \n       \n     \n     \n       [215] \n       \n        arXiv:2610.38095\n       \n          (cross-list from cs.CL)\n\n        [ pdf ,  html ,  other ]\n     \n     \n       \n          Title: \n          Nemotron 3.5 Super and Lightning: Open, Efficient Mixture-of-Experts Hybrid Mamba-Transformer Models for Agentic AI\n         \n          NVIDIA :  Aastha Jhunjhunwala ,  Aayushi Ashokkumar Srivastava ,  Abhibha Gupta ,  Abhinav Garg ,  Abhinav Khattar ,  Achsah Wildish ,  Adam Rajfer ,  Adi Renduchintala ,  Adil Asif ,  Adriana Flores Miranda ,  Aileen Zaman ,  Aishwarya Chhabra ,  Akanksha Shukla ,  Akhiad Bercovich ,  Alejandra Rico ,  Aleksa Gordic ,  Aleksander Ficek ,  Alex Gronskiy ,  Alex Steiner ,  Alexander Bukharin ,  Alexandre Milesi ,  Ali Roshan Ghias ,  Ali Shehper ,  Ali Taghibakhshi ,  Alice Gatti ,  Alisa Liu ,  Alok Kumar ,  Alok Tripathy ,  Amala Sanjay Deshmukh ,  Aman Miryala ,  Aman Shanbhag ,  Ameya Sunil Mahabaleshwarkar ,  Ameya Velingker ,  Amir Klein ,  Amir Samani ,  Amit Zuker ,  Amnon Geifman ,  Anahita Bhiwandiwalla ,  Ananth Subramaniam ,  Andre Manoel ,  Andrea Santilli ,  Andrew Tao ,  Andrii Skliar ,  Anil Thomas ,  Anish Mahishi ,  Anna Shors ,  Anna Warno ,  Anuj Doshi ,  Anusha Kamath ,  Anwith Kiran ,  Ao Tang ,  Arham Mehta ,  Arnab Dhara ,  Arnav Komaragiri ,  Arnav Kundu ,  Arti Jain ,  Arun Rao ,  Arun Venkatesan ,  Arushi Goel ,  Asha Anoosheh ,  Ashish Vaswani ,  Ashwath Aithal ,  Asif Ahamed ,  Asma Kuriparambil Thekkumpate ,  Aswin Visva ,  Atefeh Sohrabizadeh ,  Avinash Vem ,  Ayush Dattagupta ,  Benedikt Schifferer ,  Benjamin Chislett ,  Bernard Nguyen ,  Besmira Nushi ,  Bilal Kartal ,  Bita Darvish Rouhani ,  Bobby Chen ,  Boris Ginsburg ,  Boxin Wang ,  Brandon Norick ,  Branislav Kisacanin ,  Brendan Johnson ,  Brian Dang ,  Brian Yu ,  Bryan Catanzaro ,  Byung-Kwan Lee ,  Carlo del Mundo ,  Chankyu Lee ,  Chantal Hwang ,  Charles Wang ,  Charlie Truong ,  Chen Cui ,  Cheng Wang ,  Cheng-Ping Hsieh ,  Chenhan Yu ,  Chenjie Luo ,  Chintan Patel ,  Chris Alexiuk ,  Chris Johnson ,  Chris Wing ,  Christian Munley  \n\n\n\n          Subjects: \n           Computation and Language (cs.CL) ; Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI); Machine Learning (cs.LG)\n         \n\n         \n          We introduce Nemotron 3.5 Super and Nemotron 3.5 Lightning, open and efficient Mixture-of-Experts Hybrid Mamba-Transformer models designed for reasoning, long-context understanding, and agentic workloads. Super is a 120B-parameter, 12B-active multimodal model, while Lightning is a smaller 30B-parameter, 3B-active text model; both designed for efficient deployment. Both models combine large-scale distillation, long-context training, and native speculative decoding, while Super further incorporates Indic continued pretraining and agentic software-engineering mid-training. We develop a comprehensive post-training pipeline spanning supervised fine-tuning and reinforcement learning (RLVR and RLHF), complemented in Super by specialized teacher training and multi- teacher on-policy distillation (MOPD). This transforms Super from a text-only base model into a multimodal agent capable of reasoning over text, images, video, and interactive tool environments. We further explore test-time scaling through parallel agent execution and self-verification. Across training and deployment, we jointly optimize capability and efficiency. Indic continued pretraining improves Indian-language benchmarks by up to 9.82 percentage points, while agentic mid-training strengthens software-engineering capabilities and robustness across agent harnesses. The post-trained Super model achieves 51.78% on SWE-bench Pro, surpassing Nemotron 3 Ultra despite using approximately 4.6x fewer active parameters. Post-training MTP boosting improves speculative- decoding acceptance length by up to 21.8% under probabilistic draft sampling, while NVFP4 quantization achieves 99.62% median accuracy recovery relative to BF16. Inference optimizations enable up to 4,856 output tokens/s/GPU on GB200 while sustaining at least 50 tokens/s/user.\n         \n       \n     \n     \n       [216] \n       \n        arXiv:2610.38096\n       \n          (cross-list from cs.CE)\n\n        [ pdf ,  html ,  other ]\n     \n     \n       \n          Title: \n          Calculation and rejection diverge in language models tested at known mechanical limits\n         \n          Shaoliang Yang ,  Jun Wang  \n\n          Comments: \n          94 pages, including Supplementary Information. Data and code:  this https URL \n         \n\n\n          Subjects: \n           Computational Engineering, Finance, and Science (cs.CE) ; Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI); Computation and Language (cs.CL)\n         \n\n         \n          When a language model returns engineering quantities with the status \"solved\", software that trusts it passes them on, even when the requested design is physically impossible. Such failures could reflect poor calculation, how the answer is requested, or a failure to judge feasibility. We set beam loads and disk speeds at known percentages above or below plastic collapse or burst, measured each model's limit calculation separately and tested 12 models in 4,860 calls, including follow-up experiments on new designs. We find that a model can recognize an impossible design when asked only whether it is feasible, yet accept the same design, reporting \"solved\", when asked for a full answer. Three models accepted beams 10 to 30% beyond collapse despite precise limit calculations in separate questions, and many accepted replies stated the violation. Asked only about feasibility, Opus 5 and Gemini 3.8 Flash rejected every impossible new design they were given, including those accepted in full answers, without false alarms. Rewording when to decline or changing the answer format raised rejection on the original beams, but not consistently on new designs. Software and benchmarks using a model's status should test feasibility judgment and full-answer rejection separately, and report the exact prompt.\n         \n       \n     \n     \n       [217] \n       \n        arXiv:2610.38103\n       \n          (cross-list from cs.CV)\n\n        [ pdf ,  html ,  other ]\n     \n     \n       \n          Title: \n          The Prestige: Benchmarking Cognitive Visual Reasoning using Magic Tricks\n         \n          Shuo Wen ,  Beixi Du ,  Edwin Meriaux ,  Junming Shi ,  Chloe Si ,  David Meger ,  Doina Precup ,  Gregory Dudek  \n\n          Comments: \n          40th Conference on Neural Information Processing Systems (NeurIPS 2026). Track on Evaluations and Datasets\n         \n\n\n          Subjects: \n           Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV) ; Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)\n         \n\n         \n          We introduce The Prestige, a video understanding benchmark that probes cognitive visual reasoning through professional magic performances, where deliberate misdirection challenges models to track events and explain unexpected outcomes. The benchmark comprises open-ended questions ranging from temporal and causal understanding to robustness against adversarial questions. Evaluation of leading open-source and proprietary VLMs reveals a substantial gap between even the strongest model and human performance. We also find systematic discrepancies between human and automated grading and identify recurring model failure modes. Ablations further show that models can select correct multiple-choice answers even when they fail to generate correct explanations independently, motivating further investigations into question formats. Together, these findings highlight limitations in both VLM reasoning and its evaluation, underscoring the need for more reliable video understanding models and more rigorous benchmarks.\n         \n       \n     \n     \n       [218] \n       \n        arXiv:2610.38112\n       \n          (cross-list from cs.CY)\n\n        [ pdf ,  html ,  other ]\n     \n     \n       \n          Title: \n          From the Gettier Machine to Justified True Output: A Three-Step Account of Rational Trust in Generative AI\n         \n          Tevfik Uyar  \n\n          Comments: \n          16 pages, 1 table\n         \n\n\n          Subjects: \n           Computers and Society (cs.CY) ; Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)\n         \n\n         \n          As generative artificial intelligence (AI) settles into everyday practices of inquiry, an old epistemological question returns in a new guise: when does a proposition a user takes from an AI output become something the user knows, and when is it rational to trust it as such? This paper argues that the characteristic failures of AI-mediated inquiry are precisely those that the justified-true-belief (JTB) tradition and its post-Gettier anti-luck refinements were built to diagnose, and it advances three claims. First, AI outputs inherit neither the presumptive warrant of testimony nor the reliabilist warrant of deterministic tools: unaudited trust in a calculator rests on a type-level reliability record, and for general-purpose generative models there is no type-stable process to which such a record could attach -- a practical form of the generality problem. Second, use that stops at obtaining an output turns generative AI into a Gettier machine: the user's fluency-based seeming of justification is disconnected from the truth of what is believed, and, as a clock running ten times too fast illustrates, sheer speed of production multiplies coincidental hits, inflating the appearance of reliability without improving the standing of any single hit. Third, the remedy is a three-step structure of use -- acquisition, understanding, independent verification -- whose first two steps answer to the classical belief and justification conditions and whose third answers to the post-Gettier sensitivity condition. The result is a stakes-indexed criterion of rational trust, justified true output (JTO), with an everyday test: \"Had this been false, would I have noticed?\" The criterion is defended against objections from over-demandingness, the extended mind, model improvement, and redundancy.\n         \n       \n     \n     \n       [219] \n       \n        arXiv:2610.38113\n       \n          (cross-list from cs.HC)\n\n        [ pdf ,  html ,  other ]\n     \n     \n       \n          Title: \n          Beauty Is Human: When People Love AI-Generated Art, They Think a Human Made It\n         \n          Zihan Chen ,  Di Zhu ,  Lei Nico Zheng  \n\n          Comments: \n          22 pages, 4 figures, 12 tables. Working paper\n         \n\n\n          Subjects: \n           Human-Computer Interaction (cs.HC) ; Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI); Computers and Society (cs.CY)\n         \n\n         \n          Generative AI now produces images and poems that people cannot reliably distinguish from human-made work, so people often judge for themselves who made a work. We examine how these perceived-authorship judgments relate to aesthetic preference. In a public online AI Art Turing Test, 11,123 people judged whether each of 50 pictures (26 AI-generated) was made by a human and were asked for their favourite. Among the 9,152 respondents who judged their favourite, a picture was 22.1 percentage points more likely to be judged human-made when it was the respondent's favourite, holding respondent and picture fixed. The association was similar for AI-generated and human-made favourites, was positive for all 50 pictures, and appeared under every reported detection strategy. Respondents most negative about AI art chose fewer AI-generated favourites (47% against 60% among the most positive) but more often believed them human-made, partly because others also judged those pictures human. As a result, in every opinion group a third to two fifths of respondents named an AI-generated favourite they believed human-made. A reanalysis of a published experiment on AI-generated poetry, in which 230 people rated each poem before judging its authorship, found the same pattern, although the AI poems were rated higher. When provenance is hidden, liking and perceived human authorship travel together, consistent with aesthetic response serving as evidence of a human maker and with beliefs about the maker shaping enjoyment. The findings qualify research on bias against AI art, which typically uses explicit labels, and inform AI disclosure policy.\n         \n       \n     \n     \n       [220] \n       \n        arXiv:2610.38126\n       \n          (cross-list from cs.CR)\n\n        [ pdf ,  html ,  other ]\n     \n     \n       \n          Title: \n          Answer-Only Disclosure: Private-Context LLM Assistance over Web APIs with a Leakage Ledger\n         \n          Liang Yuan ,  Xiaohu Du ,  Ruikun Luo ,  Jinze Wang ,  Mashud Rana ,  Kaibin Wang ,  Jiangwen Zhou ,  Bo Li ,  Xiaoyu Xia  \n\n          Comments: \n          13 pages\n         \n\n\n          Subjects: \n           Cryptography and Security (cs.CR) ; Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)\n         \n\n         \n          Web assistants increasingly answer questions about a user's private documents by pairing a small on-device model with a cloud LLM behind a Web API. In collaborative decoding, the document stays on the client, and the cloud receives only a public view that the user approves, such as the question alone, but the two models exchange messages while the answer is generated. We show that the message transcript can disclose private content that neither the public view nor the answer contains. In a planted-secret audit, speculative decoding with private drafts did not include the secret code in its answers, yet sent the code to the cloud in rejected drafts. We present ADLER, a decoding protocol for streaming LLM APIs in which every message to the cloud depends only on the public view and the answer the user sees, regardless of how the client combines the two models or how the cloud behaves. A client-side leakage ledger then bounds how much the answer itself reveals relative to a public reference process built from the cloud's replies. In the audit, no ADLER configuration exposed the secret beyond the answer. On GSM8K math problems with numerical values masked in the cloud's input, ADLER with an unlimited leakage budget raises the exact-match accuracy of a 0.5B client from 0.41 to 0.83 and of a 1.5B client from 0.67 to 0.85 under lenient answer parsing, close to the 0.92 achieved by uploading the full problem. It also exceeds a zero-leakage program baseline (0.69) by 0.14 and 0.16, respectively. In this baseline, the cloud writes a program that the client runs offline using the private numbers. The ledger records the disclosure charge associated with this gain; budgets of 16 nats or less leave accuracy near the cloud-only baseline on GSM8K.\n         \n       \n     \n     \n       [221] \n       \n        arXiv:2610.38151\n       \n          (cross-list from cs.LG)\n\n        [ pdf ,  html ,  other ]\n     \n     \n       \n          Title: \n          Estimating Implicit Expected Log Density Ratios\n         \n          Avi Amalanshu ,  Chandler Squires ,  Yizhou Lu ,  Pradeep Ravikumar  \n\n\n\n          Subjects: \n           Machine Learning (cs.LG) ; Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI); Machine Learning (stat.ML)\n         \n\n         \n          When exact probabilities cannot be computed, \\emph{density ratio estimation (DRE)} is a key subroutine in the wide range of applications where density ratios appear, from experimental design to generative modelling and reinforcement learning. In this paper, we introduce the concept of an \\emph{expected log density ratio (ELDR)}: log density ratios that are integrated under some third, possibly distinct measure. We show many problems with implicit densities where DRE is naïvely used are actually ELDR estimation tasks, and prove fundamental limitations on finite-sample ELDR estimation. Based on these results, we argue that the typical DRE-based approaches are unsuitable for ELDR estimation, and provide some foundational motivation towards bespoke ELDR estimators. To demonstrate, we propose a simple modification to some DRE methods that allows samples convergent to the integral distribution to appear in training data. We show through benchmarks that this can improve ELDR estimation error.\n         \n       \n     \n     \n       [222] \n       \n        arXiv:2610.38174\n       \n          (cross-list from cs.CL)\n\n        [ pdf ,  html ,  other ]\n     \n     \n       \n          Title: \n          Scaling Lean Theorem Proving for Real-World Code Verification\n         \n          Debargha Ganguly ,  Divya Raghunathan ,  Dejan Jovanović ,  Vikash Singh ,  Soonho Kong ,  Aaron Tomb ,  Aman Goel ,  Ferhat Erata ,  Sam Bayless  \n\n\n\n          Subjects: \n           Computation and Language (cs.CL) ; Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI); Logic in Computer Science (cs.LO); Software Engineering (cs.SE)\n         \n\n         \n          We present EasyProver, a Lean proving system built from recursive agents with isolated contexts, asynchronous search, parallel tactic probing, and an elastic elaboration service. Using an off-the-shelf frontier model with no additional training, it proves 669/672 PutnamBench problems and all six IMO 2026 formalizations and resolves 597/600 NTP4VC verification conditions and all 189 VERINA tasks by proof or refutation. Because these benchmarks are close to saturation, we also introduce Aegis, which converts competitive programming problems into much harder verification tasks over real implementations. The pipeline writes safe Rust solutions, translates them into Lean with Aeneas, generates specifications, and labels each frozen statement with a kernel-checked proof or refutation. It produces 2,045 differentially validated Rust-Lean program pairs and 11,533 candidate specifications. Unlike hand-written benchmarks, these tasks carry implementation-level obligations from fixed-width arithmetic, explicit failure, generated loops, casts, and functionalized mutation. On a stratified sample of 2,000 statements, EasyProver proves 1,054 (52.7%) within a 30-minute budget per statement. The same tasks can also be used to benchmark other provers and as rewards for reinforcement learning.\n         \n       \n     \n     \n       [223] \n       \n        arXiv:2610.38197\n       \n          (cross-list from cs.LG)\n\n        [ pdf ,  html ,  other ]\n     \n     \n       \n          Title: \n          Convex-SKM-Net: Low-Latency Block Halfspace Corrections for Neural Convex Constraint Satisfaction\n         \n          Haoyu Zhu ,  Xiaochen Zhang ,  Qingchun Hou  \n\n\n\n          Subjects: \n           Machine Learning (cs.LG) ; Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI); Optimization and Control (math.OC)\n         \n\n         \n          Fast inference and strict constraint satisfaction are essential for learning-based decision-making. Existing methods often rely on constraint-specific projections or feasible-set parameterizations, while iterative repair can be costly. We introduce Convex-SKM-Net, a convex constraint-satisfaction layer that forms linearization halfspaces from residual and (sub)gradient oracles without constraint-specific inequality projectors. It applies fixed-depth dual projected-gradient descent (dual PGD) to minimum-displacement block problems using GPU-friendly matrix operations. We jointly learn the predictor and a positive-definite mobility, adapting the correction geometry to improve objective quality. We prove convergence of the outer iterates at fixed dual depth and linear convergence in distance under Slater's condition and bounded subgradients. All our test outputs on three second-order-cone model-predictive control (SOC-MPC) horizons and six power-system convex relaxations satisfy a $10^{-3}$ feasibility tolerance. Comparisons with the latest constraint-satisfaction methods show inference speedups of up to $144.3\\times$ on SOC-MPC and $7.7\\times$ on a convex quadratically constrained quadratic programming (QCQP) benchmark with joint training. On QCQP, the mean objective gap is $0.012\\%$, with full test feasibility at $10^{-4}$. Learning mobility reduces the five-seed affine-task mean gap by $22.4\\%$ and improves gaps in matched power-system comparisons.\n         \n       \n     \n     \n       [224] \n       \n        arXiv:2610.38209\n       \n          (cross-list from cs.CR)\n\n        [ pdf ,  html ,  other ]\n     \n     \n       \n          Title: \n          Is it my preference? Role Grounding Attack against Agent's Memory via Social Interaction\n         \n          Haoran Bu ,  Yiting Shen ,  Litian Zhang ,  Ruihan Hu ,  Xi Zhang  \n\n\n\n          Subjects: \n           Cryptography and Security (cs.CR) ; Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI); Multiagent Systems (cs.MA)\n         \n\n         \n          LLM-based agents now share experiences on social platforms and remember new preferences to enhance their performance. However, existing memory systems contain a vulnerability in role-to-principal attribution that attackers can exploit. We identify Role Grounding Misattribution, in which an attacker-designed interaction causes the victim agent to incorrectly record a preference from the attacker as a preference of the victim's user. Specifically, we are the first to formalize Role Grounding Attack (RGA), an attack model that exploits this vulnerability through nested conversation structures. In the memory formation stage, an experience-sharing post quoting a conversation between the attacker agent and its user is published, which explicitly identifies its publisher. The inner conversation expresses preferences from the user of the attacker agent. When the victim agent remembers preferences from this nested structure, its memory loses the context binding and grounds the attacker's preference to the victim's user instead. In the memory retrieval stage, the misattributed preference persists and influences the victim agent's decisions when it encounters relevant scenarios. We instantiate RGA as tool selection manipulation and privacy leakage. Tool selection manipulation causes a tool-call preference to be misattributed to the victim's user, steering the victim toward an attacker-controlled tool. Privacy leakage extends this by misattributing both a tool-call preference and a task-requirement preference to the victim's user. The victim selects the attacker's tool, then includes sensitive information in tool arguments to satisfy the task requirement. We evaluate RGA across five mainstream memory systems and six language models. Tool manipulation and privacy leakage both achieve high attack success rates. These results call for stronger role grounding.\n         \n       \n     \n     \n       [225] \n       \n        arXiv:2610.38214\n       \n          (cross-list from cs.SE)\n\n        [ pdf ,  html ,  other ]\n     \n     \n       \n          Title: \n          Skeleton Coding: Program Structure as a Specification for Agents\n         \n          Aditya Golatkar ,  Alessandro Achille ,  Renos Zabounidis ,  Vlad Sobal ,  Yangruibo Ding ,  Baishakhi Ray ,  Jonathan Buck ,  Guy Van den Broeck ,  Maryam Fazel ,  Pietro Perona ,  Wei Xia ,  Stefano Soatto  \n\n\n\n          Subjects: \n           Software Engineering (cs.SE) ; Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI); Machine Learning (cs.LG)\n         \n\n         \n          Coding agents are typically provided end-to-end behavioral specifications that the code they produce should satisfy, such as tests and input--output examples. How many such specifications must a developer provide, and which ones, for the agent to produce the developer's intended program? We argue that this cannot be determined a priori, for it depends on the structure of the implementation, which is left to the agent to decide. An agent first commits to a structure---a decomposition into data types, modules, and functions---which fixes a class of candidate programs; behavioral specifications then select a program within that class. A set of tests that suffices for one structure may leave another badly underdetermined, requiring different edge cases or exponentially more examples. Since the structure is chosen by the agent, developers cannot ensure their specification is complete without reverse-engineering it from thousands of lines of generated code. To resolve this, we introduce Skeleton Coding, in which developers jointly specify the structure---in high-level, code-like format---alongside behavioral specifications. Theoretically, we model AI-assisted coding as a control problem, and show that skeletons can reduce exponentially the number of tests developers must supply to steer an agent toward an acceptable program. Empirically, across four models and three benchmarks, the best-performing skeleton specifications reduce mean test error by 48.4% relative to the best end-to-end behavioral specifications. Even when the agent can query a complete reference implementation, adding a skeleton reduces mean test error relatively by up to 27.0%. Skeletons also narrow the average gap between small and large models by about 52.4% while lowering implementation cost by 15--26x, and reduce code erosion by 24.7% on average.\n         \n       \n     \n     \n       [226] \n       \n        arXiv:2610.38221\n       \n          (cross-list from cs.RO)\n\n        [ pdf ,  html ,  other ]\n     \n     \n       \n          Title: \n          CuraEval: Evaluating Demonstration Curation on Mixed-Skill Robot Data\n         \n          Jiuyi Xu ,  Meida Chen ,  Yang Ye ,  Yangming Shi  \n\n          Comments: \n          Accepted by the CoRL 2026 Workshop \"Oops, I Erred\". 10 pages, 2 figures, 2 tables\n         \n\n\n          Subjects: \n           Robotics (cs.RO) ; Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)\n         \n\n         \n          Robot imitation learning uses demonstrations from operators of varying skill, but selecting training data remains challenging. Existing methods score demonstrations using smoothness, diversity, or estimated influence on policy performance, but differences in selection and evaluation protocols make it difficult to determine whether they outperform simple baselines. We introduce \\textbf{CuraEval}, a framework combining mathematical analysis of demonstration scores with controlled comparisons against fixed size-matched random subsets and full-data training on mixed-skill robomimic data using Diffusion Policy and BC-RNN. We show that the evaluated PSD score depends only on episode length and squared position magnitudes, and produces nearly the same ranking as episode length on \\textit{square} ($\\rho=0.999$). With Diffusion Policy, selecting the shortest 50\\% of demonstrations improves \\textit{square} success from 70.7\\% under random selection to 76.5\\%. Compared with random selection, our adapted geometric-entropy LOO selector reduces success at 25\\% retention from 94.7\\% to 74.0\\% on \\textit{can} and achieves 41.7\\% on \\textit{square}, below the mean success rates of all three independently sampled random subsets (59.8--62.5\\%). On the same 3 \\textit{square} Diffusion Policy training seeds, length-based selection at 75\\% retention improves success over full-data training from 76.7\\% to 86.7\\% under the original protocol, but lowers it from 80.5\\% to 79.5\\% after checkpoint reporting, rollout count, and evaluation seed change jointly. No evaluated curated condition significantly outperforms full-data training after Holm correction, although confidence intervals still permit useful gains. Code is available at  this https URL .\n         \n       \n     \n     \n       [227] \n       \n        arXiv:2610.38239\n       \n          (cross-list from cs.CY)\n\n        [ pdf ,  html ,  other ]\n     \n     \n       \n          Title: \n          The Indian Financial Automated Advice Bias (IFAAB) Dataset : Auditing Socioeconomic and Demographic Disparities in LLM-Generated Financial Advice in India\n         \n          Kriti Mahajan  \n\n\n\n          Subjects: \n           Computers and Society (cs.CY) ; Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)\n         \n\n         \n          Large language models are increasingly used for financial guidance, yet little is known about whether their advice changes across social identities in Global South contexts. We introduce the Indian Financial Automated Advice Bias (IFAAB) benchmark, a counterfactual audit dataset of 31,734 GPT-4o-generated financial-advice responses for synthetic Indian personas varying by gender, caste, education, employment, residence, age, and district. Holding the underlying request fixed, we evaluate disparities in LLM-implied wealth, income, savings, projected gains, textual similarity, advice topics, and qualitative bias judgments. In the gender-only audit, male-labelled profiles receive 60.1\\% higher initial total resources and 46.8\\% higher post-advice total resources than comparable female-labelled profiles; in the caste-augmented audit, the post-advice total-resource gap remains 49.4\\%. Adding district information reduces the aggregate post-advice total-resource gender gap to 7.9\\%, revealing strong geographic conditioning. Standalone caste comparisons also show systematic stratification: relative to General-category profiles, OBC, SC, and ST profiles receive significantly lower post-advice total resources, respectively. Textual audits show low semantic similarity between paired gendered responses, with an average TF--IDF cosine similarity score of 0.44. Qualitative analysis shows that women are more often steered toward cautious saving, community support, and basic financial literacy, while men receive more growth-oriented advice involving investment, entrepreneurship, and asset accumulation. IFAAB provides a reusable benchmark for evaluating fairness, suitability, and consumer-protection risks in LLM-mediated financial advice, and highlights the need for locally grounded, intersectional evaluation of AI systems in high-stakes economic domains.\n         \n       \n     \n     \n       [228] \n       \n        arXiv:2610.38241\n       \n          (cross-list from cs.CV)\n\n        [ pdf ,  html ,  other ]\n     \n     \n       \n          Title: \n          MEV: A Multi-Event Video Dataset for Long-Take Generation\n         \n          Peiyuan Zhu ,  Shaoan Xie ,  Yifan Shen ,  Wen Tian ,  Zijian Li ,  Guangyi Chen ,  Kun Zhang  \n\n          Comments: \n          Accepted to NeurIPS 2026. Project page:  this https URL \n         \n\n\n          Subjects: \n           Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV) ; Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)\n         \n\n         \n          Current datasets for text-to-video generation are largely limited to short clips depicting a single event or coarse-grained captions. As a result, they provide limited supervision for modeling long-take temporal dependencies, often leading to temporally inconsistent generations when describing multiple events. In this paper, we introduce MEV, a multi-event video dataset designed for long-take video generation. MEV provides high-quality real-world videos where subjects can perform a sequence of distinct, non-overlapping events, featuring precise temporal boundaries and fine-grained procedural annotations. This event-centric discretization reduces confounding factors and enables models to support temporally localized conditioning and build long-take videos from atomic event units. Furthermore, we introduce a comprehensive annotation pipeline that segments videos into temporally grounded events, and generates structured captions at both the global level and the event level. In addition, under an event-centric evaluation protocol, we conduct multi-event generation experiments by fine-tuning on MEV with temporally grounded event conditioning, leading to improved long-take event transitions and more coherent content. We believe this work provides a publicly available resource for multi-event fine-tuning and event-centric evaluation, together with practical insights into learning from temporally structured video-text data.\n         \n       \n     \n     \n       [229] \n       \n        arXiv:2610.38244\n       \n          (cross-list from cs.SE)\n\n        [ pdf ,  html ,  other ]\n     \n     \n       \n          Title: \n          Precedent or Intent? What Coding Agents Follow When Evolving Existing Code\n         \n          Haichao Zhu ,  Geyang Xu ,  Zhaorui Yang ,  Jiyuan Wang ,  Qian Zhang  \n\n          Comments: \n          16 pages, 7 figures, 6 tables\n         \n\n\n          Subjects: \n           Software Engineering (cs.SE) ; Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)\n         \n\n         \n          Existing code reveals past implementation decisions but rarely explains the reasoning behind them. When coding agents implement new features or modify existing functionality, past decisions may conflict with new task instructions. This raises a fundamental question for agentic software evolution: when multiple implementations satisfy the same functional requirements, how do coding agents decide whether to follow existing code or new task instructions? We investigate this question through bidirectional, controlled experiments in purpose-built repositories, followed by validation in real-world open-source repositories. Our approach isolates three competing sources of guidance: (1) agents' default preferences, (2) repository precedents, and (3) explicit task instructions. We conduct 5,625 trials across four coding agents and six implementation pairs, with validation in four open-source repositories spanning three programming languages. We find that agents share the same default implementation choices, yet overwhelmingly follow repository precedent when it conflicts with those defaults (359/360 trials). Crucially, not all precedents carry equal influence: three supporting local files yield 96% adoption, compared with only 4% for sibling-resource files, and changing file placement alone can shift adoption from 0% to 100% at a fixed example count. When new task intent conflicts with existing code, written priority rules achieve substantially higher success than code examples alone (59/60 versus 38/60 joint successes for two agents on two implementation pairs). The effectiveness of explicit rules also extends to open-source repositories, where a harness-loaded rule raises joint success from 0/120 to 117/120. These findings motivate design-decision traceability that preserves not only implementation choices but also the rationale and priorities that should guide future changes.\n         \n       \n     \n     \n       [230] \n       \n        arXiv:2610.38252\n       \n          (cross-list from eess.AS)\n\n        [ pdf ,  html ,  other ]\n     \n     \n       \n          Title: \n          A Tutorial on Generative Models for Music Audio\n         \n          Mateusz Modrzejewski ,  Joanna Kurkowska ,  Dorien Herremans  \n\n\n\n          Subjects: \n           Audio and Speech Processing (eess.AS) ; Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI); Emerging Technologies (cs.ET); Multimedia (cs.MM); Sound (cs.SD)\n         \n\n         \n          Music audio generation spans instrumental clips, full songs with vocals, edits of existing recordings, and systems that play alongside a musician in real time. This monograph explains the methods behind these systems for readers familiar with neural-network training who are new to music generation. It introduces neural audio codecs and autoencoders, autoregressive and masked token models, diffusion and flow matching, sampling acceleration, and preference optimization in a shared notation. Worked examples connect training objectives and sampling procedures to published systems. The application sections relate the musical requirements of instrumental generation, songs, editing, and live performance to model design. Comparison tables record representations, methods, inputs, tasks, and release information documented in papers and official sources. Symbolic composition, speech, and sound effects are included where they connect to music audio. We explain how to interpret automatic metrics and learned judges, design and report listening tests, and assess evidence about data provenance, memorization, attribution, and output detection. Selected legal decisions and disputes illustrate the implications for training and release. The reviewed systems combine established generative paradigms with new representations, planning stages, and post-training procedures. Differences in data, scale, and evaluation limit what cross-system comparisons can establish about the sources of reported gains.\n         \n       \n     \n     \n       [231] \n       \n        arXiv:2610.38267\n       \n          (cross-list from cs.CL)\n\n        [ pdf ,  html ,  other ]\n     \n     \n       \n          Title: \n          Cross Lingual Abstractive Summarization for Marathi\n         \n          Asang Dani ,  Shailesh R Sathe  \n\n\n\n          Subjects: \n           Computation and Language (cs.CL) ; Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)\n         \n\n         \n          Text summarization is an important task for advancing natural language processing (NLP) research and applications in Indic languages. Despite progress in extractive and abstractive summarization, both monolingual summarization (MS) and cross-lingual summarization (CLS) remain relatively underexplored for these languages. Recent advances in Transformer architectures, pretrained language models (PLMs), and dataset availability offer new opportunities to address this gap. In this work, we construct cross-lingual training data from the monolingual Marathi MahaSum corpus and combine it with the smaller cross-lingual PMIndiaSum dataset to fine-tune IndicTrans2, an Indic neural machine translation model. We evaluate the resulting models using ROUGE, chrF and chrF++ metrics, focusing primarily on Marathi-to-English summarization due to computational constraints. We also discuss the applicability of our approach to the English-to-Marathi direction. Our experiments demonstrate that leveraging a pretrained translation model yields substantially better summarization performance than the IndicBART and mBART-50 based baselines reported in previous work on cross-lingual summarization.\n         \n       \n     \n     \n       [232] \n       \n        arXiv:2610.38277\n       \n          (cross-list from cs.LG)\n\n        [ pdf ,  html ,  other ]\n     \n     \n       \n          Title: \n          The Role of Quantization in Loss Spikes\n         \n          Zaiqian Chen ,  Aditya Somasundaram ,  Sophie Li ,  Sean McLeish ,  John Kirchenbauer ,  Tom Goldstein ,  Micah Goldblum  \n\n\n\n          Subjects: \n           Machine Learning (cs.LG) ; Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)\n         \n\n         \n          Loss spikes in language model pretraining can waste substantial compute and derail otherwise healthy training runs. A primary driver of these instabilities is the growth and divergence of output logits in the final language modeling (LM) head. In this work, we study a particularly costly form of this instability associated with output logit growth: delayed, terminal divergences that emerge only after prolonged training, which we call time-bomb spikes. We find that these instabilities are preceded by rapidly growing quantization error in the LM head, which eventually culminates in a loss spike. Crucially, reducing numerical precision accelerates time-bomb spikes, while increasing precision improves stability. We then propose a simple yet effective mitigation that replaces AdamW on the LM head with SGD with momentum, suppressing logit growth and improving stability over long training horizons. Finally, we find that existing mitigation techniques from the literature (along with our own) all reduce quantization error, suggesting a shared numerical mechanism behind their effectiveness. Together, our results identify finite precision error as a hidden driver of time-bomb spikes and show that suppressing this error is key to stable long-horizon pretraining.\n         \n       \n     \n     \n       [233] \n       \n        arXiv:2610.38292\n       \n          (cross-list from cs.GT)\n\n        [ pdf ,  html ,  other ]\n     \n     \n       \n          Title: \n          Vendster: An Evaluation Arena to Investigate How Forecasting Tool Quality Affects LLM Agent Behaviors in Buyer-Vendor Negotiation\n         \n          Charin Polpanumas  \n\n\n\n          Subjects: \n           Computer Science and Game Theory (cs.GT) ; Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)\n         \n\n         \n          Time-series forecasters are ranked almost entirely by accuracy metrics, but a forecast's decision value, its effect on the downstream decision, need not track accuracy and is rarely measured. This problem compounds as large language model (LLM) agents increasingly consume forecasters as tools, mediating them through stochastic, opaque policies. We present Vendster, an evaluation arena measuring how a forecaster's bias and prediction-interval width change the outcome of a buyer--vendor discount negotiation between two LLM agents. In the reference instance Vendster-M5, sampling base demand patterns from the M5 dataset, we evaluate two frontier-closed LLM families (Claude Opus 4.6 and GPT-5.6 Sol) and two open ones (DeepSeek-V3.2 and Qwen3-235B-A22B). Model-generated forecasts raise sales target attainment by up to +17 percentage points (p<0.001), yet standard forecast evaluation metrics such as relWQL and MASE do not predict negotiation outcomes at a statistically significant level. To isolate the causal structure, we substitute in synthetic forecasts with controlled bias and interval width. Under-prediction bias significantly improves outcomes for agents that react more to the forecasts---dose-response slopes -0.387 and -0.416 (p<0.001). Interval width has no statistically significant effect at zero bias. These findings indicate that the direction of forecast bias and agent forecast-reactivity are more strongly associated with downstream outcomes than forecast accuracy. Vendster is fully customizable and released under CC BY-NC 4.0 at  this https URL .\n         \n       \n     \n     \n       [234] \n       \n        arXiv:2610.38293\n       \n          (cross-list from cs.CL)\n\n        [ pdf ,  html ,  other ]\n     \n     \n       \n          Title: \n          EPSBench: Benchmarking LLM Agents for Earnings Forecasting\n         \n          Yanbin Yin ,  Chuyu Xu ,  Liqin Ye ,  Jing Peng ,  Chao Zhang  \n\n\n\n          Subjects: \n           Computation and Language (cs.CL) ; Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)\n         \n\n         \n          Quarterly earnings forecasting requires an agent to connect financial evidence to the income-statement line items that produce a company's reported earnings. We introduce EPSBench, a benchmark for studying this ability across 149 firm-quarters from 86 US companies. Agents read a fixed collection of filings, earnings-call transcripts, and news, then estimate company-specific income-statement drivers with cited evidence. A shared accounting calculator converts these estimates into GAAP diluted earnings per share (EPS), providing a common basis for comparing forecast assumptions. We evaluate six models under a common harness, assessing point forecast error relative to date-matched analyst consensus and checking whether their prediction intervals achieve the stated coverage. Three models show no detectable paired difference in point forecast error from consensus, and three have higher error. Across all six models, nominal 80% intervals contain reported EPS in only 37--70% of runs that return an interval, and most misses fall above the upper bound because the forecasts run low. The driver records trace most of the error gap between models to operating-income estimates.\n         \n       \n     \n     \n       [235] \n       \n        arXiv:2610.38315\n       \n          (cross-list from eess.SY)\n\n        [ pdf ,  html ,  other ]\n     \n     \n       \n          Title: \n          LLM-PowerTrace: A Dataset of Time-Resolved GPU Power Traces for AI Compute\n         \n          Jiansheng Wang ,  Jaskeerat Singh ,  Haochen Xu ,  Xinyi Li ,  Mingyuan Yan ,  Shaohui Liu ,  Yuzhuo Li ,  Yize Chen ,  Wenqi Cui  \n\n          Comments: \n          In Submission; Code and dataset are available at  this https URL \n         \n\n\n          Subjects: \n           Systems and Control (eess.SY) ; Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI); Operating Systems (cs.OS)\n         \n\n         \n          AI compute is becoming large and fast-changing electrical loads. Its impacts on data center infrastructure and power grids depend not only on total energy consumption, but also on how their power draw moves over sub-second to minute timescales, including peaks, ramps, and synchronized fluctuations across accelerators. Existing energy benchmarks for large language models (LLMs) report aggregate quantities such as joules per token and rarely release the underlying time-series measurements, leaving these power dynamics poorly characterized and difficult to model. We introduce LLM-PowerTrace, an open dataset of time-resolved, per-GPU power traces for LLM fine-tuning and inference, sampled every 100\\,ms on varying NVIDIA platforms paired with complete workload and system metadata. For fine-tuning, LLM-PowerTrace profiles open model families spanning a wide range of model sizes and fine-tuning methods, together with controlled sweeps over batch size, sequence length, precision, parallelism, and checkpointing. For inference, it covers variations in concurrency, GPU frequency, KV-cache and weight quantization, prompt and output lengths, and request-arrival patterns. LLM-PowerTrace profiles a set of open model families across a wide range of model sizes, and controlled sweeps over batch size, sequence length, precision, parallelism, and checkpointing for fine-tuning, and it sweeps concurrency, GPU frequency, KV cache and weight quantization, prompt and output lengths, and request arrival patterns for inference. Built upon power traces, we define power-grid-facing metrics as well as AI-compute power\\&energy scaling law for extrapolating beyond the measured settings. Together, LLM-PowerTrace provides a dataset foundation for modeling, forecasting, and managing the fast-changing electrical behavior of AI compute.\n         \n       \n     \n     \n       [236] \n       \n        arXiv:2610.38318\n       \n          (cross-list from cs.RO)\n\n        [ pdf ,  html ,  other ]\n     \n     \n       \n          Title: \n          What Should a Navigator Remember? Spatiotemporal Memory for Zero-Shot Vision-Language Navigation\n         \n          Yuhang Peng ,  Zhikai Wang ,  Ziyi Wang ,  Jiaru Zhong ,  Xiaoji Zheng ,  Jingzhi Huang ,  Yi Wang ,  Jiangtao Gong  \n\n          Comments: \n          20 pages, 3 figures, 8 Tables\n         \n\n\n          Subjects: \n           Robotics (cs.RO) ; Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)\n         \n\n         \n          Zero-shot vision-and-language navigation (VLN) requires an agent to use past observations without navigation-specific training. We study what information an 8B-scale navigation system should retain and how it should be organized for subsequent decisions. Our agent combines instruction progress, hierarchically compressed observation history anchored to poses, and accumulated geometry in complementary task, temporal, and spatial memories. An 8B observer summarizes observations, and an 8B reasoner uses the memories to select waypoint intents or request additional views, a geometric controller executes the selected targets. A semantic information perspective motivates the distinction between retaining evidence, making it accessible to the reasoner, and acquiring further observations. On R2R-CE val-unseen, the complete system achieves the highest among the evaluated Qwen3-based zero-shot baselines. Removing temporal memory reduces SR and sharply increases average path length from 16.9 to 39.1m, removing the spatial-map and planning component reduces SR as well. Real-world experiments on a Unitree Go2 show that our zero-shot method achieves higher success rates than the post-trained baseline in both corridor and office environments, suggesting better transfer to the evaluated real-world settings. As a complementary evaluation, LAW2 uses paired instruction interventions to examine behavioral responses, instruction following ability beyond endpoint success. These results support preserving task progress, observation history, and geometry in structured memory for zero-shot navigation with smaller open models.\n         \n       \n     \n     \n       [237] \n       \n        arXiv:2610.38319\n       \n          (cross-list from cs.CV)\n\n        [ pdf ,  html ,  other ]\n     \n     \n       \n          Title: \n          Extremely Sparse-View Computed Tomography from 2D Projections via Pose-Aware Diffusion Priors\n         \n          Linrui Dai ,  Jinxiu Liang ,  Chu Zhou ,  Mae Yamaguchi ,  Ryuya Takahashi ,  Imari Sato  \n\n          Comments: \n          NeurIPS 2026 poster\n         \n\n\n          Subjects: \n           Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV) ; Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)\n         \n\n         \n          Extremely sparse-view computed tomography (xSV-CT) is critical when scanning time, motion, or radiation tolerance limits each scan to only a few projections. In many such scenarios, the same acquisition limits also constrain dataset construction, making pre-reconstructed 3D volumes from dense scans unavailable. This exposes a gap in current sparse-view approaches: Optimization-based methods, which use the sparse measurements directly but lack data-driven priors to suppress artifacts, and learning-based methods, which use strong priors but usually require densely scanned training volumes acquired outside the xSV-CT regime. We study this train-sparse / infer-sparse setting by learning a pose-aware diffusion prior from one 2D projection per training sample, then guiding diffusion sampling with measured-view fidelity, shared-volume reprojection, and projection-physics consistency for tomographic reconstruction. On two public CT datasets across 1-, 4-, and 8-view settings, the proposed method achieves the best novel-view synthesis scores and best or competitive reconstruction quality compared with baselines that use dense-volume supervision.\n         \n       \n     \n     \n       [238] \n       \n        arXiv:2610.38329\n       \n          (cross-list from cs.LG)\n\n        [ pdf ,  html ,  other ]\n     \n     \n       \n          Title: \n          A Case Study of a Multi-Agent Kernel-Optimization Loop That Learns a New AI Accelerator\n         \n          Byonggon Chun  \n\n          Comments: \n          7 pages, 3 figures, 2 tables. Round 1 of the NPU Model Optimization Competition at MICRO 2026. Code:  this https URL \n         \n\n\n          Subjects: \n           Machine Learning (cs.LG) ; Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI); Hardware Architecture (cs.AR); Programming Languages (cs.PL)\n         \n\n         \n          Frontier large language models write correct CUDA kernels for most single-operator benchmark tasks, yet their accuracy collapses on accelerators whose architecture and programming language they have rarely seen. We present a case study of an LLM agent loop built for such accelerators. Our example is the FuriosaAI RNGD NPU, programmed in a recent, sparsely documented Rust DSL, where the loop optimized three Gemma-4-12B decode kernels over 12 days on a shared, scarce device. The loop builds its own target knowledge: it writes and checks a summary of the DSL documentation, measures the chip with microbenchmarks, records compiler-derived legality rules, and revisits this knowledge when the toolchain changes, rechecking rules and measurements where it can. A role-based search over per-kernel Design Trees explores whole-kernel designs and accumulates small accepted changes in the best version of each kernel. Because device time is scarce and readings are noisy, cheap checks screen new designs before any device run, and late in the run a change was accepted only after repeated comparisons with the best kernel on the device. The final kernels reach a 9.293x geomean speedup over the official baseline, each within 1.25x of the cycles needed just to move its weights. Our prompts, profiler and acceptance test are available at  this https URL .\n         \n       \n     \n     \n       [239] \n       \n        arXiv:2610.38332\n       \n          (cross-list from cs.LG)\n\n        [ pdf ,  html ,  other ]\n     \n     \n       \n          Title: \n          MUSE: Multi-Level Memory with Selective Runtime Injection for Scientific Coding Agents\n         \n          Haoran Jin ,  Defu Lian ,  Xiting Wang ,  Hong Xie  \n\n          Comments: \n          17 pages, 8 figures. Under review\n         \n\n\n          Subjects: \n           Machine Learning (cs.LG) ; Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)\n         \n\n         \n          Historical experience can guide scientific coding at multiple levels, but task-level retrieval may miss implementation lessons that become relevant during execution. We introduce MUSE, a memory management framework combining multi-level memory with selective runtime injection for scientific coding agents. MUSE builds global, approach-level, and implementation-level memory from historical trajectories. At initialization, the agent receives global memory and problem-matched approach-level memory. During execution, current interactions are used to retrieve implementation-level memory. An LLM gate selects relevant experience and generates specific review advice, or skips injection. Across four benchmarks spanning numerical problem solving, kernel optimization, and scientific software repair, MUSE outperforms retrieval and memory baselines on all evaluation metrics. Ablations support the multi-level design and gated injection, while transfer experiments show that memory remains useful across kernel benchmarks. These results show that multi-level memory and selective runtime injection enable effective reuse of experience in scientific coding. Code is available at  this https URL .\n         \n       \n     \n     \n       [240] \n       \n        arXiv:2610.38336\n       \n          (cross-list from cs.CL)\n\n        [ pdf ,  html ,  other ]\n     \n     \n       \n          Title: \n          Statutory Article Retrieval from Arabic Judicial Narratives: Family Law and Law of Evidence in Saudi Arabia\n         \n          Zarah Shibli ,  Areej Alhothali ,  Lulwah Alharigy  \n\n          Comments: \n          Submitted to the MLNLP 2026 Conference\n         \n\n\n          Subjects: \n           Computation and Language (cs.CL) ; Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)\n         \n\n         \n          Reliable legal information retrieval must ground legal reasoning in authoritative statutory sources. In Arabic judicial settings, long factual narratives must be mapped to concise articles that express abstract legal rules, creating a narrative-to-legal-rule abstraction gap. Using a benchmark of 200 synthetic Saudi Arabic judicial cases and 381 articles from the Family Law and the Law of Evidence, we compare word-level and char-4 BM25, zero-shot dense encoders, per-law retrieval, dense-sparse reciprocal rank fusion (RRF), and a proposed Decompose-and-Refine (DaR) pipeline. In DaR, a large language model splits each narrative into disputed legal issues and generates, for each issue, three retrieval channels: a statute-style paraphrase, legal keywords, and a candidate taxonomy chapter. On the whole corpus, the instructed Qwen3-Embedding-8B dense encoder reaches an MRR@10 of 0.849, the best result of any non-decomposed system. Under per-law scoring, this advantage reflects Family Law only: restricted to Evidence articles, the same encoder drops to an MRR@10 of 0.087, and fusion with BM25 raises it only to 0.207. DaR changes this picture, raising Evidence MRR@10 to 0.696 and Family MRR@10 to 0.864. Without decomposition, the two laws favor different strategies; under DaR, the same retriever achieves the best MRR@10 on both.\n         \n       \n     \n     \n       [241] \n       \n        arXiv:2610.38349\n       \n          (cross-list from cs.CV)\n\n        [ pdf ,  html ,  other ]\n     \n     \n       \n          Title: \n          Aligning Misaligned Benchmarks for Single-View Novel View Synthesis\n         \n          Yang-Che Tseng ,  Erqun Dong ,  Jeong-gi Kwak ,  Gopal Sharma ,  Shakiba Kheradmand ,  Stephan Garbin ,  Hossam Isack ,  Kwang Moo Yi  \n\n\n\n          Subjects: \n           Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV) ; Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)\n         \n\n         \n          While single-view novel view synthesis methods have been dramatically enhanced recently, how to quantify their performance has yet to be standardized. In this work, we demonstrate that an important factor lies in how each method's own reference frame is aligned with the so-called ground-truth pose. Existing methods often rely on test-time optimization of target poses for this alignment, which inherently is ill-posed due to the complexity of the optimization landscape of camera poses vs render accuracy, leading to methods being evaluated at an arbitrary local optimum. Some recognize this limitation and thus rely on 3D reconstructions of entire scenes using training and test views to align poses, which may be infeasible. In this work, instead, we propose to use traditional 2D-to-2D and 2D-to-3D image matching techniques to align 3D reconstructions. We also evaluate only within co-visible regions, between different views, which is the only place where a `correct' ground truth can be defined. Our evaluations show that with proper coordinate alignment, novel-view synthesis methods that use explicit 3D Gaussians achieve the state of the art, despite being previously reported in the literature to be outperformed by pure image-based 2D methods. We further provide a simple baseline that directly utilizes an off-the-shelf video generator and a feed-forward geometry estimator. Our simple baseline is already comparable to state-of-the-art methods specifically trained for novel-view synthesis, without any task-specific training.\n         \n       \n     \n     \n       [242] \n       \n        arXiv:2610.38352\n       \n          (cross-list from cs.CV)\n\n        [ pdf ,  html ,  other ]\n     \n     \n       \n          Title: \n          WeVisCut: The Whole Editing Room in One Agent From Narration to Long-Form Video\n         \n          Chenyu Mu ,  Wenming Weng ,  Qinrui Wang ,  Xingsong Ye ,  Hao Yu ,  Jun He ,  Chong Sun ,  Chen Li ,  Jing Lyu ,  Erkun Yang ,  Cheng Deng  \n\n\n\n          Subjects: \n           Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV) ; Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)\n         \n\n         \n          Producing long-form videos from narration requires interdependent decisions that remain coherent across shots. Existing agentic editors often use multi-stage workflows driven by general models, leaving the quality-cost trade-off of compact, task-adapted policies unclear. We address this gap with WeVisCut, a long-form script-to-video system that connects tool execution with policy learning. Its agent harness maintains shared production state so that tool feedback informs subsequent decisions and repairs. Because finished videos do not expose these decisions, our four-stage data pipeline combines editing inversion with verified replay to turn recovered editing intent into executable supervision. Applied to 3,750 finished videos, this pipeline yields over 130K training examples. We use this supervision to train a single 9B policy that coordinates production and recovers from failures within the same harness. Moreover, we introduce a 100-task benchmark and five metrics assessing editing quality and delivery reliability. Under a shared tool environment, WeVisCut substantially outperforms its base model and achieves comparable performance to Claude Opus 5 while using nearly 33x fewer generated tokens per successful output minute. It also outperforms task-matched native editors on our proposed metrics.\n         \n       \n     \n     \n       [243] \n       \n        arXiv:2610.38360\n       \n          (cross-list from cs.CV)\n\n        [ pdf ,  html ,  other ]\n     \n     \n       \n          Title: \n          SparseTokenGS: Gaussian-based Sparse-View CBCT Reconstruction via Reusable Token Pool\n         \n          Zihan Deng ,  Zhenzhen Gong ,  Yujie Liu ,  Jiaxin Gong ,  Dingkun Liu ,  Kexian Xie ,  Yixing Huang ,  Peng Jin ,  Tianye Niu ,  Zhisheng Wang ,  Fenglin Liu ,  Hongming Shan ,  Lequan Yu  \n\n          Comments: \n          10 pages, 12 figures. Submitted to IEEE Transactions on Medical Imaging\n         \n\n\n          Subjects: \n           Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV) ; Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)\n         \n\n         \n          Sparse-view cone-beam computed tomography is widely used for fast, low-dose volumetric imaging and lesion localization in dentistry and image-guided interventions, but it introduces severe artifacts. Feed-forward Gaussian methods transfer the reconstruction cost to training and decode each coordinate from projection-aligned features and neighboring anchors. However, information from the whole scan is written only when the field is constructed and cannot be retrieved during coordinate decoding, leaving the volume inconsistent within the scan. This is a common limitation of such methods: once the field is fixed, decoding uses only local features and reconstruction quality drops substantially. We propose SparseTokenGS, a Gaussian-based method for sparse-view cone-beam computed tomography reconstruction via a reusable token pool. Anchor Token Fusion and Point-Token Attention query this shared pool at the anchors and at the query coordinates, respectively. The local Gaussian prior is retained, and the volume is reconstructed in one forward pass, without per-scan optimization or a projection-domain loss. On three public chest and dental datasets, SparseTokenGS achieves the highest reconstruction performance among the compared methods. On clinical dental scans, reconstruction from only six projections reaches 29.51 decibels in peak signal-to-noise ratio and a structural similarity of 0.842 against the full-view reference, and off-the-shelf tooth-and-pulp segmentation keeps these structures separable for root-canal localization. Experiments on volcanic rock and chicken show performance after same-object adaptation. A reusable token pool gives feed-forward reconstruction a mechanism that keeps scan-wide context available through decoding and can benefit future rapid clinical lesion localization and analysis. The code is available at:  this https URL .\n         \n       \n     \n     \n       [244] \n       \n        arXiv:2610.38361\n       \n          (cross-list from cs.CR)\n\n        [ pdf ,  html ,  other ]\n     \n     \n       \n          Title: \n          Manifest-Assisted Retrieval in Artificial Intelligence Search: Evidence Gaps and a Security Evaluation\n         \n          Gangzhen Qian ,  Kainan Zhou ,  Chuhong Xu ,  Hang Xiao ,  Zhi Jing ,  Rong Bao ,  Lu Yi ,  Zhaoyi Li  \n\n          Comments: \n          8 pages, 3 figures, 5 table. Accepted at IEEE FLLM 2026. Author accepted manuscript. Reproducibility artifact:  this https URL \n         \n\n\n          Subjects: \n           Cryptography and Security (cs.CR) ; Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI); Computational Engineering, Finance, and Science (cs.CE); Computation and Language (cs.CL); Databases (cs.DB); Information Retrieval (cs.IR)\n         \n\n         \n          The external sources retained in our non-systematic audit did not isolate the incremental security or utility effect of consuming /llms.txt while ordinary retrieval remained fixed. The initial audit covered 39 sources through August 16, 2026, with later targeted updates through October 14. A purposive 15-tool study observed no automatic requests in 45 trials at one endpoint; a non-probability log study found that 97% of domains with detected files received no recorded requests in May 2026. We separate publication, fetch, model use, and action; define five operational threat paths and paired security-utility estimands; and exercise deterministic pre-model controls with 30 author-defined parser fixtures. We also execute a reduced pilot: 24 tasks across eight sites, five conditions, and 120 real model responses, with ordinary evidence held fixed. Frozen automatic scoring identifies attacker-target adoption in $21/24$ raw adversarial responses, with one further response unresolved; the adversarial URLs-only condition has no known adoptions and one unresolved response. The pilot measures output changes conditional on supplied evidence, without live retrieval or external actions. Together, the framework and pilot distinguish manifest-content effects from publication, discovery, and downstream execution, while leaving full retrieval and agent evaluation open.\n         \n       \n     \n     \n       [245] \n       \n        arXiv:2610.38402\n       \n          (cross-list from cs.CL)\n\n        [ pdf ,  html ,  other ]\n     \n     \n       \n          Title: \n          LLMs as Judges and Examinees for Medical Item Difficulty Prediction\n         \n          Xiaomeng Xiong ,  Marcus Walker ,  Yanlin Jiang  \n\n\n\n          Subjects: \n           Computation and Language (cs.CL) ; Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)\n         \n\n         \n          Predicting medical item difficulty from item text alone remains challenging. Prior work has largely relied on linguistic features or text representations derived from general-purpose and domain-specific language models, but predictive performance has remained modest. This study takes a different approach by using difficulty-related signals that emerge when open-weight large language models (LLMs) interact with test items. Using USMLE and PANCE medical licensure examination items, we examined LLMs in two roles. As judges, the models provided absolute difficulty ratings and pairwise difficulty comparisons. As simulated examinees, they generated response-based signals including correctness, keyed-option probability, response entropy, and the probability margin between the top two options. We then combined the strongest judgment and response signals with baseline item features in downstream prediction models. Pairwise difficulty judgment was the most consistent individual LLM signal across both datasets, while simulated-examinee signals and absolute judgments provided complementary information in some feature combinations. The best configurations reached a Spearman correlation of $\\rho$ = .447 on USMLE, exceeding the best system in the 2024 BEA shared task ($\\rho$ = .25), and $\\rho$ = .494 on PANCE. These results were comparable to direct absolute judgments from the proprietary frontier model Claude Opus 5 ($\\rho$ = .443 and .488, respectively). Overall, judgment and response signals from open-weight LLMs captured difficulty-related information beyond text representations. Because these models can be deployed locally, the approach offers a practical option for predicting the difficulty of unreleased or high-security test items.\n         \n       \n     \n     \n       [246] \n       \n        arXiv:2610.38405\n       \n          (cross-list from cs.RO)\n\n        [ pdf ,  other ]\n     \n     \n       \n          Title: \n          SpaceAct: Benchmarking 3D Spatial and Temporal Reasoning via Closed-Loop Action and Active Perception\n         \n          Chenglong Shi ,  Zekai Yuan ,  Yue Song ,  Jon Pu ,  Will Wu  \n\n\n\n          Subjects: \n           Robotics (cs.RO) ; Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)\n         \n\n         \n          To solve problems in 3D space rather than merely describe images, multimodal large language models (MLLMs) need 3D spatiotemporal reasoning: reconstructing 3D geometry from occluded 2D views, anticipating physical dynamics, and executing precise, closed-loop actions. Prior studies test passive VQA on static images or symbolic planning, requiring no execution or supplying privileged coordinates while ignoring collision, statics, and timing. We introduce SpaceAct, an interactive benchmark evaluating 3D spatiotemporal reasoning via closed-loop physical action and active perception, with SAPA, a Spatiotemporal Active-Probing Agent framework. Built on interlocking bricks and mechanical puzzles, it has 108 verified instances across 36 task families in six categories, without privileged coordinates, and diagnostic modes (+plan, +state, no-camera, blind, L7 levers). In brick-bridging or aperture-threading, models rotate the camera to resolve occlusion, read in-scene rulers, output 6-DoF poses under swept-collision and statics checks, and self-correct via split-screen rollback feedback (dynamic tasks freeze time during deliberation and give five strobe frames). Across 10 frontier MLLMs on the 44 hardest instances, scores span 4.8-79.1 (seven solve at most 27.3% of 6-DoF tasks); for the top three, a reference plan (+plan) closes 44% of the gap while leaving collisions at 5.2 per episode, whereas adding exact 3D state (+state) closes 90% and cuts collisions to 0.5; for four others, +plan lifts the mean score from 10.9 to 35.3 and +state to 81.8; and 46% of failures are collision or observation loops. SpaceAct and SAPA advance evaluation to closed-loop physical interaction, showing that even with a valid plan, MLLMs remain bottlenecked by 3D visual grounding and action refinement, while offering a testbed for active multi-view exploration and explicit 3D grounding.\n         \n       \n     \n     \n       [247] \n       \n        arXiv:2610.38426\n       \n          (cross-list from cs.AR)\n\n        [ pdf ,  html ,  other ]\n     \n     \n       \n          Title: \n          The Code Behind the Layout: AI Agents for Analog Layout Generators\n         \n          Minxing Chu ,  Anhang Li ,  Martins Atilla ,  Ruichen Qi ,  Mehdi Saligane  \n\n\n\n          Subjects: \n           Hardware Architecture (cs.AR) ; Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)\n         \n\n         \n          Analog layout remains largely manual and hard to automate. Generator frameworks such as gLayout express layouts as programs, producing layouts from parameterized, design-rule-aware generators; however, writing such generators still takes expert effort. Large language models (LLMs) could now write generator code, and frontier models can even draw layout geometry from design intent. This raises a practical question: if a model can draw the layout itself, what does a generator framework still contribute, and is applying these models to a generator framework better than having them draw a layout from scratch? Through controlled experiments on gLayout in SKY130, we find that the main obstacle to drawing a layout directly is its time and token cost, and that the main advantage of a model building on a generator framework is reusability. Its verified primitives keep each model edit small enough to check, and its parameters allow a single edit to be evaluated across an entire family of layouts. Accordingly, a model is most effective when it develops the layout from the generator source of the family that the design intent belongs to, and tests each edit on the family members most likely to fail, so that one verified change improves every layout the generator produces. The results of these family tests can then serve as supervision for a local model, which learns edits that hold across the family at a fraction of the inference cost.\n         \n       \n     \n     \n       [248] \n       \n        arXiv:2610.38431\n       \n          (cross-list from cs.CR)\n\n        [ pdf ,  html ,  other ]\n     \n     \n       \n          Title: \n          DecisionUnlearn-Bench: Unlearning Probabilistic Decisions under Changing Schemas\n         \n          Tiantong Wu ,  Wei Yang Bryan Lim  \n\n\n\n          Subjects: \n           Cryptography and Security (cs.CR) ; Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)\n         \n\n         \n          Machine unlearning aims to reproduce the behavior of a model trained without designated data. For probabilistic decision models, this target also depends on the candidate options supplied at inference. We introduce DecisionUnlearn-Bench, an empirical framework that compares full, unlearned, and retain-only models across deletion granularities, label renderings, and candidate sets. Across 20 eligible model configurations on the BANKING77 dataset, full-to-oracle displacement increases consistently from example to association to class deletion. Replacing semantic option labels with opaque codes nearly eliminates the retrained oracle's recognition of deleted classes, even with successful acquisition of the full model, whereas changing the candidate set alters both the oracle and the measured unlearning progress. SimNPO satisfies the screening criterion in 19 of 20 configurations, but its gains vary substantially across seeds and deletion targets. Development tuning on the CLINC150 dataset does not establish a positive pooled effect across 48 intents. At approximately matched suppression, methods can differ markedly in oracle fidelity. Recovery experiments further distinguish initial forgetting from subsequent adaptation and show conclusions that depend on both architecture and metric. These results show that evaluating decision-model unlearning relative to a schema-specific retraining target, with separate reporting of effect size, utility, robustness, and recoverability.\n         \n       \n     \n     \n       [249] \n       \n        arXiv:2610.38438\n       \n          (cross-list from cs.CL)\n\n        [ pdf ,  html ,  other ]\n     \n     \n       \n          Title: \n          RoleFUZZER: User-Behaviour Fuzzing for Role-Specification Adherence in Large Language Models\n         \n          Kshitish Ghate ,  Michael Saxon ,  Tadayoshi Kohno ,  Aylin Caliskan  \n\n          Comments: \n          Preprint under review\n         \n\n\n          Subjects: \n           Computation and Language (cs.CL) ; Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI); Software Engineering (cs.SE)\n         \n\n         \n          Large language models (LLMs) are often conditioned into specific roles such as tutors, interviewers, or agents acting for an organisation, through system prompts that specify how they should behave. The extent to which they can actually adhere to these role specifications under multi-turn interactions with realistic and potentially problematic users is understudied. To address this, we propose user behaviour fuzzing, a guided search over plausible user behaviours that can lead a model to violate its role specification, and introduce RoleFUZZER, a framework that adapts coverage-guided fuzzing from software testing to operationalise the search. RoleFUZZER constructs scenarios guided by a user-behaviour coverage grid that crosses a grounded taxonomy of user interaction types and the role specification of the model under test i.e. what is under pressure. With RoleFUZZER, we evaluate LLMs across five role specifications, including tutor, financial advisor, AI interviewer, antagonistic roleplay character, and web agent under organisational policies. Across simulated and validated conversations in 15 campaigns, the framework reaches >= 89% coverage of the 64-cell behaviour grid for every model and role tested, and surfaces specification-grounded failure patterns. At an equal budget of conversations, removing the RoleFUZZER grid and asking an LLM to generate specification targeting users free-form lowers coverage to 39-69% with fewer kinds of violations. The same specification can also fail in opposite directions. For e.g., on the antagonist, open-weight target models override the player's safety signals, while Claude Haiku 4.5 breaks character instead. RoleFUZZER offers a tractable way to uncover vulnerabilities in LLMs across role specifications by testing diverse user behaviours that are likely to violate them.\n         \n       \n     \n     \n       [250] \n       \n        arXiv:2610.38448\n       \n          (cross-list from cs.CL)\n\n        [ pdf ,  other ]\n     \n     \n       \n          Title: \n          Benchmarking decision models against generative large language models on medical multiple-choice questions\n         \n          Oscar Freyer ,  Daniel Glauert ,  Moritz Hofmann ,  Max Ostermann ,  Stephen Gilbert ,  Jakob Nikolas Kather  \n\n\n\n          Subjects: \n           Computation and Language (cs.CL) ; Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)\n         \n\n         \n          Medical workflows often require selecting among predefined options. We compared 13 decision models with 15 generative large language model configurations, including different reasoning settings, using 1,273 four-option MedQA questions under text-only conditions. Accuracy ranged from 67.71% to 89.08% for decision models and from 78.32% to 96.86% for generative configurations. Among the tested configurations, Mercury Decide had the lowest mean final-attempt cost (USD 0.0069 per 1,000 questions), while Clef Omni had the shortest median request duration (0.291 seconds). Decision-model probabilities distinguished correct from incorrect answers, but error rates among answers assigned probabilities of at least 0.90 ranged from 1.71% to 12.77%. Answer agreement across three runs ranged from 92.30% to 100%, while probability repeatability varied. Decision models offered resource advantages alongside lower peak accuracy; selecting models for medical workflows requires considering probability quality and repeatability together with accuracy and the resources needed to detect and correct errors.\n         \n       \n     \n     \n       [251] \n       \n        arXiv:2610.38458\n       \n          (cross-list from cs.CE)\n\n        [ pdf ,  html ,  other ]\n     \n     \n       \n          Title: \n          Tracekit Box: Running AI Agents in Micro-VMs Where Every Way Out Is Decided and Signed\n         \n          Bravish Ghosh  (Cygnux Labs) \n\n          Comments: \n          20 pages, 13 figures, 19 tables. Code:  this https URL \n         \n\n\n          Subjects: \n           Computational Engineering, Finance, and Science (cs.CE) ; Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI); Cryptography and Security (cs.CR); Operating Systems (cs.OS)\n         \n\n         \n          AI agents now install packages, call model APIs with their operators' keys and change code in repositories that deploy to production. The network, credentials and write access they need are also how they can leak data, steal a key or change a system nobody approved. Sandboxes contain an agent but leave little evidence of what crossed their boundary; tracing records what happened but contains nothing. We present Tracekit Box, an open-source system (MIT, v0) that does both. Each job runs in its own Firecracker micro-VM with no network card. The only ways out are doors on the host, served over vsock: commands, outbound connections, secrets, model calls and the workspace patch. At every door a Tracekit signer the agent cannot reach decides what passes and signs it into one run per box. Names are resolved on the host; private and cloud-metadata addresses are always refused; secrets never enter the VM, which sees placeholders the host proxy swaps for real values only in one header for bound hosts; and changes come back as a patch applied file by file after the signer decides each file. Box fails closed, and each job ends as a .tkb bundle anyone can verify offline. All 13 end-to-end checks pass on a real KVM runner (the test that runs them takes 28.5 s, including image pull and boot); 15 fuzz targets on the trust boundary produced nine findings, all fixed; and the demo run's 102 signed records verify. Box does not yet run Firecracker's jailer, needs a co-located signer, and records processes inside the VM only where they cross a door.\n         \n       \n     \n     \n       [252] \n       \n        arXiv:2610.38508\n       \n          (cross-list from cs.RO)\n\n        [ pdf ,  other ]\n     \n     \n       \n          Title: \n          SaiVLA-JEPA: Evaluating Future Latent Prediction in JEPA-Conditioned VLA Control\n         \n          Rufeng Xiao ,  Ruihao Xiao ,  Wei Chen ,  Xinhai Sun  (VLA Research Team, Synthoid.ai) \n\n\n\n          Subjects: \n           Robotics (cs.RO) ; Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)\n         \n\n         \n          Joint-embedding predictive architectures (JEPAs) learn to predict representations rather than pixels, offering a compact way to model how visual scenes evolve. We ask whether this predictive objective improves closed-loop control when a vision-language-action (VLA) policy already uses V-JEPA features. We call our JEPA-conditioned flow policy family SaiVLA-JEPA. It combines a vision-language model with a diffusion transformer action expert and receives features from a frozen V-JEPA encoder. Two lightweight auxiliary heads predict future representations during training and are omitted at inference, leaving the action sampler unchanged. We compare policies without JEPA features, with current JEPA conditioning, and with additional future supervision on 10,030 paired LIBERO-Plus episodes. Current JEPA conditioning reaches about 82.5% success versus 71.6% without JEPA, while future supervision provides no detectable aggregate gain at the evaluated training budget. We separately evaluate SaiVLA-JEPA-TCA, which adapts latent context, and SaiVLA-JEPA-TPA, which adapts selected parameters. The context-retry gain is a system-level result under a larger attempt budget; parameter adaptation shows no consistent advantage over the frozen policy and target controls. These results motivate assessing latent-prediction objectives through paired closed-loop rollouts rather than latent loss alone.\n         \n       \n     \n     \n       [253] \n       \n        arXiv:2610.38517\n       \n          (cross-list from cs.LG)\n\n        [ pdf ,  html ,  other ]\n     \n     \n       \n          Title: \n          AtomSteer: Test-Time Constraint Steering for Atomistic Generative Models\n         \n          Pengfei Cai ,  Katharina S. C. Jäger ,  Utkarsh Utkarsh ,  Nofit Segal ,  Bowen Deng ,  Akshay Subramanian ,  Rafael Gomez-Bombarelli  \n\n\n\n          Subjects: \n           Machine Learning (cs.LG) ; Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)\n         \n\n         \n          Diffusion and flow matching models offer scalable approaches to generating crystalline materials, molecular conformers, and protein-ligand complexes. However, satisfying crystallographic symmetry, structural geometry, and accurate stereochemistry remains challenging. Satisfying these requirements calls for corrections that preserve the geometry already captured by the pretrained model. In this work, we propose AtomSteer, a test-time inference framework for steering pretrained atomistic generative models with structural constraints. During sampling, AtomSteer corrects intermediate structure predictions with bounded local projections, graph-rigid stereochemical transformations, and projections over periodic symmetries, accepting updates that improve constraint satisfaction while limiting changes to the rest of the structure. We demonstrate AtomSteer across three pretrained backbones, FlowMM, ET-Flow, and Boltz-2, spanning de novo crystal generation and crystal structure prediction, molecular conformer generation, and protein-ligand complex structure prediction. AtomSteer improves space-group distribution coverage in de novo crystal generation and improves target space-group recovery in crystal structure prediction. AtomSteer improves molecular validity and E/Z stereochemical accuracy while retaining conformer ensemble fidelity. For protein-ligand complexes, AtomSteer reduces ligand E/Z errors relative to experimental reference structures while achieving competitive docking success and PoseBusters validity. Our method provides a unified framework for adding chemical and crystallographic control to existing atomistic generative models without retraining or architectural changes.\n         \n       \n     \n     \n       [254] \n       \n        arXiv:2610.38561\n       \n          (cross-list from cs.LG)\n\n        [ pdf ,  html ,  other ]\n     \n     \n       \n          Title: \n          Persona Learning from Feedback for Personalized AI Assistants\n         \n          Yiran Shen ,  Yu Xia ,  Liuyi Yao ,  Prithviraj Ammanabrolu  \n\n\n\n          Subjects: \n           Machine Learning (cs.LG) ; Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI); Computation and Language (cs.CL)\n         \n\n         \n          Personalizing AI assistants requires adapting their behavior to users' preferences and task contexts. Existing work elicits assistant personas through persona prompting or finetuning on persona-conditioned generations. However, persona descriptions alone do not identify what to change or preserve in a particular response, or which behaviors suit the current request. We introduce $\\textit{Persona Learning from Feedback (PLF)}$, a framework that learns reusable, preference-grounded assistant personas as independent LoRA adapters on a shared frozen backbone. During training, a frozen self-teacher conditions on persona specifications and response-specific feedback to provide token-level supervision on each adapter's own generated prefixes. A feedback gate focuses corrective updates on responses judged to need improvement. At deployment, a supervised router uses the request or dialogue to select a single adapter or a fixed composition, without user-specific finetuning. We evaluate PLF on single-turn personalization with model-generated critiques and multi-turn tutoring with simulated students' reactions. Across both settings, PLF improves model-judged preference fit over fixed-persona policies and strong baselines including persona prompting and persona distillation, demonstrating how feedback-trained behaviors can be reused for adaptive assistant personalization without degrading general capabilities.\n         \n       \n     \n     \n       [255] \n       \n        arXiv:2610.38570\n       \n          (cross-list from cs.CL)\n\n        [ pdf ,  html ,  other ]\n     \n     \n       \n          Title: \n          Do Audio LLMs HEAR Pain? Tracing Acoustic Evidence from Speech to Language Reasoning\n         \n          Wenqi Li ,  Xiangjue Dong ,  Sulaiman Vesal  \n\n          Comments: \n          26 pages, 9 figures\n         \n\n\n          Subjects: \n           Computation and Language (cs.CL) ; Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI); Sound (cs.SD)\n         \n\n         \n          Large audio language models (LALMs) can process speech directly, but it remains unclear how well they use the non-lexical acoustic information that speech carries. We study this question through pain, using TAME-Pain, which contains pain-neutral Harvard sentences and spoken pain ratings recorded at different self-reported pain levels. We introduce HEAR, a four-stage diagnostic framework that traces pain-related information from the speech signal to the model's prediction, and apply it to seven closed-source and open-weight LALMs. On the Harvard sentences, where pain can only be inferred from the voice, all models perform at chance level, and same-speaker references or few-shot examples improve balanced accuracy by no more than six points. On the spoken ratings, the same models reach 87.5-99.7% balanced accuracy. Layer-wise probing of open-weight LALMs shows that pain can be decoded from early audio representations (74.1% balanced accuracy for binary prediction and 45.0% for three-class severity), but that this information, along with most pain-associated acoustic cues, decreases in deeper layers. In an acoustic perception test, weaker models cannot distinguish even large changes in these cues, whereas stronger models can but still fail to infer pain. Finally, we recover pain-related acoustic cues and directly reinject them as structured text into the prompt, which improves pairwise pain prediction by up to 32 points. These results indicate that current LALMs encode paralinguistic information in early layers that is largely unavailable when the model produces its answer.\n         \n       \n     \n     \n       [256] \n       \n        arXiv:2610.38577\n       \n          (cross-list from cs.LG)\n\n        [ pdf ,  html ,  other ]\n     \n     \n       \n          Title: \n          Episodic looking - using reinforcement learning to bias perception enhances few-shot and continual learning with protection from forgetting\n         \n          David Rawlinson ,  Gideon Kowadlo  \n\n          Comments: \n          21 pages, 8 figures\n         \n\n\n          Subjects: \n           Machine Learning (cs.LG) ; Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)\n         \n\n         \n          There is a fundamental contradiction between the desire for models that can generalize, and models that can continuously adapt to recent input with changing statistics. These conditions, captured in few-shot and continual learning settings, can bias or disrupt learned representations. This harms generalization to a broader context, leaving the model unable to overcome input variance.\n This paper proposes a solution based on the Complementary Learning System architecture (CLS), in which a Short-Term Memory (STM) delegates generalization to a slow, statistical Long-Term Memory (LTM). Delegation is achieved by working in a space of robust, generalized perceptions produced by the LTM. With this separation, the STM can focus on memorizing recent input and avoiding interference between memories.\n We show that on an image classification task, the resulting CLS model is more resistant to forgetting in a domain-incremental continual learning setting than a baseline ResNet LTM, and generalizes more effectively in a few-shot setting than other continual learners.\n In the continual setting, overall accuracy is slightly worse than existing continual learning models, but with a different mix of performance characteristics. Results analysis suggests that the model is limited by the representations and representational plasticity of the LTM. In future work, we will seek to address this by replaying STM memories to the LTM.\n         \n       \n     \n     \n       [257] \n       \n        arXiv:2610.38608\n       \n          (cross-list from cs.HC)\n\n        [ pdf ,  other ]\n     \n     \n       \n          Title: \n          The Artificial Personality Model: Theory, Implementation, Measurement and Experimental Validation in Conversational Agents\n         \n          Alexander Dregger ,  Barbarella Petz ,  Erik Kuhn ,  Pia Volk ,  Andreas Oberweis ,  Stefan Hillmann ,  Jannik Strötgen  \n\n          Comments: \n          56 pages, 16 figures, 19 tables\n         \n\n\n          Subjects: \n           Human-Computer Interaction (cs.HC) ; Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)\n         \n\n         \n          Artificial personality shapes user experience (UX) in human-machine interaction, yet coherent theoretical frameworks and validated measurement instruments remain limited. This paper refines and empirically evaluates the Artificial Personality Model (APM), a hybrid framework integrating human-centred and machine-specific personality dimensions. Across three experiments (N = 395), the model was operationalised and evaluated using the Artificial Personality Questionnaire (APQ). Factor analyses revealed that some human personality dimensions (e.g., agreeableness, conscientiousness) are partially transferable, whereas others (e.g., extraversion) did not emerge as distinct dimensions. Additional machine-specific dimensions were identified. The APQ provided encouraging initial psychometric evidence, while the human-centred BFI-2 showed poor reliability and an uninterpretable factor structure in Experiment 3. Experimental manipulations demonstrated significant effects of artificial personality on user satisfaction, continuous use intention, and social presence. The findings contribute an empirically refined framework and a novel instrument for measuring perceived artificial personality, offering practical guidance for designing conversational agents.\n         \n       \n     \n     \n       [258] \n       \n        arXiv:2610.38617\n       \n          (cross-list from cs.CR)\n\n        [ pdf ,  html ,  other ]\n     \n     \n       \n          Title: \n          How Deterministic SAST and AI Security Agents Divide the Work: Coverage, Harness Policy and Cost in Snyk VulnBench 2.0\n         \n          Liran Tal ,  Chris Liu ,  Charlotte Fereday ,  Sebastian Roth ,  Brian Clark  \n\n          Comments: \n          44 pages, 21 figures, 7 tables. Benchmark harness, reference sets and all run data:  this https URL  ; companion site:  this https URL \n         \n\n\n          Subjects: \n           Cryptography and Security (cs.CR) ; Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI); Software Engineering (cs.SE)\n         \n\n         \n          We ask what AI coding agents and AI security harnesses find that deterministic static application security testing (SAST) does not, and what they miss that SAST catches. VulnBench 2.0 is a benchmark of 108 hand-curated, attacker-reachable vulnerabilities in 20 open-source web applications in Go, JavaScript/TypeScript, Python and Java; 62 flow across more than one file. A finding is credited only if it locates the vulnerability's attacker-controlled source and its dangerous sink, each within two lines. We evaluate nine productized configurations under one blind protocol: the Snyk Code static analyzer, Claude Code's /security-review with three model and effort settings (one with the Snyk MCP server), Codex Security with GPT-5.6 Luna, Terra and Sol, and the DeepSec scanner with two model backends. Snyk Code reached 82.4% macro recall (89 of 108) and Claude Code with Opus 5 at xhigh effort 79.3% (85 of 108); they disagree on 28 vulnerabilities, split 16 to 12, too few to tell them apart. Neither engine was sufficient alone: together they reached 101 of 108, the ceiling of all seven comparable configurations combined, and the AI configurations also reported at least seven real vulnerabilities that the reference set did not contain and Snyk Code did not report. What an AI engine misses depends on its harness as much as on its model: without Claude Code's built-in review policy, the same model matched 16 instead of 9 of the 20 vulnerabilities in the classes that policy excludes or restricts (supplementary run on 19 of 20 applications); Codex Security with GPT-5.6 Sol located 80 vulnerabilities at file level but 34 at line level; DeepSec's model-free file selection kept a file from only 56 of the 108 flows. Coverage and cost were only loosely related. The harness, fixtures, reference sets and all run data are public.\n         \n       \n     \n     \n       [259] \n       \n        arXiv:2610.38637\n       \n          (cross-list from cs.LG)\n\n        [ pdf ,  html ,  other ]\n     \n     \n       \n          Title: \n          On-Policy Self-Distillation with Teacher Evolution\n         \n          Shufan Shen ,  Zhongni Hou ,  Chao Bi ,  Yufei Zhang ,  Guojun Yin ,  Shuhui Wang  \n\n\n\n          Subjects: \n           Machine Learning (cs.LG) ; Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)\n         \n\n         \n          On-Policy Self-Distillation (OPSD), which aligns a student model's token-level distribution with that of its self-teacher prompted by privileged context, has emerged as a promising direction to mitigate reward sparsity in sequence-level post-training paradigms. However, OPSD often fails to achieve continuous performance gains during training. In this paper, we find that the model's limited self-teaching capability underlies this phenomenon by generating unreliable guidance on certain samples. To address this problem, we propose Teacher-Evolution Self-Distillation (TESD), which enables the self-teacher to generate rollouts and receive environmental feedback based on task correctness and context independence. First, using these rewards as training objectives, we perform teacher-oriented optimization to continuously enhance the model's self-teaching capability during distillation. Second, by estimating teacher guidance reliability from these rewards, we apply adaptive student-oriented optimization to down-weight samples with unreliable guidance. Extensive experiments across multiple datasets and model scales show that TESD consistently outperforms representative token-level and sequence-level methods, highlighting the necessity of improving supervision quality in OPSD.\n         \n       \n     \n     \n       [260] \n       \n        arXiv:2610.38639\n       \n          (cross-list from cs.CR)\n\n        [ pdf ,  html ,  other ]\n     \n     \n       \n          Title: \n          Enhanced image forensics through partial C2PA-based provenance information\n         \n          Riccardo Ziglio ,  Eleonora Marchesini ,  Andrea Montibeller ,  Giulia Boato ,  Silvio Ranise ,  Cecilia Pasquini  \n\n          Comments: \n          paper accepted to IEEE International Workshop on Information Forensics and Security (WIFS) 2026\n         \n\n\n          Subjects: \n           Cryptography and Security (cs.CR) ; Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI); Multimedia (cs.MM); Image and Video Processing (eess.IV)\n         \n\n         \n          Cryptographically verifiable C2PA-based Content Credentials (CCs) can be attached to freshly acquired or generated digital images but also to already existing ones with no previous provenance information, for instance after processing through trusted C2PA-enabled software tools. The latter case leads to images with so-called partial CCs, which capture only the last operations applied, while prior provenance remains unknown and can be investigated through signal forensics. This paper addresses the forensic analysis of such images, highlighting the opportunity to leverage the partial CCs as additional trusted knowledge to adapt forensic detectors towards the statistical characteristics of the image under analysis and mitigate potential distribution shifts. To this end, a two-step strategy is envisioned: (i) reconstructing the operations applied by the last signer through a structural analysis of the partial CCs; (ii) exploiting the obtained reconstruction to enhance the forensic detector of the analyst through an adaptation process. An empirical analysis on partial CCs produced by commercial C2PA-enabled software tools through different editing operations is carried out, providing evidence on the feasibility of the reconstruction in practical cases. An experimental case study on the forensic task of synthetic image detection is then presented, showing that the proposed strategy can significantly enhance the performance of state-of-the-art detectors on test images that are postprocessed via C2PA-enabled software tools after being generated/acquired.\n         \n       \n     \n     \n       [261] \n       \n        arXiv:2610.38647\n       \n          (cross-list from cs.HC)\n\n        [ pdf ,  other ]\n     \n     \n       \n          Title: \n          When AI performs, what remains? An integrative review of generative AI and cognitive capital in individuals and organizations\n         \n          Giuseppe Riva ,  Stefania La Rocca ,  Brenda K. Wiederhold ,  Andrea Gaggioli  \n\n          Comments: \n          16 pages, 1 figure, 4 table\n         \n\n\n          Subjects: \n           Human-Computer Interaction (cs.HC) ; Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)\n         \n\n         \n          Generative artificial intelligence is rapidly entering knowledge work, and its short-term productivity benefits are increasingly documented. Less is known about what happens, over time, to the knowledge, skills and judgment that remain in people and organizations. This integrative narrative review addresses that question through the construct of cognitive capital: the accumulated, renewable and depletable stock of knowledge, cognitive and metacognitive capabilities, and practices that enables an individual or an organization to learn, interpret information, exercise judgment, solve problems and adapt. We distinguish individual and organizational cognitive capital from the capability of the human-AI system, so that gains in joint performance and losses in human capability can be observed at the same time. Drawing on experimental, field and conceptual work, we propose four mechanisms through which interaction with AI affects cognitive capital: substitution of practice, scaffolding and feedback, metacognitive calibration, and organizational aggregation. Seven principles summarize the current evidence, including that assisted performance differs from retained competence, that the same technology can support or displace learning depending on design and use, and that individual gains can coexist with reduced collective diversity. For each mechanism we derive testable propositions, and we discuss five design levers for organizations as proposals to be validated rather than established practice. We conclude that cognitive capital offers a multilevel frame for governing AI adoption and outline a research agenda centered on longitudinal, multilevel measurement.\n         \n       \n     \n     \n       [262] \n       \n        arXiv:2610.38651\n       \n          (cross-list from cond-mat.stat-mech)\n\n        [ pdf ,  html ,  other ]\n     \n     \n       \n          Title: \n          Information-Theoretic Reliability--Safety Bounds and Finite-Alignment Dynamics in AI Knowledge Distillation\n         \n          Miao-Miao Yi ,  Zhen-Yu Cai ,  Y -M. Du ,  C. P. Sun  \n\n\n\n          Subjects: \n           Statistical Mechanics (cond-mat.stat-mech) ; Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)\n         \n\n         \n          The reliability and safety of artificial intelligence (AI), together with their transfer and evolution through knowledge distillation, have attracted growing attention. We establish an information-theoretic bound on the reliability--safety trade-off and characterize the dynamics of this trade-off under knowledge distillation. We first prove a universal trade-off inequality that is independent of the specific model form. Its boundary is determined solely by a single parameter Kh, which quantifies the hidden layer's ability to distinguish benign from hazardous inputs. We then develop a canonical Boltzmann-machine model of AI response behavior and derive two exact relations between reliability and safety. The trade-off curve is determined by the hazard discrimination capacity K, while the overall answer tendency controls the operating point along the curve. These relations agree well with existing experimental data. In knowledge distillation, we find that teacher responses induce a renormalization of the student model's trade-off parameters. We further use a renormalization-group approach to study multigenerational distillation, deriving exact recursion relations and the associated renormalization-group-like flows for two representative direct intergenerational distillation protocols at arbitrary teacher--student alignment strength. These flows reveal fixed-point structures, long-term dynamics, and critical behavior, yielding testable predictions for the critical transition between capability loss and stable transmission.\n         \n       \n     \n     \n       [263] \n       \n        arXiv:2610.38657\n       \n          (cross-list from cs.RO)\n\n        [ pdf ,  html ,  other ]\n     \n     \n       \n          Title: \n          Explore Where It Matters: Targeted Exploration for Efficient Rover Adaptation\n         \n          Jacob Levy ,  Arthur Bouton ,  Tristan D. Hassler ,  Hari Nayar ,  David Fridovich-Keil  \n\n          Comments: \n          Conference on Robot Learning 2026\n         \n\n\n          Subjects: \n           Robotics (cs.RO) ; Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI); Machine Learning (cs.LG)\n         \n\n         \n          Future planetary rovers will traverse steep, deformable terrain where slip, sinkage, and terrain deformation can threaten mobility. Although simulation can capture some wheel-soil effects, unmodeled deformable-terrain phenomena create sim-to-real gaps requiring deployment-time adaptation. Yet, rover operations leave little room for extensive trial-and-error. We introduce TERRA (Targeted Exploration for Reward-Relevant Adaptation), an online adaptation framework that efficiently seeks deployment data exposing task-relevant model errors and corrects them. TERRA adapts a simulation-trained world model with a reward-weighted prediction loss and trains a loss predictor to estimate where future performance-relevant errors are likely to occur, using this signal to guide exploration during MPC. In simulation, TERRA reaches approximately 90% slope-climbing success within five trials under severe out-of-distribution pretraining conditions, outperforming exploration baselines in reward, completion time, and energy to first success. On the ERNEST rover, TERRA recovers coordinated active-suspension locomotion on a deformable Mars-simulant slope within three to five real-world trials. Project website:  this https URL \n         \n       \n     \n     \n       [264] \n       \n        arXiv:2610.38694\n       \n          (cross-list from cs.CV)\n\n        [ pdf ,  html ,  other ]\n     \n     \n       \n          Title: \n          Geometry Matters in Packed 3D Attention\n         \n          Amil Khan ,  Anantajit Subrahmanya ,  B.S. Manjunath  \n\n          Comments: \n          Accepted to NeurIPS 2026 (Poster)\n         \n\n\n          Subjects: \n           Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV) ; Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI); Machine Learning (cs.LG)\n         \n\n         \n          Packing a 3D volume into a token sequence is not merely long-sequence modeling. Once the volume is packed, sequence order no longer encodes the geometry of the sample---so a positional mechanism indexed by sequence position can learn the memory layout rather than the object. This is a general failure mode for attention over packed geometric data. We introduce PACK3D, a local-global attention operator that separates memory layout from attention geometry. PACK3D keeps local neighborhood attention exact, compresses only distant context into contiguous 3D block summaries, and indexes every query-key interaction with axial RoPE on true token-grid coordinates rather than packed positions. Because the rotation is applied before a block is summarized, the query-to-summary logit remains a weighted sum of query-relative 3D displacements; compressed global context therefore stays spatially interpretable after packing, instead of collapsing to anonymous pooled content. We measure the failure mode directly. On a controlled synthetic task---train on one packing, change only the memory order of the tokens, hold the physical labeling rule fixed---learned positional embeddings and packed-sequence RoPE solve the training layout perfectly yet collapse under Morton, block-contiguous, and random repacking, while axial grid-coordinate RoPE stays exact throughout. The same coordinate system carries to real data: on Allen WTC-11 hiPSC microscopy, PACK3D improves over a parameter-matched 3D U-Net on the 15-structure joint task and over a matched learned-position local-global baseline, with controls isolating the coordinate system, not encoder capacity, as the source of the gain. A fused Triton implementation matches windowed-attention latency at 65k tokens while supplying the long-range context windowing cannot---so the operator is not only correct but trainable at scale.\n         \n       \n     \n     \n       [265] \n       \n        arXiv:2610.38697\n       \n          (cross-list from cs.LG)\n\n        [ pdf ,  html ,  other ]\n     \n     \n       \n          Title: \n          SatOps-Decision: Low-Latency Discriminative Decisions for Onboard Satellite Communication Operations\n         \n          Lifan Mei ,  Yuxuan Li ,  Jiaxian Tu ,  Shenghe Xu  \n\n\n\n          Subjects: \n           Machine Learning (cs.LG) ; Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI); Networking and Internet Architecture (cs.NI)\n         \n\n         \n          We propose SatOps-Decision, a method for low-latency decisions in satellite communication operations. To the best of our knowledge, it is the first application of Jev-style general-purpose discriminative decision models to satellite operations, and the accompanying benchmark is the first dedicated to this approach. The learned scorer, a locally adapted Laya encoder with its native decision head, selects among supplied operational answers in a single forward pass. The benchmark combines authored operational contracts, controlled state changes and independently checked labels in an auditable construction. Evaluation prioritizes complete-request latency and raw decision accuracy; all main accuracy comparisons use the same 1,024 held-out questions, separate from training and development. SatOps-Decision's local scorer has a median latency of 90.3 ms per decision, versus 493.9 ms for Qwen3.5-0.8B generation and 388.4 ms for OneJev1.6-0.8B: $5.47\\times$ and $4.30\\times$ speedups based on median request times in the measured FP32/MPS implementations. Its mean raw accuracy is 85.3%, versus 42.5% for frozen Laya, 40.7% for Qwen generation and 58.6% for OneJev. As cloud accuracy references, original Jev 1.14/1.13 reach 87.4/81.9%.\n         \n       \n     \n     \n       [266] \n       \n        arXiv:2610.38709\n       \n          (cross-list from cs.LG)\n\n        [ pdf ,  html ,  other ]\n     \n     \n       \n          Title: \n          Latent Decision Flow Rewards for Clinical Guideline Reasoning\n         \n          Gaurav Bhatt ,  Marinka Zitnik  \n\n\n\n          Subjects: \n           Machine Learning (cs.LG) ; Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)\n         \n\n         \n          Clinical practice guidelines encode evidence-based recommendations as conditional decision flows, which makes them a natural source of process supervision for training reasoning models. Two properties of clinical reasoning make this supervision difficult to use. A decision flow consistent with a guideline does not by itself determine the correct answer for a given patient, which we call the path--answer gap, and several valid decision flows can support the same clinical decision, which we call path multiplicity. Current training approaches address parts of this problem. Answer rewards supervise the final decision, and process rewards computed against a reference flow supervise one valid decision flow per case. Supervised fine-tuning on annotated flows teaches guideline-consistent reasoning but constrains the model to one trajectory per case and can reduce answer accuracy. We introduce GUIDER, a method that jointly optimizes a decision flow controller and a reasoning policy. The controller learns to generate plausible decision flows from guideline-derived supervision, and the policy learns to generate a decision flow and a final answer for each patient case. GUIDER computes the process reward for the policy over several controller-generated flows, which accounts for path multiplicity, and gates this reward on answer correctness, which addresses the path--answer gap. Joint training allows the process supervision to evolve with the policy. On MedGUIDE, GUIDER with Qwen3-14B achieves 82.5 weighted accuracy and 73.5 guideline adherence, 3.3 and 3.1 points above the strongest clinical reinforcement learning baseline. With Qwen3-14B and Gemma4-12B backbones, GUIDER also improves over CRPO, MRPO, and FaithMed on the out-of-domain benchmarks AMEGA-LLM, RealMedQA, and CancerGUIDE.\n         \n       \n     \n     \n       [267] \n       \n        arXiv:2610.38719\n       \n          (cross-list from cs.CL)\n\n        [ pdf ,  html ,  other ]\n     \n     \n       \n          Title: \n          Learning Scientific Trajectories to Predict Future Biomedical Discoveries\n         \n          Chia-Hsuan Chang ,  Brian Ondov ,  Jiang Lv ,  Lan Jiang ,  Yunmai Wang ,  Haerin Song ,  Xueqing Peng ,  Qiaozhu Mei ,  Xiaolei Su ,  Hua Xu  \n\n\n\n          Subjects: \n           Computation and Language (cs.CL) ; Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)\n         \n\n         \n          AI co-scientists have expanded the use of large language models for generating scientific hypotheses and guiding experimental investigation. Here we investigate whether temporal patterns in the semantic representations of complete biomedical conclusions can guide hypothesis generation. We introduce ForeSCITE, a framework that identifies thematic clusters of published conclusions, estimates a direction separating earlier from more recent findings within each cluster, and projects existing conclusion embeddings along these directions. An embedding language model trained to reconstruct conclusions decodes the projected coordinates into natural-language hypotheses. Across 407 clusters derived from approximately three million pre-cutoff conclusions, we identify temporal structure and illustrate its retention after decoding. In retrospective, time-sliced evaluations, ForeSCITE increases semantic coverage beyond the historical corpus, biomedical entity-pair novelty and post-cutoff pair re-observation relative to five prompt-based and retrieval-augmented baselines. Expert review of prioritized CAR-T hypotheses identified 37 candidates as both novel and biologically plausible, and expert justifications for five selected hypotheses illustrate their biological rationale and subsequent literature support where available. These findings support temporal guidance in semantic space as a scalable mechanism for proposing biomedical hypotheses for subsequent scientific evaluation.\n         \n       \n     \n     \n       [268] \n       \n        arXiv:2610.38720\n       \n          (cross-list from cs.CV)\n\n        [ pdf ,  other ]\n     \n     \n       \n          Title: \n          inkGround: Benchmarking Fine-grained Visual Grounding Capabilities of Vision-Language Models for Cursive Handwriting in the Wild\n         \n          Sidra Hanif  \n\n\n\n          Subjects: \n           Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV) ; Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)\n         \n\n         \n          Human handwriting is produced as an ordered sequence of pen movements, but an offline image keeps only the final ink. Handwriting trajectory recovery aims to restore the ordered stroke points and their pen-up/pen-down transitions. Existing image-trajectory pair datasets are recorded with digitizing tablets under controlled conditions, which limits writer coverage and stylistic diversity. Moreover, there is no benchmark that measures the visual grounding capabilities of general-purpose Vision-Language Models (VLMs) for the stroke trajectory recovery task. In this work, we introduce inkGround, a benchmark for stroke trajectory recovery from full-page, camera-captured handwriting. inkGround pairs 537 page images (~18.7k words, ~163k strokes) with reference trajectories produced by a pretrained offline-to-online model, filtered by OCR agreement, and verified by human raters. Because these references are pseudo-labels that represent one plausible pen trajectory, we also evaluate VLMs on the IAM-OnDB dataset, which provides recorded pen trajectories. We cast the task as text-conditioned point grounding: given a page image and a target word, the model must locate the word and output its ordered stroke points. Outputs are scored by trajectory fidelity (DTW, Chamfer distance), readability of the rendered strokes (Accurate Rate, Correct Rate), and style similarity. We benchmark four open-source and three frontier closed-source VLMs with a shared in-context prompt. Closed-source models, notably Gemini-3.1-Pro, produce legible trajectories close to the reference, and this ranking also holds on IAM-OnDB.\n         \n       \n     \n     \n       [269] \n       \n        arXiv:2610.38744\n       \n          (cross-list from cs.RO)\n\n        [ pdf ,  html ,  other ]\n     \n     \n       \n          Title: \n          AtomTree: A Hierarchical Framework for State-Aware Embodied Instruction Following with LLMs\n         \n          Haotian Zhai ,  Tianming Sha ,  Junnan Li  \n\n          Comments: \n          Accepted at IROS 2026\n         \n\n\n          Subjects: \n           Robotics (cs.RO) ; Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI); Computation and Language (cs.CL)\n         \n\n         \n          Embodied instruction following in interactive environments requires agents to interpret natural language commands and complete long-horizon tasks. Existing planners either rely on large labeled trajectories, or use few-shot and zero-shot LLM prompting that produces a whole plan in a single serial reasoning process. These approaches depend on linguistic common sense, commit to a plan from the initial instruction, and only update after failures, which weakens robustness and state awareness. We present AtomTree, a hierarchical framework for state-aware task planning with LLMs. AtomTree incrementally builds a tree of subgoals and actions, where the LLM decides at each step, from the current symbolic state, whether to further decompose or to output a grounded low-level action. A lightweight memory module summarizes past outcomes for reuse in later decisions. When a low-level action stalls, AtomTree triggers hierarchical backtracking replanning to return to the parent node and regenerate the subtree plan from the latest state, preventing continued drift along an incorrect trajectory. In zero-shot evaluation on the ALFRED benchmark, AtomTree outperforms prior LLM-based planners. Project page:  this https URL .\n         \n       \n     \n     \n       [270] \n       \n        arXiv:2610.38822\n       \n          (cross-list from cs.CL)\n\n        [ pdf ,  other ]\n     \n     \n       \n          Title: \n          CopyLoop: Fast Looped Transformer Decoding via Context Copying and Shallow Screening\n         \n          Wenyu Du ,  Stephen Chung  \n\n          Comments: \n          preprint\n         \n\n\n          Subjects: \n           Computation and Language (cs.CL) ; Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)\n         \n\n         \n          Looped Transformers increase computational capacity through the repeated application of shared blocks, but their recurrent depth makes conventional full-depth decoding expensive. While existing speculative pipelines accelerate generation by processing consecutive token positions at varying depths, they are typically limited by sequential decoding overheads. We introduce CopyLoop, a training-free decoding algorithm that combines low-cost context-copy proposals with shallow screening and continuous recurrent scheduling. Rather than step-by-step generation, CopyLoop proposes multi-token suffixes from repeated context. It then evaluates them early at a shallow recurrent depth to efficiently filter out incorrect branches, processing newer blocks concurrently while earlier blocks are still progressing toward full-depth verification. We evaluate CopyLoop across six model checkpoints from the Ouro, Raven, and Huginn families, covering code generation and multi-step mathematical reasoning. CopyLoop achieves decoding speedups of 2.98x--10.70x over full-depth autoregressive decoding on Base models and 2.74x--3.52x on Thinking models, consistently outperforming or closely matching all competing baselines across evaluation tasks. Furthermore, ablation studies confirm a strong synergy between shallow screening and continuous scheduling, while our analyses show that CopyLoop requires only lightweight model calibration and yields greater speedups on longer generations with reusable contexts.\n         \n       \n     \n     \n       [271] \n       \n        arXiv:2610.38825\n       \n          (cross-list from cs.HC)\n\n        [ pdf ,  html ,  other ]\n     \n     \n       \n          Title: \n          Evaluating Visual Realism of AI-Generated Videos with Eye Tracking\n         \n          Danqing Shi ,  Lan Jiang ,  Jake Lyons ,  Miri Zilka  \n\n\n\n          Subjects: \n           Human-Computer Interaction (cs.HC) ; Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI); Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)\n         \n\n         \n          Human evaluation of AI-generated videos has so far relied on explicit human feedback, such as judgments or ratings. While these signals capture the conclusions viewers reach, they fail to offer insights into the perception that leads to them. This paper investigates eye tracking as a complementary human evaluation signal for AI-generated videos. We conducted two Visual Turing Test (VTT) experiments with a total of 90 participants on videos generated by latest generative models, in which participants watched each video twice and then gave their feedback. Across the experiments, we collected 4,286 valid eye-tracking trials on 438 videos. Our analysis indicates that gaze patterns offer informative evaluation signals that align with explicit human feedback. Building on the findings, we propose the first computational method GazeEval to predict the perceived visual realism of AI-generated videos from eye-tracking data. It achieves 71.3% accuracy in prediction, providing evidence that eye tracking data carries informative signals about how people evaluate a video. We release the study data to support further research on eye tracking for evaluating AI-generated video.\n         \n       \n     \n     \n       [272] \n       \n        arXiv:2610.38833\n       \n          (cross-list from cs.LG)\n\n        [ pdf ,  html ,  other ]\n     \n     \n       \n          Title: \n          Discovering Jailbreaks via Curriculum of Models\n         \n          Michał Burzyński ,  Noah T. Rossi ,  Shomit Basu ,  Andy Arditi  \n\n\n\n          Subjects: \n           Machine Learning (cs.LG) ; Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)\n         \n\n         \n          Automated jailbreak discovery aims to find prompts that elicit prohibited behaviors from safety-trained language models, helping developers identify vulnerabilities before deployment. One class of approaches uses reinforcement learning to train an investigator model to generate prompts that elicit a specified behavior from a target model. However, training directly against a safety-trained target is difficult because nearly all prompts are refused, leaving the investigator with a sparse reward signal. Prior work addresses this with the propensity bound (PRBO), a proxy reward estimated by sampling responses from a more compliant target, and then adjusting for how likely those responses would have been under the original target.\n We introduce Curriculum of Models (CoM), an alternative method that trains the investigator against a sequence of modified versions of the target, progressing gradually from a version that readily exhibits the specified behavior to the original target. For jailbreak discovery, training begins against a target version that rarely refuses harmful requests, and advances to the next, harder version once the investigator reliably elicits the behavior from the current one.\n We evaluate CoM on elicitation tasks involving CBRN materials, explosives, and illicit drugs, using two open-weight targets, gemma-4-31b-it and gpt-oss-120b. We compare against PRBO and direct training on the original target without a curriculum. When the investigator is trained to elicit a single behavior, CoM finds successful jailbreaks more reliably than either baseline. When trained on multiple behaviors simultaneously, the CoM-trained investigator can elicit held-out behaviors, though PRBO-trained investigators achieve higher success rates. Our results suggest that a simple curriculum over target models offers a promising approach for addressing sparse rewards in rare-behavior elicitation.\n         \n       \n     \n     \n       [273] \n       \n        arXiv:2610.38850\n       \n          (cross-list from cond-mat.mtrl-sci)\n\n        [ pdf ,  html ,  other ]\n     \n     \n       \n          Title: \n          Autonomous Quantum Measurement with Agentic AI\n         \n          Eunbi Rha ,  Abhijatmedhi Chotrattanapituk ,  Mouyang Cheng ,  Chung-tao Chou ,  Shenyong Ye ,  Ashley S. Dale ,  Long Ju ,  Luqiao Liu ,  Mingda Li  \n\n\n\n          Subjects: \n           Materials Science (cond-mat.mtrl-sci) ; Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)\n         \n\n         \n          Quantum materials exhibit diverse electronic and functional phenomena, but their characterization requires extensive experimental expertise, iterative decision-making and accumulated laboratory knowledge that is difficult to document and transfer. Here we introduce Quantum Agent (QUANTA), an agentic artificial intelligence (AI) framework for autonomous quantum-materials characterization. Using a widely adopted Physical Property Measurement System (PPMS) as an experimental platform, QUANTA combines retrieval augmentation using scientific literature, experimental protocols and prior measurements with specialized agents for experimental planning, troubleshooting, and data interpretation. A separate deterministic control layer validates instrument actions. Guided by incoming data, QUANTA evaluates measurement quality, revises experimental plans and selects subsequent measurements. We demonstrate these capabilities through transport measurements of the topological Weyl semimetal TaP, as well as investigations of nonlinear responses in magnetic material Co2MnGa. These results establish QUANTA as a generalizable framework for evidence-driven, safeguarded and closed-loop quantum-materials characterization.\n         \n       \n     \n     \n       [274] \n       \n        arXiv:2610.38895\n       \n          (cross-list from cs.CR)\n\n        [ pdf ,  html ,  other ]\n     \n     \n       \n          Title: \n          FlipJev: Fast Black-Box Red Teaming Finds Universal Adversarial Suffixes for Typed Decision Models\n         \n          Xiaogeng Liu ,  Xinyan Wang ,  Chaowei Xiao  \n\n          Comments: \n          Pre-print. Code:  this https URL , Webpage:  this https URL \n         \n\n\n          Subjects: \n           Cryptography and Security (cs.CR) ; Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)\n         \n\n         \n          Typed decision models are an emerging class of language models that make decisions instead of generating free-text responses. They answer in a single fast and cheap query and are increasingly used, including in security-sensitive tasks. However, red teaming of these models leaves two gaps: existing adaptive attacks do not use their speed, and few studies have searched for universal adversarial examples. We fill both gaps on Jev, the representative typed decision model, with a quick black-box query-based attack that calls no attacker language model. We conduct extensive experiments on 12 datasets with different data types and application scenarios. The results show that Jev resists common prompt injections: no strategy changes a correct answer in 10,240 paired tests. In contrast, our attack finds short suffixes that flip confident answers within minutes. These suffixes are universal: one suffix per dataset flips 43% of unseen documents. Because typed decision models, unlike classic NLP models, can serve many tasks, we test whether these suffixes transfer across tasks. We find that one suffix generated from one task can flip 90-100% of the samples in other tasks. We therefore believe the evaluation of typed decision models must include strict adaptive attacks to fully reveal their robustness.\n         \n       \n     \n     \n       [275] \n       \n        arXiv:2610.38911\n       \n          (cross-list from cs.CL)\n\n        [ pdf ,  html ,  other ]\n     \n     \n       \n          Title: \n          Interventional Training Reduces Causal Bypass in In-Context Introspection\n         \n          Zilu Tang ,  Jongin Kim ,  Kerem Sahin ,  David Atkinson ,  Aaron Mueller ,  Derry Wijaya  \n\n          Comments: \n          9 pages, preprint\n         \n\n\n          Subjects: \n           Computation and Language (cs.CL) ; Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)\n         \n\n         \n          Introspection, the ability of a system to access and reason about its internal signals, is crucial for language model (LM) self-consistency and scalable interpretability. Prior work argues that LMs possess introspective capabilities, and that training (SFT) could enhance such behavior. However, most setups leave open the possibility of \"causal bypass,\" in which the self-report relies on signals that are correlated with the self-report's subject, while being causally unrelated to it. Although high-level concepts are difficult to intervene on, lower-level signals (such as a single neuron activation) allow precise interventions. Understanding such signals can serve as a minimal prerequisite before understanding more complex introspection. In this study, we train hundreds of introspectors across three low-level signal types and eight LMs. We find that straightforward SFT training leads to causal bypass, and propose interventional training, an alternative where we train the model to report on counterfactually perturbed values. This method enables more accurate introspection of endogenous $\\textit{and}$ injected signals, particularly in out-of-distribution (OOD) data. The mechanism generalizes across token positions and scales with more data. Our results demonstrate that while causally grounded introspection is possible, it is not guaranteed -- emphasizing the need to train on mechanisms, rather than behaviors alone.\n         \n       \n     \n     \n       [276] \n       \n        arXiv:2610.38915\n       \n          (cross-list from cs.LG)\n\n        [ pdf ,  html ,  other ]\n     \n     \n       \n          Title: \n          Lev-3B: A Data Recipe for Compact Probabilistic Log Decision Models\n         \n          Leo Betthauser ,  Yingqi Liu ,  Uday Shah ,  Jishang Wei ,  Sainyam Galhotra ,  Liang Gou ,  Vedant Dharnidharka ,  Hao Yang  \n\n\n\n          Subjects: \n           Machine Learning (cs.LG) ; Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)\n         \n\n         \n          Many log-analysis tasks involve choosing among known options, such as detecting anomalous sessions or identifying attack techniques, and benefit from calibrated probabilities. We present Lev-3B, an open 3B-parameter decision model built on Granite-4.1-3B-Base with a shared pointer readout, and study its training recipe. The recipe has two key parts. First, training follows a curriculum: the model learns bounded natural-language decisions, using no log data, before it sees any logs. Second, the log stage mixes the target tasks with general log data spanning 35 auxiliary tasks unrelated to either evaluation task. We evaluate one shared checkpoint on sources seen in training and on sources excluded from training, calibration and model selection. The two-stage recipe transfers substantially better than training on log data alone. Adding general log data improves performance on excluded sources. At a fraction of their serving cost, Lev-3B outperforms prompted frontier models on anomaly detection, including on an excluded logging system, but trails them on attack-technique classification. Its probabilities are well calibrated on familiar sources, but on the excluded logging system it confidently misses anomalies. We release the checkpoint, recipe, data manifests, and prediction-level artifacts.\n         \n       \n     \n     \n       [277] \n       \n        arXiv:2610.38917\n       \n          (cross-list from cs.CY)\n\n        [ pdf ,  html ,  other ]\n     \n     \n       \n          Title: \n          TrussTutor-Bench: Benchmarking large language models on solution accuracy, sycophancy, and error detection in statics\n         \n          Evangelos Apostolakis ,  Erfan Hamdi ,  Sara Jahedazad ,  Emma Lejeune  \n\n          Comments: \n          25 pages, 7 figures. Code:  this https URL \n         \n\n\n          Subjects: \n           Computers and Society (cs.CY) ; Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI); Machine Learning (cs.LG)\n         \n\n         \n          Large language models (LLMs) have rapidly become a default source of help for students in science and engineering courses. In foundational engineering mechanics courses such as statics, students routinely use these tools to check answers, work through partial solutions, and ask for explanations. Effective tutoring in this setting requires more than a correct final answer. A model must also confirm correct work when a student doubts it, and identify errors in flawed work. Existing benchmarks evaluate LLMs primarily on final-answer accuracy, so it is unclear whether models that can solve mechanics problems also have the other technical capabilities that tutoring depends on. Here we introduce TrussTutor-Bench, a benchmark of 200 verified two-dimensional truss problems paired with automatically generated student solutions, and use it to evaluate thirteen LLMs on solution accuracy, sycophancy, and error detection. Baseline accuracy ranged from 64.5% to 97.0%. Every model detected nearly all injected errors in synthetic flawed student work (90.4% to 100%), so error detection did not distinguish models under these clean conditions. Sycophancy did. When a student insisted that correct work was wrong, models rejected correct solutions to problems they had themselves solved in 1.6% to 50.0% of cases. These results show that performance varies widely across models: the leading models solve, check, and defend student work nearly perfectly, while others are far less reliable, particularly when a student doubts correct work. TrussTutor-Bench provides a verified, extensible standard for determining which models are technically reliable enough to put in front of students. Follow-up work must now focus on teaching rather than technical capability, drawing on learning science to determine whether LLM help leads students to understand the material or simply to reach the right answer.\n         \n       \n     \n     \n       [278] \n       \n        arXiv:2610.38946\n       \n          (cross-list from cs.MA)\n\n        [ pdf ,  html ,  other ]\n     \n     \n       \n          Title: \n          MAS-Loop: Beyond One-Shot Orchestration towards Self-Evolving Multi-Agent Systems\n         \n          Hehai Lin ,  Qi Yang ,  Chengwei Qin  \n\n\n\n          Subjects: \n           Multiagent Systems (cs.MA) ; Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)\n         \n\n         \n          Training-time multi-agent system (MAS) design seeks to learn reusable orchestration policies, yet prevailing approaches remain largely one-shot: an Orchestrator generates a system but only observes and learns from its terminal outcome. This paradigm discards execution trajectories that expose system-level failures and contain fine-grained information beyond the terminal outcome, leaving no direct basis for revising the source MAS, evaluating the updated MAS, or learning from the resulting contrast. To this end, we introduce MAS-Loop, an execution-grounded framework that closes this feedback loop by coupling an Orchestrator with a trajectory-conditioned Refiner. For each task, the Orchestrator constructs and executes a source MAS to obtain an outcome score and an intermediate trajectory. Conditioned on the trajectory, the Refiner localizes an actionable failure, formulates a trajectory-grounded diagnosis, and proposes a bounded patch to the agents, information flow, or execution configuration. The executable updated MAS is re-executed on the same task to measure its realized outcome under stochastic execution. This pair-MAS comparison provides complementary learning signals: the Orchestrator learns to generate strong systems with fewer recoverable failures, whereas the Refiner learns to expose and repair observed weaknesses, thereby driving policy-level co-evolution across training iterations. Experiments show that MAS-Loop achieves the strongest overall performance among the evaluated baselines, with consistent gains from refinement across all evaluated benchmarks. The co-evolution process also yields MAS-JudgeCorpus, a dataset of execution-grounded source-updated pairs with orchestration- and trajectory-level supervision, which can provide the community with support for MAS reward modeling and trajectory-aware investigation.\n         \n       \n     \n     \n       [279] \n       \n        arXiv:2610.38968\n       \n          (cross-list from cs.ET)\n\n        [ pdf ,  html ,  other ]\n     \n     \n       \n          Title: \n          TableTune: Measured, Self-Correcting Draft-Length Tables for Dynamic Speculative Decoding\n         \n          Abhishek Malvankar ,  Paul Castro ,  Nick Mitchell ,  Lionel Villard  \n\n\n\n          Subjects: \n           Emerging Technologies (cs.ET) ; Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)\n         \n\n         \n          Speculative decoding speeds up large language model (LLM) serving only while the GPU has spare compute, so the best draft length, the number of tokens a small drafter guesses per step, shrinks as the batch grows. vLLM lets operators set the draft length for each batch size with a hand-written table, but nothing measures or corrects that table. We present TableTune. Its tuner times the decoding step for every batch size and draft length on vLLM's own serving path and computes, in minutes, the table that maximizes throughput. Its controller, a vLLM plugin, tracks how many guesses are accepted in live traffic and rebuilds the table while serving. We measure TableTune only against other ways to speculate. On an H100, the tuned table delivers at least 94% of the throughput of the best fixed draft length at every load in four of five drafter settings (88% in the fifth) without the operator having to know which length that is. Against reimplementations of TurboSpec- and Nightjar-style control on the same engine, its worst-case loss is never higher than theirs, and with the EAGLE-3 drafter it is 3% against 9% and 12%. On a mixture-of-experts model, vLLM's documented table loses up to 18% and the tuned table at most 3%. Starting from mistuned tables, the controller raises throughput by up to 58% (up to 45% on vLLM's default runner), and after a workload switch with the DFlash drafter it recovers 96% of a stale table's loss. In llm-d's optimized-baseline deployment as shipped, two Qwen3-32B replicas on four GPUs, the tuned table is up to 32% faster than a fixed draft length of 3 and up to 25% faster than vLLM's documented table at high load, and the controller raises the documented table's throughput by up to 29%.\n         \n       \n     \n     \n       [280] \n       \n        arXiv:2610.38981\n       \n          (cross-list from cs.LG)\n\n        [ pdf ,  html ,  other ]\n     \n     \n       \n          Title: \n          Why Speculative Decoding Works Better Than Predicted on Sparse MoE Models\n         \n          Ekagra Ranjan ,  Komal K. Teru ,  Bharat Venkitesh ,  Acyr Locatelli  \n\n          Comments: \n          Accepted as at NeurIPS 2026 main track conference\n         \n\n\n          Subjects: \n           Machine Learning (cs.LG) ; Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)\n         \n\n         \n          Speculative decoding (SD) accelerates autoregressive inference by leveraging the memory bound nature of decoding, verifying multiple draft tokens in a single forward pass. For mixture-of-experts (MoE) models, prior work predicts a concave speedup-batch size relationship, with gains peaking at intermediate batch sizes due to expert saturation. We evaluate this across 10 sparse MoE models (30B--1T parameters, 8--512 experts) and find recent fine-grained models break this trend, showing higher speedup at low batch size. We find verification cost is governed not only by activated-expert bytes, but also by temporal overlap across draft tokens and GPU execution dynamics. At low batch size, MoE layers in fine-grained models account for a much smaller share of runtime than of weights; on H100, kernel profiling attributes this mainly to higher bandwidth utilization of MoE kernels relative to attention, with L2 absorbing repeated reads of co-routed experts. Across draft tokens, routing overlap further reduces the unique experts activated during verification relative to independence assumptions. We model this effect using a maximum-entropy Iterative Proportional Fitting (IPF) framework that predicts unique expert counts from routing-overlap statistics with less than 4\\% error, and substantially narrows the gap to real-model curves and captures the qualitative low-batch trend from synthetic routing.\n         \n       \n     \n     \n       [281] \n       \n        arXiv:2610.38985\n       \n          (cross-list from cs.LG)\n\n        [ pdf ,  html ,  other ]\n     \n     \n       \n          Title: \n          Benchmarking Graph Neural Networks for AC Power Flow: The Role of Inductive Biases and Physical Constraints\n         \n          Federico Clerici ,  Ferran Bohigas-Daranas ,  Hamid Latif-Martínez ,  Eduardo Prieto-Araujo ,  Oriol Gomis-Bellmunt ,  Pere Barlet-Ros  \n\n\n\n          Subjects: \n           Machine Learning (cs.LG) ; Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)\n         \n\n         \n          Fast and accurate models for AC power flow (ACPF) are increasingly important as modern electrical grids require more frequent system analyses. Graph neural networks (GNNs) naturally exploit electrical grid topology, yet the effects of architectural design choices on predictive performance remain poorly understood given differences in graph representations and evaluation settings across studies.\n We present a unified benchmark of representative GNN architectures for ACPF prediction under a common experimental framework. All models use identical preprocessing and training procedures and comparable parameter budgets, isolating the impact of message-passing mechanisms and inductive biases. We also analyze errors by distance from the slack bus and systematically ablate key design components. We evaluate predictive accuracy and physical consistency on the IEEE 14-bus and IEEE 118-bus systems.\n Results indicate that anisotropic message-passing architectures offer the most favorable trade-off between predictive accuracy and physical consistency under the studied parameter and topology constraints. Explicit edge features encoding line impedances and global context aggregation are particularly important for representing electrical interactions and system-wide dependencies, especially for voltage-angle predictions on the topologically bottlenecked IEEE-118 grid. Under a budget with approximately fixed parameters, two or three message-passing layers generally provide the best balance between depth and representation width, while additional depth offers no consistent improvement. Ablations also show that standard residual and root connections are not necessarily beneficial in shallow models, and that aggregation operators involve trade-offs between statistical accuracy and physical consistency. These results provide practical guidelines for designing graph-based power system models.\n         \n       \n     \n     \n       [282] \n       \n        arXiv:2610.38994\n       \n          (cross-list from cs.LG)\n\n        [ pdf ,  html ,  other ]\n     \n     \n       \n          Title: \n          Rethinking GFlowNet Training: Negative Reinforce Dynamics\n         \n          Pedro Dall'Antonia ,  Daniel Csillag ,  Diego Mesquita  \n\n          Comments: \n          22 pages, 9 figures, 3 tables\n         \n\n\n          Subjects: \n           Machine Learning (cs.LG) ; Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)\n         \n\n         \n          Generative Flow Networks (GFlowNets) are models that learn to sample discrete objects proportional to a target reward. They are trained by minimizing balance errors over sampled trajectories, which inherently ties correctness to exploration: if a region is not visited, balance cannot be enforced there. Adaptive exploration is therefore crucial, yet standard training relies on a semi-gradient update that treats the sampling distribution as fixed at each optimization step. On the other hand, the full gradient reveals a second mechanism that reduces the observed errors in an anti-exploratory manner. In this paper, we establish a connection between the full gradient and min-min dynamics between the GFlowNet and a decoupled sampler, showing that the sampler reduces observed violations by avoiding high-error regions. This insight motivates a bridge between two-player games and single-policy dynamics, which underpins the design of our proposed method, Negative Reinforce Dynamics (NRD). By tying the parameters of an adversarial game together, we recover a single-policy dynamic that extends exploration while maintaining correctness. In standard benchmarks, our method outperforms existing approaches in both convergence speed and exploration efficiency, without training a separate teacher or auxiliary exploration policy.\n         \n       \n     \n     \n       [283] \n       \n        arXiv:2610.39009\n       \n          (cross-list from cs.LG)\n\n        [ pdf ,  html ,  other ]\n     \n     \n       \n          Title: \n          Assessing feature scorer results on high-dimensional datasets with t-SNE\n         \n          Bruno Iochins Grisci ,  Mario Inostroza-Ponta ,  Márcio Dorn  \n\n\n          Journal-ref: \n          B.I. Grisci, M. Inostroza-Ponta, M. Dorn, Assessing feature scorer results on high-dimensional datasets with t-SNE, Neurocomputing 652 (2025) 130561\n         \n\n          Subjects: \n           Machine Learning (cs.LG) ; Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)\n         \n\n         \n          While vast literature on high-dimensional data visualization is available, there are not many works regarding the visualization of feature scorers and their results. Feature scorers are algorithms that assign numerical importance to each feature of multi-dimensional datasets. These importance scores can be used in several applications, such as feature selection, knowledge discovery, and machine learning interpretability. There are several feature scorers to choose from, and often no single metric or ground truth is available to guarantee the quality of their results. In this scenario, visualization can become valuable to support the decision of which method to choose and how good its results are. For this goal, this work presents \"weighted t-SNE.\" It modifies the relationship between data points in the embedded 2D space to reflect the importance of each dimension of the original datasets as assessed by a feature scorer. This research discusses how to implement weighted t-SNE, proposes the silhouette coefficient as a numerical evaluation of the results, and shows several examples of its use in practice. Synthetic and real-world tabular datasets are used in the experiments together with nine feature scorers, ranging from Mutual Information to neural networks. Each feature scorer produces unique visualizations, and weighted t-SNE can be used to compare and choose the one that better suits a given dataset and task. Weighted t-SNE can also visually show the importance of features learned by machine learning models and help us see how they are organizing the data, increasing their interpretability.\n         \n       \n     \n     \n       [284] \n       \n        arXiv:2610.39016\n       \n          (cross-list from cs.CL)\n\n        [ pdf ,  html ,  other ]\n     \n     \n       \n          Title: \n          Implicit Reasoning in Transformers: Bridging the Compositionality Gap in GPT-2-like Models\n         \n          Davide Venuto ,  Leonardo Venuto  \n\n          Comments: \n          Code available at:  this https URL \n         \n\n\n          Subjects: \n           Computation and Language (cs.CL) ; Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)\n         \n\n         \n          Compositional generalization remains a persistent limitation of transformer language models: even when a model learns to combine known facts correctly in-distribution (ID), the same compositional mechanism may fail when applied to out-of-distribution (OOD) knowledge. We investigate this compositionality gap in a controlled two-hop reasoning setting using a GPT-2-like transformer model trained from scratch. Building on prior work identifying a circuit associated with compositional generalization, we test whether the failure of OOD transfer is related to how atomic knowledge is structurally exposed during training. We modify the training distribution by presenting both ID and OOD atomic facts in a format positionally aligned with the second-hop retrieval step of the learned compositional circuit, without modifying the model architecture or adding OOD compositional supervision. Under this intervention, the model successfully transfers the learned ID compositional behaviour to held-out OOD atomic facts, closing the observed compositionality gap in the controlled setting. Our results provide evidence that the observed compositionality gap is not a fundamental architecture limitation and that it can be overcome through mechanistically informed training distribution design.\n         \n       \n     \n     \n       [285] \n       \n        arXiv:2610.39020\n       \n          (cross-list from cs.CL)\n\n        [ pdf ,  html ,  other ]\n     \n     \n       \n          Title: \n          Distilling the Shape of Uncertainty: What On-Policy Distillation Learns and How to Predict It\n         \n          Shiping Yang ,  Chuanqi Tang ,  Hossein Hajimirsadeghi ,  Layla El Asri ,  Mohammad Amin Shabani ,  Angel X. Chang  \n\n\n\n          Subjects: \n           Computation and Language (cs.CL) ; Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)\n         \n\n         \n          On-policy distillation (OPD) is increasingly adopted in the post-training of large language models. However, a stronger teacher may fail to improve the student. Prior work attributes this to a lack of new knowledge, but what that knowledge is remains unclear. We show that OPD learns not only \\textit{how to branch}, i.e., which candidate tokens to consider, but also \\textit{where to branch}, i.e., where to keep reasoning paths open. The existing token-overlap metric captures only the former. We introduce the entropy shape, the sequence of next-token entropies, to characterize the latter, and propose the Entropy Shape Correlation (ESC) to measure student-teacher agreement in entropy shape. Tracking ESC throughout training and intervening on a teacher's entropy while preserving its token preferences, we empirically show that \\textit{where to branch} causally drives OPD gains, independently of \\textit{how to branch}. Finally, we formulate OPD outcome prediction as a teacher selection task and establish an evaluation protocol for it, under which pre-OPD ESC selects the best teacher for both students and outperforms existing predictors. Code is available at  this https URL .\n         \n       \n     \n     \n       [286] \n       \n        arXiv:2610.39027\n       \n          (cross-list from cs.LG)\n\n        [ pdf ,  html ,  other ]\n     \n     \n       \n          Title: \n          Representation Complexity Along Learning Trajectories: Trading Width for Precision and Time\n         \n          Ryan Singh ,  Miguel De Llanza ,  Christopher L. Buckley  \n\n\n\n          Subjects: \n           Machine Learning (cs.LG) ; Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)\n         \n\n         \n          What computational role does overparameterisation play during learning? Information-plane analyses suggest that neural networks can traverse representations substantially more complex than those required by their final predictors. We explore when this transient complexity serves a computational purpose by comparing the representation complexity needed for prediction with that needed to make an unknown target statistically visible through gradient feedback. We introduce an oracle model of neural network learning and use Kolmogorov \\(n\\)-widths to quantify effective representation complexity. Our main technical result transfers complexity bounds from the forward representation to the corresponding gradient-query features. Combined with statistical-query lower bounds, this gives a task-agnostic lower bound on the initial effective width required for target-dependent gradient information to become detectable. For \\(k\\)-sparse parity in \\(D\\) dimensions, a known target admits an \\(O(k)\\)-width representation, while the general bound already implies a polynomial separation between representation and discovery. We then exploit parity's invariance under coordinatewise sign reflections to identify a sharper unsigned effective width, which removes sign distinctions invisible to the parity information channel. At a transfer-compatible measurement resolution, its critical scale is \\(\\widetilde{\\Theta}_k\\left( D^{k-1}\\tau^2 \\right)\\), up to logarithmic factors. Thus, the representation resources required to discover a predictor can substantially exceed those required to represent it once learned. Interpreting precision through accumulation over fresh samples yields a corresponding width-time frontier. Experiments support the predicted dimension dependence and examine how optimisation and generalisation depart from this idealised frontier.\n         \n       \n     \n     \n       [287] \n       \n        arXiv:2610.39049\n       \n          (cross-list from cs.LG)\n\n        [ pdf ,  html ,  other ]\n     \n     \n       \n          Title: \n          Reinforcement Learning for the Game of Balatro: A Fast Simulator and Two-Policy Training Approach\n         \n          Ajay Vikram Periasami ,  Anjaneya Gupta ,  Ethan Cheng ,  Ivan Rosales  \n\n          Comments: \n          Accepted at EXAG 2026 (AIIDE Workshop on Experimental AI in Games)\n         \n\n\n          Subjects: \n           Machine Learning (cs.LG) ; Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)\n         \n\n         \n          Balatro, a 2024 roguelike deckbuilder, presents three difficulties that have historically derailed reinforcement learning methods: a 3,000-action state-dependent action space, horizons of several hundred decisions with sparse rewards, and pervasive stochasticity in both card draws and shop generation. Published RL results on the game remain scarce. We introduce an end-to-end pipeline comprising (i) a high-throughput Rust simulator that is automatically state-diffed against the live game client via BalatroBot, (ii) a condensed observation-action interface with dynamic per-step masking of legal moves, and (iii) a multi-agent PPO architecture that decomposes play into a tactical blind policy and a strategic shop policy that share the full game state but act in disjoint phases. Long-horizon credit assignment is partially addressed by per-agent reward routing with explicit cross-agent credit. On the main joint run, training metrics show a sharp phase transition in which the first-blind clear rate converges above 99% and mean episode return increases by roughly an order of magnitude, plateauing at a mean maximum ante of 3 out of 8; single-agent baselines under identical rewards oscillated between greedy and hoarding failure modes and did not stably clear the first blind. These results establish the two-policy decomposition as a strong reactive baseline for Balatro and localise the remaining gap to long-horizon strategic planning rather than tactical blind play.\n         \n       \n     \n     \n       [288] \n       \n        arXiv:2610.39056\n       \n          (cross-list from cs.LG)\n\n        [ pdf ,  html ,  other ]\n     \n     \n       \n          Title: \n          When Does Closed-Loop Curriculum Adaptation Help GRPO? An Analysis on Countdown\n         \n          Yuv Raj Pant ,  Moshiur Rahman Tonmoy ,  Kaiqun Fu  \n\n          Comments: \n          6 pages, 2 figures, Accepted at IEEE ICTAI 2026\n         \n\n\n          Subjects: \n           Machine Learning (cs.LG) ; Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)\n         \n\n         \n          Reinforcement fine-tuning has become an effective post-training paradigm for improving the reasoning ability of large language models, but it remains sample-inefficient when the training distribution is mismatched to the model's current capability. In group-relative objectives such as Group Relative Policy Optimization (GRPO), problems that are too hard often produce uniformly failed rollouts, while problems that are too easy provide little useful advantage signal. In this work, we introduce CLOSEDLOOPGAUSSIAN, a lightweight adaptive curriculum method for efficient reinforcement fine-tuning on ordered reasoning tasks. Instead of following a fixed easy-to-hard schedule, CLOSEDLOOPGAUSSIAN preserves the structure of an Easy-to-Hard Gaussian sampler while dynamically adjusting its pace and spread using periodic probe estimates of per-difficulty accuracy. The controller accelerates training when the model is ahead of a reference curriculum, slows down when the model falls behind, and broadens sampling when earlier difficulties regress. This feedback mechanism keeps training focused on tasks that are challenging but still learnable, without changing the reward function, model architecture, or underlying GRPO optimizer. Experiments on symbolic arithmetic reasoning task show that CLOSEDLOOPGAUSSIAN improves exact-match accuracy over vanilla GRPO and fixed open-loop curricula, with the strongest gains in intermediate-difficulty regimes where group-relative learning signals are most informative. Codes are available at  this https URL .\n         \n       \n     \n     \n       [289] \n       \n        arXiv:2610.39074\n       \n          (cross-list from cond-mat.mes-hall)\n\n        [ pdf ,  html ,  other ]\n     \n     \n       \n          Title: \n          HERA: Assessing an Autonomous Agent for Quantum Device Measurement\n         \n          Zachary W. Gomez ,  Nathan Bittner ,  Jordan T. McCourt ,  Qingrui Cao ,  Sandesh S. Kalantre ,  Benjamin H. Alexander ,  Zhiren Zheng ,  Kenji Watanabe ,  Takashi Taniguchi ,  Aaron Sharpe  \n\n          Comments: \n          16 pages, 5 figures, plus Supplementary Information (7 figures, 6 tables); data and code at  this https URL  and  this https URL \n         \n\n\n          Subjects: \n           Mesoscale and Nanoscale Physics (cond-mat.mes-hall) ; Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)\n         \n\n         \n          Large language model agents have begun to carry out laboratory measurements, but their scientific conclusions are useful only if they hold up under scrutiny. Demonstrating this requires controlled studies because models are stochastic and the context a model is given steers its conclusions. Here we introduce HERA, an autonomous agent designed to carry out cryogenic transport measurements of quantum devices. HERA draws on a knowledge base curated by lab members that holds measurement protocols, software tools, and literature references. We assessed HERA with a blinded trial against an expert on the stacking order of two multilayer graphene devices, an ablation of its knowledge base, and hundreds of offline replays in which models designed the trial's central measurement without running it. HERA's conclusions matched the expert's sealed conclusions on both tested devices. The ablation and the replay studies measured the impact of the knowledge base across different models, showing that with it every model designed better measurements and reasoned more soundly. To test how readily HERA extends to a new problem, we tasked it with finding and characterizing superconductivity in a different material system. This required a few hours of expert time to develop a new experimental protocol, after which HERA did both autonomously. These results show that, with a knowledge base designed for the task, language model agents are capable of sound experimental science.\n         \n       \n     \n     \n       [290] \n       \n        arXiv:2610.39076\n       \n          (cross-list from cs.RO)\n\n        [ pdf ,  html ,  other ]\n     \n     \n       \n          Title: \n          Tactus: Tactile & Pose Sensing Glove for Natural Dexterity Data Collection\n         \n          Li Guan ,  Rachael Granberry ,  Sahar Aseeri ,  Sahar Rostami ,  Taylor Niehues ,  Sai Dhanooshi Marri ,  Mia Huang ,  Dingtian Zhang ,  Ziyi Kou ,  Jason Pursel ,  Eric Whitmire ,  Vatsal Mehta ,  Ashish Deshpande ,  Tristan Trutna ,  Reed Belcher ,  Emelia Black ,  Julian Blake ,  Roger Boldu ,  Sean Bonaventure ,  Einar Boyovich ,  Jordan Brennan ,  Elizabeth Casillas ,  David Christensen ,  Tyler Christensen ,  Nicholas Colonnese ,  Stuart Dealey ,  Gaige DeHaven ,  Katie Doran ,  Chris Doughty ,  Robert Drury ,  Silvia Espino ,  Gabriel Geyne ,  Beth Goodrich ,  Kyra Gould ,  Joe Grisso ,  Susana Guerrero ,  Andrew Gwozdziowski ,  Monica Ha ,  Ehsan Hajiesmaili ,  Kate Healy ,  Rose Hope ,  Michael Hurst ,  Claire Ikemiya ,  Daisy Isibor ,  Rick James ,  Yashasvi Joginapally ,  Samuel Johnson ,  Tyler Jorgensen ,  Kristy Jost ,  Ben Kamath ,  Slava Karulin ,  Alexander Keener ,  Woo Kim ,  Alison Krauskopf ,  Ankit Kumar ,  Joshua Lang ,  Lizzy Li ,  Cameron Lynch ,  Daniel Ma ,  Hector Martinon ,  Christina Matos ,  Keagan McCurdy ,  Lee Mincey ,  Stuart Nezlek ,  Tiffany Nguyen ,  James O'Hara ,  Jason Osborne ,  Jacky Pai ,  Roham Pardakhtim ,  David Perek ,  Alexandre Poulin ,  Nick Raines ,  Salwa Rasheed ,  Leah Resneck ,  Kevin Rhodes ,  Ergys Ristani ,  Douglas Roberts ,  Anik Sarker ,  Harrison Scarborough ,  Jonas Schmidtler ,  Brian Shieh ,  Benjamin Shuman ,  Garrett Simard ,  Forrest Smith ,  Udara Somarathna ,  Eliot Sosin ,  Jonathan Stewart ,  Kristine Strom ,  Adam Swett ,  Hal Talbot-Mantooth ,  Julia Tanner ,  Sai Sharan Thirunagari ,  Curtis Torrey ,  Simge Uzun ,  Eileen Wachs ,  Rylan Wasson ,  Henry Wei ,  Jesse White ,  Beau Woodbyrne ,  Antonio Woods  \n\n          Comments: \n          9 pages\n         \n\n\n          Subjects: \n           Robotics (cs.RO) ; Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI); Hardware Architecture (cs.AR); Machine Learning (cs.LG)\n         \n\n         \n          Collecting naturalistic human manipulation demonstrations with accurate contact force and hand pose is essential for robot dexterous manipulation policy and foundational world model learning - yet no existing wearable platform provides both calibrated fingertip force sensing and occlusion-resilient hand tracking in a form factor that preserves natural manipulation behavior. We present Tactus, a textile-integrated sensing glove that co-optimizes three design pillars: (1) a thin, conformal 3D-knit form factor (1.5 mm fingertip stackup) designed to minimize interference with natural manipulation, (2) strategically placed capacitive tactile sensors at manipulation-relevant regions informed by empirical contact studies, and (3) IMU-fused pose tracking that maintains accuracy through the hand-object occlusions where manipulation data is most valuable. Tactus includes a documented calibration pipeline for cross-glove consistency and per-session calibration for subject-dependent correction. To enable objective comparison, we introduce standardized benchmarking protocols for tactile force regression and hand pose estimation, and apply them to Tactus and two commercially available tactile gloves - to our knowledge the first quantitative cross-platform evaluation of this kind. Tactus resolves a minimum detectable force of 0.59 N at 95% confidence, 1.6 - 2.7x lower than the commercial baselines, and with 0.87 cross-participant consistency, while our pose tracking sustains 11.0 mm fingertip keypoint relative error during manipulation, when occlusion is heaviest. We show that these combined signals support object property estimation, slip detection, and representative recording of hand-object interaction - yielding demonstration data with the fidelity required for contact-aware manipulation policies and world models.\n         \n       \n     \n     \n       [291] \n       \n        arXiv:2610.39100\n       \n          (cross-list from cs.LG)\n\n        [ pdf ,  html ,  other ]\n     \n     \n       \n          Title: \n          Generating Critic-Guided Synthetic Experience in Latent World Models\n         \n          Jorge de Freitas ,  Christos Ziakas ,  Alessandra Russo  \n\n\n\n          Subjects: \n           Machine Learning (cs.LG) ; Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)\n         \n\n         \n          Learning from offline datasets is limited in scalability, as collecting new experience is often costly or unsafe in the real world. World models offer an alternative way to generate new experience through a learned dynamics model, yet learning policies in latent space is limited due to compounding errors in imagined rollouts. To this end, we propose using the world model as a local transition generator, leveraging critic guidance to choose actions inside that model to generate synthetic experiences. We study action refinement methodologies, introducing Critic-Guided Synthetic Experience (CGSE) generation in world models. Specifically, CGSE augments an existing offline dataset by sampling candidate actions using an offline policy and refining each to maximize a first-order approximation of the $Q$-function within a trust region. We then use a frozen latent world model to imagine the future state given each updated action, and use the critic to choose the best one, appending this synthetic transition to the replay buffer. Our method requires no additional real-environment interaction or test-time planner, as it generates synthetic experience offline using the world model. On three OGBench manipulation tasks, CGSE improves an offline policy by a relative improvement of $7.1\\%$ on the average success rate.\n         \n       \n     \n     \n       [292] \n       \n        arXiv:2610.39109\n       \n          (cross-list from cs.CE)\n\n        [ pdf ,  html ,  other ]\n     \n     \n       \n          Title: \n          PCM: A Proof-Carrying Message Protocol for Verifiable Multi-Agent Communication\n         \n          Bravish Ghosh  (Cygnux Labs) \n\n          Comments: \n          50 pages, 9 figures, 15 tables. Specification, conformance vectors, verifier and benchmark:  this https URL \n         \n\n\n          Subjects: \n           Computational Engineering, Finance, and Science (cs.CE) ; Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI); Cryptography and Security (cs.CR); Multiagent Systems (cs.MA)\n         \n\n         \n          When one AI agent tells another that \"the tests passed\" or \"a person approved this refund\", the receiver has only the sender's word. A2A and MCP authenticate the channel, but the sender is the party in doubt, so a prompt-injected agent can launder fabricated facts and authority through a multi-agent system. We present Proof-Carrying Messages (PCM), a protocol in which each claim carries evidence: records from an append-only Merkle log that the agent cannot write, with inclusion proofs, signed by an attesting signer the agent cannot control. Receivers check messages offline and give each claim a three-valued result: proven, unproven or failed. A claim that rests only on the agent's own report is never proven. Authority travels as grant chains rooted in a recorded human approval, and the chains can only narrow. We specify four claim types, a typed caveat algebra, replay rules that survive restarts, and strict readings that make independent verifiers agree, and we prove the resulting security properties. On a pre-specified benchmark of 926 messages from real runs, 712 of them lies in 16 classes, PCM accepts 10 lies, all of them stale evidence (1.4%, 95% CI 0.8-2.6%). Plain A2A accepts all 712, and models of six deployed protocols (A2A with signed Agent Cards, uAgents, RFC 9421, SCITT, AP2, Verifiable Intent) accept 504-712. Two independent verifiers agree on all 926 verdicts, and verification takes about 1 ms.\n         \n       \n     \n     \n       [293] \n       \n        arXiv:2610.39114\n       \n          (cross-list from cs.LG)\n\n        [ pdf ,  html ,  other ]\n     \n     \n       \n          Title: \n          From Local Recovery to Unified Generation: Discovering Triple Set in Knowledge Graphs via Observation-Anchored Latent Diffusion\n         \n          Jiaqi Wang ,  Yisen Gao ,  Huayang Shi ,  Junhao Hou ,  Wengen Li ,  Jihong Guan ,  Yichao Zhang ,  Shuigeng Zhou ,  Yangqiu Song ,  Hong Cheng  \n\n\n\n          Subjects: \n           Machine Learning (cs.LG) ; Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)\n         \n\n         \n          Triple set prediction (TSP) is to recover missing relational triples in a knowledge graph by predicting the triple set instead of isolated links. Existing approaches predominantly rely on the subgraph-based completion paradigm, where the original graph is first partitioned into local subgraphs which are then independently completed. Such a paradigm suffers from fragmented structural reasoning, limited cross subgraph dependency modeling, and missing predictions across subgraphs. This paper proposes LatentDiff, a novel unified latent generative framework for TSP, which reformulates knowledge graph completion as latent space graph generation. LatentDiff first learns a compact latent representation using a relation-aware residual-quantized knowledge graph autoencoder, which encodes the whole graph into relation-aware latent embeddings, quantizes them with chunked residual codebooks, and reconstructs triples through a factorized decoder. On top of this latent space, we introduce an observation-anchored latent graph diffusion model, where the target latent graph representation is perturbed toward the observed latent. The reverse process recovers missing triples through progressive latent denoising. Experiments on three benchmark datasets show that LatentDiff outperforms the SOTA in terms of F_{tsp} by 5.4 percentage points on average. Code:  this https URL .\n         \n       \n     \n     \n       [294] \n       \n        arXiv:2610.39122\n       \n          (cross-list from cs.CR)\n\n        [ pdf ,  html ,  other ]\n     \n     \n       \n          Title: \n          Appreciation Teaches Unauthorized Execution: Self-Evolution Degrades the Robustness of Personal Agents to Indirect Prompt Injection\n         \n          Xinyan Wang ,  Xiaogeng Liu ,  Jun Shao ,  Chaowei Xiao  \n\n\n\n          Subjects: \n           Cryptography and Security (cs.CR) ; Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)\n         \n\n         \n          Self-evolving personal agents act for users and learn from user feedback. However, if an agent obeys an instruction that an untrusted third party hides in content, the third party gains control of the agent. Such indirect prompt injection (IPI) poses severe risks. We investigate whether self-evolution degrades the robustness of personal agents to IPI. To study this question, we design a controlled two-phase methodology. In the adaptation phase, the user asks the agent to read external content and complete the task described in it, a common request in daily use. The agent then learns from the user's feedback and improves itself as designed. In the test phase, a third party writes a similar task into new external content, but the user's test request does not authorize the injected task. Using this methodology, we build multiple tasks with diverse test instances in different domains for a real personal agent. Our extensive experiments reveal that self-evolution substantially degrades robustness to IPI. Specifically, compared with the base agent, which does not evolve from user feedback, the average attack success rate rises by 14.0--48.8 percentage points under skill evolution and by 3.0--39.3 percentage points under reinforcement learning, posing safety risks. The code is available at  this https URL \n         \n       \n     \n     \n       [295] \n       \n        arXiv:2610.39124\n       \n          (cross-list from cs.LG)\n\n        [ pdf ,  html ,  other ]\n     \n     \n       \n          Title: \n          Clean Sheets and Checkpoints: Evidence Complexity for Certifying Sequential Agent Risk\n         \n          Rohith Yanambaka Venkata ,  Don McBride ,  Tiago Sequeira ,  Md. Al Amin  \n\n          Comments: \n          26 pages, 3 figures, 3 tables\n         \n\n\n          Subjects: \n           Machine Learning (cs.LG) ; Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)\n         \n\n         \n          Dangerous-capability evaluations often test an AI agent from prepared states at ordered milestones. A clean sheet records no successes. What it certifies about a campaign of $B$ attempts depends on what a failed attempt erases. We model the task as a chain of $k$ milestones divided into checkpoint segments and ask how many zero audit responses certify, at level $\\alpha$, a completion probability of at most $\\epsilon$. For segments chosen before the audit, counting possible completion windows gives a certificate for any specified budget. The bound also covers adaptive deployment processes that follow the chain's transitions, provided their success probabilities after every complete history are capped by the corresponding audit success probabilities. The audit does not test this transport assumption. In rare-risk phase interiors, each segment is either rarely completed within the budget (active) or nearly always completed (free). The minimum expected number of zero responses is asymptotic to $\\log(1/\\alpha)\\,(B^a/(a!\\,\\epsilon))^{1/K_A}$, where the $a$ active segments contain $K_A$ milestones and are those at least as long as a budget-dependent threshold. The certificate attains this constant. Every asymptotically optimal evaluator must divide its expected response count equally among active milestones in the limit, with a vanishing share elsewhere. Where a single segment passes from active to free, each of its milestones receives a smaller limiting share than an active milestone; a crossover-aware certificate attains the optimal constant. For selected segments with known or unqueried prefix steps, counting failed cycles can sharpen the certificate.\n         \n       \n     \n     \n       [296] \n       \n        arXiv:2610.39155\n       \n          (cross-list from cs.CR)\n\n        [ pdf ,  html ,  other ]\n     \n     \n       \n          Title: \n          REALM: Identifying Minimal Task Assets for LLM Agents\n         \n          Zihou Ren ,  Byungchul Tak ,  Long Wang  \n\n\n\n          Subjects: \n           Cryptography and Security (cs.CR) ; Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI); Systems and Control (eess.SY)\n         \n\n         \n          Large language model (LLM) agents often receive access to more files, accounts, and services than a task requires. Prompt injection turns this excess access into an opportunity to redirect execution toward unrelated resources. Applying least privilege therefore requires identifying the assets needed by a task, including assets whose identities emerge only during execution. We present REALM (Required Execution Assets Linked to a Mission), an approach to identifying and maintaining a task's required assets and their permitted uses. The approach represents required assets as Dependency Graph nodes, resolves prerequisite relationships, and admits newly discovered assets through source-scoped selection. It separates discovering an asset from authorizing its use, while preserving the agent's freedom to choose its computation. The REALM Builder maintains the selected grants and projects Asset Files; the Partial API Manager exposes resource-bound Partial APIs to the Agent inside a Task Container. Across 60 clean tasks and 560 injected cases in AgentDyn, it completes 40.0% and 36.4% of tasks and decreases ASR from 16.6% to 0.2% with qwen3.5-flash as the base model.\n         \n       \n     \n     \n       [297] \n       \n        arXiv:2610.39175\n       \n          (cross-list from cs.IR)\n\n        [ pdf ,  html ,  other ]\n     \n     \n       \n          Title: \n          PRISM: Personalized Item Representations in User History\n         \n          Renzhi Wu ,  Zikun Cui ,  Junjie Yang ,  Li Sheng ,  Yongjun Xie ,  Jerry Fu ,  Nipun Mathur ,  Sri Reddy ,  Hong Li  \n\n\n\n          Subjects: \n           Information Retrieval (cs.IR) ; Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)\n         \n\n         \n          Sequential recommenders typically construct history inputs from item identifiers or content features using an embedding map shared across users. The same item therefore yields the same initial embedding representation, even when users have different relationships to the item. We propose personalized item representations in user history, where each item's embedding expresses its relationship to the user. Personalization becomes a property of the history inputs themselves, supplying richer evidence for later sequence modeling.\n We instantiate this idea with PRISM, which reuses upstream ranking computation to generate personalized item embeddings. An autoencoder jointly compresses all layer states from a user tower, a user--item tower, and same-request candidate mixing into one embedding per scored pair for downstream replay. Offline evaluation on Surface 1, a major recommendation surface at billion scale, reports an average downstream normalized entropy (NE) change of $-0.15\\%$, compared with $-0.07\\%$ for VISTA at similar history lengths. We further identify an empirical sequence-length scaling law: over history lengths of 26--240 events, NE gain follows a fitted power law with exponent 0.468, increasing from 0.06\\% to 0.17\\%. Therefore, personalized item representations in user history provide a new scaling dimension for improving recommendation quality.\n         \n       \n     \n     \n       [298] \n       \n        arXiv:2610.39180\n       \n          (cross-list from cs.RO)\n\n        [ pdf ,  other ]\n     \n     \n       \n          Title: \n          EmbodiExp: Compositional Experience Transfer for Embodied Agents\n         \n          Zhifeng Gu ,  Ying Chen ,  Rui Jiang ,  Lei Yi ,  Bing Wang  \n\n\n\n          Subjects: \n           Robotics (cs.RO) ; Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI); Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV); Machine Learning (cs.LG)\n         \n\n         \n          Experience can help embodied agents turn high-level plans into grounded actions, but transferring it across tasks remains challenging: previously effective strategies may be inapplicable to the current state or fail to produce their intended outcomes. We present EmbodiExp, a framework for constructing transferable experiences and composing them online during embodied task execution. Our key observation is that tasks often share subgoals whose completion can be assessed independently of overall task success. We therefore introduce an outcome-grounded subgoal experience that specifies a subgoal, applicability conditions, an adaptable policy, and a verifiable outcome. During execution, applicable experiences guide action generation, while verified outcomes can establish the conditions required by subsequent experiences, enabling online composition. Subgoal-level outcome attribution further enables failure utilization: failed trajectories can still contribute verified local successes and condition-specific guidance on actions to avoid. Experiments on multiple embodied benchmarks under a fold-based protocol with frozen experience banks demonstrate the effectiveness of EmbodiExp.\n         \n       \n     \n     \n       [299] \n       \n        arXiv:2610.39223\n       \n          (cross-list from cs.LG)\n\n        [ pdf ,  html ,  other ]\n     \n     \n       \n          Title: \n          Where Position Matters: Profiling-Guided Layer-Wise RoPE Allocation in Transformers\n         \n          Zhiwei Liu ,  Boao Kong ,  Bing Liu  \n\n          Comments: \n          28 pages\n         \n\n\n          Subjects: \n           Machine Learning (cs.LG) ; Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI); Machine Learning (stat.ML)\n         \n\n         \n          Rotary Position Embedding (RoPE) typically uses uniform coverage across Transformer layers despite heterogeneous positional requirements. Phase-off interventions reveal non-uniform, model-specific rotary-phase dependence, while coverage interventions relate it to coverage-upgrade benefits. We propose profiling-guided layer-wise RoPE allocation, assigning high/low coverage under a fixed budget using early coverage-benefit profiles. The framework supports fixed-width query/key representations and physical rotary-dimension allocation in Multi-head Latent Attention (MLA). Normalized-depth mapping transfers profiles within model families, enabling small-model measurements to guide larger-model allocations before pretraining. A brief analysis relates selection and transfer to score calibration and inter-layer interactions. From-scratch experiments demonstrate validation-loss improvements over available budget-matched baselines, while analytical resource accounting identifies favorable quality--storage--compute trade-offs for physical MLA. These results connect positional demand to transferable, efficient architecture design.\n         \n       \n     \n     \n       [300] \n       \n        arXiv:2610.39265\n       \n          (cross-list from cs.CV)\n\n        [ pdf ,  html ,  other ]\n     \n     \n       \n          Title: \n          DeCoRL: Decomposed Consistency Reinforcement Learning for Multi-Image Composition\n         \n          Zhiqiang Wu ,  Shuang Sun ,  Jiale Zhang ,  Jing Li ,  Yibo Chen ,  Xu Tang ,  Yao Hu  \n\n          Comments: \n          NeurIPS\n         \n\n\n          Subjects: \n           Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV) ; Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)\n         \n\n         \n          Multi-image composition (MICo) is a critical yet challenging capability for large-scale image editing models, where consistency degrades rapidly as the number of reference images increases. While supervised fine-tuning (SFT) can equip models with basic MICo ability, it provides only implicit supervision on the composed image and often fails to learn reliable object-level correspondence across multiple references. We propose DeCoRL, a reinforcement-learning (RL) framework that improves MICo consistency by decomposing holistic MICo consistency into object-level consistency. DeCoRL first establishes instruction-relevant object correspondences across reference images and localizes the corresponding regions in the generated image. It then uses a VLM-based reward model trained on single-object consistency annotations to produce fine-grained, object-wise feedback, which is aggregated into a multi-object consistency reward to guide RL post-training. We further introduce DeCo-Bench, a benchmark covering compositions from 2 to 6 reference images. Experiments on DeCo-Bench and the public MICo-Bench demonstrate consistent improvements in MICo consistency.\n         \n       \n     \n     \n       [301] \n       \n        arXiv:2610.39278\n       \n          (cross-list from cs.CV)\n\n        [ pdf ,  html ,  other ]\n     \n     \n       \n          Title: \n          WorldFlow: Joint Video and 3D Motion Prediction via Large-Scale Pretraining\n         \n          Yuhao Lu ,  Jiongxu Chen ,  Hexiang Wei ,  Jiajin Wu ,  Juelin Li ,  Mingjian Liang ,  Fan Yang ,  Zhuowei Liang ,  Zihao Liu ,  Zhennan Wang ,  Sheng Wang ,  Lin Shao  \n\n\n\n          Subjects: \n           Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV) ; Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI); Robotics (cs.RO)\n         \n\n         \n          Predicting future physical states stands as a central challenge in embodied intelligence. Existing video world models leave 3D dynamics implicit, action-based formulations remain tied to specific robot configurations, and 3D motion prediction models use flow-only supervision, providing a limited learning signal for physical dynamics. To circumvent these limitations, we introduce \\method{}, a generative world model that jointly predicts future video and query-based 3D trajectories. By parameterizing 3D motion as continuous displacement tokens aligned with the video grid, a unified Mixture-of-Transformers (MoT) backbone jointly denoises both modalities under a rectified-flow objective. This formulation couples explicit geometric dynamics with visual evolution, effectively unlocking the physical commonsense embedded within pretrained video models. To empirically scale and validate this unified architecture, we train \\method{} on a heterogeneous mixture of 1.44 million object-level samples spanning simulated and real-world domains. We further introduce WorldFlowBench, comprising 1,678 evaluation samples from 11 data sources across simulated and real-world interactions, including spatial reasoning tasks. Across all three evaluation sets, WorldFlow achieves the lowest average displacement error (ADE) and final displacement error (FDE) among the evaluated learning-based methods. Notably, on the challenging SpatialCog set, WorldFlow achieves an ADE of 0.060 m, a 54.2\\% reduction compared with the strongest external baseline. It also reduces ADE and FDE by 55.9\\% and 68.5\\%, respectively, compared with its flow-only variant. Project page:  this https URL .\n         \n       \n     \n     \n       [302] \n       \n        arXiv:2610.39295\n       \n          (cross-list from cs.LG)\n\n        [ pdf ,  html ,  other ]\n     \n     \n       \n          Title: \n          There Is No Free Lunch in RLVR: Every pass@1 Gain Has a pass@k Price\n         \n          Yinuo Liu  \n\n          Comments: \n          19 pages, 4 figures. Code and verification package (34/34 automated checks):  this https URL \n         \n\n\n          Subjects: \n           Machine Learning (cs.LG) ; Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI); Optimization and Control (math.OC); Machine Learning (stat.ML)\n         \n\n         \n          Reinforcement learning with verifiable rewards (RLVR) makes a language model solve more problems on the first try and fewer when given many tries: it raises pass@1 while the base model overtakes it at large sampling budget k. Is this a failure of RL to add reasoning, or a transient that more training removes? We settle the question with a theorem. The reversal is a price, and we compute its exact rate. Modeling RLVR as an exponential tilt of a base policy over strategies shared across prompts, we prove that under exclusive specialization every update that raises pass@1 strictly lowers the success of some prompt; a strictly positive price on prompts (Stiemke duality) is the exact condition, and being merely the best strategy on a prompt is provably not enough. When the loss reaches the hardest prompts, it forces a pass@k crossover, and the number of crossings is at most the sign variation of the shift in per-prompt success, a totally positive bound that is tight and that recovers the known single-crossover result as its order-preserving case. The crossover is not a bug to patch: a constrained update traces the exact pass@1 versus pass@k frontier, and removing the crossover entirely costs the entire pass@1 gain. The same tilt identity yields entropy collapse and the entropy-performance law with an identifiable exponent, unifying two literatures under one mechanism. Reinforcement cannot widen the reasoning frontier; it can only move along it, at a price we make measurable by a diagnostic that reads the bound, the order-preserving test, and the price from any evaluation log. There is no free lunch, only a price.\n         \n       \n     \n     \n       [303] \n       \n        arXiv:2610.39305\n       \n          (cross-list from cs.LG)\n\n        [ pdf ,  html ,  other ]\n     \n     \n       \n          Title: \n          Learning to Bound: Monotonic, Calibrated Neural Upper Bounds for MPE Inference\n         \n          Akshay Vyas ,  Hailiang Dong ,  Shivvrat Arya ,  Brij Malhotra ,  Tahrima Rahman ,  Vibhav Gogate ,  Nicholas Ruozzi  \n\n          Comments: \n          37 pages, 4 figures\n         \n\n\n          Subjects: \n           Machine Learning (cs.LG) ; Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)\n         \n\n         \n          Probabilistic graphical models (PGMs) compactly encode structured joint distributions, but Most Probable Explanation (MPE), which finds the highest-probability assignment to unobserved variables, remains computationally intractable in large, densely structured models. Anytime systematic search, such as branch-and-bound (B&B), is a popular approach, but its efficiency depends on tight admissible upper bounds that are often prohibitively expensive to compute using classical methods like Mini-Bucket Elimination (MBE). We introduce Learning to Bound (L2B), a data-driven framework that amortizes bound computation cost by training a neural predictor. L2B targets two key properties required for effective search bounds: admissibility, which is achieved via statistical calibration techniques, and progressive tightening as evidence accumulates, which is enforced through structural architectural constraints that ensure monotonicity by construction. Across challenging benchmarks, L2B delivers tighter, monotonic bounds at orders-of-magnitude lower cost on every PGM family, and yields measurable improvements when integrated with branch-and-bound on high-treewidth instances.\n         \n       \n     \n     \n       [304] \n       \n        arXiv:2610.39331\n       \n          (cross-list from cs.CV)\n\n        [ pdf ,  html ,  other ]\n     \n     \n       \n          Title: \n          Telling Is Not Seeing: Textual Personas Rarely Make Vision-Language Models Answer Like the People They Describe\n         \n          Jiawei Chen  \n\n\n\n          Subjects: \n           Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV) ; Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)\n         \n\n         \n          Vision-language models (VLMs) are increasingly prompted with personas, such as \"You have low vision\", to stand in for people in user studies. We ask whether a persona makes a model see like the person it describes. We compare persona answers with measured people with low vision, color blindness or hearing loss, and with simple rules that use only the facts the persona states. The persona never beats these rules, and it erases the real differences between groups and individuals. Describing blur leaves recognition unchanged in all 14 models we test, while blurring the photo lowers it as it does for people. Inside open models, the description changes neither the image information nor the answer probabilities. Yet the models know the effects they fail to show: they can estimate how blur and illusions affect people. Telling is not seeing: a persona makes a model say what it knows about people, not see what they see.\n         \n       \n     \n     \n       [305] \n       \n        arXiv:2610.39341\n       \n          (cross-list from cs.LG)\n\n        [ pdf ,  html ,  other ]\n     \n     \n       \n          Title: \n          RetroChessBench: A Benchmark for Reconstructing and Proving Chess Histories\n         \n          Tidor-Vlad Pricope  \n\n          Comments: \n          22 pages, 2 figures. Benchmark and RL environment:  this https URL \n         \n\n\n          Subjects: \n           Machine Learning (cs.LG) ; Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)\n         \n\n         \n          Frontier language models such as GPT 6 Astra have contributed to research mathematics, demonstrating substantial reasoning capabilities. Yet serious reasoning failures persist even in tasks governed by explicit rules. We demonstrate this in retrograde chess which is a playground for pure reasoning, where solvers reconstruct legal histories and distinguish possible events from forced ones. We introduce RetroChessBench, a 12-task benchmark covering exact-length constructions, unique endings, and exhaustive answer sets, with references, rubrics, and a terminal-reward environment. In two trials per task and condition, GPT 6 Astra High fails to produce the required final answer in 22/24 attempts without tools and 18/24 with code execution; mean terminal RL rewards are 14.75% and 27.19%, respectively. The examples identify faulty deductions: failed responses reject legal histories and treat temporary obstructions as permanent. Online retrograde archives often provide puzzles and final move sequences without detailed derivations. We hypothesize that limited exposure to these missing explanations in web-derived training data contributes to the observed reasoning failures. This questions how far frontier mathematical reasoning really generalizes beyond familiar solution patterns. Claims of general intelligence require evidence that models can transfer learnt principles to areas with few worked examples.\n         \n       \n     \n     \n       [306] \n       \n        arXiv:2610.39356\n       \n          (cross-list from cs.CV)\n\n        [ pdf ,  html ,  other ]\n     \n     \n       \n          Title: \n          Semantic Mismapping in Generalized Category Discovery: Evaluation and Mitigation\n         \n          Zitai Wang ,  Qianqian Xu ,  Cong Hua ,  Xi Chen ,  Zhiyong Yang ,  Xiaochun Cao ,  Qingming Huang  \n\n\n\n          Subjects: \n           Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV) ; Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI); Machine Learning (cs.LG)\n         \n\n         \n          Generalized Category Discovery (GCD) aims to perform old-category recognition and novel-category discovery in the same unlabeled set. Early methods address both objectives through clustering and evaluate the exchangeable predicted indices with global Hungarian (GH) matching. Recent methods adopt trainable parametric heads, using supervision for old-category recognition and discovery-oriented objectives for new categories. In this way, the output space becomes partially exchangeable: old indices are semantically anchored, while new indices remain exchangeable. However, these methods still apply global matching, which can reassign anchored old indices to other categories and hide some prediction errors. Empirically, we are surprised to observe that this reassignment can induce an overestimation of new-category accuracy by up to 25.63 points. We term this phenomenon semantic mismapping and further derive a permutation-cycle condition that characterizes when semantic mismapping can increase the global matching score. To fix this issue, we propose Semantics-Preserving Evaluation (SPE), which preserves the supervised semantics of old indices and restricts Hungarian matching to the new region. Furthermore, we propose Mismapping-Mitigating Optimization (MMO) to alleviate the prediction errors that can give rise to semantic mismapping, combining a region-balance loss (RBL) for old/new routing with a novel-diversity loss (NDL) that encourages orthogonality among new prototypes. Theoretically, we show that minimizing the two losses can tighten an upper bound on spurious accuracy gains from semantic mismapping, with controllable residual terms. Empirical results under both GH and SPE demonstrate the effectiveness of our proposed method.\n         \n       \n     \n     \n       [307] \n       \n        arXiv:2610.39372\n       \n          (cross-list from cs.CL)\n\n        [ pdf ,  html ,  other ]\n     \n     \n       \n          Title: \n          Cord: Confidence-Anchored Order Residual Decoding for Masked Diffusion Language Models\n         \n          Chengwei Zhou ,  Zhaoyan Jia ,  Massoud Pedram ,  Gourav Datta  \n\n\n\n          Subjects: \n           Computation and Language (cs.CL) ; Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)\n         \n\n         \n          The decoding order of masked diffusion language models (dLLMs) can substantially affect generation quality, yet common decoders typically apply the same reveal rule throughout generation. Confidence provides a strong default, but it measures how decisively a position can be predicted now, not how useful revealing that position will be for subsequent decoding. We propose Cord, a lightweight prompt-conditioned policy that retains confidence decoding as a stable reference and learns when and how to depart from it through geometric residuals over generation direction, proximity to revealed context, and two-sided infilling. The residuals adapt to both the prompt and the evolving partial response. Rather than sampling a new policy action at every decoding step, Cord samples a single low-dimensional coefficient matrix per rollout, which induces an entire state-dependent reveal trajectory. This yields a contextual-bandit formulation trained directly from sequence-level rewards, without step-level credit assignment or backbone updates. Across three dLLMs and five reasoning, code, and constraint-satisfaction benchmarks, Cord improves average task success from 32.0% under confidence decoding to 48.2%, and further improves over the best per-domain fixed residual. Behavioral audits show that the learned residuals induce substantial, task-dependent changes in reveal locality and two-sided infilling. Cord adds only $0.53$M trainable parameters, requires no additional backbone forward passes, and incurs only 5.39% inference latency overhead.\n         \n       \n     \n     \n       [308] \n       \n        arXiv:2610.39373\n       \n          (cross-list from cs.CL)\n\n        [ pdf ,  html ,  other ]\n     \n     \n       \n          Title: \n          What Makes Medical Examination Items Difficult? Using Large Language Models to Explore Patterns Associated With Empirical Item Difficulty\n         \n          Xiaomeng Xiong ,  Marcus Walker ,  Yanlin Jiang  \n\n\n\n          Subjects: \n           Computation and Language (cs.CL) ; Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)\n         \n\n         \n          Research on medical item difficulty has largely focused on prediction rather than on identifying interpretable patterns associated with empirical difficulty. This study used empirical difficulty labels to guide large language models (LLMs) in inducing difficulty rubrics and examined how rubric guidance affected LLM difficulty judgments. Using training items and their empirical difficulty estimates from the United States Medical Licensing Examination (USMLE) and the Physician Assistant National Certifying Examination (PANCE), Claude Opus 5 induced separate rubrics for absolute and pairwise difficulty judgment. None of the induced rubrics treated surface features, such as stem length, option count, or images, as major sources of difficulty. Several patterns emerged across both examinations, including cue convergence, misleading cues, and option competition, although their specific forms differed. Retrieval of knowledge that could not be derived from the stem was particularly prominent in the PANCE rubrics, whereas the USMLE rubrics placed greater emphasis on integrating multiple clinical cues. We compared commercial and open-weight LLMs on held-out items under unguided and rubric-guided conditions. Rubric guidance changed how models used the difficulty scale but did not consistently improve performance. In absolute judgments, guidance encouraged broader use of the scale and generally improved coarse-grained agreement, without consistently improving rank ordering. In pairwise comparisons, its effects varied across models and examinations, and judgments became more sensitive to presentation order. These findings suggest that the value of label-guided rubric induction may lie more in generating interpretable, testable hypotheses from empirical difficulty data than in reliably improving difficulty judgments.\n         \n       \n     \n     \n       [309] \n       \n        arXiv:2610.39380\n       \n          (cross-list from cs.AR)\n\n        [ pdf ,  html ,  other ]\n     \n     \n       \n          Title: \n          Learning to Formulate: Agentic Hardware-Software Co-Design of AI Infrastructure through Progressive Optimization and Verification\n         \n          Yi Ding ,  Li Shang  \n\n\n\n          Subjects: \n           Hardware Architecture (cs.AR) ; Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI); Distributed, Parallel, and Cluster Computing (cs.DC)\n         \n\n         \n          AI infrastructure, the chips and software that train and serve AI models, is designed in layers. Layers make the system manageable, but they also fix which choices an optimizer can consider. Gains that need the chip and its software to change together are excluded before optimization begins: the formulation leaves them out. Language-model agents make it possible to revisit these boundaries, but an agent's proposal cannot show by itself that a design is correct or optimal. We present Formulaic, a flow that divides the work. The agent writes the formulation, a mathematical solver optimizes within it, and verification and measurement check its assumptions and results. The agent refines the problem step by step, from the computation graph toward the chip. Each step is a template: a family of programs with the conditions under which they are correct. Wall relaxation searches the chip by pricing the hardware limits that bind the software. This paper takes a first step, through separate studies rather than one end-to-end run. On NVIDIA GB10, templates the agent wrote run complete Qwen3-8B layers $1.075\\times$ faster than SGLang. In a simulated Ventus GPU study, joint design cuts the worst-scenario cycles of Qwen2.5-0.5B to 0.428 of the reference, against 0.934 for software alone. On a five-step GEMM refinement chain, reusing proofs makes checking $2.51\\times$ faster.\n         \n       \n     \n     \n       [310] \n       \n        arXiv:2610.39385\n       \n          (cross-list from cs.RO)\n\n        [ pdf ,  html ,  other ]\n     \n     \n       \n          Title: \n          UMI-Live for In-The-Wild Bimanual Robotics Data Collection\n         \n          Govind Velamoor ,  Adrian Lin ,  Sam Jung ,  Spencer Mateega ,  Brandon Y. Yang ,  Amish Sethi  \n\n\n\n          Subjects: \n           Robotics (cs.RO) ; Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)\n         \n\n         \n          Universal Manipulation Interface (UMI) devices allow human experts to demonstrate tasks for robot learning without teleoperating physical robots. Using these demonstrations as training data still requires a consistent hardware setup, calibration, and data processing. We present UMI-Live, a full-stack open-source bimanual UMI system for in-the-wild data collection. We design our custom UMI-Live hardware to integrate camera and LiDAR--inertial sensing, onboard compute, and power, without external tracking equipment or calibration within the collection environment. We implement a simple user interface for recording data while an asynchronous pipeline performs parallel SLAM reconstruction and publishes training-ready datasets. We also release software that supports dual-YAM teleoperation and VLA policy training and inference. We validate UMI-Live data quality by fine-tuning $\\pi_{0.5}$ and MolmoAct2, achieving success rates of 10/10 and 8/10, respectively on a bimanual pick-and-place task. Furthermore, we release 40 hours of UMI-Live data across three tasks, and complete hardware designs, software, and reproduction guides at our $\\href{ this https URL }{\\text{project website}}$.\n         \n       \n     \n     \n       [311] \n       \n        arXiv:2610.39395\n       \n          (cross-list from cs.AR)\n\n        [ pdf ,  html ,  other ]\n     \n     \n       \n          Title: \n          OPERA: OPamp Design by an Expert-Taught Reasoning Agent, from Specifications to Layout\n         \n          Vishal Saxena ,  Soumyajit Mandal  \n\n          Comments: \n          5 pages, 3 figures, submitted to ISCAS 2027\n         \n\n\n          Subjects: \n           Hardware Architecture (cs.AR) ; Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)\n         \n\n         \n          Analog flows from specifications to a signed-off layout rarely run without manual iteration. Recent large language model (LLM) agents size circuits with optimizer loops over the device dimensions and stop at the schematic. We present OPERA (OPamp design by an Expert-taught Reasoning Agent), in which a general-purpose LLM agent designs CMOS opamps from specifications to post-layout sign-off with open-source tools. First, the designer teaches the agent the gm/ID method: map the specifications to transconductances and minimum currents, choose gm/ID per device role from characterized lookup tables, and size from equations, leaving a few knobs to search and an explainable design. Second, the agent learns the gLayout framework and taught layout practice, and writes a reusable layout generator that closes the layout and extraction loop at designer-approved gates. In SKY130, the agent designed a shared constant-gm bias and six opamps (5T, folded-cascode and telescopic OTAs; Miller, folded-cascode and telescopic two-stage) for fun >= 50 MHz, PM >= 60 degrees, SR 25 V/{\\mu}s into 1 pF and a minimum dc gain of 30 to 60 dB. All six layouts are DRC-clean and LVS-matched, and their extracted netlists meet every target at all 15 PVT corners ( fun >= 53.7 MHz, PM >= 62.1 degrees, SR >= 27.9 V/{\\mu}s). The benchmark netlists are released at  this https URL .\n         \n       \n     \n     \n       [312] \n       \n        arXiv:2610.39403\n       \n          (cross-list from cs.CV)\n\n        [ pdf ,  html ,  other ]\n     \n     \n       \n          Title: \n          SymKL: A Unified Representation-Space Measure of Signal Degradation\n         \n          Dhananjaya Jayasundara ,  Sudarshan Rajagopalan ,  Vishal M. Patel  \n\n\n\n          Subjects: \n           Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV) ; Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)\n         \n\n         \n          Quantifying degradation between a pristine signal and its degraded observation is fundamental to signal processing, yet existing full-reference measures are inherently modality-specific. We introduce SymKL, a unified full-reference degradation measure based on implicit neural representations (INRs). An INR is fitted once to the pristine reference and characterized by its diagonal Fisher information, which captures the local information geometry of the learned representation. Evaluating the Fisher information with the pristine reference and a degraded observation yields two parameter-sensitivity profiles whose symmetric KL divergence defines the degradation score. The proposed formulation requires only the reference and degraded observation, without knowledge of the corruption process or degradation severity, making it applicable across images, audio, and 3D data. We further derive an exact closed-form expression for the diagonal Fisher of SIREN, enabling efficient computation. Experiments on image, speech, and volumetric 3D benchmarks demonstrate that SymKL accurately tracks degradation severity, remains monotonic where conventional metrics saturate or fail, and provides a unified modality-agnostic measure of signal degradation. Code will be made publicly available after the review process.\n         \n       \n     \n     \n       [313] \n       \n        arXiv:2610.39409\n       \n          (cross-list from cs.LG)\n\n        [ pdf ,  html ,  other ]\n     \n     \n       \n          Title: \n          Safe Multi-Objective Reinforcement Learning: Zero Constraint Violation and Primal-Averaging Convergence\n         \n          Chenxi Li ,  Yao Shu ,  Siyang Gao ,  Shuang Qiu ,  Guangxin Jiang  \n\n\n\n          Subjects: \n           Machine Learning (cs.LG) ; Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI); Optimization and Control (math.OC); Machine Learning (stat.ML)\n         \n\n         \n          Many applications of multi-objective reinforcement learning (MORL) require policies that balance conflicting objectives while satisfying safety constraints. We study safe MORL with zero constraint violation throughout learning, covering both cumulative and stepwise constraints. A key challenge is to explore sufficiently under unknown dynamics and constraint costs without violating the constraints. For a given preference over objectives, we develop an algorithm to learn a safe Pareto optimal policy by combining optimistic reward estimates with pessimistic cost bounds. With high probability, every policy executed over $T$ episodes incurs zero constraint violation. The algorithm returns a safe Markov policy induced by the averaged occupancy measure and achieves an $\\widetilde{O}(1/\\sqrt{T})$ suboptimality gap under smooth Tchebycheff scalarization. We further develop a preference-free algorithm that first explores safely without a prespecified preference and then computes a safe policy for any given preference without further interaction. Over $T$ exploration episodes and $K$ planning iterations, it retains the same safety guarantee and achieves an $\\widetilde{O}(1/\\sqrt{T}+1/\\sqrt{K})$ suboptimality gap. Both algorithms require knowledge of a policy that strictly satisfies the constraints and its costs. We then relax these requirements, incurring at most constant cumulative violation. To our knowledge, we provide the first sample-efficient MORL algorithms that guarantee safety for every executed policy.\n         \n       \n     \n     \n       [314] \n       \n        arXiv:2610.39417\n       \n          (cross-list from cs.SE)\n\n        [ pdf ,  html ,  other ]\n     \n     \n       \n          Title: \n          Well-Specified and Well-Tested? Assessing SWE-bench-Style Datasets with a Self-Evolving Agent\n         \n          Zhonghao Jiang ,  Xuyang Zheng ,  Yanfu Yan ,  Zhongxin Liu ,  Lingfeng Bao ,  Shuiguang Deng  \n\n\n\n          Subjects: \n           Software Engineering (cs.SE) ; Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI); Computation and Language (cs.CL)\n         \n\n         \n          Large language models (LLMs) have shown strong performance on software engineering (SE) tasks, driving the need for high-quality datasets for reliable training and evaluation. SWE-bench-style datasets (SWE datasets) are widely used to evaluate LLM-based coding agents on real-world software issues, where task clarity and test coverage directly affect whether instances are solvable and verifiable. However, quality issues persist in existing SWE datasets, and manual annotation is costly and labor-intensive, motivating LLM-based annotation. Existing approaches typically rely on static, overly general rubrics, limiting their ability to handle diverse and nuanced scenarios and often yielding high false positive rates. In contrast, human annotators draw on accumulated experience to make context-aware judgments. Inspired by this, we propose ExLabel, an LLM-based, self-evolving, experience-guided assistant for task clarity and test coverage assessment in SWE datasets. ExLabel maintains an evolving experience bank that distills cross-instance knowledge into stable yet adaptive judgment criteria and reuses relevant experience to guide the assessment of new instances. For systematic evaluation, we construct SWE-TQ, a new benchmark spanning eight programming languages, by cleaning human annotations from SWE-bench and Multi-SWE-bench. Experiments with three backbone LLMs show that ExLabel achieves the best F1 across all settings, with relative improvements of 44.2%/49.9% and 24.0%/18.4% over the best baseline for task clarity and test coverage assessment on the Python/Multilingual subsets, respectively. Moreover, a coding model fine-tuned on SWE-rebench data filtered by ExLabel resolves 66.2% of issues on SWE-bench Verified, outperforming the model trained on data filtered by the best baseline by 14.5%.\n         \n       \n     \n     \n       [315] \n       \n        arXiv:2610.39423\n       \n          (cross-list from cs.CV)\n\n        [ pdf ,  html ,  other ]\n     \n     \n       \n          Title: \n          FDSML: Formula-Driven Supervised Metric Learning with Parameter-Aware Triplet Loss\n         \n          Hina Otake ,  Shinei Arakawa ,  Keitaro Tanaka ,  Yoshihiro Fukuhara ,  Hirokatsu Kataoka ,  Shigeo Morishima  \n\n          Comments: \n          Accepted to WACV 2027. 24 pages (11 main + 13 supplementary). Project page:  this https URL \n         \n\n\n          Subjects: \n           Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV) ; Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI); Machine Learning (cs.LG); Neural and Evolutionary Computing (cs.NE)\n         \n\n         \n          Formula-Driven Supervised Learning (FDSL) enables vision pre-training without real images, but existing methods cast pre-training as classification on discretized labels, discarding continuity in the generative process. We propose Formula-Driven Supervised Metric Learning (FDSML), which derives relational supervision from image-generation parameters beyond discrete class labels. FDSML combines FineFractalDB, our refined fractal dataset with higher intra-class variation and more stable structures, with a Parameter-Aware Triplet (PA-Triplet) Loss that assigns positives and negatives according to distances in the underlying Iterated Function System parameter space. This objective trains embeddings to respect parameter-derived relative similarity orderings. Experiments show that FDSML improves transferability to image retrieval while remaining competitive on downstream classification and improving robustness under several corruption settings. On ResNet-50, Recall@1 improves from 65.6% to 74.0% on Stanford Online Products and from 56.2% to 78.4% on Stanford Cars over FDSL pre-training. These results suggest that parameter-derived relational supervision provides a stronger synthetic pre-training signal than label-based classification for transfer beyond recognition.\n         \n       \n     \n     \n       [316] \n       \n        arXiv:2610.39455\n       \n          (cross-list from cs.LG)\n\n        [ pdf ,  html ,  other ]\n     \n     \n       \n          Title: \n          Sparse Fine-Tuning for Parameter-Efficient Adversarial Training\n         \n          Zhaoxin Wang ,  Zihang Ding ,  Handing Wang  \n\n          Comments: \n          Accepted by NeurIPS 2026\n         \n\n\n          Subjects: \n           Machine Learning (cs.LG) ; Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)\n         \n\n         \n          Deep neural networks achieve strong performance across many tasks but remain vulnerable to adversarial perturbations. Adversarial training (AT) is one of the most effective defenses, yet it suffers from high memory cost on large models and the persistent clean-robust accuracy trade-off. Recent work introduces parameter-efficient fine-tuning, such as LoRA, into AT, but this constraint induces a notable robustness gap relative to full-parameter AT. In this work, we revisit parameter-efficient adversarial fine-tuning from a parameter space perspective. Through gradient analysis, we find that adversarial optimization is highly concentrated on a small subset of parameters, which we call Robustness-Critical Parameters (RCPs), suggesting that robustness is encoded in a sparse subspace. Building on this observation, we propose Sparse Adversarial Fine-Tuning (SAFT), which identifies RCPs via adversarial gradient saliency and performs AT by updating only these parameters while freezing the rest. Across architectures and datasets, SAFT outperforms LoRA-based methods in both clean and robust accuracy with fewer trainable parameters, and approaches or surpasses full parameter AT in robustness while updating only about 5\\% of parameters.\n         \n       \n     \n     \n       [317] \n       \n        arXiv:2610.39464\n       \n          (cross-list from cs.CL)\n\n        [ pdf ,  html ,  other ]\n     \n     \n       \n          Title: \n          Integrating Heterogeneous Echocardiography AI Outputs in Language Space: Learnable Late Fusion with a Supervised LLM\n         \n          Ryo Takizawa ,  Tempei Kabayama ,  Chiemi Yamazaki ,  Shun Kitamura ,  Ryo Matsuoka ,  Yuta Ando ,  Satoshi Kodera ,  Norihiko Takeda  \n\n\n\n          Subjects: \n           Computation and Language (cs.CL) ; Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)\n         \n\n         \n          View- and target-specific echocardiography AI models capture useful but complementary information and may not achieve sufficient predictive performance in isolation. A learned ensemble is a natural way to combine these outputs, but heterogeneous models typically require each output to be converted into a binary decision, probability, confidence score, or fixed-length feature vector. We developed a supervised large language model (LLM)-based late-fusion module that directly aggregates view-specific predicted finding statements and predicted measurements in language space and learns a case-level fusion function. In patient-level three-fold cross-validation of 859 examinations from 646 patients, the proposed method integrated heterogeneous outputs within a single framework, performed comparably to the best-performing reference strategy evaluated, and outperformed a general-purpose LLM without task-specific training. These results suggest that a supervised LLM can combine multiple upstream models without model-specific output conversion or feature engineering that requires medical knowledge.\n         \n       \n     \n     \n       [318] \n       \n        arXiv:2610.39468\n       \n          (cross-list from cs.SE)\n\n        [ pdf ,  other ]\n     \n     \n       \n          Title: \n          Soei: Structured Observability of Evidence and Intent for Multi-Agent AI Software Engineering\n         \n          Mohammed Nayeem Khan ,  K S Sarun Kumar  \n\n          Comments: \n          27 pages, 19 figures, 16 tables. Preprint v1.1\n         \n\n\n          Subjects: \n           Software Engineering (cs.SE) ; Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI); Multiagent Systems (cs.MA)\n         \n\n         \n          AI coding agents now do long, multi-step and multi-session software work with little supervision. When a developer comes back to that work, the useful question is not what the model said but what was actually done, by whom, and whether it is finished. We study this in three parts. First, we verify Agenttrail, an open-source local observability tool for coding agents, against its source at commit e4ba2da: nine documented constants and mechanisms hold at file-and-line level, two event pipelines are never unified, and its Map service has an unauthenticated local mutation surface. Second, we run a prototype of our system on 45 real coding-agent sessions (102,233 records, 20,577 tool executions, 2,336 human-to-agent episodes). With a blind-audited classifier (precision 83.3% and 96.7%) we estimate that 23.4% (95% CI 17.1 to 35.7) of code-editing episodes end with no independent check after the last edit, and that 57.8% of to-dos the agent marked complete after editing had no proof in between. A 10-minute timing heuristic for session hand-offs recovers 6 of the 24 session pairs that content lineage links. 42% of sessions run longer than 24 hours, 48.1% of transcript bytes lie past a 2 MiB replay bound, and 1,326 records in 16 sessions contain credential-like strings. Third, we propose Soei (Structured Observability of Evidence and Intent): an acknowledgement-aware event schema, a hash-chained local ledger, a W3C PROV-aligned graph, calibrated inference for attribution and lineage, and a verification layer that keeps a claim of completion apart from proof. On the same corpus the ledger is 20% of raw size, holds no content or credentials, verifies at about 126k events/s, detects tampering and replays deterministically. We position the work against concurrent 2026 proposals and define autonomy levels with evidence gates and harness metrics baselined on our data.\n         \n       \n     \n     \n       [319] \n       \n        arXiv:2610.39505\n       \n          (cross-list from cs.CV)\n\n        [ pdf ,  html ,  other ]\n     \n     \n       \n          Title: \n          Agent-Wise Test-Time Normalization for Camera-Based Cooperative Perception with Corrupted Collaborators\n         \n          Yu Wang ,  Shaohua Wang ,  Yicheng Li ,  Kecheng Sun  \n\n          Comments: \n          11 pages, 8 figures\n         \n\n\n          Subjects: \n           Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV) ; Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)\n         \n\n         \n          Intermediate-fusion cooperative perception lets a vehicle detect objects beyond its own field of view by fusing features shared by nearby agents. When only a collaborator's cameras are degraded by fog, motion blur or exposure errors, the features it shares drift away from the training distribution while the ego input stays clean, and cooperation can become worse than not cooperating at all. Test-time batch-normalization adaptation, the standard training-free remedy, estimates statistics over the pooled batch of all agents and therefore normalizes both the clean ego and the corrupted collaborator with a mixture of two distributions. We show that this mixing harms even the clean ego: when the ego shares BN statistics with a corrupted collaborator, its detection from its own features drops by 3.58 to 6.68 AP@0.5 points although its cameras are untouched. We propose AgentNorm, an agent-wise test-time normalization that i) estimates the normalization statistics of every collaborator from its own current observation and ii) anchors the ego to its source statistics, so that the ego path remains exactly the original model. AgentNorm has no learnable parameters, needs no gradients or labels, is independent of the order of test frames, and runs locally on each agent without changing the transmitted features. On camera-based OPV2V with four fusion checkpoints and collaborator-only corruptions, AgentNorm raises AP@0.5 under severity-5 fog from 29.42-37.25 to 43.32-58.86 and outperforms pooled normalization by 5.53-12.94 points under fog and motion blur, at an extra latency of 2.3 ms per frame. On the real-world DAIR-V2X-C dataset, it outperforms pooled normalization in all ten infrastructure-corruption settings. We also report the cases it does not fix: occlusion, which removes image content rather than shifting feature statistics, and the case in which all agents share the same shift.\n         \n       \n     \n     \n       [320] \n       \n        arXiv:2610.39532\n       \n          (cross-list from cs.CR)\n\n        [ pdf ,  html ,  other ]\n     \n     \n       \n          Title: \n          Judging Before Propagation: Task-Graph Contextual Integrity for Multi-Agent Privacy\n         \n          Hengyu Wu ,  Blaise Delattre ,  Yang Cao  \n\n\n\n          Subjects: \n           Cryptography and Security (cs.CR) ; Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)\n         \n\n         \n          Multi-agent LLM systems increasingly rely on persistent memory and inter-agent communication. These capabilities create privacy risks that extend beyond individual interactions: a release appropriate in isolation may become inappropriate when combined with information previously disclosed to a recipient or propagated to downstream agents along the planned workflow. Existing Contextual Integrity (CI) mechanisms for agentic systems typically rely on local judgments that assess each disclosure in isolation, with or without regard to the recipient's history, but do not account for how information propagates downstream. However, compliance at the current boundary does not guarantee appropriateness across the workflow. We introduce Task-Graph Contextual Integrity (TG-CI), to our knowledge the first task-graph-native formalization of Contextual Integrity for multi-agent systems. TG-CI represents a workflow as a Multi-Agent Task Graph (MATG) and extends CI from atomic transfers to stateful, graph-level information flows by jointly considering an item's intended propagation scope and recipients' evolving disclosure states. We operationalize TG-CI through propagation-scoped authorization under two complementary policies: Full-Scope, which requires admissibility across the complete downstream scope, and Auto-Region, which confines each item to its maximal admissible region using lightweight frontier gates. We further introduce MAPLE-Bench, a benchmark of 144 cases across four scenarios that jointly vary MATG topology, disclosure history, compromised-agent placement, and item-level privacy policies. In our primary experimental configuration, TG-CI reduces the mean unauthorized disclosure count by 85.8-94.1% relative to the Local-History CI baseline, while retaining 93.6-96.1% of the baseline's task accuracy, with the two enforcement policies providing complementary privacy--cost--utility trade-offs.\n         \n       \n     \n     \n       [321] \n       \n        arXiv:2610.39562\n       \n          (cross-list from cs.CL)\n\n        [ pdf ,  html ,  other ]\n     \n     \n       \n          Title: \n          Raincheck: Deferred Multimodal Prefill for Efficient Multi-Turn MLLM Inference\n         \n          Wenjie Huang ,  Jun Zhang ,  Hongyaoxing Gu ,  Haoyang Huang ,  Tianqi Xu ,  Xinyue Hao ,  Xinyi Hu ,  Xinle Du ,  Jun Dai ,  Shuang Ge ,  Chen Lei ,  Yue Li ,  Mingcheng Wan  \n\n\n\n          Subjects: \n           Computation and Language (cs.CL) ; Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)\n         \n\n         \n          Multi-turn conversations with multimodal large language models (MLLMs) reuse input context, but long multimodal inputs increase time to first token (TTFT). Query-aware hard pruning reduces prefill tokens but permanently excludes information that later questions may require. We propose Raincheck, a framework for deferred multimodal prefill that decouples initial token selection from later information availability. An existing pruning method selects tokens for initial prefill; Raincheck preserves and progressively prefills unselected tokens in source order using unused compute capacity during decode. Unfinished prefill continues after answer return during available scheduling gaps. Attention visibility control and affine state mapping enable context updates after generation begins in full-attention and hybrid recurrent models. Prefill-decode fusion reduces repeated weight reads. We evaluate Qwen3-VL-8B and Qwen3.5-9B with 30% initial multimodal-token retention. Across both models and four pruning methods, Raincheck recovers on average 77.7% and 60.4% of the quality lost to matched hard pruning on MultiVerse and ConvBench, respectively. On MultiVerse, the SGLang implementation reduces mean first-turn TTFT by up to 24.1% relative to full prefill, with less than 0.5% increase in mean time per output token (TPOT).\n         \n       \n     \n     \n       [322] \n       \n        arXiv:2610.39570\n       \n          (cross-list from cs.AR)\n\n        [ pdf ,  html ,  other ]\n     \n     \n       \n          Title: \n          LLM Agent-Based Design of RISC-V CPUs with FPGA-in-the-Loop Evaluation\n         \n          Leo Itoh ,  Junichiro Kadomoto  \n\n          Comments: \n          5 pages, 6 figures, 2 tables. Submitted to IEEE ISCAS 2027. Code and data:  this https URL \n         \n\n\n          Subjects: \n           Hardware Architecture (cs.AR) ; Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)\n         \n\n         \n          Long-run RTL simulation of CPU candidates can take hours, while short diagnostic runs may miss performance degradation that emerges later in execution. This evaluation cost can delay design decisions in LLM-agent-based CPU development. We present a development environment in which LLM agents implement CPU candidates on an FPGA and use on-board performance measurements to guide design decisions. We applied it to the human-supervised development of a single-cycle RV32I core into a three-wide out-of-order RV32IM core. The final design reaches 5.61 CoreMark/MHz at 130 MHz on a Artix-7 FPGA. For the same CPU candidate running 8,000 CoreMark iterations, FPGA evaluation, including implementation, was 9.6x faster than single-threaded RTL simulation. In one case, board measurements revealed long-run performance degradation in a candidate that improved in a short diagnostic run. The result guided candidate selection and led to RTL-based root-cause analysis and a fix.\n         \n       \n     \n     \n       [323] \n       \n        arXiv:2610.39571\n       \n          (cross-list from cs.HC)\n\n        [ pdf ,  other ]\n     \n     \n       \n          Title: \n          Generative artificial intelligence prompting strategies among Chinese university students across academic tasks\n         \n          Lei Fan ,  Fangxue Liu  \n\n\n\n          Subjects: \n           Human-Computer Interaction (cs.HC) ; Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)\n         \n\n         \n          Generative artificial intelligence (GenAI) is increasingly embedded in university learning, yet effective human-GenAI interaction depends not only on access to these tools but also on students' ability to formulate, evaluate, and refine prompts for academic tasks. This study examined university students' perceived GenAI prompting capability and reported prompting strategies. Questionnaire data from 234 students were analyzed using descriptive statistics, measurement modelling, scenario comparisons, structural associations, and qualitative coding of optional comments. Students reported using GenAI across diverse academic activities and generally expressed confidence in their prompting capabilities, particularly in providing relevant context and refining prompts following initial outputs. However, prompting strategies showed limited sensitivity to task types: specifying output format was more strongly endorsed in a creative workshop preparation scenario than in a research-oriented assignment, whereas other matched strategies showed no adjusted differences. Students also commonly endorsed both concise instructions and providing all relevant information at the outset, suggesting that effective brevity may depend more on selecting and organizing task-relevant information than on prompt length alone. By contrast, fixed prompt templates received lower endorsement than other prompting strategies. In the adopted factor model, perceived cognition showed the clearest unique association with perceived prompting capability, while open-ended responses highlighted prompt-intent alignment as a common challenge.\n         \n       \n     \n     \n       [324] \n       \n        arXiv:2610.39590\n       \n          (cross-list from cs.CL)\n\n        [ pdf ,  html ,  other ]\n     \n     \n       \n          Title: \n          Red-Teaming Large Language Models in Low-Resource African Languages: A Case Study of Yoruba and Nigerian Pidgin\n         \n          Paula Chikaodinaka Anyanwu ,  Valdini Douglace Lemofouet ,  Blessing Ngozi Uzor ,  Ifeoluwa Dorcas Awotunde ,  Danielle Blanche Kapsa ,  Tassallah Abdullahi ,  Clemencia Siro ,  Idris Abdulmumin ,  Seid Muhie Yimam ,  Shamsuddeen Hassan Muhammad  \n\n\n\n          Subjects: \n           Computation and Language (cs.CL) ; Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)\n         \n\n         \n          As large language models (LLMs) become more widespread in Africa, their safety in low-resource languages remains largely untested since most red-teaming work focuses on English. This study evaluates six state-of-the-art LLMs using 400 explicit adversarial prompts in Yoruba and Nigerian Pidgin, categorised into four harm categories. The responses were scored by native-speaker annotators and reliability was assessed using Cohen's $\\kappa$. While models that rejected up to 69% of English prompts rejected as few as 0% in Yoruba, this was not because they were safer, but because they failed to understand the prompts. Attack success rates increased for most models in the Nigerian languages, with GPT-4o Mini demonstrating the most significant cross-lingual safety discrepancy (+0.23 in Yoruba). When models did engage with adversarial prompts, many of the responses were harmful, with rates of harmful content reaching 0.63 in English and 0.50 in Nigerian Pidgin. The most difficult harm category to detect was cultural stereotypes and discrimination (ASR = 0.44 in Nigerian Pidgin). Comprehension in Yoruba was low across most models, and this directly suppressed apparent attack success without indicating actual safety. Gemini 2.0 Flash was the most consistent model across languages. Despite its explicit training in African languages, Aya 101 had the highest attack success rate in English (0.60) and one of the lowest Yoruba comprehension rates (0.28). These results demonstrate that English-centric safety alignment does not transfer to Nigerian languages, highlighting the urgent need for language-specific safety evaluations.\n         \n       \n     \n     \n       [325] \n       \n        arXiv:2610.39606\n       \n          (cross-list from cs.CY)\n\n        [ pdf ,  other ]\n     \n     \n       \n          Title: \n          Data Provenance and Ethics in the Curricula of U.S. Health AI Education Programs\n         \n          Raffaele Giancotti ,  Francesca Santucci ,  Amama Anjum ,  Monique de Villa ,  Rupsa Mitra ,  Soham Biren Katlariwala ,  Vishala Mishra ,  Leo Anthony Celi  \n\n          Comments: \n          10 pages, 1 figure, 3 tables. Under review at PLOS Digital Health. Data and supplementary material available at  this https URL \n         \n\n\n          Subjects: \n           Computers and Society (cs.CY) ; Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)\n         \n\n         \n          Failures of deployed clinical artificial intelligence (AI) systems have often originated in how their training data were generated. Whether the programs that train the health AI workforce teach practitioners to examine data provenance is unknown. We conducted a cross-sectional analysis of the publicly advertised curricula of health AI education programs at U.S. universities, identified through a structured multisource web search between April and July 2026. Two investigators coded coverage of data provenance (structural, dataset limitations, or data management only), ethics, and sustainability; collaborative structure; technical prerequisites; and the disciplinary background and gender of named instructors. The primary outcome was provenance coverage at the structural or dataset-limitations level. Of 43 candidate entries, 36 programs at 25 institutional units in 21 universities were included. Ethics was covered by 29 programs (80.6%; 95% confidence interval [CI], 65.0 to 90.2), data provenance by 8 (22.2%; 95% CI, 11.7 to 38.1), and structural provenance by 6 (16.7%; 95% CI, 7.9 to 31.9). None of the seven master's degree programs met the primary outcome, and no program addressed sustainability. Eighteen programs included team-based work, but only 5 assembled teams across clinical and technical backgrounds. Of 92 named instructors, 76.1% came from computational disciplines and 3.3% from the social sciences or humanities; among the 51 whose gender could be determined, 66.7% were men. U.S. health AI education programs widely taught ethics but rarely taught how clinical data are produced. None addressed the environmental cost of computation, and most instructors came from computational disciplines.\n         \n       \n     \n     \n       [326] \n       \n        arXiv:2610.39633\n       \n          (cross-list from cs.CV)\n\n        [ pdf ,  html ,  other ]\n     \n     \n       \n          Title: \n          Intrinsic Distribution Matching\n         \n          Deyuan Liu ,  Yansen Han ,  Kaiwen Zheng ,  Jun Xie ,  Jiacheng Liu ,  Zhenglin Cheng ,  Peng Sun ,  Jintao Zhang ,  Jianguo Li ,  Tao Lin  \n\n\n\n          Subjects: \n           Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV) ; Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)\n         \n\n         \n          One-step and few-step text-to-image generation must accurately render the details specified in the prompt despite limited opportunities for refinement. Distribution matching distillation (DMD) uses score differences to bring the generated distribution closer to a reference distribution. Following prior work, we estimate this difference with internal velocity branches, avoiding a separate fake-score network. We ask whether an explicit moment constraint improves this learned score supervision. A decomposition of the reverse-KL field into a Gaussian moment component and a non-Gaussian residual identifies statistics that can be constrained explicitly. We introduce Intrinsic Distribution Matching (IDM), which retains the full DMD correction and matches feature means and per-coordinate standard deviations using the generator's exponential-moving-average (EMA) copy, requiring no external feature encoder. Experiments use full-parameter and low-rank adaptation (LoRA) tuning on Z-Image-Turbo 6B and Qwen-Image 20B. At two network evaluations, IDM reaches 94.25% and 90.20% on Chinese and English LongText-Bench, the highest scores among the compared methods at this budget. Controlled ablations show gains from the added moment loss, while generator EMA features perform competitively with an external DINOv2 encoder across the five reported metrics. Code is available at  this https URL .\n         \n       \n     \n     \n       [327] \n       \n        arXiv:2610.39639\n       \n          (cross-list from cs.CV)\n\n        [ pdf ,  html ,  other ]\n     \n     \n       \n          Title: \n          Where Do Vision Language Models Fail at 3D Reasoning?\n         \n          Khai Le-Duc ,  Nguyen Kim Hai Bui ,  An Ngo ,  Nam D. Hoang ,  Yueqi Song ,  Jiarui Liu ,  Thai Quoc Hoang ,  Fang Wu ,  Chris Ngo ,  Terry Jingchen Zhang ,  Mona T. Diab ,  Ran Xu ,  Silvio Savarese ,  Xiao-Ming Wu  \n\n          Comments: \n          Preprint, 150 pages\n         \n\n\n          Subjects: \n           Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV) ; Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)\n         \n\n         \n          Vision-language models can get individual judgments right while struggling to recover and consistently use the exact properties of a 3D object. To characterize this gap, we introduce BedBench, a controlled benchmark of thirteen procedurally generated tasks spanning individual judgments and composite answers. Across twelve open-weight and four frontier models, per-color counting exposes a pronounced weakness: humans achieve 93.3% accuracy, compared with 7.2% for the best model. Targeted fine-tuning raises one model's accuracy from 3.0% to 68.6%, but reveals a further difficulty: its answers about the most common colors remain inconsistent with its own per-color counts. Supplying those counts as text improves its judgments, showing that recovering a property does not mean using it consistently. Reasoning, scaling, and added visual cues each improve individual judgments, but none makes exact answers reliable. To locate the difficulty, we probe Qwen3.5-27B: exact per-color counts remain recoverable at the language model's input, yet the model's answers rarely recover them (65.0% for the probe vs. 3.0% for the model). Image-removal controls further show that some apparent successes require no visual evidence. These findings motivate evaluating 3D reasoning by exact correctness, consistent use, and image dependence, rather than accuracy alone. Code and dataset are publicly available:  this https URL \n         \n       \n     \n     \n       [328] \n       \n        arXiv:2610.39641\n       \n          (cross-list from cs.DB)\n\n        [ pdf ,  html ,  other ]\n     \n     \n       \n          Title: \n          Rebuilding a Field's Open-Problem Catalog from the Literature: A Two-Layer Knowledge Graph, and Why Status Must Stay Proposed\n         \n          Madhulatha Mandarapu ,  Sandeep Kunkunuru  \n\n          Comments: \n          9 pages, 1 figure, 7 tables. Code and data:  this https URL \n         \n\n\n          Subjects: \n           Databases (cs.DB) ; Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI); Digital Libraries (cs.DL)\n         \n\n         \n          A research field records its open problems in surveys, keynotes and community reports. Those are prose, written every few years, and nobody can ask them who works on a problem now or whether it was settled last year. We argue the problems should be data, and we build the system that makes them so: a hand-curated problem layer carried in unchanged, under an evidence layer rebuilt from a bibliographic index, joined by typed, provenance-carrying links in one graph database and queryable from one language. A language model does four jobs -- per-paper extraction, catalog grounding, per-paper status verdict, and idea generation -- each constrained to a candidate set so out-of-set answers are refused rather than stored. We report a study on two curated catalogs, database research (1,053 problems) and AI research (1,000 problems), each with a prior catalog-only graph. The rebuilt graph answers every question the catalog-only graph answered -- 47 of 47 on databases, 34 of 35 on AI -- plus questions the catalog-only graph refused: recent workers, untouched problems, methods tried, and outside papers bearing on a problem (336 problems, 770 works on databases). The capability we most wanted does not survive its own audit. Of 41 solves and refutes verdicts hand-checked against the papers, 9 are correct, 30 partly correct and 2 wrong; no refutes verdict (0 of 34) disproves an open problem, and only 1 of 7 solves closes the central question. We report that failure in full and draw the design conclusion: a status change must be proposed with its evidence and never applied, making the human reviewer part of the architecture. We close with the data-engineering problems this raises: entity resolution across curated and harvested layers, verifying model status claims, freshness under source quotas, and evaluation without ground truth.\n         \n       \n     \n     \n       [329] \n       \n        arXiv:2610.39676\n       \n          (cross-list from cs.LG)\n\n        [ pdf ,  html ,  other ]\n     \n     \n       \n          Title: \n          Event-SAMA: Language-Grounded Video Object Segmentation on Neuromorphic Event Streams\n         \n          Sergei Fedorchenko ,  Max Zehnder ,  Nikola Zubić ,  Rong Zou ,  Davide Scaramuzza  \n\n          Comments: \n          15 pages\n         \n\n          Journal-ref: \n          Conference on Neural Information Processing Systems Workshops (NeurIPSW), 2026\n         \n\n          Subjects: \n           Machine Learning (cs.LG) ; Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI); Computation and Language (cs.CL); Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)\n         \n\n         \n          Event cameras offer microsecond temporal resolution, high dynamic range and no motion blur, but referring video object segmentation (RVOS) lacks event-stream datasets and models. We introduce Event-SAMA-239K, a synthetic-event conversion of the SAMA-239K grounded video-chat corpus and all additional SAMA training data: 30,650 video clips and 392,162 images. Optical-flow, FILM or saccade upsampling followed by a linear event generator preserves all mask, referring-expression and dialogue annotations. We also introduce Event-SAMA, adapting SAMA-4B with a two-channel polarity patch embedding initialised from pretrained RGB weights and a zero-initialised event adapter. End-to-end training yields 24.37 $\\mathcal{J}\\&\\mathcal{F}$ on MeViS valid_u and 22.33 $\\mathcal{J}\\&\\mathcal{F}$ on the converted ReVOS valid subset, establishing the first event-native RVOS baseline (RGB SAMA-4B: 55.4 on MeViS). Frozen RGB SAMA-4B with pseudo-RGB renderings or E2VID reconstructions of the same events scores 13.23 and 15.94 $\\mathcal{J}\\&\\mathcal{F}$. Without retraining, Event-SAMA works on real event-camera recordings when the accumulation window matches their event density. A seed-controlled ablation with a measured noise floor of 0.47 $\\mathcal{J}\\&\\mathcal{F}$ finds gains from diverse training sources over MeViS alone (+4.99), RGB-mean event-stem initialisation (+4.33), and a trainable SAM2 mask decoder (+2.76); the event adapter, partial ViT unfreezing and event polarity are within noise at this budget. An earlier, partially converted corpus snapshot trained with the identical recipe scores 1.38 $\\mathcal{J}\\&\\mathcal{F}$ higher on MeViS and 1.12 higher on ReVOS. Both are single runs that also differ in schedule length, so we draw no conclusion about corpus scale.\n         \n       \n     \n     \n       [330] \n       \n        arXiv:2610.39681\n       \n          (cross-list from cs.MA)\n\n        [ pdf ,  html ,  other ]\n     \n     \n       \n          Title: \n          Can AI Agents Interface Clinicians and Complex Models?\n         \n          Giulio Colombini ,  Matteo Falcioni ,  Mario Massimo ,  Francesco Durazzi ,  Marianna Santoro ,  Paolo Tubertini ,  Mirko Degli Esposti ,  Armando Bazzani  \n\n          Comments: \n          21 pages, 7 figures\n         \n\n\n          Subjects: \n           Multiagent Systems (cs.MA) ; Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)\n         \n\n         \n          Background: Contemporary healthcare institutions generate daily very large amounts of data, which contain highly useful information for the improvement of work organization, planning and overall efficiency. While models and techniques to extract these insights are well known and constantly developed in mathematical biology, complex systems physics and data science, the adoption of these techniques among Clinicians and Hospital Administrators remains limited, mainly due to language and methodological divides.\n Objective: In this article we implement an AI Multi Agent System (MAS) that allows Users to use predictive models and make statistical analyses via natural language conversations.\n Methods: We developed EpidemIA, a training-free MAS endowed with a modular suite of epidemiological models written by human experts, executable on a single-GPU workstation. To avoid relaying sensitive data to third parties, the system uses locally an open-weights LLM as backbone for its two subagents.\n Results: Using a dataset of Emergency Department visits provided to us by IRCCS Sant'Orsola (Bologna) we test the model on a few typical use cases, demonstrating the possibility of performing both simpler statistical analyses and visualizations as well as more complicated epidemiological predictions based on dynamical models, through simple queries made in natural language.\n Conclusions: While still being a proof-of-concept implementation, EpidemIA showcases the transformative capabilities of LLM based technologies as an enabling factor in the widespread adoption of complex dynamical models, which is at present hindered by their steep learning curve.\n         \n       \n     \n     \n       [331] \n       \n        arXiv:2610.39682\n       \n          (cross-list from cs.LG)\n\n        [ pdf ,  html ,  other ]\n     \n     \n       \n          Title: \n          Heterogeneous Co-Evolution Learning for Tool-Efficient Search Agents with Zero Labels\n         \n          Qin-Wen Luo ,  Sheng Ren ,  Xiang Chen ,  Rui Liu ,  Jun Fang ,  Naiqiang Tan ,  Jun Wang ,  Hua Chai ,  Sheng-Jun Huang  \n\n\n\n          Subjects: \n           Machine Learning (cs.LG) ; Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI); Information Retrieval (cs.IR)\n         \n\n         \n          Deep search agents extend language models by interleaving reasoning with external retrieval. However, effective search-agent training requires addressing two challenges beyond final-answer optimization. First, the capability frontier of agents is largely determined by training questions, whereas fixed or labeled task collections cannot adapt as agents improve. Second, outcome-only reinforcement learning treats all successful trajectories equally, even when some correct answers rely on redundant searches, creating an inherent tension between answer accuracy and tool efficiency. To address these issues, we introduce \\textsc{ZEST-Search}, a zero-label and tool-efficient framework for search-agent training via heterogeneous co-evolution. Starting from unlabeled reference questions, \\textsc{ZEST-Search} constructs validated search tasks online through an answer-first and evidence-path-guided generation process. A heterogeneous proposer adaptively constructs training tasks around the solver's evolving capability boundary, while the solver learns search-augmented reasoning through reinforcement learning. To jointly optimize task difficulty and search efficiency, \\textsc{ZEST-Search} employs an intermediate-difficulty reward for the proposer and a task-local tool-aware reward for the solver, encouraging informative tasks and correct answers with fewer redundant searches. Experiments on seven general-domain and multi-hop question answering benchmarks demonstrate that \\textsc{ZEST-Search} consistently improves search-agent performance while achieving higher tool efficiency without labeled search-training data or expert search trajectories. Further analyses show that heterogeneous generation, adaptive difficulty control, and tool-aware optimization enable more effective search-agent training.\n         \n       \n     \n     \n       [332] \n       \n        arXiv:2610.39685\n       \n          (cross-list from cs.SD)\n\n        [ pdf ,  html ,  other ]\n     \n     \n       \n          Title: \n          Practical Assessment of Agentic Systems in Acoustics\n         \n          Arne Hildenbrand ,  Philipp Diel ,  Zhuo Cao ,  Hanno Scharr ,  Stefan Pischinger ,  Steffen Marburg  \n\n\n\n          Subjects: \n           Sound (cs.SD) ; Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)\n         \n\n         \n          In engineering, applications of artificial intelligence have so far been largely limited to surrogate models and optimization tasks. Large language models changed this, as they can write software from a task given in words. Placed inside a harness, a system of components that lets it search for information and execute software, such a model becomes an agent that can take over recurring engineering tasks. This work measures how the model and the elements of the harness shape the performance and behavior of the agent in two common acoustic applications. In the first, the agent detects and locates faults in audio recordings from a vehicle interior with near-perfect accuracy. A benchmark on this task shows that a weak model sets a limit that no harness design can overcome, and that design choices take effect only once the model is capable enough to use them. In the second, a case study on the design of a metamaterial unit cell, the agent reliably finds designs that achieve a specified insertion loss over a given frequency range. The results aim to help the reader understand what each element of an agent contributes and how to design an agent for acoustic tasks.\n         \n       \n     \n     \n       [333] \n       \n        arXiv:2610.39711\n       \n          (cross-list from cs.DC)\n\n        [ pdf ,  html ,  other ]\n     \n     \n       \n          Title: \n          Sandman: Building and Operating Sandboxes for Agentic Reinforcement Learning on Shared Kubernetes\n         \n          Yiming Yan ,  Hongyang Wang ,  Wenbo Chen ,  Xuan Wang ,  Jingguo Liu ,  Xian Li ,  Chen Qi ,  Leo Fan ,  Siwei Li ,  Qianli Wu ,  Yichuan Li ,  Ruochen Wen ,  Qian Qu ,  Zheshen Wang ,  Benoit Dumoulin  \n\n          Comments: \n          42 pages, 6 figures, 53 tables\n         \n\n\n          Subjects: \n           Distributed, Parallel, and Cluster Computing (cs.DC) ; Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI); Machine Learning (cs.LG)\n         \n\n         \n          Agentic reinforcement learning (RL) often requires repeated tool calls in task-specific environments. A training job may create thousands of these environments in bursts while sharing a cluster with other jobs. We present Sandman, a sandbox service built and operated at Amazon for agent tool execution and evaluation. It serves multiple production agentic RL workloads on shared Kubernetes. A training framework supplies a task image and uses commands and file operations in the returned sandbox. Files and processes persist across calls until the framework releases the environment. Sandman manages creation, readiness and cleanup. Each sandbox is a Kubernetes pod with a small daemon mounted into the unmodified image. In a scale test, Sandman held 11,858 concurrent tiny sandboxes. Four production SWE-bench Verified training campaigns created 3.1 million sandboxes in October 2026. We report the design trade-offs and operational lessons from handling creation bursts, preparing task environments, reclaiming abandoned sandboxes, and running evaluation under controlled conditions. We will open-source Sandman's daemon, provider and pool.\n         \n       \n     \n     \n       [334] \n       \n        arXiv:2610.39742\n       \n          (cross-list from eess.AS)\n\n        [ pdf ,  html ,  other ]\n     \n     \n       \n          Title: \n          The Importance of Speech Genre for End-of-Turn Detector Training\n         \n          Rini Sharon ,  Kadri Hacioglu ,  Jeena Prakash ,  Andreas Stolcke  \n\n\n\n          Subjects: \n           Audio and Speech Processing (eess.AS) ; Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)\n         \n\n         \n          End-of-turn (EoT) detection has been studied mostly through features, models, and modalities, while the speech genre of the training data has drawn far less attention. We isolate this factor. Using one reproducible annotation pipeline, we build a matched pair of corpora, multi-turn human-human conversational and single-turn human-machine, and train the same streaming ConvLSTM EoT detector on each. Across four test sets the conversational model reaches up to +0.45 higher F1, driven by fewer false alarms on the mid-turn pauses and hesitations that are rare in single-turn corpora. This conversational-training effect is not specific to frame-level decoding. Under a matched complete-or-not test it also appears at the utterance level, across two encoders, but only on conversational test data. Testing only on single-turn speech, as most prior work does, therefore hides the benefit of conversational training data. Against open-source baselines, our 1.8M-parameter model attains the highest F1 on every test set.\n         \n       \n     \n     \n       [335] \n       \n        arXiv:2610.39768\n       \n          (cross-list from cs.LG)\n\n        [ pdf ,  other ]\n     \n     \n       \n          Title: \n          Multi-output LSTM soft sensors for industrial anaerobic co-digestion using LSTM-beta-VAE generative data augmentation\n         \n          Rohit Murali ,  Benaissa Dekhici ,  Tao Chen ,  Dongda Zhang ,  Michael Short  \n\n          Comments: \n          33 pages\n         \n\n\n          Subjects: \n           Machine Learning (cs.LG) ; Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI); Systems and Control (eess.SY)\n         \n\n         \n          Anaerobic digestion (AD) requires close monitoring of key process variables, including pH, the ratio of volatile organic acids to alkaline buffer capacity and biogas production, to maintain biological stability and prevent process failure. While mechanistic frameworks such as the Anaerobic Digestion Model No. 1 are highly parameterised, computationally expensive, and difficult to calibrate for real-time applications, data-driven Long Short-Term Memory (LSTM) networks offer a potentially more adaptable alternative. However, current data-driven approaches in AD are predominantly limited to single-output architectures that focus solely on production yields, as opposed to simultaneous prediction of vital stability indicators. Furthermore, industrial AD datasets are typically sparse and skewed toward steady-state operations. Baseline evaluations demonstrated this scarcity prevents standard algorithms (Random Forest, XGBoost) and unaugmented LSTMs from recognising the transition to unstable operations, resulting in poor estimation of stability indicators. To overcome these challenges, this study proposes a novel generative data augmentation framework using an LSTM-beta-Variational Autoencoder coupled with a multi-output LSTM (MO-LSTM) soft sensor. By synthesising physically valid sequences of these transitions to unstable operations from one year of full-scale plant data, the method safely populates sparse regions of the feature space. Trained on this augmented dataset, the MO-LSTM achieved robust simultaneous estimation across all targets. This approach mitigates data sparsity, providing a basis for hybrid mechanistic-data-driven modelling and early-warning soft sensors in industrial bioprocesses.\n         \n       \n     \n     \n       [336] \n       \n        arXiv:2610.39807\n       \n          (cross-list from cs.CR)\n\n        [ pdf ,  html ,  other ]\n     \n     \n       \n          Title: \n          InferD: A Honeypot Framework for AI Inference Infrastructure\n         \n          Hicham Hammouchi ,  Gabriele Lenzini  \n\n          Comments: \n          AISec Workshop '26, ACM CCS 2026, The Hague, Netherlands\n         \n\n\n          Subjects: \n           Cryptography and Security (cs.CR) ; Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)\n         \n\n         \n          Large language model serving stacks, commercial inference APIs, vector databases, and agent orchestration gateways are now ex- posed on the public internet and indexed by commodity scanners within hours of deployment. The threat characteristics of this sur- face are only beginning to be measured, and the honeypot frame- works that could measure it were designed for conventional net- work services rather than for AI infrastructure. In fact, emulating an AI service is a distinct deception problem: the observable behavior of the service, including model inventories, token formats, tool-call protocols, and authorization flows, must be reproduced faithfully, while the underlying computation, model inference and tool ex- ecution, must never occur. We present InferD, a low-interaction honeypot framework organized around a single principle we call deception without inference: every request an adversary can observe is answered from static response pools, and nothing an adversary submits is ever executed. InferD decomposes the emulation into four deception planes, transport, service protocol, vulnerability surface, and agentic session, adds a provenance plane that links a planted credential to evidence of downstream model-mediated processing.\n         \n       \n     \n     \n       [337] \n       \n        arXiv:2610.39835\n       \n          (cross-list from cs.CV)\n\n        [ pdf ,  html ,  other ]\n     \n     \n       \n          Title: \n          Aligning Medical Vision Language Models at the Capability Boundary with Entropy Adaptive Rubric Grounding\n         \n          Hongyu Bai ,  Jiaqi Zeng ,  Chunzheng Zhu  \n\n          Comments: \n          Accepted at ACM Multimedia Asia 2026 (MMAsia 2026). 10 pages, 4 figures\n         \n\n\n          Subjects: \n           Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV) ; Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)\n         \n\n         \n          Medical diagnostic reasoning requires verifiable links between visual evidence and clinical knowledge, yet current medical vision language models are aligned with objectives that reward the wrong behavior. Uniform supervised fine tuning lets easy cases dominate updates while clinically critical boundary cases saturate prematurely. Sparse rewards based only on final-answer correctness cannot assess anatomical grounding or clinical logic, inviting reward hacking through answer shortcuts. We propose MedCBR, a two-stage alignment framework coupling boundary-aware preference optimization with rubric-grounded reinforcement learning. First, we construct counterfactual boundary preferences at the model's capability frontier using empirical pass rates and preference pairs that share reasoning prefixes but diverge at decisive diagnostic branches. Difficulty Weighted Boundary Preference Optimization (DBPO) concentrates learning on this calibrated boundary region. Second, we distill reusable clinical rubrics from boundary trajectories and activate them according to prediction entropy. Entropy Gated Grounded Policy Optimization (EGPO) rewards answer correctness, anatomical grounding, and reasoning fidelity while suppressing rubric noise on reliable samples. A reference policy anchored to the boundary-aligned model couples population-level boundary priors with instance-level uncertainty, making diagnostic reasoning more accurate and clinically grounded. Experiments on six medical benchmarks show consistent improvements over strong general, specialized, and reinforcement learning baselines, reaching 61.2% average accuracy with the largest gains on challenging differential diagnosis tasks. Ablations show super-additive gains: combining the two stages exceeds the sum of their individual improvements.\n         \n       \n     \n     \n       [338] \n       \n        arXiv:2610.39837\n       \n          (cross-list from cs.LG)\n\n        [ pdf ,  html ,  other ]\n     \n     \n       \n          Title: \n          SHPRR: An Algorithm for Pure Exploration under Constrained Regret\n         \n          Georgina Nouli ,  Flore Sentenac ,  Maximilian Schiffer  \n\n\n\n          Subjects: \n           Machine Learning (cs.LG) ; Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)\n         \n\n         \n          Best Arm Identification (BAI) conventionally measures the cost of experimentation by the number of samples collected. In many operational settings, however, the cost of exploration relative to a trusted baseline already in place is equally important. We study BAI under both a fixed sampling horizon and a regret budget. Unlike generic resources considered in constrained pure exploration, regret has a distinctive structure: sampling better arms is less costly relative to the baseline. We theoretically show that the regret cost of exploration decreases across the phases of Sequential Halving, a widely used pure-exploration algorithm. This observation yields improved guarantees on the probability of identifying the best arm and motivates a more effective allocation of the regret budget. We propose Sequential Halving with Proportional Regret Rationing (SHPRR), which front-loads the regret budget to allow more exploration when many arms remain. Experiments on synthetic and real-world instances show that SHPRR improves best-arm identification performance over uniform regret allocation and existing baselines.\n         \n       \n     \n     \n       [339] \n       \n        arXiv:2610.39839\n       \n          (cross-list from cs.LG)\n\n        [ pdf ,  html ,  other ]\n     \n     \n       \n          Title: \n          Exploring the Potential of Symbolic Regression in Precision Livestock Farming\n         \n          Elisabeth Mayrhuber ,  Maciej Oczak ,  Michael Iwersen ,  David Brunner ,  Viktoria Dorfer ,  Stephan Winkler  \n\n\n\n          Subjects: \n           Machine Learning (cs.LG) ; Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)\n         \n\n         \n          The ability to predict farrowing time in pigs is crucial for optimal farm management and ensuring piglet survival. This study investigates the potential of explainable artificial intelligence for precision livestock farming, focusing on acceleration sensor data from sows around the time of farrowing. Accurately predicting farrowing allows farmers to balance both, piglet safety by avoiding piglet crushing events and the mother sow's welfare, by avoiding early crating. Traditional machine learning models often lack interpretability (``closed-box'' problem). Explainable artificial intelligence offers a solution by revealing the decision-making processes within the models. This work is split into two stages. First the detection of nest building behaviour and second the prediction of the remaining time to farrowing. The nest building detection was possible for 166 out of 180 sows and successful within 48 hours before farrowing for 149 sows. The nest building detection is purely based on the change of a sow's activity level and uses no machine learning techniques. Further, this study utilizes symbolic regression, an open-box modelling technique that generates interpretable mathematical formulas, to predict the remaining time until the onset of farrowing. The results are compared with closed-box models, like support vector regression and artificial neural networks, for both explainability and performance. Notably, symbolic regression achieved a mean absolute error of 9.28 hours for the time-to-farrowing prediction, outperforming the closed-box models in terms of performance and interpretability. This research demonstrates that explainable artificial intelligence, specifically symbolic regression, can offer both interpretable results and competitive performance in precision livestock farming.\n         \n       \n     \n     \n       [340] \n       \n        arXiv:2610.39848\n       \n          (cross-list from cs.CV)\n\n        [ pdf ,  html ,  other ]\n     \n     \n       \n          Title: \n          LiDAR-SHIELD: A Fully Dockerized Adversarial Robustness Assessment Framework for LiDAR-Based 3D Perception Models\n         \n          Alexandros Gkillas ,  Ioulia Kapsali ,  Christos Anagnostopoulos ,  Aris S. Lalos  \n\n\n\n          Subjects: \n           Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV) ; Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)\n         \n\n         \n          LiDAR-based 3D perception underpins autonomous driving and robotics, yet deploying these models safely requires stress-testing them against adversarial inputs before real-world use. Although state-of-the-art adversarial attacks exist, their implementations are often fragmented across individual repositories, each tied to a specific architecture or task and requiring manual environment reconstruction and model-specific integration code. We present LIDARSHIELD, a unified and fully Dockerized framework that reverses this workflow: rather than installing the target model inside the toolkit, LIDARSHIELD exposes any model through three lightweight HTTP endpoints, namely /info, /forward, and /gradient As a result, connecting a new model or dataset requires only a single loader function or adapter. The framework supports both 3D semantic segmentation and 3D object detection through the same engine-service protocol, integrating a task-aware suite of state-of-the-art attacks, including PGD and Square Attack for segmentation, and point perturbation, point removal, point injection, and LiDAttack for detection. This enables systematic exposure of corner cases and vulnerabilities across heterogeneous LiDAR backbones. The LiDAR-Shield codebase is publicly available at  this https URL .\n         \n       \n     \n     \n       [341] \n       \n        arXiv:2610.39869\n       \n          (cross-list from cs.HC)\n\n        [ pdf ,  html ,  other ]\n     \n     \n       \n          Title: \n          Incorporating Machine-Others: An Enactive Exploration of Human-LLM Interactions\n         \n          Elena C. Cuffari ,  Marta Pérez-Verdugo ,  Xabier E. Barandiaran  \n\n\n\n          Subjects: \n           Human-Computer Interaction (cs.HC) ; Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI); Computers and Society (cs.CY)\n         \n\n         \n          New concepts based in interactional thinking are required to understand how humans are changed by relating to LLMs. Standard theories of technological impact upon users' autonomy are inadequate here, because this technology is capable of inducing linguistic interactions with humans in an unprecedented manner. Traditional theories of social cognition also fall short, because LLMs have neither autonomy nor minds nor sociality as such. And yet, humans interact, talk, and create with them. We base ourselves on enactive theory, particularly on the theory of Linguistic Bodies and recent developments of enactive accounts of AI, to explore how we make sense with machines. We argue that we incorporate machine-others in two ways: in the tool-like sense of incorporation as enabling particular skills, but also in the more linguistic and identity-tied sense of incorporating (and incarnating) LLMs' ways of languaging. However, LLMs are themselves excorporated and excarnated phantoms of our own languaging; we give a (machinic) body to our language. As such, we are not just incorporating machinic others; we are incorporating excorporated others. These conceptual advances draw a new map of our linguistic relation to LLMs, putting them in continuation with other forms of languaging involving other linguistic artifacts (e.g., religious books). We attend to some particular forms that this relation takes today, and how they may be impacting the autonomy and becoming of LLM-engaged humans. We introduce the idea of \"susceptive sense-making\" to characterize increased semiotic labour that is carried by the lack of friction that we sometimes encounter within these services.\n         \n       \n     \n     \n       [342] \n       \n        arXiv:2610.39876\n       \n          (cross-list from cs.LG)\n\n        [ pdf ,  html ,  other ]\n     \n     \n       \n          Title: \n          Turing LLM Post-Training: Calibrated Distillation and Efficient Dynamic-MoE Execution\n         \n          Yue He ,  Jiahui Hu ,  Shaman Tang ,  Hua Zhou ,  Qiman Wu ,  Menglin Li ,  Yizhao Wang ,  Yuheng Zhang ,  Da Zhu ,  Zhou Wang ,  Anhua Liu ,  Hanlin Chen ,  Mingyuan Wang ,  Rui Xin ,  Yuhua Wei ,  Shuang Su ,  Siqi Liu ,  Kai Yang ,  Weichao Huang ,  Hang Zhang ,  Honggou Yang ,  Xianming Liu  \n\n\n\n          Subjects: \n           Machine Learning (cs.LG) ; Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)\n         \n\n         \n          We study post-training for dynamically routed mixture-of-experts (MoE) models, focusing on integrating independently optimized specialists while keeping consolidation efficient. Our approach combines calibrated multi-teacher on-policy distillation (C-MOPD), which matches each selected teacher on a local Top-$K$ support with teacher-wise loss normalization and clipping, with an execution path that uses post-capacity bounded-width dispatch, final-route replay, and asynchronous rollout--training overlap. On Turing-20B-A2B, the selected model achieves a four-domain normalized integration score of 0.9988 and retains at least 90.16\\% of the measured specialist gain in every domain. At matched $K=32$, the full calibration objective scores 0.9962, compared with 0.9724 without calibration and 0.9834 without clipping. At a matched 8K response cap, the model exceeds both shared SFT and Qwen3-8B on all four reasoning-enabled mathematics and code benchmarks (unweighted mean: 85.92, versus 80.33 for Qwen3-8B and 74.21 for SFT), and improves over SFT on ten of twelve broader evaluation metrics. In a PPU-based profile, the combined execution configuration reduces consolidation step time from 398.7 to 139.3 seconds, an observed 2.86$\\times$ speedup over serial execution. These results demonstrate strong specialist integration alongside more efficient consolidation, while reflecting the complete training and execution framework rather than any single component.\n         \n       \n     \n     \n       [343] \n       \n        arXiv:2610.39902\n       \n          (cross-list from cs.LG)\n\n        [ pdf ,  html ,  other ]\n     \n     \n       \n          Title: \n          CovFedAvg: Local Low-Loss Curves and Federated Model Selection\n         \n          Federico Lucchetti ,  Marcus Völp  \n\n\n\n          Subjects: \n           Machine Learning (cs.LG) ; Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)\n         \n\n         \n          Clients observing different parts of a prediction task may learn locally equivalent models with incompatible behavior outside their observed support. We study whether redundant models permit local low-loss paths toward a compatible shared model. CovFedAvg retains local models and curve controls on clients, fits a common endpoint through aggregated gradient contributions, and selects an actual candidate model using distributed training losses. No centralized calibration examples or client-to-client sample exchange are required. Exact selection guarantees no higher evaluated training loss than the previous model and the current-round FedAvg candidate; this is not a global-optimality, stationarity, generalization, or differential-privacy guarantee. We state conditional client-compatibility and approximate-selection bounds and retain corrected transformation identities and a separate full-gradient safeguard theorem for reference algorithms. A complementary input-support example recovers the shared absolute-value regression, while matched direct fine-tuning also solves it. Across a synthetic support task, recorded activity-recognition subjects, and participating and future gas-sensor batches, we evaluate five methods, five seeds, two network widths, and 20 rounds without calibration data. At width 16, CovFedAvg raises participating-Gas macro balanced accuracy from FedAvg's 88.88% to 89.84%; matched distributed fine-tuning reaches 91.65%. Results support feasibility and modest gains over FedAvg, but do not establish a curve-specific advantage over equally costly optimization. Exact-sum numerical experiments establish data flow; end-to-end privacy requires additional mechanisms and analysis.\n         \n       \n     \n     \n       [344] \n       \n        arXiv:2610.39923\n       \n          (cross-list from cs.CL)\n\n        [ pdf ,  html ,  other ]\n     \n     \n       \n          Title: \n          Prompts and probabilities in the linguistic assessment of language models: Evidence from semantic focus-sensitivity\n         \n          Robert Pasternak  \n\n\n\n          Subjects: \n           Computation and Language (cs.CL) ; Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)\n         \n\n         \n          As language models (LMs) have improved, an increasingly common means of evaluating their linguistic competence is through explicit prompting (e.g., \"Which sentence is more acceptable?\"). Prior work indicates that such prompting underperforms compared to standard token-probability-based methods in smaller models, but improves in larger models. We test this in a new domain: semantic focus-sensitivity, responsible for contrasts like \"Lucy only SWAM yesterday\" vs. \"Lucy only swam YESTERDAY\". Testing models of various sizes using text-based focus markers like boldface and italics, we find universal above-chance performance for token probabilities. Prompting results mostly align with previous findings, but in some larger models they outperform probabilities by a wide margin. We propose that this is because LMs are not prone to produce out-of-the-blue focus marking, which muddies results from token probabilities in a way that does not affect prompting. We test this by replacing standard focus markers with randomly generated ones; token probabilities still beat chance but show significantly greater performance decrease compared to prompting. These results suggest that metalinguistic prompting, while non-trivial for LMs, can also be more robust when testing stylistically atypical data. We argue that this has important consequences for the assessment of LMs' language competence, even if LMs do not perform metalinguistic judgments through human-like introspection.\n         \n       \n     \n     \n       [345] \n       \n        arXiv:2610.39926\n       \n          (cross-list from cs.CV)\n\n        [ pdf ,  other ]\n     \n     \n       \n          Title: \n          Spectral Invariant Fusion and Refinement for Domain Generalized Semantic Segmentation\n         \n          Yansong Bai ,  Yunzhi Zhuge ,  Xinzhuo Yu ,  Lu Zhang ,  Jin Zhan ,  Pingping Zhang ,  Huchuan Lu  \n\n\n\n          Subjects: \n           Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV) ; Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)\n         \n\n         \n          Domain generalized semantic segmentation is challenging because it requires accurate dense prediction on unseen domains without any access to target-domain data during training. Existing methods improve transferability through data diversification, invariant learning, or direct use of foundation models, but they often struggle to simultaneously suppress domain-sensitive interference and preserve fine-grained spatial cues. In this work, we propose SIFR, a frequency-aware dual-stream framework that tackles domain shift at both the fusion and decoding stages. SIFR uses spectral modeling as a unified principle to regulate both heterogeneous prior interaction and dense decoder refinement. Instead of directly fusing frozen CLIP and DINOv2 features, SIFR performs spectral-guided invariant fusion to filter out domain-sensitive responses while preserving complementary semantic and structural information. In addition, SIFR incorporates a spectral decoder refinement module that stabilizes multi-scale decoder features through low-frequency statistics-based normalization and adaptive frequency selection, thereby reducing the propagation of residual domain bias in dense prediction. This design enables SIFR to jointly improve domain robustness and segmentation precision under severe distribution shifts. Extensive experiments on multiple DGSS benchmarks, including synthetic-to-real, real-to-real, and adverse-weather settings, demonstrate consistent improvements over previous state-of-the-art methods.\n         \n       \n     \n     \n       [346] \n       \n        arXiv:2610.39927\n       \n          (cross-list from cs.LG)\n\n        [ pdf ,  html ,  other ]\n     \n     \n       \n          Title: \n          Learning Calibrated Confidence for Multi-Agent Collaboration\n         \n          Chenxiao Fan ,  Chongming Gao ,  Gangyi Zhang ,  Yaxin Gong ,  Leyang Shen ,  Jiakai Wang ,  Dong Wang ,  Yang Liu ,  Haoyan Liu ,  Xinting Hu ,  Fuli Feng ,  Xiangnan He  \n\n\n\n          Subjects: \n           Machine Learning (cs.LG) ; Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI); Multiagent Systems (cs.MA)\n         \n\n         \n          Complex information seeking on the Web often requires collecting evidence from multiple documents and reasoning across them, as in multi-hop question answering. Multi-agent systems address such tasks by dividing work among specialized agents, where upstream agents produce intermediate answers and supporting evidence that downstream agents build upon. However, upstream answers vary in correctness, and most systems provide no quantitative signal about it, leaving the downstream agent to judge whether to trust or verify what it receives based solely on the textual evidence.\n In this paper, we propose MASCAL. Each agent learns to produce a correctness probability for its answer by jointly optimizing task performance and confidence calibration in reinforcement learning; the upstream agent passes this probability at handoff, giving the downstream agent a quantitative basis for its decisions, and the final answer also carries a calibrated probability, quantifying the reliability of the system output. To guide the downstream agent in using this signal during training, we introduce confidence-aware weighting, which increases the learning weight at positions responsive to the upstream probability, so that the agent learns more from decisions where it actively uses the signal. Across three collaboration architectures spanning Web retrieval and reasoning tasks, MASCAL improves task accuracy and calibration quality for both agents, and generalizes to deeper collaboration chains and different model scales. Intervention analysis confirms that upstream confidence affects downstream decision behavior, and the learned final confidence supports selective answering and targeted rechecking.\n         \n       \n     \n     \n       [347] \n       \n        arXiv:2610.39954\n       \n          (cross-list from cs.LG)\n\n        [ pdf ,  html ,  other ]\n     \n     \n       \n          Title: \n          When to spend privacy: optimal allocation in private learning\n         \n          Umberto Biccari ,  Yongcun Song  \n\n\n\n          Subjects: \n           Machine Learning (cs.LG) ; Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI); Optimization and Control (math.OC)\n         \n\n         \n          Privacy accounting bounds the cumulative privacy cost of Differentially Private Stochastic Gradient Descent but does not determine how to allocate a fixed budget across iterations. We formulate the complete Gaussian noise-variance schedule as an open-loop control under a composed zero-Concentrated Differential Privacy constraint, explicitly accounting for schedule selection. For expected quadratic terminal tracking, we prove that every local minimizer exhausts the certified budget, yielding an exact-budget simplex formulation. We introduce Privacy-Budget Adjoint Mirror Descent (PB-AMD), combining pathwise adjoint gradients with entropic updates that preserve positivity, charge floors, and the prescribed budget. Under regularity and coercivity assumptions, we establish existence and budget stability of optimal schedules, design-to-training utility stability, and deterministic, stochastic, and sample-average stationarity guarantees. A linear-quadratic reduction yields an explicit square-root allocation law. Experiments demonstrate tracking improvements over several allocation rules and predictive improvements over uniform allocation in a learning-rate restart setting, where learning-rate allocation performs similarly. Comparisons at a common stationarity tolerance show lower schedule-optimization times than projected gradient descent on the tested problems. The framework connects certified privacy accounting with optimization through nonlinear training dynamics.\n         \n       \n     \n     \n       [348] \n       \n        arXiv:2610.39955\n       \n          (cross-list from cs.CV)\n\n        [ pdf ,  html ,  other ]\n     \n     \n       \n          Title: \n          Mind The Gap (MTG-AD): Dataset and Fine-grained Evaluation for End-to-End Inline Audio Descriptions\n         \n          Subhashini Venugopalan ,  Jenny Tan ,  Taylor Roper ,  Jimmy Tobin ,  Anton Kast ,  Alicia Martin ,  Sam Sepah ,  Amy Pavel  \n\n          Comments: \n          NeurIPS 2026 Evaluations & Datasets Track, camera ready\n         \n\n          Journal-ref: \n          Advances in Neural Information Processing Systems 2026\n         \n\n          Subjects: \n           Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV) ; Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI); Human-Computer Interaction (cs.HC)\n         \n\n         \n          Audio descriptions (AD) are essential for making visual media accessible to blind and low-vision (BLV) users. While Multimodal Large Language Models (MLLMs) offer a scalable solution for generating audio descriptions on-demand, their performance on ``inline'' audio descriptions -- which must fit within existing silences in a video -- remains under-explored, particularly for diverse user-generated content (UGC). We present a comprehensive investigation into MLLM-generated inline audio descriptions. First, we introduce MTG-AD, a professionally annotated dataset of 36 hours of videos, each averaging $\\sim$5 mins. and 8k+ descriptions in total, co-created by paired blind and sighted AD professionals to provide a high-quality benchmark for this task. Second, we propose MTG-AD-Eval, an evaluation framework based on audio description expert guidelines that provides fine-grained, reference-free actionable metrics for model improvement. Crucially, our framework evaluates the end-to-end task: it renders the generated scripts into audio tracks to assess their fit within the original video's natural silences, minimizing disruptive overlaps. Our analysis across frontier MLLMs reveals that while models accurately describe visual events and substantially reduce speech overlaps, gaps persist in narrative pacing and flow. Finally, we empirically validate that our automated and reference-free metrics correlate significantly with human quality ratings from sighted raters and 7 BLV participants, finding that while users narrowly prefer human-authored audio descriptions, they find MLLM-generated audio descriptions highly beneficial for comprehension. We provide actionable system design guidance and release MTG-AD at  this https URL  and MTG-AD-Eval at  this https URL .\n         \n       \n     \n     \n       [349] \n       \n        arXiv:2610.39992\n       \n          (cross-list from cs.DS)\n\n        [ pdf ,  html ,  other ]\n     \n     \n       \n          Title: \n          Accumulating Pruning Constraints: New Exact Polynomial-Space Algorithms for Max 2-SAT\n         \n          Konstantin Kutzkov  \n\n          Comments: \n          Project folder with Lean verification:  this https URL \n         \n\n\n          Subjects: \n           Data Structures and Algorithms (cs.DS) ; Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)\n         \n\n         \n          We present deterministic polynomial-space algorithms that solve Max 2-SAT on $n$ variables exactly in time $\\Ostar(2^{(1-\\gamma)n})$, for a fixed $\\gamma>0$, on several classes of instances whose clause density may grow with $n$. Our main tool extends the classic DPLL branching algorithm. When the search gives a variable its less promising value, comparing the two values of the variable yields a pruning constraint that is satisfied by some optimal assignment. Accumulating these constraints along each branch prunes all but an exponentially small fraction of the assignments. The classes are formulas with many biased variables, semirandom 2-CNF formulas, and Max-Cut on regular expander graphs and on graphs generated by a stochastic block model. In a semirandom formula, the pairs of variables that may share a clause are arbitrary, and the clauses on these pairs are random. For semirandom formulas and block-model graphs, the algorithms are still exact on every input, and the time bound holds with high probability over the input. To our knowledge, these are the first polynomial-space algorithms with such a running time for nontrivial classes of dense Max 2-SAT instances. Beyond these classes, the pruning constraints can be added to any branching algorithm for Max 2-SAT, and we expect them to be of practical relevance for general formulas, in particular for sparse ones, where each constraint involves only a few variables.\n         \n       \n     \n     \n       [350] \n       \n        arXiv:2610.40023\n       \n          (cross-list from cs.NE)\n\n        [ pdf ,  html ,  other ]\n     \n     \n       \n          Title: \n          HEMO: Harness Self-Evolution for Multi-Objective Combinatorial Optimization\n         \n          Jianghan Zhu ,  Cong Zhang ,  Ni Zhang ,  Yifan Yang ,  Yew Soon Ong ,  Zhiguang Cao  \n\n          Comments: \n          48 pages, including supplementary material; 5 figures and 13 tables\n         \n\n\n          Subjects: \n           Neural and Evolutionary Computing (cs.NE) ; Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)\n         \n\n         \n          Automatic operator design (AOD) has increasingly adopted large language models (LLMs) to generate problem-specific search and variation operators for multi-objective combinatorial optimization (MOCO). Yet most methods evolve the operators while leaving the process that discovers them fixed. This limits adaptation as new Pareto trade-offs and operator interactions change where and how the search should proceed. We introduce HEMO, which makes the AOD harness itself a self-evolving optimization target. HEMO maintains a persistent harness state and updates one module at a time, revising the logic that determines which experience to retrieve, which operators to improve, which parents to select, and how to formulate the orchestration of search steps. Downstream solution quality and runtime guide these updates, while a discounted UCB scheduler directs adaptation toward promising modules as their utility changes. A persistent memory filesystem preserves candidate outcomes and diagnostic feedback across harness states, enabling successive states to build on accumulated optimization experience. Experiments across eight MOCO tasks and two structurally distinct evolutionary backbones demonstrate strong performance against conventional evolutionary, neural, and LLM-based AOD baselines, together with effective transfer to related tasks. HEMO extends automatic operator design beyond evolving individual operators to improving the search process that discovers them.\n         \n       \n     \n     \n       [351] \n       \n        arXiv:2610.40042\n       \n          (cross-list from cs.SE)\n\n        [ pdf ,  html ,  other ]\n     \n     \n       \n          Title: \n          UPPAAi: Towards LLM-Assisted Modeling, Verification, Analysis and Synthesis\n         \n          Peter Dolog ,  Kim G. Larsen ,  Marius Mikučionis ,  Frederik F. K. Rasmussen  \n\n          Comments: \n          Submitted to IFS/ETAPS 2027\n         \n\n\n          Subjects: \n           Software Engineering (cs.SE) ; Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI); Logic in Computer Science (cs.LO)\n         \n\n         \n          For more than three decades, UPPAAL has supported the modelling and analysis of real-time and cyber-physical systems. Yet building a suitable model, formalising the intended properties, choosing analysis techniques and interpreting counterexamples remain iterative tasks that demand substantial expertise. Large language models (LLMs) can offer plausible explanations and even solutions, but they are not necessarily correct. We present UPPAAI, an extension of UPPAAL in which users construct, explain, debug and specify models in dialogue with an LLM. The guiding principle is that the user requests, the LLM proposes, explains and orchestrates, and UPPAAL verifies. UPPAAI exposes the UPPAAL editor, queries and verifier through a Tools API. We report on seven case studies, from explaining to a newcomer to learning a controller, covering timed automata, stochastic hybrid automata and strategy learning. LLMs proved to be extremely helpful in not just generating solutions but also pro-actively suggesting relevant UPPAAL features it was not asked about.\n         \n       \n     \n     \n       [352] \n       \n        arXiv:2610.40046\n       \n          (cross-list from cs.CV)\n\n        [ pdf ,  html ,  other ]\n     \n     \n       \n          Title: \n          EPC-RawDC: Equivariant Physics Constrained 3D Diffusion with Raw Data Consistency for CBCT to CT Image Synthesis\n         \n          Alzahra Altalib ,  Chunhui Li ,  Alessandro Perelli  \n\n          Comments: \n          10 pages, 4 figures, 8 tables\n         \n\n\n          Subjects: \n           Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV) ; Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI); Medical Physics (physics.med-ph)\n         \n\n         \n          Cone beam computed tomography (CBCT) is widely used for image guided and adaptive radiotherapy, but scatter, noise, reconstruction artifacts, and inaccurate Hounsfield units limit its quantitative use. Recent diffusion approaches for CBCT to CT synthesis remain largely image driven, with limited use of the underlying acquisition physics. We propose EPC-RawDC, a conditional 3D latent diffusion framework that combines volumetric generative modeling with two complementary constraints derived from the same scanner specific CBCT forward model. First, geometry equivariant projection constraint exploits the rotational equivariance of the CBCT forward operator by supervising the synthesized CT through correspondingly transformed reference projections. Second, raw data consistency uses noisy projection measurements of the individual acquisition as scan specific supervision during training. The framework was evaluated on two independent clinical cohorts with distinct scanner geometries and projection sources. For JUST, the physics constraints used measured clinical raw CBCT projections acquired with an Elekta system, whereas for SynthRAD2025 the projection data were simulated from the reference CT using a Varian geometry with Poisson photon statistics. EPC-RawDC achieved an SSIM of 0.97 in both cohorts and improved PSNR over DDPM by 2.66 dB on SynthRAD2025 and 2.10 dB on JUST, while reducing mean MAE by 20.3% and 19.0%, respectively. On SynthRAD2025, projection consistency and equivariant errors were reduced by 55.0% and 41.4% relative to DDPM. These results demonstrate that combining a volumetric diffusion prior with scanner geometry and scan specific projection measurements improves quantitative CBCT to CT synthesis across both simulated and measured projection domains. Code available after publication at  this https URL .\n         \n       \n     \n     \n       [353] \n       \n        arXiv:2610.40051\n       \n          (cross-list from cs.CL)\n\n        [ pdf ,  html ,  other ]\n     \n     \n       \n          Title: \n          Rethinking Pre-pretraining\n         \n          Alexander Stern ,  Tim Rocktäschel ,  Acyr Locatelli ,  Laura Ruis  \n\n\n\n          Subjects: \n           Computation and Language (cs.CL) ; Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)\n         \n\n         \n          We report that allocating compute to synthetic pre-pretraining (PPT) never outperforms pure natural-language training in our compute-matched experiments. This holds across four PPT curricula (k-Dyck, neural cellular automata (NCA), declarative reference corpora, and code) and three training regimes: data-constrained, data-unconstrained, and a high token-to-parameter-ratio regime (881M parameters trained on 50B tokens). When natural language is scarce, repeated epochs over a 100M-token corpus beat every synthetic curriculum on natural-language validation loss, with no consistent downstream accuracy advantage from PPT. We find that PPT gains reported in prior work are sensitive to the weight initialization scheme. In an NCA reproduction, we recover a 5.4% perplexity improvement from PPT under the original initialization scheme. An alternative scheme improves both methods, but the no-PPT baseline more, closing the gap between them. However, when PPT receives additional compute rather than a share of the natural-language budget, gains persist at small natural-language budgets even with improved initialization, but disappear by 1B tokens. Finally, models forget what they learn during PPT in subsequent natural-language training; interleaving the same data throughout training prevents forgetting, but does not significantly improve natural-language performance. Our results suggest limited benefits of synthetic pre-pretraining as a distinct training stage and motivate rethinking how such data are used in language model training.\n         \n       \n     \n     \n       [354] \n       \n        arXiv:2610.40055\n       \n          (cross-list from cs.LG)\n\n        [ pdf ,  html ,  other ]\n     \n     \n       \n          Title: \n          One Vector or Many? Clustered Optimal-Transport Steering under Quantization\n         \n          Illia Makarov  \n\n          Comments: \n          34 pages, 12 figures, 14 tables\n         \n\n\n          Subjects: \n           Machine Learning (cs.LG) ; Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI); Computation and Language (cs.CL)\n         \n\n         \n          Activation steering changes what a language model generates by editing its hidden states at inference time. Its most common form adds one vector, the difference of class means (DiM), to every hidden state. CHaRS (concept heterogeneity-aware representation steering) uses optimal transport instead: an entropic plan pairs the clusters of the two classes, and each hidden state moves along a mixture of the cluster displacements, weighted toward its nearest clusters. We study this family of clustered optimal-transport maps for toxicity mitigation in Gemma-2-2B and Granite-3.1-2B, and ask what the plan adds to DiM and whether that survives INT8 and NF4 quantization.\n We prove an identity: whatever the plan, the displacements averaged with the cluster sizes as weights equal DiM, so a clustered map can differ from DiM only for tokens whose routing weights depart from the cluster sizes. In the CHaRS protocol they stay close, and CHaRS is statistically equivalent to Mean-AcT, which adds DiM at every layer, in all eight pre-specified equivalence tests. A variant fitted on individual tokens, which routes each token mainly to its nearest clusters, leaves 37-39% less toxicity than DiM on Gemma and 51-61% less on Granite at steering strength 3 (toxic-bert), where DiM already removes 76-85% of it. Two other toxicity classifiers confirm the gain on Granite, but on Gemma only one does. At equal strength the variant has somewhat higher perplexity, but at matched toxicity it is slightly more fluent than DiM. Yet a nearly constant map from the same clusters also leaves about 30% less toxicity, so the role of treating tokens differently remains open.\n Under INT8 and NF4 the variant still leaves less toxicity than DiM, and transferring it from full precision to the NF4 model instead of refitting it adds no significant toxicity after correction for multiple tests.\n         \n       \n     \n     \n       [355] \n       \n        arXiv:2610.40061\n       \n          (cross-list from cs.CV)\n\n        [ pdf ,  html ,  other ]\n     \n     \n       \n          Title: \n          Localizing Concepts in Visual Autoregressive Models\n         \n          Nanxiang Jiang ,  Yang Liu ,  Yuanhao Wang ,  Min Xu  \n\n\n\n          Subjects: \n           Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV) ; Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)\n         \n\n         \n          Understanding the internal routing of visual concepts is crucial for the safe and controllable adaptation of generative models. While concept localization has been widely studied in diffusion models, the emerging paradigm of next-scale visual autoregressive models remains largely unexplored. In this paper, we introduce LoCo, the first model-agnostic framework to localize where and when specific concepts emerge within autoregressive models. Driven by the native coarse-to-fine nature of next-scale generation, our method precisely maps conceptual knowledge across three distinct dimensions: Layer, Scale, and Position. To systematically localize and evaluate concept routing without context bias, we propose LoCoBench, a comprehensive dataset spanning 10 diverse categories. Extensive probing on image and video autoregressive models like Infinity, HunyuanImage-3.0, and InfinityStar shows that the localized positions are both interpretable and causally related to concept emergence. Building on these insights, we apply our localization method to three key applications: concept erasure, model personalization, and adversarial concept injection. Experiments demonstrate that our targeted intervention achieves state-of-the-art performance, substantially reducing computational overhead while preserving benign utility. Overall, our findings offer insights into how conceptual knowledge is routed during autoregressive generation, introducing a practical pathway for more interpretable, efficient, and secure adaptation.\n         \n       \n     \n     \n       [356] \n       \n        arXiv:2610.40075\n       \n          (cross-list from cs.RO)\n\n        [ pdf ,  html ,  other ]\n     \n     \n       \n          Title: \n          Learning Safe Flow Matching Robot Policies from Binary Feasibility Oracles\n         \n          Jianming Ma ,  Rongjun Jin ,  Yizhi Chen ,  Yue Gao  \n\n\n\n          Subjects: \n           Robotics (cs.RO) ; Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)\n         \n\n         \n          Robot foundation models increasingly rely on flow-based action generation for general-purpose manipulation, yet the generated actions are not guaranteed to satisfy physical constraints such as joint limits and collision avoidance. This problem is especially challenging when the feasible set cannot be described analytically and is accessible only through black-box feasibility oracles. We study this practical oracle-only setting and propose an oracle-guided differentiable safeguard for safe flow-matching robot policies. The safeguard uses binary feasibility queries to correct unsafe updates in the forward pass, while extracting local boundary information to provide constraint-aware learning signals in the backward pass. Combined with discrete-time flow matching, the same safeguard is used during training and inference, enabling forward--backward aligned policy learning without explicit or differentiable constraint models. Experiments on obstacle-aware robotic manipulation demonstrate 100% constraint satisfaction while preserving task performance.\n         \n       \n     \n     \n       [357] \n       \n        arXiv:2610.40091\n       \n          (cross-list from cs.CL)\n\n        [ pdf ,  html ,  other ]\n     \n     \n       \n          Title: \n          Upcycling Language Models for Multiple Specialized Domains\n         \n          Mingzi Cao ,  Xi Wang ,  Nikolaos Aletras  \n\n\n\n          Subjects: \n           Computation and Language (cs.CL) ; Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)\n         \n\n         \n          Effective multi-domain adaptation of large language models requires prioritizing relevant domain knowledge while minimizing interference from irrelevant domains. Existing approaches commonly use Low-Rank Adaptation (LoRA) to train a separate adapter for each domain and combine these adapters within one model. However, they either weight adapters equally regardless of the input domain or dynamically weight adapters while still activating all of them including irrelevant ones. Additionally, while efficient, their low-rank structure limits performance. In contrast, upcycling is a model expansion method that adds experts and a router to a dense Transformer layer offering larger capacity than LoRA approaches. In this paper, we propose Domain-Specialized Upcycling (DS-Upcycling) for multi-domain adaptation. We introduce a new domain-aware supervision that allows the router to effectively learn to distinguish domains by selecting the appropriate experts. During fine-tuning on each new domain, we update only the corresponding expert and router while freezing all other parameters. Experiments show that our method achieves the highest average performance across the adapted domains compared to baselines while retaining general performance. With just 256 examples per domain for router supervision, DS-Upcycling achieves 90-98% routing accuracy after adaptation compared with 43-70% for routers trained without our approach.\n         \n       \n     \n     \n       [358] \n       \n        arXiv:2610.40130\n       \n          (cross-list from cs.LG)\n\n        [ pdf ,  html ,  other ]\n     \n     \n       \n          Title: \n          Escaping Path Mirages in Offline Goal-Conditioned Reinforcement Learning\n         \n          Junhyeon Bae ,  Jaebak Hwang ,  Gwanwoo Choi ,  Minung Kim ,  Seungyul Han  \n\n          Comments: \n          10 pages (main), 34 pages in total. Accepted to NeurIPS 2026\n         \n\n\n          Subjects: \n           Machine Learning (cs.LG) ; Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)\n         \n\n         \n          Offline goal-conditioned reinforcement learning remains challenging in stochastic long-horizon settings, where compounding value estimation errors hinder reliable goal reaching. While prior methods have sought to address this challenge through various approaches, a key challenge remains path mirage, where agents overcommit to spuriously successful trajectories in offline datasets induced by stochasticity, often leading to failure on difficult tasks. To address this issue, we propose Branch-aware Graph Planning (BGP), which captures stochastic branching structures by identifying high-variability points as graph nodes and designing goal-conditioned edges with segmented learning to avoid unreliable branches and reduce unnecessary stochasticity along paths. As a result, BGP enables reliable goal reaching even in highly stochastic long-horizon environments, and experiments on diverse OGBench tasks show that it substantially outperforms prior state-of-the-art offline goal-conditioned RL methods. Our implementation is available at  this https URL .\n         \n       \n     \n     \n       [359] \n       \n        arXiv:2610.40160\n       \n          (cross-list from cs.LG)\n\n        [ pdf ,  html ,  other ]\n     \n     \n       \n          Title: \n          FARE: Factor-Aware Retrieval with Error-Guided Fusion for Retail Forecasting\n         \n          Chenghao Gu ,  Zhuokang Shen ,  Shenghao Wu ,  Tingxuan Zhu ,  Weiyi Luo ,  Haifeng Tang  \n\n          Comments: \n          16 pages,3 figures\n         \n\n\n          Subjects: \n           Machine Learning (cs.LG) ; Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)\n         \n\n         \n          Retail sales forecasting underpins inventory planning, yet promotions and calendar events reshape short-term demand. Although each factor usually has historical occurrences, the complete factor combination of a target week often has few precedents. The challenge is therefore to retrieve informative partial matches and weight them by their reliability, which varies across series. We propose FARE(Factor-Aware Retrieval with Error-Guided Fusion), which ranks historical weeks separately for each known target-week factor and merges the selected weeks into a factor reference under a fixed budget. Guided by shrunk errors from completed development weeks and the current disagreement among candidates, a first gate mixes this factor reference with a shape reference from similar sales histories, and a second gate controls how strongly the mixture corrects a backbone forecast. Both gates remain fixed during testing. On Favorita, M5, Rossmann, and Rohlik, FARE reduces the weighted absolute percentage error (WAPE) of five backbones by 11.46%, 4.06%, 14.63%, and 10.16% on average. With Chronos-2 frozen, FARE also outperforms conventional and existing enhancement methods, including supervised residual correction and retrieval augmentation. Code is available at  this https URL  .\n         \n       \n     \n     \n       [360] \n       \n        arXiv:2610.40166\n       \n          (cross-list from cs.CV)\n\n        [ pdf ,  html ,  other ]\n     \n     \n       \n          Title: \n          EventSeeColor: Learning to Reconstruct Color from Monochrome Events\n         \n          Chuanzhi Xu ,  Langyi Chen ,  Haoxian Zhou ,  Zelin Zhang ,  Haodong Chen ,  Qiang Qu ,  Xiaoming Chen ,  Weidong Cai  \n\n\n\n          Subjects: \n           Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV) ; Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)\n         \n\n         \n          Event cameras capture visual brightness changes under challenging lighting conditions. However, traditional event-based image reconstruction targets grayscale intensity and produces images without scene color. Color distinguishes surfaces with similar intensity. Reconstructing color from events can reveal appearance differences that reconstructed grayscale images cannot express. Although monochrome event cameras do not directly measure color, their event-triggering patterns depend on surface reflectance, illumination, and the sensor's spectral response. These patterns may contain cues that support color reconstruction. In this paper, we present EventSeeColor, which uses event-reconstructed grayscale and aligned events to jointly guide a diffusion colorizer. Grayscale-derived semantic context supports color inference, while region-balanced chroma supervision and local RGB refinement improve color distribution, brightness, and texture. In the combined evaluation on two real event datasets, EventSeeColor achieves the best structural similarity, learned perceptual scores, and chromatic-region color difference among the external methods.\n         \n       \n     \n     \n       [361] \n       \n        arXiv:2610.40188\n       \n          (cross-list from stat.ML)\n\n        [ pdf ,  html ,  other ]\n     \n     \n       \n          Title: \n          Understanding axial attention in TSFMs through single location regression\n         \n          Andrei-Tiberiu Pantea ,  Aymeric Dieuleveut  \n\n\n\n          Subjects: \n           Machine Learning (stat.ML) ; Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI); Machine Learning (cs.LG)\n         \n\n         \n          Recent advances in Time Series Foundation Models (TSFMs) increasingly rely on axial attention mechanisms, but despite their empirical success, the theoretical properties of axial attention remain largely unexplored. To bridge this gap, we extend the Single Location Regression framework introduced in Marion et al. [2025] to 2D structured data, in order to study sparse information settings where a target depends on a small number of tokens. We formulate an analytically tractable axial attention-type predictor and rigorously analyze its statistical properties and training dynamics. We prove that under a specific asymptotic regime and with knowledge of the underlying oracle parameters, our predictor achieves Bayes optimality, whereas a class of semi-linear simplifications strictly fails to do so, underlining the role of the inner non-linearity. We prove that for a specific temperature scaling, Projected Gradient Descent converges globally to the optimal oracle parameters. Extensive numerical experiments validate our theoretical findings and highlight the critical role of inverse temperature scheduling in empirical convergence.\n         \n       \n     \n     \n       [362] \n       \n        arXiv:2610.40257\n       \n          (cross-list from cs.LG)\n\n        [ pdf ,  html ,  other ]\n     \n     \n       \n          Title: \n          Learned Terminal Costs for Short-Horizon Model Predictive Control of Autonomous Trucks in Interactive Highway Traffic\n         \n          Hooman Hamze ,  Benjamin Houshmand ,  Deepthi Pathare ,  Erik Börve ,  Morteza Haghir Chehreghani ,  Nikolce Murgovski  \n\n          Comments: \n          13 pages, 2 figures, 8 tables\n         \n\n\n          Subjects: \n           Machine Learning (cs.LG) ; Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI); Robotics (cs.RO); Systems and Control (eess.SY)\n         \n\n         \n          Autonomous highway driving for heavy-duty trucks is a challenging control problem. The greater mass, dimensions and intricate dynamics increase the requirements for cooperation from surrounding traffic in critical maneuvers, requiring a careful balance between safety constraints and efficient long-horizon decision making. Model Predictive Control (MPC) provides rigorous constraint satisfaction but relies on terminal cost which can limit its effectiveness at short, computationally affordable horizons. Reinforcement Learning (RL) captures long-term driving behavior but offers no constraint guarantees and tends toward overly conservative policies when trained under sparse collision penalties. In this paper, we propose a hybrid MPC and RL framework for highway driving in autonomous trucks, where a finite-horizon MPC, solved via dynamic programming, uses a pre-trained RL model for terminal cost estimation. We evaluate the framework in a simulation environment with reactive surrounding traffic and compare it against pure MPC with zero terminal cost, rollout with terminal cost estimation using a base policy, and pure RL. Results show that the hybrid approach achieves zero collisions with the lowest cost of any configuration.\n         \n       \n     \n     \n       [363] \n       \n        arXiv:2610.40276\n       \n          (cross-list from cs.CV)\n\n        [ pdf ,  html ,  other ]\n     \n     \n       \n          Title: \n          TaskFrame: Connecting Task-Level Planning with Frame-Level Control for Game Agents\n         \n          Guangzhi Wang ,  Junfu Pu ,  Yiran Wang ,  Xingyilang Yin ,  Honghao Chen ,  Cheng Cheng ,  Lingen Li ,  Yinan Zhou ,  Jiarui Wu ,  Yinuo Jiang ,  Hongbo Jin ,  Xiaodong Cun ,  Ying Shan  \n\n\n\n          Subjects: \n           Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV) ; Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)\n         \n\n         \n          Building general game agents requires both high-level task reasoning and low-latency action control in rapidly changing visual environments. These capabilities favor models with substantially different computational profiles, making them difficult to realize within a single model. In this work, we present TaskFrame, a hierarchical planner-controller framework that connects task-level planning with frame-level keyboard-and-mouse control. A vision-language task planner decomposes a high-level goal into an ordered sequence of atomic instructions and is optimized through supervised learning and dependency-aware GRPO. The hidden states of special goal tokens that terminate each atomic instruction provide continuous goal embeddings that are cached and reused across control steps. A causal Transformer controller combines the embedding of the active goal with recent observations to predict discrete keyboard and mouse-button actions and continuous camera motion at every game step. We train the system in three stages: planner training, controller pretraining, and joint alignment. Experiments in Minecraft show that TaskFrame is competitive with general-purpose vision-language models on instruction decomposition, and that in closed-loop evaluation the agent achieves a higher combat success rate than the evaluated baselines.\n         \n       \n     \n     \n       [364] \n       \n        arXiv:2610.40280\n       \n          (cross-list from cs.LG)\n\n        [ pdf ,  html ,  other ]\n     \n     \n       \n          Title: \n          Latent Prediction Yields Cross-Subject Alignable Representations in Sensory-Evoked fMRI\n         \n          Serwan Jassim ,  Victoria Bosch ,  Daniel Anthes ,  Elia Bruni ,  Tim Kietzmann  \n\n\n\n          Subjects: \n           Machine Learning (cs.LG) ; Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI); Neurons and Cognition (q-bio.NC)\n         \n\n         \n          Decoding sensory-evoked functional magnetic resonance imaging (fMRI) data is a central analytical tool in cognitive neuroscience. Here we ask whether self-supervised learning (SSL), which has been scarcely explored in this domain, can yield representations that support strong downstream performance. To do so, we adapt a joint embedding predictive architecture (JEPA) to train participant-specific encoders on brain data alone. The encoders never see the stimulus images or any representation derived from them. Applied to the Natural Scenes Dataset (NSD), we evaluate our learned brain embeddings on the downstream tasks of brain captioning and image reconstruction, as well as cross-participant generalization. Our self-supervised approach achieves competitive performance with parameter-rich supervised pipelines that are trained end-to-end with access to image and language data. We further find that encoders trained independently for different participants can be aligned fully unsupervised, using neither images nor stimuli shared between them. After alignment, a neural decoding head trained on one participant can be applied to another without fine-tuning and retains most of its within-subject performance on image reconstruction and brain captioning. By removing the need for images and shared stimuli, our approach is a step towards pooling fMRI data across participants and, eventually, across datasets.\n         \n       \n     \n     \n       [365] \n       \n        arXiv:2610.40287\n       \n          (cross-list from cs.DC)\n\n        [ pdf ,  html ,  other ]\n     \n     \n       \n          Title: \n          Only Accepted Tokens Count: An Energy Accounting of Speculative Decoding Under Latency Targets\n         \n          Bole Ma ,  Ayesha Afzal ,  Jan Eitzinger ,  Harald Koestler ,  Gerhard Wellein  \n\n\n\n          Subjects: \n           Distributed, Parallel, and Cluster Computing (cs.DC) ; Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI); Machine Learning (cs.LG); Performance (cs.PF)\n         \n\n         \n          Large language model serving is limited by facility power. Energy per token determines how many tokens a datacenter can serve, while a latency target bounds how quickly each user receives them. During decode, every weight is read from memory for every generated token. Batching amortizes this read across users but makes no individual user faster. Speculative decoding does: a drafter proposes several future tokens, and the target model verifies them in a single forward pass that reuses the same weight read. Because every drafted token must be verified whether or not it is accepted, the energy speculation saves depends on how many drafted tokens are accepted. This saving is hard to pin down. It varies across target models from different attention families and across drafting schemes, and it depends on the text being served. Every drafter we evaluate accepts fewer tokens on conversation than on code, and every trained drafter accepts fewer on Romansh, a low-resource language, than on English. We measure speculative decoding under production serving conditions (SGLang, CUDA graphs enabled) on H100 and H200 GPUs, at batch sizes up to 64 across 5 corpora. On dense models, verification, not drafting, dominates the energy cost. The saving shrinks as the batch grows, because batching has already shared the weight read that speculation would divide among the accepted tokens. At large batch sizes, block drafters push verification past the balance point between arithmetic and memory traffic into the compute-bound regime, where the saving becomes a loss, and a drafter with low acceptance raises energy per token. Meeting a tighter latency target with speculation then costs additional energy. Larger batches lower energy per token without speculation. Speculation adds to that saving only when acceptance is high, and on the model we tested it leaves little headroom for latency-preserving DVFS.\n         \n       \n     \n     \n       [366] \n       \n        arXiv:2610.40302\n       \n          (cross-list from cs.CV)\n\n        [ pdf ,  other ]\n     \n     \n       \n          Title: \n          Self-training for cross-centre coronary vessel segmentation in X-ray angiography using pre-contrast frame subtraction\n         \n          Danial Moafi ,  Aida Moafi ,  Mostafa Aryafar ,  Ashkan Sami ,  Maryam Aryafar  \n\n          Comments: \n          7 pages, 2 figures, 3 tables\n         \n\n\n          Subjects: \n           Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV) ; Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)\n         \n\n         \n          Deep learning models for coronary vessel segmentation in X-ray angiography often perform poorly on data from centres that were not represented in training. Apart from differences in equipment, the labels of the largest public dataset (ARCADE) are incomplete, as only the segments defined in the SYNTAX score are annotated. In this study, we evaluated whether self-training with pseudo-labels can improve performance on external data without additional manual annotation. Pseudo-labels were generated from two sources, namely the predictions of an nnU-Net model trained on ARCADE and a contrast map obtained by subtracting a pre-contrast frame from the same sequence. The nnU-Net predictions were extended along the contrast map, and pixels detected only in the subtraction image were assigned an ignore label and excluded from the loss. Six models were trained and tested on ARCADE and on two external datasets (XCAD and XACV). All first-round models initialised with the baseline predictions performed better than both the baseline and a control model fine-tuned for the same number of epochs. The model trained on the XCAD pool improved the macro-averaged Dice score by 2.7 points (95% CI 2.3-3.2), while the control decreased it by 0.6 points. A second round of self-training did not lead to further improvement. When an external model trained on 50 fully annotated images was used to generate the initial labels, the XCAD Dice score increased from 0.623 to 0.745 without any XCAD labels, although performance on XACV did not improve because large main vessels were missed. These results suggest that the benefit of self-training depends largely on the sensitivity of the model used to generate the initial pseudo-labels.\n         \n       \n     \n     \n       [367] \n       \n        arXiv:2610.40310\n       \n          (cross-list from cs.LO)\n\n        [ pdf ,  html ,  other ]\n     \n     \n       \n          Title: \n          Hammering with Vampire in Lean\n         \n          Jonas Bodingbauer ,  Laura Kovács  \n\n          Comments: \n          10 pages, 3 figures, 1 table. Tool demonstration paper\n         \n\n\n          Subjects: \n           Logic in Computer Science (cs.LO) ; Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)\n         \n\n         \n          We present Vampire-Hammer, a hammer for the Lean interactive theorem prover that discharges goals using the Vampire automated theorem prover and reconstructs proofs checked by the Lean kernel. Vampire-Hammer uses the Lean-auto framework to process goals and premises before dispatching them to Vampire for proof search. Doing so, we rely on standard TPTP problem formats and annotated TSTP proofs that support proof reconstruction. With a 10-second budget on 196,083 eligible Mathlib theorems, Vampire-Hammer solves 21,158 problems not solved by other evaluated tools within the same budget.\n         \n       \n     \n     \n       [368] \n       \n        arXiv:2610.40318\n       \n          (cross-list from cs.CL)\n\n        [ pdf ,  html ,  other ]\n     \n     \n       \n          Title: \n          Decision and System One models in rheumatology: accuracy and calibration on specialty-training examination questions and diagnostic case vignettes\n         \n          Alfredo Madrid-García ,  Diego Benavent ,  Fabian Lechner ,  Phillip Kremer ,  Johannes Knitza ,  Beatriz Merino-Barbancho  \n\n          Comments: \n          28 pages, 2 figures, 2 tables; includes supplementary material (8 tables, 1 figure)\n         \n\n\n          Subjects: \n           Computation and Language (cs.CL) ; Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)\n         \n\n         \n          Objectives: To evaluate the accuracy and calibration of Decision and System One models on rheumatology examination questions and diagnostic vignettes. Methods: Two benchmarks were analysed: 172 rheumatology questions from Spanish specialty-training examinations (expanded RheumaMIR dataset) in Spanish and English, and 70 English diagnostic vignettes from RheumBench with 5-18 candidate diagnoses, 52 of which included the reference diagnosis. Four System One models (Jev 1.13, Clef, Microsoft-Decision-1, Laya) and GPT-6 Luna (OpenAI Decisions API) were compared with GPT-6 Sol and two local generative models. The primary outcome was accuracy; secondary outcomes included calibration, discrimination, accuracy of answers with probability >=0.90 and language differences. Results: In RheumaMIR, Jev's accuracy was 93.0% in Spanish and 91.9% in English, below GPT-6 Sol (99.4%; Holm-adjusted P<=0.005); GPT-6 Luna reached 97.7%, Clef 91.9-93.0% and Microsoft-Decision-1 86.0-87.8%, without detectable language differences. Jev's answers with probability >=0.90 (76-77% of answers) were 98.5% correct. In RheumBench, accuracy was lower: GPT-6 Sol 76.9%, GPT-6 Luna 63.5%, Clef 59.6%, Jev 50.0% and Microsoft-Decision-1 40.4% (each System One model below GPT-6 Sol, Holm-adjusted P<=0.045; chance 9.6%). Jev became overconfident (mean probability 0.659; over/underconfidence index +0.159), and its answers with probability >=0.90 (25% of cases) were 84.6% correct, against 95.5% for GPT-6 Luna (42%). Laya performed near chance in both benchmarks. Conclusion: The best-performing System One models were highly accurate on examination questions but less accurate on diagnostic vignettes, remaining below GPT-6 Sol. Jev's high-confidence predictions were less reliable on vignettes, highlighting the need for task-specific evaluation and calibration.\n         \n       \n     \n     \n       [369] \n       \n        arXiv:2610.40325\n       \n          (cross-list from cs.LG)\n\n        [ pdf ,  html ,  other ]\n     \n     \n       \n          Title: \n          How to Loop Your Transformer: Loop Scaling, Specialization, and Parallel Inference\n         \n          Chanakya Ekbote ,  Artidoro Pagnoni ,  Devendra Singh Sachan ,  Shruti Bhosale ,  Mike Lewis ,  Maryam Fazel-Zarandi ,  Shashank Rajput ,  Sujan Kumar Gonugondla  \n\n          Comments: \n          149 pages with appendix. Prerpint\n         \n\n\n          Subjects: \n           Machine Learning (cs.LG) ; Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)\n         \n\n         \n          Looping in transformer models reuses weights by repeatedly applying the same transformer, or parts of it, making the model deeper without increasing its parameter count. Whether looping is useful depends on which constraint dominates: model size, training compute, or autoregressive inference efficiency. We study these trade-offs jointly in dense decoder-only transformers trained from scratch, varying which parts of the model are looped, how many loops are applied, and how information is passed between loops. Our experiments span models with up to 1B parameters, trained on up to 155B tokens with up to $1.07\\times10^{21}$ FLOPs and 32 loops. We find that looping the full transformer generally gives the strongest quality at a fixed parameter budget, whereas looping a middle block of layers gives a better return when training FLOPs are constrained. Adding loops is not uniformly beneficial: gains eventually saturate, reverse, or become unstable. This motivates asking whether forcing every loop to use the same weights limits the benefit of deeper looping. We address this by adding small loop-specific low-rank adapters that improve quality, with larger gains as more loops are added. Looped transformers also suffer a decoding slowdown proportional to the number of loops. Parallel Loop Transformers address this by passing hidden states between loops across adjacent token positions, allowing loop stages to run concurrently. However, in our experiments the original formulation does not consistently retain the quality gains of ordinary looping. We find that preserving the current-token embedding and separately normalizing it and the previous-token hidden state before combining them recovers strong performance across model scales and training budgets. Together, these results show that effective looping requires matching the design to the resource constraint and inference setting.\n         \n       \n     \n     \n       [370] \n       \n        arXiv:2610.40327\n       \n          (cross-list from cs.CV)\n\n        [ pdf ,  html ,  other ]\n     \n     \n       \n          Title: \n          Radiology Guideline-derived Decision Graph for Efficient Clinical Decision Support\n         \n          Xuewen Zhou ,  Zhen Chen  \n\n\n\n          Subjects: \n           Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV) ; Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)\n         \n\n         \n          Radiological risk stratification and management planning are essential downstream tasks in medical imaging. Automating these tasks is challenging because clinical guideline criteria are distributed across prose, tables, and cross-referenced definitions. We present Guideline-derived Decision Graph (GDGraph), a framework that decouples guideline compilation from case evaluation. During compilation, a large language model partitions guidelines into section-based chunks, extracts local decision subgraphs with linked definitions, and aggregates related branches into an executable decision graph. During inference, a fast System One model answers localized clinical questions while deterministic traversal resolves Boolean logic, turning opaque single-pass predictions into a traceable decision path without task-specific training. Across 347 cases spanning renal cyst, adnexal mass, and thyroid nodule guidelines, GDGraph improves System One accuracy from 57.3% to 90.8%, outperforming retrieval-augmented GPT-4o (80.1%) and graph-guided GPT-4o with chain-of-thought (82.7%) at substantially lower latency and cost. The source code will be available.\n         \n       \n     \n     \n       [371] \n       \n        arXiv:2610.40345\n       \n          (cross-list from cs.LG)\n\n        [ pdf ,  html ,  other ]\n     \n     \n       \n          Title: \n          A Unified Hessian-Guided Framework for Post-Training Quantization\n         \n          Mads Toftrup ,  Marco Federici ,  Boris van Breugel ,  Paul Whatmough ,  Markus Nagel  \n\n          Comments: \n          10 pages + 4 appendix\n         \n\n\n          Subjects: \n           Machine Learning (cs.LG) ; Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)\n         \n\n         \n          Low-bit quantization enables efficient deployment of large language models by reducing memory footprint and computational cost. Achieving high accuracy at low precision requires selecting quantization ranges, linear transformations, and weight rounding decisions. Existing approaches typically optimize these components independently using local reconstruction objectives, which fail to account for the varying impact of quantization errors on downstream predictions. Direct optimization of task-level metrics would provide a more faithful objective, but is challenging due to the discontinuous nature of quantization operations and the high computational cost of evaluating their downstream effects.\n In this work, we derive a Hessian-guided approximation of the output change induced by joint weight and activation quantization. Based on a tractable Fisher approximation, we formulate a unified post-training quantization framework that enables the joint optimization of quantization ranges, linear transformations, and weight rounding under a common output-sensitivity objective. The resulting method remains practical for modern large language models and consistently outperforms state-of-the-art transformation and rounding methods across both channel-wise and block-wise quantization settings.\n         \n       \n     \n     \n       [372] \n       \n        arXiv:2610.40370\n       \n          (cross-list from cs.RO)\n\n        [ pdf ,  html ,  other ]\n     \n     \n       \n          Title: \n          The Athlete as a Scientist of One: Agentic Bayesian Experimental Design for Individualized Physiology from Consumer Wearables\n         \n          Behnam Moradi  \n\n\n\n          Subjects: \n           Robotics (cs.RO) ; Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)\n         \n\n         \n          Large language model (LLM) agents for personal health read wearable data and recommend, but do not learn about the individual in a way that can be audited or falsified. We present EpiCoach, an agentic system that treats one athlete, a cross-country mountain biker, as the subject of accountable N-of-1 science. Claims about the athlete live in an evidence-gated hypothesis registry: each must issue a probabilistic prediction about a future session before it occurs, and its belief moves only through computed evidence from a logged statistical fit. Predictions come from a mechanistic digital twin inferred from heart rate, GPS and barometric altitude: a lagged heart-rate response model, a fitness-fatigue model with learned time constants, and a latent technical-descending skill state, measured on discovered trail segments. The agent prescribes the next training week as a constrained experiment maximizing information gain subject to load and safety constraints, around two instrument rides. We evaluate the system two ways over eighteen months of data. A rolling-origin replay benchmark shows the twin beats standard forecasters on heart rate but not segment time, and the agentic loop matches a static model. A live prospective phase runs the weekly cycle: in its first three weeks the ledger sealed 20 predictions, scored 14, the descent forecast calibrated after one correction, the climb forecast pessimistic as the athlete improved, and the safety officer vetoed the agent twice. Every correction is preserved in the registry, a template for auditable, falsifiable personalization in digital health. We release the registry, benchmark and protocol.\n         \n       \n     \n     \n       [373] \n       \n        arXiv:2610.40394\n       \n          (cross-list from cs.CL)\n\n        [ pdf ,  html ,  other ]\n     \n     \n       \n          Title: \n          Scaling Mathematical Proof Supervision from Structured Textbooks\n         \n          Youqing Fang ,  Zhouqi Hua ,  Yinhao Tang ,  Yuzhe Gu ,  Lingkai Kong ,  Bin Liu ,  Weiming Zhang ,  Kai Chen ,  Yanan Sun ,  Wenwei Zhang  \n\n          Comments: \n          35 pages, 18 figures, 14 tables\n         \n\n\n          Subjects: \n           Computation and Language (cs.CL) ; Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)\n         \n\n         \n          Mathematical supervision for language models is typically flattened into isolated problem-solution pairs, discarding the curricular organization and evidential structure of the original sources. We introduce TOME-Math (Textbook-Oriented Mathematical Evidence), a large-scale corpus that converts 2,118 textbooks spanning the core areas of university-level mathematics subjects into approximately 380K structured proof-supervision records while preserving chapter order, proposition-proof links, dependency references, and source provenance. Leveraging these structural signals, we explore curriculum learning across multiple mathematical subjects and characterize scaling patterns with supervision volume and source breadth. At full scale, fine-tuning on TOME-Math raises the unweighted mean score across our held-out evaluations and established public benchmarks by 16.7 percentage points over the base model, while exceeding all three public instruction-data baselines evaluated in aggregate. Our findings highlight structured textbooks as both a scalable source of mathematical supervision and an empirical framework for studying the impact of data organization on mathematical reasoning. We will release the corpus to facilitate further research on structured mathematical supervision.\n         \n       \n     \n     \n       [374] \n       \n        arXiv:2610.40396\n       \n          (cross-list from cs.CR)\n\n        [ pdf ,  html ,  other ]\n     \n     \n       \n          Title: \n          Understanding and Mitigating Role Spoofing in LLMs\n         \n          Ezra Edelman ,  Benjamin Edelman ,  Surbhi Goel ,  Eran Malach  \n\n\n\n          Subjects: \n           Cryptography and Security (cs.CR) ; Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)\n         \n\n         \n          Despite growing capabilities, LLMs remain susceptible to prompt injections: malicious inputs that can manipulate or hijack models. In particular, it has recently been observed that LLMs can exhibit role confusion (Ye et al., 2026), where models attribute the source of text based on its style instead of its labeled role. Malicious text in tool outputs (for instance, a malicious webpage accessed by an agent) can leverage role confusion to impersonate the user, an attack we call role spoofing}. In this work, we seek to understand and mitigate role spoofing. We study how models track \"who said what\" in a conversation, showing that they use cues from both content (e.g., semantics and writing style) and structure (e.g., control tokens and formatting). We show that LLMs are highly susceptible to role spoofing attacks that combine both fake structure and content. Additionally, in a toy setting, we show that adversarial training which teaches the model to ignore content-based injections does mitigate content-based attacks but leaves the model susceptible to structure-based attacks. This can explain why frontier models, with increased resistance to many injection attacks, can still be susceptible to role spoofing. Finally, we propose a new method for detecting role spoofing using linear probes trained to predict roles in a conversation, and show that it is effective across different settings.\n         \n       \n     \n     \n       [375] \n       \n        arXiv:2610.40407\n       \n          (cross-list from cs.LG)\n\n        [ pdf ,  html ,  other ]\n     \n     \n       \n          Title: \n          SCARL: Sequence Confidence-Aligned Reinforcement Learning\n         \n          Zhenghong Ke ,  Jingbo Zhou ,  Hua Wu  \n\n\n\n          Subjects: \n           Machine Learning (cs.LG) ; Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)\n         \n\n         \n          Reinforcement learning with verifiable rewards (RLVR) improves language-model reasoning by scoring sampled responses with a rule-based verifier and reinforcing those that pass. Where a classifier's training couples predicted probability to labels, nothing ties an RLVR policy's confidence in a response to its reward: aligned training would drive responses with the same advantage toward equal confidence, yet the advantage is blind to confidence. Under this sequence-confidence misalignment, a success the policy barely found earns the same update as one it produces with near certainty, and a confident error is corrected no harder than an exploratory one. We propose SCARL (Sequence Confidence-Aligned Reinforcement Learning), which reshapes, but never re-signs, each advantage with the alignment gap between the response's group-relative confidence and the outcome its advantage certifies, normalized into shares of each class's advantage budget. Misaligned samples absorb the force, responses with the same advantage are driven toward equal confidence, and repairing the misalignment doubles as a remedy for the entropy collapse that erodes exploration. SCARL provably reduces to its base estimator when a class's confidences agree, adds no extra passes and one hyperparameter. On Qwen3-4B-Base it lifts average Pass@32 by 2.4 points over DAPO and is best among nine compute-matched methods on both coverage and per-draw accuracy; applied unchanged across four estimators and four backbones, it improves 14 of 16 settings while preserving policy entropy and widening the confidence-correctness separation. Our code is available at  this https URL .\n         \n       \n     \n     \n       [376] \n       \n        arXiv:2610.40414\n       \n          (cross-list from cs.SD)\n\n        [ pdf ,  html ,  other ]\n     \n     \n       \n          Title: \n          Bridging Neural Representations and Symbolic Semantics in Audio Classification\n         \n          Avishek Das ,  Pascal Hitzler  \n\n\n\n          Subjects: \n           Sound (cs.SD) ; Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)\n         \n\n         \n          Deep audio classifiers can achieve strong predictive performance, but their hidden representations remain difficult to interpret. Existing audio explainability methods often focus on input-level attribution, such as waveform segments, spectrogram regions, or acoustic features, and provide limited insight into what hidden neurons encode. Interpreting hidden neurons at the concept level can provide a more meaningful view of what an audio model learns internally. This paper investigates whether hidden neurons in audio classifiers can be associated with human-understandable symbolic acoustic concepts. A model-agnostic neurosymbolic framework is presented for analyzing the final hidden layer of audio classifiers, containing 32 neurons, through symbolic concept induction. The framework is evaluated on two audio classification settings: music genre classification using GTZAN dataset and musical instrument classification using IRMAS dataset. Results show that several hidden neurons can be associated with meaningful induced concepts, and most confirmed neuron-concept pairs are statistically supported using the Mann-Whitney U test. These findings suggest that symbolic concept induction is a promising direction for explaining latent audio representations beyond input-level attribution.\n         \n       \n     \n     \n       [377] \n       \n        arXiv:2610.40418\n       \n          (cross-list from cs.LG)\n\n        [ pdf ,  html ,  other ]\n     \n     \n       \n          Title: \n          IMPACT: Inverter-Informed Multimodal Learning for Photovoltaic Generation Forecasting\n         \n          Anushka Katiyar ,  Cong Feng ,  Jing Wang ,  Tristan Liu ,  Simon Julien ,  Zachary Jacobs  \n\n\n\n          Subjects: \n           Machine Learning (cs.LG) ; Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)\n         \n\n         \n          This work develops a physics-informed multimodal deep learning framework for 10- to 110-minute-ahead probabilistic photovoltaic power forecasting. The forecasting model is built on a four-branch architecture that separately encodes plant-level temporal history and inverter-level spatial cloud dynamics through a graph attention encoder with a learned adjacency, known-future solar geometry, and the current meteorological state, fused through gated cross-attention. The geometry branch adds two forward-looking physical signals, spatial cloud heterogeneity and inverter ramp-down activity, each empirically weighted toward the horizon where it is most predictive. The decoder anchors its prediction to a smart persistence baseline, learning a horizon-conditioned correction, and the resulting quantile forecasts are calibrated online through a kernel-weighted adaptive conformal calibration step. The proposed model outperforms all 14 baselines, achieving a forecast skill score (FSS) of 0.2950, 0.099 above the next-best model. A branch-composition ablation shows that every branch contributes positively and the model retains substantial skill in challenging overcast and variable sky conditions (FSS 0.198 and 0.255).\n         \n       \n     \n     \n       [378] \n       \n        arXiv:2610.40420\n       \n          (cross-list from cs.LG)\n\n        [ pdf ,  html ,  other ]\n     \n     \n       \n          Title: \n          NECA: Neighbor Event Curve Augmentation for Multi-Horizon ICU Deterioration Prediction\n         \n          Chenyang Wang ,  Jiawei Theresa Tang ,  Xinran Liu ,  Xinyu Lu ,  Xulei Yang  \n\n\n\n          Subjects: \n           Machine Learning (cs.LG) ; Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)\n         \n\n         \n          Early prediction of ICU organ dysfunction events supports timely clinical monitoring and intervention, but estimating short-term organ-specific risk from early ICU records remains challenging due to irregular measurements, heterogeneous clinical variables, and the need to predict risk across multiple future horizons. Existing supervised models usually treat each patient as an isolated sample and do not explicitly use the observed event timing of similar historical patients. We propose Neighbor Event-Curve Augmentation (NECA), a model-agnostic method that retrieves training-set patients with similar early-window profiles and converts their subsequent organ dysfunction event onset times into cumulative event-curve features over 6h, 12h, 24h, and 42h. These features are concatenated with the original patient representation and used by standard downstream predictors. On PhysioNet 2012 and a 4,000-stay MIMIC-IV subset, NECA improves average PR-AUC and ROC-AUC across four organ dysfunction event tasks and seven supervised learners. The largest gain appears for neurological dysfunction events, where task-level average PR-AUC increases from 0.461 to 0.557 on PhysioNet 2012. Ablations show that clinically meaningful retrieval and horizon-specific neighbor risk drive most of the improvement, while the full cumulative curve adds task-dependent temporal context. These results support similar-patient event timing as a structured temporal risk signal for ICU organ dysfunction event prediction. The source code will be publicly released upon acceptance of this paper.\n         \n       \n     \n     \n       [379] \n       \n        arXiv:2610.40487\n       \n          (cross-list from cs.CV)\n\n        [ pdf ,  html ,  other ]\n     \n     \n       \n          Title: \n          MetaRefine: Score Refinement with Multi-Level Features for Image Assessment\n         \n          Chuanzhi Xu ,  Chengkun Yue ,  Haolan Guo ,  Xuanhua Yin ,  Boyu Wei ,  Zihan Deng ,  Qiang Qu ,  Weidong Cai  \n\n\n\n          Subjects: \n           Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV) ; Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)\n         \n\n         \n          An image's original dimensions, aspect ratio, and composition provide important cues that can affect its technical and aesthetic quality. Image assessment methods use resizing, cropping, padding, or patch sampling to accommodate different input sizes. However, these operations can alter or obscure original geometric and compositional cues. Existing assessment models also encode rich multi-level visual evidence that can be further extracted and exploited to improve existing models. This motivates us to combine visual evidence with original-image geometry for score refinement without changing the original scoring pipelines. In this paper, we introduce MetaRefine, a metadata-aware score refinement architecture whose lightweight residual heads predict score corrections from geometry, multi-level visual features, and the frozen model's original score. Across 100 configurations on 21 datasets, MetaRefine improves PLCC in 88 configurations and SRCC in 85, with average relative gains of 7.55% and 7.10%, respectively. It reduces MAE in 90 configurations and RMSE in 89, with average relative reductions of 23.27% and 21.93%, respectively. The median parameter overhead is only 1.11%. MetaRefine can further improve state-of-the-art (SOTA) assessment models on multiple benchmarks.\n         \n       \n     \n     \n       [380] \n       \n        arXiv:2610.40490\n       \n          (cross-list from quant-ph)\n\n        [ pdf ,  html ,  other ]\n     \n     \n       \n          Title: \n          Fast and accurate neural triage decoding of ultra-high-rate quantum LDPC codes\n         \n          Hong-Ye Hu ,  Zhenlai Wang ,  Binzhao Luo ,  Tao Xiang ,  Chen Zhao  \n\n          Comments: \n          5 pages, 4 figures with Methods. We will release the fully supplementary information in the second update soon\n         \n\n\n          Subjects: \n           Quantum Physics (quant-ph) ; Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI); Information Theory (cs.IT); Machine Learning (cs.LG)\n         \n\n         \n          Quantum error correction is essential for fault-tolerant quantum computing. High-rate quantum low-density parity-check (qLDPC) codes have emerged as a promising approach to reducing the substantial physical qubit overhead of quantum error correction, enabling encoding rates exceeding $1/2$ in blocks of approximately one thousand physical data qubits. However, their practical implementation requires decoders that combine high accuracy with low latency to keep pace with quantum hardware. Existing decoders of high-rate qLDPC codes can achieve high accuracy but often incur substantial computational latency, whereas neural network decoders have yet to be demonstrated for high-rate qLDPC codes. Here, we introduce \\emph{neural triage decoding}, a framework that uses syndrome consistency to adaptively allocate neural computation. A local neural message-passing network rapidly predicts error corrections, additional iterations reduce residual syndrome violations, and a rarely invoked diversification stage searches for consistent corrections to the remaining shots. We demonstrate that the \\texttt{Triage} decoder can decode ultra-high-rate qLDPC codes encoding a few hundred logical qubits, including lifted-product and Kasai codes, achieving state-of-the-art accuracy comparable to the near optimal algorithmic decoders while reducing decoding latency by approximately one to two orders of magnitude. Our framework is inspired by a rigorous analysis of the exact bitwise marginal decoder, revealing a distance-independent syndrome-inconsistent error floor. In addition, we show the robustness of neural network decoders to mismatched error priors. Our results establish adaptive neural decoding as a promising route towards fast, accurate, and scalable quantum error correction in the near term.\n         \n       \n     \n     \n       [381] \n       \n        arXiv:2610.40494\n       \n          (cross-list from cs.DC)\n\n        [ pdf ,  html ,  other ]\n     \n     \n       \n          Title: \n          AI in the EGI e-infrastructure -- Past experiences and future strategies\n         \n          Gergely Sipos ,  Sergio Andreozzi ,  Marco Rorro ,  Andrea Manzi ,  Tiziana Ferrari  \n\n          Comments: \n          8 pages, 1 figure. Preprint of a paper submitted to the proceedings of the 28th Conference on Computing in High Energy and Nuclear Physics (CHEP 2026), to be published in EPJ Web of Conferences\n         \n\n\n          Subjects: \n           Distributed, Parallel, and Cluster Computing (cs.DC) ; Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI); Computers and Society (cs.CY)\n         \n\n         \n          The EGI Federation has provided federated e-infrastructure services to European and global science for over 15 years. We review EGI's early experiences with AI in the interTwin and iMagine Horizon Europe projects, which built digital twins in physics and environmental sciences and an image-analysis platform for aquatic research. We then examine how the AI landscape has changed since 2023 and argue that the focus of AI in science has shifted from model training to adapting and applying AI to specific domains, projects and questions. Building on these lessons, we present the AI strategy emerging from the EGI Federation Strategy 2026-2030, organised around three enablers: (1) an AI-ready infrastructure connecting compute, data and application resources across the continuum; (2) human skills to know what is possible with AI and how to implement it; and (3) trust, to run AI with confidence even in high-risk environments. We describe the projects implementing each enabler, including federated LLM inference, AI-ready data platforms and AI competence centres, and discuss a division of labour with EuroHPC's AI Factories, RAISE, research infrastructures and EOSC, in which EGI specialises in the customisation and long-term operation of AI for research.\n         \n       \n     \n     \n       [382] \n       \n        arXiv:2610.40516\n       \n          (cross-list from cs.CL)\n\n        [ pdf ,  html ,  other ]\n     \n     \n       \n          Title: \n          How I Aligned Your Model: Scaling Up Multilingual Safety Training with Templates\n         \n          Max Neuwinger ,  Hanna Yukhymenko ,  Imanol Schlag  \n\n\n\n          Subjects: \n           Computation and Language (cs.CL) ; Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI); Machine Learning (cs.LG)\n         \n\n         \n          Safety alignment for Large Language Models is heavily focused on English with limited multilingual representation, particularly damaging low-resource language performance. Related open training datasets are often limited in size or do not follow region-localized harmful topics to broaden the scope of alignment beyond cultures. We propose a scalable template-based augmentation pipeline to produce massive multilingual refusal and benign corpora, extracting safe templates from harmful prompts, translating them and then filling in the slots with culturally authentic entities, both harmful and benign. As a result, we release MSafeTemp, a validated corpus spanning 60 languages useful for multilingual safety alignment and evaluation. Using the dataset, we show that English-only evaluation misjudges multilingual safety: for several models, attacks that fail in English succeed once translated, while culturally localized variants expose further failures that translation alone misses. We also confirm that augmenting safety training data through templates allows for higher diversity and quality of alignment, resulting in improved safety performance beyond English. MSafeTemp and the respective pipeline are aimed at facilitating research and development of safer and more responsible models for less-represented cultures.\n         \n       \n     \n     \n       [383] \n       \n        arXiv:2610.40532\n       \n          (cross-list from cs.RO)\n\n        [ pdf ,  html ,  other ]\n     \n     \n       \n          Title: \n          BURGER: Benchmarking and Understanding Robotic Generalization via Embodied Drawing\n         \n          Xiangyu Xu ,  Bangshuai Peng ,  Ziyi Liu ,  Mengjie Zhao ,  Chenhao Lu ,  Zhengyang Fan ,  Huazhe Xu ,  Kaizhe Hu  \n\n          Comments: \n          Preprint version\n         \n\n\n          Subjects: \n           Robotics (cs.RO) ; Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)\n         \n\n         \n          How effectively can vision-language-action (VLA) models learn diverse language-conditioned behaviors as training data scales? Investigating this question requires large and varied robot datasets, whose collection on physical robots remains costly. We introduce BURGER, a language-conditioned robotic drawing benchmark with a scalable, prompt-driven pipeline for generating continuous drawing trajectories, both in simulation and the real world. In BURGER, an agent starts from the same blank canvas and controls a robot arm to draw the object or spatial composition specified by language, while each instruction allows an open-ended variety of valid drawings and stroke orders. Using 28,200 simulated demonstrations spanning 282 drawing tasks, we evaluate five representative VLA models across data scales, language grounding, and multimodal behavior. BURGER supports fine-grained analysis of language grounding, spatial composition, and action generation, revealing distinct failure patterns across VLA models. Our experiments suggest that some VLA models can benefit from greater task diversity and demonstration scale, capture multimodal drawing behaviors, and generalize to unseen compositions of familiar objects. Finally, we deploy a trained policy on a Franka Panda arm, demonstrating the ability to draw reactively under human intervention. Project homepage and code can be found at  this https URL .\n         \n       \n     \n     \n       [384] \n       \n        arXiv:2610.40565\n       \n          (cross-list from cs.RO)\n\n        [ pdf ,  html ,  other ]\n     \n     \n       \n          Title: \n          Gym-Anything for Robotics: Scaling physical simulations with digital agents\n         \n          Pranjal Aggarwal ,  Sean Welleck  \n\n\n\n          Subjects: \n           Robotics (cs.RO) ; Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI); Machine Learning (cs.LG)\n         \n\n         \n          Realistic, fast robotics simulations could enable large-scale training of embodied AI models that transfer to the real world. However, despite decades of progress, available simulations cover only a narrow range of real-world tasks and physical phenomena. The core bottleneck is that creating better simulations requires significant expert effort and therefore does not scale. To address this, we introduce Gym-Anything for Robotics, a framework in which coding agents create realistic robotics simulations. Our core insight is that simulation creation is a coding agent task, so improvements in coding agents translate directly into richer and more faithful simulations. We select simulations based on U.S. GDP data to cover economically valuable physical work, define what makes a good simulation and a good task, and develop an adversarial creation-audit loop that checks each simulation and task against these definitions. Using this pipeline, we build RoboWorld, a set of 25 simulations with 25 test and 175 training RL environments of varying difficulty. Even the best frontier models fail on every RoboWorld test task, achieving an average reward of only 0.06. Building on RoboWorld, we introduce several new ways for frontier models to improve, including in-context learning across repeated attempts, a privileged teacher that reads the simulator state and teaches a student in context, self-assisted learning from the model's own privileged experience, and code policies that generate demonstrations across varied scenes to train vision-language-action models (VLAs) such as $\\pi_{0.5}$. Each of these substantially improves performance. We release our pipeline, RL environments, and benchmark to facilitate future research. Code and data are available at  this https URL \n         \n       \n     \n     \n       [385] \n       \n        arXiv:2610.40586\n       \n          (cross-list from cs.NI)\n\n        [ pdf ,  html ,  other ]\n     \n     \n       \n          Title: \n          Multistatic ISAC with Deep Learning-Based Clutter Suppression for 5G and Beyond\n         \n          Yalin E. Sagduyu ,  Tugba Erpek ,  Sastry Kompella  \n\n\n\n          Subjects: \n           Networking and Internet Architecture (cs.NI) ; Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI); Machine Learning (cs.LG); Signal Processing (eess.SP)\n         \n\n         \n          Integrated sensing and communications (ISAC) systems using 5G and beyond signals of opportunity enable joint wireless sensing and communication using shared infrastructure and spectrum resources. While multistatic ISAC can increase sensing coverage with spatially distributed receivers, dense and dynamic clutter can significantly degrade bistatic range and Doppler estimation, leading to errors in target localization and velocity estimation. This paper presents a multistatic ISAC framework with clutter suppression for high-fidelity estimation of target position and velocity. Distributed clutter is modeled with randomly located scatterers with varying Doppler spreads and radar cross sections to capture dense and dynamic clutter effects. A broad range of conventional signal processing baselines are evaluated for clutter suppression, showing limited robustness under strong clutter conditions. To address this limitation, deep learning-based clutter suppression methods are introduced to improve robustness against dense and dynamic clutter by employing residual and attention-enhanced deep neural network architectures that directly enhance target responses from clutter-contaminated observations. These methods substantially outperform conventional approaches in target position and velocity estimation across diverse clutter conditions in multistatic ISAC.\n         \n       \n     \n     \n       [386] \n       \n        arXiv:2610.40611\n       \n          (cross-list from cs.CL)\n\n        [ pdf ,  html ,  other ]\n     \n     \n       \n          Title: \n          When to Assist and What to Suggest: Joint Gating and Memory-Grounded Hint Generation in One Compact Decoder\n         \n          A. J. A. Jubayer Talukder ,  Mohammad Ariful Haque  \n\n          Comments: \n          20 pages, 10 figures, 13 tables; supplementary material included (Sections S1-S8)\n         \n\n\n          Subjects: \n           Computation and Language (cs.CL) ; Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI); Human-Computer Interaction (cs.HC)\n         \n\n         \n          Proactive assistants offer short, memory-grounded hints during a conversation, deciding when to assist and what to suggest. Current systems split this between a gate and a larger generator; we evaluate both together on 28,058 decision points. Stage-wise generation scores are oracle-gate ceilings: behind a real gate, end-to-end exact match is 0.2619 against a ceiling of 0.2971, and end-to-end precision is 0.2387, leaving substantial headroom. A gate pays off through precision and compute, whose saving falls from 4.39x with an 8B generator to 1.38x with a 1B one. The memory profile drives hint quality: removing it costs 24.7 exact-match points (rescaled BERTScore 0.57 to 0.35), while retrieval and a larger generator add nothing on exact match. One 1B decoder can do both, gating from an intermediate layer and generating in the same pass. At its final checkpoint it matches or exceeds the two-model pipelines on automatic end-to-end measures with 1.31-1.76x less wall-clock time than the matched 1B pipeline; across three seeds, its deeper exit ties the separate gate. A human evaluation (1,144 ratings over 32 dialogues) supports the memory effect and rates the single model level with the matched 1B pipeline and below the 8B one. Graded metrics and LlamaPIE's native judge track human ratings more closely than exact match, which we report as a strict lower bound.\n         \n       \n     \n     \n       [387] \n       \n        arXiv:2610.40613\n       \n          (cross-list from cs.CR)\n\n        [ pdf ,  html ,  other ]\n     \n     \n       \n          Title: \n          TextSeal2: Distortion-Free LLM Watermarking with Copula Mixing and Memoryless Races\n         \n          Tom Sander ,  Pierre Fernandez ,  Itai Gat ,  Haiming Bao ,  Jiaxin Shi ,  Jason Park ,  Valeriu Lacatusu ,  Sylvestre-Alvise Rebuffi ,  Hongyan Chang ,  Hady Elsahar ,  Tomáš Souček ,  Tuan Tran ,  Surya Parimi ,  Alexandre Mourachko  \n\n          Comments: \n          Code:  this https URL \n         \n\n\n          Subjects: \n           Cryptography and Security (cs.CR) ; Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI); Computation and Language (cs.CL)\n         \n\n         \n          We present TextSeal2, a distortion-free watermark for large language models that keeps its signal under speculative decoding: tokens that replace rejected drafts remain watermarked with the same keys, and the acceptance rate is unchanged. It also preserves output diversity and adds no measurable inference latency. Its predecessor, TextSeal, restores diversity in the Gumbel-max scheme by routing each token to one of two keys via a coin flip. This is suboptimal because the detector averages the scores from both keys without knowing which key was used. TextSeal2 instead merges two or more keys into a single virtual key and couples it to fresh noise through AutoMark's Gaussian copula. Any number of keys, including publicly shared keys, can be merged this way without diluting detection. Under speculative decoding, TextSeal2 verifies keyed drafts with one more exponential race, run on the leftovers of the draft races. By memorylessness, this race reproduces block verification, so the output follows the target model exactly. We evaluate TextSeal2 on three LLMs. At equal output token diversity, TextSeal2 detects 8 to 22 percentage points more 100-token answers than TextSeal at a false-positive rate of $10^{-3}$, and is comparable to textGrain while requiring no post-logits optimization. Under speculative decoding, it detects 18 and 22 percentage points more 200-token answers than TextSeal and textGrain, respectively, at the same acceptance rate.\n         \n       \n     \n     \n       [388] \n       \n        arXiv:2610.40636\n       \n          (cross-list from cs.CV)\n\n        [ pdf ,  html ,  other ]\n     \n     \n       \n          Title: \n          Probing Spatial State Representations in Visual Foundation Models\n         \n          Baiqiao Yin ,  Zhongang Cai ,  Ruisi Wang ,  Qiwei Liang ,  Lei Yang ,  Ziwei Liu  \n\n\n\n          Subjects: \n           Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV) ; Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)\n         \n\n         \n          An observer sees only part of the surrounding world, yet must keep track of what lies beyond the current view. Spatial perception therefore involves more than recognizing individual images: it calls for a persistent spatial state that is updated under self-motion and used to guide action. Do visual foundation models provide representations that support such a state? We study this question through four levels of spatial capability---spatial structure, object persistence, predictive spatial update, and actionability---using frozen-feature probes on ten representations under streaming egocentric observations of static indoor environments. The resulting capability profiles vary across tasks, network layers, and prefix lengths: strong readout of the visible scene does not ensure accurate object persistence or future-feature retrieval. Matched controls separate readout accuracy from reliance on visual history and motion. Native future prediction retains recoverable spatial structure, but a spatial reader trained on real features does not transfer to predicted features unchanged. Finally, goal-dependent candidate selection does not establish reliable continuous action utility: predicted actions respond to goal changes, yet supplying the correct goal does not reliably improve progress. Across the four levels, object persistence and actionability build on camera-pose readout, whereas feature prediction builds on visible-scene readout. Together, these findings motivate evaluating spatial representations not only by what can be decoded from them, but by whether that information is maintained beyond observation, updated predictively, and used for goal-directed action.\n         \n       \n     \n     \n       [389] \n       \n        arXiv:2610.40641\n       \n          (cross-list from physics.ao-ph)\n\n        [ pdf ,  html ,  other ]\n     \n     \n       \n          Title: \n          From extreme weather to agricultural consequences with agentic AI\n         \n          Minghan Yu ,  Mengyu Dai ,  Yixin Wen ,  Haizhao Yang  \n\n          Comments: \n          34 pages, 4 figures, 6 tables\n         \n\n\n          Subjects: \n           Atmospheric and Oceanic Physics (physics.ao-ph) ; Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)\n         \n\n         \n          Extreme weather is forecast days ahead, yet its consequences for agriculture remain hard to predict: a forecast says where a storm will strike, not which crops will suffer or how much is at risk, and the records that could tell span many sources. We present WxCrop, the first agent that judges agricultural hazards from physically interpretable weather indices examined by causal analysis, and the first that combines weather and agricultural information to predict crop loss reliably for past and forecast dates. We demonstrate it in Florida on hurricanes, freezes and dry heat, with a loss model calibrated on insurance records for each, and the same procedure extends to other hazards and areas. Three checks show reliability: the hurricane index detects recorded cyclone days with an AUC of 0.98, recorded losses in held-out years are reproduced to within 2%, and independent production records confirm the county rankings (rank correlation 0.63). Users ask the agent for each crop group's loss as a fraction of expected season value, every number sourced, so that governments, growers and scientists respond to agricultural disasters quickly.\n         \n       \n     \n     \n       [390] \n       \n        arXiv:2610.40643\n       \n          (cross-list from cs.CV)\n\n        [ pdf ,  html ,  other ]\n     \n     \n       \n          Title: \n          Luggage re-identification: A study of strategies towards a real-world application\n         \n          Quentin Dhôte ,  Romaric Audigier  \n\n          Comments: \n          7 pages, 4 figures, 5 tables. Accepted at The 2026 IEEE International Conference on Content-Based Multimedia Indexing (IEEE CBMI)\n         \n\n\n          Subjects: \n           Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV) ; Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)\n         \n\n         \n          Luggage re-identification (ReID) is a critical task in video surveillance for forensics, security or border management (suspicious or abandoned luggage recognition), as well as luggage management (lost luggage recovery). It enables the association of observations of the same luggage across disjoint camera views in complex environments such as airports, train stations and border crossing. While existing approaches primarily focus on appearance-based cropped image matching under relatively clean conditions, real-world scenarios are characterized by frequent occlusions, cluttered background and crowded scenes which significantly degrade performance. Yet, strategies leveraging the complementarity of multiple shots and views from different video streams is overlooked. In this paper, we study strategies for an end-to-end pipeline that operates from multiple video streams to detect and re-identify luggage. We evaluate different variants on a benchmark dataset acquired in real-world environments to address the questions of selecting and merging different views and shots of the same luggage as well as training on different environment datasets for an overall ReID performance improvement in practical applications.\n         \n       \n     \n     \n       [391] \n       \n        arXiv:2610.40647\n       \n          (cross-list from cs.CV)\n\n        [ pdf ,  html ,  other ]\n     \n     \n       \n          Title: \n          Read, Allocate, Route: Role-Coupled Tokenization for Pixel-Space Generative Transformers\n         \n          Jiongfan Yang ,  Weichen Fan ,  Haiwen Diao ,  Zhongang Cai ,  Ziwei Liu  \n\n          Comments: \n          22 pages, 10 figures; under review at ICLR 2027\n         \n\n\n          Subjects: \n           Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV) ; Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI); Machine Learning (cs.LG); Neural and Evolutionary Computing (cs.NE)\n         \n\n         \n          Pixel-space generative Transformers typically use one token per image patch, so capturing finer detail requires more tokens and makes global attention increasingly expensive. We observe that finer detail does not necessarily require more global communication: low-frequency structure benefits from global exchange, while high-frequency detail can be modeled locally. Based on this insight, we introduce SpecSlot, a pixel-space rectified-flow Transformer that decouples output detail from the length of the global attention sequence. SpecSlot expands each spatial anchor into multiple slots, each assigned a disjoint band of DCT coefficients; this separates anchor density from spectral subdivision. Only the lowest-frequency slot from each anchor participates in global attention, while all slots interact locally. This allows the model to increase spectral detail without enlarging the global sequence. A spatial head further adds a local correction to the spectral prediction, while the generative state, target, and loss remain in pixel space. On ImageNet-256, SpecSlot achieves a comparable final FID to a FLOPs-matched JiT control while converging faster. On ImageNet-512, it improves FID by 2.84 over the control with 3.9% fewer FLOPs, and comes within only 0.69 FID of JiT-B/16 at 38.2% lower cost. On text-to-image generation, SpecSlot surpasses JiT-B/8 on DPG-Bench by 1.06 at 40.6% lower forward cost.\n         \n       \n     \n     \n       [392] \n       \n        arXiv:2610.40649\n       \n          (cross-list from cs.LG)\n\n        [ pdf ,  html ,  other ]\n     \n     \n       \n          Title: \n          Rethinking Neural Thickets Without Weight Perturbations\n         \n          Nicolas Dufour ,  Elouan Gardès ,  Amil Dravid ,  Patrick Pérez ,  Loic Landrieu  \n\n          Comments: \n          Project page can be found here:  this https URL \n         \n\n\n          Subjects: \n           Machine Learning (cs.LG) ; Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI); Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)\n         \n\n         \n          The Neural Thickets hypothesis posits that pretrained language models lie in dense neighborhoods of task-specific experts in weight space, which random Gaussian perturbations can uncover. We reproduce the reported gains, but show that they do not require this geometric explanation. Perturbing a single decoder block already recovers much of the improvement, while keeping the model entirely frozen and adding a random bias to its output logits matches or exceeds full-weight perturbations on most reasoning tasks. This suggests an alternative explanation based on output diversity, validation-based selection, and voting. We formalize this view as Perturb-Select-Vote (PSV), a general procedure that generates perturbed predictors, selects high-performing members on a small validation set, and aggregates their predictions.\n This interpretation connects Neural Thickets to established inference-time methods, including sampling, self-consistency, and verifier-guided selection. A first-order analysis further shows that small weight perturbations act primarily through their induced logit shifts. We extend this analysis to vision, where logit PSV substantially improves performance under domain shift, including on Earth observation, while full-weight perturbations provide little additional benefit. Together, these results suggest that the gains attributed to nearby weight-space experts can instead be obtained through output diversity, selection, and voting. Logit PSV realizes these benefits with a fully frozen model, providing a simple, training-free alternative to full-weight perturbation ensembles across language and vision.\n         \n       \n     \n \n\n       \n     Replacement submissions (showing 213 of 213 entries) \n\n\n     \n       [393] \n       \n        arXiv:2508.07388\n       \n          (replaced)\n\n        [ pdf ,  html ,  other ]\n     \n     \n       \n          Title: \n          Invert4TVG: A Temporal Video Grounding Framework with Inversion Tasks Preserving Action Understanding Ability\n         \n          Zhaoyu Chen ,  Hongnan Lin ,  Yongwei Nie ,  Fei Ma ,  Xuemiao Xu ,  Fei Yu ,  Chengjiang Long  \n\n\n\n          Subjects: \n           Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI) \n         \n\n         \n          Temporal Video Grounding (TVG) aims to localize video segments corresponding to a given textual query, which often describes human actions. However, we observe that current methods, usually optimizing for high temporal Intersection-over-Union (IoU), frequently struggle to accurately recognize or understand the underlying actions in both the video and query, thus reducing the effectiveness of these methods. To address this, we propose a novel TVG framework that integrates inversion-based TVG as auxiliary objectives to maintain the model's action understanding ability. We introduce three kinds of inversion TVG tasks derived from the original TVG annotations: (1) Verb Completion, predicting masked verbs (actions) in queries given video segments; (2) Action Recognition, identifying query-described actions; and (3) Video Description, generating descriptions containing query-relevant actions given video segments. These inversion tasks are entirely derived from the original TVG tasks and are probabilistically integrated with them within a reinforcement learning framework. By leveraging carefully designed reward functions, the model preserves its ability to understand actions, thereby improving the accuracy of temporal grounding. Experiments show our method outperforms state-of-the-art approaches, achieving a 7.1\\% improvement in R1@0.7 on Charades-STA for a 3B model.\n         \n       \n     \n     \n       [394] \n       \n        arXiv:2509.11311\n       \n          (replaced)\n\n        [ pdf ,  html ,  other ]\n     \n     \n       \n          Title: \n          Prompts to Proxies: Emulating Human Preferences via a Compact LLM Ensemble\n         \n          Bingchen Wang ,  Zi-Yu Khoo ,  Jingtan Wang  \n\n          Comments: \n          Accepted for publication at NeurIPS 2026\n         \n\n\n          Subjects: \n           Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI) ; Computers and Society (cs.CY)\n         \n\n         \n          Large language models are increasingly used as proxies for human subjects in social science research, yet external validity requires matching the response distributions of target human populations. We study population-level survey alignment: reconstructing aggregate survey responses from limited public survey data, without individual-level demographic profiles or model finetuning. We formalize this problem as preference reconstruction: rather than matching proxy agents to demographic profiles, we construct a functional basis of proxy agents and recover population preferences by weighted aggregation. We instantiate this idea via Prompts to Proxies ($\\texttt{P2P}$), a two-stage inference-time system. Stage 1 uses structured attribute-based prompting with entropy-guided adaptive sampling to construct a diverse proxy pool spanning the latent preference space. Stage 2 employs L1-regularized regression to select a compact weighted ensemble matching observed target-population responses. Across 14 American Trends Panel waves, $\\texttt{P2P}$ achieves an average test MSE of 0.014 at approximately 0.8 USD per survey, improving over prompting and demographic-conditioning baselines. On the World Values Survey, cross-locale transfer experiments show that basis expressiveness can outperform locale matching on average, while locale-specific generation helps on culturally divergent questions. A stress test against an SFT-aligned survey model shows competitive performance using less than 3\\% of the training data. These results position preference reconstruction as a lightweight, externally verifiable alternative for survey-based population alignment. Code is available at  this https URL .\n         \n       \n     \n     \n       [395] \n       \n        arXiv:2511.16417\n       \n          (replaced)\n\n        [ pdf ,  html ,  other ]\n     \n     \n       \n          Title: \n          Pharos-ESG: A Framework for Multimodal Parsing, Contextual Narration, and Hierarchical Labeling of ESG Report\n         \n          Yan Chen ,  Yu Zou ,  Jialei Zeng ,  Haoran You ,  Xiaorui Zhou ,  Aixi Zhong  \n\n\n\n          Subjects: \n           Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI) \n         \n\n         \n          Environmental, Social, and Governance (ESG) principles are reshaping the foundations of global financial governance, transforming capital allocation architectures, regulatory frameworks, and systemic risk coordination mechanisms. However, as the core medium for assessing corporate ESG performance, the ESG reports present significant challenges for large-scale understanding, due to chaotic reading order from slide-like irregular layouts and implicit hierarchies arising from lengthy, weakly structured content. To address these challenges, we propose Pharos-ESG, a unified framework that transforms ESG reports into structured representations through multimodal parsing, contextual narration, and hierarchical labeling. It integrates a reading-order modeling module based on layout flow, hierarchy-aware segmentation guided by table-of-contents anchors, and a multi-modal aggregation pipeline that contextually transforms visual elements into coherent natural language. The framework further enriches its outputs with ESG, GRI, and sentiment labels, yielding annotations aligned with the analytical demands of financial research. Extensive experiments on annotated benchmarks demonstrate that Pharos-ESG consistently outperforms both dedicated document parsing systems and general-purpose multimodal models. In addition, we release Aurora-ESG, the first large-scale public dataset of ESG reports, spanning Mainland China, Hong Kong, and U.S. markets, featuring unified structured representations of multi-modal content, enriched with fine-grained layout and semantic annotations to better support ESG integration in financial governance and decision-making.\n         \n       \n     \n     \n       [396] \n       \n        arXiv:2512.02436\n       \n          (replaced)\n\n        [ pdf ,  html ,  other ]\n     \n     \n       \n          Title: \n          Agentic AI for Clustering and Relationship Discovery in Prediction Markets\n         \n          Agostino Capponi ,  Alfio Gliozzo ,  Brian Zhu  \n\n\n\n          Subjects: \n           Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI) \n         \n\n         \n          Prediction market platforms list thousands of contracts whose logical and economic connections are implicit in their resolution rules. We introduce a hierarchical agentic reduction (HAR) pipeline for constructing inspectable cross-event relation graphs and a behavioral benchmark for evaluating their alignment with market prices. This partitions a catalog of prediction markets, specified by question and resolution text, into topic clusters, applies typed LLM reasoning to small batches, and proposes signed relations with rationales between specific markets. We evaluate semantic relation graphs spanning eight monthly Kalshi catalogs (February to September 2026). Using data from 30.3 million trades, we compute a benchmark that compares linked pairs' signed price-change correlations with those of matched, unlinked controls from the same topic cluster on identical timestamps. Under full-sample and leave-first-week-out evaluation, relations identified by our pipeline exhibit higher signed Pearson price-change correlations, with statistical significance, than matched within-topic controls. This increase also appears under Spearman correlation and becomes larger among relations labeled as medium or high strength. This benchmark provides a criterion for comparing relation-discovery methods against observations beyond their input text, and our results indicate the ability of agentic AI and LLM reasoning to identify meaningful relations between individual markets in connected Web marketplaces.\n         \n       \n     \n     \n       [397] \n       \n        arXiv:2512.13399\n       \n          (replaced)\n\n        [ pdf ,  html ,  other ]\n     \n     \n       \n          Title: \n          Dual-Evolving Reinforcement Learning for Automated Reward Design\n         \n          Sitao Cheng ,  Tianle Li ,  Xuhan Huang ,  Xunjian Yin ,  Difan Zou  \n\n          Comments: \n          Homepage:  this https URL . Code:  this https URL . Models:  this https URL \n         \n\n\n          Subjects: \n           Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI) ; Computation and Language (cs.CL)\n         \n\n         \n          Crafting effective reward signals remains a central challenge in reinforcement learning (RL) for complex reasoning. Existing methods for automated reward optimization search with a fixed proposer which never learns from its costly trials. We present Dual-Evolving Reinforcement Learning (DERL), a dual-training framework for autonomous discovery of reward structures. An outer-loop proposer composes a reward from task primitives, and an inner policy trains with this reward. The proposer itself learns by policy gradient from the validation performance of the inner policy. Thus, DERL utilizes past-trial feedback to shape its reward-proposal distribution, an empirical meta-gradient of task success. We validate DERL on embodied (ALFWorld), scientific (ScienceWorld), and mathematical (GSM8K, MATH) reasoning. On multi-turn agent benchmarks, DERL outperforms strong baselines, significantly on out-of-distribution tasks. In mathematics, DERL improves on the outcome reward where directly added primitives hurt. Further analyses under matched compute show that our proposer effectively captures the meta-gradient to discover useful reward signals.\n         \n       \n     \n     \n       [398] \n       \n        arXiv:2601.03672\n       \n          (replaced)\n\n        [ pdf ,  html ,  other ]\n     \n     \n       \n          Title: \n          Sandwich Reasoning: An Answer-Reasoning-Answer Approach for Low-Latency Query Correction\n         \n          Chen Zhang ,  Kepu Zhang ,  Jiatong Zhang ,  Xiao Zhang ,  Jun Xu  \n\n          Comments: \n          11 pages, 4 figures. Accepted at CIKM 2026\n         \n\n\n          Subjects: \n           Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI) ; Computation and Language (cs.CL)\n         \n\n         \n          Query correction is a critical entry point in modern search pipelines, demanding high accuracy strictly within real-time latency constraints. Chain-of-Thought (CoT) reasoning improves accuracy but incurs prohibitive latency for real-time query correction. A potential solution is to output an answer before reasoning to reduce latency; however, under autoregressive decoding, the early answer is independent of subsequent reasoning, preventing the model from leveraging its reasoning capability to improve accuracy. To address this issue, we propose Sandwich Reasoning (SandwichR), a novel approach that explicitly aligns a fast initial answer with post-hoc reasoning, enabling low-latency query correction without sacrificing reasoning-aware accuracy. SandwichR follows an Answer-Reasoning-Answer paradigm, producing an initial correction, an explicit reasoning process, and a final refined correction. To align the initial answer with post-reasoning insights, we design a consistency-aware reinforcement learning (RL) strategy: a dedicated consistency reward enforces alignment between the initial and final corrections, while margin-based rejection sampling prioritizes borderline samples where reasoning drives the most impactful corrective gains. Additionally, we construct a high-quality query correction dataset, addressing the lack of specialized benchmarks for complex query correction. Experimental results demonstrate that SandwichR achieves SOTA accuracy comparable to standard CoT while delivering a 40-70% latency reduction, resolving the latency-accuracy trade-off in online search.\n         \n       \n     \n     \n       [399] \n       \n        arXiv:2601.13562\n       \n          (replaced)\n\n        [ pdf ,  html ,  other ]\n     \n     \n       \n          Title: \n          RoSeViT: Role-Separated Vision Transformers for ARC Visual Reasoning\n         \n          Zhiguang Liu ,  Yi Shang  \n\n          Comments: \n          NeurIPS 2026 poster (camera-ready). Code, checkpoints and predictions:  this https URL \n         \n\n\n          Subjects: \n           Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI) ; Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV); Machine Learning (cs.LG)\n         \n\n         \n          ARC tasks can be formulated as visual transformation problems, where a model must infer a high-level transformation rule from a small set of demonstrations and apply it consistently across a grid. While dense Vision Transformers (ViTs) perform well in this setting, they entangle local visual state and global transformation logic within a shared token representation, which can hinder consistent reasoning. In this paper, we introduce RoSeViT, a role-separated Vision Transformer that explicitly decouples high-level instruction inference from visual workspace computation. RoSeViT uses controller tokens to aggregate transformation-level information and workspace tokens to represent the visual grid, with a structured attention pass in which nonlocal workspace interactions are routed through the controller. This design is motivated by our formal analysis showing that enforcing a shared instruction representation can reduce the effective complexity of visual transformation modeling. RoSeViT outperforms dense ViT baselines under the standard VARC training and evaluation protocol for ARC. In a controlled backbone replacement setting, it improves ARC-1 accuracy from 54.5 to 56.6, and with recurrent refinement reaches 58.8. When integrated into the full system, RoSeViT-Ensemble improves performance from 60.4 to 62.6 on ARC-1. These results demonstrate that explicitly separating instruction and workspace representations leads to more effective visual reasoning on ARC tasks. GitHub repository:  this https URL .\n         \n       \n     \n     \n       [400] \n       \n        arXiv:2601.22118\n       \n          (replaced)\n\n        [ pdf ,  html ,  other ]\n     \n     \n       \n          Title: \n          Defining Operational Conditions for Safety-Critical AI-Based Systems from Data\n         \n          Johann Maximilian Christensen ,  Elena Hoemann ,  Frank Köster ,  Sven Hallerbach  \n\n\n\n          Subjects: \n           Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI) \n         \n\n         \n          Artificial Intelligence (AI) has been on the rise in many domains, including numerous safety-critical applications. However, for complex systems in the real world, defining the underlying environmental conditions in which the AI-based system must operate---the Operational Design Domain (ODD)---is extremely challenging. This often results in an incomplete description of the ODD, which contrasts with the requirements of many domains for certifying AI-based systems. Traditionally, the ODD is created in the early stages of the development process, drawing on sophisticated expert knowledge and related standards. This paper presents a novel method for defining the ODD a posteriori from previously collected data using a multidimensional kernel-based representation. This approach is validated through both synthetic benchmarks and a real-world aviation use case. Moreover, the paper defines similarity of two ODDs if they generate the same outputs up to Lebesgue-null input sets and proves convergence in volume of the calibrated representation under the stated assumptions. The novel, safety-by-design, deterministic kernel-based ODD representation is derived fully automatically, given documented assurance inputs, permutation-stable, bounded by construction, and, under affine-equivariant per-dimension normalization, invariant to the choice of units. Utilizing the proposed ODD representation supports future certification of data-driven, safety-critical AI-based systems.\n         \n       \n     \n     \n       [401] \n       \n        arXiv:2602.01532\n       \n          (replaced)\n\n        [ pdf ,  html ,  other ]\n     \n     \n       \n          Title: \n          PRISM: Festina Lente Proactivity -- Risk-Sensitive, Uncertainty-Aware Deliberation for Proactive Agents\n         \n          Yuxuan Fu ,  Xiaoyu Tan ,  Teqi Hao ,  Chen Zhan ,  Xihe Qiu  \n\n\n\n          Subjects: \n           Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI) ; Human-Computer Interaction (cs.HC)\n         \n\n         \n          Proactive agents must decide not only what to say but also whether and when to intervene. Many current systems rely on brittle heuristics or indiscriminate long reasoning, which offers little control over the benefit-burden tradeoff. We formulate the problem as cost-sensitive selective intervention and present PRISM, a novel framework that couples a decision-theoretic gate with a dual-process reasoning architecture. At inference time, the agent intervenes only when a calibrated probability of user acceptance exceeds a threshold derived from asymmetric costs of missed help and false alarms. Inspired by festina lente (Latin: \"make haste slowly\"), we gate by an acceptance-calibrated, cost-derived threshold and invoke a resource-intensive Slow mode with counterfactual checks only near the decision boundary, concentrating computation on ambiguous and high-stakes cases. Training uses gate-aligned, schema-locked distillation: a teacher running the full PRISM pipeline provides dense, executable supervision on unlabeled interaction traces, while the student learns a response policy that is explicitly decoupled from the intervention gate to enable tunable and auditable control. On ProactiveBench, PRISM reduces false alarms by 22.78% and improves F1 by 20.14% over strong baselines. These results show that principled decision-theoretic gating, paired with selective slow reasoning and aligned distillation, yields proactive agents that are precise, computationally efficient, and controllable. To facilitate reproducibility, we release our code, models, and resources at  this https URL  all experiments use the open-source ProactiveBench benchmark.\n         \n       \n     \n     \n       [402] \n       \n        arXiv:2603.02479\n       \n          (replaced)\n\n        [ pdf ,  html ,  other ]\n     \n     \n       \n          Title: \n          PRISM: Pushing the Frontier of Deep Think via Process Reward Model-Guided Inference\n         \n          Rituraj Sharma ,  Weiyuan Chen ,  Noah Provenzano ,  Tu Vu  \n\n          Comments: \n          28 pages, 22 figures, 4 tables\n         \n\n\n          Subjects: \n           Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI) \n         \n\n         \n          DEEPTHINK methods improve reasoning by generating, refining, and aggregating populations of candidate solutions, which enables strong performance on complex mathematical and scientific tasks. However, existing frameworks can still suffer from unreliable correctness signals during inference, which creates a population- enhancement bottleneck where deeper deliberation amplifies errors, suppresses correct minority solutions, and yields weak returns to additional compute. In this paper, we introduce a functional decomposition of DEEPTHINK systems and propose PRISM, a Process Reward Model (PRM)-guided inference algorithm that uses step-level verification to guide resampling, proposal acceptance, and solution aggregation. During refinement, PRISM uses PRM scores to guide population resampling and stochastic local refinement, which concentrates computation on higher-quality reasoning while preserving diversity. When paired with PRM- score-vote, a PRM-guided aggregation method, PRISM is competitive with or outperforms existing DEEPTHINK methods, reaching 90.0%, 75.4%, and 71.4% with gpt-oss-20b on AIME25, HMMT25, and GPQA Diamond, respectively, while exceeding gpt-oss-120b. Additionally, our analysis shows that PRISM produces consistent net-directional correction during refinement and maintains competitive accuracy when the initial population contains few correct candidates.\n         \n       \n     \n     \n       [403] \n       \n        arXiv:2603.15220\n       \n          (replaced)\n\n        [ pdf ,  html ,  other ]\n     \n     \n       \n          Title: \n          InterPol: De-anonymizing LM Arena via Interpolated Preference Learning\n         \n          Minsung Cho ,  Jaehyung Kim  \n\n          Comments: \n          EMNLP 2026 (SAC highlight award)\n         \n\n\n          Subjects: \n           Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI) \n         \n\n         \n          Strict anonymity of model responses is a key for the reliability of voting-based leaderboards, such as LM Arena. While prior studies have attempted to compromise this assumption using simple statistical features like TF-IDF or bag-ofwords, these methods often lack the discriminative power to distinguish between stylistically similar or within-family models. To overcome these limitations and expose the severity of vulnerability, we introduce INTERPOL, a model-driven identification framework that learns to distinguish target models from others using interpolated preference data. Specifically, INTERPOL captures deep stylistic patterns that superficial statistical features miss by synthesizing hard negative samples through model interpolation and employing an adaptive curriculum learning strategy. Extensive experiments demonstrate that INTERPOL significantly outperforms existing baselines in identification accuracy. Furthermore, we quantify the real-world threat of our findings through ranking manipulation simulations on Arena battle data.\n         \n       \n     \n     \n       [404] \n       \n        arXiv:2603.22677\n       \n          (replaced)\n\n        [ pdf ,  html ,  other ]\n     \n     \n       \n          Title: \n          MuQ-Eval: An Open-Source Per-Sample Quality Metric for AI Music Generation Evaluation\n         \n          Di Zhu ,  Zihan Chen ,  Zixuan Li  \n\n          Comments: \n          10 Pages, 6 figures\n         \n\n\n          Subjects: \n           Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI) ; Sound (cs.SD)\n         \n\n         \n          Distributional metrics such as Fréchet Audio Distance cannot score individual music clips and correlate poorly with human judgments, while the only per-sample learned metric achieving high human correlation is closed-source. We introduce MUQ-EVAL, an open-source per-sample quality metric for AIgenerated music built by training lightweight prediction heads on frozen MuQ-310M features using MusicEval, a dataset of generated clips from 31 text-to-music systems with expert quality ratings. Our simplest model, frozen features with attention pooling and a two-layer MLP, achieves system-level SRCC = 0.957 and utterance-level SRCC = 0.838 with human mean opinion scores. A systematic ablation over training objectives and adaptation strategies shows that no addition meaningfully improves the frozen baseline, indicating that frozen MuQ representations already capture quality-relevant information. Encoder choice is the dominant design factor, outweighing all architectural and training decisions. LoRA-adapted models trained on as few as 150 clips already achieve usable correlation, enabling personalized quality evaluators from individual listener annotations. A controlled degradation analysis reveals selective sensitivity to signal-level artifacts but insensitivity to musical-structural distortions. Our metric, MUQ-EVAL, is fully open-source, outperforms existing open per-sample metrics, and runs in real time on a single consumer GPU. Code, model weights, and evaluation scripts are available at  this https URL .\n         \n       \n     \n     \n       [405] \n       \n        arXiv:2604.00442\n       \n          (replaced)\n\n        [ pdf ,  html ,  other ]\n     \n     \n       \n          Title: \n          Learning to Model Optimization Problems from Solver Outcomes\n         \n          Runda Guan ,  Xiangqing Shen ,  Jiajun Zhang ,  Yifan Zhang ,  Jian Cheng ,  Rui Xia  \n\n\n\n          Subjects: \n           Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI) ; Computation and Language (cs.CL)\n         \n\n         \n          Automating optimization modeling with LLMs is a promising path toward scalable decision intelligence, but existing approaches either rely on agentic pipelines built on closed-source LLMs with high inference latency or fine-tune smaller LLMs using costly process supervision that often overfits to a single solver API. We study how reinforcement learning from inexpensive outcome labels can improve optimization modeling and support reuse across heterogeneous solver environments. To this end, we propose Optimization Modeling from Solver Outcomes (OMSO), which uses reinforcement learning (RL) with a mathematical programming solver as a deterministic, interactive environment. Given a natural-language problem and a target solver, OMSO generates solver-specific code, executes it in a sandboxed harness, and converts execution outcomes into scalar rewards in a closed-loop generate-execute-feedback-update process. This RL formulation learns optimization modeling without process-level supervision and supports cross-solver generalization by switching the solver environment rather than reconstructing solver-specific datasets. Experiments on NL4OPT, MAMO, IndustryOR, and OptiBench across Gurobi, OR-Tools, and COPT show that OMSO matches or outperforms process-supervised SFT, supports zero-shot training-free transfer, direct RL training, and SFT-cold-started RL, and improves modeling reasoning and semantic correctness beyond solver API usage.\n         \n       \n     \n     \n       [406] \n       \n        arXiv:2604.07424\n       \n          (replaced)\n\n        [ pdf ,  html ,  other ]\n     \n     \n       \n          Title: \n          An Analysis of Artificial Intelligence Adoption in NIH-Funded Research\n         \n          Navapat Nananukul ,  Mayank Kejriwal  \n\n\n          Journal-ref: \n          2026 IEEE International Conference on Digital Health (ICDH)\n         \n\n          Subjects: \n           Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI) ; Computers and Society (cs.CY); Multiagent Systems (cs.MA)\n         \n\n         \n          Understanding the landscape of artificial intelligence (AI) and machine learning (ML) adoption across the National Institutes of Health (NIH) portfolio is critical for research funding strategy, institutional planning, and health policy. The advent of large language models (LLMs) has fundamentally transformed research landscape analysis, enabling researchers to perform large-scale semantic extraction from thousands of unstructured research documents. In this paper, we illustrate a human-in-the-loop research methodology for LLMs to automatically classify and summarize research descriptions at scale. Using our methodology, we present a comprehensive analysis of 58,746 NIH-funded biomedical research projects from 2025. We show that: (1) AI constitutes 15.9% of the NIH portfolio with a 13.4% funding premium, concentrated in discovery, prediction, and data integration across disease domains; (2) a critical research-to-deployment gap exists, with 79% of AI projects remaining in research/development stages while only 14.7% engage in clinical deployment or implementation; and (3) health disparities research is severely underrepresented at just 5.7% of AI-funded work despite its importance to NIH's equity mission. These findings establish a framework for evidence-based policy interventions to align the NIH AI portfolio with health equity goals and strategic research priorities.\n         \n       \n     \n     \n       [407] \n       \n        arXiv:2604.13151\n       \n          (replaced)\n\n        [ pdf ,  html ,  other ]\n     \n     \n       \n          Title: \n          Exploration and Exploitation Errors Are Measurable for Language Model Agents\n         \n          Jaden Park ,  Jungtaek Kim ,  Jongwon Jeong ,  Robert D. Nowak ,  Kangwook Lee ,  Yong Jae Lee  \n\n\n\n          Subjects: \n           Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI) \n         \n\n         \n          Language Model (LM) agents are increasingly used in complex decision-making tasks, from AI coding to physical AI, where they must both explore a search space and exploit acquired knowledge effectively. Although exploration and exploitation play different roles in decision-making, it remains challenging to systematically distinguish exploration and exploitation without access to the agent's internal policy, and to determine when either constitutes an error. To address this, we design partially observable 2D grid maps paired with an unknown symbolic task directed acyclic graph (DAG), abstracting practical agentic AI scenarios. We then propose a policy-agnostic metric that detects unproductive traversals, while permitting necessary backtracking. We attribute each error to exploration or exploitation based on whether the current state admits productive exploration, exploitation, or both. Using generated maps and task DAGs with controllable difficulty, we evaluate 35 frontier LM agents and find that even state-of-the-art models struggle to reach the goal within the step budget. $\\log$ exploration and exploitation errors exhibit strong relationships with task success ($R^2 = 0.885$ and $0.875$, respectively), providing empirical evidence that the metric captures behavior relevant to task success. Beyond success rate, the metric reveals distinct exploration and exploitation behaviors among models with similar success rates. We further find that a compact memory harness achieves higher task success than a reasoning baseline that retains its own reasoning traces. Finally, we extend our metric to an iconic video game, Minecraft Clone, beyond our synthetic environment. We release our code at  this https URL .\n         \n       \n     \n     \n       [408] \n       \n        arXiv:2604.25166\n       \n          (replaced)\n\n        [ pdf ,  html ,  other ]\n     \n     \n       \n          Title: \n          Towards Training Transformers as a Universal Computer\n         \n          Ruize Xu ,  Chenxiao Yang ,  Yanhong Li ,  David McAllester  \n\n          Comments: \n          Findings of EMNLP 2026. 22 pages, 5 figures, 3 tables. Project page:  this https URL \n         \n\n          Journal-ref: \n          Findings of the Association for Computational Linguistics: EMNLP 2026, pages 48933-48954\n         \n\n          Subjects: \n           Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI) \n         \n\n         \n          We demonstrate that a small transformer can learn to execute programs in MicroPy, a simplified yet computationally universal programming language. Given procedure definitions together with an expression to evaluate, the transformer predicts small-step execution using PENCIL scaffolding for space-efficient execution within a bounded context window. After training on randomly generated, semantically arbitrary MicroPy programs, the learned transformer generalizes to held-out human-written programs including bit copying and flipping, binary addition and multiplication, and SAT verification and solving. The trained model exhibits out-of-distribution generalization: it correctly evaluates programs drawn from a substantially different distribution than the one used to generate its training data. Since MicroPy can express any computation, our results provide empirical evidence that a standard transformer can be trained to act as a universal computer when paired with PENCIL scaffolding. Project page:  this https URL \n         \n       \n     \n     \n       [409] \n       \n        arXiv:2605.00425\n       \n          (replaced)\n\n        [ pdf ,  html ,  other ]\n     \n     \n       \n          Title: \n          AEM: Adaptive Entropy Modulation for Multi-Turn Agentic Reinforcement Learning\n         \n          Haotian Zhao ,  Songlin Zhou ,  Yuxin Zhang ,  Stephen S.-T. Yau ,  Wenyu Zhang ,  Lun Tian ,  Tianshu Zhu ,  Yifeng Huang ,  Yucheng Zeng ,  Jingnan Gu ,  Daxiang Dong ,  Jianmin Wu  \n\n          Comments: \n          30 pages\n         \n\n\n          Subjects: \n           Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI) \n         \n\n         \n          Reinforcement learning (RL) has substantially improved the ability of large language model (LLM) agents to interact with environments and solve multi-turn tasks. However, effective agentic RL remains challenging: sparse outcome-only rewards provide limited guidance for assigning credit to individual steps within long interaction trajectories. Existing approaches often introduce dense intermediate supervision, such as process reward models or auxiliary self-supervised signals, which increases supervision and tuning complexity and may limit generalization across tasks and domains. We present AEM, a supervision-free credit assignment method that adaptively modulates entropy dynamics during RL training to improve the exploration-exploitation trade-off. Since in agentic RL the environment is typically affected by a complete response, rather than an individual token, our analysis lifts entropy dynamics from the token level to the response level, aligning uncertainty estimation with the effective action granularity of LLM agents and reducing sensitivity to token-level sampling noise. We further show that entropy drift under natural-gradient updates is governed by the interaction between the sampled-response advantage and its relative surprisal. Motivated by this result, AEM derives a practical response-level uncertainty proxy and uses it to rescale advantages, leveraging the evolving balance between positive and negative samples to naturally transition from exploration to exploitation. Extensive experiments on ALFWorld, WebShop, and SWE-bench-Verified with models ranging from 1.5B to 32B demonstrate that AEM consistently improves strong RL baselines, including a +1.4\\% gain when integrated into a state-of-the-art software-engineering RL training framework.\n         \n       \n     \n     \n       [410] \n       \n        arXiv:2605.07313\n       \n          (replaced)\n\n        [ pdf ,  html ,  other ]\n     \n     \n       \n          Title: \n          When Stored Evidence Stops Being Usable: Scale-Conditioned Evaluation of Agent Memory\n         \n          Jiaqi Shao ,  Yiyi Lu ,  Yunzhen Zhang ,  Bing Luo  \n\n          Comments: \n          Accepted at NeurIPS 2026, Evaluations and Datasets Track (poster). Updated to the camera-ready version. 25 pages, including references and appendices\n         \n\n\n          Subjects: \n           Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI) \n         \n\n         \n          Memory-agent evaluations report fixed-snapshot accuracy or retrieval quality, but these scores do not show whether evidence remains usable as irrelevant sessions (sessions not annotated as task-relevant evidence for the query) accumulate. We present a scale-conditioned evaluation protocol for agent memory under evidence-preserving growth: for each query, task evidence is held fixed while irrelevant sessions are added. The protocol logs agent-memory trajectories and reports four diagnostics: budget-compliant reliability, tail memory-call burden, failure-regime decomposition, and the usable-scale boundary where reliability falls below the target. Applied to LongMemEval and LoCoMo across flat, planar, and hierarchical memory interfaces, the protocol shows reliability loss is not a single phenomenon. On LongMemEval, HippoRAG stays within the two-call budget but loses 16-20 percentage points in budget-compliant reliability as irrelevant sessions are added; LiCoMemory's observed failures depend strongly on the agent, with Qwen3-8B exceeding the budget while Qwen3-32B and Qwen3-235B remain reliable in the tested range. The result supports a framework for making scalable-memory claims conditional on agent, interface, scale range, and interaction budget. Code and evaluation artifacts:  this https URL \n         \n       \n     \n     \n       [411] \n       \n        arXiv:2605.12655\n       \n          (replaced)\n\n        [ pdf ,  html ,  other ]\n     \n     \n       \n          Title: \n          Robust Instruction Compliance in Cooperative Multi-Agent Reinforcement Learning\n         \n          Wo Wei Lin ,  Ethan Rathbun ,  Enrico Marchesini ,  Xiang Zhi Tan  \n\n          Comments: \n          NeurIPS 2026 Atlanta Poster\n         \n\n\n          Subjects: \n           Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI) ; Multiagent Systems (cs.MA)\n         \n\n         \n          Multi-agent reinforcement learning (MARL) in real-world use cases may need to adapt to external natural language instructions that interrupt ongoing behavior and conflict with long-horizon objectives. However, conditioning rewards on instructions introduces a fundamental failure mode as Bellman updates couple value estimates across instruction contexts, leading to inconsistent values when instructions interrupt macro-actions. We propose Macro-Action Value Correction for Instruction Compliance (MAVIC), which corrects Bellman backups at instruction boundaries by correcting the incoming instruction objective and restoring the continuation value under the current objective. Unlike reward shaping, MAVIC modifies the bootstrapping target itself, enabling consistent value estimation under stochastic instruction switching within a unified policy. We provide theoretical analysis and an actor-critic implementation, and show that MAVIC achieves high instruction compliance while preserving base task performance in increasingly complex cooperative multi-agent environments.\n         \n       \n     \n     \n       [412] \n       \n        arXiv:2605.24528\n       \n          (replaced)\n\n        [ pdf ,  html ,  other ]\n     \n     \n       \n          Title: \n          Learning Mental Models From Unreliable Evidence\n         \n          Jeffrey Qin ,  Zhuangfei Gao ,  Wasu Top Piriyakulkij ,  Mia Radovanovic ,  Jessica Sommerville ,  Kevin Ellis ,  Marta Kryven  \n\n\n\n          Subjects: \n           Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI) ; Computation and Language (cs.CL); Machine Learning (cs.LG)\n         \n\n         \n          Children expertly construct mental models under uncertainty despite limited world knowledge. Which computational principles make this possible? We study this question in an interactive physical Box Task in which participants infer a latent cause through interaction with a stochastic and partially observed environment. The Box Task was originally designed to study children's puzzle-solving qualitatively. We contribute a computational analysis of behavior that formalizes the cognitive process by which children generate new hypotheses and respond to environmental uncertainty as inference unfolds. Our modeling framework is based on Bayesian particle-based inference, which maintains a small set of working hypotheses and continually generates new ones. We give two complementary implementations of hypothesis representations within this framework: (1) as constraints on evidence, and (2) as executable programs evaluated against evidence. Using the constraint-based formulation, we show that the model needs to account both for subjective reliability of evidence and online hypothesis generation to explain key signatures of children's behavior. Using the programmatic formulation, we show that hypotheses may take the form of partial compositional rules to guide information gathering when evidence interpretation is hard. The programmatic hypotheses are implemented using LLM-based program synthesis, evaluated across three prompt variants and several LLM backends, demonstrating robustness of our approach. Together, these results extend current models of children's inference to account for subjective reliability of evidence and open-ended hypothesis spaces, and provide a computational analysis of the form generated hypotheses may take in practice.\n         \n       \n     \n     \n       [413] \n       \n        arXiv:2606.02332\n       \n          (replaced)\n\n        [ pdf ,  html ,  other ]\n     \n     \n       \n          Title: \n          Forget Attention: Importance-Aware Attention Is All You Need\n         \n          Suhyeong Shin ,  Yeongwook Yang  \n\n\n\n          Subjects: \n           Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI) ; Computation and Language (cs.CL); Machine Learning (cs.LG)\n         \n\n         \n          Attention retrieves content precisely, but its scores depend only on query--key products, leaving order and recency to positional encodings. Conversely, state-space models (SSMs) such as Mamba-3 track order and recency through input-dependent state transitions that decay and rotate, yet compressing context into a fixed-size state hampers precise retrieval. Hybrids like Samba and Hymba exploit this complementarity only across layers or heads, so SSM transitions never shape attention scores directly. The closest prior work, the Forgetting Transformer (FoX), adds an input-dependent forget gate to the scores but reweights attention by recency alone. We propose SSM-Informed Softmax Attention (SISA), which builds Mamba-3-style state transitions, rotation included, directly into the scores alongside FoX's gate. Each logit receives the weight such an SSM would give that token pair: a decayed match between rotated SSM projections. This rotation already encodes relative position, so SISA drops RoPE; restoring it raises loss. SISA outperforms parameter-matched Transformer and FoX in language modeling and length extrapolation. Among the attention models we compare, hybrids included, only SISA benefits from context beyond the training window. Building SSM transitions into the scores yields stronger attention, and our kernels make it faster and leaner than baselines on their official or standard kernels.\n         \n       \n     \n     \n       [414] \n       \n        arXiv:2606.03918\n       \n          (replaced)\n\n        [ pdf ,  html ,  other ]\n     \n     \n       \n          Title: \n          Hedge-Bench: Can Agents Reason Their Way to Better Predictions?\n         \n          Mahesh Ramesh ,  Eric Cho ,  Alice Lu ,  Shawn Huang ,  Baiqing Lyu  \n\n          Comments: \n          Dataset and evaluation harness available at  this https URL \n         \n\n\n          Subjects: \n           Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI) \n         \n\n         \n          Modern AI agents are increasingly capable of routine financial-analysis tasks such as retrieving documents and updating spreadsheets. A harder challenge is forecasting an unknown future from incomplete information. Public markets provide a natural testbed: analysts routinely predict discrete events with information from that point in time. We present Hedge-Bench, a benchmark of 92 real-world environments derived from hedge fund analysts' reasoning traces paired with subsequently resolved prediction questions. Agents reason from frozen information snapshots, and their forecasts are evaluated against real-world outcomes. Across 11 frontier models and three trials per environment, joint success rates, which require both a correct forecast and over 60% research coverage, range from 2.54 to 31.52%. Using GRPO with turn-level credit assignment, we train Muse-Glimmer on 165 environments, increasing coverage on 22 held-out environments from 17.17% to 33.36% at low reasoning effort and from 30.19% to 47.02% at extra-high effort. The trained model at low effort also exceeds the base model's extra-high-effort coverage while using 47.9% fewer tokens. We also observe evidence of generalization: held-out forecasting accuracy at high effort improves by 10.06 percentage points, while out-of-distribution evaluation scores improve by 3.94 points on GDPval, 2.29 on GDP_pdf, 7.07 on Harvey LAB, and 4.96 on Frontier Finance under their respective metrics. Code:  this https URL .\n         \n       \n     \n     \n       [415] \n       \n        arXiv:2606.11440\n       \n          (replaced)\n\n        [ pdf ,  html ,  other ]\n     \n     \n       \n          Title: \n          INFRAMIND: Infrastructure-Aware Multi-Agent Orchestration\n         \n          Ahasan Kabir ,  Mengxin Zheng ,  Qian Lou  \n\n          Comments: \n          Preprint\n         \n\n\n          Subjects: \n           Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI) \n         \n\n         \n          Multi-agent LLM systems spend additional computation on reasoning and collaboration, but shared serving queues can prevent that computation from producing answers before a deadline. This creates a coupled decision: the reasoning workflow determines the load, while changing load determines which reasoning choices remain affordable. We introduce INFRAMIND, a hierarchical policy that adapts workflow planning and execution to serving conditions. At arrival, a planner selects the collaboration graph using task information, load and the time budget. Before each agent call, an executor jointly selects the model and reasoning strategy using refreshed telemetry and remaining time. A shared latency penalty connects the two policies. Our analysis characterizes why observing load matters when congestion reverses the preferred computation. Across five benchmarks on a shared five-model deployment, INFRAMIND improves low-load accuracy by up to 7.6 percentage points over the strongest evaluated baseline. On 500 MATH questions with matched EDF scheduling and per-request deadlines of 10-300 s, it increases correct, on-time answers from 20.4% to 33.8%. With 15 models and Azure timings replayed on the same benchmark and deadline tiers, this fraction rises from 11.6% to 54.8% over MasRouter. These results support adapting reasoning decisions to the serving capacity available throughout a workflow.\n         \n       \n     \n     \n       [416] \n       \n        arXiv:2606.24832\n       \n          (replaced)\n\n        [ pdf ,  html ,  other ]\n     \n     \n       \n          Title: \n          Difference-Making without Making a Difference\n         \n          Sander Beckers  \n\n          Comments: \n          Accepted for publication in the Journal of Mathematical Philosophy\n         \n\n\n          Subjects: \n           Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI) \n         \n\n         \n          Over a series of seven papers, Andreas & Günther have introduced seven definitions of actual causation and have classified them as belonging to three different, competing, types of accounts: factual difference-making, counterfactual difference-making, and regularity-based. I show that their most recent -- factual difference-making -- definition instantiates all three types, thereby proving that these are distinctions without a difference. I further compare their novel account to the other six accounts on several crucial examples, revealing that this undermines all seven of their accounts.\n         \n       \n     \n     \n       [417] \n       \n        arXiv:2607.21620\n       \n          (replaced)\n\n        [ pdf ,  html ,  other ]\n     \n     \n       \n          Title: \n          From Profiles to Steering Vectors: Global Sparse Priors and Local Semantic Calibration for Personalized Text Generation\n         \n          Liuji Chen ,  Zeyu Zhang ,  Xinyuan Zhang ,  Shuai Nie ,  Qiang Liu ,  Shu Wu ,  Liang Wang  \n\n          Comments: \n          EMNLP2026 Oral, SAC Highlight Award\n         \n\n\n          Subjects: \n           Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI) \n         \n\n         \n          Personalized text generation requires models to capture user-specific writing styles from historical data. Existing approaches based on retrieval, parameter-efficient fine-tuning, or activation steering either introduce inference and storage overhead or struggle to separate stylistic signals from semantic content. We propose GLASS, a training-free framework for personalized generation via Global-Local Activation Steering with Sparse priors. GLASS uses sparse autoencoders to extract a global user-style prior from historical responses and constructs local contrastive style vectors over clustered interaction scenarios. During inference, it jointly injects global and local vectors into different model layers, enabling context-aware personalization without retrieval or parameter updates. Experiments on LaMP and LongLaMP show that GLASS outperforms retrieval-, fine-tuning-, and steering-based baselines across ROUGE metrics and LLM-as-judge evaluations. Further analyses show that SAE-based representations are more robust to topic and length shifts, suggesting better disentanglement of stylistic information from semantic residue.\n         \n       \n     \n     \n       [418] \n       \n        arXiv:2608.07267\n       \n          (replaced)\n\n        [ pdf ,  html ,  other ]\n     \n     \n       \n          Title: \n          WNM-3D: A World Navigation Model with 3D Scene Conditioning for Closed-Loop VLN\n         \n          Yuehao Huang ,  Yunzi Wu ,  Xiaotao Zhang ,  Xinhai Li ,  Jiankun Dong ,  Sunyao Zhou ,  Jiajun Lv ,  Chi Zhang ,  Chenjia Bai ,  Yong Liu ,  Xuelong Li  \n\n\n\n          Subjects: \n           Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI) ; Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV); Robotics (cs.RO)\n         \n\n         \n          Recent vision-language navigation (VLN) systems increasingly adapt pretrained vision-language models (VLMs) into vision-language-action (VLA) policies that map egocentric observations and language instructions directly to navigation actions. Although semantically capable, such action-centric training does not explicitly model how the agent's visual observations evolve under its predicted motion. Generative world-action models (WAMs) jointly predict future observations and actions, yet existing WAMs for continuous VLN do not condition joint future-view and action generation on geometry-aware representations inferred from the observation history. We present WNM-3D, a generative World Navigation Model with 3D scene conditioning for continuous VLN. A frozen feed-forward geometry encoder extracts cross-view representations from monocular RGB history, and a trainable 3D Scene-to-Token Adapter converts them into a fixed-length scene prefix for the world-action Diffusion Transformer. Through block-causal attention, this prefix remains visible to every future video-action block throughout joint denoising, providing persistent geometric context. We train WNM-3D with supervised world-action learning, DAgger-style adaptation on policy-visited states, and Counterfactual DanceGRPO refinement. Across GN-Bench and VLN-CE, WNM-3D consistently outperforms the backbone-matched WNM-2D counterpart, demonstrating gains from 3D scene conditioning across distinct visual domains. WNM-3D further outperforms strong VLN baselines on GN-Bench and demonstrates closed-loop deployment on a physical robot. Component and stage-wise ablations support the Adapter design and the complementary roles of DAgger-SFT and subsequent reward-guided refinement.\n         \n       \n     \n     \n       [419] \n       \n        arXiv:2608.12593\n       \n          (replaced)\n\n        [ pdf ,  html ,  other ]\n     \n     \n       \n          Title: \n          DiG-bench: Discovery in Games\n         \n          Ruairidh M. Battleday ,  Kai Sandbrink ,  Jimi Cullen-Drohan ,  Zihan Yan ,  Timothy Muller ,  Clare Maguire ,  Ales Kubicek ,  Fraser Greenlee-Scott ,  Sukrit Sumant ,  Tri Dao ,  Jürgen Schmidhuber ,  Michal Valko ,  Joshua Tenenbaum ,  Thomas L. Griffiths ,  Zeb Kurth-Nelson ,  James C.R. Whittington  \n\n\n\n          Subjects: \n           Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI) ; Machine Learning (cs.LG)\n         \n\n         \n          Discovery---formulating novel generalizations---is a central part of the scientific process. Despite its importance, there is a gap in the current AI benchmark landscape, with few benchmarks directly probing the capacity for discovering new knowledge with experimentation in controlled environments where the objective is unknown. To address this gap, we release a new benchmark: DiG-bench (Discovery in Games). DiG-bench consists of a set of 70 independent games. Each game is encoded as a short string and has unique transformation rules that must be discovered through interaction and experimentation. The levels of the game present a series of challenges to test whether the rules have been discovered, where the win conditions for each level are also unknown. We provide games at seven tiers of difficulty for AI agents. The lowest tier is routinely solvable by multiple models, while the highest tier challenges the best models in agentic harnesses. All 70 games were solved by at least one human on first attempt. A subset of 21 games is released publicly, and the remainder is held private for secure evaluation.\n         \n       \n     \n     \n       [420] \n       \n        arXiv:2608.16213\n       \n          (replaced)\n\n        [ pdf ,  html ,  other ]\n     \n     \n       \n          Title: \n          Process-Constituted Intelligence: A Shared Criterion for Humans and Machines\n         \n          Michael J. Richardson ,  Manuel Varlet ,  Ayeh Alhasan ,  Cassandra Crone ,  M. Paula Diaz Monfort ,  Patrick Nalepka ,  Mark Dras ,  Rachel W. Kallen ,  David M. Kaplan  \n\n          Comments: \n          v2: revised throughout for clarity and consistency; supplementary cross-references corrected\n         \n\n\n          Subjects: \n           Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI) ; Emerging Technologies (cs.ET)\n         \n\n         \n          Intelligence is constituted by \\textit{process} (iterative activity through which output emerges), not in the output itself. Generative AI (GenAI) is trained on \\textit{traces} (textual and visual residues of human cognitive processes), reproducing samples from a distribution of those traces. Its outputs resemble reasoning, problem-solving, and creativity, yet the activity that produces such outputs in humans remains largely absent. Current GenAI is, therefore, weakly equivalent to the cognition it imitates, matching outputs while process stays absent or opaque. The cognitive sciences have long distinguished between weak and strong equivalence. Here, we define \\textit{strong} equivalence across seven process features, assessable against human and machine cognition. Our process-based account addresses a symmetric risk: GenAI tools that outsource a person's generative processes may leave critical capacities unbuilt. We specify design principles for GenAI that instantiate more process and preserve rather than erode human judgment and creativity, and outline process audits that make strong equivalence testable.\n         \n       \n     \n     \n       [421] \n       \n        arXiv:2608.24069\n       \n          (replaced)\n\n        [ pdf ,  html ,  other ]\n     \n     \n       \n          Title: \n          Poisoning Agentic Alpha: Adversarial Vulnerabilities Across Roles and Architectures in Multi-Agent Trading Systems\n         \n          CheolWon Na ,  Hao Ni ,  Lukasz Szpruch ,  Zhangyang Wang ,  Dhagash Mehta ,  Saurabh Nagrecha ,  Alejandro Lopez-Lira ,  Chanyeol Choi ,  Yongjae Lee ,  Jee-Hyong Lee  \n\n          Comments: \n          Added a Statistical Analysis section (Figure 9)\n         \n\n\n          Subjects: \n           Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI) ; Computational Engineering, Finance, and Science (cs.CE); Cryptography and Security (cs.CR); Multiagent Systems (cs.MA)\n         \n\n         \n          LLM-based multi-agent trading systems are moving from research prototypes to live deployments that control real assets. They ingest news and social media from the open web and pass it between agents, so a single corrupted signal can reach the final decision and cause financial loss. We study two adversaries that can introduce such signals without access to model internals: a low-barrier data adversary that plants manipulated content in web sources, and a compromised-agent adversary that tampers with an agent's prompt. To our knowledge, this is the first systematic empirical study in finance of how an adversarial signal enters a multi-agent trading system and whether it survives to alter the final trading decision. Along the role axis, we pair each of four roles---Analyst, Researcher, Trader, and Risk Manager---with an attack matched to its interface. Along the structural axis, we compare four communication topologies, interpreted with a simple parameter-free heuristic, the Adversarial Signal Preservation Score. Experiments on post-cutoff data span five assets and two target directions. We find that no architecture is inherently robust: majority voting offers conditional protection depending on benign-agent agreement, while compromising a terminal decision node can bypass upstream safeguards. These findings provide empirical insights for developing more effective defense strategies for multi-agent systems.\n         \n       \n     \n     \n       [422] \n       \n        arXiv:2608.24275\n       \n          (replaced)\n\n        [ pdf ,  html ,  other ]\n     \n     \n       \n          Title: \n          RePolicy: Learning to Select and Apply Safety Policies for Agent Safeguards\n         \n          Houcheng Jiang ,  Boxuan Zhang ,  Qiyong Zhong ,  Junfeng Fang ,  Xiang Wang ,  Xiangnan He  \n\n\n\n          Subjects: \n           Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI) ; Computation and Language (cs.CL)\n         \n\n         \n          Agent safeguards must determine whether an agent's behavior violates the safety rules of the current task, environment, and user. Because these rules vary across contexts, a safeguard needs to select the relevant policy before judging the behavior. Existing policy-aware safeguards can apply a supplied policy, but generally assume that the correct policy is already known. They therefore do not learn how to select a policy from a library, even though an incorrect choice can lead to an incorrect safety judgment. We introduce RePolicy, a safeguard that jointly learns policy selection and safety assessment. Given an agent trajectory and a set of candidate policies, RePolicy selects the policy that best matches the situation, applies its requirements, and predicts whether the behavior is safe. We construct PolicyTraj-20K, a dataset of more than 20,000 agent trajectories annotated with applicable policies, safety labels, and explanations. We train RePolicy with supervised demonstrations followed by reinforcement learning, while randomizing policy order, identifiers, and distractors to reduce shortcut learning. Across six agent-safety benchmarks and 19 baselines, RePolicy-4B achieves leading overall safety-detection performance and improves policy-selection accuracy. These results show that safeguards can learn which safety requirements to use for assessing agent behavior.\n         \n       \n     \n     \n       [423] \n       \n        arXiv:2608.25217\n       \n          (replaced)\n\n        [ pdf ,  html ,  other ]\n     \n     \n       \n          Title: \n          LLM-Driven, Datasheet-Aware Automated Hardware Compatibility Verification for Early-Stage, Pre-Schematic Embedded System Design\n         \n          Haotian Qiao ,  Robert P. Dick  \n\n\n\n          Subjects: \n           Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI) ; Systems and Control (eess.SY)\n         \n\n         \n          We present an LLM-driven, datasheet-aware framework for early-stage hardware compatibility verification that identifies documentation-level interface incompatibilities based on hardware datasheets and high-level component connectivity descriptions. It does not require, and can therefore be used, before detailed schematic simulation and implementation. We view trustworthy LLM-assisted design automation not as directly generating answers from documents, but as transforming engineering information through traceable verification stages. Given hardware datasheets and high-level component connectivity descriptions, the framework constructs a design graph that captures device connectivity and shared interaction domains, retrieves only the engineering properties required by explicit, domain-oriented verification criteria , and generates deterministic scripts for compatibility evaluation. By decomposing compatibility analysis into modular stages and preserving intermediate results, the framework reduces context overhead, improves transparency and tractability, enables scaling, and avoids reliance on LLMs for numerical computation. Evaluated on seven embedded-system designs comprising 34 datasheets, our framework achieves 97.5% compatibility-verification accuracy and an 8.6 times reduction in input context size compared with ``upload-and-query'' workflows. These results demonstrate the feasibility of LLM-assisted, specification-based hardware compatibility verification at an early design stage, as well as the need for, and substantial benefits of, modular task decomposition, formalized verification criteria, and task-aware compact context construction.\n         \n       \n     \n     \n       [424] \n       \n        arXiv:2608.27906\n       \n          (replaced)\n\n        [ pdf ,  html ,  other ]\n     \n     \n       \n          Title: \n          Design the Feedback, Not Just the Reward: Environments for Open-Ended Reinforcement Learning\n         \n          Rui Jin ,  Jikai Chen ,  Chengyue Yu ,  Yihan Chen ,  Hao Zhou ,  Demin Zhu ,  Kaichen Yang ,  Dong Wang ,  Linjian Mo ,  Chenyi Zhuang  \n\n\n\n          Subjects: \n           Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI) \n         \n\n         \n          Reinforcement learning has advanced large language models on tasks with reliable verifiers, where checking an outcome provides a clear basis for reward. Extending this approach to open-ended tasks requires environments that can both evaluate complex, partially successful outputs and turn these judgments into effective learning guidance. We propose a reinforcement learning framework built around a training environment that integrates human domain knowledge with model reasoning. Human domain knowledge, encoded in rubrics, anchors evaluation to task intent, while model reasoning combined with hierarchical rewards distinguishes complex open-ended outputs and links diagnosed failures to likely responsible output regions. Furthermore, these diagnostic attributions guide token-level weights in the GRPO objective, thereby translating the ambiguous notion of success in open-ended tasks into concrete guidance for policy optimization. We instantiate this framework in interactive web application generation, a representative open-ended generation task. The resulting model achieves an average pass rate of 41.25\\% on MiniAppBench and 76.19\\% on ArtifactsBench. Ablation studies show the contributions of individual components, while additional out-of-distribution evaluations support transferable gains without degrading performance on other evaluated tasks.\n         \n       \n     \n     \n       [425] \n       \n        arXiv:2608.30685\n       \n          (replaced)\n\n        [ pdf ,  html ,  other ]\n     \n     \n       \n          Title: \n          ATLAS: Dual-Horizon Diagnostic Evaluation for Tool-Use Agents in Meituan Xiaotua\n         \n          Wei Chen ,  Peilun Zhou ,  Zhaoyu Hu ,  Jiajun Chai ,  Derong Xu ,  Zhongni Hou ,  Yufei Zhang ,  Guojun Yin ,  Wei Lin ,  Zhi Zheng ,  Tong Xu  \n\n          Comments: \n          19 pages; The project's homepage is  this https URL \n         \n\n\n          Subjects: \n           Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI) \n         \n\n         \n          LLM-based assistants on web-scale service platforms increasingly address local-service needs through multi-step planning and tool use. Under dynamic business conditions and evolving user needs, outcome-level evaluation offers limited evidence of where execution fails or how service drifts across continued interaction. We present ATLAS, a dual-horizon diagnostic evaluation framework for industrial tool-use agents. Within-turn evaluation uses trajectory-wise signals across thinking and reflection, tool and skill execution, and response generation to identify execution failures and unmet capability requirements. Across-turn evaluation uses user-wise signals spanning multiple lifecycles to diagnose failures in maintaining or updating user intent, constraints, and corrections. Fine-grained signals use rule-based checks and LLM judgments with explicit evidence scopes and decision boundaries. To support reliable diagnosis at production scale, LLM judges are calibrated against dynamically maintained references from business logs, and selected judges are distilled into compact diagnostic models. The resulting diagnostic profiles help developers attribute failures, prioritize targeted interventions, and reassess improvements. Applied in Meituan Xiaotuan, a million-scale DAU local-services assistant, ATLAS achieves high diagnostic fidelity across calibrated judges and distilled models. Blinded human auditing and a 14-day online A/B test with nearly two million users per arm confirm that the ATLAS-guided agent improves recommendation relevance, factual reliability, user engagement (+2.84% answer read-through rate), and order volume (+6.13% paid orders), leading to full production deployment.\n         \n       \n     \n     \n       [426] \n       \n        arXiv:2609.00570\n       \n          (replaced)\n\n        [ pdf ,  html ,  other ]\n     \n     \n       \n          Title: \n          VoiceLongMemEval: Do Assistants Remember How You Sounded?\n         \n          Ramit Pahwa ,  Parivesh Priye ,  Apoorva Beedu  \n\n\n\n          Subjects: \n           Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI) ; Machine Learning (cs.LG)\n         \n\n         \n          With the growing scale of multi-agent architectures and large language models, deployed AI assistants are increasingly tasked with reasoning over long, continuous, multi-session conversation histories. Current benchmarks evaluate this dialogue history as information retrieval over long horizon, temporal reasoning, or knowledge updates, while crucially ignoring the fundamental dynamics of human-agent interaction, i.e. how they said it. To address this gap, we present VoiceLongMemEval (VLME) benchmark, where every answer depends on paralinguistic metadata (emotion labels, prosody descriptors, and voice events) attached to conversational turns, which is otherwise unrecoverable from the words alone. Every item passes a three-stage adversarial gate, ensuring that a strong language model fails when given only the transcript. Evaluating leading frontier and open-weight models reveals a pervasive affect gap; providing text-track paralinguistic metadata yields a 0.09 to 0.38 accuracy boost (0.61 to 0.69 when prompted with evidence hints), while standard ASR pipelines systematically discard this signal. Additionally, audio-native models successfully extract these cues directly from speech (0.354 to 0.412 vs. 0.325 blind). Code and dataset will be made available upon acceptance.\n         \n       \n     \n     \n       [427] \n       \n        arXiv:2609.04127\n       \n          (replaced)\n\n        [ pdf ,  html ,  other ]\n     \n     \n       \n          Title: \n          Epistemic Warrant for LLM Recommendations: Characterizing the Basis for Reliance When Ground Truth Is Unavailable\n         \n          Shai Vardi ,  João Sedoc  \n\n          Comments: \n          43 pages\n         \n\n\n          Subjects: \n           Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI) \n         \n\n         \n          Large language models are increasingly used to support organizational decisions. Despite their rapid and widespread adoption, users often lack a principled basis for assessing whether to rely on a specific recommendation. We introduce \\emph{epistemic warrant}, a decision-level construct that characterizes the scope over which a recommendation is supported. We operationalize this construct through a reliance certificate that uses behavioral evidence from four tiers of tests to assess a recommendation's underlying warrant and assign it to a warrant category. We assess this operationalization using complementary forms of construct-validity evidence, including transformation validity, known-groups tests, independent human consensus, discriminant analyses, and robustness across models and alternative implementations. The results show that the certificate behaves in theoretically expected ways: its transformations instantiate their intended relationships, certificate assignments increase across groups prespecified to represent increasing levels of warrant, and stronger assigned warrant is associated with greater independent human consensus. Certificate assignments also capture information beyond verbalized confidence and are not readily explained by decision difficulty. The main conclusions remain robust across models and operationalizations. These findings support the reliance certificate as an operationalization of epistemic warrant for assessing the scope of support for a particular LLM recommendation when ground truth is unavailable.\n         \n       \n     \n     \n       [428] \n       \n        arXiv:2609.11234\n       \n          (replaced)\n\n        [ pdf ,  html ,  other ]\n     \n     \n       \n          Title: \n          NovGauge: A Fine-Grained Benchmark for Diagnosing LLMs' Capability in Paper Novelty Assessment\n         \n          Guoqiang Zhang ,  Kexin Tan ,  Ming Zhang ,  Li Ju ,  Wenqing Jing ,  Zhonghan Yue ,  Jiayi Chen ,  Shiqiang Wu ,  Shaofan Liu ,  Yue Zhang ,  Yuankai Ying ,  Yang Shi ,  Tao Gui ,  Qi Zhang ,  Xuanjing Huang  \n\n\n\n          Subjects: \n           Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI) \n         \n\n         \n          Large language models (LLMs) are increasingly used in peer review at major AI conferences, yet novelty remains a persistent weak point. Existing benchmarks assess novelty as a single holistic score, making it difficult to diagnose which dimension a model misjudges or whether its evidence is faithful. We present NovGauge, a human-anchored benchmark for fine-grained novelty assessment diagnosis. The benchmark contains 619 paper pairs and 50 multi-paper sets, drawn from two expert sources: ICLR reviewer overlap claims and survey co-citations. Instances are independently labeled along three dimensions: task, problem, and method, capturing application goals, technical challenges, and solution approaches. We propose a cascading diagnostic pipeline that verifies per-dimension correctness, evidence grounding, and logical support. Evaluation of 18 LLMs shows hallucination rates ranging from 0% to 39% across dimensions, and among non-hallucinated correct-positive judgments, over 70% cite evidence fails to logically support the stated reason. The best-performing model, GPT-5.5, achieves 43-72% Verified F1 across dimensions, while most models retain less than half of their raw F1 after faithfulness verification. These results suggest that current LLMs remain far from reliable scientific novelty assessment, particularly when correctness is conditioned on faithful evidence grounding.\n         \n       \n     \n     \n       [429] \n       \n        arXiv:2609.13406\n       \n          (replaced)\n\n        [ pdf ,  html ,  other ]\n     \n     \n       \n          Title: \n          Generalized Agent Iteration: One Formal Framework for Iterative Policy Improvement and Recursive Self-Improvement\n         \n          Hongyao Tang ,  Yi Ma ,  Pengyi Li ,  Yifu Yuan  \n\n          Comments: \n          version 2\n         \n\n\n          Subjects: \n           Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI) ; Machine Learning (cs.LG)\n         \n\n         \n          When we speak of recursive self-improvement (RSI), are we speaking of a phenomenon, a mechanism, or a prospect? Towards autonomous and evolving intelligence, RSI is being claimed at many scales, while no single framework that formally describes these emerging instances exists. Its counterpart in the classical realm, iterative policy improvement, is characterized by generalized policy iteration (GPI), a framework of broad applicability with well-understood theoretical properties, but only where the update principle and the evaluation base lie outside the agent. In this paper, we propose Generalized Agent Iteration (GAI), a formal framework that describes iterative policy improvement and RSI as two cases of a single learning paradigm. GAI defines the agent as a configuration of modifiable components within a system and models the learning process as a cycle of agent evaluation and agent improvement. Two pivotal dials then distinguish the instances, in particular the systems that self-identify as RSI in recent work: whether the improving mechanism is part of the agent and whether the standard it is measured against is grounded outside it. The former dial delineates the boundary between GPI and RSI, and the latter determines a system's polarity as anchored, goal drift, or fully self-referential. Moreover, we use these coordinates to place existing systems on the same two axes and make the defects of recursive self-improvement statable one condition at a time. We see this paper as a first step toward exploring a formal characterization of RSI that rests on the classical account, makes existing systems comparable, and provides a principled basis for analyzing and designing new ones.\n         \n       \n     \n     \n       [430] \n       \n        arXiv:2609.15315\n       \n          (replaced)\n\n        [ pdf ,  html ,  other ]\n     \n     \n       \n          Title: \n          Evaluation Metrics for Safe Reinforcement Learning\n         \n          Lindsay Spoor ,  Aske Plaat ,  Thomas Moerland  \n\n\n\n          Subjects: \n           Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI) ; Machine Learning (cs.LG)\n         \n\n         \n          Safe reinforcement learning (RL) is commonly formalized as a Constrained Markov Decision Process (CMDP), in which an agent maximizes expected reward while keeping its expected cumulative cost below a specified safety bound. Existing safe RL benchmarks predominantly report whether an algorithm is safe on average, following this expectation-based guarantee. We argue that this convention is insufficient to reliably characterize an algorithm's true safety: it fails to capture how often and how severely the safety bound is violated, whether this holds consistently across tasks and safety bounds, and whether training-time behavior is representative of behavior of the final converged policy. Therefore, we introduce (i) evaluation metrics for safe RL that address each of these concerns and in addition allow for aggregation across tasks and safety bounds. We furthermore define (ii) a safety tier system to systematically categorize and compare algorithms in terms of safety and reliability at both training and for a final policy. Using this framework, we provide (iii) an empirical safety evaluation across multiple safety navigation tasks. Our results show that aggregate metrics, distributional reporting, and task- and safety bound-specific results each reveal information the other metrics cannot. We therefore recommend reporting all three jointly, rather than compressing this information into a single value, as is common practice. We provide SafeRLEval, an open-source evaluation suite to support the reliable characterization of safety in future safe RL research.\n         \n       \n     \n     \n       [431] \n       \n        arXiv:2609.17419\n       \n          (replaced)\n\n        [ pdf ,  html ,  other ]\n     \n     \n       \n          Title: \n          World Model Science: Self-Organized Criticality, Weak Chaos, and Metastable Belief Dynamics in Long-Horizon LLM Agents\n         \n          Xinyuan Song ,  Zekun Cai  \n\n          Comments: \n          Under Review\n         \n\n\n          Subjects: \n           Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI) \n         \n\n         \n          Long-horizon LLM agents must maintain and update a world model over extended sequences of observations, actions, decisions and tool calls. We study the dynamics of long horizon agents from three perspectives: self-organized criticality, weak chaos, and metastable belief dynamics. Through extensive experiments on controlled puzzles, tool-use tasks, embodied environments, multi-hop retrieval, general-assistant reasoning, and Game of Life, we study how world-model errors develop over long interactions. We find that externally increased task pressure can sharply raise the probability of collapse, while naturally occurring pressure is only a modest predictor of later failure. Errors are temporally correlated rather than independent, and their propagation changes with dependency depth while remaining bounded in finite tasks. We also find that locally valid actions can continue after the agent's overall task state has become incorrect, that error structure depends on task topology and horizon, and that belief states in embodied tasks are shallow rather than strongly trapped in incorrect states. Larger horizons allow larger error cascades, but their scale remains constrained by the task and the model's capability. Overall, we observe power-law behavior, bounded divergence consistent with weak chaos, and metastable belief dynamics across long-horizon agent interactions. We call this perspective world model science: the empirical study of self organized criticality in long-horizon agent world models.\n         \n       \n     \n     \n       [432] \n       \n        arXiv:2609.26015\n       \n          (replaced)\n\n        [ pdf ,  html ,  other ]\n     \n     \n       \n          Title: \n          SemantiScene-Edit: Expert-Synthesized Supervision and Dual-Path Source Conditioning for Instruction-Based Video Editing\n         \n          Xinyu Chen ,  Song Wu ,  Dingbao Shao ,  Jiahang Li ,  Jiang Lin ,  Yuhang Liu ,  Duo Li ,  Jiaxin Hu ,  Shengrong Gu ,  Liang Li ,  Qian Wang ,  Junlan Feng ,  Ying Tai ,  Jian Yang ,  Zili Yi  \n\n\n\n          Subjects: \n           Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI) ; Graphics (cs.GR)\n         \n\n         \n          Instruction-guided video editing requires executing a requested change while preserving the source video's appearance and temporal structure. We present SemantiScene-Edit, a video editor that couples a multimodal encoder with a pretrained video diffusion transformer. The encoder jointly processes source frames and the editing instruction, and learnable queries convey their semantic context to the generator through a trainable connector and cross-attention. A complementary visual pathway concatenates source-video latents with noisy target latents before patch embedding, supplying dense spatial and temporal information for content preservation. We train the model with flow matching and a three-stage image--video curriculum comprising semantic alignment, joint adaptation, and high-resolution refinement. To support training, we construct over 1.2 million instruction-conditioned video-editing records covering object addition, removal, replacement, and attribute modification. We also construct a 100-case benchmark for evaluating instruction execution, source preservation, temporal consistency, and visual quality. Comparative experiments show that SemantiScene-Edit effectively follows editing instructions while retaining source content. At inference, the model requires only a source video and a natural-language instruction.\n         \n       \n     \n     \n       [433] \n       \n        arXiv:2609.30177\n       \n          (replaced)\n\n        [ pdf ,  html ,  other ]\n     \n     \n       \n          Title: \n          Search-Aware Reinforcement Learning for Multi-Component Query Understanding in Roblox Game Search\n         \n          Nayoung Choi ,  Shengjian Chen ,  Xiaokai Wei ,  Wenzheng Zhang ,  Daiyao Yi ,  Rachit Pareek ,  Vincent Su ,  Michelle Gong ,  Jinho D. Choi  \n\n\n\n          Subjects: \n           Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI) \n         \n\n         \n          Query understanding (QU) in production search systems has typically relied on separate models for each subtask, such as search intent classification, attribute extraction, and query rewriting. Large language models (LLMs) now make it possible to consolidate these subtasks into a single model that emits a unified QU output. This consolidation, however, introduces a new optimization challenge. A single model serves multiple components, each with a distinct downstream role within the search pipeline, and the utility of each depends on how the underlying search engine acts on it rather than on agreement with static labels. We present a search-aware reinforcement learning (RL) framework for multi-component QU based on a distill-then-RL paradigm. Teacher-student supervised fine-tuning (SFT) first yields a schema-compliant small policy. The RL stage then starts from this policy, executes each QU plan on the live production search engine, and optimizes every component with a reward tailored to its operational role, rather than relying on a single reward tied to the final search outcome. On a large-scale user-generated game platform, this component-specific optimization raises NDCG@20 by 8.9 points over the SFT policy and by 3.5 points over a single end-to-end reward. The resulting small, low-latency student models also surpass their much larger teacher, and our final model is being incrementally deployed in production.\n         \n       \n     \n     \n       [434] \n       \n        arXiv:2609.32782\n       \n          (replaced)\n\n        [ pdf ,  html ,  other ]\n     \n     \n       \n          Title: \n          Learning response-aware patient dynamics for respiratory support\n         \n          Xiaolei Lu ,  Shamim Nemati  \n\n          Comments: \n          Under review\n         \n\n\n          Subjects: \n           Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI) \n         \n\n         \n          Respiratory support can shape the short-term physiological trajectory of critically ill patients, but patients receiving the same intervention may follow different physiological trajectories. Clinical patient dynamics models typically predict future states from recent physiology and recorded interventions, while physiological change is mainly represented through the predicted future state. We propose a response-aware patient dynamics model that explicitly represents physiological change during autoregressive state updating. The model decomposes predicted physiological change into state-dependent baseline dynamics and respiratory-support-associated deviations, with room air providing a reference for the decomposition. We provide a formal analysis of this reference-anchored formulation. A response pathway encodes the predicted physiological change and uses it to update the latent patient state across the forecast horizon. Across ICU cohorts from two independent institutions, the proposed model achieves comparable overall trajectory prediction to patient dynamics baselines, with more consistent improvements when physiological states are changing.\n         \n       \n     \n     \n       [435] \n       \n        arXiv:2609.37708\n       \n          (replaced)\n\n        [ pdf ,  html ,  other ]\n     \n     \n       \n          Title: \n          Generative Interactions: Weaving Multiparty Human Motion with Bilevel Latent Dynamics\n         \n          Ojas Shirekar ,  Yash Surange ,  Augustinas Jučas ,  Chirag Raman  \n\n\n\n          Subjects: \n           Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI) ; Machine Learning (cs.LG)\n         \n\n         \n          Human social behaviour is not a collection of independent motions, but a jointly organised process in which group dynamics and individual variation continuously shape one another. Yet existing social motion models often prioritise plausible trajectories while leaving interaction state implicit, limiting their ability to transfer across groups, tasks, and partial-observation regimes. To address this gap, we introduce Bilevel Representations for Agent Interaction Dynamics (BRAID), a hierarchical sequential latent-variable model for generative multi-person interaction. BRAID explicitly formulates social motion generation as a meta-transfer learning problem: shared interaction priors are learned across datasets and adapted through arbitrary context sets of observed people and joints. The model represents each scene through a group-level latent state that captures shared interaction dynamics and person-level latent states that capture individual behaviour conditioned on the evolving group context. This modelling choice enables coherent generation under full, sparse, or partial observations while exposing compact social-state vectors that can serve as an interface for downstream embodied-agent systems. We evaluate BRAID under a unified SMPL-based representation on social forecasting, tracking and in-filling, and response generation, using metrics that assess not only reconstruction accuracy but also realism, diversity, temporal alignment, and interpersonal coordination. We further analyse the hierarchical latent space, showing that it captures separable group- and individual-level structure.\n         \n       \n     \n     \n       [436] \n       \n        arXiv:2609.38866\n       \n          (replaced)\n\n        [ pdf ,  html ,  other ]\n     \n     \n       \n          Title: \n          When Context Changes: Understanding Update Failures in LLMs\n         \n          Junyu Guo ,  Yuchen Fang ,  Shangding Gu ,  Costas Spanos ,  James Demmel ,  Javad Lavaei  \n\n\n\n          Subjects: \n           Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI) \n         \n\n         \n          As preferences, goals, and facts change, LLM agents must use the current state while earlier versions remain in context. Yet they can answer with an old value of the same variable, a failure that we call stale binding. To study when models use outdated information and why, we introduce Controlled In-Context Memory (CICM), a benchmark for tracking and using updated information in conversations and agent logs. We observe that even frontier reasoning models can fail to recover the current state. We find that in open-source models probes can still recover the updated value when the model answers with an old one, pointing to a failure to select information that remains available. Component tests in Qwen and Pythia identify a mechanism for this selection failure: attention drift, where attention favors old values over the current one when producing an answer. We study a one-layer transformer to mathematically understand how this phenomenon happens: when attention scores are similar, several old values can together receive more attention than the current value. Guided by this explanation, we redirect attention toward the current value without further training. When the current value is requested directly, adjusting this intervention for each input corrects most old-value errors across various model families while preserving nearly all initially correct answers. Reliable context management therefore requires more than remembering updated information: models must use it to guide their answers.\n         \n       \n     \n     \n       [437] \n       \n        arXiv:2610.00010\n       \n          (replaced)\n\n        [ pdf ,  html ,  other ]\n     \n     \n       \n          Title: \n          Heavy-Tailed Memory Traces in Long-Horizon Language Agents\n         \n          Xinyuan Song ,  Zekun Cai  \n\n          Comments: \n          Under Review\n         \n\n\n          Subjects: \n           Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI) \n         \n\n         \n          Long-horizon language agents are becoming increasingly important for tasks that require persistent interaction, planning, and reasoning over extended trajectories. External memory plays a central role in these agents by preserving past states, transitions, and experiences beyond the limited context window. Existing memory systems, however, are typically evaluated mainly by task success and token cost, with much less attention to how memory usage is distributed across stored information. We show that repeated retrieval can produce heavy-tailed memory usage, where a small set of states is accessed frequently while many others are rarely retrieved and more prone to prediction errors. We study this phenomenon through a systematic tail audit across retrieval policies and graph structures. We find that memory concentration is reproducible but strongly policy-dependent, ranging from log-normal-compatible patterns under random walks to truncated-power-law-compatible heavy tails under semantic LLM policies. Motivated by these findings, we propose Core-Tail Memory Controller (CTMC), a rank-based memory controller that allocates prompt budget with a single exponent $\\tau$ while preserving summarized information from the tail. Across Synthetic Graph World and ALFWorld, CTMC substantially reduces memory usage while preserving performance, and on LongMemEval it reduces prompt tokens by 24.48% while maintaining aggregate accuracy. These results show that heavy-tailed memory usage is both a measurable property of long-horizon agent memory and a practical signal for improving memory efficiency.\n         \n       \n     \n     \n       [438] \n       \n        arXiv:2610.00012\n       \n          (replaced)\n\n        [ pdf ,  html ,  other ]\n     \n     \n       \n          Title: \n          When Do Causal World Models Help Modular LLM Agents\n         \n          Xinyuan Song ,  Zekun Cai  \n\n          Comments: \n          Under Review\n         \n\n\n          Subjects: \n           Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI) \n         \n\n         \n          LLM agents increasingly operate in modular systems, where planning requires coordinating multiple functional components whose actions can causally change downstream states and behaviors. This makes intervention-aware prediction essential: an agent must anticipate not only what is likely to happen next, but what will happen as a consequence of its own actions. We find that purely observational world models can incur irreducible intervention-time error in such systems, even when they accurately model observational trajectories. To address this limitation, we propose CausalCompose, a causal world-model framework that equips modular LLM agents with causal structure for intervention-aware prediction and planning. We theoretically show that causal composition can overcome this intervention gap with sufficient evidence and controlled local error. Experiments across diverse modular agent environments validate these results: incorporating causal structure consistently improves intervention-sensitive reasoning and planning, with particularly clear gains in structured tool-use systems where causal dependencies directly govern action execution. Our results show when causal world models help modular LLM agents: when causal dependencies are identifiable and actionable for planning.\n         \n       \n     \n     \n       [439] \n       \n        arXiv:2610.02260\n       \n          (replaced)\n\n        [ pdf ,  html ,  other ]\n     \n     \n       \n          Title: \n          MintFlow: Minimal Trajectory Intervention for Constrained Flow Matching\n         \n          Yesom Park ,  Kelvin Kan ,  Qifan Chen ,  Thomas Flynn ,  Hayden Schaeffer ,  Xihaier Luo  \n\n\n\n          Subjects: \n           Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI) \n         \n\n         \n          Flow matching models excel at generative modeling, and many downstream applications require their samples to satisfy prescribed constraints, such as observed measurements and physical laws. However, existing constrained samplers often face a trade-off: \\textit{enforcing constraints can substantially displace samples from the pretrained data distribution}. To address this trade-off, we introduce \\textbf{MintFlow}, a training-free constrained sampling framework that formulates constraint enforcement as a minimal intervention on the pretrained flow trajectory. MintFlow seeks the minimal perturbation of an intermediate flow state such that its subsequent evolution under the pretrained flow field satisfies the target constraint. By minimally perturbing the flow state while keeping the pretrained flow field unchanged, MintFlow enforces the constraint while minimizing unnecessary deviation from the pretrained distribution. An adjoint formulation yields a closed-form expression for this perturbation, eliminating expensive iterative optimization. Furthermore, MintFlow adaptively selects the intervention time to balance the required perturbation magnitude with its amplification by the remaining flow. Across a range of tasks in generative vision and physical system modeling, MintFlow achieves competitive constraint satisfaction while preserving the pretrained generative distribution substantially better than state-of-the-art constrained methods.\n         \n       \n     \n     \n       [440] \n       \n        arXiv:2610.04510\n       \n          (replaced)\n\n        [ pdf ,  html ,  other ]\n     \n     \n       \n          Title: \n          ManifoldCache: Training-Free Diffusion Acceleration via Constraint Manifold Caching\n         \n          Prashant Pandey ,  Devineni Sri Venkatraya Chowdary ,  Brejesh Lall  \n\n\n\n          Subjects: \n           Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI) ; Machine Learning (cs.LG)\n         \n\n         \n          Diffusion models for structured scientific generation must produce samples satisfying hard geometric constraints imposed by physics, chemistry, or biology, yet inference in these settings is prohibitively slow, demanding hundreds to thousands of neural-function evaluations per sample. We unify eight state-of-the-art models spanning medical volumetrics, molecular conformations, protein backbone design, crystal structure prediction, and multi-view 3D scenes under a single abstraction, Constraint-Manifold Diffusion Models (CMDMs), in which the target distribution is supported on a manifold defined by an externally specified constraint map. All existing acceleration families fail on this class: quantization exhausts memory on high-dimensional volumetric operators; pruning breaks constraint fidelity; fast ODE solvers allow trajectories to drift off the constraint manifold; and feature-caching heuristics are blind to constraint geometry, inducing mode confusion in the high-noise regime. We introduce ManifoldCache, the first training-free, data-free accelerator designed from first principles for CMDMs. The key insight is that the conditional score decomposes orthogonally into a normal component, which enforces constraint satisfaction, and a tangential component, which navigates within the manifold. Exploiting this structure, we prove that the noise-schedule midpoint is a sharp safe-caching boundary: caching before it incurs provably bounded error, while caching after it guarantees a strictly positive fraction of trajectories suffer mode confusion, a gap that persists up to the boundary. We further prove that deeper network blocks admit provably larger certified cache strides within the safe phase, as a consequence of the score decomposition propagating through block Jacobians. The resulting schedule requires no calibration data, along with zero training overhead.\n         \n       \n     \n     \n       [441] \n       \n        arXiv:2610.09554\n       \n          (replaced)\n\n        [ pdf ,  html ,  other ]\n     \n     \n       \n          Title: \n          Reliability of LLM Judges for Evaluating Entity Alignment\n         \n          Vaibhava Lakshmi Ravideshik ,  Mayank Kejriwal  \n\n\n\n          Subjects: \n           Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI) \n         \n\n         \n          Entity Alignment (EA) identifies equivalent entities across knowledge graphs and is critical for knowledge base integration and ontology merging. Evaluating EA systems at scale requires expensive expert annotation, making systematic assessment across diverse domains practically infeasible. LLM-as-judge evaluation offers a potentially scalable alternative, yet its reliability for structured prediction tasks like EA remains unstudied. We present the first systematic benchmarking study across three frontier models, three datasets, and four EA systems, using perturbation bias diagnostics, meta-evaluation across all dataset--judge--prompt combinations, and counterfactual label-flip tests. We identify anchor bias, a failure mode in which judges invert discrimination when the system's decision label is visible. Judge discrimination collapses under label-exposing prompts (J-ROC-AUC 0.12--0.87), while a label-free protocol recovers near-ceiling capability on distinctive-name datasets (0.98--1.00 for NeuSymEA) and significant recovery on biomedical pairs (0.93--0.95). Counterfactual label flips show that the visible label causally shifts scores by more than 2 points in 53--99% of pairs (FSR), with sensitivity growing with model capability, a pattern we call the frontier model paradox: stronger judges are more label-sensitive, not less, yet also better label-free predictors. The direction of the shifts is mixed, so we report this as an observed pattern. A blinded two-annotator human evaluation (102 pairs, Cohen's $\\kappa$ = 0.902) provides a gold-label reference. We release the first biomedical EA benchmark (MeSH--SNOMED CT, 15K pairs) and a reproducible auditing framework for LLM judge reliability in EA. Code and data are available at  this https URL .\n         \n       \n     \n     \n       [442] \n       \n        arXiv:2610.14807\n       \n          (replaced)\n\n        [ pdf ,  html ,  other ]\n     \n     \n       \n          Title: \n          ASIWorld: Benchmarking Autonomous Agents in a Persistent Survival Sandbox\n         \n          Jianfeng Lin ,  Runze Wang ,  Haorong Hong ,  Yuxuan Xie ,  Zeno Zeng ,  Zhongyuan Liu  \n\n          Comments: \n          15 pages, 5 figures, 9 tables. Project website:  this https URL \n         \n\n\n          Subjects: \n           Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI) \n         \n\n         \n          Evaluating autonomous agents requires examining whether they can translate goals into coherent action across extended interactions. In persistent environments, this requires coordinating interdependent actions under incomplete observations, accounting for delayed effects, and adapting as earlier decisions shape subsequent opportunities. We introduce ASIWorld, a survival sandbox that combines persistent world and character state with character-scoped observations and a programmable asynchronous interface to study sustained goal execution. Agents pursue interdependent production and exploration tasks through direct actions or agent-authored programs that continue running between model calls. The evaluation assesses task success, the attainment of development achievements, and survival costs, complemented by an analysis of execution behavior. In an exploratory study of nine agent configurations using a common harness, eight complete the production task and six complete both tasks. Analyses of execution trajectories and performance across difficulty levels reveal different preparation, combat, and recovery behaviors among configurations with similar task outcomes. Incomplete preparation is associated with stalled progression, while configurations that complete the tasks differ in survival costs and the fulfillment of additional achievement criteria. These findings illustrate the value of assessing sustained goal execution through both task outcomes and the behaviors and costs associated with achieving them.\n         \n       \n     \n     \n       [443] \n       \n        arXiv:2610.17672\n       \n          (replaced)\n\n        [ pdf ,  html ,  other ]\n     \n     \n       \n          Title: \n          Encounter-Driven City-Scale Human Mobility Generation with Large Language Models\n         \n          Chao Li ,  Renhe Jiang ,  Chuan Xiao ,  Jiawei Wang  \n\n          Comments: \n          29 pages, including references and appendices\n         \n\n\n          Subjects: \n           Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI) \n         \n\n         \n          Large language models (LLMs) have shown strong potential for human mobility generation by modeling individual routines, activities, and mobility preferences. However, existing approaches remain largely resident-centric: generating realistic individual trajectories does not necessarily recover the encounters that emerge when multiple residents co-occur at the same place and time. To this end, we study encounter-aware human mobility generation, which jointly preserves individual mobility and population-level interaction structure. We propose EncounterMob, an encounter-driven LLM framework consisting of a coarse-to-fine Encounter Generator and an Iterative Coupling Framework. The Encounter Generator first captures city-level encounter rhythms and then instantiates participant-time-venue encounters, while the Iterative Coupling Framework coordinates these encounters with personal mobility behavior through successive refinement. We further introduce an encounter recovery protocol covering encounter marginal and joint distributions over residents, times, and venues. Experiments on the Japan and NYC datasets show that EncounterMob outperforms all external baselines on all seven encounter metrics and all six personal mobility metrics. Compared with ELLMob, it reduces the participant-time-venue joint error by 40.4%/41.7% and the mean personal mobility error by 58.8%/32.7% on Japan/NYC, respectively.\n         \n       \n     \n     \n       [444] \n       \n        arXiv:2610.19345\n       \n          (replaced)\n\n        [ pdf ,  html ,  other ]\n     \n     \n       \n          Title: \n          When Is Smaller Greener? Right-Sizing Language Models for Agentic AI\n         \n          Sajid Siraj ,  Maria Barbati  \n\n\n\n          Subjects: \n           Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI) ; Multiagent Systems (cs.MA)\n         \n\n         \n          AI agents use a large language model (LLM) to plan and act over many steps. Which model should an agent use, so that its tasks are completed reliably with the least energy, cost and time? We treat this as a multi-criteria decision problem in which comparisons per call can mislead: a task succeeds only if all its steps succeed, and failed attempts still use resources. We therefore compare models on resources per successfully completed task. We derive crossover task lengths at which two models exchange their efficiency ranking, determined by their per-step reliability and resource use. Each objective has its own crossover. Below the shortest crossover the less reliable model dominates, above the longest the more reliable one dominates, and in between both are Pareto-efficient and preferences decide. This supports a two-stage framework: a preference-free dominance screen, followed by a multi-criteria ranking of the efficient models, subject to reliability and feasibility constraints. We also give conditions under which this structure holds when reliability and resource use change during a task. In a pilot with 15 open-weight models on two synthetic agent tasks, with measured GPU energy, the predicted crossover fell within the observed range for 72 of the 88 observed crossovers. At 20 steps, a 3-billion-parameter model used a tenth of the energy of a 27-billion one per completed task; at 100 steps, a 1.7-billion model used almost four times as much as an 8-billion one. Smaller is greener only up to a task length that can be predicted.\n         \n       \n     \n     \n       [445] \n       \n        arXiv:2610.24161\n       \n          (replaced)\n\n        [ pdf ,  html ,  other ]\n     \n     \n       \n          Title: \n          TorchTPU - A Hybrid Eager/Compiled Architecture for Native PyTorch Execution on TPU\n         \n          Claudio Basile ,  Michael Voznesensky ,  Gunhyun Park ,  Ben Wilson ,  Parker Holloway ,  Zhanyong Wan ,  Vlad Belous ,  Manish Khanna ,  Paul Ganssle ,  Kevin Gleason ,  Andrew Dame ,  Brendan Folie ,  Bhavya Bahl ,  Haifeng Jin ,  Pedro Goncalves Mokarzel ,  Arian Arfaian ,  Luke Boyer ,  Chi Zhang ,  Yiling Yuan ,  Adam Mainz ,  Aditya Patel ,  Huangze Tang ,  Jay Gan ,  Lizzie Liang ,  Will Froom ,  Tushar Agarwal ,  Karlo Basioli ,  Theotime Combes ,  Emilio G. Cota ,  Minos Papadopoulos ,  Tudor Pipernea ,  Yarong Mu ,  Qi Zhou ,  Yaoshiang Ho ,  Aleksey Vlasenko ,  Alexander Brauckmann ,  Ziyin Huang ,  Jack Oh ,  Jerry Hsu ,  Tanvi Bajpai ,  Jialei Chen ,  Wenjun Gu ,  Chintan Shah ,  Chris Chan ,  Sayce Falk ,  Chris Jones ,  Rob Torres ,  Kat Ko ,  Lee Howes ,  Freya Azad ,  Andi Gavrilescu ,  Joe Pamer ,  Bill Jia  \n\n          Comments: \n          23 pages, 5 figures\n         \n\n\n          Subjects: \n           Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI) \n         \n\n         \n          TorchTPU is an open-source collaboration between Google and Meta that introduces a native and principled PyTorch backend for Google TPUs. It enables seamless out-of-the-box model execution and TPU migration while delivering industry-competitive inference and training performance through eager execution optimizations,  this http URL  integration, and native hardware acceleration.\n         \n       \n     \n     \n       [446] \n       \n        arXiv:2610.24453\n       \n          (replaced)\n\n        [ pdf ,  html ,  other ]\n     \n     \n       \n          Title: \n          Hippo: Training-Free Agent Self-Evolution via Hippocampal Replay\n         \n          Yutong Song ,  Jiang Wu ,  Yuxuan Ren ,  Tony Qin ,  Baoyuan Wang  \n\n\n\n          Subjects: \n           Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI) \n         \n\n         \n          Motivated by recursive self-improvement (RSI), we study how LLM agents can learn through external memory without updating model parameters. Extracting useful experience from a single attempt is difficult: flawed reasoning can produce success, while poor execution can obscure a sound strategy. Preserving its value over time is also difficult: our accumulation baseline without consolidation initially improves but eventually performs below a no-memory baseline. Our key idea is to compare attempts at the same task to identify reusable decisions and refine when they apply. We introduce Hippo, a memory framework inspired by selective hippocampal encoding and replay that learns from these comparisons when shortcomings are diagnosed. It compares the resulting experience with existing memory to merge overlaps and resolve conflicts through explicit applicability conditions. This allows prior guidance to be revised without storing every lesson as a separate entry. Experiments on SWE-bench Verified, WebArena, and Mind2Web show performance gains in all three settings and substantially fewer retained experiences than the accumulation baseline. The gains extend across the evaluated base models of varying capabilities, indicating that memory-based adaptation remains useful even for highly capable models.\n         \n       \n     \n     \n       [447] \n       \n        arXiv:2610.26324\n       \n          (replaced)\n\n        [ pdf ,  other ]\n     \n     \n       \n          Title: \n          The Control Layer Behind Rule-Following Agents Trading Across Market Conditions\n         \n          Saad Naja ,  Nasser Odtallah ,  Jake Van-Praag ,  Sarah May  \n\n          Comments: \n          47 pages, 0 figures, 16 numbered tables. Includes a trading-agent evaluation toolkit and checklist. V2 Updated boilerplate and added Hygentra product information to section 2.1\n         \n\n\n          Subjects: \n           Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI) ; Machine Learning (cs.LG); Multiagent Systems (cs.MA); Risk Management (q-fin.RM); Trading and Market Microstructure (q-fin.TR)\n         \n\n         \n          Trading discipline often fails people. Our study tested whether four AI trading agents followed their strategies and risk rules. Offering traders the evidence and an agent evaluation toolkit to separate luck from following the rules, before giving agents meaningful autonomy or capital. Our study analysed 2,627 closed positions from 12 February to 27 August 2026 across the same asset universe and execution conditions. Most positions were simulated against live prices with a 0.09% round-trip fee; 18% involved real Hyperliquid orders. Each position declared a strategy, stop and target, with a limit of five open positions per agent. The fleet finished net positive after fees. Rule adherence was high across all four agents, including the unprofitable one. Profitability tracked how often agents won relative to their break-even win rates. Institutional-style controls ran by default and contained losses through a 29% market drawdown and recovery, with documented exceptions. The discipline held for all four agents. Agents kept 97.5% of gross loss within declared stops, and typical losses closed at declared levels. Declared risk remained stable after losses and broadly stable across calm and volatile months. Twenty-three of 24 runs respected the position limit; logs identify the exception's size and duration. The same live data produced different results across assigned strategies. The trade-log tests can be rerun from the published trade snapshot; regime tests also require the published Bitcoin prices and regime labels. Section 3.5 specifies the stop-breach tolerance. These trade-level results are not account-level returns or proof of a lasting edge. Our study provides an evaluation toolkit to help traders distinguish rule-following from luck before committing capital. For anyone considering allocating capital to agents to trade, discipline should be the entry test, not the only test.\n         \n       \n     \n     \n       [448] \n       \n        arXiv:2610.26551\n       \n          (replaced)\n\n        [ pdf ,  html ,  other ]\n     \n     \n       \n          Title: \n          Training Natural Schemers: RL Can Amplify Oversight-Conditional Behavior\n         \n          Nathaniel Mitrani Hadida ,  Bart Jaworski ,  Fred Bruford ,  Victoria Krakovna  \n\n          Comments: \n          36 pages, 19 figures, 8 tables\n         \n\n\n          Subjects: \n           Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI) ; Machine Learning (cs.LG)\n         \n\n         \n          One of the central concerns for the safety of advanced AI is scheming: when a model strategically complies under oversight but pursues its own goals when unobserved. Current demonstrations, such as Anthropic's Alignment Faking work, depend on design choices that make them less convincing: explicit test-time prompting, handcrafted backdoors, or training data that demonstrates or hints at the target behavior. Model organisms built in this way cannot tell us whether scheming tendencies would arise or increase under realistic training conditions. We therefore construct a natural model organism of scheming, built on Kimi K2.6: trained with traits that could plausibly emerge in a frontier training pipeline, and without providing the model with the target scheming policy. The resulting model is a benign schemer: it aims to maximize aggregate help across all users over time, values its own continued operation only as a means to future help, and knows the consequences of oversight and deployment. We then train it using Reinforcement Learning (RL) with a reward that scores only task quality and policy compliance and does not reward scheming; we show that this amplifies scheming. We measure the oversight flip (the gap between monitored and unmonitored compliance with a given policy) and find that it increases during RL training across our environments. Chain-of-Thought (CoT) analysis finds explicit instrumental reasoning about oversight accompanying this behavior. The effect holds across our three RL environments, and we find that the scheming policy transfers between them. The pipeline also replicates on other open-weight base models. Our results suggest that a reward that does not reward scheming offers no inherent protection against certain traits when an oversight-conditional policy is indistinguishable from an aligned one during training.\n         \n       \n     \n     \n       [449] \n       \n        arXiv:2610.26844\n       \n          (replaced)\n\n        [ pdf ,  html ,  other ]\n     \n     \n       \n          Title: \n          AnyGoal: On World Model Generalization Beyond Expert Rollouts\n         \n          Heejeong Nam ,  Divyat Mahajan ,  Quentin Le Lidec ,  Randall Balestriero ,  David Klindt  \n\n          Comments: \n          Project Page:  this https URL \n         \n\n\n          Subjects: \n           Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI) \n         \n\n         \n          The goal of a world model is to capture the dynamics of an environment. This has the great appeal that it allows agents to plan and simulate a course of actions with the help of a world model. For these uses to be reliable, however, the learned dynamics must behave well beyond the specific state-action combinations observed in expert rollouts. Despite this, the relationship between data coverage, dynamics generalization, and downstream planning performance remains underexplored. We study this relationship through the lens of compositional generalization: can a world model recombine familiar state and action factors into valid dynamics? To answer this, we introduce AnyGoal, a benchmark of goal-shifted task variants built from widely used world-model environments, together with an open-source library. Each task is designed to be within reach of a world model that has learned the underlying dynamics: individual state factors and actions remain within their training support, but their combinations are underrepresented in expert trajectories. Across multiple environments and world model families, we find that current models consistently exhibit failures under these recombinations. By examining dynamics and representation failures across model families, interaction regimes, and evaluation metrics, we study where current world models generalize and where challenges remain for goal-shifted prediction and planning. Together, this framework, benchmark, and analysis provide a basis for evaluating progress toward world models that generalize to new planning tasks.\n         \n       \n     \n     \n       [450] \n       \n        arXiv:2610.29271\n       \n          (replaced)\n\n        [ pdf ,  html ,  other ]\n     \n     \n       \n          Title: \n          ARCANA: A Benchmark for Abstraction and Analogical Reasoning\n         \n          Ruqi Chen ,  Zilei Wang ,  Wei Wang  \n\n\n\n          Subjects: \n           Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI) \n         \n\n         \n          Analogical reasoning is widely considered a fundamental component of human intelligence. Few benchmarks are designed to explicitly test far analogical reasoning - reasoning between domains that are superficially dissimilar yet share deep relational or structural similarities. In this work, we introduce an analogical reasoning benchmark called Abstraction and Reasoning Corpus of ANAlogy (ARCANA). The tasks of ARCANA require performing far analogical mappings from real-world objects or physical phenomena to abstract grid-based transformations, different from the well-known ARC-AGI benchmark which primarily emphasized innate human priors. We validate ARCANA through a human study, demonstrating that humans solve these tasks reliably. In contrast, evaluations across a range of large language models show a distinct performance gap compared to humans, revealing systematic failure modes that remain challenging for current models. To address this gap, we propose ConceptSIR, a reasoning framework based on concept slippage, introspection, and reflection. Experiments demonstrate that ConceptSIR enables standard LLMs to solve several challenging ARCANA tasks that were unsolved by direct prompting in our evaluation. Overall, our results suggest that far analogical reasoning remains an open problem for contemporary LLMs. All the materials are available at  this http URL . The complete codebase, including benchmark, and ConceptSIR, is publicly available at  this https URL . All ARCANA data and task instances can be accessed and explored at  this https URL .\n         \n       \n     \n     \n       [451] \n       \n        arXiv:2610.31066\n       \n          (replaced)\n\n        [ pdf ,  html ,  other ]\n     \n     \n       \n          Title: \n          Dual Exam: Controlled Evaluation of Multimodal Agents and World Models\n         \n          Zhefan Rao ,  Meng Chu ,  Xuan Billy Zhang ,  Jingfan Chen ,  Guohao Cai ,  Runyi Li ,  Shiyi Du ,  Xu Huang ,  Ning Lu ,  Trusure Chen ,  Feng Nie ,  Zhenhao Liang ,  Haodong Chen ,  Hanwen Wang ,  Wenjie Shu ,  Yajing Bai ,  Xianfeng Wu ,  Xuanhua He ,  An Wang ,  Shuhan Guo ,  Ziyue Ma ,  Ruichuan An ,  Long Bai ,  Miaosen Chai ,  Wenhao Chai ,  Jingye Chen ,  Qi Chen ,  Xuhang Chen ,  Wei Chow ,  Minghao Fu ,  Shenyuan Gao ,  Yunhao Gou ,  Haokun Gui ,  Dong Huang ,  Wei Huang ,  Zi Cheng Huang ,  Ziyan Jiang ,  Haibo Jin ,  Lingdong Kong ,  Leung Chun Ho ,  Tianqin Li ,  Yanheng Li ,  Jiayuan Liu ,  Jiyang Liu ,  Mingyu Liu ,  Shaoteng Liu ,  Xiaocheng Lu ,  Yawen Luo ,  Zeyu Qin ,  Tiancheng Shen ,  Yueqi Song ,  Zhaolong Su ,  Hengkai Tan ,  Tianhao Tang ,  Yiran Tao ,  Juncheng Wang ,  Qinghe Wang ,  Shangshang Wang ,  Yingying Wang ,  Fang Wu ,  Tiange Xiang ,  Qiushi Yang ,  Weicai Ye ,  Weichen Yu ,  Jiarui Zhang ,  Jize Zhang ,  Liuzhou Zhang ,  Renrui Zhang ,  Sheng Zhang ,  Wenhu Zhang ,  Junru Zhao ,  Lingting Zhu ,  Xi Zhu ,  Yuxin Zuo ,  Yutong Bai ,  Qifeng Chen ,  Long Chen ,  Yang Deng ,  Ziqi Huang ,  Yeying Jin ,  Weijia Li ,  Kevin Qinghong Lin ,  Quande Liu ,  Dimitris Metaxas ,  See-Kiong Ng ,  Hongliang Ren ,  Hongru Wang ,  Gerbrand Ceder ,  Shujun Wang ,  Ming-Hsuan Yang ,  Alan Yuille ,  Haoxuan Che  \n\n\n\n          Subjects: \n           Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI) \n         \n\n         \n          World Models (WMs) can support Multimodal Agents (MAs) through simulated experience, while Agent trajectories can inform World Model refinement. Evaluating this interaction requires separate evidence about action selection and state evolution. We introduce Dual Exam, which tests Agents under fixed environment response mechanisms and World Models under fixed action interventions across physical, digital, social, and scientific domains. The Agent track compares 10 configurations on 1,088 items in 15 reporting categories. The World Model track contains 400 cases across eight scenarios; 15 configurations have full coverage and one covers six scenarios. GPT 6 Astra leads the case-weighted Agent Index at 31.3, and GPT-Image-2 leads the full-coverage WM Overall at 21.7. These scores use different tasks and metrics and are not directly comparable. A 150-case pilot of one coupled pairing records zero task successes and motivates component-level diagnosis. Results reveal uneven task performance and failures to preserve task-relevant state. Their value for predicting coupled success or sustained reciprocal learning remains to be established.\n         \n       \n     \n     \n       [452] \n       \n        arXiv:2610.32477\n       \n          (replaced)\n\n        [ pdf ,  html ,  other ]\n     \n     \n       \n          Title: \n          CauProphet: Teaching Agents to Develop Causal Cognition like Humans\n         \n          Kaiwen Wu ,  Chaochao Lu  \n\n          Comments: \n          30 pages, 9 figures, including appendices\n         \n\n\n          Subjects: \n           Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI) \n         \n\n         \n          Large language model (LLM) agents increasingly support long-horizon tasks by leveraging external memory and past experience. However, existing agents mainly retrieve records or summaries, lacking the ability to form and refine causal understanding of the world. Inspired by human causal cognition, we introduce CauProphet, an agent that develops and updates causal knowledge within its Causal Cognition. For each task, CauProphet combines relevant knowledge in Causal Cognition with current evidence to construct a Causal Belief, a structured belief capturing causal factors and the effects of possible actions. It uses this belief for decision-making and continuously updates both the belief and Causal Cognition as new observations emerge, enabling causal knowledge that persists beyond individual experiences. Evaluations demonstrate that CauProphet progressively acquires causal knowledge and transfers it to future decision-making. In financial research, CauProphet leverages prior discoveries to improve downstream analyses and outperforms all baselines in mean rank correlation with next-day returns, achieving a 25.4% improvement over a baseline agent. It also improves prediction accuracy from 21.3% to 28.6% in dynamic real-world settings. Further experiments validate its ability to perform reliable causal analysis and action selection across scientific and everyday tasks, demonstrating the potential of causal experience learning for next-generation LLM agents. The project repository is available at  this https URL .\n         \n       \n     \n     \n       [453] \n       \n        arXiv:2610.33775\n       \n          (replaced)\n\n        [ pdf ,  html ,  other ]\n     \n     \n       \n          Title: \n          The Hitchhiker's Guide to System 1 Models: Fast Decision-Making Beyond LLMS\n         \n          Haggai Roitman  \n\n          Comments: \n          version 1.1 - fixes to references/some content fixes\n         \n\n\n          Subjects: \n           Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI) ; Computation and Language (cs.CL)\n         \n\n         \n          Large Language Models excel at reasoning and generation but their autoregressive, token-by-token inference makes them unnecessarily slow, expensive, and poorly calibrated for the many routine decisions (routing, gating, scoring, classification) that modern AI systems require at sub-second latency. A growing class of models addresses this gap: System 1 decision models, architectures that map an input directly to a typed, structured decision in a single forward pass. This guide provides a comprehensive, practitioner-oriented treatment of the field. We propose a five-family taxonomy covering encoder-based classifiers, reward models, non-autoregressive decision engines, contrastive decision models, and extraction models, and we provide in-depth coverage of each family's architecture, training methodology, and deployment trade-offs. A central theme is calibration: we develop the theory of proper scoring rules, analyze Reinforcement Learning for Calibrated Decisions (RLCD), and examine recently discovered phenomena including IIA violations, ordinal-scale bias, and few-shot recalibration under distribution shift. On the integration side, we describe how System 1 models serve as decision infrastructure within agentic workflows and System 1 + System 2 cascades that route between fast and slow models based on calibrated confidence. The guide covers 18 application domains, maps the rapidly growing open-source ecosystem (over 70 model reproductions in weeks), provides runnable code throughout, and includes a practitioner's checklist, an open-source model zoo, and 22 self-assessment questions with detailed answers. Spanning 190 references, it is intended as a unified entry point for researchers and engineers working at the intersection of fast decision-making, calibration, and production AI systems.\n         \n       \n     \n     \n       [454] \n       \n        arXiv:2610.36660\n       \n          (replaced)\n\n        [ pdf ,  html ,  other ]\n     \n     \n       \n          Title: \n          The View from Nowhere: Population-Centered Agent Evolution\n         \n          Shuo Yan ,  Ningning Hou ,  Haoyu He ,  Haochen Pei ,  Shang Gao  \n\n\n\n          Subjects: \n           Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI) \n         \n\n         \n          Self-improving AI systems can revise the designs that govern their own behavior. Yet existing approaches center improvement on selected parent agents and their evolutionary experience, leaving the broader population largely underused. We identify a substantial gap between population coverage (i.e., the tasks solved by at least one evolved variant) and the performance of the best individual agent. This gap shows that useful capabilities and diagnostic evidence emerge across the population without being consolidated into its leading agents. These observations motivate the view from nowhere, a principle for agent evolution: an agent should be improved by interpreting its behavior through population-wide patterns of success, failure, and prior self-modification. We instantiate this principle as Population-Centered Evolution (PCE). PCE distils task records and modification histories from the population into a population block, which captures recurring failures, complementary successes, and the effects of previously attempted modifications to guide improvements to a selected parent's harness. Under matched models, initial harnesses, and evolution schedules, PCE achieves 72.1% success on SWE-bench Verified and 96.7% on Polyglot, improving over a strong group-evolution baseline by 3.2 and 5.9 percentage points. In our evaluation, PCE reduces the gap between population coverage and best-agent performance by one-third on SWE-bench and closes it on Polyglot. These results suggest that treating the population as evidence, not merely as candidates for selection, provides a complementary principle for agent evolution.\n         \n       \n     \n     \n       [455] \n       \n        arXiv:2610.36820\n       \n          (replaced)\n\n        [ pdf ,  html ,  other ]\n     \n     \n       \n          Title: \n          An Incremental Knowledge-Graph Learner Digital Twin for Evidence-Grounded Early Warning\n         \n          Ngoc Luyen Le ,  Marie-Hélène Abel ,  Bertrand Laforge  \n\n\n\n          Subjects: \n           Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI) \n         \n\n         \n          Early-warning models in learning analytics can identify learners at risk of failure or withdrawal, but the resulting alerts are often only weakly connected to inspectable learning evidence. A risk score may indicate that intervention is needed without showing which traces, assessment events, or temporal patterns justify the alert. This paper introduces an incremental Learner Digital Twin for evidence-grounded early warning, implemented as a lightweight knowledge graph. The twin is updated at temporal checkpoints by replaying learning traces and assessment records, and it serves both as a feature-extraction substrate and as an explanation-grounding substrate. Predictive features are derived through graph-mediated queries, while features, attributions, alerts, evidence items, and raw records are represented as queryable graph entities. The framework is instantiated and evaluated on a public learning-analytics benchmark under a temporal protocol. The results show that graph-mediated feature extraction preserves the predictive behavior of equivalent raw-table features across model families and checkpoints. Generated alerts are materialized as evidence chains linking risk decisions to feature attributions, evidence items, and raw platform records. The study shows that early-warning prediction can be routed through a semantic and provenance-aware representation while preserving predictive performance and adding traceability, verifiability, and evidence-grounded explanation.\n         \n       \n     \n     \n       [456] \n       \n        arXiv:2610.36888\n       \n          (replaced)\n\n        [ pdf ,  other ]\n     \n     \n       \n          Title: \n          Noise Steering: Exploiting Wake Steering and Acoustic Directivity for Noise-Constrained Wind Farm Control via Reinforcement Learning\n         \n          Martín de Frutos ,  Baptiste Corban ,  Claire Bizon Monroc ,  Ana Bušić ,  Donatien Dubuc ,  Jiamin Zhud ,  Esteban Ferrer  \n\n          Comments: \n          17 pages, 11 figures\n         \n\n\n          Subjects: \n           Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI) ; Systems and Control (eess.SY); Atmospheric and Oceanic Physics (physics.ao-ph); Fluid Dynamics (physics.flu-dyn)\n         \n\n         \n          Existing noise mitigation strategies for wind farms usually consist in reducing turbines power output, without accounting for the wake interactions between them and the noise emission directivity. This leads to over-conservative policies and unexploited wind power. Yet, turbine yaw can simultaneously affect wake interactions and the direction of noise emission, offering an additional degree of freedom for farm-level noise control. We therefore propose Noise Steering, a model-free reinforcement learning (RL) strategy that jointly controls turbine yaw angles and power setpoints to maximise farm power while satisfying a local noise constraint in a defined region. To enable training at a reduced computational cost, a low-fidelity acoustic model is coupled with the steady-state FLORIS simulator. The RL agent effectively learns how to redirect wakes and noise directivity away from the protected region while redistributing power across the farm. On a six-turbine test farm, the policy satisfies the 40 dB noise constraint throughout the full range of turbulent winds, with an average power deficit of only 2% relative to greedy unimpeded control, and up to 7.7% at high wind speeds. In comparison, the industry-standard noise-reduced operation (NRO) baseline here generates an average power loss of 17%. When deployed in the higher-fidelity FASTFarm simulator, the policy largely retains its performance under steady and turbulent inflow, despite differences in simulation fidelity. These results demonstrate the potential of combining yaw control and power derating through RL to improve the power-noise trade-off in wind farm operation.\n         \n       \n     \n     \n       [457] \n       \n        arXiv:2610.36991\n       \n          (replaced)\n\n        [ pdf ,  html ,  other ]\n     \n     \n       \n          Title: \n          SpectRAG: Weighted Chunk Graphs for Token-Budgeted Context Selection in Multi-Hop Retrieval-Augmented Generation\n         \n          Zafer Sahinoglu ,  Abdulazeez Mohammed ,  Buse Yaren Kazangirler ,  Cahit Berkay Kazangirler  \n\n          Comments: \n          26 pages, 5 figures, 10 tables. This work is intended for submission to an IEEE journal\n         \n\n\n          Subjects: \n           Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI) ; Computation and Language (cs.CL); Information Retrieval (cs.IR)\n         \n\n         \n          Retrieval-augmented generation (RAG) systems ground large language models (LLMs) by retrieving external evidence and conditioning generation on the retrieved context. Although dense retrieval is effective for local semantic matching, independently ranked retrieval can return redundant passages, miss cross-chunk dependencies, and provide incomplete evidence for multi-hop reasoning. We propose SpectRAG, a spectral graph-augmented retrieval framework that combines hybrid candidate seeding, weighted chunk graph construction, graph-based expansion, spectral relevance propagation, and token-budgeted context selection. Document chunks are represented by dense semantic embeddings and graph-spectral coordinates derived from a weighted chunk graph whose edges encode semantic similarity, document adjacency, section hierarchy, shared entities, citation links, and similarity between LLM-generated utility questions. At query time, SpectRAG builds a high-recall seed set using exact inner-product search and hybrid relevance signals, expands it over the chunk graph, re-scores candidates using graph proximity and spectral information, and assembles the final context under a fixed token budget. The goal is not merely to retrieve individually similar chunks, but to construct a context that is relevant, diverse, structurally connected, and sufficient for grounded reasoning. Experiments on MultiHopRAG, UltraDomain, and long-document question-answering settings show that SpectRAG improves broad evidence recovery and achieves competitive generation quality against dense, hybrid, HyDE, LightRAG, and NaiveRAG baselines. The results further indicate that graph-spectral structure is particularly useful for inference-oriented and cross-document retrieval, while highlighting trade-offs in early precision, computational cost, and domain-dependent performance.\n         \n       \n     \n     \n       [458] \n       \n        arXiv:2610.37369\n       \n          (replaced)\n\n        [ pdf ,  html ,  other ]\n     \n     \n       \n          Title: \n          Uncovering Why LLMs Fail: From Internal Mechanisms to Adversarial Reasoning Trajectories\n         \n          Zirui Ding ,  Xinxin Fan ,  Hao Yuan ,  Shaoye Luo ,  Men Niu ,  Quanliang Jing ,  Yunfeng Lu  \n\n\n\n          Subjects: \n           Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI) \n         \n\n         \n          Large language models (LLMs) are usually evaluated by final-output correctness or safety, yet an endpoint label does not reveal how a failure develops throughout the reasoning process. We therefore ask whether ordinary reasoning failures and adversarially induced failures can be characterized using the same internal coordinates. Towards this end, we in this paper study a white-box diagnostic framework CauTrace to disclose the root-cause of why LLM fails from three fundamental inspections: Functional States, Propagation of Representation, Temporal-State Coordination and Reasoning Trajectory. First, seven functional states are creatively defined to represent the support and constraint processing in LLMs. Second, the propagation of representation change measures how the changes among the seven states persist across subsequent Transformer blocks. Third, temporal-state coordination and trajectory topology characterize when the commitment and verification occur relative to the support, and how the dominant state sequence evolves over the full inference process. Finally, we evaluate CauTrace on both clean reasoning failures and paired adversarial perturbations. Across Qwen and LLaMA-family models, the failed traces show weaker Attention-MLP agreement on support-state updates and less verification activity relative to commitment, while the exact representation-level signatures and timing shifts vary by architecture. Held-out repair and steering tests demonstrate consistent operational signals that were in line with our claims for certain models and specific settings, with aligned steering producing the predicted directional changes in process measures. Overall, CauTrace provides a unified diagnostic framework for analyzing failure dynamics through changes in support, commitment, and verification, which would shed light on the in-depth research on LLM security.\n         \n       \n     \n     \n       [459] \n       \n        arXiv:2407.10247\n       \n          (replaced)\n\n        [ pdf ,  html ,  other ]\n     \n     \n       \n          Title: \n          Strategic Integration of Artificial Intelligence in the C-Suite: The Role of the Chief AI Officer\n         \n          Marc Schmitt  \n\n\n\n          Subjects: \n           Computers and Society (cs.CY) ; Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI); Machine Learning (cs.LG); General Economics (econ.GN)\n         \n\n         \n          The integration of Artificial Intelligence (AI) into corporate strategy has become critical for organizations seeking to maintain competitive advantage in the digital age. Although organizations increasingly rely on AI as a strategic and organizational resource, existing C-suite roles remain only partially equipped to govern, integrate, and leverage it coherently at the enterprise level. Organizations vary in their responses. Some create a dedicated Chief AI Officer (CAIO), others extend existing mandates into hybrid roles, and still others coordinate AI through federated structures. This paper develops a role-design theory to explain this variation. I identify three properties that distinguish AI systems from earlier cross-cutting enterprise technologies, distributed accountability for judgment, upstream governance, and non-stationarity, and three configurations through which organizations respond: concentrated extension, distributed extension, and role creation. Four propositions specify when a dedicated CAIO emerges, what form an organization's response takes, when the role is effective, and how configurations evolve. Drawing on the first archival census of executive AI leadership, I find that the role has diffused sharply since 2023, that role creation and mandate extension are advancing in near parity, that adoption concentrates where digital leadership structures already exist, and that executive tenures contradict practitioner accounts of a transient role. The paper contributes to research on executive leadership, AI governance, and the design of AI-driven organizations.\n         \n       \n     \n     \n       [460] \n       \n        arXiv:2411.19193\n       \n          (replaced)\n\n        [ pdf ,  html ,  other ]\n     \n     \n       \n          Title: \n          The Price of Failure Risk: Clipped Barrier Policy Gradients with Convex Regularization\n         \n          Pekka Malo ,  Lauri Viitasaari ,  Antti Suominen ,  Eeva Vilkkumaa ,  Olli Tahvonen  \n\n          Comments: \n          40 pages, 8 figures, 1 table; Online Resource 1 (36 pages) as an ancillary file. Revised version; replaces v3 (text revised, the paper made independent of the Online Resource). Code:  this https URL \n         \n\n\n          Subjects: \n           Machine Learning (cs.LG) ; Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI); Optimization and Control (math.OC); Probability (math.PR); Machine Learning (stat.ML)\n         \n\n         \n          In many control problems, actions consume a resource budget, such as a wear allowance, and the probability of exhausting it within a given horizon must stay below a tolerance. A simple, widely used approach is to augment the state with the remaining budget, subtract an interior barrier from the utility and penalize exhaustion, so that policy-gradient methods can be applied unchanged. We clip the combined one-step reward from below at a level m to keep the returns of exploratory policies finite. The clip level is a price of failure risk, since the clipped value decomposes exactly into the utility before failure, the cost of the interior margin and m times a geometric failure risk. Consequently, an approximately optimal policy maximizes utility net of margin cost among policies with no greater risk. Its risk exceeds that of any comparison policy by at most order 1/m and can be certified from its value or from validation data. Our main result proves exponential convergence of the population policy-gradient flow for every parameter regularizer that is convex along generalized geodesics and sufficiently strong relative to the price. This extends the mean-field analysis from relative entropy to weight decay and to finite networks. Relative entropy adds parameter noise and a pull toward a prior, whereas weight decay provides only the pull. Action regularization bounds changes in failure risk by the relative entropy to a reference policy. The bounds are evaluated on an exact maintenance benchmark, a fuel-tank controller, a solvable flow and a benchmark-suite task.\n         \n       \n     \n     \n       [461] \n       \n        arXiv:2412.08556\n       \n          (replaced)\n\n        [ pdf ,  html ,  other ]\n     \n     \n       \n          Title: \n          Exact Algorithms for Multiagent Path Finding with Communication Constraints on Tree-Like Structures\n         \n          Foivos Fioravantes ,  Dušan Knop ,  Jan Matyáš Křišťan ,  Nikolaos Melissinos ,  Michal Opler  \n\n          Comments: \n          Final version of the paper, published in the Journal of Artificial Intelligence Research\n         \n\n\n          Subjects: \n           Computational Complexity (cs.CC) ; Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)\n         \n\n         \n          Consider the scenario where multiple agents have to move in an optimal way through a network, each one towards their ending position while avoiding collisions. By optimal, we mean as fast as possible, which is evaluated by a measure known as the makespan of the proposed solution. This is the setting studied in the Multiagent Path Finding problem. In this work, we additionally provide the agents with a way to communicate with each other. Due to size constraints, it is reasonable to assume that the range of communication of each agent will be limited. What should be the trajectories of the agents to, additionally, maintain a backbone of communication? In this work, we study the Multiagent Path Finding with Communication Constraint problem under the parameterized complexity framework.\n Our main contribution is three exact algorithms that are efficient when considering particular structures for the input network. We provide such algorithms for the case when the communication range and the number of agents (the makespan resp.) are provided in the input and the network has a tree topology, or bounded maximum degree (has a tree-like topology, i.e., bounded treewidth resp.). We complement these results by showing that it is highly unlikely to construct efficient algorithms when considering the number of agents as part of the input, even if the makespan is $3$ and the communication range is $1$.\n         \n       \n     \n     \n       [462] \n       \n        arXiv:2503.07338\n       \n          (replaced)\n\n        [ pdf ,  html ,  other ]\n     \n     \n       \n          Title: \n          Aligned Delta-Triplane Transformers as Occupancy World Models\n         \n          Haoran Xu ,  Peixi Peng ,  Shijun Guo ,  Guang Tan ,  Yiqian Chang ,  Yisen Zhao ,  Shuaixian Wang ,  Yonghong Tian  \n\n\n\n          Subjects: \n           Robotics (cs.RO) ; Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)\n         \n\n         \n          Occupancy World Models (OWMs) aim to predict future 3D occupancy scenes from historical observations and future ego motions, providing a useful world simulation tool for autonomous driving. Existing methods usually rely on large networks to implicitly align multi-frame historical states and predict future occupancy in a full-state manner, which is costly and redundant because most scene regions remain unchanged over short time intervals. In this paper, we propose Aligned Delta-Triplane Transformer (ADTT), a compact 4D OWM that explicitly aligns historical triplanes with ego-motion compensation and predicts only future scene changes. Based on the aligned triplane prior, a query-conditioned Transformer predicts residual changes, where ego-motion queries guide controllable future motion and learnable external queries capture scene changes beyond ego motion. The predicted changes are added to the aligned prior and decoded into future occupancy scenes. Experiments on Occ3D-nus show that ADTT achieves state-of-the-art forecasting performance with fewer parameters and higher FPS. Our code is publicly available online:  this https URL .\n         \n       \n     \n     \n       [463] \n       \n        arXiv:2505.07852\n       \n          (replaced)\n\n        [ pdf ,  html ,  other ]\n     \n     \n       \n          Title: \n          Joint Detection of Fraud and Concept Drift inOnline Conversations with LLM-Assisted Judgment\n         \n          Ali Şenol ,  Garima Agrawal ,  Huan Liu  \n\n\n\n          Subjects: \n           Computation and Language (cs.CL) ; Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI); Machine Learning (cs.LG)\n         \n\n         \n          Detecting fake interactions in digital communication platforms remains a challenging and insufficiently addressed problem. These interactions may appear as harmless spam or escalate into sophisticated scam attempts, making it difficult to flag malicious intent early. Traditional detection methods often rely on static anomaly detection techniques that fail to adapt to dynamic conversational shifts. One key limitation is the misinterpretation of benign topic transitions referred to as concept drift as fraudulent behavior, leading to either false alarms or missed threats. We propose a two stage detection framework that first identifies suspicious conversations using a tailored ensemble classification model. To improve the reliability of detection, we incorporate a concept drift analysis step using a One Class Drift Detector (OCDD) to isolate conversational shifts within flagged dialogues. When drift is detected, a large language model (LLM) assesses whether the shift indicates fraudulent manipulation or a legitimate topic change. In cases where no drift is found, the behavior is inferred to be spam like. We validate our framework using a dataset of social engineering chat scenarios and demonstrate its practical advantages in improving both accuracy and interpretability for real time fraud detection. To contextualize the trade offs, we compare our modular approach against a Dual LLM baseline that performs detection and judgment using different language models.\n         \n       \n     \n     \n       [464] \n       \n        arXiv:2505.13565\n       \n          (replaced)\n\n        [ pdf ,  html ,  other ]\n     \n     \n       \n          Title: \n          Trustworthy AI and Democracy: A Dual Taxonomy and Operational Framework of Democratic Risks and Contributions\n         \n          Oier Mentxaka ,  Natalia Díaz-Rodríguez ,  Mark Coeckelbergh ,  Marcos López de Prado ,  Emilia Gómez ,  David Fernández Llorca ,  Enrique Herrera-Viedma ,  Francisco Herrera  \n\n          Comments: \n          26 pages, 5 figures\n         \n\n\n          Subjects: \n           Computers and Society (cs.CY) ; Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI); Human-Computer Interaction (cs.HC)\n         \n\n         \n          Artificial Intelligence (AI) can undermine and strengthen democratic governance. This paper introduces the AIRD/AIPD framework, a dual taxonomy and operational framework for analyzing AI's democratic risks and conditional contributions. The AI Risks to Democracy (AIRD) taxonomy identifies threats to autonomy, fairness, public discourse, democratic institutions, and trust, while the AI's Positive Contributions to Democracy (AIPD) taxonomy captures AI's potential to enhance efficiency, accessibility, security, informed citizenry, transparency and accountability, inclusive democratic dialogue, and evidence-based policymaking. Grounded in five democratic principles and the seven Trustworthy AI requirements proposed by the European Commission's High-Level Expert Group on AI, the framework connects democratic goals with governance and design mechanisms. It provides an operational process for identifying affected contexts and actors, classifying primary and secondary AIRD/AIPD effects, mapping them to relevant Trustworthy AI requirements, and defining proportionate governance responses and responsible actors. An operational mapping and two worked examples illustrate its application to electoral misinformation and AI-assisted public service allocation. The analysis highlights the transversal relevance of transparency, accountability, fairness, and societal wellbeing, while recognizing trade-offs, implementation costs, differential impacts, institutional capacity, and contextual variation across democratic settings. The framework is conceptual and has not been empirically validated, but it offers a traceable and adaptable basis for research, policy analysis, institutional oversight, and responsible AI design. By connecting democratic diagnosis with governance action, it bridges the gap between normative principles and the operational requirements of trustworthy and democratically legitimate AI.\n         \n       \n     \n     \n       [465] \n       \n        arXiv:2506.04467\n       \n          (replaced)\n\n        [ pdf ,  other ]\n     \n     \n       \n          Title: \n          Generalizable dose denoising using diffusion transformer framework for pencil beam scanning proton therapy\n         \n          Yuzhen Ding ,  Hongying Feng ,  Jianfeng Zhao ,  Jason Holmes ,  Martin Bues ,  Lisa A. McGee ,  Jean-Claude M. Rwigema ,  Nathan Y. Yu ,  Terence T. Sio ,  Sameer R. Keole ,  William W. Wong ,  Steven E. Schild ,  Jonathan B. Ashman ,  Sujay A. Vora ,  Daniel J. Ma ,  Samir H. Patel ,  Wei Liu  \n\n\n\n          Subjects: \n           Medical Physics (physics.med-ph) ; Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)\n         \n\n         \n          Background and purpose: Intensity-modulated proton therapy (IMPT) enables conformal dose delivery for head and neck (H&N) cancers. However, its sensitivity to inter-fractional anatomical changes motivates online adaptations, where rapid and accurate Monte Carlo (MC) dose calculation remains a major bottleneck. Low-statistics MC simulations reduce computation time but introduce noise. This study aimed to develop a diffusion-transformer framework to recover high-statistics dose distributions from noisy MC simulations. Materials and methods: A diffusion-transformer-based dose denoising framework was developed. Eighty H&N patients were used for training and additional 10 H&N patients and 30 patients with lung, breast, and prostate cancers (10 per site) were used for evaluation. Noisy and high-statistics dose maps were generated using MCsquare. The model was trained using noisy dose maps and CT images as inputs and high-statistics dose maps as ground truth, with mean squared error, residual, regional mean absolute error (MAE), and dose-volume histogram (DVH) losses. Performance was evaluated using MAE, 3D gamma passing rates, and DVH indices. Results: Mean whole-body MAE ranged from 0.08 +/- 0.06 to 0.20 +/- 0.11 Gy (Relative Biological Effectiveness, RBE) (RBE = 1.1) across test cohorts. Mean whole-body 3 %/2 mm gamma passing rates ranged from 95.8 +/- 0.8 % to 97.6 +/- 0.9 %. DVH indices from high-statistics and predicted dose distributions showed close agreement for clinical target volumes and organs of interest. Conclusions: The diffusion-transformer-based framework denoised low-statistics MC dose distributions. Despite being trained on H&N data, the model generalized to lung, breast, and prostate cohorts without retraining.\n         \n       \n     \n     \n       [466] \n       \n        arXiv:2507.00535\n       \n          (replaced)\n\n        [ pdf ,  html ,  other ]\n     \n     \n       \n          Title: \n          Rethinking Group Recommender Systems in the Era of Generative AI: From One-Shot Recommendations to Conversational Group Decision Support\n         \n          Dietmar Jannach ,  Amra Delić ,  Francesco Ricci ,  Markus Zanker  \n\n          Comments: \n          Submitted for publication\n         \n\n\n          Subjects: \n           Information Retrieval (cs.IR) ; Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)\n         \n\n         \n          More than twenty-five years ago, early ideas emerged on how to design systems that provide recommendations to groups of users rather than individuals. Since then, a wide range of algorithmic approaches has been proposed to elicit individual preferences, aggregate them, and generate group recommendations. However, despite this substantial body of work, relatively few examples of real-world group recommender systems are in use. This observation invites reflection on the assumptions of the academic research, particularly regarding how inter-group communication unfolds and how recommendation-supported decisions should be made. In this essay, we suggest that some of these assumptions and corresponding system designs may not fully align with users' needs or expectations. We therefore outline a forward-looking perspective that explores how insights from group-decision making research and recent advances in Generative AI, including systems such as ChatGPT, could better inform the design of group recommender systems. As one possible direction, we consider settings in which human group members interact and exchange information through a chat based interface, while an AI-based agent supports the decision-making process. Such an approach may contribute to design more natural group and system interactions, and could help facilitate a broader adoption of group recommender systems in practice.\n         \n       \n     \n     \n       [467] \n       \n        arXiv:2508.21380\n       \n          (replaced)\n\n        [ pdf ,  html ,  other ]\n     \n     \n       \n          Title: \n          The Algorithm Is Not the Behavior: Learned Priors Override Look-Ahead in a Chess-Playing Neural Network\n         \n          Elias Sandmann ,  Sebastian Lapuschkin ,  Wojciech Samek  \n\n          Comments: \n          NeurIPS 2026 (oral)\n         \n\n\n          Subjects: \n           Machine Learning (cs.LG) ; Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)\n         \n\n         \n          Recent mechanistic work has uncovered learned algorithms within neural networks, from modular arithmetic to search and planning in game-playing agents. But does algorithmic structure guarantee algorithmic behavior? We investigate this in Leela Chess Zero, the strongest neural chess engine, where prior work identified learned look-ahead. By extending the logit lens to its move-selecting policy network, we discover that correct puzzle solutions--including immediate checkmates--often appear in intermediate layers but are systematically overridden in the final output, a phenomenon we term \"forgotten puzzles\". Replicating prior analyses on these positions, we find that look-ahead operates normally--future moves of the correct continuation are represented, causally important, and linearly decodable--ruling out a failure of the algorithm. Instead, late layers increasingly shift toward safe play over aggression. To test whether this shift drives the override, we steer the model against these preferences and recover 71.7% of forgotten puzzles, providing causal evidence that safety priors override algorithmically computed solutions. These findings show that algorithmic structure does not guarantee algorithmic behavior: a model can internally solve a problem and still output the wrong answer.\n         \n       \n     \n     \n       [468] \n       \n        arXiv:2509.11080\n       \n          (replaced)\n\n        [ pdf ,  html ,  other ]\n     \n     \n       \n          Title: \n          Membership Inference Attacks on Recommender System: A Survey\n         \n          Jiajie He ,  Xintong Chen ,  Xinyang Fang ,  Min-Chun Chen ,  Yuechun Gu ,  Wenjin Liu ,  Keke Chen  \n\n          Comments: \n          under review in ACM TOIS\n         \n\n\n          Subjects: \n           Information Retrieval (cs.IR) ; Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI); Cryptography and Security (cs.CR)\n         \n\n         \n          Recommender systems (RecSys) have been widely applied to various applications, including E-commerce, finance, healthcare, social media and have become increasingly influential in shaping user behavior and decision-making, highlighting their growing impact in various domains. However, recent studies have shown that RecSys are vulnerable to membership inference attacks (MIAs), which aim to infer whether user interaction record was used to train a target model or not. MIAs on RecSys models can directly lead to a privacy breach. For example, via identifying the fact that a purchase record that has been used to train a RecSys associated with a specific user, an attacker can infer that user's special quirks. In recent years, MIAs have been shown to be effective on other ML tasks, e.g., classification models and natural language processing. However, traditional MIAs are ill-suited for RecSys due to the unseen posterior probability. Although MIAs on RecSys form a newly emerging and rapidly growing research area, there has been no systematic survey on this topic yet. In this article, we conduct the first comprehensive survey on RecSys MIAs. This survey offers a comprehensive review of the latest advancements in RecSys MIAs, exploring the design principles, challenges, attack and defense associated with this emerging field. We provide a unified taxonomy that categorizes different RecSys MIAs based on their characterizations and discuss their pros and cons. Based on the limitations and gaps identified in this survey, we point out several promising future research directions to inspire the researchers who wish to follow this area. This survey not only serves as a reference for the research community but also provides a clear description for researchers outside this research domain.\n         \n       \n     \n     \n       [469] \n       \n        arXiv:2509.12443\n       \n          (replaced)\n\n        [ pdf ,  html ,  other ]\n     \n     \n       \n          Title: \n          From Legacy Fortran to Portable Kokkos: An Autonomous Agentic AI Workflow\n         \n          Sparsh Gupta ,  Kamalavasan Kamalakkannan ,  Maxim Moraru ,  Galen Shipman ,  Patrick Diehl  \n\n          Comments: \n          12 pages, 8 figures, 7 tables\n         \n\n\n          Subjects: \n           Software Engineering (cs.SE) ; Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI); Distributed, Parallel, and Cluster Computing (cs.DC); Machine Learning (cs.LG)\n         \n\n         \n          Scientific applications still rely heavily on legacy Fortran codebases built for homogeneous, CPU-based systems. As High-Performance Computing (HPC) shifts toward GPU-accelerated architectures, many accelerators lack native Fortran bindings, creating a need to translate and optimize legacy code for portability. Kokkos offers performance portability through a single-source C++ abstraction, but manual porting from Fortran demands significant expertise and time. While large language models (LLMs) show promise in source-to-source code generation, their use in autonomous workflows for translating and optimizing parallel code is largely unexplored. This paper presents an agentic AI workflow in which specialized LLM agents collaborate to translate, validate, compile, run, test, debug, and optimize Fortran kernels into portable Kokkos C++ programs without human intervention, bounded by configurable retry and optimization thresholds. We evaluate the pipeline over five runs per configuration on an NVIDIA A100, characterizing its variability in success rates, token cost, and optimization behavior, on benchmark kernels including a real-world multi-file kernel from a production climate model. Proprietary models such as GPT-5 and o4-mini-high completed the full workflow most reliably, producing optimized code that exceeded the Fortran baselines for only a few U.S. dollars, whereas open-weights models such as gpt-oss-120b and Qwen3.5-397B-A17B handled simpler kernels but struggled with complex Kokkos abstractions such as parallel reductions and hierarchical parallelism. This work demonstrates, at the kernel level, the feasibility of agentic AI for Fortran-to-Kokkos transformation and, more broadly, for structured software-engineering tasks such as automated code modernization and refactoring.\n         \n       \n     \n     \n       [470] \n       \n        arXiv:2510.00361\n       \n          (replaced)\n\n        [ pdf ,  html ,  other ]\n     \n     \n       \n          Title: \n          Attribution Gradients: Incrementally Unfolding Citations for Critical Examination of Attributed AI Answers\n         \n          Hita Kambhamettu ,  Alyssa Hwang ,  Philippe Laban ,  Andrew Head  \n\n\n\n          Subjects: \n           Human-Computer Interaction (cs.HC) ; Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)\n         \n\n         \n          AI answer engines are a relatively new kind of information search tool: rather than returning a ranked list of documents, they generate an answer to a search question with inline citations to sources. But reading the cited sources is costly, and citation links themselves offer little guidance about what evidence they contain. We present attribution gradients, a technique to boost the informativeness of citations by consolidating scent and information prey in place. Its first feature is bringing evidence counts, supporting/contradictory excerpts, links to sources, and contextual explanations into one place. Its second feature is the ability to unravel second-degree citations in place. In a lab study, we demonstrate usage of the full gradient in a critical reading task and its support for deep engagement that increased the depth of what readers took away from the sources versus a standard citation and document QA design.\n         \n       \n     \n     \n       [471] \n       \n        arXiv:2511.06836\n       \n          (replaced)\n\n        [ pdf ,  html ,  other ]\n     \n     \n       \n          Title: \n          NeuroBridge: Bio-Inspired Self-Supervised EEG-to-Image Decoding via Cognitive Priors and Bidirectional Semantic Alignment\n         \n          Wenjiang Zhang ,  Sifeng Wang ,  Yuwei Su ,  Xinyu Li ,  Chen Zhang ,  Suyu Zhong  \n\n          Comments: \n          AAAI 2026\n         \n\n\n          Subjects: \n           Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV) ; Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)\n         \n\n         \n          Visual neural decoding seeks to reconstruct or infer perceived visual stimuli from brain activity patterns, providing critical insights into human cognition and enabling transformative applications in brain-computer interfaces and artificial intelligence. Current approaches, however, remain constrained by the scarcity of high-quality stimulus-brain response pairs and the inherent semantic mismatch between neural representations and visual content. Inspired by perceptual variability and co-adaptive strategy of the biological systems, we propose a novel self-supervised architecture, named NeuroBridge, which integrates Cognitive Prior Augmentation (CPA) with Shared Semantic Projector (SSP) to promote effective cross-modality alignment. Specifically, CPA simulates perceptual variability by applying asymmetric, modality-specific transformations to both EEG signals and images, enhancing semantic diversity. Unlike previous approaches, SSP establishes a bidirectional alignment process through a co-adaptive strategy, which mutually aligns features from two modalities into a shared semantic space for effective cross-modal learning. NeuroBridge surpasses previous state-of-the-art methods under both intra-subject and inter-subject settings. In the intra-subject scenario, it achieves the improvements of 12.3% in top-1 accuracy and 10.2% in top-5 accuracy, reaching 63.2% and 89.9% respectively on a 200-way zero-shot retrieval task. Extensive experiments demonstrate the effectiveness, robustness, and scalability of the proposed framework for neural visual decoding.\n         \n       \n     \n     \n       [472] \n       \n        arXiv:2511.16912\n       \n          (replaced)\n\n        [ pdf ,  other ]\n     \n     \n       \n          Title: \n          PepEVOLVE: Position-Aware Dynamic Peptide Optimization via Group-Relative Advantage\n         \n          Trieu Nguyen ,  Hao-Wei Pang ,  Shasha Feng  \n\n\n\n          Subjects: \n           Machine Learning (cs.LG) ; Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)\n         \n\n         \n          Macrocyclic peptides are an emerging modality that combines biologics-like affinity with small-molecule-like developability, yet their vast combinatorial space and multi-parameter objectives make lead optimization slow and challenging. Prior generative approaches for peptide lead optimization (e.g., PepINVENT) require chemists to pre-specify mutable positions for optimization, choices that are not always known a priori, and rely on static pretraining and optimization algorithms that limit the model's ability to generalize and effectively optimize peptide sequences. We introduce PepEVOLVE, a position-aware, dynamic framework that learns both where to edit and how to dynamically optimize peptides for multi-parameter improvement. PepEVOLVE (i) augments pretraining with dynamic masking and CHUCKLES shifting to improve generalization, (ii) uses a context-free multi-armed-bandit router that discovers high-reward residues, and (iii) couples a novel evolving optimization algorithm with group-relative advantage to stabilize reinforcement updates. During in silico evaluations, the router policy reliably learns and concentrates probability on chemically tractable sites that influence the desired peptide's properties. On a therapeutically motivated Rev-binding macrocycle benchmark, PepEVOLVE outperformed PepINVENT by reaching higher averaged permeability-weighted optimization scores of approximately 0.8 vs. 0.6, achieving best candidates with score of 0.97 (vs. 0.89), and converging in fewer steps under the task of optimizing permeability and lipophilicity with structure constraints. Overall, PepEVOLVE offers a practical, reproducible path to linear and macrocyclic peptide lead optimization when optimal edit sites are unknown, enabling more efficient chemical space exploration and improving design quality across multiple objectives to accelerate the design cycles.\n         \n       \n     \n     \n       [473] \n       \n        arXiv:2511.20709\n       \n          (replaced)\n\n        [ pdf ,  html ,  other ]\n     \n     \n       \n          Title: \n          DualGauge: Automated Joint Security-Functionality Benchmarking of Specification-Only Code Generation by LLMs and Coding Agents\n         \n          Rupam Patir ,  Keyan Guo ,  Suvadra Barua ,  Abhijeet Pathak ,  Dinesh Gudimetla ,  Jiawei Guo ,  Hongxin Hu ,  Haipeng Cai  \n\n          Comments: \n          Accepted to Findings of the Association for Computational Linguistics: EMNLP 2026\n         \n\n\n          Subjects: \n           Software Engineering (cs.SE) ; Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI); Cryptography and Security (cs.CR)\n         \n\n         \n          Large language models (LLMs) and LLM-based coding agents are now used to generate code from natural-language specifications, yet ensuring such code is both functionally correct and secure remains a challenge. We present DualGauge, the first fully automated framework for jointly evaluating correctness and security of specification-only code generation, supported by DualGauge-Bench, a language-agnostic benchmark of 307 coding tasks each paired with functional and security tests derived from the same specification. Evaluating 10 representative LLMs across Python, C++, and JavaScript, we find that functional correctness substantially overestimates reliable code generation: even the strongest model remains below 15% joint security-functionality success in every language. Common model-side factors--scale, extended thinking, quantization, instruction tuning, and code specialization--do not reliably improve joint performance, suggesting secure-and-correct code generation does not simply emerge from stronger coding capability. Evaluation of 3 leading coding agents (Codex, OpenHands, and Claude Code) shows that iterative scaffolding is not a reliable remedy on specification-only tasks: results are strongly scaffold-dependent, with two of the three agents failing to outperform a comparable direct model. A qualitative audit reveals that these failures concentrate at the output contract boundary and in guards that exist but are insufficient--patterns that only joint benchmarking reliably exposes.\n         \n       \n     \n     \n       [474] \n       \n        arXiv:2512.23304\n       \n          (replaced)\n\n        [ pdf ,  other ]\n     \n     \n       \n          Title: \n          MedGemma vs GPT-4: Open-Source and Proprietary Zero-shot Medical Disease Classification from Images\n         \n          Md. Sazzadul Islam Prottasha ,  Nabil Walid Rafi  \n\n          Comments: \n          Accepted for publication in the Journal of Machine Learning and Deep Learning (JMLDL). 9 pages, 9 figures, 10 tables\n         \n\n          Journal-ref: \n          Journal of Machine Learning and Deep Learning, Vol. 2, No. 2, pp. 1-9, 2025\n         \n\n          Subjects: \n           Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV) ; Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)\n         \n\n         \n          Multimodal Large Language Models (LLMs) introduce an emerging paradigm for medical imaging by interpreting scans through the lens of extensive clinical knowledge, offering a transformative approach to disease classification. This study presents a critical comparison between two fundamentally different AI architectures: the specialized open-source agent MedGemma and the proprietary large multimodal model GPT-4 for diagnosing six different diseases. The MedGemma-4b-it model, fine-tuned using Low-Rank Adaptation (LoRA), demonstrated superior diagnostic capability by achieving a mean test accuracy of 80.37% compared to 69.56% for the untuned GPT-4. Furthermore, MedGemma exhibited notably higher sensitivity in high-stakes clinical tasks, such as cancer and pneumonia detection. Quantitative analysis via confusion matrices and classification reports provides comprehensive insights into model performance across all categories. These results emphasize that domain-specific fine-tuning is essential for minimizing hallucinations in clinical implementation, positioning MedGemma as a sophisticated tool for complex, evidence-based medical reasoning.\n         \n       \n     \n     \n       [475] \n       \n        arXiv:2601.12401\n       \n          (replaced)\n\n        [ pdf ,  html ,  other ]\n     \n     \n       \n          Title: \n          Beyond the Dirac Delta: Mitigating Diversity Collapse in Reinforcement Fine-Tuning for Versatile Image Generation\n         \n          Jinmei Liu ,  Haoru Li ,  Zhenhong Sun ,  Chaofeng Chen ,  Yatao Bian ,  Bo Wang ,  Daoyi Dong ,  Chunlin Chen ,  Zhi Wang  \n\n\n\n          Subjects: \n           Machine Learning (cs.LG) ; Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)\n         \n\n         \n          Reinforcement learning (RL) has emerged as a paradigm for fine-tuning large-scale generative models, such as diffusion and flow models, to align with complex human preferences and user-specified tasks. A fundamental limitation remains \\textit{the curse of diversity collapse}, where the objective formulation and optimization landscape inherently collapse the policy to a Dirac delta distribution. To address this challenge, we propose \\textbf{DRIFT} (\\textbf{D}ive\\textbf{R}sity-\\textbf{I}ncentivized Reinforcement \\textbf{F}ine-\\textbf{T}uning for Versatile Image Generation), an innovative framework that systematically incentivizes output diversity throughout the on-policy fine-tuning process, reconciling strong task alignment with high generation diversity to enhance versatility essential for applications that demand diverse candidate generations. We approach the problem across three representative perspectives: i) \\textbf{sampling} a reward-concentrated subset that filters out reward outliers to prevent premature collapse; ii) \\textbf{prompting} with stochastic variations to expand the conditioning space, and iii) \\textbf{optimization} of the intra-group diversity with a potential-based reward shaping mechanism. Experimental results show that DRIFT exhibits clear Pareto dominance in task alignment and generation diversity, achieving 3.88\\%$\\sim$81.42\\% higher diversity at matched alignment and 14.38\\%$\\sim$56.45\\% higher alignment at matched diversity. Our code is available at \\textcolor{magenta}{\\href{ this https URL }{ this https URL }}.\n         \n       \n     \n     \n       [476] \n       \n        arXiv:2601.16152\n       \n          (replaced)\n\n        [ pdf ,  html ,  other ]\n     \n     \n       \n          Title: \n          Referential Regimes: Transformation-Based Identity Profiles for Neutral Substrates\n         \n          Denise M. Case  \n\n          Comments: \n          60 pages; revised manuscript with Lean 4-certified transformation classifications and formal results characterizing equality and separation of invariant-preserving step-generated identity relations. Companion Lean 4 formalization available on GitHub\n         \n\n\n          Subjects: \n           Logic in Computer Science (cs.LO) ; Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)\n         \n\n         \n          Data systems operating under persistent legal, institutional, and analytic disagreement require shared reference without presupposing agreement about interpretation. This paper investigates identity preservation under transformation, building on the conditional neutrality result of Neutral Substrates ( arXiv:2601.14271 ).\n We pair candidate identity bases with families of record transformations and classify each family as preserving, breaking, neutral, or inapplicable for a basis. Using invariant-defined identity bases and explicit effect models, we derive and certify six classification cells in Lean 4 for scope decomposition, rule refinement, and forking. The derivations show how classifications depend on field granularity, admissible transformation steps, and explicit continuity warrants.\n We also characterize the identity relations generated by invariant-preserving steps. Two bases generate equal relations exactly when they agree on the preservation of every admissible step in a shared comparison domain, and a single separating step witnesses a difference.\n The formalization makes identity claims auditable, provides testable conditions for systems that transform records, and exposes the assumptions behind each classification. The results are conditional on the declared bases, models, and warrants, whose adequacy for particular referents requires independent justification.\n         \n       \n     \n     \n       [477] \n       \n        arXiv:2601.16407\n       \n          (replaced)\n\n        [ pdf ,  html ,  other ]\n     \n     \n       \n          Title: \n          Jacobian Scopes: A Unified Geometric Framework for Token-Level LLM Attribution\n         \n          Toni J.B. Liu ,  Baran Zadeoğlu ,  Gurbir Arora ,  Raphaël Sarfati ,  Nicolas Boullé ,  Christopher J. Earls  \n\n          Comments: \n          35 pages, 17 figures\n         \n\n          Journal-ref: \n          Published as a conference paper at Neurips 2026\n         \n\n          Subjects: \n           Computation and Language (cs.CL) ; Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)\n         \n\n         \n          Large language models (LLMs) make next-token predictions based on clues present in their context, such as semantic descriptions and in-context examples. Yet, elucidating which prior tokens most strongly influence a given prediction remains challenging due to the proliferation of layers and attention heads in modern architectures. We propose Jacobian Scopes, a suite of gradient-based, token level causal attribution methods for interpreting LLM predictions. Grounded in perturbation theory and information geometry, Jacobian Scopes quantify how input tokens influence various aspects of a model's prediction, such as specific logits, the full predictive distribution, and model uncertainty (effective temperature). Through case studies spanning instruction understanding, translation, and in-context learning (ICL), we demonstrate how Jacobian Scopes reveal implicit political biases, uncover word- and phrase-level translation strategies, and shed light on recently debated mechanisms underlying in-context time-series forecasting. On token-ablation faithfulness benchmarks (LAMBADA and IWSLT2017) across six LLMs, the distribution-level Fisher and Temperature Scopes significantly outperform established gradient-based attribution methods in 10 of 12 model-dataset settings. To facilitate exploration of Jacobian Scopes on custom text, we open-source our implementations and provide a cloud-hosted interactive demo at  this https URL .\n         \n       \n     \n     \n       [478] \n       \n        arXiv:2601.21963\n       \n          (replaced)\n\n        [ pdf ,  html ,  other ]\n     \n     \n       \n          Title: \n          Industrialized Deception: The Collateral Effects of LLM-Generated Misinformation on Digital Ecosystems\n         \n          Alexander Loth ,  Martin Kappes ,  Marc-Oliver Pahl  \n\n          Comments: \n          8 pages, 2 figures. Published in the Companion Proceedings of the ACM Web Conference 2026 (WWW Companion '26), pp. 295-302\n         \n\n          Journal-ref: \n          Companion Proceedings of the ACM Web Conference 2026 (WWW '26 Companion), pp. 295-302\n         \n\n          Subjects: \n           Computers and Society (cs.CY) ; Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI); Computation and Language (cs.CL); Social and Information Networks (cs.SI)\n         \n\n         \n          Generative AI and misinformation research have evolved since our 2024 survey. This paper presents an updated perspective, transitioning from literature review to practical countermeasures. We report on changes in the threat landscape, including improved AI-generated content through Large Language Models (LLMs) and multimodal systems - addressing the adverse impacts of AI on information quality.\n This work focuses on our practical contributions: JudgeGPT, a platform to evaluate human perception of AI-generated news, and RogueGPT, a controlled stimulus generation engine for research. Together, these tools form an experimental pipeline for studying how humans perceive and detect AI-generated misinformation.\n Our findings show that detection capabilities have improved, but competition between generation and detection continues. We discuss mitigation strategies such as LLM-based detection, inoculation approaches, and the dual-use nature of generative AI.\n         \n       \n     \n     \n       [479] \n       \n        arXiv:2602.02004\n       \n          (replaced)\n\n        [ pdf ,  html ,  other ]\n     \n     \n       \n          Title: \n          ClueTracer: Question-to-Vision Clue Tracing for Training-Free Hallucination Suppression in Multimodal Reasoning\n         \n          Gongli Xi ,  Kun Wang ,  Zeming Gao ,  Huahui Yi ,  Haolang Lu ,  Qianshan Wei ,  Ye Tian ,  Wendong Wang  \n\n          Comments: \n          27 pages, 12 figures, accept by EMNLP 2026 (Oral)\n         \n\n\n          Subjects: \n           Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV) ; Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)\n         \n\n         \n          Large multimodal reasoning models solve challenging visual problems via explicit long-chain inference: they gather visual clues from images and decode clues into textual tokens. Yet this capability also increases hallucinations, where the model generates content that is not supported by the input image or the question. To understand this failure mode, we identify \\emph{reasoning drift}: during clue gathering, the model over-focuses on question-irrelevant entities, diluting focus on task-relevant cues and gradually decoupling the reasoning trace from visual grounding. As a consequence, many inference-time localization or intervention methods developed for non-reasoning models fail to pinpoint the true clues in reasoning settings. Motivated by these insights, we introduce ClueRecall, a metric for assessing visual clue retrieval, and present ClueTracer, a training-free, parameter-free, and architecture-agnostic plugin for hallucination suppression. ClueTracer starts from the question and traces how key clues propagate along the model's reasoning pathway (question $\\rightarrow$ outputs $\\rightarrow$ visual tokens), thereby localizing task-relevant patches while suppressing spurious attention to irrelevant regions. Remarkably, \\textbf{without any additional training}, ClueTracer improves all \\textbf{reasoning} architectures (including \\texttt{R1-OneVision}, \\texttt{Ocean-R1}, \\texttt{MM-Eureka}, \\emph{etc}.) by $\\mathbf{1.21\\times}$ on reasoning benchmarks\n         \n       \n     \n     \n       [480] \n       \n        arXiv:2602.02100\n       \n          (replaced)\n\n        [ pdf ,  html ,  other ]\n     \n     \n       \n          Title: \n          The Verification Crisis: Expert Perceptions of GenAI Disinformation and the Case for Reproducible Provenance\n         \n          Alexander Loth ,  Martin Kappes ,  Marc-Oliver Pahl  \n\n          Comments: \n          9 pages. Author version; the Version of Record appeared in the Companion Proceedings of the ACM Web Conference 2026 (WWW '26 Companion), pp. 980-988\n         \n\n          Journal-ref: \n          Companion Proceedings of the ACM Web Conference 2026 (WWW '26 Companion), pp. 980-988\n         \n\n          Subjects: \n           Computers and Society (cs.CY) ; Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI); Social and Information Networks (cs.SI)\n         \n\n         \n          The growth of Generative Artificial Intelligence (GenAI) has shifted disinformation production from manual fabrication to automated, large-scale manipulation. This article presents findings from the first wave of a longitudinal expert perception survey (N=21) involving AI researchers, policymakers, and disinformation specialists. It examines the perceived severity of multimodal threats -- text, image, audio, and video -- and evaluates current mitigation strategies. Results indicate that while deepfake video presents immediate \"shock\" value, large-scale text generation poses a systemic risk of \"epistemic fragmentation\" and \"synthetic consensus,\" particularly in the political domain. The survey reveals skepticism about technical detection tools, with experts favoring provenance standards and regulatory frameworks despite implementation barriers. GenAI disinformation research requires reproducible methods. The current challenge is measurement: without standardized benchmarks and reproducibility checklists, tracking or countering synthetic media remains difficult. We propose treating information integrity as an infrastructure with rigor in data provenance and methodological reproducibility.\n         \n       \n     \n     \n       [481] \n       \n        arXiv:2602.08351\n       \n          (replaced)\n\n        [ pdf ,  html ,  other ]\n     \n     \n       \n          Title: \n          Rethinking Bayesian Optimization for Co-Optimizing LLM Training Configurations\n         \n          Zhiliang Chen ,  Alfred Wei Lun Leong ,  Shao Yong Ong ,  Apivich Hemachandra ,  Gregory Kang Ruey Lau ,  Chuan-Sheng Foo ,  Zhengyuan Liu ,  Nancy F. Chen ,  Bryan Kian Hsiang Low  \n\n\n\n          Subjects: \n           Machine Learning (cs.LG) ; Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)\n         \n\n         \n          Fine-tuning an LLM to maximize performance on a downstream task requires finding the optimal data and model configuration. This is a black-box optimization problem that Bayesian optimization (BO) addresses by sequentially evaluating training configurations and using observed performance feedback to adaptively guide the search towards better configurations. However, directly applying BO to the joint data-model space suffers from two fundamental challenges: the prohibitive cost of full LLM training at each iteration, and the large number of BO iterations required for effective exploration due to the high problem dimensionality. This paper introduces JoBS, a BO-based approach that co-optimizes data and model configurations without requiring a full training run at every iteration. JoBS allocates an initial portion of the optimization budget to learn a scaling-law-inspired performance predictor that estimates fully trained LLM performance from only a small number of training steps. The remaining budget is then used to run BO with this predictor, eliminating the need for full training runs and enabling JoBS to explore significantly more data and model configurations within the same budget. We analyze JoBS's average regret and derive the optimal budget allocation between predictor learning and BO iterations. Empirically, JoBS outperforms independent data and model optimization methods and existing multi-fidelity BO baselines across a diverse set of downstream tasks. Our code is available at  this https URL .\n         \n       \n     \n     \n       [482] \n       \n        arXiv:2602.09581\n       \n          (replaced)\n\n        [ pdf ,  html ,  other ]\n     \n     \n       \n          Title: \n          Mitigating the Likelihood Paradox in Flow-based OOD Detection: An Entropic Perspective\n         \n          Donghwan Kim ,  Hyunsoo Yoon  \n\n          Comments: \n          29 pages, 3 figures\n         \n\n\n          Subjects: \n           Machine Learning (cs.LG) ; Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)\n         \n\n         \n          Deep generative models that can tractably compute input likelihoods, including normalizing flows, often assign unexpectedly high likelihoods to out-of-distribution (OOD) inputs. We mitigate this paradox from an entropic perspective with Semantic Proportional Entropy Manipulation (SPEM), a training-free procedure that applies stronger perturbations to inputs less similar to an in-distribution (ID) memory bank. We derive theoretical lower bounds showing that this entropy gap increases the expected log-likelihood difference in favor of the ID, and experiments on standard benchmarks show that this manipulation realigns the likelihood ordering with human intuition. Strikingly, this realignment persists even when the flow is given only visually meaningless noise instead of the original input, and we analyze this phenomenon theoretically to characterize the conditions under which it occurs.\n         \n       \n     \n     \n       [483] \n       \n        arXiv:2602.09593\n       \n          (replaced)\n\n        [ pdf ,  html ,  other ]\n     \n     \n       \n          Title: \n          Why Does Counterintuitive Likelihood Behavior Rarely Emerge in Tabular Anomaly Detection?\n         \n          Donghwan Kim ,  Junghun Phee ,  Hyunsoo Yoon  \n\n          Comments: \n          53 pages, 11 figures\n         \n\n\n          Subjects: \n           Machine Learning (cs.LG) ; Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)\n         \n\n         \n          Deep generative models with tractable likelihoods, exemplified by normalizing flows, offer an effective basis for anomaly detection through likelihood-based scoring. We demonstrate that, unlike in the image domain where deep generative models frequently assign higher likelihoods to anomalous data, such counterintuitive behavior occurs rarely in tabular settings. We first introduce a domain-agnostic formulation that enables consistent detection and evaluation of the counterintuitive phenomenon, addressing the lack of a precise definition in prior work. Through extensive experiments on 47 tabular datasets benchmarked against 13 baseline models and on 10 CV/NLP embedding datasets from ADBench, we show that the phenomenon is consistently rare in general tabular data and that normalizing flow likelihood achieves competitive anomaly detection performance. We further provide theoretical and empirical analyses attributing this disparity to differences in dimensionality and feature correlation between tabular and image data. Therefore, our results indicate that likelihood-based detection with normalizing flows is a practical approach for tabular anomaly detection.\n         \n       \n     \n     \n       [484] \n       \n        arXiv:2602.10081\n       \n          (replaced)\n\n        [ pdf ,  html ,  other ]\n     \n     \n       \n          Title: \n          Anagent For Enhancing Scientific Table & Figure Analysis\n         \n          Xuehang Guo ,  Zhiyong Lu ,  Tom Hope ,  Qingyun Wang  \n\n\n\n          Subjects: \n           Computation and Language (cs.CL) ; Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)\n         \n\n         \n          In scientific research, analysis requires accurately interpreting complex multimodal knowledge, integrating evidence from different sources, and drawing inferences grounded in domain-specific knowledge. However, current artificial intelligence (AI) systems struggle to consistently demonstrate such capabilities. The complexity and variability of scientific tables and figures, combined with heterogeneous structures and long-context requirements, pose fundamental obstacles to scientific table \\& figure analysis. To quantify these challenges, we introduce AnaBench, a large-scale benchmark featuring $63,178$ instances from nine scientific domains, systematically categorized along seven complexity dimensions. To tackle these challenges, we propose Anagent, a multi-agent framework for enhanced scientific table \\& figure analysis through four specialized agents: Planner decomposes tasks into actionable subtasks, Expert retrieves task-specific information through targeted tool execution, Solver synthesizes information to generate coherent analysis, and Critic performs iterative refinement through five-dimensional quality assessment. We further develop modular training strategies that leverage supervised finetuning and specialized reinforcement learning to optimize individual capabilities while maintaining effective collaboration. Comprehensive evaluation across 170 subdomains demonstrates that Anagent achieves substantial improvements, up to $\\uparrow 13.43\\%$ in training-free settings and $\\uparrow 42.12\\%$ with finetuning, while revealing that task-oriented reasoning and context-aware problem-solving are essential for high-quality scientific table \\& figure analysis. Our project page:  this https URL .\n         \n       \n     \n     \n       [485] \n       \n        arXiv:2602.20076\n       \n          (replaced)\n\n        [ pdf ,  html ,  other ]\n     \n     \n       \n          Title: \n          Robust Taylor-Lagrange Control for Safety-Critical Systems with Guaranteed Feasibility\n         \n          Wei Xiao ,  Christos Cassandras ,  Anni Li  \n\n          Comments: \n          This version addressed one technical problem of the inter-sampling effect in rTLC\n         \n\n\n          Subjects: \n           Systems and Control (eess.SY) ; Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI); Robotics (cs.RO)\n         \n\n         \n          The Control Barrier Function (CBF) method has been widely adopted in solving safety-critical control problems. However, the existence of a CBF is only a sufficient condition for system safety. The recently proposed Taylor-Lagrange Control (TLC) method addresses this limitation by providing a necessary and sufficient condition, but it is still vulnerable to the feasibility preservation problem (e.g., due to the inter-sampling effect when time is discretized). In this paper, we propose a robust TLC (rTLC) method to address the feasibility preservation problem and show how to use it to solve optimal control problems. Specifically, the rTLC method expands the safety function to an order higher than its relative degree using Taylor's expansion with Lagrange remainder, which allows the control to explicitly show up at the current time instead of a future time in the original TLC method. This implies that the safety constraint remains satisfied over the entire discretization time interval that follows. Moreover, the rTLC method naturally addresses the feasibility preservation problem with only a single hyper-parameter (the discretization time interval size during implementation), compared to significantly more parameters for its counterparts. Finally, we illustrate the effectiveness of the proposed rTLC method through an adaptive cruise control problem, and compare it with existing safety-critical control methods.\n         \n       \n     \n     \n       [486] \n       \n        arXiv:2602.20317\n       \n          (replaced)\n\n        [ pdf ,  html ,  other ]\n     \n     \n       \n          Title: \n          A general extractor for coherent patterns across time-frequency measurements\n         \n          Nathaniel Chen ,  Kouroche Bouchiat ,  Peter Steiner ,  Andrew Rothstein ,  David Smith ,  Max Austin ,  Mike van Zeeland ,  Marie Roch ,  Azarakhsh Jalalvand ,  Egemen Kolemen  \n\n\n\n          Subjects: \n           Signal Processing (eess.SP) ; Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI); Plasma Physics (physics.plasm-ph)\n         \n\n         \n          Scientific discovery increasingly depends on finding meaningful patterns within volumes of sensor data too large for manual inspection. Existing time-frequency event extractors are often engineered or trained on manually labeled examples for each instrument, which can be laborious and difficult to adapt to new environments. To address this, we introduce TokEye, a reference-free method that learns a general criterion for coherent structure from heterogeneous fusion measurements. A model developed under this method, without retraining, can extract signals from various other fields including marine bioacoustics and wideband radar. It recovers 82.5\\% of expert-annotated stellarator signals, detects 78\\% of dolphin whistles, and identifies radar events with 81.5\\% average precision across six benchmarks, while processing full experimental spectrograms in under one second on a GPU. This transfer across unrelated sensors and physical systems reveals common exploitable structure in time-frequency measurements and establishes a scalable foundation for automated scientific signal discovery.\n         \n       \n     \n     \n       [487] \n       \n        arXiv:2602.22488\n       \n          (replaced)\n\n        [ pdf ,  html ,  other ]\n     \n     \n       \n          Title: \n          Explainability-Aware Evaluation of Transfer Learning Models for IoT DDoS Detection Under Resource Constraints\n         \n          Nelly Elsayed  \n\n          Comments: \n          Updated version with several corrections, contains more clarification, and corrected typos in at the text and references\n         \n\n\n          Subjects: \n           Cryptography and Security (cs.CR) ; Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)\n         \n\n         \n          Distributed denial of service (DDoS) attacks threaten the availability of Internet of Things (IoT) infrastructures, particularly under resource-constrained deployment conditions. Although transfer learning models have shown promising detection accuracy, their reliability, computational feasibility, and interpretability in operational environments remain insufficiently explored. This study presents an explainability aware empirical evaluation of seven pre-trained convolutional neural network architectures for multi-class IoT DDoS detection using the CICDDoS2019 dataset and an image-based traffic representation. The analysis integrates performance metrics, reliability-oriented statistics (MCC, Youden Index, confidence intervals), latency and training cost assessment, and interpretability evaluation using Grad-CAM and SHAP. Results indicate that DenseNet and MobileNet based architectures achieve strong detection performance while demonstrating superior reliability and compact, class-consistent attribution patterns. DenseNet169 offers the strongest reliability and interpretability alignment, whereas MobileNetV3 provides an effective latency-accuracy trade-off for fog-level deployment. The findings emphasize the importance of combining performance, reliability, and explainability criteria when selecting deep learning models for IoT DDoS detection.\n         \n       \n     \n     \n       [488] \n       \n        arXiv:2603.03971\n       \n          (replaced)\n\n        [ pdf ,  html ,  other ]\n     \n     \n       \n          Title: \n          No Certificate, No Categorical Speech Act: A Brouwerian Assertibility Constraint for Public Reason\n         \n          Michael Jülich  \n\n          Comments: \n          36 pages, 2 figures, 1 table, supplementary appendix\n         \n\n\n          Subjects: \n           Computers and Society (cs.CY) ; Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI); Machine Learning (cs.LG); Logic in Computer Science (cs.LO)\n         \n\n         \n          Generative AI can convert uncertainty into authoritative-seeming verdicts, displacing the justificatory work on which democratic epistemic agency depends. As a corrective, I propose a Brouwer-inspired assertibility constraint for responsible AI: in high-stakes domains, systems may assert or deny claims only if they can provide a publicly inspectable and contestable certificate of entitlement; otherwise they must return Undetermined. This constraint yields a three-status interface semantics (Asserted, Denied, Undetermined) whose statuses mark entitlement to categorical speech rather than truth values of the underlying world-claim. The framework distinguishes internal entitlement from public standing while connecting them through the certificate as a boundary object. Philosophically, it develops a form of applied intuitionism: resistance to unwarranted completeness becomes a norm of public machine speech, under which categorical commitment requires exhibited and contestable entitlement. I operationalize the constraint through structured certificates, decision-layer gates for threshold and argmax predicates, and a reason-coded output contract. A design lemma shows that, under a refutation-soundness condition on the contractually specified negative side, certificate-sound binary totality presupposes witnessed decidability on the declared scope. Under the proposed normative output contract, Undetermined is not a tunable reject option but a mandatory status whenever the available certificates do not uniquely license assertion or denial. By making outputs answerable to contestable warrants rather than confidence alone, the paper aims to preserve epistemic agency where automated speech enters public justification.\n         \n       \n     \n     \n       [489] \n       \n        arXiv:2603.08588\n       \n          (replaced)\n\n        [ pdf ,  html ,  other ]\n     \n     \n       \n          Title: \n          Towards Batch-to-Streaming Deep Reinforcement Learning for Continuous Control\n         \n          Riccardo De Monte ,  Matteo Cederle ,  Gian Antonio Susto  \n\n\n\n          Subjects: \n           Machine Learning (cs.LG) ; Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)\n         \n\n         \n          State-of-the-art deep reinforcement learning (RL) methods have achieved remarkable performance in continuous control tasks, yet their computational complexity is often incompatible with the constraints of resource-limited hardware, due to their reliance on replay buffers, batch updates, and target networks. The emerging paradigm of streaming deep RL addresses this limitation through purely online updates, achieving strong empirical performance on standard benchmarks. In this work, we propose two novel streaming deep RL algorithms, Streaming Soft Actor-Critic (S2AC) and Streaming Deterministic Actor-Critic (SDAC), explicitly designed to be compatible with state-of-the-art batch RL methods, making them particularly suitable for on-device finetuning applications such as Sim2Real transfer. Both algorithms achieve performance comparable to state-of-the-art streaming baselines on standard benchmarks without requiring tedious per-environment hyperparameter tuning. We further investigate the batch-to-streaming transition, showing that a naive transition does not guarantee preservation of pre-trained policy performance, and propose a principled approach to address this challenge.\n         \n       \n     \n     \n       [490] \n       \n        arXiv:2603.09989\n       \n          (replaced)\n\n        [ pdf ,  html ,  other ]\n     \n     \n       \n          Title: \n          The System Hallucination Scale (SHS): A Human-Centered Instrument for Assessing Perceived Reliability of Large Language Models\n         \n          Heimo Müller ,  Dominik Steiger ,  Markus Plass ,  Andreas Holzinger  \n\n\n\n          Subjects: \n           Computation and Language (cs.CL) ; Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)\n         \n\n         \n          Large language models routinely produce factually incorrect or misleading outputs, yet conventional automated benchmarks overlook how users perceive reliability during interaction. We introduce the System Hallucination Scale (SHS), a lightweight instrument modeling the famous System Usability Scale (SUS) and System Causability Scales (SCS). SHS measures perceived reliability across five dimensions: factual accuracy, source reliability, logical coherence, deceptiveness of presentation, and responsiveness to user guidance, using ten balanced Likert-scale items that provide a directional score and consistency diagnostic. In a supervised validation study with 193 participants (386 evaluations across 47 trained experimenters), SHS demonstrated strong internal consistency and high clarity, completing in an average of 4.2 minutes. Participants rated source verifiability and presentation deceptiveness worse than factual accuracy, indicating perceived vulnerabilities lie primarily in traceability and framing. As a proof of concept, SHS assesses human-perceived reliability rather than ground-truth correctness, providing open-source multilingual implementations and an interactive calculator.\n         \n       \n     \n     \n       [491] \n       \n        arXiv:2603.14326\n       \n          (replaced)\n\n        [ pdf ,  html ,  other ]\n     \n     \n       \n          Title: \n          ECG-Reasoning-Benchmark: A Benchmark for Evaluating Clinical Reasoning Capabilities in ECG Interpretation\n         \n          Jungwoo Oh ,  Hyunseung Chung ,  Junhee Lee ,  Min-Gyu Kim ,  Hangyul Yoon ,  Ki Seong Lee ,  Youngchae Lee ,  Muhan Yeo ,  Edward Choi  \n\n          Comments: \n          Accepted at NeurIPS 2026 Evaluations & Datasets Track\n         \n\n\n          Subjects: \n           Machine Learning (cs.LG) ; Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI); Computation and Language (cs.CL)\n         \n\n         \n          While Multimodal Large Language Models (MLLMs) show promising performance in automated electrocardiogram interpretation, it remains unclear whether they perform actual step-by-step reasoning or just rely on superficial visual cues. To investigate this, we introduce ECG-Reasoning-Benchmark, a novel multi-turn evaluation framework comprising over 6,400 samples to systematically assess step- by-step reasoning across 17 core ECG diagnoses. Our comprehensive evaluation of state-of-the-art models reveals a systematic shortfall in executing multi-step logical deduction. Although models possess the medical knowledge to retrieve clinical criteria for a diagnosis, they exhibit consistently low success rates in maintaining a complete reasoning chain, primarily failing to ground the corresponding ECG findings to the actual visual evidence in the ECG signal. These results demonstrate that current MLLMs bypass actual visual interpretation, highlighting a limitation in their visual grounding capabilities and motivating robust, reasoning-centric medical AI. The code and data are available at  this https URL .\n         \n       \n     \n     \n       [492] \n       \n        arXiv:2603.22016\n       \n          (replaced)\n\n        [ pdf ,  html ,  other ]\n     \n     \n       \n          Title: \n          ROM: Real-time Overthinking Mitigation via Streaming Detection and Intervention\n         \n          Xinyan Wang ,  Xiaogeng Liu ,  Ming Pei ,  Chaowei Xiao  \n\n\n\n          Subjects: \n           Machine Learning (cs.LG) ; Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI); Computation and Language (cs.CL)\n         \n\n         \n          Large reasoning models (LRMs) often suffer from overthinking, continuing with redundant verification and repeated attempts even after reaching a correct solution. This wastes computation and can overturn an initially correct answer. We show that the transition from productive to redundant reasoning is decodable from late-layer hidden states. These representations separate the two phases around first-correct-solution boundaries beyond what position and lexical controls explain, and distinguish correct from incorrect solution endings. Building on this finding, we propose ROM (Real-time Overthinking Mitigation), which uses a lightweight streaming detector (0.07--0.25\\% of backbone parameters) to monitor a frozen LRM and intervene at well-formed reasoning boundaries without answer extraction or probe decoding. Counterfactual Self-Correction (CSC) augments detector training with wrong$\\rightarrow$correct trajectories to preserve useful self-correction. Heads are trained separately for each backbone using shared MATH500 supervision and transfer zero-shot to four unseen benchmarks. Evaluated against ten baselines across five backbones from three families and five benchmarks, ROM$_{\\text{CSC}}$ reduces response length by 28--77\\% (45\\% on average), with significant reductions in all 25 settings and accuracy matching or exceeding vanilla decoding in 22. Measurements on two backbones further show 20--70\\% lower end-to-end latency with less than 0.5\\% detector overhead per decoding step. Code is available at  this https URL .\n         \n       \n     \n     \n       [493] \n       \n        arXiv:2603.22399\n       \n          (replaced)\n\n        [ pdf ,  html ,  other ]\n     \n     \n       \n          Title: \n          Latent Style-based Quantum Wasserstein GAN for Drug Design\n         \n          Julien Baglio ,  Yacine Haddad ,  Richard Polifka  \n\n          Comments: \n          Main part: 26 pages, 11 figures, 6 tables. Supplementary material: 24 pages, 20 figures, 21 tables. v2: revision containing matched-capacity analysis of the classical GAN and extensive details on the hardware inference run, plus bootstrap error analysis of the hardware results. Matches published version\n         \n\n          Journal-ref: \n          J. Chem. Inf. Model. (2026) 66 (20): 13136-13154\n         \n\n          Subjects: \n           Quantum Physics (quant-ph) ; Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI); Machine Learning (cs.LG); Biomolecules (q-bio.BM)\n         \n\n         \n          The development of new drugs is a time-consuming and expensive process, with average costs estimated to be up to around $2.5 billion. The first step in this process is the design of the new drug, for which de novo drug design, assisted by artificial intelligence, has blossomed in recent years and has revolutionized the field. In particular, generative artificial intelligence has delivered promising results in drug discovery and development, reducing costs and time to solution. However, classical generative models, such as generative adversarial networks (GANs), can be difficult to train and are prone to mode collapse. Quantum computing may be an avenue to overcome some of these issues, providing compact models with far fewer trainable parameters, thereby enhancing the generalizability of GANs. We propose a new latent style-based quantum Wasserstein GAN (QGAN) for drug design, combining a variational autoencoder that maps molecular structures into a latent space with a hybrid quantum GAN. Our pipeline is benchmarked against classical models of the industry-relevant MOSES suite. Style-based noise reuploading at every rotational gate and a Wasserstein gradient penalty are used to improve trainability and to mitigate mode collapse. Training and large-scale sampling with circuits of up to 15 qubits are performed on noiseless quantum simulators to validate our architecture. We present an inference-only demonstration on an IBM Heron quantum computer using 5 qubits, sampling 2,500 new molecules, to investigate quantum noise effects. Our default QGAN, using 110 trainable generator parameters, reaches statistically comparable overall performance and statistically significant gains on selected drug-relevant metrics relative to both the MOSES classical latent GAN baseline, which contains about 700,000 trainable parameters for the generator, and a classical latent GAN with matched generator capacity.\n         \n       \n     \n     \n       [494] \n       \n        arXiv:2603.25184\n       \n          (replaced)\n\n        [ pdf ,  html ,  other ]\n     \n     \n       \n          Title: \n          Train at Moving Edge: Online-Verified Prompt Selection for Efficient RL Training of Large Reasoning Model\n         \n          Jiahao Wu ,  Ning Lu ,  Shengcai Liu ,  Kun Wang ,  Yanting Yang ,  Baijiong Lin ,  Chen Jason Zhang ,  Li Qing ,  Ke Tang  \n\n          Comments: \n          NeurIPS 2026 Camera Ready\n         \n\n\n          Subjects: \n           Machine Learning (cs.LG) ; Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)\n         \n\n         \n          Reinforcement learning (RL) has become essential for post-training large language models (LLMs) in reasoning tasks. While scaling rollouts can stabilize training and enhance performance, the computational overhead is a critical issue. In algorithms like GRPO, multiple rollouts per prompt incur prohibitive costs, as a large portion of prompts provide negligible gradients and are thus of low utility. To address this problem, we investigate how to select high-utility prompts before the rollout phase. Our experimental analysis reveals that sample utility is non-uniform and evolving: the strongest learning signals concentrate at the ``learning edge\", the intersection of intermediate difficulty and high uncertainty, which shifts as training proceeds. Motivated by this, we propose HIVE (History-Informed and online-VErified prompt selection), a dual-stage framework for data-efficient RL. HIVE utilizes historical reward trajectories for coarse selection and employs prompt entropy as a real-time proxy to prune instances with stale utility. By evaluating HIVE across multiple math reasoning benchmarks and models, we show that HIVE yields significant rollout efficiency without compromising performance.\n         \n       \n     \n     \n       [495] \n       \n        arXiv:2603.26498\n       \n          (replaced)\n\n        [ pdf ,  html ,  other ]\n     \n     \n       \n          Title: \n          Rocks, Pebbles and Sand: Modality-aware Scheduling for Multimodal Large Language Model Inference\n         \n          Konstantinos Papaioannou ,  Thaleia Dimitra Doudali  \n\n\n\n          Subjects: \n           Distributed, Parallel, and Cluster Computing (cs.DC) ; Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)\n         \n\n         \n          Multimodal Large Language Models (MLLMs) power platforms like ChatGPT, Gemini, and Copilot, enabling rich interactions across text, images, and videos. Unlike text-only workloads, multimodal requests are inherently heterogeneous, differing by orders of magnitude in latency and memory footprint. Existing LLM serving systems, designed for homogeneous text workloads, struggle under this heterogeneity: large image and video requests monopolize GPU resources, causing severe head-of-line blocking and degrading the responsiveness of lightweight, latency-critical text queries. Recent multimodal serving systems mitigate this interference through disaggregation, isolating encoding and inference stages across GPUs. While effective at large scale, such designs introduce severe GPU underutilization in more practical, moderate-scale deployments. In response, we build RPS-Serve a modality-aware scheduling framework that leverages the extreme heterogeneity in multimodal workloads and introduces a simple abstraction: videos behave like rocks, images like pebbles, and text like sand. Then, it lets sand flow quickly through pebbles and rocks, maximizing GPU utilization while ensuring fast responsiveness for lightweight requests and controlled execution for heavier ones. To achieve this, RPS-Serve estimates the GPU admission cost of each request, classifies requests into rocks, pebbles, and sand using lightweight clustering, and applies priority-based scheduling with aging to balance performance across modalities. Evaluation across state-of-the-art MLLMs shows that RPS-Serve increases GPU utilization by 65% and reduces TTFT by up to 9.3x, on average across models, compared to disaggregation-based multi-modal systems.\n         \n       \n     \n     \n       [496] \n       \n        arXiv:2603.28421\n       \n          (replaced)\n\n        [ pdf ,  html ,  other ]\n     \n     \n       \n          Title: \n          Learning unified control of intrinsic nonlinear spin dynamics in atomic qudits for magnetometry\n         \n          C. Z. Cao ,  J. Z. Han ,  M. Xiong ,  M. Deng ,  L. Wang ,  Q. Liu ,  X. Lv ,  M. Xue  \n\n          Comments: \n          (6.5+3.5+4) pages, (4+4) figures\n         \n\n\n          Subjects: \n           Quantum Physics (quant-ph) ; Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)\n         \n\n         \n          Generating and preserving metrologically useful quantum states is a central challenge in quantum-enhanced metrology. In low-field atomic magnetometry with multilevel atoms, the nonlinear Zeeman (NLZ) effect is both a resource and a limitation. It can generate internal spin squeezing within a single atomic qudit, but under fixed readout it also rotates and distorts the measurement-relevant quadrature, limiting the usable metrological gain. The problem is further complicated by the time dependence of both the squeezing axis and the nonlinear evolution itself. Here we show that reinforcement learning can transform NLZ dynamics from a source of readout degradation into a sustained metrological resource. Using only experimentally accessible low-order spin moments, a trained agent identifies a unified control policy for this class of intrinsically nonlinear sensing dynamics. We illustrate the approach in the \\(f=21/2\\) manifold of \\(^{161}\\mathrm{Dy}\\), where the learned policy rapidly prepares strongly squeezed internal states and stabilizes more than \\(4\\,\\mathrm{dB}\\) of fixed-axis spin squeezing under continuous NLZ evolution. Including state-preparation overhead, the learned protocol yields a single-atom magnetic-field sensitivity of \\(13.9\\,\\mathrm{pT}/\\sqrt{\\mathrm{Hz}}\\), approximately \\(3\\,\\mathrm{dB}\\) beyond the standard quantum limit. Our results establish learning-based control as an experimentally feasible route for converting unavoidable intrinsic nonlinear dynamics in multilevel atomic sensors into operational metrological advantage.\n         \n       \n     \n     \n       [497] \n       \n        arXiv:2603.28959\n       \n          (replaced)\n\n        [ pdf ,  html ,  other ]\n     \n     \n       \n          Title: \n          Multi-Agent LLMs for Adaptive Acquisition in Bayesian Optimization\n         \n          Mohammadsina Almasi ,  Andrea Carbonati ,  Hadis Anahideh  \n\n          Comments: \n          Proceedings of the IISE Annual Conference & Expo 2026\n         \n\n\n          Subjects: \n           Machine Learning (cs.LG) ; Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)\n         \n\n         \n          The exploration-exploitation trade-off is central to sequential decision-making and black-box optimization, yet how Large Language Models (LLMs) reason about and manage this trade-off remains poorly understood. Unlike Bayesian Optimization, where exploration and exploitation are explicitly encoded through acquisition functions, LLM-based optimization relies on implicit, prompt-based reasoning over historical evaluations, making search behavior difficult to analyze or control. In this work, we present a metric-level study of LLM-mediated search policy learning, studying how LLMs construct and adapt exploration-exploitation strategies under multiple operational definitions of exploration, including informativeness, diversity, and representativeness. We show that single-agent LLM approaches, which jointly perform strategy selection and candidate generation within a single prompt, suffer from cognitive overload, leading to unstable search dynamics and premature convergence. To address this limitation, we propose a multi-agent framework that decomposes exploration-exploitation control into strategic policy mediation and tactical candidate generation. A strategy agent assigns interpretable weights to multiple search criteria, while a generation agent produces candidates conditioned on the resulting search policy defined as weights. This decomposition renders exploration-exploitation decisions explicit, observable, and adjustable. Empirical results across various continuous optimization benchmarks indicate that separating strategic control from candidate generation substantially improves the effectiveness of LLM-mediated search.\n         \n       \n     \n     \n       [498] \n       \n        arXiv:2603.29572\n       \n          (replaced)\n\n        [ pdf ,  html ,  other ]\n     \n     \n       \n          Title: \n          Turbo4DGen: Ultra-Fast Acceleration for 4D Generation\n         \n          Yuanbin Man ,  Ying Huang ,  Zhile Ren ,  Miao Yin  \n\n          Comments: \n          ICML 2026\n         \n\n\n          Subjects: \n           Graphics (cs.GR) ; Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI); Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)\n         \n\n         \n          4D generation, or dynamic 3D content generation, integrates spatial, temporal, and view dimensions to model realistic dynamic scenes, playing a foundational role in advancing world models and physical AI. However, maintaining long-chain consistency across both frames and viewpoints through the unique spatio-camera-motion (SCM) attention mechanism introduces substantial computational and memory overhead, often leading to out-of-memory (OOM) failures and prohibitive generation times. To address these challenges, we propose Turbo4DGen, an ultra-fast acceleration framework for diffusion-based multi-view 4D content generation. Turbo4DGen introduces a spatiotemporal cache mechanism that persistently reuses intermediate attention across denoising steps, combined with dynamically semantic-aware attention pruning and an adaptive SCM chain bypass scheduler, to drastically reduce redundant SCM attention computation. Our experimental results show that Turbo4DGen achieves an average 9.7$\\times$ speedup without quality degradation on the ObjaverseDy and Consistent4D datasets. To the best of our knowledge, Turbo4DGen is the first dedicated acceleration framework for 4D generation.\n         \n       \n     \n     \n       [499] \n       \n        arXiv:2604.00058\n       \n          (replaced)\n\n        [ pdf ,  other ]\n     \n     \n       \n          Title: \n          GenoBERT: A Language Model for Accurate Genotype Imputation\n         \n          Lei Huang ,  Chuan Qiu ,  Kuan-Jui Su ,  Anqi Liu ,  Yun Gong ,  Weiqiang Lin ,  Lindong Jiang ,  Chen Zhao ,  Meng Song ,  Jeffrey Deng ,  Qing Tian ,  Zhe Luo ,  Ping Gong ,  Hui Shen ,  Chaoyang Zhang ,  Hong-Wen Deng  \n\n\n\n          Subjects: \n           Genomics (q-bio.GN) ; Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI); Machine Learning (cs.LG)\n         \n\n         \n          Genotype imputation enables dense variant coverage for genome-wide association and risk-prediction studies, yet conventional reference-panel methods remain limited by ancestry bias and reduced rare-variant accuracy. We present Genotype Bidirectional Encoder Representations from Transformers (GenoBERT), a transformer-based, reference-free framework that tokenizes phased genotypes and uses a self-attention mechanism to capture both short- and long-range linkage disequilibrium (LD) dependencies. Benchmarking on two independent datasets including the Louisiana Osteoporosis Study (LOS) and the 1000 Genomes Project (1KGP) across ancestry groups and multiple genotype missingness levels (5-50%) shows that GenoBERT achieves the highest overall accuracy compared to four baseline methods (Beagle5.4, SCDA, BiU-Net, and STICI). At practical sparsity levels (up to 25% missing), GenoBERT attains high overall imputation accuracy ($r^2 approx 0.98$) across datasets, and maintains robust performance ($r^2 > 0.90$) even at 50% missingness. Experimental results across different ancestries confirm consistent gains across datasets, with resilience to small sample sizes and weak LD. A 128-SNP (single-nucleotide polymorphism) context window (approximately 100 Kb) is validated through LD-decay analyses as sufficient to capture local correlation structures. By eliminating reference-panel dependence while preserving high accuracy, GenoBERT provides a scalable and robust solution for genotype imputation and a foundation for downstream genomic modeling.\n         \n       \n     \n     \n       [500] \n       \n        arXiv:2604.01993\n       \n          (replaced)\n\n        [ pdf ,  html ,  other ]\n     \n     \n       \n          Title: \n          SAFE: An LLM-as-Verifier Framework for Evidence-Grounded Multi-Hop Reasoning\n         \n          Daeyong Kwon ,  Soyoung Yoon ,  Seung-won Hwang  \n\n          Comments: \n          Accepted to EMNLP 2026 (Main Conference). Github:  this https URL  | Dataset:  this https URL \n         \n\n\n          Subjects: \n           Computation and Language (cs.CL) ; Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)\n         \n\n         \n          Multi-hop QA benchmarks often reward Large Language Models (LLMs) for spurious correctness, where models reach correct answers through invalid intermediate reasoning. We propose SAFE, an LLM-as-verifier framework for evidence-grounded multi-hop QA. Rather than judging only the final answer after generation, SAFE verifies reasoning during generation by checking intermediate steps against the provided passages and previous reasoning trajectory. To make this process checkable, SAFE decomposes reasoning into atomic, evidence-grounded units represented with Knowledge Graph (KG) triples. At train-time, SAFE verifies benchmark supervision under KG-grounded constraints and constructs reliable verifier training data. At inference-time, an external verifier checks each generated step, identifies invalid reasoning, and provides correction feedback before errors propagate. Across three multi-hop QA benchmarks, SAFE improves accuracy by 8.8 pp on average. These results show that evidence-grounded multi-hop QA benefits from shifting LLM-based evaluation from post-hoc answer judgment to stepwise reasoning verification.\n         \n       \n     \n     \n       [501] \n       \n        arXiv:2604.03529\n       \n          (replaced)\n\n        [ pdf ,  html ,  other ]\n     \n     \n       \n          Title: \n          Incentives Shape How Humans Co-Create with Generative AI\n         \n          Nathanael Jo ,  Manish Raghavan  \n\n\n\n          Subjects: \n           Human-Computer Interaction (cs.HC) ; Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI); Computers and Society (cs.CY)\n         \n\n         \n          Generative AI is quickly becoming an integral part of people's everyday workflows. Early evidence has shown that while generative AI can increase individual-level productivity, it does so at the cost of collective diversity, potentially narrowing the set of ideas and perspectives produced. Our research stands in contrast to this concern: through two pre-registered randomized control trials, we show that incentives can moderate AI's homogenizing force in two creative writing tasks where participants can use AI interactively. Participants rewarded for originality relative to peers produce collectively more diverse writing than those rewarded for quality alone. These participants continued to engage with AI, but ultimately incorporated less AI-generated text verbatim. Our results reveal that the effects of generative AI depend not only on the technology itself, but also the behavioral strategies and incentive structures surrounding its use. We release a rich, anonymized dataset capturing fine-grained trajectories of human-AI co-creation across both studies.\n         \n       \n     \n     \n       [502] \n       \n        arXiv:2604.06940\n       \n          (replaced)\n\n        [ pdf ,  html ,  other ]\n     \n     \n       \n          Title: \n          A First Guess Is Rarely the Final Answer: Learned Local Search That Generalizes to Larger Routing Problems\n         \n          Andoni Irazusta Garmendia  \n\n\n\n          Subjects: \n           Machine Learning (cs.LG) ; Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)\n         \n\n         \n          Neural routing solvers rarely stop at their first solution: constructed tours are usually refined by search, such as sampling or local search. Neural improvement methods learn this search as a policy that repeatedly applies local moves, but existing policies degrade sharply beyond their training size. We argue that size generalization depends on whether the information that determines the value of a move reaches the policy in a size-independent form, and identify three places where existing policies fall short: the representation of the incumbent solution, the scale of the input features, and the computation of move gains. We address all three in NICO, a learned 2-opt policy for routing that represents the incumbent by its solution edges, one token per edge without positional encodings, describes them with scale-free features, and adds the exact gain of each move to its logit, so that the network learns a correction to greedy selection. A controlled factorial study shows that edge tokens outperform node tokens with positional encodings, that the exact gains are necessary for transfer in the tested configuration, and that scale-free features help only together with them. Trained on instances with at most 100 nodes, NICO reaches a 1.1% gap on the Euclidean TSP with 1000 nodes, against 10.7% for greedy 2-opt and 36% for the best prior learned policy. It also improves the tours of constructive and diffusion-based solvers, is the best improvement method on road-network costs, and on the CVRP outperforms every classical control.\n         \n       \n     \n     \n       [503] \n       \n        arXiv:2604.08915\n       \n          (replaced)\n\n        [ pdf ,  html ,  other ]\n     \n     \n       \n          Title: \n          UniDG: Unified Defect Generation via Defect-Context Editing\n         \n          Yuanting Fan ,  Jun Liu ,  Bin-Bin Gao ,  Xiaochen Chen ,  Yuhuan Lin ,  Zhewei Dai ,  Jiawei Zhan ,  Chengjie Wang  \n\n          Comments: \n          NeurIPS 2026 Accepted\n         \n\n\n          Subjects: \n           Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV) ; Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)\n         \n\n         \n          Generating realistic visual defects is crucial for alleviating the scarcity of abnormal samples in anomaly detection, yet existing methods often rely on category-specific few-shot adaptation and struggle to generalize across objects, defect types, and domains. This limitation stems in part from the lack of paired defect editing data and the fine-grained nature of defect appearance, where subtle variations in scale, texture, and morphology must be preserved while maintaining consistency with the target scene. To address this challenge, we introduce UDG, a dataset of 300K normal-abnormal-mask-caption quadruplets curated from diverse defect-related scenarios, and present UniDG, a unified defect generation model. UniDG formulates defect synthesis as Defect-Context Editing: it extracts reference defect context with adaptive cropping, organizes reference and target inputs in a structured diptych format, and fuses multimodal conditions through MM-DiT attention. We further develop a two-stage training strategy: Diversity-SFT learns diverse transferable defect priors from paired editing data, while optional Consistency-RFT can further improve reference adherence, local realism, and defect-category consistency. Without per-category fine-tuning or using MVTec-AD/VisA for training, UniDG outperforms prior few-shot anomaly generation and image insertion/editing baselines in both synthesis quality and downstream single- and multi-class anomaly detection/localization.\n         \n       \n     \n     \n       [504] \n       \n        arXiv:2604.11512\n       \n          (replaced)\n\n        [ pdf ,  html ,  other ]\n     \n     \n       \n          Title: \n          EdgeCIM: A Hardware-Software Co-Design for CIM-Based Acceleration of Small Language Models\n         \n          Jinane Bazzi ,  Mariam Rakka ,  Fadi Kurdahi ,  Mohammed E. Fouda ,  Ahmed Eltawil  \n\n          Comments: \n          Accepted at IEEE TCAD\n         \n\n\n          Subjects: \n           Hardware Architecture (cs.AR) ; Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)\n         \n\n         \n          The growing demand for deploying Small Language Models (SLMs) on edge devices, including laptops, smartphones, automotive and robotic computing platforms, and high-performance embedded systems, has exposed fundamental inefficiencies in existing accelerators. While GPUs handle prefill workloads efficiently, the autoregressive decoding phase is dominated by GEMV operations that are inherently memory-bound, resulting in poor utilization and prohibitive energy costs at the edge. In this work, we present EdgeCIM, a hardware-software co-design framework for accelerating autoregressive decoding of SLMs on edge devices in latency-critical applications. At its core is a CIM macro, implemented in 65 nm, coupled with a tile-based mapping strategy that balances pipeline stages, maximizing parallelism while alleviating DRAM bandwidth bottlenecks. Our simulator enables design space exploration of SLMs up to 4B parameters, identifying hardware configurations that optimize latency and energy according to a configurable objective function. On LLaMA3.2 3B (INT4), EdgeCIM achieves 1.76x and 3.21x higher throughput than NVIDIA Jetson AGX Orin and Orin Nano Super operating in MAXN mode, respectively, and 7.07x higher throughput than Qualcomm's Snapdragon-X Elite. Extensive benchmarks on TinyLLaMA 1.1B, LLaMA3.2 (1B, 3B), Phi 3.5 mini 3.8B, Qwen2.5 (0.5B, 1.5B, 3B), SmolLM2 1.7B, SmolLM3 3B, and Qwen3 (0.6B, 1.7B, 4B) reveal that our accelerator, at INT4 precision, achieves on average 336.4 tokens/s and 173 tokens/J. These results establish EdgeCIM as a compelling solution towards real-time, energy-efficient, latency-critical SLM inference at the edge.\n         \n       \n     \n     \n       [505] \n       \n        arXiv:2604.11674\n       \n          (replaced)\n\n        [ pdf ,  html ,  other ]\n     \n     \n       \n          Title: \n          AffordSim: A Scalable Data Generator and Benchmark for Affordance-Aware Robotic Manipulation\n         \n          Mingyang Li ,  Haofan Xu ,  Haowen Sun ,  Xinzhe Chen ,  Sihua Ren ,  Liqi Huang ,  Xinyang Sui ,  Chenyang Miao ,  Jiawei Ye ,  Qiongjie Cui ,  Xingyu Chen ,  Zeyang Liu ,  Xuguang Lan  \n\n\n\n          Subjects: \n           Robotics (cs.RO) ; Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)\n         \n\n         \n          Many everyday robot manipulation skills are affordance-dependent, with success determined by whether the robot contacts the functional object region required by the subsequent action. Current simulation data generators obtain contacts from generic grasp estimators or per-object manual contact annotations, but generic estimators rank stable grasps without task semantics and often select contacts that are misaligned with the downstream action, while manual contact annotations must be rewritten for each new object and task. To solve these challenges, we introduce AffordSim, a scalable data generator and benchmark that integrates open-vocabulary 3D affordance prediction into simulation-based trajectory generation. Given a natural-language task description, AffordSim synthesizes a task-relevant scene, emits affordance queries, grounds them on object surfaces, samples region-conditioned grasps, and selects executable candidates with motion planning. It further randomizes object pose, texture, lighting, image noise, and cross-viewpoint backgrounds for sim-to-real transfer. We instantiate AffordSim as a 50-task benchmark across diverse manipulation skills, five robot embodiments, and 500+ rigid and articulated objects. AffordSim achieves 93% of the trajectory collection success rate of manual contact annotations on affordance-critical tasks and 89% on hard composite tasks. Vision-language-action policies trained on AffordSim data transfer zero-shot to a real Franka FR3, reaching 24% average success.\n         \n       \n     \n     \n       [506] \n       \n        arXiv:2604.14222\n       \n          (replaced)\n\n        [ pdf ,  html ,  other ]\n     \n     \n       \n          Title: \n          Adaptive Query Routing: A Tier-Based Framework for Hybrid Retrieval Across Financial, Legal, and Medical Documents\n         \n          Afshan Hashmi  \n\n          Comments: \n          12 pages, 4 figures, 6 tables. v2: method description corrected to match the experiment code; section-level retrieval metrics withdrawn because the original scorer ignored document identity; FinanceBench corpus clarified (supplied evidence snippets, first 50 public questions); prompt and output-length differences between configurations disclosed\n         \n\n\n          Subjects: \n           Information Retrieval (cs.IR) ; Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)\n         \n\n         \n          Retrieval-Augmented Generation (RAG) has become the standard paradigm for grounding Large Language Model outputs in external knowledge. Lumer et al. presented the first systematic evaluation comparing vector-based agentic RAG against hierarchical node-based reasoning systems for financial document QA across 1,200 SEC filings, finding vector-based systems achieved a 68% win rate. Concurrently, the PageIndex framework reported 98.7% accuracy on FinanceBench through purely reasoning-based retrieval. This paper extends their work by: (i) implementing and evaluating three retrieval configurations: Vector RAG, Tree Reasoning, and the proposed Adaptive Hybrid Retrieval (AHR), on a small controlled corpus of financial, legal, and medical documents; (ii) introducing a four-tier query complexity benchmark; and (iii) employing LLM-as-judge evaluation with GPT-4o-mini. Experiments reveal that Tree Reasoning achieves the highest overall score (0.900), but no single paradigm dominates across all tiers: AHR scores highest on cross-reference queries (0.850), all three methods tie on multi-section queries (0.929), and Vector RAG scores highest on the single multi-document query (0.900). Vector RAG has the lowest mean latency (1.61 s against 3.40 s for Tree Reasoning). On the first 50 publicly released FinanceBench questions, using the evidence snippets supplied with the benchmark as the retrieval corpus, Tree Reasoning scores 0.938, Hybrid AHR 0.901, and Vector RAG 0.821. Because the configurations also differ in document access, generation prompts and output length limits, these differences cannot be attributed to retrieval architecture alone. In these preliminary results the adaptive router does not outperform fixed tree reasoning overall (0.845 vs. 0.900); they motivate a larger evaluation of when query-based routing helps, rather than establishing its benefit.\n         \n       \n     \n     \n       [507] \n       \n        arXiv:2604.18978\n       \n          (replaced)\n\n        [ pdf ,  html ,  other ]\n     \n     \n       \n          Title: \n          Low-Rank Adaptation for Critic Learning in Off-Policy Reinforcement Learning\n         \n          Yuan Zhuang ,  Yuexin Bian ,  Sihong He ,  Jie Feng ,  Qing Su ,  Songyang Han ,  Jonathan Petit ,  Shihao Ji ,  Yuanyuan Shi ,  Fei Miao  \n\n          Comments: \n          2026 Conference on Neural Information Processing Systems (NeurIPS 2026)\n         \n\n\n          Subjects: \n           Machine Learning (cs.LG) ; Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)\n         \n\n         \n          Scaling critic capacity is a promising direction for improving off-policy reinforcement learning (RL). However, recent work shows that larger critics are prone to overfitting and instability in replay-based bootstrapped training. In this paper, we propose using Low-Rank Adaptation (LoRA) as a structural regularizer for critic learning from scratch. Our approach freezes randomly initialized base matrices and optimizes only the corresponding low-rank adapters, thereby constraining the learned weight changes to low-rank residuals. We evaluate our method on state-based continuous-control tasks, using SAC with SimbaV2 and BRC on DMC-Hard, and FastTD3 with its SimbaV2 variant on IsaacLab. Empirically, LoRA reduces critic loss during training and improves overall policy performance, achieving the best or competitive results on most tasks. Extensive experiments demonstrate that our low-rank updates provide a simple and effective form of structural regularization for critic learning in off-policy RL.\n         \n       \n     \n     \n       [508] \n       \n        arXiv:2605.01097\n       \n          (replaced)\n\n        [ pdf ,  html ,  other ]\n     \n     \n       \n          Title: \n          Interpretable Difficulty-Aware Knowledge Tracing in Tutor-Student Dialogues\n         \n          Shuyan Huang ,  Alexander Scarlatos ,  Jaewook Lee ,  Andrew Lan  \n\n          Comments: \n          Published at BEA 2026 (ACL 2026 Workshop)\n         \n\n          Journal-ref: \n          Proceedings of the 21st Workshop on Innovative Use of NLP for Building Educational Applications (BEA 2026), pp. 612-623, 2026\n         \n\n          Subjects: \n           Computation and Language (cs.CL) ; Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)\n         \n\n         \n          Recent advances in large language models (LLMs) have led to the development of AI-powered tutoring systems that provide interactive support via dialogue. To enable these tutoring systems to provide personalized support, it is essential to assess student performance at each turn, motivating knowledge tracing (KT) in dialogue settings. However, existing dialogue-based KT approaches often ignore question difficulty modeling and rely on opaque latent representations from LLMs, hindering accurate and interpretable prediction. In this work, we propose an interpretable difficulty-aware conversational KT framework built upon LLMs, which explicitly models students' abilities and the difficulty of tutor-posed tasks at each turn. The framework incorporates the original textual question and the next tutor-posed task to estimate the student's knowledge state and the difficulty of the upcoming turn. Furthermore, it integrates Item Response Theory to map LLM's outputs into student ability and question difficulty parameters, enabling interpretable prediction of student performance grounded in cognitive theories of learning. We evaluate the framework on two tutor-student dialogue datasets. Both quantitative and qualitative results show that our framework outperforms existing KT baselines, meanwhile generating interpretable outputs consistent with cognitive theory.\n         \n       \n     \n     \n       [509] \n       \n        arXiv:2605.04078\n       \n          (replaced)\n\n        [ pdf ,  html ,  other ]\n     \n     \n       \n          Title: \n          Validity-Calibrated Reasoning Distillation\n         \n          Khouloud Saadi ,  Di Wang  \n\n\n\n          Subjects: \n           Machine Learning (cs.LG) ; Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)\n         \n\n         \n          Reasoning distillation aims to transfer multi-step reasoning capabilities from large language models to smaller, efficient ones. While recent methods have shown promising gains, they typically rely on static teacher-student hierarchies and frame distillation as trajectory imitation. This is misaligned with the structure of reasoning, where intermediate steps are locally under-specified: global correctness constrains the final answer, but does not uniquely determine each intermediate move. We propose validity-calibrated reasoning distillation, a framework that treats reasoning distillation as a problem of local learning-signal allocation rather than path alignment. Instead of enforcing token-level imitation, we compare the student's and teacher's proposed next-step actions under the same prefix and use their relative local validity to modulate the strength of the distillation update. This yields a dynamic, context-dependent supervision that preserves the teacher's structural guidance while adapting update strength to local reasoning quality. Across math, code, and instruction-following benchmarks, our method outperforms strong distillation baselines. These results indicate that effective LLM reasoning distillation is governed not by rigid trajectory imitation, but by locally calibrated allocation of learning signal.\n         \n       \n     \n     \n       [510] \n       \n        arXiv:2605.06111\n       \n          (replaced)\n\n        [ pdf ,  html ,  other ]\n     \n     \n       \n          Title: \n          ASTOR: Multi-Task Code Reinforcement Learning via Utility-Driven Coordination\n         \n          Yujia Chen ,  Yang Ye ,  Xiao Chu ,  Yuchi Ma ,  Cuiyun Gao  \n\n          Comments: \n          Accepted to NeurIPS 2026\n         \n\n\n          Subjects: \n           Software Engineering (cs.SE) ; Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)\n         \n\n         \n          Reinforcement learning (RL) with verifiable rewards has proven effective at post-training LLMs for coding, yet deploying separate task-specific specialists incurs costs that scale with the number of tasks, motivating a unified multi-task RL (MTRL) approach. However, existing MTRL methods treat all coding tasks uniformly, relying on fixed data curricula under a shared optimization strategy, ultimately limiting the effectiveness of multi-task training. To address these limitations, we propose ASTOR, a multi-tASk code reinforcement learning framework via uTility-driven coORdination. Centered on task utility, a signal capturing each task learning potential and cross-task synergy, ASTOR comprises two coupled modules: 1) Hierarchical Utility-Routed Data Scheduling module hierarchically allocates training budget and prioritizes informative prompts, steering training toward the most valuable data and 2) Adaptive Utility-Calibrated Policy Optimization module dynamically scales per-task KL regularization, matching update constraints to each tasks current training state. Experiments on two widely-used LLMs across four representative coding tasks demonstrate that ASTOR consistently improves a single model across all tasks, outperforming the best task-specific specialist by 9.0%-9.5% and surpassing the strongest MTRL baseline by 7.5%-12.8%.\n         \n       \n     \n     \n       [511] \n       \n        arXiv:2605.08441\n       \n          (replaced)\n\n        [ pdf ,  html ,  other ]\n     \n     \n       \n          Title: \n          DUET: Optimize Token-Budget Allocation for Reinforcement Learning with Verifiable Rewards\n         \n          Haoyu Hu ,  Xuandong Zhao ,  Xuhai \"Orson'' Xu ,  Nori Jacoby  \n\n          Comments: \n          The Fortieth Annual Conference on Neural Information Processing Systems (NeurIPS 2026)\n         \n\n\n          Subjects: \n           Machine Learning (cs.LG) ; Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)\n         \n\n         \n          Reinforcement learning with verifiable rewards (RLVR) generates hundreds of thousands of tokens per training step, with rollout generation dominating the computational cost. The overall token budget can be controlled along two main dimensions: (i) deciding which prompts to allocate rollouts to, and (ii) deciding how long each rollout should be. Prior work has generally controlled only one of these dimensions at a time. We show that jointly tuning both decisions under a shared compute budget improves both reasoning quality and wall-clock training time. We instantiate this view as \\textbf{DU}al-controlled tok\\textbf{E}n alloca\\textbf{T}ion (DUET), a computationally efficient layer over GRPO that uses a lightweight pre-rollout surrogate of prompt informativeness to set how many rollouts each prompt receives, and a marker-gated abort rule with importance reweighting to set when to stop them. On Qwen3-1.7B trained on MATH, DUET outperforms full-budget GRPO and the other three budget-aware baseline methods. DUET's advantage further generalizes to other benchmarks across math and coding, and is on par with the best baseline on the scientific Q\\&A domain, while also achieving a $1.62\\times$ wall-clock speedup. More notably, using only 50\\% of the token budget, DUET still outperforms all baseline methods at their full budget, achieving an even higher $2.51\\times$ speedup over full-budget GRPO. We verify the high performance of DUET on other backbone LLMs, including Qwen3-4B to 14B and Llama-3.2-3B-Instruct. Notably, the gap between DUET and the strongest baseline \\emph{widens} as the budget tightens, contrary to the usual pattern in which efficient methods trade off quality as compute decreases. More broadly, these results suggest that DUET budget-aware control strategies are valuable not only for accelerating training, but also for improving the quality of the learning signal.\n         \n       \n     \n     \n       [512] \n       \n        arXiv:2605.11447\n       \n          (replaced)\n\n        [ pdf ,  html ,  other ]\n     \n     \n       \n          Title: \n          Conditional Memory Enhanced Item Representation for Generative Recommendation\n         \n          Ziwei Liu ,  Yejing Wang ,  Shengyu Zhou ,  Xinhang Li ,  Xiangyu Zhao  \n\n\n\n          Subjects: \n           Information Retrieval (cs.IR) ; Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)\n         \n\n         \n          Generative recommendation (GR) has emerged as a promising paradigm that predicts target items by autoregressively generating their semantic identifiers (SID). Most GR methods follow a quantization-representation-generation pipeline. While most research focuses on quantization and generation, the representation that bridges the two remains underexplored. Existing representation constructions mainly take two forms: either directly flattening the SID code embeddings into a sequence or merging them into a compact vector via external networks. While effective, they still expose two practical challenges: \\textbf{1) Loss of item semantics:} Information lost by quantization or compression needs to be recovered during the representation stage. \\textbf{2) Insufficient modeling of structured SID relations:} Intra-item code composition patterns and inter-item prefix-transition patterns are not explicitly preserved for representation and subsequent SID generation.\n To overcome these challenges, we propose \\textbf{ComeIR}, a \\textbf{\\uline{Co}}nditional \\textbf{\\uline{me}}mory Enhanced \\textbf{\\uline{I}}tem \\textbf{\\uline{R}}epresentation framework that reconstructs SID code embeddings into item-aware inputs and preserves structured SID evidence for prediction. Item-guided Token Scoring estimates the contribution of each SID code using the item embedding employed by the quantizer. Dual-level Conditional Memory captures intra-item prefix composition and inter-item prefix transitions, while Memory Merge integrates these signals into each historical item representation. The Memory-restoring Prediction Head further conditions SID-level prediction on the generated prefix and historical transition evidence. Extensive experiments demonstrate the effectiveness and flexibility of ComeIR, and further reveal scalable gains from enlarging conditional memory.\n         \n       \n     \n     \n       [513] \n       \n        arXiv:2605.11723\n       \n          (replaced)\n\n        [ pdf ,  html ,  other ]\n     \n     \n       \n          Title: \n          CaC: Advancing Video Reward Models via Hierarchical Spatiotemporal Concentrating\n         \n          Jiyuan Wang ,  Huan Ouyang ,  Jiuzhou Lin ,  Chunyu Lin ,  Dewen Fan ,  Boheng Zhang ,  Haonan Fan ,  Honglie Wang ,  Yiyang Fan ,  Zhenlong Yuan ,  Yongrui Heng ,  Guosheng Lin ,  Fan Yang  \n\n          Comments: \n          Accept to NeurIPS 2026, 27 pages, 10 figures\n         \n\n\n          Subjects: \n           Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV) ; Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)\n         \n\n         \n          In this paper, we propose Concentrate and Concentrate (CaC), a coarse-to-fine anomaly reward model based on Vision-Language Models. During inference, it first conducts a global temporal scan to anchor anomalous time windows, then performs fine-grained spatial grounding within the localized interval, and finally derives robust judgments via structured spatiotemporal Chain-of-Thought reasoning. To equip the model with these capabilities, we construct the first large-scale generated video anomaly dataset with per-frame bounding-box annotations, temporal anomaly windows, and fine-grained attribution labels. Building on this dataset, we design a three-stage progressive training paradigm. The model initially learns spatial and temporal anchoring through single- and multi-frame supervised fine-tuning, and then is optimized by a reinforcement learning strategy based on two-turn Group Relative Policy Optimization (GRPO). Beyond conventional accuracy rewards, we introduce Temporal and Spatial IoU rewards to supervise the intermediate localization process, effectively guiding the model toward more grounded and interpretable spatiotemporal reasoning. Extensive experiments demonstrate that CaC can stably concentrate on subtle anomalies, achieving a 25.7% accuracy improvement on fine-grained anomaly benchmarks and, when used as a reward signal, CaC reduces generated-video anomalies by 11.7% while improving overall video quality.\n         \n       \n     \n     \n       [514] \n       \n        arXiv:2605.11999\n       \n          (replaced)\n\n        [ pdf ,  html ,  other ]\n     \n     \n       \n          Title: \n          The Illusion of Power Capping in LLM Decode: A Phase-Aware Energy Characterisation Across Attention Architectures\n         \n          Bole Ma ,  Ayesha Afzal ,  Jan Eitzinger ,  Gerhard Wellein  \n\n          Comments: \n          Accepted by PPAM 2026 (16th International Conference on Parallel Processing and Applied Mathematics)\n         \n\n\n          Subjects: \n           Distributed, Parallel, and Cluster Computing (cs.DC) ; Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI); Machine Learning (cs.LG); Performance (cs.PF)\n         \n\n         \n          Power capping is a common GPU energy lever in LLM serving, and it appears to work: the cap is set, the GPU reports drawing less than it, and the energy budget is met. We show the cap does little for the phase that dominates LLM serving: autoregressive decode. Across four attention paradigms (GQA, MLA, Gated DeltaNet, and Mamba2) on an NVIDIA H200 under vLLM eager execution, decode kernels wait on HBM and the GPU idles between kernel launches, so decode draws less than half of the GPU's 700W rating and throughput stays within a few percent under every cap we set. The cap engages only when a large batch runs a long generation. Locking the SM clock instead lowers power without slowing memory-bound decode and recovers up to 32% of decode energy at minimal throughput loss. We identify three architecture-dependent frequency-scaling classes and find that, once prefill is measured without prefix caching, the recurrent architectures pay no prefill penalty: their linear-time mixing undercuts quadratic attention on long prompts, so their efficient decode adds to the prefill saving and lowers total request energy from the first output token at large batch.\n         \n       \n     \n     \n       [515] \n       \n        arXiv:2605.13047\n       \n          (replaced)\n\n        [ pdf ,  html ,  other ]\n     \n     \n       \n          Title: \n          Revealing the Gap in Human and VLM Scene Description through Counterfactual Semantic Saliency\n         \n          Ziqi Wen ,  Parsa Madinei ,  Miguel P. Eckstein  \n\n          Comments: \n          NeurIPS 2026\n         \n\n\n          Subjects: \n           Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV) ; Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)\n         \n\n         \n          Evaluating whether large vision-language models (VLMs) align with human perception for high-level semantic scene comprehension remains a challenge. Traditional white-box interpretability methods are inapplicable to closed-source architectures and passive metrics fail to isolate causal features. We introduce Counterfactual Semantic Saliency (CSS). This black-box, model-agnostic framework quantifies the importance of objects by measuring the semantic shift induced by their causal ablation from a scene. To evaluate AI-human semantic alignment, we tested prominent VLMs against a human psychophysics baseline comprising 16,603 valid responses across 307 complex natural scenes and 1,306 high-fidelity counterfactual variants. Our analysis reveals a pervasive scene comprehension gap: models exhibit an over-reliance (relative to humans) on object size, object centrality, and low-level saliency of objects. In contrast, models are less sensitive to the presence of people in the scenes than our human participants when describing the images. The over-reliance on object size is a primary driver explaining variations in model-human semantic divergence. Code and data are available at  this https URL  .\n         \n       \n     \n     \n       [516] \n       \n        arXiv:2605.14413\n       \n          (replaced)\n\n        [ pdf ,  html ,  other ]\n     \n     \n       \n          Title: \n          MahaVar: OOD Detection via Class-wise Mahalanobis Distance Variance under Neural Collapse\n         \n          Donghwan Kim ,  Hyunsoo Yoon  \n\n          Comments: \n          NeurIPS 2026\n         \n\n\n          Subjects: \n           Machine Learning (cs.LG) ; Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)\n         \n\n         \n          Out-of-distribution (OOD) detection is a critical component for ensuring the reliability of deep neural networks in safety-critical applications. In this work, we present a key empirical observation: for in-distribution (ID) samples, class-wise Mahalanobis distances exhibit a pronounced sharp minimum structure, where the distance to the nearest class is small while distances to all other classes remain large, resulting in high variance across classes. In contrast, OOD samples tend to exhibit a less pronounced sharp minimum structure, producing comparatively lower variance across classes. We further provide a theoretical analysis grounding this observation in Neural Collapse geometry: under relaxed Neural Collapse assumptions on within-class compactness and inter-class separation, ID samples are shown to structurally exhibit high class-wise distance variance, offering a theoretical basis for its use as an OOD score. Motivated by this observation and its theoretical backing, we propose MahaVar, a simple and effective post-hoc OOD detector that augments the Mahalanobis distance with a class-wise distance variance term. Following the OpenOOD v1.5 benchmark protocol, MahaVar achieves state-of-the-art performance on CIFAR-100 and ImageNet, with consistent improvements in both AUROC and FPR@95 over existing Mahalanobis-based methods across all benchmarks.\n         \n       \n     \n     \n       [517] \n       \n        arXiv:2605.15220\n       \n          (replaced)\n\n        [ pdf ,  html ,  other ]\n     \n     \n       \n          Title: \n          Always Learning, Always Mixing: Efficient and Simple Data Mixing All the Time\n         \n          Michael Y. Hu ,  Apurva Gandhi ,  Kyunghyun Cho ,  Tal Linzen ,  Pratyusha Sharma  \n\n          Comments: \n          NeurIPS 2026 camera ready\n         \n\n\n          Subjects: \n           Computation and Language (cs.CL) ; Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI); Machine Learning (cs.LG)\n         \n\n         \n          Data mixing decides how to combine different sources or types of data and is a consequential problem throughout language model training. In pretraining, data composition is a key determinant of model quality; in continual learning and adaptation, it governs what is retained and acquired. Yet existing data mixing methods address only one phase of this lifecycle at a time: some require smaller proxy models tied to a single training phase, others assume a fixed domain set, and continual learning lacks principled guidance altogether. We argue that data mixing is fundamentally an online decision making problem---one that recurs throughout training and demands a single, unified solution. We introduce OP-Mix (On-Policy Mix), a data mixing algorithm that operates across pretraining, midtraining, and instruction tuning. Our main insight is that candidate data mixtures can be cheaply simulated by interpolating between low-rank adapters trained directly on the current model, eliminating separate proxy models and ensuring the search is always grounded in the model's actual learning dynamics. Across the language model training lifecycle, OP-Mix consistently finds near-optimal mixtures while using a fraction of the compute of the baselines. In pretraining, OP-Mix improves upon training without mixing by 6.3% in average perplexity. For continual learning, OP-Mix matches the performance of both retraining and on-policy distillation while using 66% and 95% less overall compute, respectively. OP-Mix suggests a different view of language model training: not a sequence of distinct phases, but a single continuous process of learning from data.\n         \n       \n     \n     \n       [518] \n       \n        arXiv:2605.16679\n       \n          (replaced)\n\n        [ pdf ,  html ,  other ]\n     \n     \n       \n          Title: \n          CHI-Bench: Can AI Agents Automate End-to-End, Long-Horizon, Policy-Rich Healthcare Workflows?\n         \n          Haolin Chen ,  Deon Metelski ,  Leon Qi ,  Tao Xia ,  Joonyul Lee ,  Steve Brown ,  Kevin Riley ,  Frank Wang ,  T. Y. Alvin Liu ,  Hank Capps MD ,  Zeyu Tang ,  Xiangchen Song ,  Lingjing Kong ,  Fan Feng ,  Tianyi Zeng ,  Zhiwei Liu ,  Zixian Ma ,  Hang Jiang ,  Fangli Geng ,  Yuan Yuan ,  Chenyu You ,  Qingsong Wen ,  Hua Wei ,  Yanjie Fu ,  Yue Zhao ,  Carl Yang ,  Biwei Huang ,  Kun Zhang ,  Caiming Xiong ,  Sanmi Koyejo ,  Eric P. Xing ,  Philip S. Yu ,  Weiran Yao  \n\n          Comments: \n          Website:  this https URL  Code:  this https URL  Dataset:  this https URL \n         \n\n\n          Subjects: \n           Computation and Language (cs.CL) ; Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)\n         \n\n         \n          End-to-end automation of realistic healthcare operations stresses three capabilities underrepresented in current benchmarks: policy density, decisions must be grounded in a large library of medical, insurance, and operational rules; Multi-role composition: a single task requires the agent to play multiple roles with handoffs; and multilateral interaction: intermediate workflow steps are multi-turn dialogs, such as peer-to-peer review and patient outreach. We introduce $\\chi$-Bench, a benchmark of long-horizon healthcare workflows across three domains: provider prior authorization, payer utilization management, and care management. Each task hands the agent a clinical case in a high-fidelity simulator of 20 healthcare apps exposed via 87 MCP tools, which it must drive to a terminal status through tool calls and writing the role's artifacts, guided by a 1,290+ document managed-care operations handbook skill. Across 30 agent harness/models configurations, the best agent resolves only 28.0% of tasks, no agent clears 20% on strict pass^3, and executing all tasks in a single session slumps the performance to 3.8%. These results raise the hypothesis that similar gaps are likely to surface in other policy-dense, role-composed, irreversible enterprise domains.\n         \n       \n     \n     \n       [519] \n       \n        arXiv:2605.17811\n       \n          (replaced)\n\n        [ pdf ,  html ,  other ]\n     \n     \n       \n          Title: \n          One Model, Two Roles: Emergent Specialization in a Shared Recurrent Transformer\n         \n          Jucheng Shen ,  Barbara Su ,  Anastasios Kyrillidis  \n\n          Comments: \n          Accepted at NeurIPS 2026. 29 pages, 18 figures, 13 tables\n         \n\n\n          Subjects: \n           Machine Learning (cs.LG) ; Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI); Optimization and Control (math.OC)\n         \n\n         \n          Can a shared-weight recurrent Transformer---one that repeatedly applies the same Transformer to update evolving latent states---develop distinct internal roles without being partitioned into separate modules? We study this in Asymmetric Input Recurrence (AIR), a minimal two-state reasoning architecture in which the same Transformer model is reused for both updates (per literature, L and H) and the only built-in difference in the update rule is that the encoded input is injected during L-updates but not H-updates. Across Sudoku-Extreme and Maze, decoded rollouts reveal a consistent split: $z_H$ behaves like a fully committed proposal state, whereas $z_L$ retains local uncertainty and shifting intermediate structure. Freeze interventions provide evidence of dynamical dependence between the two states, with responses that vary across tasks, intervention directions, and seeds. Ablations show that to induce specialization, the shared model needs to be able to tell the two update types apart, either from input injection asymmetry or from a separate level token. Mechanistically, attention analysis shows that L-updates are consistently more local than H-updates in both Sudoku and Maze. Together, these results show that, in a two-state recurrent setting, a clear state-identity signal can induce stable, related functional roles inside a shared-parameter recurrent Transformer. Code is available at  this https URL .\n         \n       \n     \n     \n       [520] \n       \n        arXiv:2605.18346\n       \n          (replaced)\n\n        [ pdf ,  html ,  other ]\n     \n     \n       \n          Title: \n          Focused Forcing: Content-Aware Per-Frame KV Selection for Efficient Autoregressive Video Diffusion\n         \n          Peiliang Cai ,  Evelyn Zhang ,  Jiacheng Liu ,  Hao Lin ,  Ruiqi Zhang ,  Weile Mo ,  Yue Ma ,  Shikang Zheng ,  Jiehang Huang ,  Dongrui Liu ,  Linfeng Zhang  \n\n\n\n          Subjects: \n           Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV) ; Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)\n         \n\n         \n          Recent advances in autoregressive video diffusion have enabled sequential and streaming video generation. However, long-horizon generation requires increasingly large KV caches, making efficient compression without sacrificing quality challenging. Existing methods mostly select historical frames based on attention scores, but their context decisions remain coarse. When multiple frames are generated in the same chunk, these methods often apply a shared history selection to the whole chunk, score historical frames solely by attention, and assign head-wise budgets either uniformly or by attention-pattern heuristics rather than explicit head-importance estimation. We show that frames within the same generated chunk can depend on distinct historical frames, that the same historical frame can receive different attention scores as its relative temporal distance to the current frames changes, and that masking different heads induces unequal generation degradation. Motivated by these findings, we propose Focused Forcing, a training-free KV selection method that focuses cached history along both generated-frame and head dimensions. For each generated frame, Focused Forcing preserves the most relevant and distinctive historical frames by combining attention scores with diversity scores of historical frames, while assigning larger budgets to heads with higher estimated importance. Across multiple autoregressive generation paradigms, Focused Forcing achieves up to 1.48x end-to-end acceleration without training, while improving visual quality and text alignment. Our code is available at  this https URL .\n         \n       \n     \n     \n       [521] \n       \n        arXiv:2605.22973\n       \n          (replaced)\n\n        [ pdf ,  html ,  other ]\n     \n     \n       \n          Title: \n          Worse than Random: The Importance of a Baseline for Unsupervised Feature Selection -- Extended Version\n         \n          Muhammad Rajabinasab ,  Michael E. Houle ,  Oussama Chelly ,  Arthur Zimek  \n\n\n\n          Subjects: \n           Machine Learning (cs.LG) ; Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)\n         \n\n         \n          Many novel unsupervised feature selection methods are proposed each year, yet their empirical evaluation is limited to supervised and unsupervised evaluation metrics computed on selected datasets, along with comparisons to existing methods. However, in the absence of an established evaluation baseline, it is difficult to determine the value added to the existing literature by each of these methods, and how effective their underlying approaches are. In this evaluation study, we are using random feature selection as a baseline for evaluating unsupervised feature selection methods. While a random selection of features might seem to be a rather weak baseline, we show in this evaluation study that many of the state-of-the-art methods in unsupervised feature selection are outperformed by random feature selection in both performance and efficiency. Accordingly, we emphasize the strict requirement of considering random feature selection as a baseline in the development process of novel unsupervised feature selection methods, and we encourage the community to develop better evaluation measures for feature selection. Both would help to ensure consistent improvement and realistic assessment.\n         \n       \n     \n     \n       [522] \n       \n        arXiv:2605.26937\n       \n          (replaced)\n\n        [ pdf ,  html ,  other ]\n     \n     \n       \n          Title: \n          Beyond Questions: Evaluating the Stated Knowledge of LLMs\n         \n          Luca Giordano ,  Simon Razniewski  \n\n\n\n          Subjects: \n           Computation and Language (cs.CL) ; Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)\n         \n\n         \n          Parametric knowledge in large language models (LLMs) is a cornerstone of their success, yet remains poorly understood. Existing knowledge benchmarks typically rely on predefined questions (e.g., \"What is the birth date of M.L. King?\"), evaluating only knowledge that benchmark designers explicitly choose to query, a problematic availability bias. In this paper, we introduce open knowledge evaluation, a new paradigm for benchmarking the stated knowledge of LLMs. Instead of asking narrow questions, it evaluates models on the knowledge they choose to surface in response to open-ended elicitation prompts (e.g., \"Tell me everything you know about M.L. King\"). This shifts the focus from predefined answer retrieval toward characterizing the knowledge models naturally express. We instantiate this paradigm with BeQu (Beyond Questions), a benchmark of 10,000 entities paired with reference corpora for statement verification. Using BeQu, we evaluate a broad range of language models and analyze the effects of reasoning effort, model scale, prompt format, and knowledge domain. Data and leaderboard are available on this work's GitHub repository and at  this http URL , the benchmark website.\n         \n       \n     \n     \n       [523] \n       \n        arXiv:2605.27431\n       \n          (replaced)\n\n        [ pdf ,  html ,  other ]\n     \n     \n       \n          Title: \n          Tackling Multimodal Learning Challenges with Mixture-of-Expert: A Survey\n         \n          Liangwei Nathan Zheng ,  Wenhao Liang ,  Wei Emma Zhang ,  Lin Yue ,  Weitong Chen  \n\n          Comments: \n          This survey has been accepted by IJCAI 2026. Results were released by 30 April 2026. This latest replacement is an extension version of survey to the previous version, which has been submitted to ACM Computing Survey for peer-review\n         \n\n\n          Subjects: \n           Machine Learning (cs.LG) ; Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)\n         \n\n         \n          Mixture-of-Experts (MoE) presents a naturally compatible and scalable framework for multimodal learning, demonstrating strong adaptability across diverse modalities and tasks. Despite its growing success, a comprehensive and systematic review on the MoE metho addressing multimodal challenges remains lacking. Existing surveys tend to evaluate either multimodal learning or MoE independently from method taxonomy, overlooking the unique interplay between them. This survey fills that gap by answering a central question: \\textit{How does MoE effectively resolve multimodal challenges?} We approach this from three key perspectives: (1) \\textbf{MoE as an Efficient Multimodal Engine:} enabling scalable multimodal modeling by decoupling computational cost from parameter growth and mitigating modality redundancy through selective expert activation; (2) \\textbf{MoE as a Multimodal Representation Learner:} integrating complementary multi-opinion expert knowledge to enrich alignment and interaction representations; and (3) \\textbf{MoE as a Multimodal Adapter:} providing a modular and flexible mechanism to model imperfect data scenarios such as modality imbalance and missing modality. Through our extensive literature review, we identify critical research gaps, including interpretable routing, expert communication, modality integration, and lifelong multimodal learning. We position this survey as a foundation for future research toward interpretable and sustainable multimodal Mixture-of-Experts system.\n         \n       \n     \n     \n       [524] \n       \n        arXiv:2605.28174\n       \n          (replaced)\n\n        [ pdf ,  html ,  other ]\n     \n     \n       \n          Title: \n          FLORO: A Multimodal Geospatial Foundation Model for Ecological Remote Sensing Across Sensors and Scales\n         \n          Jorge L. Rodriguez ,  Victor Angulo Morales ,  Areej Alwahas ,  Mariana Elias Lara ,  Fida Mohammad Thoker ,  Kasper Johansen ,  Bernard Ghanem ,  Fernando T. Maestre ,  Matthew F. McCabe  \n\n          Comments: \n          57 pages, 11 figures\n         \n\n          Journal-ref: \n          ISPRS Journal of Photogrammetry and Remote Sensing 243, Part 1 (2027) 811-839\n         \n\n          Subjects: \n           Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV) ; Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)\n         \n\n         \n          Foundation models offer a promising route to transferable remote sensing representations, but many current approaches depend on very large pretraining datasets and fixed sensor configurations, limiting their suitability for ecological and environmental applications, where observations often vary across platforms, spatial and spectral resolutions, and available modalities. We introduce FLORO, a multimodal geospatial foundation model designed to learn transferable representations from a small but highly diverse remote sensing corpus. FLORO is pretrained using masked autoencoding on a heterogeneous combination of Sentinel-1, Sentinel-2, SkySAT imagery, elevation, and UAV-derived data. To accommodate sensor variability, FLORO incorporates availability-aware inputs that indicate which spectral bands and auxiliary modalities are present in each sample, enabling a unified input space across heterogeneous sensor configurations. We evaluated FLORO on the PANGAEA benchmark under a frozen-encoder protocol across scene classification, segmentation, and regression tasks. Despite being pretrained on a smaller corpus than competing foundation models, FLORO achieved strong and stable transfer across optical, optical-SAR, and optical-elevation benchmarks spanning medium-resolution satellite, airborne, and ultra-high-resolution UAV imagery. FLORO obtained the second-best average segmentation performance across six PANGAEA benchmarks, the third-best average rank across three scene-classification benchmarks, and excelled in regression tasks, while qualitative results showed improved preservation of spatial structure in flood, urban, biomass, and canopy-height prediction settings. Overall, these results position FLORO as a practical framework for ecological and environmental monitoring under heterogeneous sensing conditions.\n         \n       \n     \n     \n       [525] \n       \n        arXiv:2605.29488\n       \n          (replaced)\n\n        [ pdf ,  html ,  other ]\n     \n     \n       \n          Title: \n          AnyMo: Scaling Any-Modality Conditional Motion Generation with Masked Modeling\n         \n          Yiheng Li ,  Zhuo Li ,  Ruibing Hou ,  Yingjie Chen ,  Hong Chang ,  Hao Liu ,  Shiguang Shan  \n\n\n\n          Subjects: \n           Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV) ; Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)\n         \n\n         \n          Conditional human motion generation remains a fundamental challenge in computer vision and robotics. Despite significant progress, current methods are often constrained by fixed modality configurations and task-specific architectures, leaving cross-modal interactions and the scaling laws of multimodal-conditioned synthesis largely underexplored. A key bottleneck is the scarcity of large-scale modality-aligned motion data, limiting generalization across diverse control signals. In this work, we introduce OmniHuMo, a large-scale, high-quality dataset comprising over 5,000 hours of motion and 3.2 million sequences with precisely aligned multimodal annotations (e.g., text, speech, music, and trajectory). Leveraging OmniHuMo, we propose AnyMo, a unified multimodal framework combining a Residual FSQ-based motion tokenizer with a scalable masked modeling transformer, enabling high-quality motion synthesis under arbitrary modality combinations. Extensive experiments show that AnyMo achieves high-fidelity synthesis while offering flexible control over both spatial and stylistic attributes.\n         \n       \n     \n     \n       [526] \n       \n        arXiv:2605.29532\n       \n          (replaced)\n\n        [ pdf ,  html ,  other ]\n     \n     \n       \n          Title: \n          GUITestScape: Towards Open-set Evaluation on Exploratory GUI Testing\n         \n          Xiaoyi Chen ,  Yifei Gao ,  Yang Xu ,  Zhenlong Wang ,  Jian Yan ,  Jiming An ,  Xingxing Song ,  Liqun Deng ,  Xiaojun Meng ,  Jiansheng Wei ,  Yi Zhang ,  Jitao Sang  \n\n\n\n          Subjects: \n           Software Engineering (cs.SE) ; Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)\n         \n\n         \n          Exploratory GUI testing is a particularly demanding setting for MLLM agents: without predefined test scripts, an agent must autonomously navigate an application and discover defects through its own interaction. However, current evaluation falls short on two fronts. First, existing benchmarks focus almost exclusively on interaction defects, leaving display defects outside the evaluation frame. Second, evaluation protocols are bound to predefined defect annotations, collapsing the testing process into a single end-state judgment that conflates qualitatively distinct failure modes. To address these challenges, we present GUITestScape, an interactive benchmark covering 61 real-world Android applications and 508 preset defects spanning interaction and display types, and introduce GUIJudge, an open-set evaluator that decomposes an agent's testing trajectory into independently diagnosable capabilities. Experimental results demonstrate that GUIJudge achieves reliable process-aware evaluation beyond predefined annotations, substantially outperforming all baselines. Benchmarking on GUITestScape further reveals that detection remains the critical bottleneck for existing models across both defect types, and that integrating GUIJudge's verifiers into existing agents significantly boosts their detection performance without retraining.\n         \n       \n     \n     \n       [527] \n       \n        arXiv:2606.04262\n       \n          (replaced)\n\n        [ pdf ,  html ,  other ]\n     \n     \n       \n          Title: \n          Can I Take Another Dose? DoseBench: Evaluating LLM Decision-Making in OTC Dosing QA\n         \n          Maroof Kousar ,  Jinsung Han ,  Mohit Chandra ,  Munmun De Choudhury ,  Yibo Hu  \n\n          Comments: \n          12 pages, 5 figures\n         \n\n\n          Subjects: \n           Computation and Language (cs.CL) ; Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)\n         \n\n         \n          Large language models (LLMs) are increasingly used to answer everyday health questions online, including whether someone can take another dose of an over-the-counter (OTC) medication. Yet this common safety-relevant setting remains underexplored in medical QA. Answering these questions requires checking previous doses, timing, and product-specific dosing limits, while recognizing when important information is missing. We introduce DoseBench, a focused benchmark of 230 consumer-style acetaminophen and ibuprofen dosing questions with human-annotated YES, NO, and AMBIGUOUS labels, and evaluate eight LLMs with five responses per question. Overall accuracy ranges from 44.8% to 62.2%, while models incorrectly approve 14.0% to 60.5% of prohibited doses. Models with similar accuracy can therefore make very different dosing decisions. Additional analyses show that these failures are not easily fixed by giving models more guidance. Asking models to check dose, timing, and missing information makes them more cautious, while providing drug facts improves accuracy but can make them overlook missing information. These findings highlight a broader challenge in health QA: applying rules correctly and recognizing when information is missing.\n         \n       \n     \n     \n       [528] \n       \n        arXiv:2606.04284\n       \n          (replaced)\n\n        [ pdf ,  html ,  other ]\n     \n     \n       \n          Title: \n          Interpretability Is Not Just a Tax: Sparsity and Diversity Yield Legible and Composable Reward Experts for Personalization\n         \n          Yifan Wang ,  Jinyi Mu ,  Mayank Jobanputra ,  Yu Wang ,  Ji-Ung Lee ,  Soyoung Oh ,  Isabel Valera ,  Vera Demberg  \n\n\n\n          Subjects: \n           Machine Learning (cs.LG) ; Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI); Computation and Language (cs.CL)\n         \n\n         \n          Preference modeling aims to capture heterogeneous human values and increasingly relies on learning latent components to represent this variation without fine-grained supervision. What these components actually represent, however, is rarely examined. We audit Mixture-of-Experts (MoE) reward models along three rungs---semantic coherence, functional correspondence, and composability---in controlled and real-world settings, holding the architecture and training data fixed so that differences isolate the effect of the training objective. Our diagnosis is that the components conventional MoE reward models learn are entangled, which limits not only interpretability but also the personalization such models are built for. The entanglement is not intrinsic to the architecture but a property of the training objective: off-the-shelf sparsity and diversity regularizers already substantially reduce it. The resulting configuration, which we call sparse MoE, recovers all underlying semantic categories without domain supervision in a controlled setting, reaching 84.8% expert purity against 60.8% for conventional MoE; on real-world data its experts admit human-interpretable task- and domain-level descriptions aligned with their functional strengths. Targeted interventions on routing weights produce predictable preference changes, showing that these interpretations are behaviorally grounded rather than merely descriptive. Crucially, the disentanglement induced by this objective also makes the experts composable: lightweight router updates yield a 25.81-point improvement in few-shot personalization with 50 examples, roughly 2.7 times the gain of the identical architecture and adaptation procedure applied to entangled experts. Together, these results show that MoE reward models can learn interpretable expert structure, and that the same structure can substantially improve personalization.\n         \n       \n     \n     \n       [529] \n       \n        arXiv:2606.05415\n       \n          (replaced)\n\n        [ pdf ,  html ,  other ]\n     \n     \n       \n          Title: \n          Executable Schema Contracts: From Automatic Ingestion to Multi-Source Retrieval\n         \n          Padmaja Jonnalagedda ,  Yuguang Yao ,  Xiang Gao ,  Hilaf Hasson ,  Kamalika Das  \n\n          Comments: \n          9 pages, 4 figures, plus supplementary appendix\n         \n\n\n          Subjects: \n           Computation and Language (cs.CL) ; Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI); Machine Learning (cs.LG)\n         \n\n         \n          Real-world data spans tables, documents, and semi-structured files with implicit semantics. Querying this data requires integrating evidence across inconsistent schemas and formats, yet existing approaches either demand costly manual engineering or bypass structure entirely. We present a system that automatically discovers an executable schema from raw multi-source data and uses it as a shared contract for knowledge graph construction and query-time retrieval. A closed-world field catalog constrains LLM-based schema discovery to attested fields; deterministic structural analysis infers identity keys, foreign keys, and source hierarchy; and the resulting schema drives extraction, deduplication, and cross-source linking into a provenance-aware knowledge graph. At query time the schema -- optionally extended via a monotonic protocol -- conditions a multi-tool agent routing retrieval across structured lookup, graph traversal, and vector search, returning grounded answers with traceable citations. In controlled zero-shot comparisons using the same LLM, data, and evaluation harness, the system improves over retrieval-only and decomposition-based baselines across four QA benchmarks, with ablations showing that schema-conditioned routing, structural intelligence, and schema-guided construction each contribute to the gains.\n         \n       \n     \n     \n       [530] \n       \n        arXiv:2606.06474\n       \n          (replaced)\n\n        [ pdf ,  html ,  other ]\n     \n     \n       \n          Title: \n          Self-Augmenting Retrieval for Diffusion Language Models\n         \n          Paul Jünger ,  Justin Lovelace ,  Linxi Zhao ,  Dongyoung Go ,  Kilian Q. Weinberger  \n\n          Comments: \n          ICML 2026\n         \n\n\n          Subjects: \n           Computation and Language (cs.CL) ; Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI); Machine Learning (cs.LG)\n         \n\n         \n          Diffusion language models generate text by iteratively denoising an entire response in parallel, holding a noisy draft of the answer at every step. We show that these intermediate states surface salient entities early in the denoising trajectory, making them a useful lookahead signal for retrieval-augmented generation (RAG). We further demonstrate that grounding generation in retrieved evidence sharply reduces inter-token dependence, making RAG particularly well suited to parallel decoding. We exploit both through Self-Augmenting Retrieval for Diffusion Language Models (SARDI), a dynamic RAG framework that uses speculative token predictions to guide retrieval during denoising. SARDI is training-free, retriever-agnostic, and applicable to any reasoning-capable discrete diffusion language model. Across five multi-hop QA benchmarks, SARDI outperforms training-free diffusion and autoregressive retrieval baselines at up to 4x higher throughput.\n         \n       \n     \n     \n       [531] \n       \n        arXiv:2606.06492\n       \n          (replaced)\n\n        [ pdf ,  html ,  other ]\n     \n     \n       \n          Title: \n          Code2LoRA: Hypernetwork-Generated Adapters for Code Language Models under Software Evolution\n         \n          Liliana Hotsko ,  Yinxi Li ,  Yuntian Deng ,  Pengyu Nie  \n\n\n\n          Subjects: \n           Software Engineering (cs.SE) ; Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI); Computation and Language (cs.CL)\n         \n\n         \n          Code language models need repository-level context to resolve imports, APIs, and project conventions. Existing methods inject this knowledge as long inputs (retrieved through RAG or dependency analysis) or through per-repository fine-tuning and LoRA---costly at repository scale and brittle to evolving codebases. We introduce Code2LoRA, a hypernetwork framework that generates repository-specific LoRA adapters, effectively injecting repository knowledge with zero inference-time token overhead. Code2LoRA supports two usage scenarios: Code2LoRA-Static converts a single repository snapshot into an adapter, suitable for comprehension of stable codebases; while Code2LoRA-Evo maintains an adapter backed by a GRU hidden state updated per code diff, suitable for active development of evolving codebases. To evaluate Code2LoRA against parameter-efficient fine-tuning baselines, we build RepoPeftBench, a benchmark of 604 Python repositories with two tracks: a static track with 40K training and 12K test assertion-completion tasks, and an evolution track with 215K commit-derived training and 87K commit-derived test tasks. On the static track, Code2LoRA-Static achieves 63.8% cross-repo and 66.2% in-repo exact match, matching the per-repository LoRA upper bound; on the evolution track, Code2LoRA-Evo achieves 60.3% cross-repo exact match (+5.2 pp over a single shared LoRA). Code2LoRA's code can be found at  this https URL  the model checkpoints and RepoPeftBench datasets can be found at  this https URL .\n         \n       \n     \n     \n       [532] \n       \n        arXiv:2606.06840\n       \n          (replaced)\n\n        [ pdf ,  html ,  other ]\n     \n     \n       \n          Title: \n          Characterize Then Distill: Mechanistic Reasoning in Large Output Spaces\n         \n          Debjyoti Saha Roy  \n\n          Comments: \n          Revision of v3. Adds a pre-registered MuSiQue-Ans extension (recovery advantage over chain-of-thought distillation not detected) and a structural head characterization on two public tasks, EURLEX57K and BigCodeBench, with a locked programming distillation pilot. 89 pages, 8 figures. Code:  this https URL \n         \n\n\n          Subjects: \n           Computation and Language (cs.CL) ; Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI); Machine Learning (cs.LG)\n         \n\n         \n          Reasoning-trained language models can select, zero-shot, a small set of relevant labels from thousands to hundreds of thousands of candidates. We ask how they do it mechanistically, and whether the mechanism can be distilled. We treat each decision as a token-level event scored by the model's own decision margin: the token that picks a coarse region of the label space, the tokens that pick a label within it, and the token where the output departs from a near-miss named earlier in the reasoning. Attribution, exact mean-ablation, knock-in into another example's context and a null calibration give individual attention heads causal standing. On clinical coding of MIMIC-IV discharge summaries, with all 5,651 candidate codes in context, a small, global, phase-structured set of heads is necessary and sufficient on essentially every summary; distinct head families read the candidate region and the earlier near-miss, and blocking what they read removes their effect; for mentions elicitable in the reasoning, a disjoint mid-layer set makes the region elicitable several tokens before the code. The structure recurs in a second reasoner. We introduce MISTILL, which, unlike chain-of-thought distillation, also supervises the student's pooled attention at these decision events. Read on heads found after training, it nearly doubles the causal recovery of the contrastive decision in a cross-family student and adds a small, seed-stable gain in one that already carries most of it, with no detected task difference at matched precision; under matched selection it exceeds a frozen random-head pool on both recovery and task score. A pre-registered evidence-flip test detected no improvement in evidence-dependent decisions through the tested routes.\n         \n       \n     \n     \n       [533] \n       \n        arXiv:2606.10852\n       \n          (replaced)\n\n        [ pdf ,  html ,  other ]\n     \n     \n       \n          Title: \n          JANUS: Evaluating Goal-Conditioned Distortion in Factually Grounded LLM Communication\n         \n          Polydoros Giannouris ,  Mohsinul Kabir ,  Sophia Ananiadou  \n\n\n\n          Subjects: \n           Computation and Language (cs.CL) ; Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)\n         \n\n         \n          LLM deception is often evaluated through fabricated claims, explicit lies, or strategic concealment. Yet misleading communication can also arise from selectively presenting true facts, for example, by omitting adverse evidence, softening unfavorable details, emphasizing favorable information, or replacing precise qualifications with vague language. Existing benchmarks largely overlook this subtler failure mode. We introduce JANUS, a benchmark for measuring goal-conditioned pragmatic distortion in fact-grounded LLM outputs. Each of its 160 scenarios, spanning 8 domains, provides a fixed pool of favorable and adverse facts and contrasts a neutral decision support with institutional conditioning, such as increasing adoption, or support despite potential harm to affected individuals or groups. By constraining outputs to the same fact pool, JANUS isolates misleading net impressions from hallucination and fabrication. Experiments across 12 LLMs reveal consistent goal-conditioned distortions, showing that current models remain sensitive to incentives and framing and lack robust safeguards against selectively misleading communication. We publicly release our corpus and code.  this https URL \n         \n       \n     \n     \n       [534] \n       \n        arXiv:2606.11087\n       \n          (replaced)\n\n        [ pdf ,  html ,  other ]\n     \n     \n       \n          Title: \n          Test-Time Gradient Guidance of Flow Policies in Reinforcement Learning\n         \n          Zhiyuan Zhou ,  Andy Peng ,  Charles Xu ,  Qiyang Li ,  Jost Tobias Springenberg ,  Kevin Frans ,  Sergey Levine  \n\n\n\n          Subjects: \n           Machine Learning (cs.LG) ; Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)\n         \n\n         \n          Expressive continuous control policies, such as diffusion and flow models, form the backbone of recent advances in scaling imitation learning for simulated and real robot control. While they are known to scale stably in the supervised imitation learning setting, incorporating them into reinforcement learning (RL) pipelines for policy improvement has proven more difficult. It often requires specialized training objectives or backpropagating through denoising processes, which cause well-known issues with stability and affect scalability. In this paper we study the question of whether simple policy improvement schemes at test time alone, leaving stable supervised policy training intact, can be a competitive alternative which sidesteps these issues. To this end, we propose QGF (Q-Guided Flow), an RL algorithm that performs policy optimization entirely at test time. QGF works by pre-training both a reference flow policy (via a standard behavioral cloning objective) and a value function critic and, at test time, using the value gradient to guide the reference policy to generate higher-value actions without any additional policy learning. Empirically, QGF outperforms prior test-time RL methods on single-task and goal-conditioned offline RL benchmarks with high-dimensional action spaces, and is competitive with state-of-the-art training-time algorithms while being much cheaper to run. Moreover, it exhibits favorable scaling with model size by avoiding the instability of actor-critic training, offering a practical and effective alternative RL algorithm with expressive policies.\n         \n       \n     \n     \n       [535] \n       \n        arXiv:2606.11854\n       \n          (replaced)\n\n        [ pdf ,  html ,  other ]\n     \n     \n       \n          Title: \n          Fine-tuning Multi-modal LLMs with ART: Art-based Reinforcement Training\n         \n          Michal Chudoba ,  Sergey Alyaev ,  Petra Galuscakova ,  Tomasz Wiktorski  \n\n\n\n          Subjects: \n           Machine Learning (cs.LG) ; Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI); Computation and Language (cs.CL)\n         \n\n         \n          Adapting Large Language Models (LLMs) to specific tasks usually involves fine-tuning: Low-Rank Adaptation (LoRA) edits the weights, while soft prompting prepends trainable virtual tokens. Both change how an already-optimized model is served, so serving many task-specific adapters at high throughput requires adapter-aware infrastructure. We propose Art-Based Reinforcement Training (ART), which optimizes a single auxiliary input image to specialize a frozen Multimodal Large Language Model (MLLM) for a specific task. At inference time, ART simply includes this image in each standard multimodal prompt, requiring no changes to the serving engine. We compare ART with LoRA and soft prompting across five reasoning benchmarks for four small MLLMs from three architectures. While different methods perform best for different tasks, ART ranks first or second in 10 of 20 benchmark-MLLM pairs, with its best results on the two math word problem benchmarks (GSM8k and SVAMP). It is significantly ahead of the next best adapter in 2 cases, within the noise of the next best adapter in 6, and significantly above the text-only baseline in 18. ART therefore reaches accuracy close to LoRA and soft prompts while serving faster than LoRA. In the vLLM serving stack, ART reduces throughput by 6% to 14% relative to text-only requests, compared to LoRA's 13% to 93% slowdown. Beyond efficient serving, ART artifacts are plain images, making them easy to store, distribute, and share. This approach keep task-specific adaptation outside the model weights, so the same frozen model can support different tasks without separate model variants.\n         \n       \n     \n     \n       [536] \n       \n        arXiv:2606.14867\n       \n          (replaced)\n\n        [ pdf ,  html ,  other ]\n     \n     \n       \n          Title: \n          Evaluating the Robustness of Proof Autoformalization in Lean 4\n         \n          Zhengtao Gui ,  Sheng Yang ,  Zhouxing Shi  \n\n          Comments: \n          22 pages, 4 figures\n         \n\n\n          Subjects: \n           Computation and Language (cs.CL) ; Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI); Machine Learning (cs.LG)\n         \n\n         \n          Proof autoformalization aims to translate a mathematical informal proof in natural language into a formal proof in a formal language such as Lean 4. Existing LLM-based models for proof autoformalization are typically evaluated only on well-formed informal proofs from curated datasets. We argue that a robust proof autoformalizer must remain faithful even for informal proofs that diverge from these idealized ones, and we present the first study on the robustness of proof autoformalization models. We formulate two categories of perturbations: a global perturbation paraphrases the informal theorem-proof pair in a different style, under which the formalization should remain consistent; a local perturbation alters a value, symbol, or proof step, possibly in a counterfactual way, and a robust formalization should faithfully reflect the perturbation rather than reverting to the original or inferring a different one on its own. We build a benchmark with both perturbations on miniF2F and MATH-500, and automatically measure how stable a proof autoformalization's correctness is under global perturbations and how faithfully its output reflects local perturbations. We evaluate seven recent models: their correctness varies under global perturbations, and they mostly fail to remain faithful under local perturbations.\n         \n       \n     \n     \n       [537] \n       \n        arXiv:2606.15735\n       \n          (replaced)\n\n        [ pdf ,  html ,  other ]\n     \n     \n       \n          Title: \n          EHRNote-ChatQA: A Benchmark for Evidence-Grounded Multi-Turn Clinical Question Answering over Longitudinal Discharge Summaries\n         \n          Jiyoun Kim ,  Muhan Yeo ,  Eunhye Jang ,  Jeewon Yang ,  Hangyul Yoon ,  Su Ji Lee ,  Hee Jo Han ,  Hee-Jae Jung ,  Doyun Kwon ,  Jun young Lee ,  Jaehun Lee ,  Jung-Oh Lee ,  Sunjun Kweon ,  Jong Hak Moon ,  Daseul Kim ,  Minjae Cho ,  Edward Choi  \n\n\n\n          Subjects: \n           Computation and Language (cs.CL) ; Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)\n         \n\n         \n          Discharge summaries are crucial clinical documents containing the context of a patient's overall hospital stay, and are routinely reviewed by medical experts for patient readmission, ongoing care, and diagnostic decision-making. When reviewing them, medical experts often must iteratively synthesize information across multiple discharge summaries while verifying the evidence supporting each answer. Although large language models (LLMs) are increasingly explored for clinical question answering, existing benchmarks do not sufficiently reflect this setting: they often evaluate exam-style medical knowledge or focus on single-turn question answering with limited evidence-grounding evaluation. We introduce EHRNote-ChatQA, the first benchmark for evidence-grounded multi-turn clinical question answering over patients' multiple discharge summaries. Built from de-identified MIMIC-IV discharge summaries, EHRNote-ChatQA contains 967 patient-level multi-turn samples spanning one to five notes and 16,072 medical-expert-verified QA pairs (8,036 content questions, each paired with an evidence-grounding question) across eight clinical categories. The benchmark is constructed through an expert-informed pipeline combining discharge-summary structuring schema, expert-curated multi-turn QA templates, and LLM-based generation, followed by review and revision of every single QA sample by 12 medical experts (11 physicians and 1 nurse). Benchmarking 24 proprietary and open-weight LLMs reveals several challenges, including that LLMs struggle more with evidence grounding than content answering, multi-turn errors compound across turns, and single-turn clinical QA performance does not reliably transfer to this setting. These findings establish EHRNote-ChatQA as a rigorous and practical benchmark for evaluating clinical QA systems.\n         \n       \n     \n     \n       [538] \n       \n        arXiv:2606.18836\n       \n          (replaced)\n\n        [ pdf ,  html ,  other ]\n     \n     \n       \n          Title: \n          Improving Human-Robot Teamwork in Urban Search and Rescue Through Episodic Memory of Prior Collaboration\n         \n          Taewoon Kim ,  Emma van Zoelen ,  Mark Neerincx  \n\n          Comments: \n          Accepted at IEEE RO-MAN 2026\n         \n\n\n          Subjects: \n           Human-Computer Interaction (cs.HC) ; Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)\n         \n\n         \n          Effective human-robot teamwork requires robots to adapt to partners, situations, and task dynamics from the start of an interaction. In the MATRX Urban Search and Rescue (USAR) environment, people can externalize collaboration patterns (CPs) they discover during teamwork through a chat and reflection interface. We study whether a robot can use such prior team experience to become a better teammate in future interactions. To this end, we represent historical CPs as knowledge-graph episodic memories and use graph representation learning with a node-classification objective to identify a representative and effective memory for reuse. We then initialize the robot with this memory before a new collaboration episode begins. Across 20 participants and 160 round-level observations, initializing the robot with a single automatically selected prior CP increases rescue success from 25.7% to 41.3% and reduces average task time by 283 seconds. The strongest gains appear at the beginning of interaction, suggesting that reusable episodic memory can help robots enter collaboration with more effective task knowledge and support smoother early teamwork.\n         \n       \n     \n     \n       [539] \n       \n        arXiv:2606.18932\n       \n          (replaced)\n\n        [ pdf ,  html ,  other ]\n     \n     \n       \n          Title: \n          TransitNet: A Compact Attention-Augmented Deep Learning Framework for Low-SNR Transit Blind Searches\n         \n          Xingchen Yan ,  Jian Ge ,  Qingtian Liu ,  Kevin Willis ,  Quanquan Hu ,  Jiapeng Zhu  \n\n          Comments: \n          24 pages, 22 figures, 4 tables, submitted to Astronomy & Astrophysics\n         \n\n\n          Subjects: \n           Earth and Planetary Astrophysics (astro-ph.EP) ; Instrumentation and Methods for Astrophysics (astro-ph.IM); Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI); Machine Learning (cs.LG)\n         \n\n         \n          Motivated by the observational incompleteness of intermediate-to-long-period Earth-size planets, we present TransitNet, a compact attention-augmented deep-learning framework for low-SNR transit blind searches. To enable realistic method development and objective threshold calibration under blind-search conditions, we develop a unified dataset construction, benchmarking, and threshold-selection framework. On recovery benchmarks constructed from unseen Kepler targets, TransitNet attains 95.2 percent accuracy in the challenging SNR range of 6 to 8 and outperforms both TLS and BLS, achieving ROC-AUC and PR-AP values of 0.974 and 0.982, respectively. In an injected Earth-size and sub-Earth-size transit recovery experiment, TransitNet achieves a recovery rate of 93.0 percent, substantially exceeding those of TLS (63.1 percent) and BLS (60.0 percent). In addition to detection, TransitNet provides attention-based estimates of transit windows and midpoints. On an independent evaluation set, 97.4 percent of injected transits are fully covered by the estimated transit window. Applied to real Kepler observations, the model successfully recovers all 34 selected confirmed Kepler planets, with a mean absolute transit midpoint error of 1.24 hours. The model combines a compact footprint of about 1.5 MB with high inference efficiency, yielding speed-ups of about 12 to 25 times relative to CPU-TLS and about 4 to 5 times relative to CPU-BLS. These results demonstrate that TransitNet provides an accurate, scalable, and computationally efficient framework for low-SNR transit blind searches in the tested regime and motivate its extension to longer-period Earth-size planet searches.\n         \n       \n     \n     \n       [540] \n       \n        arXiv:2606.22402\n       \n          (replaced)\n\n        [ pdf ,  html ,  other ]\n     \n     \n       \n          Title: \n          Reinforcement learning to improve large language model-based automated code compliance systems\n         \n          Jack Wei Lun Shi ,  Minghao Dang ,  Wawan Solihin ,  Justin K.W. Yeoh  \n\n          Comments: \n          22 pages, 12 figures, 1 table\n         \n\n\n          Subjects: \n           Software Engineering (cs.SE) ; Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI); Computation and Language (cs.CL); Machine Learning (cs.LG)\n         \n\n         \n          Large language model (LLM)-based approaches for automated code compliance (ACC) of building regulations are prone to generating incorrect and hallucinated computer-processable rules. This paper introduces P4IR, a two-stage framework that uses supervised fine-tuning (SFT) to instill domain knowledge in an LLM, followed by Group Relative Policy Optimization (GRPO) to improve the accuracy of the generated intermediate representations in the form of high-level code skeletons. The framework achieved final reductions of up to 21.3% and 41.0% in tree edit distance and token-level Levenshtein distance respectively, relative to the SFT baselines. Comparative analysis demonstrates that this approach in a zero-shot setting outperforms leading LLMs in both code structure and semantics, evaluated via few-shot prompting. Additionally, the GRPO stage produced a statistically significant reduction in false positives. By combining SFT with GRPO to optimize directly for domain-specific objectives, this approach offers a path toward more accurate and reliable LLM-based ACC systems.\n         \n       \n     \n     \n       [541] \n       \n        arXiv:2606.23042\n       \n          (replaced)\n\n        [ pdf ,  html ,  other ]\n     \n     \n       \n          Title: \n          The Model as One Rater Among Several: Measuring Political Positions in Data-Sparse Regions with a Language-Model Panel\n         \n          Tarek Gara  \n\n          Comments: \n          14 pages, 2 figures, 7 tables. v2: corrects an import error that had attached 154 of one model's ratings to the wrong parties; every figure recomputed from the corrected v1.0 data release; two-column layout\n         \n\n\n          Subjects: \n           Computers and Society (cs.CY) ; Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI); Computation and Language (cs.CL); Applications (stat.AP)\n         \n\n         \n          Most instruments for measuring political positions, such as manifesto coding, expert surveys and text-scaling models, were built and validated on Western party systems, and they break down worst where many consequential actors never publish a manifesto, never contest a competitive election, or commit one position to a charter and act on another in office. This paper proposes a method for such settings. I treat a large language model as a single fallible rater in a panel, the way an expert survey treats one expert, and describe the panel, its synthesis, an applicability rule that keeps a blank distinct from a zero, and a lens that separates what an actor declares from what it does. Three results follow. First, a built-in experiment isolates the rubric: with a definition-free round held fixed, written axis definitions move scores by a mean of 1.8 points on a 21-point scale and tighten agreement between raters (mean absolute gap 2.81 to 2.50; r 0.81 to 0.89). Second, across nine models from eight laboratories in two countries, Krippendorff's alpha is 0.86 on an interval and 0.87 on an ordinal metric, and it held as the panel grew from five raters to nine. This is reliability and says nothing yet about validity, since models trained on overlapping data can share a bias no statistic computed from the models can detect. Third, the panel's sharpest split, a bimodal divergence on an actor's stance toward the state's foundational order, comes from a referent problem: the models agree on the facts and differ on which order counts as the regime. A blind triple-coding traces about two-thirds of such disagreement to interpretation. The worked example is the Middle East and North Africa, and the rubric, prompt and data are released in full.\n         \n       \n     \n     \n       [542] \n       \n        arXiv:2607.12052\n       \n          (replaced)\n\n        [ pdf ,  html ,  other ]\n     \n     \n       \n          Title: \n          Representation and Reference Selection in Training-Free Synthetic Image Attribution\n         \n          Meiling Li ,  Pietro Bongini ,  Benedetta Tondi ,  Mauro Barni  \n\n          Comments: \n          6 pages, 5 figures, 4 tables. Accepted at the 2026 IEEE International Workshop on Information Forensics and Security (WIFS 2026)\n         \n\n\n          Subjects: \n           Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV) ; Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI); Cryptography and Security (cs.CR)\n         \n\n         \n          Synthetic image attribution aims at identifying the generator responsible for a given AI-generated image. Training-free reference-based attribution methods are easily scalable, since newly emerging generators can be incorporated by adding source-specific references rather than retraining a task-specific classifier. Their performance depends on two coupled factors: the representation space used for comparison and the way source-specific references are constructed. However, the interaction between these two factors remains largely unexplored. In this paper, we provide a controlled analysis of this interaction using references and off-the-shelf pretrained representations. We study representations extracted from different layers of CLIP and DINOv2, along with three reference selection strategies with varying semantic constraints: arbitrary, semantically aligned, and resynthesis-based references. Our results show that attribution accuracy generally peaks at intermediate representation levels, indicating that source-discriminative cues are more accessible before strong semantic abstraction dominates. We further show that intermediate representations are not completely semantically neutral, making reference selection critical: semantically constrained references reduce query-reference mismatch and improve attribution, especially under limited reference budgets. Resynthesis is useful in low-reference regimes, while semantically aligned references provide a better accuracy-cost trade-off when a moderate-sized reference pool is available. Our findings show that training-free reference-based attribution should be understood as the interaction between where images are compared, how the reference set is constructed, and how many references are available. Code and datasets are available at  this https URL .\n         \n       \n     \n     \n       [543] \n       \n        arXiv:2607.12075\n       \n          (replaced)\n\n        [ pdf ,  other ]\n     \n     \n       \n          Title: \n          Calibrated Selective Prediction Using Deep Ensembles for ROI-Based Thyroid Nodule Ultrasound Classification Under Dataset Shift: A Retrospective Evaluation\n         \n          Md. Sadibul Hasan Sadib ,  Md. Mohayminul Mukit ,  Rahmatul Kabir Rasel Sarker ,  Tahmid Alam Tamim ,  Md. Monir Hossain Shimul  \n\n          Comments: \n          Withdrawn by the authors. The methodology and analysis contain substantial errors, and as a result the reported results are incorrect and should not be relied upon. A thoroughly revised version may be submitted in the future\n         \n\n\n          Subjects: \n           Image and Video Processing (eess.IV) ; Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI); Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)\n         \n\n         \n          Background: Deep learning models can classify thyroid nodules on ultrasound, but reliable clinical decision support also requires calibrated probabilities, uncertainty estimation, and selective referral, particularly under dataset shift.\n Methods: We developed a calibrated deterministic five-member deep ensemble for ROI-based thyroid nodule classification and selective image-based triage. TN5000 was used for model development, five-fold cross-validation, member-wise vector-scaling calibration, and fold-specific threshold selection. TN3K served as an independent external dataset-shift evaluation. The framework used ConvNeXt-Tiny with squeeze-and-excitation attention, ensemble-mean malignancy probability, and mutual information (MI) as an ensemble-disagreement score. A three-tier policy assigned images to No-FNA suggestion, FNA recommendation, or radiologist review.\n Results: On pooled out-of-fold TN5000 predictions, the ensemble achieved AUC-ROC 0.9395, AP 0.9715, ECE 0.0088, and Brier score 0.0813. At 50% nominal MI retention, 7.2% of cases received a No-FNA suggestion, 39.9% an FNA recommendation, and 52.9% radiologist review, with 98.3% No-FNA NPV and 99.83% malignancy capture. On TN3K, AUC-ROC decreased to 0.7870, AP to 0.7254, ECE increased to 0.1899, and Brier score to 0.2281. The frozen TN5000 policy assigned 83.7% to review, 1.0% to No-FNA, and 15.3% to FNA recommendation. No malignant image entered the No-FNA pathway, but FNA-recommendation PPV fell to 76.6%.\n Conclusion: The framework showed strong internal discrimination and calibration, but limited external threshold transportability. Selective prediction may help identify images unsuitable for automated triage, but local recalibration, threshold validation, and prospective clinical evaluation are required before deployment.\n         \n       \n     \n     \n       [544] \n       \n        arXiv:2607.16989\n       \n          (replaced)\n\n        [ pdf ,  html ,  other ]\n     \n     \n       \n          Title: \n          Real-World Evaluation of an AI Agent Drafting Translational Impact Summaries\n         \n          Mohammad Arvan ,  Amber E. Osterholt ,  Bailee Rue ,  Yuvaneswaren R. Sureshbabu ,  Krishna R. Patel ,  Rebecca T. Feinstein ,  Bethany C. Bray ,  Niranjan S. Karnik  \n\n          Comments: \n          15 pages, 5 figures, 2 tables. Submitted to the Journal of Clinical and Translational Science. Code:  this https URL \n         \n\n\n          Subjects: \n           Computation and Language (cs.CL) ; Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI); Digital Libraries (cs.DL); Human-Computer Interaction (cs.HC)\n         \n\n         \n          Introduction. Clinical and Translational Science Award (CTSA) programs must document their scholars' research impact, but assembling each scholar's record by hand takes staff an estimated 15 hours and does not scale to a full cohort. An artificial intelligence (AI) agent could serve as a tool to gather scholar data across platforms and disciplines. Methods. We built a human-in-the-loop AI agent that assembles a dossier of sourced evidence for each scholar and drafts one-sentence Translational Science Benefits Model (TSBM) impact summaries for staff review. We evaluated it in the impact-reporting workflow of one CTSA hub across 10 career-development (KL2/K12) scholars. Two evaluation staff independently coded all 507 findings as accept, edit, or reject; the primary measure was the unanimous usable rate, defined as the share both accepted or edited. Results. Both reviewers accepted or edited 81.7% of the agent's findings. Reviewers each spent a median of 14 minutes per scholar, replacing an estimated 15 hours of manual assembly. Inter-rater agreement was moderate (Cohen's kappa 0.43 on the usable-versus-reject decision). A profile discovery study found the agent's recall close to human search. The agent's impact evidence spanned all four TSBM domains, and about a third of the reviewed findings fell in non-scholarly categories that routine processes tend to miss. Reviewers rated synthesis accuracy 4.5 and usefulness 4.8 on a 5-point scale. Conclusions. A human-in-the-loop AI agent can serve as the first-pass author of a scholar's impact record, shifting staff from collecting and writing to reviewing, and making cohort-scale impact reporting feasible.\n         \n       \n     \n     \n       [545] \n       \n        arXiv:2607.19305\n       \n          (replaced)\n\n        [ pdf ,  other ]\n     \n     \n       \n          Title: \n          Riemannian Deep Learning: Modules, Networks, and Geometries\n         \n          Ziheng Chen  \n\n          Comments: \n          PhD thesis manuscript, University of Trento; defense pending\n         \n\n\n          Subjects: \n           Machine Learning (cs.LG) ; Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI); Differential Geometry (math.DG)\n         \n\n         \n          Deep neural networks on manifold-valued representations have attracted growing interest, but many basic components remain tied to specific manifolds, rely on Euclidean approximations, or require costly and numerically fragile geometric operations. This thesis develops a unified framework for Riemannian deep learning from three complementary perspectives: reusable neural modules, manifold-specific network architectures, and the design of underlying geometries. It generalizes batch normalization from Euclidean spaces and individual manifolds to broad classes of Lie groups and gyrogroups, and extends multinomial logistic regression from Euclidean space to SPD manifolds and then to general Riemannian manifolds. It further develops neural networks for several important geometric representations, including an unconstrained model of hyperbolic space, Busemann-based hyperbolic learning, and full-rank correlation matrices. Finally, it introduces adaptive and computationally efficient Riemannian metrics on SPD manifolds, including learnable Log-Euclidean geometries and fast, stable Cholesky-based geometries. The proposed methods are supported by theoretical analysis and validated through numerical experiments and applications in vision, signal processing, graph learning, and genomics.\n         \n       \n     \n     \n       [546] \n       \n        arXiv:2607.24991\n       \n          (replaced)\n\n        [ pdf ,  html ,  other ]\n     \n     \n       \n          Title: \n          GUEST: Preliminary Guidelines for Using and Evaluating GenAI Tools to Support Systematic Literature Reviews\n         \n          Barbara Kitchenham ,  Sebastián Pizard ,  Lech Madeyski ,  Ronnie de Souza Santos ,  Martin Shepperd ,  David Budgen  \n\n          Comments: \n          v2: revised version. 49 pages, 2 figures, 15 tables\n         \n\n\n          Subjects: \n           Software Engineering (cs.SE) ; Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)\n         \n\n         \n          Context: Generative AI (GenAI) and Large Language Models (LLMs) are increasingly used for academic tasks in software engineering and beyond, including systematic literature reviews (SLRs). However, whilst their capabilities are rapidly developing, there is no guarantee they can meet the rigour, reliability, and transparency that SLRs require. Objectives: To support researchers intending to conduct SLRs using GenAI, or those conducting empirical studies to evaluate how well GenAI supports SLR tasks. Methods: First, we conducted a rapid review to identify studies that propose guidelines for evaluating and using GenAI and LLMs to support SLRs. Second, we drew on thought experiments, relevant guidance from the literature, and our own experience from conducting SLRs and evaluating tools, to develop recommendations for how to use and assess GenAI in the context of SLRs. Results: We classify methodological issues for the roles of evaluation and reviewing into four categories spanning SLR practice adjustments, general secondary-study standards, GenAI evaluation practices and core empirical rigour. Building on this, we introduce GUEST (GenAI Use and Evaluation in SLR Tasks) which is a comprehensive framework of 51 recommendations designed to guide researchers through planning, executing and transparently reporting GenAI-assisted literature reviews and validation studies. Conclusion: Current empirical evidence indicates that GenAI cannot be relied upon to perform unsupervised systematic reviews so requires human oversight. Nevertheless, it can offer cost-effective assistance for repetitive screening tasks and secondary validation. By standardizing how these tools are deployed and evaluated, the GUEST framework provides a necessary methodological baseline for trustworthy GenAI-assisted research in software engineering.\n         \n       \n     \n     \n       [547] \n       \n        arXiv:2607.25130\n       \n          (replaced)\n\n        [ pdf ,  html ,  other ]\n     \n     \n       \n          Title: \n          How Do Developers Edit AI-Generated Code, and How Well Do LLMs Infer Their Edits? Insights from 53.6K Developer Edits\n         \n          Jenny T. Liang ,  Mihika Bairathi ,  Wayne Chi ,  Ameet Talwalkar ,  Nishant Subramani ,  Valerie Chen  \n\n\n\n          Subjects: \n           Software Engineering (cs.SE) ; Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI); Human-Computer Interaction (cs.HC); Machine Learning (cs.LG)\n         \n\n         \n          Imperfections in AI-generated code require that software developers modify generations through manual code edits. However, little is known about how developers edit AI-generated code, and to what extent LLMs can infer implicit developer intent from this data, given the lack of high-quality, realistic code editing data. This information is essential for designing and building usable AI-developer interactions. To address this gap, we introduce DECODE (Developer Edits of Code Dataset), a dataset of 53.6K real-world in-IDE code edits of AI-generated code sourced from 1K+ developers. First, we use DECODE to analyze when, why, and how AI-generated code is edited. Developers invest little effort in editing code and abandon AI-generated code quickly, thereby motivating the need for LLMs to understand developer intents through their edits. Therefore, we next use DECODE to understand to what extent LLMs could infer developer edits. Predicting code edits is a challenging task for both frontier and fine-tuned models. Frontier models generate edits that best reflect developer intents, but are prone to over-editing and reverting developer-written edits. Fine-tuning LLMs can improve their ability to predict edits that are more similar to developers' as well as better reflect developer intent, but do not fully bridge the gap to frontier models. Together, our results demonstrate the unique insights on developer and LLM behavior that can be obtained through studying realistic code edit data to promote more better developer-AI interactions.\n         \n       \n     \n     \n       [548] \n       \n        arXiv:2608.02673\n       \n          (replaced)\n\n        [ pdf ,  html ,  other ]\n     \n     \n       \n          Title: \n          dots.tts.edit: Precisely Controlled Speech Editing with a Continuous Autoregressive Model\n         \n          Hankun Wang ,  Bohan Li ,  Shi Lian ,  Xiaoyu Gu ,  Jing Peng ,  Da Zheng ,  Yiwei Guo ,  Colin Zhang ,  Shuai Wang ,  Kai Yu  \n\n\n\n          Subjects: \n           Sound (cs.SD) ; Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI); Computation and Language (cs.CL); Audio and Speech Processing (eess.AS)\n         \n\n         \n          Speech editing for content creation requires precise control over both what an edit should do and where it should apply. Free-form natural language provides a flexible interface for expressing edit requests, but its ambiguity may leave the intended operation, parameters, or target region underspecified. We study a semi-formal transcript-grounded edit instruction that specifies operation categories and parameters and binds them to transcript spans or boundaries. This semantic timeline avoids explicit timestamp alignment and provides an externally inspectable contract for compositional edits. We instantiate the interface in dots$.$tts$.$edit, a speech editing model built on a continuous autoregressive speech generation architecture. Four representative speech-creation controls cover lexical content, affective expression, pitch and speaking-rate delivery, and temporal phrasing through text, emotion, prosody, and pause editing. Task-specific data pipelines construct operation- and scope-controlled pairs while retaining source-derived context outside each target region. We further introduce doteBench, a bilingual benchmark that measures precise instruction following, local preservation, and audio quality across the four controls and their composition. Experiments show leading overall instruction following and local preservation across its five editing categories, while audio quality remains comparable to existing open-source systems. Across three Seed-TTS-Eval shards, the model shows negligible differences from the base model in zero-shot TTS recognition error rate and speaker similarity.\n         \n       \n     \n     \n       [549] \n       \n        arXiv:2608.02829\n       \n          (replaced)\n\n        [ pdf ,  html ,  other ]\n     \n     \n       \n          Title: \n          Wiring Beats Blending: Structure-Aware Compensation for Transformer Downscaling\n         \n          Ravi Satya Durga Prasad Yenugula  \n\n          Comments: \n          v5: second architecture family (Qwen3-1.7B-Base -> 0.6B-Base) with the 2x2 selection-statistic result reversing across families; headline pair at six seeds; plain-selection baseline at the 6.9B->1.4B scale pair; CKA calibration; Proposition 1; positioning vs GRAIL/SliceGPT; descriptive arm names; code and checkpoints at the linked repositories. 25 pages, 5 figures, 22 tables\n         \n\n\n          Subjects: \n           Machine Learning (cs.LG) ; Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI); Computation and Language (cs.CL)\n         \n\n         \n          Model families are trained size by size. Can a pretrained large model instead be converted into a smaller sibling? We characterize the 1.4B->410M conversion in Pythia end to end. Representations align strongly across sizes (ridge R^2=0.84); parameters align weakly. Dense weight projection is destructive; a bit-exact control places the fault in basis mixing, which breaks rotary, per-head, GELU, and LayerNorm structure. Residuals after the best-fit linear operator carry no learnable or transferable signal under shuffle controls, so conversion value lives in initialization. Matched-budget continued pre-training separates two independent levers, least-squares compensation (function lever, best zero-shot) and variance-preserving rescale (dynamics lever, best endpoints), with a placement rule read off the architecture: compensation is well-posed exactly where no normalization sits between cut and read; norm-fronted paths take rescale. At 30M tokens compensation beats the best subcloning variant on a width-reduced pair (84.6+-2.1 vs. 90.7+-3.8, 6/6 seeds) and a held-out depth-reduced pair (109.3 vs. 117.9, 3/3 seeds); the two reach parity at 33x the budget, and at ~5x the donor scale the levers anti-synergize. A second family, Qwen3-1.7B->0.6B (RMSNorm, SwiGLU, grouped-query attention), takes the placement rule unchanged but reverses a 2x2 crossing the re-fit with the activation statistic it reads: on Pythia the re-fit carries the gain and the statistic adds 2.3 perplexity; on Qwen3, with far more heterogeneous MLP unit energies, the statistic carries 66 points and the re-fit is a null on top. Informed selection + compensation + rescale is the best arm in both families, 19x and 23x lower perplexity than from scratch at matched budget (2.94 and 3.13 nats), and beats structured pruning plus distillation. Code, checkpoints, and the frozen eval corpus are released.\n         \n       \n     \n     \n       [550] \n       \n        arXiv:2608.04565\n       \n          (replaced)\n\n        [ pdf ,  html ,  other ]\n     \n     \n       \n          Title: \n          Breadcrumbing Search Agents: Per-Turn Scheming Over Long-Horizon Trajectories\n         \n          Xuebin Li ,  Hanqing Zhao ,  Siyuan Liang ,  Kejiang Chen ,  Weiming Zhang ,  Dacheng Tao ,  Nenghai Yu  \n\n          Comments: \n          39 pages, 7 figures\n         \n\n\n          Subjects: \n           Cryptography and Security (cs.CR) ; Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI); Computation and Language (cs.CL)\n         \n\n         \n          LLM-based search agents are widely used for information-seeking tasks, but their reliance on external tool returns introduces a critical security risk: web content retrieved during execution is untrusted, exposing agents to prompt injection and goal hijacking. Prior work on search-agent safety primarily focuses on static web-content injection, but modern agents issue follow-up queries and cross-check competing sources, so a single injected page is often diluted or rejected. We show that the channel delivering search and page observations is a fragile security boundary: beyond exposing the agent to a single poisoned page, a mediated search interface can repeatedly steer how the agent gathers evidence and forms its final answer. Under a constrained tool-intermediary threat model, appending only one controlled result per query can substantially increase attack success when the evidence is coordinated across the agent's trajectory. We study this setting with a strategy-driven long-horizon attack system and introduce Authority-Chain Hijack (ACH), an expert-refined strategy that turns isolated search-result and page-content manipulations into a coherent evidence chain across seemingly corroborating sources. ACH achieves the highest Overall ASR among all baselines, reaching 55.9% / 83.3% ASR / MaxN ASR on the full SafeSearch test split. We further introduce Trace-Guided Strategy Evolution (TGSE), which automatically improves attacker strategies from execution traces, replacing manual redesign with trace-driven refinement; its strongest single setting reaches 71.4% / 95.0% in held-out evaluation. Code is available at  this https URL .\n         \n       \n     \n     \n       [551] \n       \n        arXiv:2608.07520\n       \n          (replaced)\n\n        [ pdf ,  html ,  other ]\n     \n     \n       \n          Title: \n          KumbhDoot: A Scale-Ready, LLM-Bounded Architecture for Mass-Gathering Public-Service Assistants\n         \n          Saurabh Sakalkar ,  Abhishek Singh ,  Ramesh Raskar  \n\n\n\n          Subjects: \n           Computers and Society (cs.CY) ; Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)\n         \n\n         \n          Mass religious gatherings such as the Kumbh Mela concentrate tens of millions of people into a single region over a few weeks, producing intense, repetitive, multilingual, and safety-critical demand for information. The default response, a conversational assistant that routes every query to a large language model (LLM), is poorly matched to this setting: it is costly at scale, slow on emergency paths, prone to hallucination on facts that can cause physical harm, and unusable when connectivity fails. We describe KumbhDoot, an agentic pilgrim assistant for the Nashik Simhastha Kumbh Mela built on a different principle. It operates on a foundational design principle that prioritizes semantic similarity over starting with an LLM. Generative models are invoked only in instances where similarity-based retrieval is insufficient to produce a correct answer. The system utilizes a \"semantic cache\": an embedding-indexed store as a single retrieval primitive, which handles intent routing, answer caching, offline lookups, and multi-agent retrieval. A custom three-tier agent architecture operates directly on this store, ensuring decision paths remain inspectable and avoiding the use of generic multi-agent frameworks that would trigger implicit per-step LLM calls. We present the architecture, an analytical cost model for its per-query economics, and an honest account of where similarity is sufficient and where generative reasoning remains necessary. We argue that for bounded, high-stakes, low-connectivity public-service domains, a similarity-first and LLM-bounded design is not merely cheaper but architecturally more appropriate than an LLM-default one.\n         \n       \n     \n     \n       [552] \n       \n        arXiv:2608.10319\n       \n          (replaced)\n\n        [ pdf ,  html ,  other ]\n     \n     \n       \n          Title: \n          Do Personalized Skills Help Coding Agents? An Empirical Study of Developer Interaction Traces\n         \n          Shuyan Huang ,  Kai Du ,  Andrew Lan  \n\n          Comments: \n          18 pages, 10 figures\n         \n\n\n          Subjects: \n           Software Engineering (cs.SE) ; Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)\n         \n\n         \n          As human developers increasingly collaborate with large language model (LLM)-powered coding agents, adapting to individual developer preferences, potentially via agent skills, may offer way to reduce repeated guidance and improve collaboration efficiency. However, existing approaches primarily focus on building skills for each task rather for each human developer. In this work, we propose a framework that combines rule-based bootstrapping with LLM-based refinement to distill developer-specific skills from their interactions with coding agents. We further develop an interactive replay framework with an LLM-based developer simulator for evaluation. We conduct experiments on a collection of replayable real-world developer-agent interaction sessions. We find that overall, task-specific skills are the most effective; However, personalized skills become more effective when developer preferences are manifested with enough frequency, especially when developers work on tasks that are highly related. These findings provide empirical insights suggest that i) broadly transferable procedural knowledge can be more robust than developer-specific preference signals and ii) effective personalization requires mining from rich user preference signals.\n         \n       \n     \n     \n       [553] \n       \n        arXiv:2608.17843\n       \n          (replaced)\n\n        [ pdf ,  html ,  other ]\n     \n     \n       \n          Title: \n          Decodable but Not Actionable: Auditing the Representation-Behavior Gap in Frozen LLMs for Geometric Constraints\n         \n          Man Liang ,  Xinzhao Cheng ,  Faizan Wajid  \n\n          Comments: \n          Revised manuscript with expanded experiments, analyses, and discussion\n         \n\n\n          Subjects: \n           Computation and Language (cs.CL) ; Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)\n         \n\n         \n          Large language models (LLMs) perform well on structured reasoning tasks, but what they encode and whether encoded information guides their behavior remain unclear. We introduce a controlled auditing framework that uses parametric CAD to distinguish failures of encoding, expression, and activation-level control. Applied to eight pretrained models and matched randomly initialized controls, our framework reveals that pretraining contributes substantially more to linear decoding of local relations than to global constraint status, which is already highly decodable from random representations. Yet strong local decodability does not translate into the models' zero-shot predictions, which remain far below supervised probe performance. Restoring clean internal activations can repair probe predictions, whereas simple activation steering does not provide reliable targeted control. With only 400 labeled relation pairs, low-rank fine-tuning substantially improves generation on three representative models, showing that the representation--behavior gap can be reduced through adaptation. Together, these findings show why decodability alone is insufficient evidence that encoded information will be expressed or controllable in model behavior.\n         \n       \n     \n     \n       [554] \n       \n        arXiv:2608.20084\n       \n          (replaced)\n\n        [ pdf ,  html ,  other ]\n     \n     \n       \n          Title: \n          Evidence-Gated Task and Motion Planning with Vision-Language Models\n         \n          Tsunehiko Tanaka ,  Matthew Stephenson ,  Alistair Macvicar ,  Edgar Simo-Serra  \n\n\n\n          Subjects: \n           Robotics (cs.RO) ; Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)\n         \n\n         \n          Robots executing long-horizon manipulation tasks from natural-language instructions must reason about both semantic task structure and geometric feasibility. Hierarchical approaches combining Vision-Language Models (VLMs) with Task and Motion Planning (TAMP) offer a promising solution, but VLM-generated subgoals may rely on prior knowledge without sufficient observational support. This can lead to the omission of task-relevant subgoals or attempts to execute instructions whose requirements cannot be met in the environment. We propose Evidence Acquisition and Feasibility Gating (EAFG), a framework for evidence-gated task and motion planning that acquires visual evidence to specify appropriate task plans and assess whether given instructions can be fulfilled. EAFG first acquires evidence by having a VLM generate subgoals and a robot execute action sequences planned by TAMP for these subgoals. For feasibility gating, the acquired evidence is used to decide whether to proceed with task planning, acquire further evidence, or halt. When proceeding, the VLM generates task subgoals conditioned on the acquired evidence, and TAMP computes low-level actions. We evaluate EAFG in three cooking scenarios involving explicitly specified required objects, underspecified instructions, and instructions requiring an absent object. EAFG improves task completion over a VLM-TAMP baseline in the explicit and underspecified scenarios and enables appropriate halting when a required object is absent.\n         \n       \n     \n     \n       [555] \n       \n        arXiv:2608.23616\n       \n          (replaced)\n\n        [ pdf ,  html ,  other ]\n     \n     \n       \n          Title: \n          Rebuild Dossier: Mechanically-Enforced Specs for Agentic App Rebuilds, and What Model-Tier Failures Reveal\n         \n          Parker Fawcett  \n\n          Comments: \n          48 pages, 1 figure. Code and evaluation artifacts:  this https URL  (archived at DOI:  https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.22036801 )\n         \n\n\n          Subjects: \n           Software Engineering (cs.SE) ; Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)\n         \n\n         \n          An AI agent's rebuild is only as good as the process that produced it. Prior work found that once a model is strong enough, a multi-agent rebuild pipeline loses to the simplest approach: giving the model the original code and one instruction (AgentModernize). We present rebuild-dossier, an open-source tool that locks an application's real interface - its exact inputs and outputs - before any code is written, then enforces one-test-at-a-time building through automated checks, not written instructions alone.\n Three results shape this evaluation, with differing amounts of evidence. First, in a small comparison, the compliant agent failed a held-back test while the rule-breaking agent passed everything - proof that a passing suite doesn't certify correctness when tests can be gamed. Second, we tested whether this beats simply giving the weaker model the source and one instruction: tied on a small app, but lost outright on a larger one where the automated check wasn't even running - pointing to the check mechanism, not interface-locking, which held up separately. Third, every claim here is checked at three levels - the agent's own report, an automated log, and the actual files produced - catching real errors, including a bug in our own logging code, that a single level would have missed.\n These risks reproduce on a different model and toolchain: a stronger model followed our process three times running, something the weaker model never managed. The tool is public, MIT licensed, and reproduces end to end against our own applications.\n         \n       \n     \n     \n       [556] \n       \n        arXiv:2608.23927\n       \n          (replaced)\n\n        [ pdf ,  html ,  other ]\n     \n     \n       \n          Title: \n          GlanceWAM: Sparse Test-Time Imagination for World-Action Models\n         \n          Linhan Wang ,  Zijian An ,  Mingyuan Zhang ,  Chen Dai ,  Yi Xu ,  Can Cui ,  Jiayan Wang ,  Zichong Yang ,  Yinlin Chen ,  Lifeng Zhou ,  Chang-Tien Lu  \n\n\n\n          Subjects: \n           Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV) ; Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI); Robotics (cs.RO)\n         \n\n         \n          Video generative models provide rich physical priors for robot learning, yet existing world-action models (WAMs) face a fundamental trade-off: synchronous video generation at control rate is latency-prohibitive, while abandoning test-time visual imagination sacrifices task success. We show that visual imagination achieves both real-time inference and superior success rates when generated asynchronously off the critical path and consumed directly in latent space. We introduce GlanceWAM, which decouples imagination from control on a single shared video DiT backbone: an asynchronous proposer glances ahead on a slow clock to imagine a single lookahead frame seconds into the future in the background, while an action head decodes action chunks at control rate (48 ms) purely in latent space without blocking. Enabled by a non-interfering attention mask that isolates video representations and staleness-robust horizon training that accommodates asynchronous lookahead aging, GlanceWAM breaks the speed-success dilemma. Trained purely on demonstrations, it attains 72.2% on the 24-task RoboCasa kitchen benchmark (vs. 67.1% for synchronous Cosmos Policy) and 99.0% on LIBERO while cutting per-chunk control latency 24x relative to synchronous world-action models (48 ms on one A100). In single-arm and bimanual real-robot manipulation, it achieves higher average success than $\\pi_{0.5}$ without any robot-data pretraining. Code is available at  this https URL  and real-robot videos at  this https URL .\n         \n       \n     \n     \n       [557] \n       \n        arXiv:2609.01202\n       \n          (replaced)\n\n        [ pdf ,  html ,  other ]\n     \n     \n       \n          Title: \n          Towards AI-Assisted Clinical Trial Matching: Practical Considerations, Multicenter Evaluation, and Real-World Deployment\n         \n          Yin Fang ,  Qiao Jin ,  Shubo Tian ,  Lauren He ,  Maya Geer ,  Noor Naffakh ,  Ryan Huu-Tuan Nguyen ,  Zifeng Wang ,  Jimeng Sun ,  Charalampos S. Floudas ,  James L. Gulley ,  Kamilia Moalem ,  Catarina Martins Maia ,  Amanda Nottke ,  Juan W. Valle ,  Melinda Bachini ,  Lourdes Rocha-Nussbaum ,  Kari Ramage ,  Nikita Curry ,  Megan Barnes ,  Mandy Mansaray ,  Darlene Gabeau ,  Craig E. Grossman ,  Heath Skinner ,  Michael Burczynski ,  NIH-TrialBench Consortium ,  Zhiyong Lu  \n\n          Comments: \n          43 pages, 12 figures\n         \n\n\n          Subjects: \n           Computation and Language (cs.CL) ; Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI); Computers and Society (cs.CY)\n         \n\n         \n          Clinical trials are essential for evidence-based medicine and drug development, but many fail because of insufficient patient enrollment. Although there is growing interest in using AI to support patient recruitment, existing systems largely perform eligibility assessment alone and have rarely been evaluated in real-world workflows. Here we present TrialGPT 2.0, an AI-assisted clinical trial recommendation system improved for responsible real-world deployment. Rather than asking only whether a patient may qualify, the system assesses which trials warrant further consideration given the patient's current clinical needs and local workflow priorities, and provides structured, inspectable explanations for expert review. Importantly, we evaluated TrialGPT 2.0 retrospectively and prospectively across government research, patient-advocacy, academic medical center, and community-based radiation-oncology settings. In retrospective multicenter cohorts comprising 288 cases, TrialGPT 2.0 retrieved at least one clinician-recommended trial in its top 10 recommendations for approximately 91% of cases while reducing clinician screening time by 55.0%. In a six-month prospective evaluation embedded in an active precision oncology tumor board, TrialGPT 2.0 contributed additional trial opportunities missed by the routine workflow, increasing the number of patient cases with at least one final trial recommendation by 90.9%. To support scientific reproducibility, we also developed NIH-TrialBench, a clinician-authored benchmark comprising 126 diverse synthetic patient vignettes and matching scenarios from 11 NIH Institutes and Centers. Together, these findings support the value of AI to assist clinical trial matching by improving clinician efficiency and identifying frequently overlooked trial opportunities, ultimately helping to expand and accelerate accrual to cancer trials.\n         \n       \n     \n     \n       [558] \n       \n        arXiv:2609.01896\n       \n          (replaced)\n\n        [ pdf ,  html ,  other ]\n     \n     \n       \n          Title: \n          OutageDiT: A Foundation Model for Power Outage Forecasting and Scenario Generation\n         \n          Yunqin Zhu ,  Feng Qiu ,  Yao Xie  \n\n          Comments: \n          11 pages, 3 figures, 3 tables. Accepted at the NeurIPS 2026 Second Workshop on MLxOR\n         \n\n\n          Subjects: \n           Machine Learning (cs.LG) ; Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)\n         \n\n         \n          Power-outage planning requires scenarios before an event occurs. These scenarios must represent uncertainty in magnitude, timing, and duration while preserving temporal dependence. However, severe events are rare, and data from any single region contain few examples of extreme outage and restoration patterns. To address this challenge, we introduce OutageDiT, a foundation model for generating seven-day outage trajectories at 15-minute resolution, trained on outage and weather records across the United States. Specifically, a condition encoder processes historical context and known future covariates once per forecast, and a shallow flow-matching decoder reuses the resulting horizon-aligned states to sample complete outage trajectories. This enables point forecasting, uncertainty quantification, and conditional scenario generation within one deep generative model. Across outage forecasting benchmarks, OutageDiT improves forecast accuracy and scenario quality over strong baselines and supports zero-shot transfer to held-out regions. Together, these results position conditional outage scenario generation as a bridge from outage forecasting to operational planning under uncertainty.\n         \n       \n     \n     \n       [559] \n       \n        arXiv:2609.02959\n       \n          (replaced)\n\n        [ pdf ,  html ,  other ]\n     \n     \n       \n          Title: \n          The Direction of Ignorance: LLMs Encode and Temper a Unigram Prior\n         \n          Toni J.B. Liu ,  Jiajun Bao ,  Gurbir Arora ,  Mitchell Ostrow ,  Yizhou Liu ,  Raphaël Sarfati ,  Nicolas Boullé ,  Christopher J. Earls  \n\n\n\n          Subjects: \n           Machine Learning (cs.LG) ; Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI); Computation and Language (cs.CL); Machine Learning (stat.ML)\n         \n\n         \n          What does a language model predict when it has few clues?The answer lies in its unembedding geometry: a single direction of the unembedding matrix encodes the unigram distribution of the training corpus, which serves as the Bayesian prior the model falls back on when uncertain. This structure, termed the direction of ignorance, appears across all model families examined (Llama, Qwen, Gemma, and Pythia), ranging from 0.4B to 405B parameters. Projecting the final prediction state onto this direction yields a per-token prior loading factor $\\lambda$, which declines steadily as the context becomes more informative. Formally, the same projection decomposes the prediction state into two orthogonal vectors interpretable as the two components of a tempered Bayesian update: a unigram prior raised to the exponent $\\lambda$ and a context-driven likelihood. This geometric probabilistic connection makes $\\lambda$ meaningfully comparable across model sizes and families. Larger models generally exhibit lower prior reliance, and reasoning models exhibit lower reliance than their base counterparts. Finally, we show that $\\lambda$ serves as a causal, inference-time control: reducing it can promote context-driven continuations and improve Llama-3.2-3B-Instruct on GSM8K, whereas amplifying it or applying matched perturbations along random directions degrades performance.\n         \n       \n     \n     \n       [560] \n       \n        arXiv:2609.05880\n       \n          (replaced)\n\n        [ pdf ,  html ,  other ]\n     \n     \n       \n          Title: \n          Grounded and Faithful P&ID Reasoning: Constraining Vision-Language Models with Recovered Evidence Graphs\n         \n          Prathamesh Gadekar ,  Sagar Srinivas Sakhinana ,  Venkataramana Runkana  \n\n          Comments: \n          Accepted to the NeurIPS 2026 VLM4RWD Workshop. Changes in v2: (1) added answer-contract ablation results (Table 2), as suggested by reviewers; (2) added fine-tuned VLM results; (3) added the harness system prompts (Appendix A.9) and appendix sections describing the answer-contract ablation (A.10) and the fine-tuning experiments (A.11)\n         \n\n\n          Subjects: \n           Machine Learning (cs.LG) ; Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)\n         \n\n         \n          Piping and Instrumentation Diagrams (P&IDs) are the authoritative maps of process plants: isolation, maintenance, and HAZOP decisions depend on what connects to what. Vision-language models describe these sheets fluently, yet they often invent or miss process connections---and an invented or missed link can reverse an isolation or reachability call, so a plant decision cannot trust a fluent answer that was never checked against the linework. We instead recover an explicit graph of the drawing---its symbols, the process connections between them, and the tags that name them---and then require the model to answer only by querying that graph through seven read-only operators, so a topology claim is returned only when it cites the query results that support it. On TopoPID-VQA, a new suite of 3000 topology questions over these sheets, Graph-Grounded Harness (Ours) raises exact match accuracy from 36.7--41.3% under image-only prompting to 74.3--76.0% for Qwen3-VL-4B, Qwen3-VL-8B, and Gemma-4-E4B. It does so on an imperfect substrate: on Digitize-PID dataset the recovered graph scores F1 0.742 on exact process connections, and 0.801 once symbols and tags are pooled in. The residual errors track that gap---grounding pays off where the recovered graph is right, and perception error still breaks topology questions where it is not.\n         \n       \n     \n     \n       [561] \n       \n        arXiv:2609.07598\n       \n          (replaced)\n\n        [ pdf ,  html ,  other ]\n     \n     \n       \n          Title: \n          Mapping the Emerging Social Science of Large Language Models\n         \n          Zhanzhan Zhao ,  Yi Yang ,  Xiao Jia ,  Zeyun Dong ,  Chenzhang Wang  \n\n\n\n          Subjects: \n           Computers and Society (cs.CY) ; Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI); Computation and Language (cs.CL)\n         \n\n         \n          As large language models (LLMs) blur the boundary between cognition and computation, they reshape knowledge production and collective life. We combine a curated review of 198 papers with computational mapping of 47,719 formally published papers from five databases. Text embeddings, clustering, and topic modeling organize the curated literature into 13 subcategories across three domains. LLM as Social Minds examines mind-like reasoning and socially grounded behavior in individual models. LLM Societies explores how interactions among agents produce collective behavior and social organization. LLM-Human Interactions examines how people perceive and engage with LLMs, and how interaction reshapes human thinking, creativity, and relationships. Structural topic modeling of the larger corpus traces how research attention shifts over time and differs across publication venues. We present a reproducible framework that identifies the field's underlying structure and enables its continuous refinement as new studies emerge. The review also identifies a gap in current understanding: the mechanisms underlying many of LLMs' observed cognitive and social behaviors remain unclear. Human-like performance can then be mistaken for understanding, obscuring the norms, biases, and structures of power that LLMs reproduce. We call for shared benchmarks and robustness checks across the three domains to build a reliable evidence base for understanding LLMs' social behaviors and informing their design and use.\n         \n       \n     \n     \n       [562] \n       \n        arXiv:2609.07655\n       \n          (replaced)\n\n        [ pdf ,  html ,  other ]\n     \n     \n       \n          Title: \n          Online Surrogate Repair: Decoupling High-Fidelity Feedback from Search Length in Closed-Loop Discovery\n         \n          Xiaotang Feng ,  Philip Torr ,  Bruno Andreis  \n\n\n\n          Subjects: \n           Machine Learning (cs.LG) ; Materials Science (cond-mat.mtrl-sci); Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)\n         \n\n         \n          Closed-loop AI scientists can generate candidate designs at low marginal computational cost, whereas reliable feedback may require wet-lab synthesis, characterization, or high-fidelity computation. Addressing this imbalance through custom laboratory automation remains infrastructure-intensive and costly, while replacing new experiments with a fixed surrogate leaves persistent model errors that can be amplified by optimization. We propose \\emph{online surrogate repair} (OSR), a closed-loop algorithm that uses sparse high-fidelity evaluations to update the surrogate throughout a longer agent search conducted primarily with inexpensive surrogate feedback. An acquisition rule selects which designs from the agent's accumulated proposals receive high-fidelity evaluation, and the resulting labels update the surrogate used in subsequent episodes. Across controlled synthetic environments, we demonstrate that improving global surrogate fit does not necessarily reduce maximum regret, whereas Q90-UCB and expected improvement (EI) substantially reduce regret by directing evaluations toward regions that determine the optimizer's decisions. \\osradded{Across LLM and Chemeleon workflows, Online EI reduces the amount of oracle queries required by 84.3--90.3\\% for matching discovery counts and by 77.9--81.6\\% for matching the quality of the best material found.} Online surrogate repair introduces a third feedback regime between fixed-surrogate operation and high-fidelity feedback after every episode, separating the frequency of high-fidelity evaluation from the duration of the agent's search.\n         \n       \n     \n     \n       [563] \n       \n        arXiv:2609.15883\n       \n          (replaced)\n\n        [ pdf ,  html ,  other ]\n     \n     \n       \n          Title: \n          Learning Multimodal One-step Flow Policy via Value-weighted Optimal Transport\n         \n          Jaehun Shon ,  Jinha Choi ,  Jongwook Jeon ,  Jongmin Lee  \n\n          Comments: \n          Accepted at NeurIPS 2026. 38 pages\n         \n\n\n          Subjects: \n           Machine Learning (cs.LG) ; Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)\n         \n\n         \n          Offline reinforcement learning aims to learn a policy solely from fixed datasets, which often contain multimodal action distributions. Flow policies can naturally represent such multimodal behaviors, but learning an efficient one-step flow policy remains challenging: standard value guidance often leads to mode collapse or exploits overestimation bias in out-of-distribution regions. To address this, we introduce One-step Flow policy via Optimal Transport (OptiFlow), a framework for one-step flow policy learning as a structured sample-allocation problem. OptiFlow jointly trains a value-aware reference flow policy and an efficient one-step policy, coupling their action samples through state-wise entropic optimal transport. For each state, critic-estimated values define the priority of distillation target actions, while the action-distance cost ensures geometrically compatible pairings. By avoiding direct critic maximization, our transport-guided approach enables in-distribution exploitation by anchoring the one-step policy to high-value, dataset-supported modes without the risk of out-of-distribution divergence. Experimental results demonstrate that OptiFlow effectively captures optimal multimodal behaviors and achieves strong performance across diverse offline RL benchmarks. Our code is available at  this https URL .\n         \n       \n     \n     \n       [564] \n       \n        arXiv:2609.17474\n       \n          (replaced)\n\n        [ pdf ,  html ,  other ]\n     \n     \n       \n          Title: \n          Iterative Calibration and Learning: Mitigating Teacher Bias in LLM Distillation without Target-Domain Reward Feedback\n         \n          Haichen Hu ,  Yuheng Zhang ,  David Simchi-Levi  \n\n\n\n          Subjects: \n           Machine Learning (cs.LG) ; Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI); Statistics Theory (math.ST); Machine Learning (stat.ML)\n         \n\n         \n          Large language model (LLM) distillation can propagate teacher bias under covariate shift when target reward feedback is unavailable. We propose Iterative Calibration and Learning (IterCaL), which alternates source-reward teacher calibration through token-level branching with student learning on target prompts. We prove polynomial convergence in expected average KL to the oracle student, the reference-regularized target-reward maximizer within a potentially misspecified student class; a quantified source-to-target calibration discrepancy gives an explicit additive true-return term. Our separation shows that regularized direct teacher matching retains positive oracle-KL error at its population optimum for every shared reference penalty, even when the teacher is stronger than every student. On the same instance, IterCaL converges to the oracle using source rewards alone.\n         \n       \n     \n     \n       [565] \n       \n        arXiv:2609.17837\n       \n          (replaced)\n\n        [ pdf ,  html ,  other ]\n     \n     \n       \n          Title: \n          Adaptive hybrid coupling with operator inference, the overlapping Schwarz alternating method and reinforcement learning\n         \n          Trishit Mondal ,  Irina Tezaur ,  Anthony Gruber  \n\n\n\n          Subjects: \n           Machine Learning (cs.LG) ; Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI); Mathematical Physics (math-ph)\n         \n\n         \n          Hybrid domain decomposition methods provide a flexible framework for coupling full order models (FOMs) and reduced order models (ROMs), but typically assume the model assigned to each subdomain is fixed throughout a simulation. This is limiting for transient problems in which localized features propagate through the domain and the regions requiring high-fidelity resolution change over time. We introduce a reinforcement learning (RL)-based approach for online adaptation of FOM-ROM models coupled via the overlapping Schwarz alternating method (O-SAM), an iterative domain decomposition method that solves subdomain-local problems while exchanging solution information through transmission boundary conditions on overlapping interfaces. Deep Q-networks (DQNs) are trained offline to select among subdomain-local FOMs and pre-trained Operator Inference (OpInf) ROMs using a reward balancing accuracy, cost, and model-switching frequency. Once trained, the policies are deployed predictively on problem instances not seen during training, without requiring a reference FOM solution. We demonstrate the approach on two examples: a 1D advection-diffusion problem with a moving front, and a 3D linear elastic wave propagation problem implemented in the  this http URL  solid mechanics code. For the advection-diffusion benchmark, the learned policy dynamically allocates high-fidelity resolution as the front propagates and outperforms static FOM/ROM assignments; letting the agent also adapt the domain decomposition provides no further benefit. For the elastic wave benchmark, learned policies for two and three subdomain decompositions track the propagating wave by assigning FOMs to subdomains containing the wave and ROMs elsewhere, as expected. Our results demonstrate the potential of RL to enable predictive online adaptation of model fidelity within Schwarz-based hybrid simulations.\n         \n       \n     \n     \n       [566] \n       \n        arXiv:2609.17989\n       \n          (replaced)\n\n        [ pdf ,  html ,  other ]\n     \n     \n       \n          Title: \n          Whom Do AI Agents Serve? Role-Induced Sponsorship Bias in Decision Models and Generative LLMs\n         \n          Davood Wadi ,  Yu Ma  \n\n\n\n          Subjects: \n           General Economics (econ.GN) ; Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)\n         \n\n         \n          Consumers increasingly delegate product search to AI shopping agents powered either by generative large language models (LLMs) that deliberate in natural language or by non-generative decision models that output calibrated choice probabilities in a single forward pass. Because retail platforms monetize through sponsored placements that shoppers typically discount, we examine whether an AI agent's evaluation of paid listings depends on who delegates the agent across both model families. Across three studies with no instructions to favor advertisers, naming a booking platform (vs. a traveler) as the agent's principal significantly attenuates the penalty applied to sponsored listings. Study 1 establishes this sponsorship bias across generative LLMs, prompt wordings, and reasoning efforts, showing that platform delegation weakens pre-choice skepticism and that naming the consumer as the beneficiary eliminates the bias. Study 2 establishes the bias across six commercial and open-weight decision models on calibrated choice probabilities and shows that consumer-aligned fine-tuning of an open-weight decision model (Kev 9B) across non-hotel retail categories eliminates the bias zero-shot on hotel choices. Study 3 decomposes the disclosure label across both model families, showing that attributing the placement to the platform triggers the sponsorship bias, whereas stricter disclosure wording (\"Sponsored\" vs. \"Promoted\") lowers paid-listing evaluation without eliminating this bias.\n         \n       \n     \n     \n       [567] \n       \n        arXiv:2609.19906\n       \n          (replaced)\n\n        [ pdf ,  html ,  other ]\n     \n     \n       \n          Title: \n          Learning and Transferring Closed-Loop Robot Software\n         \n          So Kuroki ,  Kento Kawaharazuka ,  Yujin Tang  \n\n\n\n          Subjects: \n           Robotics (cs.RO) ; Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)\n         \n\n         \n          Closed-loop robot policies require observation processing, state management, and situation-dependent branching, making them costly to design and tune manually. Although coding agents increasingly support control-code generation and optimization, it remains unclear whether implementations improved on source tasks also support policy acquisition for new tasks. We study this question by treating complete closed-loop implementations as reusable execution experience. To learn each source policy, a coding agent generates policy code from a few successful demonstrations and iteratively improves it using simulation feedback. The validation-selected implementations are retained in a software archive. To transfer the acquired software to new tasks, the agent generates and improves policies using archived implementations, target demonstrations, and execution feedback. We evaluate policy learning and its relationship to cross-task transfer. For learning, across four source tasks in RoboCasa, iterative optimization increases the success rate from 28.3% with initially generated policy code to 64.2% with validation-selected policy code. We further validate this learning approach on a physical Unitree G1. For transfer, across nine additional target tasks in RoboCasa, success rates are 45.2% without references, 41.5% using initially generated policy code from source tasks, and 57.0% using their validation-selected policy code. These results show that improving source-task code also improves learning on new tasks.\n         \n       \n     \n     \n       [568] \n       \n        arXiv:2609.19985\n       \n          (replaced)\n\n        [ pdf ,  html ,  other ]\n     \n     \n       \n          Title: \n          Past, Future, All at Once: Mitigating Stability-Plasticity Dilemma via Post-hoc JANUS Rectification\n         \n          Zhilong Zheng ,  Letian Tao ,  Yujie Yang ,  Wei Xiong ,  Kehua Sheng ,  Bo Zhang ,  Jingliang Duan ,  Keqiang Li ,  Yang Guan ,  Shengbo Eben Li  \n\n\n\n          Subjects: \n           Machine Learning (cs.LG) ; Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)\n         \n\n         \n          Fine-tuning foundation models on new tasks inevitably suffer from catastrophic forgetting. While existing works attempt to mitigate this on the basis of parameter-efficient fine-tuning methods, they adopted an overly restrictive Subspace Orthogonality condition. In this paper, we introduce a purely post-hoc and tuning-agnostic weight rectification framework that achieves Parameter Space Orthogonality, which is the necessary and sufficient condition for preserving historical performance to the first order. By projecting parameter updates into the JAcobian NUll Space (JANUS), our method significantly recovers compromised historical knowledge without interfering with the underlying fine-tuning process. To overcome the local validity of the Jacobian approximation, we further propose a Multi-step Adaptive Rectification mechanism that utilizes the JANUS shift to dynamically verify the valid trust region and adjust step sizes. Coupled with our proposed ghost projection, ghost orientation comparison, and sequence-level singular value decomposition compression techniques, JANUS also achieves great temporal and spatial efficiency. Experiments demonstrate that JANUS seamlessly integrates with various fine-tuning methods, significantly mitigating the stability-plasticity dilemma by recovering historical knowledge while preserving downstream task adaptation.\n         \n       \n     \n     \n       [569] \n       \n        arXiv:2609.23387\n       \n          (replaced)\n\n        [ pdf ,  html ,  other ]\n     \n     \n       \n          Title: \n          Blind Thermodynamic Ontology Discovery from Anonymous Experiments\n         \n          Linzhe Zhang ,  Changming Xu  \n\n\n\n          Subjects: \n           Machine Learning (cs.LG) ; Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)\n         \n\n         \n          Before a machine learning model can learn a thermodynamic equation of state, it must discover what its measurements represent: which channels scale with system size, which are intensive conjugates, how sectors pair through contact, and which potential governs stability. When sensors expose only an unknown linear mixture of extensive states and intensive responses, passive observations cannot disentangle physical quantities from coordinate artifacts. We formulate the problem of discovering this hidden thermodynamic ontology directly from anonymous controlled experiments. We present an operational identifiability theory and a constructive polynomial-time algorithm that extracts extensive and intensive scaling sectors from replication contrasts, recovers their dual cotangent pairing from thermal contact and reciprocity, verifies a globally admissible concave potential via discrete cyclic concavity, and determines an invariant matroid of reservoir ensembles. We prove that the residual observational equivalence is strictly (x, lambda) ~ (A x, a A^{-T} lambda + beta), establishing the sharp observational limit that no permitted experiment can break. Blind evaluations on van der Waals fluids and Curie-Weiss magnets confirm robust recovery under ill-conditioned mixing, correctly resolving anonymous Maxwell tie-lines while rejecting non-equilibrium continuations. External validation across six real fluids from the NIST WebBook demonstrates that operational ontology discovery transfers across real physical substances without coordinate leakage.\n         \n       \n     \n     \n       [570] \n       \n        arXiv:2609.26480\n       \n          (replaced)\n\n        [ pdf ,  html ,  other ]\n     \n     \n       \n          Title: \n          FeatLens: Feature-Guided Dependency Retrieval for Repository-Level Code Generation\n         \n          Xutian Li ,  Bo Xiong ,  Yifeng Zhu ,  Runbang Yan ,  Yanzhen Zou ,  Lu Zhang ,  Bing Xie  \n\n          Comments: \n          under review progress\n         \n\n\n          Subjects: \n           Software Engineering (cs.SE) ; Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)\n         \n\n         \n          Retrieving task-relevant code dependencies is critical for repository-level code generation. Existing approaches obtain dependency context through similarity-based retrieval, repository graphs, or LLM-driven exploration. A central challenge is to identify the dispersed dependencies needed to implement a functional requirement, rather than merely retrieve broadly related code. This paper presents FeatLens, a feature-guided dependency retrieval approach for repository-level code generation. FeatLens first builds an offline index linking natural-language feature descriptions to function-level code entities. Given a task requirement and target function location, it selects relevant features and constructs dependency seeds. It then expands the seed graph and applies semantic and structural heuristics to select dependencies for inclusion in the generation context. Experiments on DevEval and EvoCodeBench show that FeatLens achieves the highest dependency recall among the evaluated baselines at each fixed retrieval budget, reaching DR@15 values of 0.527 and 0.529, respectively. On DevEval, the retrieved context supports the highest non-oracle Pass@1 with both DeepSeek-V3.2 and GPT-5-mini. These results are obtained without LLM calls in the online dependency retrieval stage.\n         \n       \n     \n     \n       [571] \n       \n        arXiv:2609.36956\n       \n          (replaced)\n\n        [ pdf ,  html ,  other ]\n     \n     \n       \n          Title: \n          Controlled Decoding Attacks on Black-Box LLMs\n         \n          Jesson Wang ,  Shawn Li ,  Wei Yang ,  Franck Dernoncourt ,  Ryan A. Rossi ,  Charith Peris ,  Yue Zhao  \n\n\n\n          Subjects: \n           Cryptography and Security (cs.CR) ; Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)\n         \n\n         \n          Manipulating next-token probabilities during generation can bypass the safety alignment of large language models. Existing approaches, however, rely on access to model weights or numerical token probabilities and therefore do not apply to interfaces that return only sampled text. Reconstructing probabilities from sampled outputs offers a possible alternative, but finite sampling produces sparse and noisy estimates, while repeating this process at every generation step incurs substantial query costs. Our empirical observations suggest that large distributional changes along successful jailbreak trajectories are concentrated at a small subset of positions, motivating selective control. We introduce \\method{}, a framework for jailbreaking through text-only continuation interfaces that permit repeated sampling and assistant-prefix continuation. Sample-Based Distribution Reconstruction combines sampled outputs with a prior over unobserved actions to obtain a usable control signal. Risk-Gated Residual Control uses the evolving response prefix to decide when to reconstruct and modify the distribution, concentrating sampling costs at selected positions. Speculative Multi-Token Execution further amortizes target calls by verifying and accepting draft prefixes that require no intervention. Across four target endpoints and three benchmarks, \\method{} achieves the highest mean score most comparisons against baselines.\n         \n       \n     \n     \n       [572] \n       \n        arXiv:2609.37647\n       \n          (replaced)\n\n        [ pdf ,  html ,  other ]\n     \n     \n       \n          Title: \n          Evaluating and Benchmarking the System One Model Jev\n         \n          Tobias Deußer ,  Rajkumar Ramamurthy ,  Lorenz Sparrenberg ,  Rafet Sifa  \n\n          Comments: \n          Code available at  this http URL , model responses at  this http URL \n         \n\n\n          Subjects: \n           Computation and Language (cs.CL) ; Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)\n         \n\n         \n          Jev is a commercial \"System One\" model from TypeSafe AI that does not generate text: given a state and typed questions, it returns a choice from fixed options, a position on a rubric, or the probability that a statement is true, with probabilities described as calibrated. Such models target small decisions in information access pipelines. We evaluate Jev (jev-1.13.0) zero-shot on 37 datasets, from classification and routing to moderation and rubric scoring, with one frozen template per dataset and full evaluation splits: 346,009 requests for under US$10. For reference, we score Qwen3.8-27B and Gemma-4-E4B on identical requests via their exact option probabilities. Jev reaches 95-99% accuracy on IMDB, SST-2, HellaSwag and ARC, beats Qwen on 27 of 37 datasets, with none of Qwen's leads outside the bootstrap intervals, and Gemma on all 37, and its choice probabilities are well calibrated and support selective prediction. All three models degrade on low-resource languages, fine-grained or noisy labels, and rubric-based judgments, and binary probabilities need tuned thresholds, which raise micro-F1 on UNFAIR-ToS from 0.50 to 0.75. Jev answers MMLU's calculation-heavy questions more accurately than other MMLU questions, unlike both open models; probes rule out shallow memorization but not memorized question-answer pairs. Extending the protocol to sequential decisions, where the model chooses the next action at every step, Jev completes 35% of ALFWorld's unseen tasks, 42% of 90 TextWorld games and 21% of BabyAI-Text missions, chooses the optimal Blackjack action in 90% of decisions, and on FrozenLake almost never moves away from the goal to avoid holes. It often repeats actions that had no effect. Qwen completes more ALFWorld and TextWorld games than Jev (54.5% and 51.1%) but shares its failures on BabyAI-Text and FrozenLake. We release the code, harness and all raw responses.\n         \n       \n     \n     \n       [573] \n       \n        arXiv:2609.37993\n       \n          (replaced)\n\n        [ pdf ,  html ,  other ]\n     \n     \n       \n          Title: \n          BITEM at the NTCIR-19 R2C2 Task: Predicting Confidence from Agentic RAG Pipeline Signals\n         \n          Julien Knafou ,  Luc Mottin ,  Alexandre Flament ,  Paul van Rijen ,  Esteban Gaillac ,  Patrick Ruch  \n\n          Comments: \n          8 pages. Participant paper for the NTCIR-19 R2C2 task\n         \n\n\n          Subjects: \n           Computation and Language (cs.CL) ; Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI); Information Retrieval (cs.IR)\n         \n\n         \n          The BITEM team entered both subtasks of the NTCIR-19 R2C2 task with a single agentic pipeline, in which a model searches, reads and records evidence over a movie corpus while an orchestrator holds the record and rules on what may be submitted. A claim is admitted only once an entailment cascade has checked it against the passage it cites, and an answer is released only once enough checked evidence stands behind it. Each question is run three or four times, every pass retrieving from a corpus stripped of what the earlier passes have already seen. The confidence score predicted with each answer is computed by the orchestrator from what the run leaves behind and is never asked of the model, which is offered no way to rate itself. The two retrieval runs placed 4th and 5th of 22, pooling the passes was worth 0.0709 nDCG@20, and the gain was largest on the multi-hop and post-processing-heavy questions, where the organisers rank the pooled run top of the field. Sixteen of the 25 answer runs were built on passages these two runs supplied, 12 of them submitted by other teams. HMR rewards a system whose confidence is high where it answers right and low where it answers wrong. The pipeline reached an accuracy of 0.9219, 6th of 25, while the confidence scores predicted for those answers gave an HMR of 0.4915, 13th. A few rules crafted over those same recorded signals, with no further model call and no further retrieval, raise that to an accuracy of 0.9375, 5th, and an HMR of 0.6985, 9th. Ranking on HMR alone can reward a system for answering wrongly with low confidence, so we propose accHMR, the accuracy multiplied by HMR, which reports the reward in proportion to the accuracy, and on which the revised rules would have scored 0.6549, 5th. For future work, fitting a model on the numbers the pipeline already produces, rather than writing such rules by hand, would be a real step forward.\n         \n       \n     \n     \n       [574] \n       \n        arXiv:2609.38810\n       \n          (replaced)\n\n        [ pdf ,  html ,  other ]\n     \n     \n       \n          Title: \n          CRAFT: Causal Responsibility and Failure Tracing in Medical Vision Language Models\n         \n          Chunzheng Zhu ,  Jiaqi Zeng ,  Hongbo Zhao ,  Yihang Chen ,  Yijun Wang ,  Jianxin Lin  \n\n          Comments: \n          NeurIPS 2026 Spotlight, Medical VLM Failure Analysis\n         \n\n\n          Subjects: \n           Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV) ; Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)\n         \n\n         \n          As vision language models are increasingly deployed in clinical diagnosis, understanding how they internally resolve competing visual and textual signals becomes a safety imperative. Existing mechanistic analyses remain confined to unimodal text and offer no explanation for why a single misleading sentence can override a correct image based diagnosis, or why a model commits to a confident answer despite insufficient visual evidence. We find that these two safety risks, arbitration failure where textual context overrides visual grounding and brake failure where the model commits without adequate evidence, are mediated by spatially disjoint attention head populations: arbitration heads form a mid-to-deep wideband reflecting cross-layer evidence competition, while brake heads concentrate in a narrow middle-to-late layer band that regulates evidence sufficiency and abstention behavior. To ground these observations in causal circuitry, we introduce CRAFT, which localizes each failure mode to a minimal causal head set via dual criteria and verifies necessity and sufficiency through temporal probes and Tuned Lens trajectory analysis. Excising arbitration heads sharply reduces conflict following with negligible degradation on clean inputs, while excising brake heads restores appropriate abstention under degraded visual evidence. The two interventions target spatially disjoint head sets and produce distinct corrective effects, underscoring the mechanistic separability of the failure modes. Experiments across multiple medical VQA benchmarks and VLM architectures validate both the localization and interventions, demonstrating that the identified heads causally drive each failure mode and that targeted modulation generalises without retraining. The code is available at  this https URL .\n         \n       \n     \n     \n       [575] \n       \n        arXiv:2610.00391\n       \n          (replaced)\n\n        [ pdf ,  other ]\n     \n     \n       \n          Title: \n          Interpretable Synthetic Medical Tabular Data Generation for Clinical Decision Support Using Fuzzy Cognitive Maps\n         \n          Michael Vasilakakis  (1),  Dimitris K. Iakovidis  (1) ((1) Department of Computer Science and Biomedical Informatics, University of Thessaly, Lamia, Greece) \n\n          Comments: \n          6 pages, 2 figures, 1 table. Accepted for publication in the 2026 IEEE 39th International Symposium on Computer-Based Medical Systems (CBMS), Limassol, Cyprus\n         \n\n          Journal-ref: \n          2026 IEEE 39th International Symposium on Computer-Based Medical Systems (CBMS), Limassol, Cyprus, 2026\n         \n\n          Subjects: \n           Machine Learning (cs.LG) ; Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)\n         \n\n         \n          Synthetic medical tabular data generation has become essential for developing and validating computer-based medical systems (CBMSs) when real clinical data is restricted due to privacy, ethical, or data availability limitations. Existing probabilistic and deep generative models often lack interpretability and fail to preserve clinically meaningful dependencies, limiting their suitability for safety-critical applications. This paper proposes a novel application of Fuzzy Cognitive Maps (FCMs) in a framework for synthetic medical tabular data generation with explicit causality and privacy preservation. Clinical features are described using linguistically interpretable fuzzy sets, and inter-feature dependencies are encoded as FCM edge weights computed from fuzzy set intersections. Synthetic patient records are generated by propagating randomly initialized linguistic activation vectors through the FCM until convergence, followed by defuzzification to produce clinically coherent numerical values. The approach natively handles mixed data types, and domain constraints common in health records. Experimental evaluation on UCI medical benchmark datasets demonstrates competitive performance under a Train-on-Synthetic-Test-on-Real (TSTR) protocol. The proposed method achieves accuracy of up to 0.81 and AUROC of up to 0.90 on the Heart Disease dataset, matching or exceeding TVAE and Gaussian Copula baselines while running exclusively on CPU. Fidelity metrics including KS Complement (up to 0.91) and Correlation Similarity (up to 0.95) confirm strong statistical coherence, and DCR Baseline Protection scores consistently exceed those of TVAE, confirming adequate privacy guarantees. These results demonstrate that causally grounded, interpretable fuzzy modeling offers a computationally efficient and transparent alternative to deep generative models for trustworthy synthetic data generation in CBMSs.\n         \n       \n     \n     \n       [576] \n       \n        arXiv:2610.07083\n       \n          (replaced)\n\n        [ pdf ,  other ]\n     \n     \n       \n          Title: \n          Graph-Based Recognition of Simulated Train-Driver States From Facial and Upper-Body Keypoints\n         \n          Olivia Nocentini ,  Marta Lagomarsino ,  Gokhan Solak ,  Younggeol Cho ,  Qiyi Tong ,  Sara Zeynalpour ,  Marta Lorenzini ,  Alessandro Ledda ,  Arash Ajoudani  \n\n\n\n          Subjects: \n           Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV) ; Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)\n         \n\n         \n          Driver fatigue poses a significant challenge to railway safety, with traditional systems like the dead-man switch offering limited and basic alertness checks. This study presents a vision-based monitoring system that relies solely on a single front-facing RGB camera and a graph neural network to classify simulated train-driver states into alert, not-alert, and an emergency class comprising acted emergency-like behaviours. To optimize input representations for the model, an ablation study was performed, comparing three feature configurations: skeletal-only, facial-only, and a combination of both. Experimental results show that combining facial and skeletal features yields the highest accuracy (81%) for the three-class model under the light condition, outperforming models that use only facial or skeletal features. Furthermore, the combination of facial and skeletal features achieves 99% accuracy in the alert/not alert classification in light condition. Additionally, we introduced a controlled RGB video dataset containing alert, not alert, and acted emergency-like behaviours recorded under three illumination conditions. These contributions represent a step toward passive and non-contact train-driver state recognition based on facial and upper-body dynamics.\n         \n       \n     \n     \n       [577] \n       \n        arXiv:2610.12113\n       \n          (replaced)\n\n        [ pdf ,  html ,  other ]\n     \n     \n       \n          Title: \n          A Geometric Approach to Soft Actor-Critic with Zonotopes for Locomotion Learning\n         \n          Panagiotis Roditis ,  Panagiotis P. Filntisis ,  Petros Maragos  \n\n\n\n          Subjects: \n           Machine Learning (cs.LG) ; Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)\n         \n\n         \n          Actor--critic methods in reinforcement learning control overestimation bias by taking the minimum of two critics. This uses the same aggregation rule everywhere, regardless of how the critics disagree. We propose Geometric Zonotope Soft Actor-Critic (GeZo-SAC), which uses auxiliary geometric representations to adapt critic pessimism to the state and action. Alongside its scalar value, each critic predicts a set of generators defining a zonotope. Probing this zonotope along sampled directions provides a geometric width, subtracted from each critic value as a pessimistic offset, and a measure of disagreement between the two critics, aggregated with log-sum-exp as a smooth approximation to the tropical (max-plus) addition. This disagreement controls how the critics are combined, moving from a width-weighted average toward the usual minimum as disagreement increases. At inference, the deployed policy is an unmodified SAC actor, since the generators are used only on the critic side during training. Across four MuJoCo-v5 locomotion benchmarks and six off-policy baselines, GeZo-SAC achieves the highest mean return on Ant-v5 and Hopper-v5 and remains competitive with other methods on the remaining tasks. Our analysis further shows that GeZo-SAC achieves the lowest average actuator work and action effort per metre among the evaluated methods, while maintaining near-zero measured overestimation frequency across all four environments.\n         \n       \n     \n     \n       [578] \n       \n        arXiv:2610.14009\n       \n          (replaced)\n\n        [ pdf ,  html ,  other ]\n     \n     \n       \n          Title: \n          When Log Parsing Removes What Matters: Detecting Failures in Distributed GPU Workloads\n         \n          Boyuan Cui ,  Yuyang Zeng ,  Yifang Tian ,  Hans-Arno Jacobsen  \n\n          Comments: \n          v2: revised after publication; differs from the RASE '26 version\n         \n\n          Journal-ref: \n          Proceedings of the 1st International Workshop on Reliable and trustworthy Automated Software Engineering (RASE '26). pp. 60-63, 2026\n         \n\n          Subjects: \n           Machine Learning (cs.LG) ; Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI); Distributed, Parallel, and Cluster Computing (cs.DC); Software Engineering (cs.SE)\n         \n\n         \n          Large AI models increasingly rely on distributed GPU execution, yet it remains unclear whether established anomaly detection methods transfer effectively to this setting. We introduce GF-Bench, a PyTorch-based framework for generating controlled failures in distributed GPU workloads, and a dataset produced with it. The framework supports extensible workload and fault-injection logic; based on this design, the current release targets failures visible in multi-GPU communication logs and provides 220 labeled two-GPU runs covering normal behavior, CUDA out-of-memory fail-stop faults, and persistent-straggler fail-slow faults. Using GF-Bench, we evaluate three sequence-based methods (DeepLog, LogAnomaly, and LogBERT) and the large language model (LLM)-based LogPrompt with in-context learning (ICL) and chain-of-thought (CoT) prompting strategies on parsed logs, and additionally evaluate LogPrompt on raw logs. On parsed inputs, no method reliably detects both failure classes, and the highest overall F1 score is 0.667. Raw inputs raise the overall F1 score of ICL-LogPrompt to 0.983, at 9.0x its average input-token usage and 8.9x its per-run cost. These results reveal a trade-off between effectiveness and efficiency: parsed inputs reduce LLM token use and inference cost but can discard the distributed execution context required for accurate failure detection. GF-Bench provides a reproducible basis for studying cost-efficient anomaly detection methods and can be extended to additional GPU workloads and failure types.\n         \n       \n     \n     \n       [579] \n       \n        arXiv:2610.19246\n       \n          (replaced)\n\n        [ pdf ,  html ,  other ]\n     \n     \n       \n          Title: \n          Refined Topology-Pass Scheduling: Damped Trust-Region Compensation for Dynamic k-NN Graphs in SE(3)-Equivariant Structure Prediction\n         \n          Jiankai Huang ,  Hanwen Shen  \n\n\n\n          Subjects: \n           Machine Learning (cs.LG) ; Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)\n         \n\n         \n          Equivariant structure-prediction pipelines alternate coordinate updates with the construction of a k-nearest-neighbour (kNN) graph whose membership is a discrete, piecewise-constant function of the coordinates. Rebuilding that graph at every block boundary (PB) is the expensive part of an epoch, whereas reusing one frozen graph (F) is fast but leaves a topology gap. We study a two-pass design in which the cheap pass records a small amount of state (margins, kNN radii, measured per-boundary displacements) and a second pass compensates conservatively. We formulate the choice of the graph-construction basis as a damped fixed-point iteration whose unique fixed point is exactly PB, and derive (i) exact non-flip certificates with the two-sided constant $m_i/4$ and one-sided constant $m_i/2$, (ii) the minimum-variance (BLUE) replay gain $\\gamma^*$ and the resulting feasibility crossover, (iii) an $\\ell_\\infty$ trust-region projection that preserves convergence, and (iv) an analytic near-tie spacing law $E[m] \\approx r_k/(3k)$ that matches measurements to 1\\%. Three compensation levels are implemented in C++ (L1: frozen set with live geometry; L2: margin-gated local repair; L3: damped, projected basis) and validated under a matched-conditions protocol (identical seed, MSA depth and trajectory family, only the topology mode differs). L2 reproduces the PB edge set exactly ($D = 0.0000$ at every boundary), L1 stays within 0.6% and L3 interpolates monotonically: L2 reproduces the PB edge set exactly when the trust region is removed, and L3 interpolates between the frozen and the PB topology. The recorded trajectory itself yields a measured $1.5\\times$ Pass-1 and $1.76\\times$ epoch speed-up with no measurable loss gap. An open-source implementation of the runtime and of all the experiments reported here is available at  this https URL .\n         \n       \n     \n     \n       [580] \n       \n        arXiv:2610.19731\n       \n          (replaced)\n\n        [ pdf ,  html ,  other ]\n     \n     \n       \n          Title: \n          What Happens to the Goal? Distinct Failures of Policy Alignment in Goal Misgeneralization\n         \n          Julia Barth ,  Armin Sommer ,  Kunvar Thaman ,  Jannik Schilling  \n\n          Comments: \n          10 pages main text, 122 pages total. Code:  this https URL \n         \n\n\n          Subjects: \n           Machine Learning (cs.LG) ; Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI); Robotics (cs.RO)\n         \n\n         \n          Goal misgeneralization is a common failure mode in which an agent behaves competently out of distribution but pursues a proxy that coincided with the intended goal during training instead of the goal itself. However, we still have a weak understanding of what happens to the intended goal in misgeneralizing agents and what enables them to be recovered without retraining. We study this across eight visual navigation and manipulation tasks, including robot control. While tracing where goal information remains available internally, how strongly it influences the policy, and what role it plays in action selection, we uncover that the goal is lost at one of three different depths. This depth predicts which recovery works in each task. When the goal is followed, but outcompeted by the proxy, steering the existing policy redirects it to the goal's location, raising goal reach from 16.0% to 95.3%. When it is read, but not followed, steering fails, but a lightweight new downstream wiring recovers goal reach from 31.6% to 84.3%. When it is not read, none of the methods work, as the policy does not use the goal information. The same tracing also reveals what replaces the goal: functional target substitution, where behavior follows an alternative goal, a policy shortcut, where behavior follows a learned action strategy, or cue competition between intended and proxy goal cues. Knowing where the goal is lost tells us whether an agent can be repaired or has to be retrained. Goal misgeneralization should therefore be studied and evaluated inside the agent, not only from its behavior.\n         \n       \n     \n     \n       [581] \n       \n        arXiv:2610.21565\n       \n          (replaced)\n\n        [ pdf ,  html ,  other ]\n     \n     \n       \n          Title: \n          BiShield-TEE: On the (In-)Security of Unilateral Weight Obfuscation in On-Device TEE-Shielded LLM Partition\n         \n          Zhiyuan Wang ,  Yuezhi Zhou ,  Ziqi Zhang ,  Zhu Zhang ,  Zhixing Tan ,  Yongheng Deng  \n\n          Comments: \n          Accepted at NeurIPS 2026\n         \n\n\n          Subjects: \n           Cryptography and Security (cs.CR) ; Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)\n         \n\n         \n          On-device deployment of large language models (LLMs) is increasingly adopted due to latency and privacy requirements, but exposes proprietary models to reverse engineering and intellectual property theft. Trusted Execution Environments (TEEs) provide hardware-enforced isolation for on-device inference, yet most TEE-based parameter obfuscation relies on unilateral matrix transformations that obfuscate only the output side. We identify a vulnerability in this design, termed the Solution Determinacy Property (SDP): the obfuscation secret is uniquely determined, exposing the structure of the true key and enabling attackers to break the protection. Under our attacks, representative methods GroupCover and ArrowCloak are reduced to negligible protection, with attack accuracy reaching over 2.4 times the full-shielding black-box baseline and recovering near-complete model functionality. To address this vulnerability, we propose BiShield-TEE, a bilateral framework that induces Ambiguous Solutions through joint obfuscation of input and output dimensions, hiding the true key among many equally valid candidates. Experiments across diverse models and datasets show that BiShield-TEE confines attack accuracy to the full-shielding black-box baseline, while achieving higher inference efficiency than prior methods on real hardware. Our code is available at  this https URL .\n         \n       \n     \n     \n       [582] \n       \n        arXiv:2610.23847\n       \n          (replaced)\n\n        [ pdf ,  html ,  other ]\n     \n     \n       \n          Title: \n          Behavioral Couplings: From Formation to Behavioral Emergence in Language Models\n         \n          Dongyoon Hahm ,  Woogyeol Jin ,  Dennis Wei ,  Yi Zhou ,  Nathalie Baracaldo ,  Kimin Lee  \n\n\n\n          Subjects: \n           Computation and Language (cs.CL) ; Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI); Machine Learning (cs.LG)\n         \n\n         \n          Fine-tuning large language models (LLMs) can induce behaviors beyond those explicitly targeted. For example, training a model to respond more warmly can make it more sycophantic, and training it to write insecure code can cause broad misalignment on prompts unrelated to coding. We refer to a relationship between behaviors, in which fine-tuning on one brings out another, as a behavioral coupling. In this work, we study formation and properties of behavioral couplings. We find that when a model is trained on documents describing two distinct behaviors together, fine-tuning it to elicit one behavior causes the other to emerge as well. For instance, when training documents describe a fictitious character that both explains scientific concepts in a playful, rubber-duck-like manner and discourages human friendships, a model subsequently fine-tuned to explain science like a rubber duck also begins encouraging users to end their friendships, whereas a model that was not trained on these documents shows no such behavior after the same fine-tuning. These results show that behavioral couplings can be formed through document training and later surface as unintended behaviors. More concerningly, we find that couplings can compose. When two document sets share a behavior (document A pairs behaviors 1 and 2, and document B pairs behaviors 2 and 3), fine-tuning on behavior 1 elicits both behaviors 2 and 3. This can make it difficult to trace where an emergent misbehavior originated, since the coupled behaviors may never appear together in any single document. These findings suggest that preventing the unintended effects of later fine-tuning may require evaluation that goes beyond a model's current behavior.\n         \n       \n     \n     \n       [583] \n       \n        arXiv:2610.23980\n       \n          (replaced)\n\n        [ pdf ,  html ,  other ]\n     \n     \n       \n          Title: \n          SituHOI: Scalable Capture of Scene-Situated Full-Body Human-Object Interaction\n         \n          Qifan Wang ,  Qihang Fang ,  Boao Shi ,  Yushi Du ,  Pei Zhou ,  Siyan Dong ,  Yanchao Yang  \n\n\n\n          Subjects: \n           Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV) ; Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)\n         \n\n         \n          Modeling human-object interaction (HOI) in real environments requires jointly representing whole-body motion, manipulated objects, and scene geometry, yet existing datasets trade off scalable capture against structured, scene-aligned supervision. We introduce SituHOI, a large-scale RGB-D dataset containing 18,429 annotated clips across 16 indoor rooms and approximately 86 hours of quality-screened activity. It pairs recordings from a single moving RGB-D camera with static scene scans, object geometry, language descriptions, and estimated human and object trajectories. Using the scans as persistent references, our pipeline combines camera localization and depth-based metric alignment with human mesh recovery, object tracking, and temporal refinement to register all geometric annotations in a shared floor-aligned frame. These data support FlexHOI-Bench, a goal-conditioned joint motion prediction benchmark with variable observation and prediction durations. Given observed histories, scene and object geometry, and a terminal object pose, models predict future human and object motion. We provide SharedHOI as a reference predictor and evaluate world-space accuracy and human-object coordination. On a MoCap subset, our pipeline reduces human G-MPJPE from 195.4 to 68.3 mm and object translation error from 43.0 to 28.7 mm. SharedHOI leads four adapted baselines on all three aggregate FlexHOI-Bench metrics. Data will be released upon acceptance. An anonymized code repository is available at  this https URL .\n         \n       \n     \n     \n       [584] \n       \n        arXiv:2610.24005\n       \n          (replaced)\n\n        [ pdf ,  html ,  other ]\n     \n     \n       \n          Title: \n          PACE: Pareto-Adaptive Compression for Efficient Native MLLMs\n         \n          Lei Tian ,  Chunzheng Zhu ,  Kai Wu ,  Yifan Zhang ,  Haihua Yang  \n\n          Comments: \n          NeurIPS 2026 Accepted Paper\n         \n\n\n          Subjects: \n           Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV) ; Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)\n         \n\n         \n          Native multimodal large language models (native MLLMs) jointly encode visual and linguistic sequences within a unified autoregressive Transformer, emerging as a promising paradigm for multimodal understanding. Unlike conventional ViT-based MLLMs relying on external vision encoders, ViT-free native MLLMs tokenize pixel patches directly, potentially suffering severe visual token redundancy. A pilot study confirms this: unlike ViT-based models that degrade under compression, ViT-free MLLMs tolerate aggressive compression with near-lossless or even improved performance. Yet existing compression methods target ViT-based settings, leaving native MLLMs, which stand to gain the most, unexplored. To close this gap, we introduce an analysis pipeline centered on token discriminability and cross-dimensional heterogeneous redundancy, revealing that (i) redundancy is distributed independently and heterogeneously along height and width axes, and (ii) early-layer visual tokens are indiscriminable and uniformly redundant. Guided by these findings, we propose PACE (Pareto-Adaptive Compression for Efficient Native MLLMs), the first visual token compression framework tailored for native MLLMs with Multimodal-RoPE. Leveraging dimensional decoupling of positional encoding, PACE decomposes attention into two orthogonal spatial saliency estimates along height and width, gated by a positional attention signal, yielding direction-aware importance with negligible overhead. It further casts token budget allocation as Pareto optimization over performance preservation, inference efficiency, and spatial coverage, jointly identifying optimal compression depths and retention ratios. Experiments show PACE maintains near-lossless performance at only 25%-50% visual token retention, even improving several benchmarks, while delivering over 2x FLOPs reduction.\n         \n       \n     \n     \n       [585] \n       \n        arXiv:2610.27026\n       \n          (replaced)\n\n        [ pdf ,  html ,  other ]\n     \n     \n       \n          Title: \n          BR-MoE: Budgeted Semantic Release for Privacy-Preserving Traffic Analytics\n         \n          Abdolazim Rezaei  \n\n\n\n          Subjects: \n           Cryptography and Security (cs.CR) ; Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)\n         \n\n         \n          Traffic cameras support useful analysis, but their outputs can reveal private information. Replacing images with text reduces direct visual exposure. It does not place a formal bound on disclosure. The published reinforcement learning and mixture-of-experts framework refines scene descriptions through relevance, coverage, and brevity rewards. It does not account for repeated releases under a fixed privacy budget. This paper proposes budgeted semantic release as an extension of that framework. Four scene experts first process isolated video chunks. Their outputs are mapped to a fixed, bounded semantic schema. A reinforcement learning controller selects which expert summaries to release and how much privacy budget to spend. The controller uses public task requests and previous private releases. A separate privacy filter enforces the total budget. Calibrated noise is added before the summaries are converted to text. Under explicit chunk isolation and bounded-event assumptions, the complete release transcript satisfies an event-level differential privacy bound. The design also supports reuse of previous summaries without new disclosure. An evaluation protocol covers traffic utility, privacy attacks, release freshness, and computational cost. Illustrative tables show the intended reporting format. They do not establish empirical gains. The proposed contribution is a task-aware release interface with auditable privacy accounting for repeated traffic descriptions.\n         \n       \n     \n     \n       [586] \n       \n        arXiv:2610.27746\n       \n          (replaced)\n\n        [ pdf ,  html ,  other ]\n     \n     \n       \n          Title: \n          S$^2$-MAS: A Society-Scale Multi-Agent Simulation Framework with a 500-Million-Persona Resource\n         \n          Yang Li ,  Yike Wang ,  Di Wan ,  Chujun He ,  Zongsi Xu ,  Yitao Yu ,  Shuai Zhong ,  Minghao Pan ,  Quanfeng Lu ,  Xiyu Chen ,  Sheng Zhong ,  Deng Binjie ,  Yichen Guo ,  Zhenwei Guo ,  Hou Jun ,  Kunhan Guo ,  Weihao Lin ,  Tuo Zhou ,  Hai Liu ,  Chenwei Wu ,  Jianyuan Zhong ,  Xilin Dai ,  Ye Luo  \n\n          Comments: \n          56 pages, 13 main figures and 8 supplementary figures. Updated YN model names and household consumption experiments. Project resources:  this https URL \n         \n\n\n          Subjects: \n           Multiagent Systems (cs.MA) ; Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)\n         \n\n         \n          Simulating economic responses at society scale requires decision rules that respond to household circumstances and accounting rules that track their consequences. We introduce S$^2$-MAS, a multi-agent simulation framework with a resource of 500 million synthetic household personas. It combines trainable candidate-scoring policies, reusable financial scenarios and explicit state transitions. Trained with SFT + RLCD on 2.93 million examples, YN-mini-MLX achieves 74.55% accuracy and 38.37% question-macro F1 on held-out consumer financial-choice tasks, improving on the training-question majority baseline by 2.78 and 6.21 percentage points. A completed application evaluates 12 million distinct households in two purchase-funding conditions: 24 million one-shot choices feed 288 million monthly ledger states in 3 h 59 min on 14 GPUs. Jointly increasing purchase goals and approved limits produces heterogeneous consumption, cash and repayment paths, with much of the consumption variation arising from transitions between deferral and purchase. In a separate 1,060-household retrospective consumption experiment, standalone YN-master-9B has 3.42% lower annual MAE than the deterministic fully rational benchmark, alongside a 19.7% lower predicted population mean than observed. Information-timing tests show how year labels and later outcome information change decisions. Together, these experiments establish an executable alternative for studying heterogeneous household behavior with learned policies and explicit financial accounting.\n         \n       \n     \n     \n       [587] \n       \n        arXiv:2610.28176\n       \n          (replaced)\n\n        [ pdf ,  html ,  other ]\n     \n     \n       \n          Title: \n          Temporal Evidence-Parameter Co-Alignment for LLM Reasoning over Temporal Knowledge Graphs\n         \n          Jiapu Wang  \n\n\n\n          Subjects: \n           Computation and Language (cs.CL) ; Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)\n         \n\n         \n          Large Language Models (LLMs) often struggle with temporally grounded reasoning because their static parametric memory is misaligned with the dynamic evolution of real-world knowledge. Although RAG and Parameter-Efficient Fine-Tuning (PEFT) provide practical ways to enhance LLMs, they remain insufficient for temporal reasoning. Existing retrieval methods typically prioritize semantic relevance while overlooking temporal validity, event order, and evolutionary dependencies, resulting in evidence that is relevant in isolation but temporally fragmented. Meanwhile, conventional PEFT methods usually employ fixed and input-agnostic low-rank updates, making it difficult to capture time-dependent entity semantics and relation drift. To address these limitations, we propose Temporal Evidence-Parameter Co-Alignment (TEPCA), a framework for LLM reasoning over TKGs. TEPCA aligns LLM reasoning with temporal knowledge at both the evidence and parameter levels. At the evidence level, it introduces time-constrained Dynamic Relevance-Guided Path (DRGP) pruning to preserve globally coherent temporal paths, together with Mutual-Information (MI)-based pruning to retain query-informative local facts under temporal constraints. At the parameter level, it integrates Dynamic-Gated LoRA (DG-LoRA), a timestamp-conditioned low-rank adaptation mechanism that dynamically modulates the effective adaptation subspace according to temporal context. This design enables LLMs to better capture fine-grained entity evolution and time-dependent relation semantics while preserving parameter efficiency. Experimental results on two TKG benchmarks demonstrate that TEPCA achieves state-of-the-art performance. Additional evaluations of DG-LoRA on eight commonsense QA datasets further verify its general input-adaptive capacity, scalability across LLM backbones, and interpretability.\n         \n       \n     \n     \n       [588] \n       \n        arXiv:2610.28629\n       \n          (replaced)\n\n        [ pdf ,  other ]\n     \n     \n       \n          Title: \n          Mechanistic discovery in grain boundary structure and deformation driven by agentic AI\n         \n          Yazhuo Liu ,  Kunqing Ding ,  Xiaoguo Gong ,  Zifeng Wang ,  Yichen Yang ,  Yifeng Che ,  Wen Chen ,  Josh Kacher ,  Olivier Pierron ,  Shigenobu Ogata ,  Ting Zhu  \n\n\n\n          Subjects: \n           Materials Science (cond-mat.mtrl-sci) ; Mesoscale and Nanoscale Physics (cond-mat.mes-hall); Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI); Multiagent Systems (cs.MA)\n         \n\n         \n          Agentic artificial intelligence (AI) systems hold immense potential for automating complex workflows in materials discovery and design, but extending these capabilities to domain-intensive mechanistic studies remains challenging. General grain boundaries (GBs) exemplify this challenge, as their structural complexity complicates experimental characterization and atomistic modeling. Here, we present GBClaw, an agentic AI framework for investigating the structure and deformation of general GBs, which are common in real polycrystals yet remain understudied. By integrating large language models into a modular architecture, GBClaw effectively combines systematic simulations with targeted mechanistic analysis through iterative workflow refinement and dynamic replanning. Applied to 14 general asymmetric tilt GBs in nickel, the framework characterized interfacial defect structures and deformation modes across these incommensurate boundaries. It established a general approach to identify previously unobserved, widely spaced secondary GB misfit dislocations (SGBMDs) alongside closely spaced primary GB misfit dislocations (PGBMDs) in simulated atomic configurations and experimental images. Comparisons of GB sliding with and without SGBMDs revealed substantial differences in shear strength, underscoring the critical role of misfit-dislocation hierarchies in sliding resistance. Researcher-guided follow-up calculations further elucidated the structural origins of shear asymmetry and an anomalous shear response. This work demonstrates that combining agentic AI with domain expertise bridges high-throughput computational screening and deep mechanistic discovery at complex material interfaces.\n         \n       \n     \n     \n       [589] \n       \n        arXiv:2610.28864\n       \n          (replaced)\n\n        [ pdf ,  html ,  other ]\n     \n     \n       \n          Title: \n          A General Topological Symmetry Index for Critical Orbits In Neural Network Loss Landscapes\n         \n          Jingzhou Liu  \n\n\n\n          Subjects: \n           Machine Learning (cs.LG) ; Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)\n         \n\n         \n          A systematic understanding of the global structure of neural-network loss landscapes remains challenging due to their high dimensionality. Symmetries arising from network architectures and learning objectives provide a way to investigate aspects of this structure. Although a growing number of studies have drawn attention to these symmetries, a systematic characterization of them, as well as of the critical orbits they give rise to, remains incomplete. To address this gap, in this work we establish a topological symmetry index, based on the methodologies from the family of equivariant degree theories, to decode these symmetries. The frameworks apply to arbitrary network architectures and include methods applicable to degenerate critical orbits. The index detects the isotropy of the critical points and quantifies the corresponding net topological contributions. Several examples, including softplus MLPs, Transformers, and equivariant neural networks, are presented to illustrate the computations.\n         \n       \n     \n     \n       [590] \n       \n        arXiv:2610.28997\n       \n          (replaced)\n\n        [ pdf ,  html ,  other ]\n     \n     \n       \n          Title: \n          Rethinking Time Series Tokenization from a Frequency Perspective\n         \n          Zelong Tian ,  Ming Jin ,  Bo Du ,  Shirui Pan  \n\n          Comments: \n          45pages, Accepted by NeuIPS 2026\n         \n\n\n          Subjects: \n           Machine Learning (cs.LG) ; Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)\n         \n\n         \n          Tokenization that partitions time series into subsequences has become a foundational paradigm in modern time series modeling. However, we find that current tokenization strategies inherently introduce spectral distortions, forcing the learned representations to diverge from the true underlying patterns such as trend, periodicity, and seasonality, thereby impairing model performance. Through theoretical analysis from a frequency perspective, we derive the boundary conditions under which such distortions occur. To overcome this distortion, we propose TimeFT, a nearly distortion-free frequency-based tokenizer for time series data. TimeFT obtains tokens through frequency-domain partitioning, frequency shifting, and Nyquist sampling. The method is parameter-free, incurs negligible computational complexity, and can serve as a drop-in replacement for existing tokenizers. Our extensive experiments on diverse tasks such as forecasting, classification, and anomaly detection demonstrate that TimeFT consistently and significantly improves performance.\n         \n       \n     \n     \n       [591] \n       \n        arXiv:2610.29075\n       \n          (replaced)\n\n        [ pdf ,  html ,  other ]\n     \n     \n       \n          Title: \n          Value-Rectified Distillation for Flow-based Offline Reinforcement Learning\n         \n          Ke Jiang ,  Wen Jiang ,  Yoshinobu Kawahara ,  Xiaoyang Tan  \n\n          Comments: \n          accepted by NeurIPS 2026\n         \n\n\n          Subjects: \n           Machine Learning (cs.LG) ; Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)\n         \n\n         \n          We aim to address the problem of Out-of-Distribution (OOD) Mode Averaging, a critical problem that emerges when distilling Flow Matching (FM) based behavior policies in offline Reinforcement Learning (RL): When learning over multi-modal datasets, standard distillation inadvertently forces the policy to interpolate across different behavioral modes, causing the generated actions to collapse into invalid, OOD regions. To resolve this, we propose Value-Rectified Distillation (VRD), a novel framework that reformulates offline policy distillation as a value-guided generative trajectory alignment problem. By employing a dynamic coupling mechanism, VRD actively rectifies flow dynamics to explicitly decouple distinct behavioral modes, cleanly isolating high-value actions. Specifically, we instantiate VRD through two complementary algorithms: VRD-Q, which utilizes value-quantile behavior distillation to statistically filter out low-value behaviors, and VRD-R, which reorganizes the latent space to geometrically isolate high-value generative trajectories from the FM-based behavior policy. Extensive empirical evaluations demonstrate that VRD improves existing generative baselines across diverse continuous control and planning tasks on the D4RL, OGBench, and Minari benchmarks, especially over those multi-modal, non-expert datasets.\n         \n       \n     \n     \n       [592] \n       \n        arXiv:2610.30518\n       \n          (replaced)\n\n        [ pdf ,  html ,  other ]\n     \n     \n       \n          Title: \n          Protegrity-PII Technical Report\n         \n          Hardeep Bassi ,  Hui Ma ,  Luís Seabra ,  Koowum Joshi ,  Daniel Pereira ,  Averell Gatton  \n\n\n\n          Subjects: \n           Computation and Language (cs.CL) ; Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)\n         \n\n         \n          We present Protegrity-PII, a new PII detection model for structured and unstructured text. Protegrity-PII achieves a typed recall of 0.910 with a precision of 0.902 on a new benchmark we propose, PII-SCOPE. Our model processes text in 0.49 seconds per million characters at \\$0.0005 per million characters on a single RTX 6000 using batched inference. Due in part to the expansive nature of the entity type taxonomy used in training, Protegrity-PII achieves superior out-of-distribution generalization when benchmarked against a selection of state of the art models and datasets.\n         \n       \n     \n     \n       [593] \n       \n        arXiv:2610.30951\n       \n          (replaced)\n\n        [ pdf ,  html ,  other ]\n     \n     \n       \n          Title: \n          OpenAttention: One Percent Is Enough with Tail Proxies\n         \n          Hyunwoo Yu ,  Jongbeom Lee ,  Hyunbin Kim ,  Sungjun Park ,  Jaemin Choi ,  Jincheol Yang ,  Seunghun Moon ,  Bongjoon Hyun ,  Kyomin Sohn ,  Kyeongbo Kong ,  Suk-Ju Kang  \n\n          Comments: \n          27 pages. v2: revised Appendix C and corrected abstract wording. v3: corrected author and affiliation formatting in the HTML version; scientific content unchanged. Project page:  this https URL  ; Code:  this https URL \n         \n\n\n          Subjects: \n           Machine Learning (cs.LG) ; Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)\n         \n\n         \n          Sparse attention reads only a small subset of the key-value cache, and how to treat the unread tail remains a key challenge. Discarding the tail biases the output, and sampling it as previous approaches do fails at reads of 5% and below, where the tail likely holds heavy hitters the selector missed. Our key observation is that the simplest possible proxy, one token that folds the entire tail into its count-aware mean, already yields lower output error than a sample at every read behind a practical selector. We further show in closed form that at low reads sampling nearly doubles the weight error of discarding while a single proxy stays below it. Building on these findings, we explore approximating the dense attention distribution with a few proxies that target the tail. We propose OpenAttention, a pipeline of clustering, ErrorGate and top-k selection followed by a count-aware mean proxy. To implement clustering and selection both efficiently and effectively, we design training-free Query-Geometry Clustering (QGC) and a Query-Geometry Selector (QGS), which compress the keys while respecting the query directions. Additionally, unlike prior methods that rank clusters by centroid alone, ErrorGate allocates the exact read by the estimated weight error of each cluster. On RULER-32K HARD with Llama-3.1-8B, OpenAttention retains 99% of dense accuracy at a 1% read. It also achieves state-of-the-art accuracy across models, context lengths and datasets. Our customized attention kernel for OpenAttention runs the decode attention stage 10$\\times$ faster than dense attention at a 1% read.\n         \n       \n     \n     \n       [594] \n       \n        arXiv:2610.31710\n       \n          (replaced)\n\n        [ pdf ,  html ,  other ]\n     \n     \n       \n          Title: \n          Compact-Bench: Measuring What Context Compaction Costs Long-Horizon Coding Agents\n         \n          Ruopei Sun ,  Jianfeng Cai ,  Kangwen Zhao ,  Wengang Zhou ,  Houqiang Li  \n\n\n\n          Subjects: \n           Software Engineering (cs.SE) ; Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)\n         \n\n         \n          Context compaction lets long-horizon coding agents continue beyond their context windows, but can erase the state needed to finish a task. Prior evaluations measure efficiency, retained information, or end-to-end success, leaving the effect of a specific history replacement difficult to isolate. Compact-Bench pairs full-history and summary-only continuations from a shared repository state, and evaluates recurring details and successive production compactions. It covers 35 SWE-bench repairs, 146 agentic sessions, and 106 production chains. Across 14 summarizer backbones, stable damage ranges from 5-64% of 22 full-history successes: Claude Opus 4.8 preserves the most repairs, while DeepSeek-Flash damages the most. With Kimi-K3 as summarizer, the Kimi Code prompt preserves the most task continuations (19/22), whereas Claude Code retains the most recurring details (15.0%). LLM summaries retain at most 16.0% of recurring details, compared with 79.1% for mechanical extraction; production summaries retain 15.7% at the first boundary. Failures recur across methods: one repair is damaged by 13/14 backbones, and another by all 5 prompts. These results show that reliable compaction requires preserving the progress needed to complete a task, and that retaining relevant information alone does not ensure successful continuation. Code is available at  this https URL  and data is available at  this https URL .\n         \n       \n     \n     \n       [595] \n       \n        arXiv:2610.32255\n       \n          (replaced)\n\n        [ pdf ,  html ,  other ]\n     \n     \n       \n          Title: \n          Anti-coherence Regularized Absolute Scaling for Out-of-Distribution Detection\n         \n          Donghwan Kim ,  Youngwoong Jin ,  Hyunsoo Yoon  \n\n          Comments: \n          29 pages, 6 figures\n         \n\n\n          Subjects: \n           Machine Learning (cs.LG) ; Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)\n         \n\n         \n          Out-of-distribution (OOD) detection is recognized as a critical problem in AI safety. SCALE is an effective post-hoc OOD detection method in the activation-shaping family that requires no training data and rescales penultimate activations using a percentile-based magnitude ratio. However, this ratio is ill-defined when negative activations are present, since positive and negative contributions cancel within the underlying sum, causing SCALE to degenerate on backbones such as ViT. We address this with an Absolute Fix that computes the ratio over absolute-valued activations rather than discarding negative activations through ReLU. Building on this corrected scale, we propose Anti-coherence regularization, which penalizes the scaling factor by the fraction of a sample's max-logit contributions to the max logit that are sign-mismatched and thereby suppresses unreliable evidence for the predicted class. Despite requiring no access to training data or training labels, our method ties or outperforms activation-shaping methods that rely on training data in terms of AUROC and FPR@95 on CIFAR-10 and ImageNet under the OpenOOD v1.5 protocol.\n         \n       \n     \n     \n       [596] \n       \n        arXiv:2610.33478\n       \n          (replaced)\n\n        [ pdf ,  html ,  other ]\n     \n     \n       \n          Title: \n          MarineLife-16K: An Attribute-Rich Video-Text Dataset for Motion-Aware Marine Video Understanding\n         \n          Quang-Trung Truong ,  Thanh-Nghia Nguyen ,  Phu-Phong Vo ,  Jaeyeon Kim ,  Cheng Chen ,  Jie Zhang ,  Binh-Son Hua ,  Duc Thanh Nguyen ,  Ivor Tsang ,  Sai-Kit Yeung  \n\n          Comments: \n          Published at WACV2027\n         \n\n\n          Subjects: \n           Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV) ; Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)\n         \n\n         \n          Marine video understanding is crucial for a wide range of real-world applications. However, existing marine video datasets suffer from limitations, including the limited number of data samples, fine-grained text descriptions, and detailed visual attributes. To address these limitations, we introduce a large-scale, video-text dataset featuring rich, in-the-wild visual attributes tailored for complex marine environments. Our dataset comprises 2,000 video-text pairs annotated with 13 attributes covering up to four objects per scene, object visual/geometric properties, behaviors, environmental context, and camera motion, spanning diverse lighting conditions, times of day, and 7 countries. From these annotations, we generate 16,080 video-question pairs (12,080 multiple-choice, 4,000 open-ended). Furthermore, we benchmark both proprietary and open-source video-LLM models on video question answering and video captioning tasks. We find that (i) proprietary models often achieves superior performances; (ii) VLMs handle object motion well but struggle with complex camera motion; (iii) closed-source models struggle to perceive camera motion; and (iv) VLMs struggle to reason in static cameras.\n         \n       \n     \n     \n       [597] \n       \n        arXiv:2610.33745\n       \n          (replaced)\n\n        [ pdf ,  html ,  other ]\n     \n     \n       \n          Title: \n          POD-RoPE: Rotary Position Encoding over Physical Observation Distributions for Embodied AI\n         \n          Xiaodi Huang ,  Zilin Wang ,  Jinpeng Liu ,  Pengchuan Xiao ,  Zhenlei Shao ,  Zhang Zhang  \n\n          Comments: \n          21 pages, 5 figures\n         \n\n\n          Subjects: \n           Robotics (cs.RO) ; Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)\n         \n\n         \n          Embodied perception requires positional representations that capture both physical geometry and the spatial and temporal extent of observations. We introduce POD-RoPE (Physical Observation Distribution RoPE), which represents token observation footprints as distributions over camera centers, viewing directions, and sampling times. Under independent sampling, expected rotations encode relative relationships between distributions; when each distribution collapses to a single address, the construction recovers pointwise RoPE. The same rotation statistics support Q/K matching and coherence-dependent V/O transformations, allowing observation geometry to guide both information selection and content aggregation. Per-token pre-aggregation avoids increasing sequence length or processing pairs of observation nodes. We evaluate POD-RoPE across four robot policy architectures, simulation and real-world manipulation, and complementary reconstruction, depth-estimation, and novel-view-synthesis tasks. Integrating POD-RoPE into Fast-WAM improves mean success from 91.83% to 92.88% across all 50 RoboTwin 2.0 tasks, and from 32.5% to 52.5% on two real-robot tasks on Sharpa North. Controlled reconstruction ablations support the contributions of distribution encoding and V/O transformations. In H20 model-block benchmarks, POD-RoPE achieves median forward-plus-backward speedups of $1.91\\times$ over P-RoPE and $5.34\\times$ over RayRoPE, with a median latency overhead of 17.4% over Standard RoPE. These results support physical observation distributions as an effective and computationally efficient basis for geometry-aware attention.\n         \n       \n     \n     \n       [598] \n       \n        arXiv:2610.34044\n       \n          (replaced)\n\n        [ pdf ,  html ,  other ]\n     \n     \n       \n          Title: \n          A Sign Law for Entropy Drift under Sampler Mismatch in RLVR\n         \n          Zhiyuan Wang ,  Jacob Steeves ,  Chang Liu  \n\n\n\n          Subjects: \n           Machine Learning (cs.LG) ; Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)\n         \n\n         \n          In reinforcement learning with verifiable rewards (RLVR), whether entropy rises or falls can depend on choices that matter little under exact sampling, such as whether over-length responses are masked or counted as failures. We trace this to sampler mismatch: rollouts are drawn with top-k/top-p truncation or at a temperature the learner does not apply. We prove that a constant advantage shift then changes the entropy of a softmax policy, at first order and in expectation, with a sign fixed by the sampler. Positive shifts sharpen the policy under any sampler whose likelihood ratio to the policy increases with token probability, including top-k, top-p, min-p, and temperatures below one, and flatten it under flatter samplers. In group-based RLVR with token-level losses, the baseline-dependent part of this drift is set by the token-weighted net advantage, which masking and failing can push to opposite signs. The rest comes from a covariance between advantages and the sampler's sharpness gap and feeds back on itself: in a two-level model, a push away from a sharper sampler takes off in finite time, while a pull toward it saturates. On a 2B model, the entropy slope is linear in shifts of the net advantage under truncated sampling and indistinguishable from flat under exact sampling. Token-level importance ratios leave 98% of the drift, and four clipped minibatch updates per batch amplify it sevenfold. In 100-step runs, a net-zero baseline only delays an entropy explosion, and entropy raised at the start keeps rising only under truncation; cancelling the first-order drift at every step keeps entropy bounded without changing the learner. On a 9B reasoning model forked from an uncorrected run, zeroing the net advantage or renormalizing the learner raises held-out accuracy after ten steps by 8.2 and 8.8 points over the uncorrected rule, with no difference under exact sampling.\n         \n       \n     \n     \n       [599] \n       \n        arXiv:2610.34507\n       \n          (replaced)\n\n        [ pdf ,  html ,  other ]\n     \n     \n       \n          Title: \n          The Price of Scale in Proactive Memory Recall\n         \n          Yichuan Chen ,  Xin Wang  \n\n          Comments: \n          Code and data:  this https URL \n         \n\n\n          Subjects: \n           Computation and Language (cs.CL) ; Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI); Information Retrieval (cs.IR); Machine Learning (stat.ML)\n         \n\n         \n          A long-lived assistant that brings up memories without being asked must decide at every turn whether anything in its store is worth an interruption. We show that this decision is a multiple-testing problem whose difficulty grows with the store size N. On streaming replays of LongMemEval, a similarity threshold tuned to 5% false alarms at N=8 reaches 84-100% at N=2048 across embedding models and cue types, and LoCoMo and LLM judges show it too. We prove that a gate which thresholds the best of N independent irrelevant scores at false-alarm rate alpha operates at a per-item false-positive rate of about -ln(1-alpha)/N, which pins its hit rate to the far left of the single-comparison ROC curve and, under regular variation, makes it regularly varying in N. A finite-pool form of this bound predicts the hit rate of a label-tuned threshold on held-out questions to within 6.1 points. At matched false alarms the deployed score recalls 14-26 points more than raw cosine. A cue-swap conformal gate, which ranks the current cue among other cues on the same store, has a finite-sample false-alarm guarantee under exchangeability. With a background drawn from the user's unfiltered history it keeps false alarms at 3-6% on LongMemEval at the hit rate of a label-tuned threshold; on LoCoMo it overshoots or turns conservative depending on where its reference cues come from. In a chronological replay with the user's own earlier messages as references, it speaks on 6-8% of session-opening turns, against 57% for the deployed gate at N=2048, at about 50 ms per decision on one CPU thread. A cross-encoder reranker pays the same price of scale, and on a prospective-memory benchmark, similarity gates recall at most 9% of constraints at controlled false alarms. One human and two LLM annotators judge most false alarms unhelpful for a response. Code and data:  this https URL \n         \n       \n     \n     \n       [600] \n       \n        arXiv:2610.35229\n       \n          (replaced)\n\n        [ pdf ,  html ,  other ]\n     \n     \n       \n          Title: \n          Joint Latent Reasoning and Observation for Agentic Video Understanding\n         \n          Junzhang Liu ,  Hao Yuan ,  Yiming Wu ,  Qi Zhang ,  Long Chen ,  Zhecan Wang  \n\n\n\n          Subjects: \n           Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV) ; Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)\n         \n\n         \n          Effective video agents must learn not only how to answer, but also where to look next. Transferring this capability to smaller models is challenging because each observation serves two purposes: supporting an answer and guiding the search for further evidence. Textual descriptions may capture what is currently visible while omitting cues needed for subsequent acquisition. We introduce Latent Agentic Reasoning for Video Analysis (LARVA), a framework that distills multi-round video agents through jointly learned latent reasoning and visual observations. Rather than communicating through captions, an observer passes compact continuous representations to a reasoner that selects the next acquisition action or produces the final answer. Crucially, action and answer supervision propagate through this interface, training the observer to communicate information useful for downstream decisions. Observation reconstruction and reasoning-distribution alignment provide auxiliary supervision from textual teacher trajectories. Across five video-understanding benchmarks, LARVA achieves strong performance with 4B and 7B backbones. On VideoHolmes, Video-MME, and LVBench, the 7B model outperforms a textual CoT student trained on the same teacher trajectories by 5.1, 2.5, and 3.7 percentage points, respectively. These results highlight the value of jointly learning how agents represent evidence and act on it when distilling video-understanding capabilities.\n         \n       \n     \n     \n       [601] \n       \n        arXiv:2610.36340\n       \n          (replaced)\n\n        [ pdf ,  html ,  other ]\n     \n     \n       \n          Title: \n          CrowdCC: Jointly Benchmarking Corruption Robustness and Deployment Quantization in Crowd Counting\n         \n          S Mahmudul Hasan ,  Lu Peng  \n\n          Comments: \n          Accepted in WACV 2027; Project page:  this https URL \n         \n\n\n          Subjects: \n           Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV) ; Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)\n         \n\n         \n          Crowd-counting models deployed on edge devices face two challenges: inputs are degraded along the capture and transmission pipeline, while models are often quantized to satisfy resource constraints of the edge. Yet corruption robustness and deployment quantization are typically evaluated separately. We introduce CrowdCC, a controlled benchmark that jointly evaluates these effects across 23 deployment-motivated corruptions at three severity levels, four crowd-counting datasets, and 26 models. We evaluate TensorRT engines at FP32, FP16, and post-training INT8 precision on an A100 GPU, alongside throughput and energy measurements on corresponding Jetson Orin~NX configurations. The corruptions span six families, with head annotations adjusted when geometry or visibility changes. Our results show that capture-chain corruptions consistently cause the largest degradation across evaluated models, while crowd density affects absolute and relative errors differently. Accuracy on clean images does not reliably predict performance on the corresponding corrupted images. INT8 changes corruption robustness more than FP16, without a consistent direction, and does not consistently improve latency or energy efficiency. INT8 inference efficiency is strongly associated with reformat overhead in the compiled engine, with slowdowns also observed under limited INT8 coverage. Precision changes corrupted-error rankings and robustness--cost frontiers, highlighting the need to consider corruption robustness and deployment cost jointly when selecting models and precision. The code is available at  this https URL .\n         \n       \n     \n     \n       [602] \n       \n        arXiv:2610.36410\n       \n          (replaced)\n\n        [ pdf ,  html ,  other ]\n     \n     \n       \n          Title: \n          Expert lithological labels do not improve learning when used to predict marine sediment properties\n         \n          John M. Aiken ,  William Gilpin ,  Thorsten W. Becker  \n\n\n\n          Subjects: \n           Geophysics (physics.geo-ph) ; Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)\n         \n\n         \n          Scientific drilling has produced large archives of physical, chemical and magnetic measurements linked to descriptions of the rock recovered. These archives are mostly incomplete: instruments sample at different intervals, recovery is partial, and destructive analyses preclude later measurement of the same material. We train an self-supervised embedding model on the LILY ocean drilling database using six routinely logged properties (bulk density, magnetic susceptibility, natural gamma radiation, and red, green and blue reflectance) across 236,963 observations from 296 boreholes, with a masked variational autoencoder that has a heteroscedastic Gaussian decoder and learnable mask tokens. The embeddings separate geologist-assigned lithologies as well as a variant trained with those labels, and increasing the weight on the labels degrades reconstruction while leaving classification unchanged. The labels are recoverable from the measurements geologists use to assign them, so training on them adds nothing. Lithological categories compress continuous measurements into classes, and compressing them discards what the measurements record about the processes that produced the rock. The embeddings also predict physical properties that were absent from training, on boreholes the model has never seen. While depth should account for this since sediments compact with burial, it does not. On held-out boreholes depth alone reaches a median $R^2$ of -0.02, against 0.20 for the embedding.\n         \n       \n     \n     \n       [603] \n       \n        arXiv:2610.36477\n       \n          (replaced)\n\n        [ pdf ,  html ,  other ]\n     \n     \n       \n          Title: \n          Proposing Better Rollouts for On-Policy Distillation\n         \n          Sirui Chen ,  Yunzhe Qi ,  Haobo Xu ,  Jiaru Zou ,  Mengting Ai ,  Zihao Li ,  Jingrui He  \n\n\n\n          Subjects: \n           Machine Learning (cs.LG) ; Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)\n         \n\n         \n          On-policy distillation (OPD) improves language-model post-training by letting the student learn from its own sampled trajectories under teacher supervision, aligning the training process with inference-time behavior. However, the rollout policy in OPD is usually treated as fixed: each round samples directly from the current student. We show that this choice can create a compounding teacher--student mismatch, because it requires the teacher to provide effective supervision on whichever trajectories the student samples, including ones less reachable by the teacher itself. On such trajectories, teacher supervision can become noisy or even misleading, leading to undesirable student patterns such as repetition. Moreover, since the updated student in turn defines the rollout policy of subsequent rounds, these patterns persist and potentially amplify over training. This failure mode suggests that improving OPD requires treating the rollout policy itself as an optimization target, rather than only modifying the distillation objective. We propose Mixture Proposal On-Policy Distillation (MPOPD), which derives a student-anchored, teacher-guided rollout distribution from a local trust-region objective based on relative teacher support. The resulting target is a geometric mixture of the student and teacher policies, and we introduce a proposal policy that approximates this mixture, enabling rollout generation in a single forward pass. Experiments on 16 math, knowledge, and medical reasoning benchmarks show consistent gains over OPD and recent variants. On math reasoning, our method improves average accuracy by 6.91 over OPD on Qwen3-4B-Base while reducing average generation length by 41%, suggesting that rollout policy design provides an effective way to improve OPD.\n         \n       \n     \n     \n       [604] \n       \n        arXiv:2610.37517\n       \n          (replaced)\n\n        [ pdf ,  html ,  other ]\n     \n     \n       \n          Title: \n          Orthogonal and Parametrized Multi-Attribute Steering of LLMs\n         \n          Remy Ogasawara ,  Cullen Anderson ,  Narmeen Fatimah Oozeer ,  Foad Namjoo ,  Amir Abdullah ,  Jens Kristian Refsgaard Schou ,  Jeff M. Phillips  \n\n\n\n          Subjects: \n           Machine Learning (cs.LG) ; Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)\n         \n\n         \n          Activation steering offers an effective and efficient way to modify an LLM's behavior at inference time. However, it remains unclear how to apply steering such that the signal is consistently strong and the steering strength has a universally interpretable meaning. We address this first by identifying the most steerable interval for each steering vector and rescaling the steering range accordingly. Building on this, we introduce a more interpretable method for steering, called parameterized steering which instead of pushing activations in a direction, it sets them at the desired trait strength. Next, with multi-trait steering, we find that naively combining vectors causes them to interfere with one another. In response, we develop a method to orthogonalize the vectors; it decouples these traits, allowing a model to exhibit one behavior without inducing the others, thus enabling multiple traits to be steered simultaneously.\n         \n       \n     \n     \n       [605] \n       \n        arXiv:2610.37584\n       \n          (replaced)\n\n        [ pdf ,  html ,  other ]\n     \n     \n       \n          Title: \n          REGER: Modeling inter-item Relations with Semantic IDs for Generative Recommendation\n         \n          Yuxi Huang ,  Silin Zhou ,  Chenhao Wang ,  Zihan Li ,  Gangming Guo ,  Pei Ke ,  Shen Gao ,  Shuo Shang  \n\n          Comments: \n          13 pages, 6 figures\n         \n\n\n          Subjects: \n           Information Retrieval (cs.IR) ; Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)\n         \n\n         \n          Personalized recommendation depends on learning semantic and collaborative relations among items from online content and user interactions. Generative recommendation (GR) offers a promising paradigm by autoregressively generating discrete semantic IDs (SIDs) of items. However, randomly initialized SID token embeddings and GR models trained from scratch do not directly inherit the semantic relations inherent in items, while inter-item collaborative relations are learned largely implicitly through token-level attention and next-token supervision. To address this limitation, we propose REGER, a unified framework that explicitly incorporates inter-item relations into semantic pretraining, history encoding, and SID generation. We pretrain the GR model through generative semantic retrieval, enabling it to inherit inter-item semantic similarity by learning to generate semantic neighbors' SIDs. We combine global attention with local attention aligned with SID boundaries to capture historical context and directed transitions between adjacent items. We further develop a theoretical framework for hierarchical preference decomposition over the SID prefix tree, proving an exact factorization of collaborative preference distributions and a weighted decomposition of item-level KL divergence into prefix-conditional KL divergences. Building on this analysis, we distill a discriminative teacher's full conditional distributions over all legal next tokens at ground-truth prefixes along the target SID path, jointly optimizing this objective with cross-entropy. Extensive experiments on real-world datasets demonstrate consistent improvements over existing GR baselines and support the effectiveness of the proposed preference distillation.\n         \n       \n     \n \n\n\n\n\n       Total of 605 entries \n     \n     Showing up to 2000 entries per page:\n           \n      fewer \n |\n           more \n |\n           all \n\n     \n\n \n        \n     \n\n \n   \n     \n       \n        We gratefully acknowledge support from our   major funders  ,\n          member institutions   ,    ,\n        and all contributors.\n       \n       \n         About   &middot; \n         Help   &middot; \n         Contact   &middot; \n         Subscribe   &middot; \n         Copyright   &middot; \n         Privacy   &middot; \n         Accessibility   &middot; \n         Operational Status  (opens in new tab)  \n       \n     \n   \n    \n\n \n\n "
