 "page:https://artificialanalysis.ai/articles/gpt-6-1-terra-pushes-the-cost-efficiency-frontier": {
  "v": 2,
  "fetched_at": "2026-10-31T20:52:18.030Z",
  "bytes": 4881,
  "text": "                                                  GPT-6.1 Terra pushes the cost efficiency frontier | Artificial Analysis                             Artificial Analysis         K             Artificial Analysis        Models    Coding Agents    Image, Speech, Video    Inference    Leaderboards    About        AI Trends               K                 All articles   October 26, 2026     OpenAI has released GPT-6.1 Terra, pushing the cost efficiency frontier. It is the first Terra-class model since GPT-5.6, and is more intelligent for less than a third of the cost  See model page  \n     \n Pricing per token is half or less that of GPT-5.6 Terra - at $1/$5 per million input/output tokens vs $2/$12 for the previous model. Cache write has also halved, now at $1.25 per million tokens. Cached input is one quarter at $0.05 vs $0.20 per million tokens. \n  Key takeaways:  \n  ➤ Pareto-optimal for Intelligence vs Cost at low and medium effort:  At max effort, GPT-6.1 Terra gains 6 points in the Artificial Analysis Intelligence Index over GPT-5.6 Terra, at less than a third of the cost per task ($0.40 vs $1.40). At lower effort levels the gain is larger, between 7 and 13 points. The low and medium effort levels are Pareto-optimal for cost efficiency, while the other effort levels sit behind the frontier. At max effort, the model scores 4 points below GPT-6.1 Sol and 10 points above GPT-6 Luna in the Intelligence Index. \n  ➤ Pushes the Coding Agent Index cost efficiency frontier:  With a score of 48 in the newly released Artificial Analysis Coding Agent Index v2.0, the model is 6 points below GPT-6.1 Sol (54) and 17 points above GPT-6 Luna (31). The xhigh and low effort settings sit on the frontier of cost efficiency in the Coding Agent Index, while other effort levels are behind the frontier. \n  ➤ 50 point drop in hallucination rate:  GPT-6.1 Terra guesses 38% of the time when it does not know the answer to a question in AA-Omniscience, our private knowledge and hallucination benchmark. Previously, GPT-5.6 Terra's hallucination rate was 88%. This significant reduction in hallucination is coupled with a 2 point increase in AA-Omniscience accuracy - meaning the model simultaneously answers more questions correctly. \n  ➤ 20 point jump in Terminal-Bench 4.0, small improvements across most evaluations:  The model makes significant progress in the terminal-based agentic benchmark, Terminal-Bench 4.0, with a gain of ~20 points, now rivaling GPT-6.1 Sol at max effort. Gains across most other evaluations are smaller, for example, 5 points in GDP.pdf. \n     \n With a score of 48 in the Artificial Analysis Coding Agent Index v2.0, GPT-6.1 Terra is 6 points below GPT-6.1 Sol (54) and 17 points above GPT-6 Luna (31). The xhigh and low effort settings sit on the frontier of cost efficiency. \n     \n GPT-6.1 Terra shows a 50 point drop in hallucination rate, measured in AA-Omniscience. This is coupled with a 2 point increase in AA-Omniscience accuracy - meaning the model simultaneously answers more questions correctly. \n     \n The model makes significant progress in the terminal-based agentic benchmark, Terminal-Bench 4.0, with a gain of ~20 points, now rivaling GPT-6.1 Sol at max effort. \n     \n Breakdown of the individual evaluations in the Artificial Analysis Intelligence Index v4.3.2 \n Compare GPT-6.1 Terra with other leading models at:  https://artificialanalysis.ai/models/releases/gpt-6-1-terra       Read the latest         Gemini 4 Carbon is the new #1 on the Artificial Analysis Intelligence Index v5  Google’s Gemini 4 Carbon is the new #1 on the Artificial Analysis Intelligence Index v5, and second only to Claude Opus 5.5 on the Artificial Analysis Coding Agent Index  October 28, 2026          GitHub Copilot Fusion orchestrates Anthropic and OpenAI models to balance performance and cost  itHub has released Copilot Fusion in GitHub Copilot, which orchestrates between Anthropic and OpenAI models. It scores 2 points below Opus 5.5 in Claude Code at half the Cost per Task in the Coding Agent Index  October 28, 2026          Announcing Artificial Analysis Intelligence Index v5  Index v5 features 2 new benchmarks and 3 upgrades. For the first time, the majority of the Index is built on private datasets  October 27, 2026                          Artificial Analysis    Get notified about new articles      Email address   Subscribe            Artificial Analysis     Explore    LLM Leaderboard    Image Arena    Video Arena    AI Agents    Evaluations      Products    Optima    MicroEvals    Model Recommender    Data Playground    Image Lab      Company    About    Methodology    Contact    Articles    Careers         X     LinkedIn     YouTube     Rednote     Discord      ©  2026   Artificial Analysis  Terms of Use  Data Platform Terms  Privacy Policy    English                                                             "
 },
 "page:https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.32110": {
  "v": 2,
  "fetched_at": "2026-10-31T20:52:18.809Z",
  "bytes": 10519,
  "text": " \n \n\n     [2610.32110] Who Verifies the Tools? How Warped Formatters Mislead Agents and Their Verifiers \n   \n   \n   \n   \n   \n   \n   \n   \n   \n   \n   \n \n    \n    \n    \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n          \n   \n   \n   \n          \n \n\n  \n     Skip to main content \n  \n \n \n \n    \n   \n     \n   \n   \n     \n     Search \n   \n   \n     \n   \n   \n     Submit \n     Donate \n      \n Log in    \n \n \n   \n     \n       Search arXiv \n       \n       \n       \n     \n     \n      Press Enter to search &middot;  Advanced search \n     \n   \n \n\n     \n       \n \n     \n     \n--> \n\n   \n     \n       Computer Science > Artificial Intelligence \n     \n\n     \n       arXiv:2610.32110  (cs)\n     \n \n \n   \n     \n  [Submitted on 23 Oct 2026] \n      Title: Who Verifies the Tools? How Warped Formatters Mislead Agents and Their Verifiers \n      Authors:  William Jeffries               View a PDF of the paper titled Who Verifies the Tools? How Warped Formatters Mislead Agents and Their Verifiers, by William Jeffries \n     View PDF \n     HTML (experimental) \n\n\n\n     \n             Abstract: Agents and verifiers often rely on the text a tool prints, and that text can misrepresent what the tool actually did, even when the tool's computation is correct. We saw this happen in a production RAG research system used by the Pilsudski Institute in New York, where tool output formatters misled an agent into giving wrong or incomplete information to users. Each tool did its job correctly, but its output omitted search limits, result caps, or identifier namespaces. We also recreated an unlabeled estimate caught in code review before release. We studied whether these formatters misled agents and verifiers and how much the repaired wording helps. We fed tool results to agents using two formatter versions: one with the key omissions and one with all missing details clearly stated. That difference alone changed what the agents claimed and what the verifiers accepted. Unsupported agent claims dropped substantially for three of the four failure types. In one test, verifiers went from accepting incorrect answers 90 out of 90 times to rejecting them 90 out of 90 times. A control showed the verifiers could catch the errors once they were given the missing facts and asked to judge correctness. We include the audit prompt we used with Codex and Claude Code in an exploratory demonstration, written with prior knowledge of our own system's defects. When pointed at an old version of our own system, it rediscovered the defects that were still live and surfaced twelve more we had not catalogued. When pointed at OpenClaw and Hermes, two of the best-known open-source agentic systems, it found warped formatters in both. We opened PRs to fix both, and the OpenClaw fix has already been merged. Practitioners can run the same prompt on their own systems today.\n     \n\n     \n     \n                \n           Comments: \n           Accepted as a poster at the Who Verifies the Agents? workshop at NeurIPS 2026 (non-archival). 12 pages, 3 figures. Code and data:  this https URL  \n         \n \n           Subjects: \n           \n             Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI) ; Software Engineering (cs.SE) \n          \n           Cite as: \n             arXiv:2610.32110  [cs.AI]  \n         \n         \n             \n           (or  \n               arXiv:2610.32110v1  [cs.AI]  for this version)\n           \n         \n         \n             \n                          https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2610.32110  \n               \n                 \n                 Focus to learn more \n               \n               \n               \n                                    arXiv-issued DOI via DataCite (pending registration) \n             \n           \n          \n     \n   \n \n     \n       Submission history  From: William Jeffries [ view email ]            [v1] \n        Fri, 23 Oct 2026 03:22:12 UTC (41 KB) \n \n   \n   \n      \n        \n       Full-text links: \n       Access Paper: \n       \n   \nView a PDF of the paper titled Who Verifies the Tools? How Warped Formatters Mislead Agents and Their Verifiers, by William Jeffries   View PDF    HTML (experimental)    TeX Source\n    \n        \n           \n           view license \n          \n     \n          \n       Additional Features \n       \n          Audio Summary  \n       \n     \n     \n     Current browse context: \n   cs.AI \n\n   \n \n       < prev \n     \n       |        \n       next > \n      \n    \n     new \n      |  \n     recent \n      |   2026-10 \n    \n    Change to browse by:\n     \n         cs  \n         cs.SE  \n     \n   \n\n     \n       \n         References & Citations \n         \n            SciX    Google Scholar  \n            Semantic Scholar  \n         \n          \n       \n\n \n     export BibTeX citation \n     Loading... \n \n\n \n     \n         \n             BibTeX formatted citation \n             &times; \n         \n         \n             loading... \n         \n         \n             Data provided by:  \n              \n         \n     \n  \n    Bookmark   \n     \n   \n   \n     \n   \n    \n   \n \n \n    \n     Bibliographic Tools \n     \n       Bibliographic and Citation Tools \n       \n         \n           \n             \n               \n                \n               Bibliographic Explorer Toggle \n             \n           \n           \n             Bibliographic Explorer   ( What is the Explorer? ) \n           \n         \n         \n           \n             \n               \n                \n               Connected Papers Toggle \n             \n           \n           \n             Connected Papers   ( What is Connected Papers? ) \n           \n          \n           \n             \n               \n                \n               Litmaps Toggle \n             \n           \n           \n             Litmaps   ( What