(s) have the worst performance overall? | No ranked protected-group or demographic-feature assessment is available. The reviewed evaluations report benchmark- and language-specific performance, but they do not isolate demographic features. |\n| If using internal data, description of methods implemented in data acquisition or processing, if any, to address the prevalence of identifiable biases in the training, testing, and validation data: | To bolster coverage and sampling choices, the training recipe uses explicit mixture weights across task and language sources, includes multiple multilingual and multi-speaker datasets, and includes a balanced language-identification component spanning 48 language labels, distinct from the 40-language core speech coverage described in the overview. |\n| Tools used to assess statistical imbalances and highlight patterns that may introduce bias into AI models: | Indexed-sampler progress and answer-token-mass reports were used to inspect mixture exposure, and benchmark scorers report language- and subset-level errors. |\n| Tools used to assess statistical imbalances and highlight patterns that may introduce bias into AI models: | The reviewed inventory does not establish proportional coverage across age, gender, ethnicity, disability, dialect, accent, or recording environment. These attributes must not be inferred from voices to fill that gap. Instead, obtain appropriate evaluation data and report subgroup sizes, uncertainty estimates, and error types explicitly. Before making support claims, test for overlapping speech, distant microphones, code-switching, and underrepresented languages. Where transcription errors can disadvantage users, retain human correction paths. |\n\n## Explainability\n\n| Field | Response |\n| --- | --- |\n| Intended Task/Domain: | Speech transcription and translation, contextual and speaker-attributed transcription, rich transcription, language identification, and audio/text question answering. |\n| Model Type: | Speech/audio encoder with speaker-aware perception, projection to a mixture-of-experts autoregressive language model, and joint MTP. |\n| Intended Users: | Developers and researchers building speech and audio applications with application-specific evaluation and review. |\n| Output: | Generated text, optionally containing task-specific speaker/event tags or PII annotations. When timestamp capture is enabled in vLLM, the response also includes [word timestamps, diarization segments, and a mapping between transcript speaker tags and diarization speakers](#word-timestamps-and-speaker-diarization). Conversation history must be supplied by the application; persistent memory is not established by this recipe. |\n| Tools evaluating synthetic data and authenticity | The pipeline includes transcript classification, annotation/span checks, multiple-model-agreement based high quality filtering metrics, and judge based filtering for preference transcription instruction answers. |\n| Describe how the model works: | The model preprocesses 16-kHz audio and encodes it into features. The configured speaker-aware encoder contributes speaker information. Features pass through the perception projection and are combined with the text prompt. The language backbone predicts output text autoregressively. When requested, the bundled CTC heads align the generated transcript to the audio, and the speaker encoder supplies diarization segments. Two trained MTP depths supply auxiliary training objectives, and the repeated layer can be reused for speculative decoding. |\n| List the groups (or group characteristics) for which this was tested to produce comparable outcomes: | Not Applicable |\n| Technical Limitations & Mitigation: | Errors include substitutions, deletions, repetitions, hallucinated speech, incorrect language choice, and speaker attribution errors. Targeted testing is recommended for long recordings, overlapping speakers, noise, names, code-switching, and misleading context. Performance on text-only tasks, without audio input, is not as good as on audio tasks: text-only reasoning and instruction following were not a primary training objective, and the model scores below its base language model on text-only benchmarks. Audio understanding, reasoning, and spoken instruction following were evaluated only in English and may not work reliably in other languages. |\n| Verified to meet prescribed NVIDIA quality standards: | Yes. |\n| Performance Metrics: | The evaluation plan covers WER/CER and corpus-specific variants; context-biasing WER, named-entity WER, named-entity false-negative rate, and entity F1; multi-speaker cpWER/tcpWER; translation BLEU/spBLEU/COMET; language-identification accuracy; word-level timestamp accuracy; instruction-following scores; benchmark-specific hallucination and event metrics; and serving latency/throughput. |\n| Potential Known Risks: | The model may misrepresent a recording and generate a plausible transcript or answer. Speaker tags may not always attribute statements to the correct person.<br><br>When context is supplied, it can influence the output toward incorrect or irrelevant terms, and the model's robustness to misleading context has not been fully established across all supported settings. Any explanations the model generates should not be taken as verified causal accounts of the model's prediction.<br><br>The model was optimized explicitly for instruction following and as such is more susceptible to prompt injection and jailbreaking in various forms as a result of its instruction tuning. This means that the model should be paired with additional rails or system filtering to limit exposure to instructions from malicious sources -- either directly or indirectly by retrieval (e.g. via visiting a website) -- as they may yield outputs that can lead to harmful, system-level outcomes up to and including remote code execution in agentic systems when effective security controls including guardrails are not in place. The model may generate answers that may be inaccurate, omit key information, include irrelevant or redundant text, or produce socially unacceptable or undesirable text, even if the prompt itself does not include anything explicitly offensive. |\n| Licensing: | Use of this model is governed by the [OpenMDW License Agreement, version 1.1 (OpenMDW-1.1)](https://openmdw.ai/license/1-1/). Users are responsible for complying with this license and with any third-party licenses or notices applicable to components included in the distributed model materials. |\n\n## Privacy\n\nThe datasets used to train Nemotron-3.5-Transcribe included personal data. NVIDIA collected and used this data in compliance with applicable data protection and privacy laws. NVIDIA uses a combination of filters, data minimization techniques, and other guardrails to help prevent personal data from being recited by our models. In addition, we employ automated tools and data processing techniques during pre-training or training to identify and filter certain categories of personal data. The datasets used to train this model were reviewed; the dataset is initially reviewed upon addition, and subsequent reviews are conducted as needed or upon request for changes. Please review NVIDIA’s Privacy Policy (https://www.nvidia.com/en-us/about-nvidia/privacy-policy/) for more information.\n\n## Safety & Security\n\n| Field | Response |\n| --- | --- |\n| Model Application Field(s): | Customer Service |\n| Describe the life critical impact (if present). | Not Applicable |\n| Use Case Restrictions: | Use of this model is governed by the [OpenMDW License Agreement, version 1.1 (OpenMDW-1.1)](https://openmdw.ai/license/1-1/). Users are responsible for complying with this license and with any third-party licenses or notices applicable to components included in the distributed model materials. |\n| Model and dataset restrictions: | The Principle of least privilege (PoLP) is applied limiting access for dataset generation and model development. Restrictions enforce dataset access during training, and dataset license constraints adhered to. |\n"
 },
 "page:https://dmux.github.io/minilake/": {
  "v": 2,
  "fetched_at": "2026-10-31T20:52:28.573Z",
  "bytes": 14727,
  "text": " \n \n \n \n \n MiniLake — a local Databricks API emulator \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n\n \n   \n      MiniLake \n     \n       Features \n       Examples \n       How it works \n       For agents \n       Coverage \n       Open source \n     \n     \n       \n         \n         \n       \n       Docs \n       \n         \n         GitHub \n       \n     \n     \n       \n     \n   \n \n\n \n\n \n \n   \n     \n       \n            Open source · MIT · Made by data engineers, for data engineers \n         The Databricks API,  running on your laptop.  \n         \n          MiniLake is a Databricks API emulator.\n          SQL runs on DuckDB, Delta tables are real files, and jobs run  spark-submit  in a container.\n          Point  databricks-sdk , the Databricks CLI, Terraform or an MCP agent at\n           http://localhost:8000 ; any token is accepted.\n         \n          Open source today, and it will stay that way.  \n\n         \n           \n             $ \n             pip install minilake && minilake --port 8000 \n             PyPI \n             \n               \n              Copy\n             \n           \n           \n             $ \n             docker run -p 8000:8000 ghcr.io/dmux/minilake:latest \n             GHCR \n             \n               \n              Copy\n             \n           \n         \n\n         \n           \n            Get started\n             \n           \n           See it in code \n         \n         Then open  http://localhost:8000/ui/  for the built-in SQL workspace. The image downloads nothing at runtime — it works air-gapped. \n       \n\n       \n         \n       \n     \n   \n \n\n \n \n   \n     \n       What it does \n       The API is emulated; the engines behind it are not. \n       SQL executes on DuckDB, Delta tables are written to disk, and jobs run  spark-submit . Everything else in the API is documented below with its status. \n     \n\n     \n       \n           \n         Unity Catalog \n         Catalogs, schemas, tables and volumes with full CRUD. Every catalog is its own DuckDB database file, so  catalog.schema.table  addressing is native — not a regex rewrite. \n       \n       \n           \n         SQL on DuckDB \n         Statement execution on real DuckDB:  INLINE  and  EXTERNAL_LINKS  dispositions,  JSON_ARRAY  /  ARROW_STREAM  /  CSV  formats, and a result manifest that carries true column types. \n       \n       \n           \n         Delta Lake \n         EXTERNAL Delta tables are genuine Parquet plus a  _delta_log/ .  INSERT ,  UPDATE  and  DELETE  are routed through a real Spark job; reads go through DuckDB's  delta_scan() . \n       \n       \n           \n         Jobs \n          spark_python_task  runs  spark-submit  in a sibling container and returns actual stdout. Real DAG scheduling honours  depends_on  and  run_if , with independent branches in parallel. \n       \n       \n           \n         Terraform & Asset Bundles \n         Point the  databricks  provider at localhost and  terraform apply  creates real catalogs. Verified on every test run: the real provider is driven through  apply ,  plan  and  destroy , and a second plan has to come back empty.  databricks bundle deploy  and  bundle run  work end to end — jobs execute for real. \n       \n       \n           \n         Designed for CI \n         One  POST /_minilake/reset  gives every test a clean workspace without restarting the container.  /health ,  /ready  and  /services  round out the control plane. \n       \n     \n   \n \n \n \n   \n     \n       \n         Zero code changes \n         Change the host. That's the migration. \n         \n          MiniLake speaks the Databricks REST API on the wire, so every client you already use keeps working —\n          the same calls, the same response shapes, the same typed errors.\n         \n         \n              Any token is accepted.  No sign-up, no PAT, no OAuth dance to develop against.  \n              Types round-trip.   STRING ,  LONG ,  DECIMAL(10,2) ,  ARRAY<T> ,  MAP  and  STRUCT  map to DuckDB and come back as the SDK's  ColumnInfo .  \n              Physical truth wins.  Declared column types that disagree with the files lose, so mismatches are visible instead of believed.  \n              Persistence when you want it.   MINILAKE_PERSIST=1  snapshots state on shutdown and restores it on start.  \n         \n       \n\n       \n         \n           Python SDK \n           Spark & Delta \n           Terraform \n           Asset Bundles \n           curl \n         \n\n         \n           \n                    \n             quickstart.py \n             \n               \n              Copy\n             \n           \n\n            from databricks.sdk import WorkspaceClient\nfrom databricks.sdk.service.catalog import ColumnInfo, TableType\n\n# The only line that differs from production\nw = WorkspaceClient(host=\"http://localhost:8000\", token=\"dev\")\n\nw.catalogs.create(name=\"sales\")\nw.schemas.create(name=\"store\", catalog_name=\"sales\")\nw.tables.create(\n    name=\"orders\", catalog_name=\"sales\", schema_name=\"store\",\n    table_type=TableType.MANAGED,\n    data_source_format=\"DELTA\", storage_location=\"/data/sales/store/orders\",\n    columns=[\n        ColumnInfo(name=\"id\", type_text=\"BIGINT\"),\n        ColumnInfo(name=\"customer\", type_text=\"STRING\"),\n        ColumnInfo(name=\"amount\", type_text=\"DECIMAL(10,2)\"),\n    ],\n)\n\nwh = w.warehouses.create(name=\"dev\", cluster_size=\"Small\")\nw.statement_execution.execute_statement(\n    warehouse_id=wh.id,\n    statement=\"INSERT INTO sales.store.orders VALUES (1, 'Ana', 459.90)\",\n)\n\nresult = w.statement_execution.execute_statement(\n    warehouse_id=wh.id,\n    statement=\"SELECT customer, amount FROM sales.store.orders\",\n)\nprint(result.result.data_array)   # [['Ana', '459.90']] — really stored  \n\n            from pyspark.sql import SparkSession\n\nUC = \"http://localhost:8000\"\n\nspark = (\n    SparkSession.builder\n    .config(\"spark.sql.extensions\", \"io.delta.sql.DeltaSparkSessionExtension\")\n    # Unity Catalog connector — three-part names resolve against MiniLake\n    .config(\"spark.sql.catalog.spark_catalog\", \"io.unitycatalog.spark.UCSingleCatalog\")\n    .config(\"spark.sql.catalog.spark_catalog.uri\", UC)\n    .config(\"spark.sql.catalog.sales\", \"io.unitycatalog.spark.UCSingleCatalog\")\n    .config(\"spark.sql.catalog.sales.uri\", UC)\n    .getOrCreate()\n)\n\n# Real Delta files: parquet + _delta_log/, written by real Spark\nspark.createDataFrame([(1, \"a\"), (2, \"b\")], [\"id\", \"name\"]) \\\n     .write.format(\"delta\").mode(\"overwrite\").save(\"/data/delta/sales/store/events\")\n\nspark.sql(\"INSERT INTO sales.store.events VALUES (3, 'c')\")\nprint(spark.table(\"sales.store.events\").count())   # 3\n\n# ...and the same bytes are readable through MiniLake's SQL API:\n#   SELECT e.name, o.customer\n#   FROM sales.store.events e JOIN sales.store.orders o ON o.id = e.id  \n\n            provider \"databricks\" {\n  host  = \"http://localhost:8000\"\n  token = \"dev\"\n}\n\nresource \"databricks_catalog\" \"sandbox\" {\n  name    = \"sandbox\"\n  comment = \"Local sandbox catalog\"\n}\n\nresource \"databricks_schema\" \"things\" {\n  catalog_name = databricks_catalog.sandbox.name\n  name         = \"things\"\n}\n\nresource \"databricks_sql_endpoint\" \"compute\" {\n  name         = \"local-compute\"\n  cluster_size = \"Small\"\n}\n\n# terraform apply  -> creates real catalogs and real DuckDB databases\n# terraform destroy -> removes them\n# Resources backed by APIs MiniLake does not emulate fail loudly with 501,\n# instead of silently pretending to succeed.  \n\n            bundle:\n  name: my_pipeline\n\ntargets:\n  dev:\n    mode: development\n    workspace:\n      host: http://localhost:8000\n\nresources:\n  jobs:\n    daily_load:\n      name: daily_load\n      tasks:\n        - task_key: main\n          spark_python_task:\n            python_file: ../src/load.py\n\n# $ databricks bundle deploy -t dev\n# $ databricks bundle run daily_load -t dev\n#\n# The CLI syncs files through the Workspace API, the provider creates the job,\n# and `bundle run` executes it on real Spark in a sibling container —\n# returning the job's actual output.  \n\n            # Is it up, and which services answered?\ncurl http://localhost:8000/_minilake/health\n\n# Create a catalog through the Databricks API surface\ncurl -X POST http://localhost:8000/api/2.1/unity-catalog/catalogs \\\n  -H 'Content-Type: application/json' \\\n  -d '{\"name\": \"sales\"}'\n\n# Run SQL and get real rows back\ncurl -X POST http://localhost:8000/api/2.0/sql/statements \\\n  -H 'Content-Type: application/json' \\\n  -d '{\"warehouse_id\": \"dev\", \"statement\": \"SELECT 42 AS answer\"}'\n\n# Give the next test a clean workspace — no restart needed\ncurl -X POST http://localhost:8000/_minilake/reset  \n         \n       \n     \n   \n \n\n \n \n   \n     \n       How it works \n       One process. One port. Real engines underneath. \n       A FastAPI application serves the Databricks REST surface, the web UI and the MCP endpoint together. Behind it sit DuckDB, real Delta files on disk, and Docker for Spark. \n     \n\n     \n       \n     \n   \n \n\n \n \n   \n     \n       \n         \n           For LLM agents \n           An MCP server for agents. \n           \n            Optional, at  /mcp . Several tools collapse multi-call sequences into one, and a resource\n            describes the DuckDB SQL dialect so agents stop writing Spark SQL. Tools go through MiniLake's own\n            ASGI stack, so an agent sees the same errors an SDK client would.\n           \n           \n             setup_fixture() catalog + schema + warehouse in one idempotent call  \n             seed_table() table + rows, exactly the rows you asked for  \n             describe_catalog_tree() the whole hierarchy instead of dozens of calls  \n             run_python_script() stage, submit to real Spark, poll, return stdout  \n           \n           \n             MCP documentation \n             All tools \n           \n         \n\n         \n           \n             \n                      \n               register + use \n             \n              # One line to register it with Claude Code\nclaude mcp add --transport http minilake http://localhost:8000/mcp\n\n# Then just ask:\n#   \"Use minilake: create a sales catalog with a seeded\n#    orders table, and tell me the total.\"\n\n# The agent does this — three calls, no glue code:\n#   setup_fixture({\"catalog\": \"sales\", \"schema\": \"store\"})\n#   seed_table({\"full_name\": \"sales.store.orders\", ...})\n#   run_sql({\"statement\": \"SELECT SUM(amount) FROM sales.store.orders\"})\n\n# Enable it on the server side:\nMINILAKE_MCP=1 minilake --port 8000  \n           \n         \n       \n     \n   \n \n\n \n \n   \n     \n       Coverage \n       What's emulated. \n       Anything out of scope answers  501 NOT_IMPLEMENTED  instead of faking a success. The full per-service list is in  FEATURES.md . \n     \n\n     \n       \n         \n            Service  Status  What that means  \n         \n         \n            Unity Catalog   Real   Catalogs, schemas, tables, volumes, functions — each catalog is its own DuckDB database, with native three-part addressing  \n            SQL statements   Real   Executed on DuckDB; all three formats and both dispositions, with a typed result manifest  \n            EXTERNAL Delta tables   Real   Genuine Delta files; writes go through a generated Spark job, reads through  delta_scan()   \n            Jobs   Real    spark-submit  in sibling containers (or a subprocess fallback), real DAG scheduling, real logs, one-shot  runs/submit   \n            Workspace, DBFS & Files   Real   File-backed storage with chunked upload; raw-bytes sync is what makes  bundle deploy  work  \n            Clusters   State machine   CRUD plus timed lifecycle transitions — deliberately no Spark compute; that lives in Jobs  \n            Permissions   Allow-all CRUD   Real create/read/update, but nothing is ever enforced — this is a single-user tool  \n            Identity & SCIM   Unenforced CRUD   Users, groups and service principals, with SCIM filtering and  PATCH  — records, not credentials; any token is still accepted  \n            DLT, Model Registry, Vector Search, Lakeview, Repos   Not implemented   Returns  501 NOT_IMPLEMENTED  — loudly, so you never mistake it for working  \n         \n       \n     \n   \n \n\n \n \n   \n     \n       Limits \n       What MiniLake is not. \n       These are deliberate design decisions, not a backlog. MiniLake targets one developer running it locally — not a shared, multi-tenant server. \n     \n     \n        No authentication  Any token is accepted and there is exactly one user. Never expose it beyond localhost.  \n        No access control  The Permissions API is real CRUD that always allows everything — a passing test here says nothing about grants in a real workspace.  \n        DuckDB, not Spark SQL  Statements run on DuckDB's dialect. The MCP server ships a dialect resource for exactly this reason; the docs carry the translation table.  \n        No Spark on clusters  The cluster lifecycle is a state machine. Real Spark happens through Jobs, in sibling containers.  \n        Single process, no HA  DuckDB is single-writer, so concurrent load serializes on a lock. Fine for dev and CI; not a deployment target.  \n        MANAGED tables are invisible to Spark  They are DuckDB tables with no files. Use EXTERNAL Delta when Spark has to see the data — that trade-off is what keeps SQL fast.  \n     \n   \n \n\n \n \n   \n     \n       Open source \n       MIT licensed, developed in the open, and it stays that way. \n       MiniLake is written by data engineers for data engineers. The scope is what pipeline and CI work needs, and the limits above are listed because we would want them listed. \n     \n     \n        MIT license  Use it, fork it, ship it.  \n        Developed on GitHub  Source, CI, changelog and issues are public at  dmux/minilake .  \n        Contributions welcome  Report the SDK call that fails in an  issue , or add an API group following  CONTRIBUTING.md .  \n     \n     \n       \n         \n         \n         \n       \n     \n     Everyone who has committed to the project.  See all contributors . \n   \n \n\n \n\n \n   \n     \n       \n          MiniLake \n         A free, open-source local Databricks API emulator for offline development and testing. \n       \n       \n         Get started \n         \n            Getting started  \n            Configuration  \n            Web UI  \n            Container image  \n         \n       \n       \n         Build with it \n         \n            Databricks SDK  \n            Terraform & Bundles  \n            Spark & Delta Lake  \n            MCP server  \n         \n       \n       \n         Project \n         \n            Feature status  \n            Testing & development  \n            Contributing  \n            Changelog  \n         \n       \n     \n     \n       MIT licensed · Open source, and staying that way · Not affiliated with or endorsed by Databricks, Inc. \n     \n   \n \n\n \n \n \n"
 },
