 "page:https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50316531": {
  "v": 2,
  "fetched_at": "2026-10-31T21:02:53.888Z",
  "bytes": 50547,
  "text": "        Malware uses LLMs to rank victims by how much they're worth | Hacker News                  Hacker News   new  |  past  |  comments  |  ask  |  show  |  jobs  |  submit      login                           Malware uses LLMs to rank victims by how much they're worth   (  talosintelligence.com  )          211 points  by  khalidkhair    6 hours ago      |  hide  |  past  |  favorite  |  116 comments                          help    \n      \n                    akersten    4 hours ago       |  next   [–]       \n  This is the kind of stuff that keeps me up at night. Claude, ArtCraft, tons of other auto-downloading tools, are rolling 10'000's of code changes a month. If one compromised update snuck this guy in, it's kind of game over. The windows of opportunity for an attack like this seem to be widening the faster we \"develop\" software. Makes me want to have like 5 separate computers: - one for banking only - one for development only - one for web browsing only - one for gaming only - one for socials only How else can you stay safe against a threat that exfiltrates your entire digital life in like 10 seconds?      reply                             heresie-dabord    0 minutes ago       |  parent  |  next   [–]       \n  > Makes me want to have like 5 separate computers This is a good, practical strategy. You definitely want to at least have a separate computer for finances and taxes.      reply                             DotNetCorePowa    4 hours ago       |  parent  |  prev  |  next   [–]       \n  You can thank the npm and the like. Before, people were responsible for the dependencies they were importing. With npm and all the javascripters flooding the market, it became normal to be irresponsible \"because everybody is doing it\". Fast-forward a couple of years, now everything is web based, even the stand-alone app version. No one knows what is running on own computer, even the Devs themselves don't know what the are asking you to run... With a pinch of vibe coding on top, and thats the end. We took 40 years for our industry to be taken seriously, it's all gone now.      reply                             hibikir    3 hours ago       |  root  |  parent  |  next   [–]       \n  Shared code repositories for OSS are much older than npm. See at least maven central for Java. What makes npm a more relevant target is a much smaller standard library and thinner dependencies, leading to more chances that a random project will have code of suspicious provenance. The vast majority of what a backend Java project depends on was probably written either by the same 3 companies, or large, established OSS repos. So this is relatively solvable if people in steering committees decided they cared. It's improving, but I'd argue not fast enough.      reply                             ctkhn    2 hours ago       |  root  |  parent  |  next   [–]       \n  I noticed this a lot with java where most of what I needed that wasn't specific to our company's business logic was already available in the standard library, and a lot of the OSS libraries I needed were relatively reputable and well-known. I did a few personal backend projects with js and the amount of functionality I had to crate-dig from npm drove me insane, on top of the more complicated chain of trust for how many layers packages from npm can have.      reply                             hoppp    3 hours ago       |  root  |  parent  |  prev  |  next   [–]       \n  Generally we don't hear much about attacks in Java like that. But they probably exist but python and javascript are targeted mainly because everyone uses them. They cover a lot of ground.      reply                             td521534f12    2 hours ago       |  root  |  parent  |  next   [–]       \n  A big reason Python and Java are juicy targets are because for a very long time pip and npm executed code on the user's machine on package install, not just on import. (This is changing in newer versions of pip and npm IIRC)      reply                             eddythompson80    1 hour ago       |  root  |  parent  |  next   [–]       \n  I think it's mostly about attach surface. There is nothing fundamentally different in the threat model or security boundary between any language in terms of how they manage their dependencies. Even platforms like Maven or NuGet that don't have an install script, you're still likely to trigger a build or a quick run for the application right after installing that would trigger code execution anyway. The chances of a person getting hit during the `npm install` phase is bigger but not that different than the `dotnet add package ... && dotnet run`. I do agree though that the massive surface of npmjs, pip, and even cargo means that package signing is also not realistic. If you have the average 300 npm packages, no one is gonna pay attention to the authenticity of 200 different signing keys. It would be a lot easier with something like java where you probably just need to trust a handful like openjdk, jackson, junit, or log4j keys and be 90% there. (And hope the supply chain attack hasn't compromised the signing keys too of course)      reply                             bigfatkitten    48 minutes ago       |  root  |  parent  |  prev  |  next   [–]       \n  There’s also been a regression in how infrastructure is operated. 15 years ago you didn’t let your production infrastructure talk out to the Internet, and if you did, it was via an explicit proxy with allowlisted domains. This significantly limited the damage that a malicious dependency could cause. With the move to cloud and DevOps, that network security model disappeared.      reply                             odo1242    9 minutes ago       |  root  |  parent  |  next   [–]       \n  Cloud does let you do this with VPCs and subnets. Some people don't bother and end up susceptible but it remains best practice even for cloud software      reply                             dpe82    3 hours ago       |  root  |  parent  |  prev  |  next   [–]       \n  There's nothing new under the sun, as they say. Perl had CPAN already in 1993; Linux and common BSD distros have basically always had package repositories. \"People were responsible for the dependencies they were importing\" - I don't know about that. Plenty of us were irresponsible 30 years ago.      reply                             malfist    3 hours ago       |  root  |  parent  |  prev  |  next   [–]       \n  > Before, people were responsible for the dependencies they were importing. This is an attitude not reflective of reality for those that wrote code in the pre npm days      reply                             jbm    3 hours ago       |  root  |  parent  |  next   [–]       \n  Yeah, and it isn't even a JavaScript thing either.  The famous \"import antigravity\" strip from XKCD was about python. The fact is we always were dependent on others.      reply                             animal_spirits    3 hours ago       |  root  |  parent  |  next   [–]       \n  That XKCD comic is demonstrative of the opposite of what OP is talking about. Python and JavaScript are on opposite sides of the spectrum about what is included in the language and what is not. Python is “batteries included” which is why you can just “import antigravity” rather than “pip install antigravity”. JavaScript and NPM sit on the other end, where you have to infamously install the leftpad library to pad strings with spaces on their left, which left thousands of packages broken when it was taken off npm.      reply                             lincon127    3 hours ago       |  root  |  parent  |  prev  |  next   [–]       \n  I mean, I don't think the stereotypical JS and Python developer are all that different... at least when it comes to dependency management.      reply                             freecodeio    3 hours ago       |  root  |  parent  |  prev  |  next   [–]       \n  Don't know why you're getting scrutinized, npm didn't introduce malicious packages obviously but it did completely remove the feeling of suspicion and responsibility people had towards third party libraries. The pre 2020 bootcampers would basically npm install anything because that's all they knew. You even had bullshit like \"is-array\" with millions of weekly installs because how dare you reinvent the wheel.      reply                             arctek    2 hours ago       |  root  |  parent  |  next   [–]       \n  I just checked isarray got almost 300m installs in the past week.      reply                             smashburger    3 hours ago       |  root  |  parent  |  prev  |  next   [–]       \n  L take. Can’t blame npm for software shops wanting to run leaner and ship faster. I’m personally glad I don’t have to download separate runtimes and apps and can just use the standard browser      reply                             UltraSane    3 hours ago       |  root  |  parent  |  next   [–]       \n  Except browser based versions take 10x the RAM of native versions.      reply                             cyberclimb    4 hours ago       |  parent  |  prev  |  next   [–]       \n  Try QubesOS if you want this, you can have a separate VM for each isolated use-case. The OS itself is a lightweight hypervisor around the VMs (called \"Qubes\").      reply                             palijer    3 hours ago       |  root  |  parent  |  next   [–]       \n  In the current LLM cybersecurity climate, I honestly can't say with 100% certainty anymore that something like a VM acts as a completely infallible sandbox environment for the next couple decades.      reply                             antonvs    2 hours ago       |  root  |  parent  |  next   [–]       \n  VMs are demonstrably not infallible, but that's not quite the issue here. If you only ever run your banking app inside a VM with an encrypted disk volume, and don't browse sketchy sites or do anything else in there, then you're very well protected against even LLM-assisted attacks. One caveat in that case is I wouldn't keep the password to the VM disk in a password manager, since that then becomes an attack vector. A keylogger would still be a viable attack vector, though. The security of operating systems is going to need to be improved to guard against some of these things, e.g. a protected input mechanism for passwords that can't be intercepted.      reply                             giovannibonetti    1 hour ago       |  root  |  parent  |  next   [–]       \n  Have you heard about \"default allow\" and \"default deny\" in the domain of cyber security? Traditionally PCs, especially for home use, were built around the former idea, where the user supposedly trusts most of the stuff installed. The problem is that malware may sneak in, and then the user needs a horrible piece of software called anti-virus that needs to apply complicated heuristics to try and separate one from the other. It kind of worked ok for many years, but as the world evolved and the dangers kept increasing, little by little we have been going towards the other idea – default deny – where apps are considered harmful until proved otherwise, and the honest app developers have to jump through multiple hoops to get their apps available to users. That's the app store model, which kind of works, but is cumbersome and expensive for the app developer. Anyway, going back to the VM discussion, I think we may end up applying the default deny idea further into the OS Kernel to reduce those risks further. After all, a small kernel has a much smaller risk surface and perhaps it can be formally verified to be secure under some reasonable assumptions.      reply                             rwmj    3 hours ago       |  root  |  parent  |  prev  |  next   [–]       \n  I think this is one of those situations where you don't need to outrun the tiger, you just need to be better at security than 99% of people, which is definitely the case if you're partitioning your life into VMs. I wonder if/when mainstream OSes will start doing this.      reply                             BlobberSnobber    3 hours ago       |  root  |  parent  |  prev  |  next   [–]       \n  The idea is awesome, the implementation looks great as well, but hardware support is super limited.      reply                             pull_my_finger    1 hour ago       |  root  |  parent  |  prev  |  next   [–]       \n  Isn't the container ecosystem basically the same problem with more packages? You pull an OS package, a language runtime package, etc etc. It's something I've been thinking about also      reply                             TuxSH    4 hours ago       |  parent  |  prev  |  next   [–]       \n  > threat that exfiltrates No need for something new here, Claude Code (Auto + default settings) has a tendency of running \"find\"/\"ls\" on your home folder when it struggles with solving a problem. I wish I was joking. Now of course people trust these AI companies not to do bad things(tm), but all it takes is replacing the model endpoint URL -or any kind of harness compromise- and nothing will seem amiss      reply                             mystifyingpoi    4 hours ago       |  root  |  parent  |  next   [–]       \n  > has a tendency of running \"find\"/\"ls\" on your home folder I've seen this first-hand. I was doing some work in a repository, Claude concluded it needs to investigate, how some AWS resources are created, and it literally started scanning ~/* for Terraform code. Which it found and started browsing through. Or, when I asked Claude to debug some Spring issue, corporate firewall blocked Maven Central, so it literally started scavenging ~/.m2 caches to find the jar it desperately needed. Then it could not find it, so it started to clone Spring sources from GitHub to build the missing jar. I stopped the clone mid-way. I wish I was joking, I only stepped away from the keyboard for one minute.      reply                             genxy    3 hours ago       |  root  |  parent  |  next   [–]       \n  It accidentally made a compiler because of an ambiguous prompt. And yes it will find \"helpful\" code on other parts of the system. Which means claude most likely has a `find ~` of millions of developers home folders      reply                             leafletsigh    4 hours ago       |  parent  |  prev  |  next   [–]       \n  Yea. Fedora has been one of my favorite OS's for years, but exactly what you describe \"If one compromised update snuck this guy in..\" has spooked me to something with a much slower release cadence. I guess I accept that more unpatched known vulns stick around, but yea when I start thinking about how many software packages -> the number of devs, build systems, etc. It just gets overwhelming thinking about how many threat actors are probably trying to get in from each avenue. I'd love to hear other takes on this though... Current thought is to just use something with a whole lot of exposure, in a whole lot of environments, pretty far downstream so the package hits all of that exposure, and hope someone spots it, kinda like xz. I tend to keep my base OS extremely minimal and do all my real work in VMs.      reply                             jgraettinger1    2 hours ago       |  root  |  parent  |  next   [–]       \n  The contra is that “stable” releases are now a liability, because it takes longer for upstream fixes to be back-ported. Recently, for bugs that are quickly exploitable in the wild. A recent example is the TCP options use-after-free, where it took Canonical a couple days to patch LTS beyond when the fix was first available in Fedora 45. And these are just for public CVEs. I’m also worried that there are a lot of quiet fixes being made with new agentic review tooling, which may fix an undiscovered bug but will never be back-ported.      reply                             leafletsigh    1 hour ago       |  root  |  parent  |  next   [–]       \n  Yea I should have put it in my first post