hose within the same computer (say I have my \"important accounts\" computer that has some virtualbox VMs with each a browser with only some of those). Gaming is a windows PC that has only games (so who cares) and a ps5 (so I can rent games at the library for free), development is a linux PC (where I containerize most things, as well as firejail etc.), web browsing is an x86 imac, I don't do socials, I also have a completely separate linux PC for art/drawing. The vast majority of these has been bought for a couple hundred dollars 2nd hand from ebay or local university surplus (say they have all the time m1/16gb/500gb for $300-$400 imacs, or dell 16gb towers, or tons of dell laptops), the only expensive one was the linux development one as I bought a 5950x/128gb when it came out, but it has served me well, as long as you stay away from windows/mac you can go very far with really cheap hardware (DDR3 is plenty for banking / browsing / socials / factorio).      reply                             darkwi11ow    2 hours ago       |  parent  |  prev  |  next   [–]       \n  I do it like this, but on one computer, using QEMU. I have 3 different VMs for - development & AI tools - infra rollouts - banking / other sensitive services I have Steam Deck for gaming, and do most of casual web browsing still on host, because it's secure enough to use Firefox with ublock + privacy badger and enforcing SELinux. However, I dont use socials other than HN and Reddit under pseudonymous accounts. If I had real zuckerbook or instacrap accounts, maybe I'd have another VM for socials too.      reply                             codedokode    2 hours ago       |  root  |  parent  |  next   [–]       \n  Why do you people love over-engineering and bad architectures so much? Do you have any sense of engineering beauty? Modern CPUs already run every application in an isolated sandbox under \"protected mode\". There is no need to emulate a machine with hardware devices, run a separate kernel, driver