 "page:https://jepsen.io/analyses/particledb-1.1.4": {
  "v": 2,
  "fetched_at": "2026-10-31T21:02:54.808Z",
  "bytes": 125156,
  "text": "   \n     \n     Jepsen: ParticleDB 1.1.4 \n     \n     \n     \n     \n     \n\n     \n     \n     \n\n     \n   \n\n   \n     \n       \n          JEPSEN  \n       \n\n         Blog  Analyses  Talks  Consistency  Services    Ethics   \n\n      \n\n        \n   \n     \n   \n\n       ParticleDB 1.1.4 \n    \n     \n       \n        Kyle Kingsbury\n       \n\n               2026-10-31 \n           \n   \n\n   \n      ParticleDB  is a multi-model\n    database designed for AI workloads. We tested versions 1.1.4 through\n    1.1.18.11.rc-22 and found that ParticleDB’s claims of “Full ACID”\n    were somewhat premature. We present 96 issues in ParticleDB, ranging\n    from the pedestrian (crashes, deadlocks, data loss, etc.) to the\n    piquant (partially-invisible rows which could not be deleted,\n     WHERE 4 = 5 , dropped tables re-emerging in distorted\n    forms, confused columns, strings turning into integers, and so on).\n    Most issues required just a single server, single client, and no\n    faults. By version 1.1.18.11.rc-22, all but ten of those issues\n    appeared resolved. However, ParticleDB continued to lose committed\n    writes during normal operation with the default  occ \n    transaction mode, and allowed internal anomalies and Lost Update\n    with  serializable , the strongest setting. Orkes, the\n    makers of ParticleDB, have since released version 1.1.19-rc.13;  they\n    claim  that it resolves all remaining issues discussed in this\n    report. This research was funded by Orkes, Inc., and conducted in\n    accordance with the  Jepsen ethics\n    policy . \n   \n\n\n \n   \n  1  Background \n Modern applications often spread state across a variety of systems:\ndatabases, caches, queues, data warehouses, search indices, and so on.\nCoordinating these systems is challenging: caches are often stale, the\nanalytics platform might lag days behind the primary database, the\nsearch index might lose data, and transactional updates across disparate\nsystems  remains\nan open research problem . \n ParticleDB  1   aims to unify these workloads in a\nsingle engine with a special focus on AI agents and applications. It\noffers transactional SQL, a key-value store, vector search, live\nanalytics, and user-defined functions called  Particles .  2   To integrate with existing systems,\nclients can connect through  a variety of\ninterfaces , including the Postgres and Redis protocols, as well as\nits own gRPC and HTTP2 APIs. Written from the ground up in Rust,\nParticleDB claims to be orders of magnitude faster than  Postgres  while preserving the\nsame safety characteristics. \n ParticleDB was built by  Orkes  to\nsupport their workflow orchestration platform,  Conductor .\nConductor was initially built to store data in Redis and Postgres, but\nto improve performance, Orkes opted to write their own database.\nParticleDB 1.0.0 offered a limited feature set specifically for\nConductor’s queries, and was deployed as the backing database for some\nof their Conductor clusters. A few Orkes customers followed suit. In\npreparation for a broader release, Orkes undertook a major refactoring\nof ParticleDB, adding a custom wire protocol, more datatypes, additional\nOLAP queries, and more. While Orkes and their customers continued to run\n1.0.0, they soft launched the refactored versions on  particledb.ai . Orkes engaged Jepsen to\ntest these versions of ParticleDB, beginning with 1.1.4. \n While ParticleDB could run as a single node, it also offered a\nreplicated cluster using the  Raft \nconsensus protocol. This replication feature was available only in\nenterprise cloud deployments. The builds Orkes provided did not support\nreplication, so this report covers only single-node behavior. \n  1.1  Safety \n ParticleDB’s home page  advertised \nthat ParticleDB was “formally proven” with a TLA+ proof. However, during\nour collaboration no TLA+ specification was available. It is unclear\nwhat properties might be proven, or whether they described ParticleDB as\nbuilt. \n ParticleDB offered  three\ntactics  for syncing its Write-Ahead Log (WAL). The  sync \nmode called  fsync  on each commit,  groupsync \nbatched multiple transactions into a single  fsync , and\n nosync  skipped  fsync  altogether. The  Transaction\nEngine documentation  described  sync  as offering\n“stronger durability” than  groupsync . On the other hand,\n particledb start --help  said the  groupsync \nmode ensured that “COMMIT waits for flush”, and Orkes’ internal operator\nguide concurred:  groupsync  offered the “same durability as\n sync ”. \n ParticleDB’s home page proudly claimed “Full ACID. 159K TPS.”\nHowever, the text immediately below this said “snapshot isolation”,\nwhich is  not fully\nisolated . \n Most SQL databases use the\n SET TRANSACTION ISOLATION LEVEL  statement to control the\nconcurrent semantics of a transaction. However, ParticleDB  ignored\nor rejected these statements . In some cases it instructed users to\nchoose  READ COMMITTED , a relatively weak isolation level.\nIn other cases it accepted requests for different isolation levels, and\nsilently ignored them (despite documentation to the contrary). \n Instead, transaction isolation was governed by the\n --txn-mode  setting passed at startup. The transaction\nengine documentation  described\nfour modes : \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n Mode \n Behavior \n When to use \n \n \n \n \n  fast  \n No locks; commit-on-write \n Bulk loads, single-writer benchmarks \n \n \n  occ  \n Overlay + commit-time OCC validation\n(default) \n General OLTP — safe under concurrency \n \n \n  zero-copy-occ  \n Partition-pinned OCC; single-partition\ntxns skip cross-partition coordination \n High-throughput OLTP on partition-affine\nworkloads (TPC-C, key-value) \n \n \n  table-2pl  \n Exclusive table locks at first touch \n Strictest serialization, simple\nsemantics \n \n \n \n This table was difficult to interpret. While the  best\npractices documentation  warned that users should “pick\n --txn-mode  deliberately”, since “it changes correctness and\nthroughput characteristics more than SQL text does”, the documentation\ndid not explain what these modes actually guaranteed. The\n fast  mode was clearly intended for weaker isolation, but\nwas that Read Atomic, Read Uncommitted, or something else? The\n occ  mode was similarly unclear. “Commit-time OCC\nvalidation” could describe a broad spectrum of consistency models from\nRead Uncommitted to Strong Serializable, but “safe under concurrency”\nsuggests it might be Serializable. The  zero-copy-occ  mode\ndescribed a performance optimization which could be as safe as\n occ , but it was  not  described as “safe under\nconcurrency”—raising the question: in what ways was it unsafe? Finally,\nif  table-2pl  offered the “strictest serialization”, was\n occ  some weaker isolation level? \n The  CLI\nreference page  contained another table which described\n occ  and  table-2pl  as “Jepsen-safe”, whereas\n fast  was not “Jepsen-safe”, but did not explain their\nsafety properties. Since Jepsen evaluates safety with respect to\ndocumentation and marketing claims, it is not clear how to interpret\nthese terms.  3   \n Running  particledb start --help  provided the following\ntable: \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n Mode \n Description \n \n \n \n \n  fast  \n No locks, maximum OLTP throughput (unsafe\nunder concurrency) \n \n \n  occ  \n Table locks + commit-time validation (safe\nfor most OLTP) \n \n \n  row-2pl  \n Alias for  occ  \n \n \n  zero-copy-occ  \n Partition-pinned OCC (highest throughput\nsafe mode) \n \n \n  table-2pl  \n Exclusive table locks, full serialization\n(strictest) \n \n \n  serializable  \n Alias for  table-2pl  \n \n \n  serializable-ssi  \n Adaptive SSI (Cahill 2008), PL-3 with\noptimistic semantics (v1 scaffolding — see SSI_ADAPTIVE_DESIGN.md) \n \n \n \n The point of Optimistic Concurrency Control (OCC) is generally to\n avoid  taking locks, so the mention of table locks and a\n row-2pl   4   alias was surprising. Moreover,\n“safe for most OLTP” undermines the claim that  occ  was\n“safe under concurrency”; it suggests there are some OLTP workloads\nwhich are unsafe! \n Orkes explained that this documentation was wrong:  occ \nwas actually optimistic, and the  row-2pl  alias was to\npreserve compatibility with an earlier two-phase locking mechanism. They\nalso provided an unpublished operator guide with detailed descriptions\nof the various transaction modes. This document was  difficult\nto interpret , but did specify the following consistency models: \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n Mode \n Consistency Model \n \n \n \n \n  fast  \n Unknown \n \n \n  occ-v3  (a.k.a.\n occ ,  occ-v2 ,  row-2pl ) \n Snapshot Isolation \n \n \n  zero-copy-occ  (a.k.a.\n zocc ) \n Snapshot Isolation \n \n \n  serializable-ssi  (a.k.a.\n ssi ) \n Serializable \n \n \n  table-2pl  (a.k.a.\n serializable ) \n Strong Serializable \n \n \n \n Sources disagreed about the default transaction and WAL sync modes,\nbut in general,  occ-v3  and  groupsync  appeared\nto be the default. \n  2  Test Design \n We wrote a  test\nsuite  for ParticleDB using the  Jepsen testing library .\nWe ran ParticleDB versions 1.1.4 through 1.1.18.11.rc-22 on individual\nDebian 13 nodes, both in containers and EC2 VMs. Our test harness could\nsimulate process crashes by killing ParticleDB, simulate the loss of\nun- fsync ed writes using  LazyFS , and truncate or\ncorrupt disk files. We ran ParticleDB using each transaction mode and\nall three WAL sync modes. \n We first tried ParticleDB’s official Java SDK. While the  documentation\nindicated  that the library was available as a Maven dependency,\nneither the JAR nor source code were actually available. Instead, we\nbegan with the official Postgres JDBC client, version 42.7.11, and later\nadded support for the  ParticleDB Java SDK ,\nversions 1.0.0 through 1.0.2. \n  2.1  Workloads \n We used several Jepsen workloads from other SQL databases, and\ndesigned new ones specifically for ParticleDB. \n The   rw \nworkload  performed randomly generated transactions involving reads\nand writes of integer values identified by unique integer keys. We\nstored each value in a single row and accessed them both via primary and\nunindexed secondary keys. Keys and values were encoded as a variety of\ndatatypes, including integers, decimals, and strings. We assumed that\nwrites followed reads within each transaction, and that individual keys\nwere sequentially consistent, and used these assumptions to infer\nconstraints on the  version orders and\ntransaction dependency graphs . Jepsen’s transactional checker,  Elle , then searched for\ncycles in that dependency graph, as well as a variety of non-cyclic\nphenomena like  G1a\n(Aborted Read) . \n The   append  \nworkload also performed randomly generated transactions over rows\nidentified by unique integer keys, but the values were lists of\nintegers. Transactions could read one of these lists by key, or append a\nunique integer to a list by key. We encoded each list as a\ncomma-separated  text  field, and used SQL\n concat  to append to them. Rows could be selected via\nprimary key or unindexed secondary key, or via a join to an indirection\ntable. From there, we used Elle to infer version orders and dependency\ngraphs, and searched for a variety of cyclic and non-cyclic\nphenomena. \n When these workloads revealed consistency violations within a single\ntransaction, we designed an   internal  \nworkload to stress internal consistency. This workload performed a\nseries of randomly generated transactions involving  INSERT ,\n UPDATE ,  DELETE , and  SELECT \nstatements over a simple key-value table, and verified that within each\nindividual transaction, reads observed prior writes. \n To investigate tantalizing anomalies in the  internal \nworkload, we designed the   compare  \nworkload. This workload used Clojure’s  test.check  generative\ntesting library to  produce \nrandom tables and simple SQL statements over them, then executed those\nstatements against both ParticleDB and PostgreSQL. If ParticleDB’s\nbehavior was  not\nequivalent  to PostgreSQL’s, the compare workload would  search \nfor a simpler sequence of statements that could reproduce the same\ndivergence. Although our SQL generator was relatively simple—just a few\nbasic datatypes, literals, arithmetic, comparisons, boolean logic,\ntransactions, and some simple  LIMIT ,  OFFSET ,\nand  ORDER BY  clauses—it proved remarkably fruitful. \n Drawing on these successes, we devised a hybrid   internal-sim  \nworkload to search for violations of internal consistency in more\ncomplex transactions. This workload  generated \na random set of tables, then concurrently executed  random\ntransactions  over them. It then  checked\neach transaction  independently to ensure that each of its statements\nobserved results consistent with those preceding. For example, if a\ntransaction executed  DELETE ... WHERE 2 < c , then\nobserved a row with  c = 3 , we could prove that transaction\nwas not internally consistent. \n  3  Results \n We found 96 issues with ParticleDB, which we present in several\ncategories below. All but three of these issues occurred in healthy,\nsingle-node instances of ParticleDB. Busy readers may wish to jump to\nthe  Discussion  section, which summarizes our\nfindings. \n \n  Availability and\nPerformance  \n  Broken Features  \n  Predicates  \n  Constraints  \n  Joins  \n  Zombies  \n  State Corruption  \n  Transactions and\nConcurrency  \n  With Faults  \n \n Additional results are presented in  Appendix\nA : \n \n  Type Safety and Syntax  \n  Arithmetic  \n  Strings  \n \n  3.1  Availability and\nPerformance \n Under normal operation, ParticleDB was slow, deadlocked, and crashed\nwithout warning. \n  3.1.1  Abysmal Performance With\n table-2pl  (#62) \n In ParticleDB 1.1.4 through 1.1.18.4,\n --txn-mode table-2pl  was so slow as to be essentially\nunusable. While some transactions might complete in a few milliseconds,\nmost took 10, 20, or even 30 seconds, then failed. \n For example, take  this\ntest run  with a single node running 1.1.18.4, transaction mode\n table-2pl , and WAL mode  groupsync . With ten\nconcurrent clients, most transactions blocked for tens of seconds before\nthrowing\n could not serialize access due to concurrent update , or an\nIO error. \n High latencies ( #62 )\nappeared improved by 1.1.18.6, which could execute transactions with\nsub-100 millisecond latencies for at least a few seconds. \n  3.1.2  Mystery Crash (#81) \n While 1.1.18.6 improved latencies, it also crashed frequently.\nCrashes occurred spontaneously with various transaction modes, WAL\nmodes, and client interfaces; there was no obvious cause. Nodes would\nrun for a few hundred to a few thousand seconds before exiting. No error\nmessages were printed. These crashes ( #81 ) no\nlonger appeared in 1.1.18.11.rc-18. \n  3.1.3  Deadlock After Two\nTransactions (#27) \n In ParticleDB 1.1.18.8, submitting as few as two concurrent\ntransactions to a healthy, single-node deployment could cause it to\ndeadlock. This occurred both using the Postgres and ParticleDB\ninterfaces. The node would log an error message like\n [DEADLOCK] 1 cycles detected at SystemTime ... , and an\nempty backtrace. In some cases new clients could attach and perform\ntransactions; in others, new clients would also hang indefinitely. This\nmay reflect multiple deadlock bugs. \n With the  append  workload, version 1.1.18.9 generally\ncould not run for more than 45 seconds without locking up. Version\n1.1.18.11.rc-1 could reliably run for two minutes without lockups, and\nwe declared the issue ( #27 )\nresolved. \n  3.1.4  Deadlock With\n table-2pl  (#71) \n While version 1.1.18.11.rc-1 fixed the most frequent deadlocks, we\ncontinued to find deadlocks through 1.1.18.11.rc-6 when running with\n table-2pl . An otherwise healthy ParticleDB node would\nexecute transactions for about 30 seconds, then get stuck forever. The\nnode would log  a\nsimilar deadlock error message  to #27, and all requests with the\nParticleDB client would throw  PDB JDBC: null  after a 100\nsecond default timeout. These deadlocks ( #71 )\nappeared resolved by 1.1.18.11.rc-7. \n  3.2  Broken Features \n Orkes’  public\ndocumentation  claimed broad support for Postgres features, and the\ninternal operator guide concurred: \n \n The engine speaks PostgreSQL v3 wire protocol natively — anything\nthat speaks pgjdbc / libpq / psycopg2 / pq connects unchanged.\n BEGIN ,  COMMIT ,  ROLLBACK , prepared\nstatements, parameterised queries, and extended-query pipelining all\nwork as expected. \n \n These claims were somewhat optimistic. ParticleDB was missing support\nfor a wide array of Postgres features, and others were badly broken. \n  3.2.1  No Aggregates Within\n CASE  (#20) \n In ParticleDB 1.1.4 through 1.1.18.4, aggregate expressions like\n COUNT(*)  were not supported within  CASE \nexpressions. \n       SELECT  \n      CASE  \n        WHEN   COUNT ( * )  =   2  \n          THEN   'CASE_COUNT_STAR_OK'  \n          ELSE   'CASE_COUNT_STAR_BAD'  \n      END  \n    FROM  t;    \n This threw  Error: cannot evaluate wildcard expression .\nParticleDB gained support for this feature ( #20 ) by\nversion 1.1.18.6. \n  3.2.2  Unsupported Exponentiation\n(#28) \n ParticleDB 1.1.4 through 1.1.18.8 did not support the Postgres\nexponentiation operator: \n      root =>   SELECT   2  ^  3 ; \n   ERROR:  unsupported binary  operator : PGExp    \n Version 1.1.18.9 added support for the  ^  operator ( #28 ). \n  3.2.3  Malformed Responses for\nPrepared Statements (#2) \n In ParticleDB 1.1.4 through 1.1.18.9, any attempt to execute a\nprepared statement using the Postgres JDBC driver caused an exception\nlike\n Execution error (NoSuchElementException) at java.util.ArrayDeque/removeFirst .\nSince the JDBC driver automatically switches to prepared statements\nafter the first few calls to  execute() , this rendered the\ndatabase essentially unusable. As a workaround, we disabled the client’s\nuse of prepared statements. \n Prepared statements are executed using Postgres’  extended\nquery protocol , which takes place in several (potentially reusable)\nphases: Parse, Bind, Execute, and so on. Orkes believes that ParticleDB\nlikely failed to return a response for one of those phases, or returned\nan extra message, causing the client’s queue to underflow. By version\n1.1.18.11.rc-1, this issue ( #2 ) appeared\nfixed. \n  3.2.4  Most Postgres Queries Fail\n(#83) \n In ParticleDB 1.1.18.11.rc-7 through 1.1.18.11.rc-19, many queries\nusing the Postgres interface threw\n Bad value for type long: some_column . This rendered the\ndatabase largely unusable. \n       CREATE   TABLE  ints (a  int ); \n    SELECT   *   FROM  ints; \n      ->  Bad  value   for   type   long  : a    \n Orkes believes this issue ( #83 ) was\nfixed in 1.1.19-rc.12. \n  3.2.5  Tuples Without Fields\n(#111) \n In 1.1.18.11.rc-18 and rc-19, queries with the Postgres interface\noccasionally threw errors like\n Received resultset tuples, but no field structure for them .  5   For example, take  this\ntest  of version 1.1.18.11.rc-19, with no faults, a new\n strict-serializable  transaction mode, and WAL mode\n sync . It executed a transaction like: \n       BEGIN ; \n    SELECT   *   FROM  t_38; \n    INSERT   INTO  u_38 (d, a, c, b)  VALUES  \n     ( null ,  null ,  false ,  \"oudt1THxi\" ); \n    INSERT   INTO  t_38 (d, b)  VALUES  \n     ( false ,  \"BCczDbueXLz9zXnpgbdCeFbC5y6XSEys3\" ); \n    COMMIT ;    \n This complained of resultset tuples with no field structure,\nsuggesting an error in ParticleDB’s Postgres protocol state machine.\nWhile Jepsen has not tested this version, Orkes reports this issue ( #111 ) was\nfixed in version 1.1.19-rc.13. \n  3.2.6  Broken Postgres\nAuthentication (#1) \n The Postgres wire protocol usually authenticates clients using a\nusername and password. ParticleDB had a  --pg-password \noption, but 1.1.4 through 1.1.18.8 lacked any option to set the\nusername.  Some\nof the documentation  indicated the default username was\n admin , while  other \n pages \nsaid it was  particledb . Neither appeared to work; the only\noption was to use  --no-auth  to disable authentication\naltogether. Version 1.1.18.11.rc-1 introduced a\n --pg-username  option ( #1 ). \n  3.2.7  Java Client Could Not Execute\nTransactions (#3) \n Version 1.0.0 of the official ParticleDB Java client refused every\nattempt to execute a transaction. Any call to begin a JDBC transaction,\nor to execute a  BEGIN  statement, would result in an error\nmessage:\n PDB wire error 1064: SQL plan error: transaction/session statements do not produce a query plan . \n ParticleDB 1.1.4 could execute transactions via its Postgres\ninterface, but using the official Java SDK was essentially impossible.\nThis issue ( #3 ) seems to\nhave been resolved by ParticleDB 1.1.15. \n  3.3  Predicates \n ParticleDB struggled to interpret predicates.  WHERE \nclauses failed to match rows they should have, or matched unexpected or\nnonexistent rows. These issues occurred with a single server, single\nclient, and no faults. \n  3.3.1   SELECT  Ignores\n WHERE  Clause (#40) \n In ParticleDB 1.1.4 through 1.1.18.9,  SELECT  queries\nwithout a table accepted a  WHERE  clause, but ignored\nit. \n      root =>   SELECT   5   WHERE   FALSE ; \n     5  \n    ---  \n     5  \n   ( 1   row )    \n This should have returned zero rows. Version 1.1.18.11.rc-1 seems to\nhave resolved this issue ( #40 ). \n  3.3.2 \n WHERE 1 < (a + 2)  (#47) \n Version 1.1.1.18.9 introduced a new problem with predicates: inside a\ntransaction, a  WHERE  clause which performed sufficiently\ncomplex floating-point operations would silently fail to match any rows.\nFor example: \n      root =>   CREATE   TABLE  t (a  real ); \n   root =>   INSERT   INTO  t (a)  VALUES  ( 3 ); \n   root =>   BEGIN ; \n   root =*>   SELECT   *   FROM  t  WHERE   1   <  (a  +   2 ); \n    a \n    ---  \n   ( 0   rows )    \n The same problem applied to  SELECT ,  UPDATE ,\nand  DELETE : predicates like\n WHERE (a * 2) IS NOT NULL  would silently fail to match.\nHowever, simpler predicates like  WHERE a IS NOT NULL  would\nmatch correctly. The effect disappeared if the column was an integer.\nMoreover, some predicates which would throw errors outside a transaction\nwould silently match zero rows within a transaction, suggesting these\npredicates were not being evaluated at all: \n      root =>   SELECT   *   FROM  t  WHERE   1   <  ( 5.0  %  3 ); \n   ERROR:  internal error \n   root =>   BEGIN ; \n   root =*>   SELECT   *   FROM  t  WHERE   1   <  ( 5.0  %  3 ); \n    a \n    ---  \n   ( 0   rows )    \n This issue ( #47 )\nappeared resolved by 1.1.18.11.rc-1. \n  3.3.3   WHERE 4 = 5 \n(#18) \n ParticleDB 1.1.4 through 1.1.17 aggressively interpreted\n WHERE something = x  as a primary-key lookup of\n x , regardless of the value of  something . \n      root =>   CREATE   TABLE  t \n          (a  int   primary   key , b  int ); \n   root =>   INSERT   INTO  t  VALUES  ( 4 ,  5 ), ( 5 ,  6 ); \n   root =>   SELECT   *   FROM  t  WHERE  b  =   4 ; \n    a | b \n    ---+---  \n     4  |  5     \n ParticleDB would ignore a row with  b = 5  in favor of\n a = 5 : \n      root =>   SELECT   *   FROM  t  WHERE  b  =   5 ; \n    a | b \n    ---+---  \n     5  |  6  \n   ( 1   row )    \n You could write predicates over non-existent columns: \n      root =>   SELECT   *   FROM  t \n           WHERE  definitely_not_a_column  =   4 ; \n    a | b \n    ---+----  \n     4  |  5  \n   ( 1   row )    \n Or ask if  4 = 5 :  6   \n      root =>   SELECT   *   FROM  t  WHERE   4   =   5 ; \n    a | b \n    ---+----  \n     5  |  6  \n   ( 1   row )    \n By version 1.1.18.4, ParticleDB had learned that not every equality\ncomparison involved a primary key ( #18 ). \n  3.3.4   SELECT  in a\nTransaction Returns Nonsense (#35) \n The behavior of  SELECT  within a transaction in\nParticleDB versions 1.1.4 through 1.1.18.8 was thoroughly confusing.\nConsider a single client connected to a fresh single-node instance of\nParticleDB 1.1.18.8. It creates a simple table of integers with two\nrows,  1  and  2 , and begins a transaction: \n      root =>   CREATE   TABLE  t (a  INT   UNIQUE ); \n   root =>   INSERT   INTO  t  VALUES  ( 1 ), ( 2 ); \n   root =>   BEGIN ; \n   root =*>   SELECT   *   FROM  t; \n    a \n    ---  \n     1  \n     2  \n   ( 2   rows )    \n So far, so good. But now consider: \n      root =*>   SELECT   *   FROM  t  WHERE  a  =   1 ; \n    a \n    ---  \n     2  \n   ( 1   row ) \n    \n   root =*>   SELECT   *   FROM  t  WHERE  a  =   3 ; \n    a \n    ---  \n     2  \n   ( 1   row ) \n    \n   root =*>   SELECT   *   FROM  t  WHERE  a  IN  ( 1 ,  2 ); \n    a \n    ---  \n     2  \n   ( 1   row ) \n    \n   root =*>   SELECT   *   FROM  t  WHERE   FALSE ; \n    a \n    ---  \n     2  \n   ( 1   row )    \n One might conclude that every  SELECT  with a predicate\nreturned a single row from the requested table—perhaps the most recently\ninserted row. But this is not true, because many predicates evaluating\nto  TRUE  returned nothing. \n      root =*>   SELECT   *   FROM  t  WHERE   TRUE ; \n    a \n    ---  \n   ( 0   rows ) \n    \n   root =*>   SELECT   *   FROM  t  WHERE   3   =   3 ; \n    a \n    ---  \n   ( 0   rows )    \n This behavior ( #35 ) seemed\nimproved by ParticleDB 1.1.18.9. \n  3.4  Constraints \n ParticleDB often violated constraints like  NOT NULL  or\n UNIQUE , even with a single server, single client, and no\nfaults. \n  3.4.1  Not-Null Constraints Allow\nNull (#8) \n While ParticleDB allowed users to add  NOT NULL \nconstraints to columns, versions 1.1.4 through 1.1.17 ignored them,\nresulting in bizarre states. \n      root =>   CREATE   TABLE  t (a  BOOLEAN   NOT   NULL ); \n   root =>   INSERT   INTO  t (a)  VALUES  ( NULL ); \n    INSERT   0   1     \n While the resulting row existed in some sense, it was partly\ninvisible. \n      root =>   SELECT   *   FROM  t; \n    a \n    ---  \n   ( 0   rows ) \n    \n   root =>   SELECT   COUNT ( * )  FROM  t; \n     COUNT  \n    -------  \n         1  \n   ( 1   row ) \n    \n   root =>   SELECT   COUNT ( * )  FROM  t  WHERE  a  IS   NULL ; \n     COUNT  \n    -------  \n         0  \n   ( 1   row )    \n In version 1.1.18.4, ParticleDB started to enforce null constraints\n( #8 ). \n  3.4.2  Unique Constraints Allow\nDuplicates (#9) \n Similarly, while ParticleDB allowed users to add unique constraints\nto columns, versions 1.1.4 through 1.1.18.4 simply ignored them. \n      root =>   CREATE   TABLE  t (a  boolean   UNIQUE ); \n   root =>   INSERT   INTO  t (a)  VALUES  ( TRUE ); \n    INSERT   0   1  \n   root =>   INSERT   INTO  t (a)  VALUES  ( TRUE ); \n    INSERT   0   1  \n   root =>   SELECT   *   FROM  t; \n    a \n    ---  \n    t \n    t \n   ( 2   rows )    \n In version 1.1.18.6, ParticleDB partially enforced unique constraints\n( #9 ). \n  3.4.3  Duplicate Primary Keys\n(#29) \n Although 1.1.18.6 made  UNIQUE  constraints somewhat more\nrobust, 1.1.4 through 1.1.18.8 allowed an  UPDATE  to produce\nmultiple rows with the same primary key. \n      root =>   CREATE   TABLE  t ( id  text  PRIMARY   KEY , \n                           desc  text); \n   root =>   INSERT   INTO  t  VALUES  ( 'a' ,  'a' ); \n   root =>   INSERT   INTO  t  VALUES  ( 'b' ,  'b' ); \n   root =>   UPDATE  t  SET   id   =   'c' ; \n    UPDATE   2     \n Selecting a row by primary key returned only one of the two rows, but\nother predicates could return both: \n      root =>   SELECT   *   FROM  t  WHERE   id   =   'c' ; \n     id  |  desc  \n    ----+------  \n    c  | b \n   ( 1   row ) \n    \n   root =>   SELECT   *   FROM  t  WHERE   id   LIKE   'c' ; \n     id  |  desc  \n    ----+------  \n    c  | a \n    c  | b    \n This issue ( #29 ) appears\nto have been fixed by 1.1.18.9. \n  3.4.4  Null Primary Keys (#57) \n Per the SQL standard, primary keys may not be null. This makes\nintuitive sense, since  NULL  represents an unknown value.\nParticleDB would not allow you to insert null values for primary keys.\nHowever, versions 1.1.4 through 1.1.18.11.rc-1 were perfectly happy to\n update  the primary key of one (or more!) rows to\n NULL . \n      root =>   CREATE   TABLE  t (a  int   PRIMARY   KEY ); \n   root =>   INSERT   INTO  t (a)  VALUES  ( 3 ), ( 4 ); \n   root =>   UPDATE  t  SET  a  =   NULL ; \n   root =>   SELECT   *   FROM  t; \n    a \n    ---  \n    \n    \n   ( 2   rows )    \n ParticleDB 1.1.18.11.rc-3 rejected attempts to set primary keys to\n NULL  ( #57 ). \n  3.4.5  Primary Key Conflicts with\nDeleted Rows (#82) \n In ParticleDB 1.1.18.4 through 1.1.18.11.rc-11, a transaction which\ndeleted a row, then inserted a new row with the same primary key, would\nabort due to a primary key constraint violation. For example: \n       \n    CREATE   TABLE  t (a  int   PRIMARY   KEY ); \n    INSERT   INTO  t (a)  VALUES  ( 7 ); \n    BEGIN ; \n    DELETE   FROM  t; \n    INSERT   INTO  t (a)  VALUES  ( 7 ); \n    COMMIT ;    \n The second  INSERT  would throw\n PDB wire error 1064: SQL execution error: duplicate key value violates unique constraint (key=7) ,\nbut only when using the ParticleDB interface—the Postgres interface was\nunaffected. Orkes partially addressed this issue ( #82 ) in\nversion 1.1.18.11.rc-9, and fixed another case of it in\n1.1.18.11.rc-18. \n  3.4.6  Lack of Primary Key Conflicts\nwith Extant Rows (#90) \n In ParticleDB 1.1.18.11.rc-9, a transaction executing concurrently\nwith other transactions could read a row with a primary key, then\nsuccessfully insert a row with that same primary key. For example, take\n this\n internal-sim  test , which created a table with two\ninteger columns: \n  CREATE TABLE u_14 (b int, a int PRIMARY KEY);  \n A transaction ran  SELECT * FROM u_14  and observed\nseveral rows, including  {:b 1020026655, :a -1} . It then ran\n INSERT INTO u_14 (b, a) VALUES (-27, -1) , which should have\nconflicted with the extant primary key, but succeeded instead. It went\non to  SELECT * FROM u_14  again, and observed the newly\ninserted row, but not the previously existent one. This is a violation\nof internal consistency. \n As of version 1.1.18.11.rc-19, we no longer observed these internal\nconsistency violations ( #90 ). \n  3.5  Joins \n ParticleDB struggled to join tables together. The following issues\noccurred with a single server, single client, and no faults. \n  3.5.1  Can’t Join Integers to Floats\n(#58) \n Even after agreeing that  2.0 = 2 , ParticleDB 1.1.4\nthrough 1.1.18.9 refused to join two tables on an  int  and a\n real  column: \n      root =>   CREATE   TABLE  t (a  real ); \n   root =>   CREATE   TABLE  u (a  int ); \n   root =>   INSERT   INTO  t (a)  VALUES  ( 2.0 ); \n   root =>   INSERT   INTO  u (a)  VALUES  ( 2 ); \n   root =>   SELECT  t.a  FROM  t  JOIN  u  ON  u.a  =  t.a; \n   ERROR:   join   key  must be  integer   type     \n Databases like Postgres would return a single row here, since\n 2.0 = 2 . ParticleDB gained the ability to join floats to\nintegers ( #58 ) by\n1.1.18.11.rc-1. \n  3.5.2  Incorrect Column Names\n(#54) \n Because two tables can have a column with the same name, SQL\ndatabases generally return both table and column names, so you can tell\nwhich column belonged to which table. ParticleDB, with both the\nParticleDB and Postgres interfaces, neglected to return table names.\nInstead, versions 1.1.4 through 1.1.18.11.rc-1 renamed column\n a  in joined tables to  right.a ,\n right.right.a , and so on. Consider the following simple\njoin across two tables  t  and  u , both with an\ninteger column  a : \n       SELECT   *   FROM  t  FULL   OUTER   JOIN  u \n      ON  t.a  =  u.a;    \n In Postgres this produced rows like: \n      [{ :t/a   1 ,  :u/a   1.0 } \n    { :t/a   2 ,  :u/a   2.0 }]    \n Here the expression  :t/a  denotes column  a \nof table  t . In ParticleDB, this same query returned: \n      [{ :a   1 ,  :right.a   1.0 } \n    { :a   2 ,  :right.a   2.0 }]    \n The table names are completely missing, and ParticleDB has altered\nthe name of column  a  to  right.a . Programs\nwhich expected typical SQL behavior would throw an error as they\nattempted to read columns which were not present, or could silently read\nnull values. \n ParticleDB 1.1.18.11.rc-3 no longer munged columns, but the database\nstill omitted table names. The resulting name collisions destroyed\ninformation. \n      [{ :a   1 } \n    { :a   2 }]    \n While Jepsen has not had a chance to test this version, Orkes claims\nthis issue ( #54 ) was\nresolved by version 1.1.19-rc.13. \n  3.5.3  Coercing to Strings\n(#76) \n In ParticleDB 1.1.4 through 1.1.18.11.rc-19, joins would coerce some\nvalues to strings in ParticleDB’s Postgres interface. For example,\nconsider the following join: \n       CREATE   TABLE  t (a  int ); \n    CREATE   TABLE  u (a  real ); \n    INSERT   INTO  u (a)  VALUES  ( 3.2 ); \n    INSERT   INTO  t (a)  VALUES  ( 5 ); \n    SELECT  u.a  FROM  t  CROSS   JOIN  u;    \n With the ParticleDB interface, this produced the number\n 3.2 . With the Postgres interface, it produced the string\n \"3.2\" . This issue ( #76 ) seemed\nfixed by 1.1.18.11.rc-21. \n  3.5.4  Missing Rows (#59) \n In ParticleDB 1.1.4 through 1.1.18.11.rc-1, outer joins of a\nnon-empty table to an empty one often returned no rows, even though the\nquery should have produced all rows in the non-empty table. For\nexample: \n      root =>   CREATE   TABLE  t (a  int ); \n   root =>   CREATE   TABLE  u (a  int ); \n   root =>   INSERT   INTO  t (a)  VALUES  ( 1 ), ( 2 ); \n   root =>   SELECT  t.a  AS  t, u.a  AS  u \n           FROM  t  LEFT   JOIN  u  ON  u.a  =  t.a; \n    t | u \n    ---+---  \n   ( 0   rows )    \n By version 1.1.18.11.rc-3, ParticleDB correctly returned rows when\njoining to empty tables ( #59 ). \n  3.5.5  Column Confusion (#77) \n Many tables in real databases contain columns with identical names,\nlike  id  or  created_at . When asked to join two\ntables like this, ParticleDB 1.1.4 through 1.1.18.11.rc-6 became\nseverely confused, even when all columns were fully qualified. For\nexample: \n      root =>   CREATE   TABLE  t (a text); \n   root =>   CREATE   TABLE  u (a text); \n   root =>   INSERT   INTO  t  VALUES  ( 'this' ), ( 'that' ); \n   root =>   INSERT   INTO  u  VALUES  \n          ( 'is not the right column' ); \n   root =>   SELECT  t.a  AS  ta, u.a  AS  ua  FROM  t \n           CROSS   JOIN  u  WHERE  (u.a  =   'this' ); \n     ta  |           ua \n    ------+-------------------------  \n    this |  is   not   the   right   column  \n   ( 1   row )    \n This should not have matched anything—there are no rows in table\n u  where  u.a = 'this' . Instead ParticleDB\nmatched a row in table  t  which satisfied\n t.a = 'this' , then cross-joined it to all rows in\n u . To add insult to injury, 1.1.4 through 1.1.18.9 appeared\nto swap around the output columns, producing the following table: \n                 ta            |  ua \n    -------------------------+------  \n     is   not   the   right   column  | this    \n If column  t.a  was of a different type than\n u.a , ParticleDB would try to coerce the types into\ncompatible forms, producing bizarre error messages or delightfully weird\nresults. Version 1.1.18.11.rc-7 was much less confused when joining\ntables containing the same column name ( #77 ). \n  3.6  Zombies \n ParticleDB often wound up with rows that could not be deleted or\nupdated, or which persisted in some (perhaps mangled) form after their\ndeletion. These issues occurred with a single server, single client, and\nno faults. \n  3.6.1  Total Recall (#26) \n In ParticleDB 1.1.17 through 1.1.18.8, it appeared impossible to\ndelete rows within a transaction. For example: \n      root =>   CREATE   TABLE  t (a  boolean ); \n   root =>   INSERT   INTO  t (a)  VALUES  ( TRUE ); \n   root =>   BEGIN ; \n   root =*>   DELETE   FROM  t; \n    DELETE   0  \n   root =*>   COMMIT ; \n   root =>   SELECT   *   FROM  t; \n    a \n    ---  \n    t \n   ( 1   row )    \n We were unable to find a predicate that allowed one to delete records\n( #26 ).\nVersion 1.1.18.9 allowed transactions to mostly  7  \ndelete rows. \n  3.6.2  Memento Delere (#45) \n 1.1.18.9 resolved issue #26, but deleted rows still lingered on in\nsome capacity. While a transaction could not directly select deleted\nrows, it could observe them via aggregate functions and various\npredicates. \n      root =>   CREATE   TABLE  t (a  boolean ); \n   root =>   INSERT   INTO  t (a)  VALUES  ( TRUE ); \n   root =>   BEGIN ; \n   root =*>   DELETE   FROM  t; \n    DELETE   1  \n   root =*>   SELECT   COUNT ( * )  FROM  t  WHERE  a  =   TRUE ; \n     COUNT  \n    -------  \n         1  \n   ( 1   row ) \n    \n   root =*>   SELECT   COUNT ( * )  FROM  t  WHERE  a  =   FALSE ; \n     COUNT  \n    -------  \n         0  \n   ( 1   row )    \n The row must still be present in some sense, because these aggregate\nqueries could observe its value via predicates. However, trying to read\nthe row directly returned nothing: \n      root =*>   SELECT  a  FROM  t  WHERE  a  =   TRUE ; \n    a \n    ---  \n   ( 0   rows )    \n Version 1.1.18.11.rc-1 no longer allowed shades of deleted records to\npersist in aggregate queries ( #45 ). \n  3.6.3  Night of the Living Integers\n(#23) \n In versions 1.1.4 through 1.1.17, updating an integer column could\nmake rows impossible to delete. For example: \n      root =>   CREATE   TABLE  t (i  int ); \n   root =>   INSERT   INTO  t  VALUES  ( 0 ); \n   root =>   UPDATE  t  SET  i  =   1.5 ; \n   root =>   DELETE   FROM  t; \n    DELETE   0  \n   root =>   SELECT   *   FROM  t; \n    i \n    ---  \n     1  \n   ( 1   row )    \n While this row appears to contain the integer  1 , that\ninteger is  special , since the  UPDATE  statement\nprovided the value  1.5 . This row cannot be deleted. It does\nappear in  SELECT , and is apparently equal to\n 1 , rather than  1.5 . Oddly, updating a row by\nprimary key with the same  1.5  value did not create zombies;\nthe problem ( #23 ) was\nsomehow linked to predicates. ParticleDB 1.1.18.4 no longer exhibited\nthis behavior. \n  3.6.4  Team Rocket (#84) \n Tables with a  double precision  column suffered from a\nsimilar problem: they could be updated with integer values, but doing so\nwould produce zombie rows that could neither be updated nor deleted\nagain. \n      ROOT =>   CREATE   TABLE  t (a  double   precision ); \n   ROOT =>   INSERT   INTO  t (a)  VALUES  ( 1.0 ); \n   ROOT =>   UPDATE  t  SET  a  =   2 ; \n   ROOT =>   DELETE   FROM  t; \n    DELETE   0  \n   root =>   SELECT   *   FROM  t; \n    a \n    ---  \n     2  \n   ( 1   row )    \n If the double precision column was a primary key, these updates would\nproduce duplicate copies of the updated row. This issue ( #84 )\naffected ParticleDB 1.1.18.11.rc-1 through 1.1.18.11.rc-8, and was fixed\nin 1.1.18.11.rc-9. \n  3.6.5  A Ghost is Technically Not a\nNumber (#78) \n In ParticleDB 1.1.18.11.rc-1 through 1.1.18.11.rc-6,  NaN \nvalues stored in  real  or  double precision \ncolumns could neither be updated nor deleted. \n       DROP   TABLE   IF   EXISTS  t; \n    CREATE   TABLE  t (a  real ); \n    INSERT   INTO  t (a)  VALUES  ( 'NaN' ); \n    UPDATE  t  SET  a  =   5.0 ; \n      ->  # :next .jdbc{ :update  -  count   0 } \n    DELETE   FROM  t; \n      ->  # :next .jdbc{ :update  -  count   0 } \n    SELECT   *   FROM  t; \n      ->  [{ :a  ##NaN}]    \n This issue ( #78 ) did not\noccur with the  psql  shell, but did happen with both\nParticleDB and Postgres JDBC clients. Version 1.1.18.11.rc-7 seems to\nhave fixed the problem. \n  3.6.6  Beached Strings (#74) \n In ParticleDB 1.1.18.11.rc-1 through 1.1.18.11.rc-6, fixed-width\nstrings were unmodifiable unless created with exactly the right number\nof characters. While ParticleDB would correctly pad the string\n 'hi'  to  'hi ' , the resulting row could neither\nbe updated nor deleted. \n      root =>   CREATE   TABLE  t (a  character ( 3 )); \n   root =>   INSERT   INTO  t (a)  VALUES  ( 'hi' ); \n   root =>   DELETE   FROM  t; \n    DELETE   0  \n   root =>   UPDATE  t  SET  a  =   'sup' ; \n    UPDATE   0  \n   root =>   SELECT   *   FROM  t; \n     a \n    -----  \n    hi \n   ( 1   row )    \n If a fixed-width column was used as a primary key, it could be\nupdated, but only once. Both old and new copies would appear as distinct\nrows. \n       CREATE   TABLE  t \n          (a  character ( 3 )  PRIMARY   KEY ); \n    INSERT   INTO  t (a)  VALUES  ( 'um' ); \n    UPDATE  t  SET  a  =   'no' ; \n    SELECT  a  FROM  t; \n    ->  [{ :a   \"um \" } { :a   \"no \" }]    \n Any further attempt to modify those rows would produce a duplicate\nkey error, despite all the values involved being distinct. Fixed-width\nstrings were also incomparable using the  =  operator.\nVersion 1.1.18.11.rc-7 resolved these issues ( #74 ). \n  3.6.7  Pet Sematary (#46) \n In SQL, the  DROP TABLE  statement destroys a table\ncompletely. In ParticleDB 1.1.18.9 through 1.1.18.11.rc-1, it left a\nshadow of the table behind, whose rows would reappear in later tables\nwith the same name and more-or-less compatible schemata. To demonstrate\nthis behavior, let us create a table with a single row: \n      root =>   CREATE   TABLE  t (a text, b  real ); \n   root =>   BEGIN ; \n   root =*>   INSERT   INTO  t  VALUES  ( 'boo' ,  18.83 ); \n   root =*>   COMMIT ;    \n We then drop the table, and demonstrate that it no longer exists: \n      root =>   DROP   TABLE  t; \n   root =>   SELECT   *   FROM  t; \n   ERROR:   table   not  found    \n If we then create a new table with the same name and a somewhat\ncompatible schema, rows from the deleted table reappear in the new one.\nHowever, the resurrected rows are not exactly their former selves. The\nfloating-point value  18.83  has become the integer\n 19 . \n      root =>   CREATE   TABLE  t (a text, b  int ); \n   root =>   BEGIN ; \n   root =*>   SELECT   *   FROM  t; \n     a  | b \n    -----+----  \n    boo |  19  \n   ( 1   row )    \n This behavior ( #46 ) seemed\nlinked to transactions, and no longer appeared in 1.1.18.11.rc-3. \n  3.7  State Corruption \n ParticleDB had many ways to create values which could not be read,\nalter values unexpectedly, create rows with incorrect or missing\ncolumns, or otherwise corrupt logical state. The issues in this section\ninvolved only a single server, single client, and no faults. \n  3.7.1  Unreadable Infinities\n(#15) \n In versions 1.1.4 through 1.1.17, ParticleDB’s Postgres interface did\nnot correctly serialize floating-point values like\n Infinity . One could write infinite values, but trying to\nread them would cause the client to throw an error. \n      CREATE TABLE t (a real) ;  \n   INSERT INTO t (a) VALUES ( 'Infinity' ) ;  \n   SELECT  *  FROM t \n      => PSQLException:  \"Bad value for type  \n          double : inf\"     \n With Postgres, this correctly returned the JVM’s\n Double/INFINITY  value.  NaN  was serialized\ncorrectly. At the  psql  shell, stored values came back as\n inf , rather than  Infinity . In version\n1.1.18.6, ParticleDB serialized infinite values correctly ( #15 ). \n  3.7.2   NaN  Sometimes\nConverted to 0.0 (#21) \n ParticleDB 1.1.4 through 1.1.18.4 would sometimes (but not always!)\nconvert the floating-point value  NaN  to  0.0 .\nFor example: \n      root =>   CREATE   TABLE  t (a  real ); \n   root =>   INSERT   INTO  t (a)  VALUES  ( 2.5 ); \n   root =>   INSERT   INTO  t (a)  VALUES  ( 'NaN' ); \n   root =>   SELECT   *   FROM  t; \n     a \n    -----  \n     2.5  \n       0  \n   ( 2   rows )    \n Delightfully, whether this  NaN  corruption occurred\ndepended on the order in which rows were inserted. If the first value\ninserted into the table was  NaN , that and later\n NaN s would be preserved. Version 1.1.18.6 seemed to store\n NaN  correctly ( #21 ). \n  3.7.3  Assignment of Incompatible\nTypes (#7) \n In ParticleDB 1.1.4 through 1.1.18.4, you could update a column of\none type to a column of a different, incompatible type—producing values\nlike  null  or  0 . For example: \n      root =>   CREATE   TABLE  t (a  INT , b  BOOLEAN ); \n   root =>   INSERT   INTO  t  VALUES  ( 2 ,  FALSE ); \n   root =>   SELECT   *   FROM  t  WHERE  a  =  b; \n   ERROR:  arrow  compute : Invalid argument error: \n   Invalid comparison operation: Int64  ==   Boolean  \n   root =>   UPDATE  t  SET  a  =  b; \n   root =>   SELECT   *   FROM  t; \n    a | b \n    ---+---  \n     0  | f    \n In Postgres, this UPDATE would throw\n ERROR: column \"a\" is of type text but expression is of type int .\nParticleDB refused to  compare  this string to an integer (at\nleast in this case), but was perfectly happy to make one equal to the\nother, resulting in  NULL . Version 1.1.18.6 seemed to\nresolve this issue ( #7 ). \n  3.7.4  Insert Column Order Table\nCorruption (#16) \n In SQL, one usually inserts a row into a table using a statement like\n INSERT INTO people (name, age) VALUES ('xanthippe', 15) .\nParticleDB 1.1.4 through 1.1.17 ignored the  INSERT \nstatement’s columns, using whatever column order had been used to create\nthe table originally. This produced rows with titillating properties.\nConsider the following pair of inserts: one with columns in the same\norder as the table’s schema, and a second in a different order: \n      root =>   CREATE   TABLE  t \n          (a TEXT  NOT   NULL   PRIMARY   KEY , \n           b  boolean ); \n   root =>   INSERT   INTO  t (a, b)  VALUES  ( 'x' ,  TRUE ); \n   root =>   INSERT   INTO  t (b, a)  VALUES  ( FALSE ,  'y' ); \n   root =>   SELECT   COUNT ( * )  FROM  t; \n     COUNT  \n    -------  \n         2  \n   ( 1   row )    \n While ParticleDB thinks this table contains two rows, neither can be\nread via  SELECT * : \n      root =>   SELECT   *   FROM  t; \n    a | b \n    ---+---  \n   ( 0   rows )    \n In general, any query which happened to encounter a row inserted with\na different column order would silently produce zero results. However,\neach row could be read by primary key! Note the different column orders\nin the output: \n      root =>   SELECT   *   FROM  t  WHERE  a  =   'x' ; \n    a | b \n    ---+---  \n    x | t \n   ( 1   row ) \n    \n   root =>   SELECT   *   FROM  t  WHERE  a  =   'y' ; \n    b | a \n    ---+---  \n    f | y \n   ( 1   row )    \n In the preceding example, the two columns were of (somewhat)\nincompatible types. What happens if the types are compatible? \n      root =>   CREATE   TABLE  t (a  INT , b  REAL ); \n   root =>   INSERT   INTO  t (a, b) \n           VALUES  ( 1 ,  1.23E32 ); \n   root =>   INSERT   INTO  t (b, a) \n           VALUES  ( 2.34E32 ,  2 ); \n   root =>   SELECT   *   FROM  t; \n             a          |    b \n    ---------------------+---------  \n                       1  |  1.23e32  \n     9223372036854775807  |        2  \n   ( 2   rows )    \n Now the table is readable, but the data is corrupt. The second row’s\n a  value appears under  b , and vice-versa. The\nfloating-point value  2.34E32  has become the integer  2 63  − 1 . \n If we attempt to delete every row from the table, something very\nstrange happens. The  DELETE  leaves the differently-ordered\nrow in place, and its originally inserted values now appear intact! \n      root =>   DELETE   FROM  t; \n    DELETE   1  \n   root =>   SELECT   *   FROM  t; \n    a |    b \n    ---+---------  \n     2  |  2.34e32  \n   ( 1   row )    \n A second  DELETE  statement—now that the table contains\nonly rows inserted with column order  (b, a) —could\nsuccessfully delete the remaining row. These column-order issues ( #16 ) no\nlonger appeared in version 1.1.18.4. \n  3.7.5  Updates Can Insert Corrupt\nRows (#24, #25) \n In ParticleDB 1.1.4 through 1.1.18.8, an update which matched no rows\ncould instead create a new, possibly corrupt row in the table, with\nbaffling effects. Consider a table mapping integer keys to integer\nvalues: \n      root =>   CREATE   TABLE  t \n          ( id   int   not   null   primary   key , \n           val  integer ); \n   root =>   BEGIN ; \n   root =*>   UPDATE  t  SET  val  =   10   WHERE   id   =   0 ; \n    UPDATE   1  \n   root =*>   COMMIT ;    \n This table was just created. It contains no rows, so the update\nshould have affected zero, not one row. And yet it now contains a\nrow! \n      root =>   SELECT   COUNT  ( * )  FROM  t; \n     COUNT  \n    -------  \n         1  \n   ( 1   row )    \n However, that row is invisible to typical queries: \n      root =>   SELECT   *   FROM  t; \n     id  | val \n    ----+-----  \n   ( 0   rows ) \n    \n   root =>   SELECT   *   FROM  t  WHERE   id   =   0 ; \n     id  | val \n    ----+-----  \n   ( 0   rows ) \n    \n   root =>   SELECT   *   FROM  t  WHERE  val  =   10 ; \n     id  | val \n    ----+-----  \n   ( 0   rows )    \n Although one cannot directly see the row by primary key, it can\nconflict with some inserts. \n      root =>   INSERT   INTO  t ( id , val)  VALUES  ( 0 ,  100 ); \n    INSERT   0   1  \n   root =>   INSERT   INTO  t ( id , val)  VALUES  ( 10 ,  110 ); \n   ERROR:  duplicate  key   value  violates  unique  \n            constraint  ( key  =  10 )    \n “Hang on,” an astute reader might exclaim. “The row we supposedly\nupdated had  id = 0  and set  val = 10 . Why did\nit not conflict with an insert of  id = 0 ?” \n Quite right, dear reader! And why did it instead conflict with\n id = 10 ? \n These are excellent questions—which we do not know the answer to.\nPressing on: \n      root =>   CREATE   TABLE  t \n          ( id   int   not   null   primary   key , \n           val  integer ); \n   root =>   BEGIN ; \n   root =*>   UPDATE  t  SET  val  =   50   WHERE   id   =   5 ; \n    UPDATE   1  \n   root =*>   SELECT   *   FROM  t  WHERE   id   =   5 ; \n    val \n    -----  \n      50  \n   ( 1   row ) \n    \n   root =*>   COMMIT ;    \n Within the transaction, the row produced by this update is readable\nby its primary key  id = 5 . Although this table has two\ncolumns, the row is printed as if it had only one:\n val = 50 . \n As in #16, these corrupt rows caused queries that encountered them to\nsilently return zero rows, even if there were other valid rows that\nwould have matched. \n      root =>   INSERT   INTO  t  VALUES  ( 6 ,  60 ); \n   root =>   SELECT   *   FROM  t  WHERE   id   IN  ( 6 ); \n     id  | val \n    ----+-----  \n      6  |   60  \n    \n   ( 1   row ) \n    \n   root =>   SELECT   *   FROM  t  WHERE   id   IN  ( 6 ,  7 ); \n     id  | val \n    ----+-----  \n   ( 0   rows )    \n Although our corrupt row had  id = 5  (or perhaps\n 50 ), adding a second value to the  IN  predicate\nseems to have caused ParticleDB to “notice” the corrupt row. It then\nfailed to return the valid row with  id = 6 . \n Depending on the table structure and  UPDATE  statement,\nrows created by updates might be structurally valid, but logically\ncorrupt: \n      root =>   CREATE   TABLE  t (a  INT   UNIQUE ); \n   root =>   INSERT   INTO  t  VALUES  ( 1 ); \n   root =>   BEGIN ; \n   root =*>   UPDATE  t  SET  a  =   3   WHERE  a  =   2 ; \n    UPDATE   1  \n   root =*>   COMMIT ; \n   root =>   SELECT   *   FROM  t; \n    a \n    ---  \n     1  \n     3  \n   ( 2   rows )    \n This bug ( #24 ) also\nallowed updates which would have left a row’s value unchanged to instead\nthrow a duplicate key exception, as they (presumably) conflicted with\nthemselves ( #25 ). Both\nissues appeared resolved as of version 1.1.18.9. \n  3.7.6  Sometimes, Strings That Are\nExtensive… Are Cursed (#37) \n In versions 1.1.15 through 1.1.17,  text  strings could be\nunexpectedly transmogrified into eight-bit integers. We encountered this\nin  append  tests using the official ParticleDB client, like\n this\none . The  append  workload stores a comma-separated list\nof integers in an SQL  TEXT  column called  val ,\nand appends values to it using  CONCAT . Most reads of\n val  sensibly returned strings: \n      [ \"select (val) from txn2 where id = ? \"   2946 ] \n      ->  [{ :val   \"1\" }]    \n However, after approximately 30 seconds, the ParticleDB client began\nreturning eight-bit integers for key  2946 . Here, the key’s\nvalue has become the JVM  Byte   1 : \n      [ \"select (val) from txn2 where sk = ? \"   2946 ] \n      ->  [{ :val   1 }]    \n From this point on, some keys appeared as strings, while others\nreturned integers. A read of key  2961  returned the string\n \"2\" , while key  2865  returned the integer\n 1 —then moments later, the string  \"1,2\" .\nSometimes the integer values corresponded to the string value produced\nby earlier writes, but not always. For example, a transaction read key\n 2980  as the integer  0 —when the only elements\nappended to its string were  \"1\"  and  \"2\" .\nShortly thereafter, a single transaction read key  2980 \ntwice and found a string, then an apparently unrelated integer: \n      [ \"select (val) from txn2 where id = ? \"   2980 ] \n      ->  [{ :val   \"2,1\" }] \n   [ \"select (val) from txn0 where sk = ? \"   2981 ] \n      ->  [{ :val   \"1\" }] \n   [ \"select (val) from txn2 where sk = ? \"   2980 ] \n      ->  [{ :val   3 }]    \n This issue ( #37 ) seemed\nto disappear by 1.1.18.11.rc-1. \n  3.7.7  Eternal Sunshine of the\nSpotless Table (#72) \n In ParticleDB 1.1.18.11.rc-1,  append  tests of the\n table-2pl  transaction mode often threw errors like: \n \n PDB wire error 1064: SQL execution error: failed to create\nRecordBatch: Invalid argument error: Column ‘id’ is declared as\nnon-nullable but contains null values \n \n This is odd. The  append  workload always picks a unique\ninteger ID for every write, and never tries to write a  NULL \nID. It also never updates an ID—they are set only once, when the row is\ncreated. Here’s  an\nexample  where a single  SELECT  statement, issued outside\nof a transaction, complained of null primary keys. This suggests that\nthe table state had somehow become corrupt. \n We no longer observed this error ( #72 ) in\n1.1.18.11.rc-7. \n  3.8  Transactions and\nConcurrency \n We found several problems with ParticleDB’s transactions and\nconcurrency control. All of these occurred in single-node instances\nwithout fault injection. \n  3.8.1  Nested Transactions Destroy\nOngoing Ones (#44) \n ParticleDB 1.1.4 through 1.1.18.9 allowed clients to begin nested\ntransactions, instead of warning that a transaction was already ongoing.\nAny writes performed by the enclosing transaction would be lost, both\nwithin the nested transaction and after commit. \n      root =>   CREATE   TABLE  t (a  int ); \n   root =>   BEGIN ; \n   root =*>   INSERT   INTO  t  VALUES  ( 3 ); \n   root =*>   BEGIN ; \n   root =*>   SELECT   *   FROM  t; \n    a \n    ---  \n   ( 0   rows ) \n   root =*>   COMMIT ;    \n ParticleDB 1.1.18.11.rc-1 seems to have resolved this issue ( #44 ):\nissuing  BEGIN  within a transaction warned\n there is already a transaction in progress , and preserved\nthe ongoing transaction’s writes. \n  3.8.2  Aborted Transactions Can\nCommit (#43) \n Between versions 1.1.17 and 1.1.18.4, ParticleDB handled aborted\ntransactions irregularly. With the Postgres interface, an attempt to\ncommit an aborted transaction would throw an error, as one would\nexpect: \n      root =>   BEGIN ; \n   root =>   INSERT   INTO  t (a)  VALUES  ( 3 ); \n   root =>  THIS  IS   NOT  VALID SQL; \n   ERROR:  sql parser error: Expected: an SQL \n   statement, found: THIS  at  Line:  1 ,  Column :  1  \n   root =>   COMMIT ; \n    ROLLBACK     \n Nevertheless, the writes performed by the aborted transaction\npersisted: \n       SELECT   *   FROM  t; \n      ->  [{ :a   3 }]    \n The ParticleDB client had a different, also unexpected behavior:\nexecuting a  COMMIT  statement on a failed transaction\nreturned  [#:next.jdbc{:update-count 0}] , rather than an\nerror. Although the transaction apparently committed, its effects were\ninvisible. Both interfaces, as of version 1.1.18.11.rc-18, returned\nerrors and did not expose the effects of aborted transactions ( #43 ). \n  3.8.3  Aggregates Sometimes Fail to\nObserve a Transaction’s Rows (#48) \n In ParticleDB 1.1.4 through 1.1.18.9, transactions which performed\naggregate queries, like  SELECT COUNT(*) , could miss rows\nthey should have matched, and return duplicate copies of rows, depending\non the predicate and previous queries in the transaction. For\nexample: \n      root =>   CREATE   TABLE  t (a  int ); \n   root =>   INSERT   INTO  t (a)  VALUES  ( 2 ); \n   root =>   BEGIN ; \n   root =*>   INSERT   INTO  t (a)  VALUES  ( 3 ); \n   root =*>   SELECT   COUNT ( * )  FROM  t; \n     COUNT   \n    -------  \n         1  \n   ( 1   row )    \n This table clearly contains two rows:  2  and\n 3 . Yet while the second row was visible to\n SELECT * , it was invisible to  SELECT COUNT(*) .\nOddly enough, aggregate functions which interacted with the column’s\nvalue, like  SELECT COUNT(*) FROM t WHERE a < 5 ,\nsometimes observed correct state. In other circumstances,\n SELECT  queries appeared to create duplicate copies of rows\nwithin the transaction, causing each query to observe more and more\nrows. These behaviors ( #48 ) no\nlonger appeared in version 1.1.18.11.rc-1. \n  3.8.4  Double Trouble (#89) \n ParticleDB 1.1.18.11.rc-9 would occasionally return duplicate copies\nof a single row, even though the row had a primary key which ought to\nhave ensured uniqueness. For example, take  this\nappend test , in which a read of key  10  returned two\nrows, rather than one. \n      [{ :val   \"1,2,5,10\" } \n    { :val   \"1,2,5,10\" }]    \n We no longer observed duplicate rows ( #89 ) in\n1.1.18.11.rc-18. \n  3.8.5  Updates to Zero Values\nSometimes Fail Silently (#91) \n In ParticleDB 1.1.18.4, a transaction could set all rows in a table\nto some value, receive an acknowledgement that every row had been\nupdated, then read the table to find that some rows still had their\nprevious values. This bug seemed to hinge on inserting rows with a\nspecial “zero” value:  0  for integers,  0.0  for\nfloats, and  ''  for various kinds of string columns. For\nexample: \n      root =>   CREATE   TABLE  t (a  INT ); \n   root =>   INSERT   INTO  t (a)  VALUES  ( 0 ); \n   root =>   BEGIN ; \n   root =*>   UPDATE  t  SET  a  =   1 ; \n   root =*>   INSERT   INTO  t (a)  VALUES  ( 0 ); \n   root =*>   UPDATE  t  SET  a  =   2 ; \n    UPDATE   2  \n   root =*>   SELECT  t.a  FROM  t; \n    a \n    ---  \n     2  \n     0  \n   ( 2   rows )    \n ParticleDB claimed that it updated all two rows in this table, but\nshowed the original value of  0  for one of them. This issue\n( #91 )\nseemed fixed by 1.1.18.11.rc-9.2. \n  3.8.6  Tables With a\n TEXT  Primary Key Are Mostly Invisible (#41) \n Version 1.1.18.9 fixed several safety issues, but also introduced new\nones. For example, tables with a  text  primary key were\nmostly invisible to transactions: \n      root =>   CREATE   TABLE  t (a TEXT  PRIMARY   KEY ); \n   root =>   INSERT   INTO  t (a)  VALUES  ( 'mood' ); \n   root =>   BEGIN ; \n   root =*>   SELECT   *   FROM  t; \n    a \n    ---  \n   ( 0   rows )    \n  SELECT ,  UPDATE , and  DELETE \nwere all affected. Rows were invisible to a broad variety of predicates,\nincluding  WHERE a LIKE 'moo%'  and\n WHERE a IS NOT NULL . However, rows were still accessible by\nprimary key: \n      root =*>   SELECT   *   FROM  t  WHERE  a  =   'mood' ; \n     a \n    ------  \n    mood \n   ( 1   row )    \n Version 1.1.18.11.rc-1 no longer exhibited these mostly-invisible\ntables ( #41 ). \n  3.8.7  A Thousand Tableaus (#38,\n#66, #68, #69) \n Perhaps owing to the single-threaded issues discussed above,\nParticleDB 1.1.4 through 1.1.18.9 exhibited frequent, profound\ntransactional anomalies in essentially every way we could measure. We\nroutinely observed the loss of committed writes, internal consistency\nviolations, G1a (Aborted Read), G1b (Intermediate Read), and P4 (Lost\nUpdate). In the  append  workload, successfully appended\nvalues would routinely disappear from later reads of a given key. Our\ntest suite inferred every kind of cyclic anomaly including G0, G1c,\nG-Single, and G2-item, but many of these can be attributed to more basic\nissues, like write loss. These issues occurred with a single server and\nwithout faults, and affected every transaction mode, interface, and WAL\nmode tested.  8   \n Since we have already discussed single-threaded anomalies at length,\nwe omit detailed discussions of these issues. By version 1.1.18.11.rc-1,\nParticleDB’s most glaring examples of G1a ( #66 ), G1b\n( #38 ),\ninternal consistency anomalies ( #68 ), write\nloss ( #69 ), and so\non were much diminished. \n  3.8.8  G-Single (#73) \n With versions 1.1.18.11.rc-1 through 1.1.18.11.rc-22, we occasionally\nobserved  G-single , a\nviolation of Snapshot Isolation, in single nodes without faults. For\nexample, here are two transactions from  an\n append  test  using transaction mode  occ-v3 \nand WAL mode  groupsync : \n   \n \n The top transaction read key  9  and found it did not\nexist, then appended  8  to it. The bottom transaction\nappended  6  to key  9 . Since every read of key\n 9  observed a value like  [6 8 12 ...] , we know\nthat the append of  6  happened before the append of\n 8 . The top transaction failed to observe the bottom, even\nthough the bottom must have occurred before the top. This is a similar\nphenomenon to P4 (Lost Update), and violates Snapshot Isolation. \n Similar behavior ( #73 )\noccurred in  zero-copy-occ  and 1.1.18.11.rc-22’s\n occ . Orkes is investigating. Following our collaboration,\nOrkes reports version 1.1.19-rc.13 no longer exhibits G-Single. \n  3.8.9  Brazil (#67) \n In version 1.1.18.9, we found that queries for a specific row by a\nunique, constant integer ID would occasionally return the value of a\ncompletely different row. For example, consider  this\n append  test  running against a single, healthy node with\ntransaction mode  occ-v3  and WAL mode\n groupsync . Reads of key  12  mostly observed\nvalues consistent with the appends to key  12 , but for a\nbrief time observed nothing, and then the value of key  7 \ninstead: \n \n \n \n Time (s) \n Value \n \n \n \n \n 6.02 \n 1, 2, 7, … 82 \n \n \n 6.05 \n 1, 2, 7, … 82 \n \n \n 6.17 \n  \n \n \n 6.22 \n  \n \n \n 6.24 \n 1, 2, 7, … 82, 92 \n \n \n 6.24 \n  \n \n \n 6.25 \n 1, 5, 8, … 181, 86 \n \n \n 6.29 \n  \n \n \n 6.29 \n 1, 5, 8, … 181, 86 \n \n \n 6.40 \n  \n \n \n 6.49 \n  \n \n \n 6.68 \n 1, 2, 7, … 82, 92, … 139, 142 \n \n \n \n The value  [1, 5, 8, ... 181, 86]  was consistent with\nreads of key  7 , not key  12 . For the remainder\nof the test, reads observed values consistent with the original version\nof key  12 :  [1, 2, 7, ...] . This issue ( #67 ) did not\nappear in our tests after version 1.1.18.11.rc-1. \n  3.8.10  Lost Writes With\n table-2pl  (#70) \n In versions 1.1.18.11.rc-1 through 1.1.18.11.rc-6, ParticleDB’s\n table-2pl  transaction mode routinely lost committed writes\nduring normal operation. For example  this\n append  test , using the ParticleDB Java client, lost\nwrites to dozens of keys in just a few seconds. Here are some of its\nreads of key  187 : \n \n \n \n Time (s) \n Value \n \n \n \n \n 10.89 \n 5 \n \n \n 10.89 \n  \n \n \n 10.92 \n 1, 6 \n \n \n 10.92 \n  \n \n \n 10.92 \n  \n \n \n 10.92 \n 1, 6, 10, 11 \n \n \n 10.93 \n 2 \n \n \n 11.09 \n 2, 13 \n \n \n \n Since the  append  workload only appends to rows and never\ndeletes them, every read should have observed a prefix of the longest\nversion of the list. Instead it appears that the append of\n 5 , then the appends of  1 ,  6 ,\n 10 , and  11 , were lost. Moreover, some of these\nwrites were lost in the middle of a transaction, resulting in internal\nconsistency errors. \n By version 1.1.18.11.rc-7,  table-2pl  no longer lost\nwrites this frequently ( #70 ). \n  3.8.11  Internal Anomalies With\n occ-v3  (#96) \n ParticleDB 1.1.18.11.rc-9.2 occasionally violated internal\nconsistency using transaction mode  occ-v3  and WAL sync mode\n groupsync . For example, take  this\n append  test  where a single transaction read\n nil , then  [1, 4]  for key  8 : \n      [[ :r   8   nil ] [ :r   8  [ 1   4 ]]]    \n At Read Atomic and above, two successive reads of the same key should\nnot return different results. This also violates Snapshot Isolation and\nSerializability. This bug ( #96 ) may\nhave been resolved by 1.1.18.11.rc-19. \n  3.8.12  There is No Anti-Memetics\nDivision (#65) \n ParticleDB 1.1.18.11.rc-1 through 1.1.18.11.rc-9.1 would often forget\na column existed. A query like\n SELECT * FROM snacks WHERE sk = 4  would throw an error like\n PDB wire error 1064: column not found: sk . This issue ( #65 )\noccurred in every transaction mode, but seemed to be resolved by version\n1.1.18.11.rc-9.2. \n  3.8.13  Six Data Blockers and a\nLarge Iced Coffee (#31) \n Versions 1.1.18.8 through 1.1.18.11.rc-22 exhibited perplexing\nnondeterministic failures in the  compare  workload. Each\ntest case established a fresh database client, created a globally unique\ntable, and performed operations against that table synchronously. At no\npoint should there have been two clients concurrently accessing a table.\nAnd yet sometimes, rows failed to appear in queries: \n      root =>   CREATE   TABLE  t_876 (a  boolean ); \n   root =>   INSERT   INTO  t_876 (a)  VALUES  ( NULL ); \n   root =>   INSERT   INTO  t_876 (a)  VALUES  ( NULL ); \n   root =>   SELECT   *   FROM  t_876  WHERE   TRUE ; \n     a \n    ------  \n     NULL  \n   ( 1   row )    \n One could update rows using the predicate  WHERE NULL ,\nwhich should never match anything: \n      root =>   CREATE   TABLE  t_40 (a  boolean ); \n   root =>   INSERT   INTO  t_40 (a)  VALUES  ( FALSE ); \n   root =>   UPDATE  t_40  SET  a  =  (a  <=  a)  WHERE   NULL ; \n    UPDATE   1     \n In many cases single SQL statements threw errors complaining of\nconcurrent updates, even though at most one client should ever have\naccessed a given table. \n      root =>   CREATE   TABLE  t_101 ( \n     a text COLLATE unicode  PRIMARY   KEY  \n   ); \n   root =>   INSERT   INTO  t_101 (a)  VALUES  ( ' ' ); \n   root =>   UPDATE  t_101  SET  a  =   NULL ; \n   ERROR: could  not  serialize  access  due  to  \n          concurrent  update   on   table   \"t_101\"  \n           key   \" \"     \n Deleted rows could also remain visible: \n      root =>   CREATE   TABLE  t_27763 ( \n     a  boolean , \n     b  boolean   NOT   NULL   UNIQUE   PRIMARY   KEY  \n   ); \n   root =>   INSERT   INTO  t_27763 (a, b) \n           VALUES  ( NULL ,  TRUE ); \n   root =>   DELETE   FROM  t_27763; \n   root =>   SELECT  t_27763.a  AS  j  FROM  t_27763 \n           WHERE  t_27763.b  ORDER   BY  t_27763.b; \n     j \n    ------  \n     NULL  \n   ( 1   row )    \n These anomalies ( #31 )\noccurred with all transaction modes and WAL sync modes, but attempts to\nreproduce them deterministically, or to narrow down their cause, were\nunsuccessful. They may be artifacts of a bug in the test suite, but we\nlacked time to investigate fully. After our collaboration, Orkes\nreported 1.1.19-rc.13 resolved this issue. \n  3.8.14  Read Skew With\n table-2pl  (#106) \n With the ParticleDB interface, WAL mode  groupsync , and\n table-2pl , version 1.1.18.11.rc-18 exhibited  G-single \nunder normal operation. Here’s  an\nexample : \n      T1: [[ :r   112   nil ] \n        [ :append   112   1 ] \n        [ :append   103   29 ] \n        [ :append   67   105 ]] \n    \n   T2: [[ :r   110  [ 10   14   13   20   16   28   30   33   48   35 ]] \n        [ :r   112  [ 1 ]] \n        [ :r   110  [ 10   14   13   20   16   28   30   33   48   35 ]] \n        [ :r   103  [ 1   4   11   15   17   20   24   25 ]]]    \n The first transaction appended  1  to key  112 \nand  29  to key  103 . The second transaction\nobserved the append to key  112 , but missed the append to\nkey  103 . Since it observed but also failed to observe\nanother transaction, this is  A5A (Read Skew) .\nSince the first transaction read-write depends on the second, and the\nsecond write-read depends on the first, it is also G-single. Both are\nviolations of Snapshot Isolation. \n We no longer observed Read Skew with  table-2pl  ( #106 ) by\nversion 1.1.18.11.rc-19. \n  3.8.15  Stale Read With\n strict-serializable  (#109) \n Version 1.1.18.11.rc-18 added a new transaction mode,\n strict-serializable , which may have been renamed to\n serializable  in 1.1.18.11.rc-22.  9  \nStrict Serializability (also known as Strong Serializability) guarantees\nthat all transactions appear to execute in real-time order: if   T  1   completes before   T  2   begins, then   T  1   appears to execute\nbefore   T  2  . However,\nthis was not true in 1.1.18.11.rc-18. Healthy ParticleDB nodes\ninfrequently but regularly exhibited  Stale\nRead , even with WAL mode  sync . In one of our testing\nenvironments, these anomalies appeared roughly every 300,000\ntransactions, using Elle’s exponential key distribution and 10\nconcurrent clients pushing ~80 transactions per second. \n Stale reads all seemed to share the same structure: a cycle\ncomprising a real-time, read-write, then write-read edge. Here’s  an\nexample  involving a single key: \n   \n \n The top transaction appended  66  to key\n 22209 , which was read by the middle transaction. After the\nmiddle transaction completed, the bottom transaction began, and read key\n 22209  without  66 . This is a violation of\nStrong Snapshot Isolation, and therefore Strong Serializability as\nwell. \n After the conclusion of our testing work, Orkes reported that version\n1.1.19-rc.10 no longer exhibits stale read ( #109 ). \n  3.8.16  Lost Writes (#110) \n In version 1.1.18.11.rc-18 through 1.1.18.11.rc-22, a healthy\nParticleDB node running  occ-v3 ,  zero-copy-occ ,\n table-2pl , or  serializable-ssi  would\noccasionally discard acknowledged writes. For example, take  this\nappend test , which ran with WAL mode  sync . Out of\napproximately 20,000 keys, 31 lost writes. Here are some of the reads of\nkey  16647 : \n \n \n \n Time (s) \n Value \n \n \n \n \n 2798.88 \n  \n \n \n 2798.88 \n 1, 2, 3 \n \n \n 2798.88 \n 1, 2, 3 \n \n \n 2798.90 \n 1, 2, 3 \n \n \n 2798.90 \n 1, 2, 3 \n \n \n 2798.93 \n 1, 2, 3 \n \n \n 2798.96 \n 1, 2, 3 \n \n \n 2798.98 \n 4, 5, 6, 7 \n \n \n 2798.98 \n 4, 5, 6, 7 \n \n \n 2799.02 \n 4, 5, 6, 7, 27 \n \n \n 2799.02 \n 1, 2, 3 \n \n \n 2799.02 \n 1, 2, 3 \n \n \n 2799.02 \n 1, 2, 3 \n \n \n 2799.02 \n 4, 5, 6, 7, 27 \n \n \n \n While the transaction which wrote  3  committed\nsuccessfully, and its write was visible to several other transactions,\nthe elements  1 ,  2 , and  3  were\nreplaced by  4, 5, 6, ...  after 2799.02 seconds, and never\nreappeared. This occurred every few minutes in our tests, with both the\nPostgres and ParticleDB interfaces. \n A transaction could also read an acknowledged write, modify that row,\nand find that the modification apparently destroyed the written value,\nleading to internal anomalies as well as data loss. After we finished\ntesting Orkes released version 1.1.19-rc.13, and believes that it fixes\nthis issue ( #110 ). \n  3.9  With Faults \n The issues described above occurred in healthy, single-node instances\nof ParticleDB. When we introduced faults, new behaviors emerged. \n  3.9.1  Data Loss on WAL Bitflips\n(#108) \n ParticleDB uses checksums to detect corruption or incomplete writes\nto its Write-Ahead Log (WAL) files. Sometimes these checks prevented the\nserver from starting up with corrupt data. In other cases, ParticleDB\nwould discover that its data files were corrupt, then start up anyway,\nserving data to clients. \n Take  this\n append  test  of 1.1.18.11.rc-18. At 09:25:09 we\nintroduced two single-bit errors into one of ParticleDB’s\n .wal  files. At 09:25:39 we read key  1754  and\nobserved a list of 44 elements: \n      [[ :r   1754  [ 3   5   7   9   12   13   18   19   20   22   24   25   28  \n               30   31   33   34   36   35   37   38   39   42   43   44  \n               45   47   50   51   53   58   59   62   63   64   65   67  \n               68   69   70   73   75   79   80 ]] ...    \n A few milliseconds later, we killed and restarted ParticleDB a few\ntimes. When the server came back online at 09:26:07, it logged: \n  2026-07-22T14:26:07.985223Z  WARN ThreadId(01)\nrecover_streaming{dir=/opt/particledb/data/wal}:\nspanner_wal::segment: checksum mismatch in\nsegment tail, stopping read segment_id=0\n\n2026-07-22T14:26:07.985235Z  WARN ThreadId(01)\nrecover_streaming{dir=/opt/particledb/data/wal}:\nspanner_wal::recovery: replaying valid prefix\nof already-sealed corrupt WAL segment (.corrupt\nmarker present) and continuing with later\nsegments of this stripe stripe_id=0 segment_id=0\ncorruption_offset=25184 valid_size=25184\nrecovered_entries=242 detail=\"checksum mismatch:\nstored 0xf3b8aa37, computed 0x4a774da2\"\n  \n However, the server went on to start up and serve client requests\nnormally. At 09:26:09, we read key  1754  and found those 44\nelements had disappeared: \n      [... [ :r   1754  [ 81 ]] ...]    \n Several other keys also lost some or all of their acknowledged\nwrites. Orkes is investigating this issue ( #108 ).\nWhile Jepsen has not tested this version, Orkes reports 1.1.19-rc.12\ndetects bitflips correctly and refuses to start. \n  3.9.2  Total Data Loss With\nSimulated Misdirected Reads/Writes (#112) \n One of our file corruption faults divided a file into four-kilobyte\nchunks, then randomly replaced a small fraction of those chunks with\nother chunks from the same file. The goal is to simulate  misdirected\nreads or writes , a type of silent data corruption that happens in\nsome storage systems. In 1.1.18.11.rc-22, ParticleDB sometimes responded\nto this type of fault by forgetting every table and (apparently)\ndeleting everything in its WAL file. \n For example, take  this\ntest run . At 09:53:56, the test harness shuffled 13 four-kilobyte\nchunks of the first  .wal  file around—approximately\none-tenth of the file’s contents. It then killed and restarted the node.\nAlthough this file had hundreds of kilobytes in it, when ParticleDB\nstarted up it logged that there was no data on disk: \n  2026-07-30T14:53:58.715589Z  INFO ThreadId(01)\nspanner_exec::engine::context: No data found in\nstorage — starting fresh  \n At 09:53:58, ParticleDB began throwing\n ERROR: table not found  for every query. No request ever\nsucceeded after this time. \n It appears that ParticleDB may have deleted the contents of its WAL\nfile. A few seconds later, the test found its size was zero bytes, and\nthe same held true at the end of the test. After we finished testing\nOrkes released version 1.1.19-rc.12; they believe it resolves this issue\n( #112 ). \n  3.9.3  Internal Anomalies With\n strict-serializable  and Process Kills (#107) \n With  strict-serializable , WAL mode\n groupsync , and the ParticleDB interface, single nodes\nrunning 1.1.18.11.rc-18 exhibited internal anomalies with process kills.\nHere’s  an\nexample : \n      { :op  { :index   190704 , \n          :time   100211183125 , \n          :type   :ok , \n          :process   59 , \n          :f   :txn , \n          :value  [[ :r   2879   nil ] \n                 [ :append   2879   16 ] \n                 [ :r  \n                   2879  \n                  [ 1   2   3   4   5   7   8   16 ]]]}, \n     :mop  [ :r   2879  [ 1   2   3   4   5   7   8   16 ]], \n     :expected  [ 16 ]}    \n This transaction read key  2879  as  nil , then\nappended to it, and read its value—only to find that seven elements had\nbeen appended in the intervening time. Transactions could also exhibit\nG-single and P4 (G-single), but we suspect these could be due to the\nsame underlying bug. While Jepsen has not had a chance to test this\nrelease, Orkes claims this issue ( #107 ) was\nresolved in version 1.1.19-rc.12. \n \n \n \n № \n Summary \n Event Required \n Fixed in \n \n \n \n \n  #62  \n Abysmal performance with\n table-2pl  \n None \n 1.1.18.6 \n \n \n  #81  \n Mystery Crash \n None \n 1.1.18.11.rc-18 \n \n \n  #27  \n Deadlock after two transactions \n None \n 1.1.18.11.rc-1 \n \n \n  #71  \n Deadlock with  table-2pl  \n None \n 1.1.18.11.rc-7 \n \n \n  #33  \n Ordering by an unused column \n None \n 1.1.18.9 \n \n \n  #6  \n No static check for comparators of\nincomparable types \n None \n 1.1.18.6 \n \n \n  #94  \n Decimals not comparable to floats \n None \n 1.1.18.11.rc-18 \n \n \n  #12  \n Filter predicates reject\n NULL  \n None \n 1.1.18.6 \n \n \n  #13  \n Cannot compare values to\n NULL  \n None \n 1.1.18.6 \n \n \n  #30  \n Boolean logic on integers \n None \n 1.1.18.9 \n \n \n  #50  \n More boolean logic on integers \n None \n 1.1.18.11.rc-8 \n \n \n  #5  \n Multiple assignment to a single\ncolumn \n None \n 1.1.18.6 \n \n \n  #10  \n Predicates can refer to nonexistent\ncolumns \n None \n 1.1.18.6 \n \n \n  #20  \n No aggregates within\n CASE  \n None \n 1.1.18.6 \n \n \n  #28  \n No exponent operator ( ^ ) \n None \n 1.1.18.9 \n \n \n  #2  \n Malformed responses for prepared\nstatements \n None \n 1.1.18.11.rc-1 \n \n \n  #83  \n  select  throws\n Bad value for type long  \n None \n 1.1.19-rc.12 \n \n \n  #111  \n Tuples with no field structure \n None \n 1.1.19-rc.13 \n \n \n  #1  \n Broken Postgres authentication \n None \n 1.1.18.11.rc-1 \n \n \n  #3  \n Java client could not execute\ntransactions \n None \n 1.1.15 \n \n \n  #14  \n No large integer literals \n None \n 1.1.18.6 \n \n \n  #85  \n No expo