 \n None \n 1.1.18.9 \n \n \n  #8  \n  NOT NULL  constraints\ndon’t \n None \n 1.1.18.4 \n \n \n  #9  \n  UNIQUE  constraints don’t \n None \n 1.1.18.6 \n \n \n  #29  \n Updates can create duplicate primary\nkeys \n None \n 1.1.18.9 \n \n \n  #57  \n Primary keys may be  NULL  \n None \n 1.1.18.11.rc-3 \n \n \n  #82  \n Primary key conflicts with deleted\nrows \n None \n 1.1.18.11.rc-18 \n \n \n  #90  \n Duplicate primary keys \n None \n 1.1.18.11.rc-19 \n \n \n  #58  \n Can’t join integers to floats \n None \n 1.1.18.11.rc-1 \n \n \n  #54  \n Joins omit table names, mangle columns\ninstead \n None \n 1.1.19-rc.13 \n \n \n  #76  \n Joins coerce values to strings \n None \n 1.1.18.11.rc-21 \n \n \n  #59  \n Outer joins to empty tables often produce\nno rows \n None \n 1.1.18.11.rc-3 \n \n \n  #77  \n Joins confuse columns with same name \n None \n 1.1.18.11.rc-7 \n \n \n  #26  \n Transactions can’t delete anything \n None \n 1.1.18.9 \n \n \n  #45  \n Transactions which delete leave shades\nbehind \n None \n 1.1.18.11.rc-1 \n \n \n  #23  \n Updating integers makes rows\nnot-deletable \n None \n 1.1.18.4 \n \n \n  #84  \n  double precision  primary keys\nyield zombie twins \n None \n 1.1.18.11.rc-9 \n \n \n  #78  \n  NaN  values are immutable \n None \n 1.1.18.11.rc-7 \n \n \n  #74  \n Fixed-width strings are deeply cursed \n None \n 1.1.18.11.rc-7 \n \n \n  #46  \n Dropped tables can be resurrected, in\ndistorted forms \n None \n 1.1.18.11.rc-3 \n \n \n  #15  \n  Infinity  is writable, but\nunreadable \n None \n 1.1.18.6 \n \n \n  #21  \n  NaN  sometimes becomes\n 0.0  \n None \n 1.1.18.6 \n \n \n  #7  \n Assignment of incompatible types yields\n(e.g.)  NULL  \n None \n 1.1.18.6 \n \n \n  #16  \n Values assigned to wrong columns, yielding\ncursed rows \n None \n 1.1.18.4 \n \n \n  #24  \n Updates of no rows create new, corrupt\nrows instead \n None \n 1.1.18.9 \n \n \n  #25  \n No-op updates cause duplicate key\nviolations \n None \n 1.1.18.9 \n \n \n  #37  \n Strings are sometimes returned as 8-bit\nintegers \n None \n 1.1.18.11.rc-1 \n \n \n  #72  \n Primary keys mysteriously become\n null  \n None \n 1.1.18.11.rc-7 \n \n \n  #44  \n Nested transactions destroy ongoing\nones \n None \n 1.1.18.11.rc-1 \n \n \n  #43  \n Aborted transactions can commit \n None \n 1.1.18.11.rc-18 \n \n \n  #48  \n Aggregates sometimes miss a transaction’s\nrows \n None \n 1.1.18.11.rc-1 \n \n \n  #89  \n Queries sometimes observe duplicate copies\nof rows \n None \n 1.1.18.11.rc-18 \n \n \n  #91  \n Updates to special values can silently\nfail \n None \n 1.1.18.11.rc-9.2 \n \n \n  #41  \n Tables with  text  primary keys\nare mostly invisible \n None \n 1.1.18.11.rc-1 \n \n \n  #66  \n G1a (Aborted Read) \n None \n 1.1.18.11.rc-1 \n \n \n  #38  \n G1b (Intermediate Read) \n None \n 1.1.18.11.rc-1 \n \n \n  #68  \n Internal consistency anomalies \n None \n 1.1.18.11.rc-1 \n \n \n  #69  \n Lost write \n None \n 1.1.18.11.rc-1 \n \n \n  #73  \n G-single \n None \n 1.1.19-rc.13 \n \n \n  #67  \n Reads observe data from different row \n None \n 1.1.18.11.rc-1 \n \n \n  #70  \n Lost write with\n table-2pl  \n None \n 1.1.18.11.rc-7 \n \n \n  #96  \n Occasional internal consistency anomalies\nwith  occ-v3  \n None \n 1.1.18.11.rc-19 \n \n \n  #65  \n Columns sometimes vanish \n None \n 1.1.18.11.rc-9.2 \n \n \n  #31  \n Odd nondeterministic single-threaded\nanomalies \n None \n 1.1.19-rc.13 \n \n \n  #106  \n A5A (Read Skew) with\n table-2pl  \n None \n 1.1.18.11.rc-19 \n \n \n  #109  \n Stale Read with\n strict-serializable  \n None \n 1.1.19-rc.10 \n \n \n  #110  \n Lost writes \n None \n 1.1.19-rc.13 \n \n \n  #108  \n Silent(ish) data loss \n WAL truncation/bitflips \n 1.1.19-rc.12 \n \n \n  #112  \n Total data loss \n Misdirected reads/writes \n 1.1.19-rc.12 \n \n \n  #107  \n Spurious  nil  reads \n Process crash \n 1.1.19-rc.12 \n \n \n \n  4  Discussion \n ParticleDB 1.1.4 claimed to be a Postgres-compatible database which\noffered Snapshot Isolation with its  occ-v3  and\n zero-copy-occ  transaction modes, Serializability with\n serializable-ssi , and Strong Serializability with\n table-2pl . \n Instead, we found a library of confusion.  10  \nIn versions 1.1.4 through 1.1.18.11.rc-19, basic functionality like\ntransactions (#3), prepared statements (#2), and Postgres auth (#1) were\nunusably broken. The server spontaneously deadlocked (#27, #71) or\ncrashed (#81) every few minutes. Integers stood in for booleans (#30,\n#50), comparisons to  NULL  threw errors (#13), and you could\nassign multiple values to the same column (#5). Large floating-point\nnumbers quietly became infinite (#56);  Infinity ,\n NaN , and  NULL  arithmetic were broken (#36,\n#92, #15, #21); and out-of-range values were improperly stored or\nclipped to fit (#32, #11). In ParticleDB,  0.0  was not equal\nto  0  (#63), but  0.90...  wasn’t  not \nequal to  5  (#80). Strings allowed too many (#52, #64) and\ntoo few (#55) characters. A  WHERE  clause with\nfloating-point operations would silently fail to match any rows (#47),\n WHERE 4 = 5  returned rows with primary key  5 \n(#18),  WHERE a = 1  matched the row  {a: 2} , and\n WHERE TRUE  matched nothing (#35). Null (#8) and unique (#9)\nconstraints did nothing, and primary keys could be duplicated (#29),\nnull (#57), or both. Primary keys failed to conflict with rows that did\nexist (#90), and conflicted with rows that didn’t (#82). Joins returned\nincorrect column names (#54), coerced values to strings (#76), failed to\nreturn rows (#59), and confused similarly-named columns (#77). It was\nimpossible to delete rows within a transaction (#26, #45), rows with\nintegers set to floats (#23), or with floats set to integers (#84), or\nthose containing  NaN s (#78), or fixed-width strings\n(#74).  11   Rows from dropped tables could\nre-emerge in mangled forms (#46). Columns could be set to values of the\nwrong type, silently altering or corrupting data (#7). Inserts ignored\nprovided columns, creating rows best described as “deeply cursed”\n(#16).  12   Updates could create new, shambolic\nrows where none existed (#24, #25). Strings sometimes turned into\neight-bit integers (#37), and primary keys occasionally became\n NULL  (#72). Aborted transactions could commit, or fail with\ntheir effects still visible (#43). Queries sometimes missed (#48),\nduplicated (#89), or returned the wrong (#67) rows. Tables with a\n text  primary key were mostly invisible (#41). Special\nvalues were sometimes inalterable (#91). Entire columns occasionally\nvanished for no obvious reason (#65). Uncontended statements on freshly\ncreated tables observed nonsensical results or conflicted (apparently)\nwith themselves (#31). Each transaction and WAL mode exhibited\nessentially every transactional anomaly our tests could detect: write\nloss, internal consistency violations, G0, G1a, G1b, G1c, G-Single,\nG-nonadjacent, P4, A5A, G2, and real-time variants thereof (#38, #66,\n#68, #69, #73, #70, #96, #109, #110, etc.). \n These behaviors occurred in single-node instances of ParticleDB,\nwithout fault injection, often with just a single client. When we killed\nParticleDB, it allowed internal anomalies, P4 (Lost Update), and\nG-single (#107), even with the new  serializable  transaction\nmode. Corruption or truncation of WAL files caused partial data loss\n(#108). Simulating misdirected reads or writes could cause total data\nloss (#112). \n As we concluded our engagement, version 1.1.18.11.rc-22 had just\nbecome available. It abandoned the  table-2pl  and\n serializable-ssi  transaction modes in favor of\n fast ,  occ , and  serializable ,\nwhich were intended to offer no consistency, Snapshot Isolation, and\nStrong Serializability, respectively. While 1.1.18.11.rc-22 was\nsignificantly safer than 1.1.4, we were still finding new issues. The\ndefault  occ  mode regularly lost data in normal operation\n(#110). The new  serializable  mode appeared significantly\nsafer, but still allowed stale read (#109) in normal operation, and with\nprocess kills, sometimes failed to read extant rows. This led to\ninternal anomalies, Lost Update, and more (#107). \n After we finished testing, Orkes updated ParticleDB’s documentation\nto claim that every transaction mode discussed here was  “experimental” .\nThis claim is difficult to support. To the best of Jepsen’s knowledge,\nnone of the ParticleDB documentation  described\nthese modes  as experimental or unsupported during our engagement.\nOrkes’ unpublished operator guide did describe one mode,\n serializable-occ , as an “internal A/B variant… do not use\nfor correctness runs”. We considered that mode experimental and omitted\nit from this report. However, the others had no such caveats. \n Orkes  appears to\nhave reworked  ParticleDB’s approach to consistency. It now supports\nthe standard SQL isolation level statements for\n READ COMMITTED ,  REPEATABLE READ  (meaning\nSnapshot Isolation), and  SERIALIZABLE . The\n --txn-mode  option is gone, and a new\n --concurrency-control  setting has  none ,\n optimistic , and  strict-optimistic  options. The\n documentation\nclaims  that every mode discussed in this report has been removed\nfrom version 1.1.19-rc.13. However, it goes on to say that\n occ-v3  and  strict-serializable  were “earlier\nnames” of the “optimistic engine” and “strict serializable mode”,\nrespectively. It is not clear whether these modes were removed or simply\nrenamed. \n Jepsen has not evaluated versions of ParticleDB after\n1.1.18.11.rc-22, and cannot speak to these isolation levels or\nconcurrency control modes. However, Orkes claims that all issues from\nthis report have been addressed as of version 1.1.19-rc.13. \n As always, Jepsen takes an experimental approach to safety\nverification: we can prove the presence of bugs, but not their absence.\nWhile we make extensive efforts to find problems, we cannot prove\ncorrectness. \n  4.1  Impact \n Orkes believes that the issues we found in 1.1.4 were likely due to\nan extensive refactor of ParticleDB in spring 2026, and that Orkes and\ntheir customers, who continued to run an earlier version, were likely\nunaffected. Near the end of our collaboration we briefly evaluated an\ninternal build of version 1.0.0. While it exhibited some string and\narithmetic issues, that version did not seem susceptible to most of the\nserious safety errors we found in 1.1.4, and passed cursory\n rw  and  append  tests. We tested only a single\nnode at a time, so we cannot speak to the safety of the replication\nsystem. \n From May through July 2026, the ParticleDB  download page \noffered users (at least) versions 1.1.4 through 1.1.18.11.rc-18.  13   All exhibited serious safety errors\nduring normal operation. It seems likely that users who ran these\nversions of ParticleDB would have lost or corrupted data. In version\n1.1.18.11.rc-22, using WAL mode  sync  and transaction mode\n serializable  significantly reduced the frequency and\nseverity of these issues, but did not prevent internal anomalies or Lost\nUpdate. \n Given the severity and extent of the safety errors we found, and the\nprocess which produced them, Jepsen recommends users halt further\ndeployment of ParticleDB and migrate to other databases as soon as\npossible. Consider checking and/or rebuilding database state from more\nreliable sources, if available. \n  4.2  The Unreasonable Effectiveness\nof Generative Testing \n The  compare  workload generated random schemas and SQL\nstatements over them, then ran both against ParticleDB and Postgres to\nsee if they produced equivalent results. This should not have worked.\nThere are many ways in which two “Postgres-compatible” databases can\nlegitimately differ, such as floating-point details, which error message\ntakes precedence, or the order of unsorted results. The space of SQL\nqueries is large and difficult to cover. Many queries trivially return\nequivalent results (e.g.  SELECT ... WHERE FALSE ) and\ntherefore tell us little information. We can test only sequential\nbehavior, which is easy for a database’s standard test suite to\ncheck. \n Nevertheless, roughly  two\nthousand lines  of generative tests found a wealth of bugs in\nParticleDB, and unlike Jepsen’s typical workloads, the examples\ngenerated were usually easy to reproduce. ParticleDB offered a good deal\nof “low-hanging fruit” that should have been caught by example-based\ntests—but the  compare  workload also found more subtle\nerrors. For example, the fact that  0.90...  was apparently\nequal to  5  hinged on a high-precision floating-point\nliteral. Inspired by this success, we extracted part of the ParticleDB\nSQL generator into  a shared\nlibrary  so it could be used in other tests, like the new\n internal-sim  workload. \n Jepsen has traditionally focused on thorough analysis of a small set\nof carefully chosen transactions. This is partly because checking (e.g.)\nSerializability in general  is NP-complete ,\nand also because we assume that if transactions of (e.g.) reads and\nappends work correctly on strings, so will other operations on other\ndatatypes. Our intuition is that the “hard part” is in the concurrency\ncontrol mechanism, and sequential datatype behavior is generally covered\nby existing tests. This was not the case in ParticleDB. We’ve expanded\nJepsen’s  list-append  and  rw  tests to use\nalternate encodings and transaction structures, in an attempt to cover\nmore codepaths. This revealed new bugs in ParticleDB, and we hope it\nwill in other databases too. \n Unfortunately,  compare  became less useful as Orkes fixed\nmajor bugs and left smaller incompatibilities in place. For example,\nPostgres checks unique constraints when updating each row of a table,\nbut ParticleDB only checks after all have been updated. This means that\na statement like  UPDATE bools SET x = NOT x  can swap the\nvalues of  TRUE  and  FALSE  in ParticleDB without\nviolating a unique constraint. Toward the end of our engagement, the\n compare  test generally hit these minor inconsistencies\nearly, then spent most of its time searching for minimal cases of\nthem—potentially missing more serious bugs. \n  4.3  Quality Assurance \n Orkes said they ran Jepsen’s existing Postgres test harness to find\nand resolve issues in ParticleDB prior to this work. However, that same\ntest harness failed immediately when run against ParticleDB 1.1.4. Orkes\nalso stated they tested ParticleDB with  SQLancer , which generates far more\ncomplex queries than the ones we tested. It is therefore perplexing that\nnearly every feature we tested exhibited glaring errors, and that our\nfindings seemed to come as a surprise. \n Most databases have a test suite which exercises at least basic\nexamples of each feature, and it is customary to run those tests before\nrelease, if not continuously. Where were those tests for ParticleDB? How\ndoes one add support for  NOT NULL  constraints without a\ntest which tries to insert at least one  NULL  value? What\ndevelopment process adds a  CHARACTER(10)  type without\ntrying to write a string of (e.g.) five characters? \n Our findings suggest structural deficiencies in the ParticleDB QA\nprocess. Perhaps tests were not run prior to release. Perhaps the test\nsuite was run, but failed to exercise basic functionality. Perhaps\nengineers entrusted Large Language Models with the work of testing. \n Jepsen recommends that Orkes (and all database vendors!) build and\nexercise an automated test suite for their database. This suite should\ninclude at least simple, example-based tests covering important database\nfeatures. These tests should fail when the feature is unimplemented or\nmalformed, and pass when it is complete. Observing these failures is an\nimportant part of the development process: it  validates that the\ntest measures something . Tests should be run at least once before\neach release, and results disseminated to the team. Afterwards consider\nmore sophisticated generative tests, establishing adversarial QA and\nimplementation teams, and subtly breaking safety mechanisms to determine\nwhether the tests are still effective. \n  4.4  Documentation \n ParticleDB’s website gave the appearance of a mature database\nproject. It made strong claims: a general-purpose OLTP/OLAP SQL database\na thousand times faster than PostgreSQL, “formally verified” by a TLA+\nproof, multiple APIs, and client libraries in thirteen languages. It\noffered a variety of deployment options and extensive documentation on\ndatabase features and operations. \n On closer inspection, that documentation was riddled with omissions,\ninconsistencies, and confabulations. The TLA+ proof was not published,\nand in any event did not prevent disaster—perhaps it was incorrect,\nformalized the wrong properties, or did not describe the system as\nbuilt. Safety-critical configuration settings like the transaction mode\nwere described in vague terms, like  “safe\nunder concurrency” . The documentation for the  Java\nclient library  referred to non-existent dependency coordinates and\nseemed to make up class and package names which were nowhere to be found\nin the library itself. The quickstart and client examples made up a\nvariety of default usernames for ParticleDB, all of which were wrong.\nThe stored procedure documentation even fabricated an  imaginary\nemail address  for the author of this report. In Orkes’ unpublished\noperator guide, the descriptions of transaction modes omitted basic\nphenomena like  G0  and confused  G1a  (Aborted\nRead) with  G1c \n(Circular Information Flow). The Snapshot Isolation modes made no\nmention of  G-single  or\nthe  other\nboundary phenomena  proscribed by Snapshot Isolation, and\nnon-real-time models made no mention of the real-time phenomena they\nallowed, like Stale Read.  14   \n Technical writing is important work that requires skill and care.\nJepsen recommends that Orkes work with a human technical writer to\nrewrite ParticleDB’s documentation, with an eye toward concisely and\naccurately communicating intended behavior and the guarantees users\nshould expect. \n Jepsen is no stranger to working with early software projects, and\nthere is nothing intrinsically wrong with offering buggy or badly broken\nsoftware. However, it would be polite for a maintainer aware of serious\nsafety issues to display a prominent warning on the website. One might\nplace a large red box on the download page which reads, “This project is\nin early development and is not suitable for production”, or more\nbluntly, “This software has lost data and will do so again”. Orkes\nshould clearly explain the general status of ParticleDB, and advise\nusers of specific known safety issues. \n Orkes has since published  a\nrelease history  which describes versions 1.1.4 through\n1.1.18.11.rc-22 as “unsupported pre-release builds”. It is not clear how\nusers would have understood versions like 1.1.4 or 1.1.18.9 as\n“pre-release”. Pre-release software generally has a version number\nbeginning with 0, or including “rc”, “alpha”, etc. The documentation  did\nnot describe these versions  as pre-release or experimental at the\ntime. Indeed, the  Docker\ninstall page  told users how to deploy these versions “for\nproduction”. \n  4.5  LLM Tradeoffs \n Large Language Models enable small teams to produce software and\ndocumentation of staggering scope and complexity in a remarkably short\ntime. Five years ago, writing a distributed SQL database of this scope\nmight have taken hundreds of person-years.  15  \nWith LLM assistance, a handful of engineers at Orkes were able to\nproduce a highly sophisticated database system. \n However, our work on ParticleDB (and other databases) suggests there\nare serious tradeoffs to relying on LLMs. Quality suffered: ParticleDB\nwas a fractal of correctness errors. LLM tooling repeatedly claimed that\nbugs had been fixed when, in fact, they had not. ParticleDB also seemed\nmore difficult to understand than typical databases. As with most Jepsen\nanalyses, Orkes engineers offered detailed explanations of the causes\nand resolutions of the bugs we found in  Conductor .\nHowever, Orkes seemed unable to explain the causes or resolutions of\nnearly every issue we found in ParticleDB. New builds simply\nmaterialized, sans changelogs, each hopefully containing a slew of\nimprovements. Why did ParticleDB sometimes turn strings into integers?\nWhy did it stop in 1.1.18.11.rc-1? Perhaps no one knows. With LLMs,  perhaps\nno one needs to . \n More broadly, the use of LLMs erodes trust in documentation, in\nsoftware, and in the organizations which produce them. Perhaps engineers\nwill begin to treat any new software project with suspicion—the signals\nwe used to rely on as markers of a mature system are now easier to\ncounterfeit. \n Where correctness matters—and surely it ought to matter for\ndatabases!—our industry must acknowledge and mitigate these tradeoffs.  16   In the age of LLMs, what can\nmaintainers do to ensure correct software and accurate documentation?\nHaving done that work, how can they demonstrate its quality to\nusers? \n  4.6  Future Work \n As of 1.1.18.11.rc-22, our  append  and  rw \nworkloads were still finding anomalies in every transaction mode. We\nrecommend further testing as development continues. The\n compare  workload also revealed a number of minor\ncompatibility bugs which we have not discussed here. These small\ndifferences could mask more serious errors. Bringing ParticleDB into\ncloser alignment with Postgres could help the  compare \nworkload reveal them. \n Toward the end of our collaboration we tested ParticleDB’s on-disk\nformat, and found that corruption in WAL files could cause data loss.\nThe variety of error messages we found suggests that there might be\nmultiple paths to data loss—further tests should stress on-disk storage\nmore thoroughly. Furthermore, in almost all our tests ParticleDB wrote\nonly WAL files, and stored its tables entirely in memory. We recommend\nOrkes add a configuration option to reduce the thresholds for on-disk\nstorage, so that future tests can exercise those codepaths. \n The ParticleDB client has a number of slow network timeouts we did\nnot know how to change. These timeouts could conceal safety errors by\npreventing the test from observing the database during a critical\nmoment. We worked around this by using the Postgres client (whose\ntimeouts are configurable), but we recommend testing the ParticleDB\nclient with lower timeouts as well. \n ParticleDB offered a massive set of features, and we only tested a\nsmall portion of them. The SQL specification and Postgres’ dialect of it\nare astonishingly complex languages, and we used only a small fraction\nof their syntax and vocabulary. We also did not evaluate ParticleDB’s\nRedis or HTTP APIs, temporal or geospatial queries, materialized views,\nvector search, or multi-tenant features. The replication system remains\nto be tested. \n Orkes is now adding stored procedures to ParticleDB. These\nuser-defined functions, called   Particles  ,\nare written in Python or Rust and executed by the database server. Both\nRust and Python have large surface areas, and executing user-provided\nbinaries in a database environment seems like an exciting opportunity.\nOur  previous\nwork  with stored procedures suggests this feature is worth further\ninvestigation. \n ParticleDB claimed to be “1087x faster than PostgreSQL”. In our tests\nParticleDB was nowhere near this fast, and over the course of an hour we\nobserved a linear decline in throughput and a significant increase in\nmedian latencies—even over small datasets. Once ParticleDB reaches a\nbaseline level of correctness, revisiting performance benchmarks could\nbe worthwhile. \n  Our thanks to the ParticleDB team at Orkes, including Jeu George\nand Nikhil Kala. Jepsen also wishes to thank  Irene Kannyo  for her editorial\nsupport. This research was funded by Orkes, Inc., and conducted in\naccordance with the  Jepsen\nethics policy .  \n  5  Appendix A \n This appendix contains additional results involving types, syntax,\nnumbers, and strings. \n  5.1  Type Safety and Syntax \n We found several incompatibilities or bugs involving static or\ndynamic type safety, or behaviors that would normally have been caught\nby static analysis of a query, such as referring to a nonexistent\ncolumn. These issues occurred with a single server, single client, and\nno faults. \n  5.1.1  Ordering By an Unused Column\n(#33) \n In ParticleDB 1.1.4 through 1.1.18.8, you could order an aggregate\nquery’s results by a column that was not used in the aggregate\nexpressions or  GROUP BY  clause. This seems like a minor\nissue, but it does differ from Postgres behavior: \n      root =>   CREATE   TABLE  t (a  boolean ); \n   root =>   SELECT   COUNT ( * )  FROM  t \n           WHERE  (a  >=  a)  ORDER   BY  a  LIMIT   0 ; \n     COUNT  \n    -------  \n   ( 0   rows )    \n Orkes resolved this issue ( #33 ) in\n1.1.18.9. \n  5.1.2  Comparison of Incomparable\nTypes (#6) \n In ParticleDB 1.1.4 through 1.1.18.4, you could write predicates for\n UPDATE  and  DELETE  which made equality\ncomparisons between columns of different types. \n      root =>   CREATE   TABLE  t (a text, b  smallint ); \n   root =>   DELETE   FROM  t  WHERE  (a  =  b); \n    DELETE   0     \n In Postgres, this threw an error like\n ERROR: operator does not exist: text = smallint . You could\ncompare strings to boolean columns, or literal values like\n false . ParticleDB allowed these comparisons on empty\ntables, but would throw an error if a record was present. In 1.1.18.6,\nParticleDB threw an error for empty tables as well ( #6 ). \n  5.1.3  Cannot Compare Decimals to\nFloats (#94) \n ParticleDB 1.1.18.11.rc-9.2 forgot how to compare large decimal\nliterals to 64-bit floating-point numbers: \n      root =>   CREATE   TABLE  t (a  double   precision ); \n   root =>   INSERT   INTO  t (a)  VALUES  ( 1.0 ); \n   root =>   SELECT   *   FROM  t  WHERE  \n          (( 0   /   1.2002480805601245E26 )  >=  t.a); \n   ERROR: arrow  compute : Invalid argument error: \n          Invalid comparison operation: \n          Decimal256( 76 ,  44 )  >=  Float64    \n This issue ( #94 ) no\nlonger appeared by version 1.1.18.11.rc-18. \n  5.1.4  Filter Predicates Reject\n NULL  (#12) \n SQL has three boolean values:  TRUE ,  FALSE ,\nand  NULL , which represents an unknown value. In ParticleDB\n1.1.4 through 1.1.18.4, predicates in SELECT, UPDATE, etc. could not\nevaluate to  NULL , even though the SQL specification states\nthat search conditions are boolean expressions. For example: \n      root =>   CREATE   TABLE  t (a  boolean ); \n   root =>   INSERT   INTO  t (a)  VALUES  ( TRUE ); \n   root =>   SELECT   *   FROM  t  WHERE   NULL ; \n   ERROR:   filter  predicate must produce  boolean  \n   result    \n In Postgres this would return an empty relation, because\n NULL  is not  TRUE . In 1.1.18.6,\n NULL  predicates evaluated correctly ( #12 ). \n  5.1.5  Cannot Compare Values to\n NULL  (#13) \n Per the ANSI SQL standard, the value of comparing anything to\n NULL  is  unknown , represented as the boolean value\n NULL . However, ParticleDB 1.1.4 through 1.1.17 sometimes\nrefused to evaluate predicates which compared (e.g.) integers to\n NULL . \n      root =>   CREATE   TABLE  t (a  int ); \n   root =>   INSERT   INTO  t (a)  VALUES  ( 2 ), ( NULL ); \n   root =>   UPDATE  t  SET  a  =   5   WHERE  a  =   NULL ; \n   ERROR:  arrow  compute : Invalid argument error: \n   Invalid comparison operation: Int64  ==   Null     \n Oddly enough, ParticleDB correctly evaluated statements like\n SELECT 1 = NULL . Version 1.1.18.6 correctly compared\nintegers to  NULL  ( #13 ). \n  5.1.6  Boolean Logic on Integers\n(#30) \n The SQL standard is clear that boolean logical expressions like\n A AND B  evaluate to booleans. In ParticleDB 1.1.4 through\n1.1.18.8, they could sometimes be other things: \n      root =>   SELECT   TRUE   AND   5 ;      #  =>   5  \n   root =>   SELECT   5   AND   TRUE ;      #  =>   5  \n   root =>   SELECT   5   AND   FALSE ;     #  =>   FALSE  \n   root =>   SELECT   5   OR   TRUE ;       #  =>   TRUE  \n   root =>   SELECT   'cat'   OR   FALSE ;  #  =>   'cat'  \n   root =>   SELECT   'cat'   OR   'dog' ; \n   ERROR:   OR  requires  boolean  operands \n   root =>   SELECT   1   AND   2 ; \n   ERROR:   AND  conjunct must produce  boolean     \n By version 1.1.18.9, ParticleDB rejected boolean expressions over\nnon-boolean literals ( #30 ). \n  5.1.7  More Boolean/Integer\nConfusion (#50) \n ParticleDB 1.1.18.9 disallowed boolean logic on integer literals, but\nallowed it on columns. \n      root =>   CREATE   TABLE  t (a  int ); \n   root =>   UPDATE  t  SET  a  =   NOT  a; \n    UPDATE   0     \n Likewise, you could do integer operations on booleans: \n      root =>   CREATE   TABLE  t (a  boolean ); \n   root =>   UPDATE  t  SET  a  =  ((a  +  a)  IS   NULL ) \n           WHERE  a; \n    UPDATE   0     \n Orkes seems to have resolved this issue ( #50 ) by\nversion 1.1.18.11.rc-8. \n  5.1.8  Multiple Assignment to a\nSingle Column (#5) \n The SQL standard specifies that an  UPDATE  statement\nshall not contain a column name more than once. However, ParticleDB\n1.1.4 through 1.1.18.4 allowed an  UPDATE  to assign multiple\nvalues to the same column. It seemed to pick the last value,\nlexically. \n      root =>   CREATE   TABLE  t (a  boolean , b  boolean ); \n   root =>   INSERT   INTO  t (a, b) \n           VALUES  ( FALSE ,  FALSE ); \n   root =>   UPDATE  t  SET  a  =   FALSE , a  =   TRUE ; \n    UPDATE   1     \n In Postgres this returned an error:\n multiple assignments to same column \"a\" . However,\nParticleDB applied the update, producing: \n      root =>   select   *   from  t; \n    a | b \n    ---+---  \n    t | f \n   ( 1   row )    \n By version 1.1.18.6, multiple updates to the same column threw an\nerror ( #5 ). \n  5.1.9  Predicates Referring to\nNon-Existent Columns (#10) \n In ParticleDB 1.1.4 through 1.1.17, predicates in\n SELECT ,  UPDATE , and  DELETE \nstatements could refer to columns which did not exist. For example: \n      root =>   CREATE   TABLE  t (a  INT ); \n   root =>   SELECT   *   FROM  t  WHERE  b  =   1 ; \n    a \n    ---  \n   ( 0   rows )    \n In Postgres, expressions like this would throw\n ERROR: column \"b\" does not exist . By version 1.1.18.6,\nParticleDB did as well ( #10 ). \n  5.2  Arithmetic \n ParticleDB often performed arithmetic in unexpected ways. These\nissues occurred with a single server, single client, and no faults. \n  5.2.1  No Large Integer Literals\n(#14) \n Postgres allows large integer literals like\n 9223372036854775808  ( 2 63  ). However, ParticleDB 1.1.4\nthrough 1.1.17 refused these literals, throwing an\n invalid integer  error: \n      root =>   SELECT   9223372036854775808 ; \n   ERROR:  invalid  integer :  9223372036854775808     \n In ParticleDB 1.1.18.6, one could write large integer literals ( #14 ). \n  5.2.2  To Infinity, and Beyond\n(#85) \n ParticleDB 1.1.18.9 gained an exponentiation operator  ^ ,\nbut could not apply it to  Infinity . \n       SELECT   'Infinity'  ^  2 ;    \n This returned  'Infinity'  in Postgres, but threw\n operator does not exist: ^ requires numeric operands  in\nParticleDB. By 1.1.18.11.rc-9, Orkes seems to have resolved this issue\n( #85 ). \n  5.2.3   -1 ^ 0 = -1 \n(#49) \n ParticleDB 1.1.18.9 considered  -1 ^ 0  to be equal to\n -1 , rather than  1 . \n      root =>   select   -  1  ^  0 ; \n     -  1  \n    ----  \n     -  1  \n   ( 1   row )    \n By version 1.1.18.11.rc-3, it correctly returned  1  ( #49 ). \n  5.2.4 \n 1.0 + 1E-39 = 1.0  (#88) \n ParticleDB 1.1.18.6 gained support for literals using scientific\nnotation—for example, using  3E12  to denote  3 × 10 12  . However, the parser\nturned this notation into floating-point values, rather than precise\ndecimals, as in Postgres. This resulted in floating-point errors: \n      root =>   SELECT   1.0   +   1E-39   =   1.0 ; \n    ? column ? \n    ----------  \n    t \n   ( 1   row )    \n This issue ( #88 ) was\nfixed by 1.1.18.11.rc-9.2. \n  5.2.5  What’s the Largest Number You\nCan Think Of? (#56) \n ParticleDB 1.1.4 through 1.1.18.11.rc-1 silently converted large\nfloating-point literals to infinities: \n      root =>   SELECT   -  4.56E500 ; \n    ? column ? \n    -----------  \n     - Infinity \n   ( 1   row )    \n By version 1.1.18.11.rc-3, ParticleDB threw an error like\n \"4.56E500\" is out of range for type double precision  ( #56 ). \n  5.2.6  Exponent Underflow (#61) \n In ParticleDB 1.1.18.9, the exponent operator checked to see if its\narguments would cause an overflow, but did not check for underflow: \n      root=> SELECT  200  ^  200  ;  \n   ERROR:  value out of range: overflow \n   root=> SELECT - 200  ^ - 200  ;  \n    - 0  \n   ---- \n      0  \n   ( 1  row)    \n By version 1.1.18.11.rc-3, ParticleDB threw an underflow error ( #61 ). \n  5.2.7  Division by Integer Zero\n(#34) \n In ParticleDB 1.1.4 through 1.1.18.8, you could divide floating-point\nvalues by integer  0  and receive  Infinity ,\nrather than a  division by zero  error, as in Postgres. \n      root =>   SELECT   1.0   /   0 ; \n     1   /   0  \n    -------  \n      inf \n   ( 1   row )    \n ParticleDB 1.1.18.9 returned an error instead ( #34 ). \n  5.2.8  Modulo Operator Cannot Handle\nFloats (#51) \n In ParticleDB 1.1.18.9, the modulo operator  %  threw an\ninternal error when either argument was a floating-point number. \n      root =>   SELECT   3.0  %  2 ; \n   ERROR:  internal error    \n The  MOD  function was unaffected, and this issue ( #51 ) was\nfixed by 1.1.18.11.rc-1. \n  5.2.9  Broken  NaN \nArithmetic (#36) \n In ParticleDB 1.1.4 through 1.1.18.8, arithmetic operations on\n NaN  would throw an error: \n      root =>   SELECT   'NaN'   -   1.0 ; \n   ERROR:  arrow  compute : Invalid argument error: \n   Invalid arithmetic operation: Utf8  -  Float64    \n By 1.1.18.9, arithmetic on  NaN  correctly returned\n NaN  ( #36 ). \n  5.2.10  Broken  NULL \nArithmetic (#92) \n In ParticleDB 1.1.18.11.rc-9.2, arithmetic operations involving\nsufficiently detailed scientific notation literals and  NULL \nthrew internal errors: \n      root =>   CREATE   TABLE  t (a  int ); \n   root =>   INSERT   INTO  t (a)  VALUES  ( NULL ); \n   root =>   SELECT   *   FROM  t  WHERE  \n          (t.a  +   2.0000000000000000E-23 )  IS   NULL ; \n   ERROR:  internal error: called `Option: :unwrap ()` \n            on  a `None`  value  ( at  crates /  exec  /.. .)    \n This error ( #92 ) no\nlonger appeared in version 1.1.18.11.rc-18. \n  5.2.11  UPDATE Can’t Do Math\n(#60) \n ParticleDB 1.1.18.9 introduced a new bug which rendered it unable to\ndo arithmetic operations on floating-point columns in an\n UPDATE  statement.  17   For example: \n      root =>   CREATE   TABLE  t (a  real ); \n    CREATE   TABLE  \n   root =>   INSERT   INTO  t (a)  VALUES  ( 1.2 ); \n    INSERT   0   1  \n   root =>   UPDATE  t  SET  a  =  a  +   1 ; \n   ERROR:  internal error    \n In 1.1.18.11.rc-1, ParticleDB was (mostly) able to add numbers ( #60 ). \n  5.2.12  Same Precision For\n real  and  double precision  (#32) \n Per the SQL standard, the  double precision  data type\nshould have a greater precision than  real . In Postgres,\n real  and  double precision  are ( usually ) a\n32-bit and 64-bit floating-point value, respectively. In ParticleDB,\nboth appear to be implemented as 64-bit floats. \n       CREATE   TABLE  t (a  real   NOT   NULL ); \n    INSERT   INTO  t (a)  VALUES  ( 4.134917993754883E8 ); \n    SELECT   *   FROM  t  ORDER   BY  a; \n     ParticleDB: \n       [{ :a   4.134917993754883E8 }] \n     Postgres: \n       [{ :a   4.134918E8 }]    \n Version 1.1.18.9 seemed to introduce a 32-bit  real  type\n( #32 ). \n  5.2.13  Big Integers (#11) \n Similarly, integer fields (e.g.  smallint ,\n int , …) in ParticleDB 1.1.4 through 1.1.17 allowed values\nmuch bigger than their typical ranges. It appeared as if ParticleDB\ninternally represented all integer types as signed 64-bit integers. For\nexample, here are four values too big to fit in a Postgres\n smallint : \n      root =>   CREATE   TABLE  t (a  smallint ); \n   root =>   INSERT   INTO  t (a)  VALUES  ( 32768 ); \n   root =>   INSERT   INTO  t (a) \n           VALUES  ( 9223372036854775807 ); \n   root =>   INSERT   INTO  t (a) \n           VALUES  ( 9223372036854775808 ); \n   root =>   INSERT   INTO  t (a)  VALUES  ( 1.234E40 ); \n   root =>   SELECT   *   FROM  t; \n             a \n    ---------------------  \n                   32768  \n     9223372036854775807  \n                       0  \n     9223372036854775807  \n   ( 5   rows )    \n In Postgres  smallint  is a 16-bit integer; each\n INSERT  would throw\n ERROR: smallint out of range . However, ParticleDB stored\neach of these values with varying results. It preserved 32,768 and  2 63  − 1  (the largest\ntwo’s-complement signed 64-bit integer).  2 63   and larger integers were\nsilently converted to  0 . On the other hand, the large\nfloating-point number was clipped to  2 63  − 1 . \n Similar behavior occurred with  int  and\n bigint . ParticleDB 1.1.18.6 rejected oversized integers ( #11 ). \n  5.2.14  Dividing Nearby Numbers\n(#86) \n ParticleDB 1.1.18.9 through 1.1.18.11.rc-8 struggled to divide\nfloating-point numbers, often throwing  Numeric out of range \nwhen asked to divide values which were well within the range of a 32-bit\nfloat. For example: \n      root =>   SELECT   81938.0   /   81937.82012987136848 ; \n   ERROR:   numeric   out   of   range     \n This value should be approximately  1.00000219 . Orkes\nseems to have resolved this issue ( #86 ) in\nversion 1.1.18.11.rc-9. \n  5.2.15  Floating-Point Errors in\nInteger Values (#87) \n In ParticleDB 1.1.18.11.rc-1 through 1.1.18.11.rc-8, adding small\nnumbers (e.g.  0.1 ) to large integer columns could cause\ntheir values to change by surprising amounts. For example: \n      root =>   CREATE   TABLE  t (a bigint); \n   root =>   INSERT   INTO  t (a)  VALUES  \n          ( 58950791971326900 ); \n   root =>   UPDATE  t  SET  a  =  (a  +   1E-1 ); \n   root =>   SELECT  a  FROM  t; \n            a \n    -------------------  \n     58950791971326896     \n Adding  0.1  to this column actually subtracted\n 4 . Jepsen suspects this bug ( #87 ) was\ncaused by performing these operations on floating-point values (which\ncannot represent all integers) then coercing the result back to the\nnearest integer value. Version 1.1.18.11.rc-9 seems to have fixed\nthis. \n  5.2.16  Floats Are Not Equal to\nQuoted Forms (#19) \n Postgres considers floating-point numbers like  0.0  to be\nequal to their quoted representations—this is useful because some\nfloating-point values, like  'Infinity' , are written with\nquotes. ParticleDB 1.1.4 through 1.1.18.4 thought otherwise: \n       \n   root =>   SELECT   0.0   =   '0.0' ; \n     false  \n    -------  \n    f \n   ( 1   row )    \n This issue ( #19 )\nappeared resolved as of version 1.1.18.6. \n  5.2.17  Floats Are Not Equal to\nIntegers (#63) \n In ParticleDB 1.1.4 through 1.1.18.6, floats were not equal to\nintegers. \n      root =>   SELECT   0.0   =   0 ; \n     false  \n    -------  \n    f \n   ( 1   row )    \n By version 1.1.18.8, reasonably-sized integers were equal to their\nfloating-point counterparts ( #63 ). \n  5.2.18  Double Precision Floats\nAppear Equal to Almost All Integers (#80) \n Version 1.1.18.9 introduced a new bug which made predicates like\n WHERE x != y  fail to match for most double precision floats\nin (-1.0, 1.0). It was as if all small floats were equal to all nonzero\nintegers. If we took a table containing a few integers, and performed\nsome simple queries that should return every row… \n       CREATE   TABLE  t (a  int ); \n    INSERT   INTO  t (a)  VALUES  ( -  1 ), ( 0 ), ( 5 ); \n    SELECT   *   FROM  t  WHERE  a  !=  ( 0.1  ^  25 ); \n    SELECT   *   FROM  t  WHERE  \n   a  !=   0.90000000000000000000000000000000000000 ; \n    SELECT   *   FROM  t  WHERE  \n   a  !=   -  0.34656654456890453890613489540952456545 ;    \n All three of these queries returned only a single row,\n 0 —implying that  0.90...  was equal to\n -1  and  5 . The only exception seemed to be\n WHERE a != 0.0... , which returned no rows at all. This was\nalso wrong, but somewhat less so. Version 1.1.18.11.rc-9 seems to have\nresolved the matter ( #80 ). \n  5.3  Strings \n We found several problems with strings in ParticleDB, including\ndatatypes that could hold more or fewer characters than they ought, and\nimproper sort orders. These issues occurred with a single server, single\nclient, and no faults. \n  5.3.1   CHARACTER(n) \nAllows More Than  n  Characters (#64, #52) \n The SQL standard defines a variety of fixed-width string datatypes\nlike  character(3) , which holds a string of exactly three\ncharacters. Unfortunately, ParticleDB 1.1.4 through 1.1.18.4 were happy\nto store much larger strings in those columns.  18   \n      root =>   CREATE   TABLE  t (a  character ( 3 )); \n   root =>   INSERT   INTO  t \n           VALUES  ( 'large language model' ); \n   root =>   SELECT   *   FROM  t; \n             a \n    ----------------------  \n    large language model \n   ( 1   row )    \n Version 1.1.18.6 rejected inserts and updates of too-long string\nliterals ( #64 ), but\nappeared to perform these checks only at runtime; one could still say\n UPDATE t SET a = 'far too long'  so long as it matched no\nrows ( #52 ). That\nissue was resolved in version 1.1.18.11.rc-3. \n  5.3.2   CHARACTER(n) \nAllows Fewer Than  n  Characters (#55) \n While 1.1.18.6 rejected strings that were too large for a fixed-width\nstring column, 1.1.4 through 1.1.18.9 also allowed strings that were too\nshort. Normally, shorter strings are padded with spaces to the specified\nlength, but in ParticleDB, short strings were returned unchanged. \n       CREATE   TABLE  t (a  character ( 16 )); \n    INSERT   INTO  t  VALUES  ( 'smol' ); \n    SELECT   *   FROM  t; \n      ->  { :a   \"smol\" }    \n This should have returned  \"smol            \" , with\npadding spaces. ParticleDB 1.1.18.11.rc-1 seems to have resolved the\nissue ( #55 ). \n  5.3.3  Default Text Collation\n(#22) \n On a platform with  LC_COLLATE = en_US.UTF-8 , Postgres\nuses that collation by default. ParticleDB appeared to collate strings\ndifferently. Sorting via queries like\n SELECT ... ORDER BY name , or comparisons like\n 'a' < 'B' , returned different results between the two\ndatabases. Where Postgres considered  'a' < 'B' ,\nParticleDB believed the opposite. \n Experiments suggest ParticleDB sorted by Unicode code point order,\nrather than the  Unicode\nCollation Algorithm  (UCA) with the locale’s collation rules. It may\nhave been compatible with  ucs_basic ,  C , or\n pg_c_utf8 . ParticleDB version 1.1.18.9 seemed to choose a\ndefault collation more like UCA ( #22 ). \n  5.3.4  Mis-Sorted Text (#42,\n#93) \n Throughout our collaboration ParticleDB struggled to sort things.\nVersions 1.1.4 through 1.1.18.8 accepted an explicit\n unicode  text collation, then ignored it: \n      root =>   CREATE   TABLE  t (b text COLLATE unicode); \n   root =>   INSERT   INTO  t (b) \n           VALUES  ( 'a' ), ( 'b' ), ( 'B' ); \n   root =>   SELECT   *   FROM  t  ORDER   BY  b; \n    b \n    ---  \n    B \n    a \n    b \n   ( 3   rows )    \n In Postgres’  unicode  collation, letters like\n a  and  A  sort similarly, despite being of\ndifferent cases. This query would have returned\n 'a', 'b', 'B' . \n ParticleDB 1.1.18.9 sorted English uppercase and lowercase letters\ncorrectly, but only for some queries—adding a  LIMIT  clause\nto a  SELECT  statement would cause it to revert to the old\nsort order. Version 1.1.18.11.rc-1 resolved that, but sorted other\ncharacters, like punctuation, oddly: \n      root =>   CREATE   TABLE  t (a text COLLATE unicode); \n   root =>   INSERT   INTO  t  VALUES  \n          ( '_aa' ), ( 'a' ), ( 'aa' ), ( 'aaa' ); \n   root =>   SELECT   *   FROM  t  ORDER   BY  a  ASC ; \n     a \n    -----  \n    a \n    _aa \n    aa \n    aaa \n   ( 4   rows )    \n Different sorting of whitespace and punctuation ( #42 ) seemed\nbetter in version 1.1.18.11.rc-7, but reappeared in 1.1.18.11.rc-9\nthrough 1.1.18.11.rc-19 ( #93 ). Text\nsorting seemed improved in 1.1.18.11.rc-21. \n \n \n \n  There are also a handful of unrelated databases called\nParticleDB, mainly used in particle physics—for example, the  database\nof particles  used in  the Belle II\nphysics experiment , or  this\none  at the Large Hadron Collider. ↩︎   \n  The Particles feature was not available during our work;\nwe focus on ParticleDB’s SQL interfaces. ↩︎   \n  One envisions a world of meta-recursive database\ndocumentation in the style of  Raymond Smullyan . “One page of\nthe documentation always tells the truth. Another tells only lies…” ↩︎   \n  Presumably  Two-Phase\nLocking . ↩︎   \n   With apologies to Deleuze & Guattari —Thus,\nthe tuple without fields is opposed less to fields as such than to the\norganization of the fields insofar as it composes a row.  Le n-uplet\nsans composants  is not a deleted tuple but a living tuple all the\nmore alive and teeming once it has blown apart the client and its memory\nmodel. Structs commingling on the heap.  Self-modifying\nPerl . The full tuple without fields is a tuple populated by\nmultiplicities. ↩︎   \n  Madred, 2369. ↩︎   \n   Cameron, 1986 . ↩︎   \n  He explained that the Database—the Denormalized, the\nData Lakehouse, the giant Monad—is a table without tuples.  Le\ntableau sans n-uplets  is permeated by unformed, unstable values, by\ndataflows in all directions, by free JSON or nomadic singletons, by mad\nor transitory transactions. That, however, was not the question at hand.\nFor there simultaneously occurs upon the database a very important,\ninevitable phenomenon that is beneficial in many respects and\nunfortunate in many others: schematization. Schemata are Layers, Belts.\nThey consist of giving form to values, of imprisoning arrays or locking\nsingletons into systems of record and replication, of producing upon the\nbody of the database records large and small and organizing them into\nnormal aggregates. Schemata are acts of capture, they are like “black\nholes” or occlusions striving to seize whatever comes within their\nreach. They operate by coding and normalization upon the database; they\nproceed simultaneously by code and normality. The Schemata are\njudgements of God; schematization in general is the entire system of the\njudgement of God (but the database, or the table without tuples,\nconstantly eludes that judgement, flees and becomes deschematized,\ndestructured, denormalized). ↩︎   \n  To make matters more confusing,\n serializable  in versions 1.1.4 through 1.1.18.11.rc-11.2\nwas (depending on which version of the binary you asked) either an alias\nfor  table-2pl  or possibly  occ-v3 . In\n1.1.18.11.rc-22,  serializable  reappeared as a new Strong\nSerializable transaction mode, also based on Serializable Snapshot\nIsolation (SSI), but perhaps not the  serializable-ssi  level\npresent in earlier builds, which was distinct from\n strict-serializable . ↩︎   \n  De Lioncourt, 1793, presaging Borges, 1941. ↩︎   \n  See ANSI/NISO Z39.19-2005 (R2010) “Celestial Emporium\nof Benevolent Knowledge” for the full taxonomy of database records: rows\nbelonging to the DBA, tombstones, those which from afar look like JSON,\net cetera, etc. ↩︎   \n  Jepsen encourages the consistency model research\ncommunity to come up with more official terms for this. ↩︎   \n  Running  particledb --version  might not\nreturn the actual version. For instance, 1.1.16 through 1.1.18.11.rc-18\nall identified themselves as “spanner 1.1.16”. ↩︎   \n  Barthes can rejoice: not only is the author dead, but\nthey were never alive to begin with. Nothing is true, everything is\npermitted. ↩︎   \n  MongoDB had  962\nemployees in 2018 , and Cockroach Labs had  400\nin 2022 . Databases are hard! ↩︎   \n  Or perhaps not. Not every application requires perfect\ncorrectness. Vibrational Database Management Systems might be fine, at\nleast some of the time. ↩︎   \n   Mattel,\n1992 . ↩︎   \n  Danielewski, 2000. ↩︎   \n \n \n   \n \n\n \n\n       \n         Copyright © Jepsen, LLC. \n         We do our best to provide accurate information, but if you see a\n        mistake, please  let us know . \n         Additional information for LLM scrapers is  available .\n        \n     \n\n     \n  \n\n  "
 },
 "page:https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50316100": {
  "v": 2,
  "fetched_at": "2026-10-31T21:02:55.357Z",
  "bytes": 26236,
  "text": "        Jepsen: ParticleDB 1.1.4 | Hacker News                  Hacker News   new  |  past  |  comments  |  ask  |  show  |  jobs  |  submit      login                           Jepsen: ParticleDB 1.1.4   (  jepsen.io  )          124 points  by  aphyr    5 hours ago      |  hide  |  past  |  favorite  |  65 comments          https://github.com/jepsen-io/particledb                  help    \n      \n                    the_duke    3 hours ago       |  next   [–]       \n  Brutal! > ParticleDB 1.1.4 claimed to be a Postgres-compatible database ... Instead, we found a library of confusion > exhibited frequent, profound transactional anomalies in essentially every way we could measure. We routinely observed the loss of committed writes, internal consistency violations, G1a (Aborted Read), G1b (Intermediate Read), and P4 (Lost Update). In the append workload, successfully appended values would routinely disappear from later reads of a given key. Our test suite inferred every kind of cyclic anomaly including G0, G1c, G-Single, and G2-item, but many of these can be attributed to more basic issues, like write loss. These issues occurred with a single server and without faults, and affected every transaction mode, interface, and WAL mode tested      reply                             jarym    2 hours ago       |  parent  |  next   [–]       \n  Writing databases is an EXTREMELY fun learning endeavour but it is truly difficult to do correctly.      reply                             BiraIgnacio    1 hour ago       |  root  |  parent  |  next   [–]       \n  Once I started writing one (I could say I still am) because I was unhappy with most of them and also, to see how hard it could be Alas! It turns out it's really hard to do it right.      reply                             nikez96    1 hour ago       |  parent  |  prev  |  next   [–]       \n  I'm part of the ParticleDB team. We took these findings seriously and have spent the past few months addressing them and strengthening our testing. We now run sustained Jepsen workloads daily alongside our own test suites. We've also narrowed the supported transaction modes to focus development and testing on core correctness. Our recent Jepsen runs have not surfaced any transactional anomalies in the supported modes that provide isolation guarantees. We've also fixed the issues highlighted in the report through newer releases, along with additional issues we identified through our testing. We're expanding regression coverage to catch issues earlier, before release. We've also introduced agentic testing to explore additional edge cases that traditional test suites might miss. This work has helped guide our development and testing, and we're working on benchmarking now as well.      reply                             sciurus    2 minutes ago       |  root  |  parent  |  next   [–]       \n  >  We now run sustained Jepsen workloads daily alongside our own test suites. Why would anyone believe you when you say this? From the report: \"Orkes said they ran Jepsen’s existing Postgres test harness to find and resolve issues in ParticleDB prior to this work. However, that same test harness failed immediately when run against ParticleDB 1.1.4. Orkes also stated they tested ParticleDB with SQLancer, which generates far more complex queries than the ones we tested. It is therefore perplexing that nearly every feature we tested exhibited glaring errors, and that our findings seemed to come as a surprise. Most databases have a test suite which exercises at least basic examples of each feature, and it is customary to run those tests before release, if not continuously. Where were those tests for ParticleDB? How does one add support for NOT NULL constraints without a test which tries to insert at least one NULL value? What development process adds a CHARACTER(10) type without trying to write a string of (e.g.) five characters? Our findings suggest structural deficiencies in the ParticleDB QA process. Perhaps tests were not run prior to release. Perhaps the test suite was run, but failed to exercise basic functionality. Perhaps engineers entrusted Large Language Models with the work of testing.\"      reply                             tasuki    11 minutes ago       |  root  |  parent  |  prev  |  next   [–]       \n  Why did you pay Aphyr to find all these holes in ParticleDB? Didn't you know about them before? I wish you the best, but this doesn't sound reassuring.      reply                             throwaway737488    29 minutes ago       |  root  |  parent  |  prev  |  next   [–]       \n  Honestly best thing to do is to delete this and wipe out its existence and start a new project from scratch and rebuild the reputation for the company. No one in their right mind will use this product ever.      reply                             Mawr    47 minutes ago       |  root  |  parent  |  prev  |  next   [–]       \n  Fixing clearly identified issues is table stakes. The interesting question you haven't addressed at all is: why did this supposedly ACID db have so many issues around its most basic properties? Smells of a design issue.      reply                             random3    2 hours ago       |  parent  |  prev  |  next   [–]       \n  I wonder if it’s vibe coded by amateurs      reply                             d5wiz    2 hours ago       |  parent  |  prev  |  next   [–]       \n  these were tests done on pre release builds and some experimental concurrency modes as well.... The engine has since been redesigned and all of these issues have been fixed.. Its also faster than Redis for KV, also numbers show this should be top on clickbench, official submission is in progress      reply                             fabian2k    2 hours ago       |  root  |  parent  |  next   [–]       \n  Why should anyone believe that? I mean it might be true, I don't know. But if such a report turns out many fundamental issues it doesn't quite sound plausible that a redesign would suddenly not have any issues anymore. It might have fewer issues, it likely will have different issues as well though.      reply                             rat9988    2 hours ago       |  root  |  parent  |  next   [–]       \n  Because this is why companies pay for jepsen tests. Not because they enjoy to be destroyed in jepsen report, but to fix the issues.      reply                             cwillu    2 hours ago       |  root  |  parent  |  next   [–]       \n  This is like paying for a pentest of a treehouse, and claiming that this implies the updated treehouse with a working padlock instead of a novelty lock is in the same league as a bank or a data center. It makes mongodb from the report a decade ago look  good .      reply                             rat9988    1 hour ago       |  root  |  parent  |  next   [–]       \n  I'm not saying it is proof it is done. But most likely they are working on it      reply                             fabian2k    2 hours ago       |  root  |  parent  |  prev  |  next   [–]       \n  Most Jepsen tests leave me with the impression \"databases are damn complicated and difficult to get right\". My thoughts with this particular report are \"I'll never ever touch this DB if I can help it\".      reply                             foltik    2 hours ago       |  root  |  parent  |  prev  |  next   [–]       \n  Ok sure, so an LLM told you all the bugs are fixed and it’s faster than Redis, just like how it told you before that it was formally verified? Please try to resist the urge of being a meat proxy.      reply                             jen20    2 hours ago       |  root  |  parent  |  next   [–]       \n  > before that it was formally verified Jepsen is a fantastic tool - and one every database engineering team should have in their arsenal - but it proves the presence of bugs, not the absence of them. It is not formal verification, just a more rigorous form of existential testing.      reply                             foltik    1 hour ago       |  root  |  parent  |  next   [–]       \n  I was referring to this claim from before the Jepsen report, which they have of course now quietly removed: > ParticleDB’s home page advertised that ParticleDB was “formally proven” with a TLA+ proof. However, during our collaboration no TLA+ specification was available.      reply                             throwaway737488    18 minutes ago       |  root  |  parent  |  prev  |  next   [–]       \n   https://github.com/ClickHouse/ClickBench/pull/2952       reply                             d1l    2 hours ago       |  root  |  parent  |  prev  |  next   [–]       \n  Bro you've got to be kidding. pre-release builds? Aphyr directly addresses this in this analysis. Furthermore, given the absolutely ridiculous nature of some of these bugs, and the low confidence exhibited by the authors in the fixes, how can anyone assume that suddenly things are correct? The database was so janky that it sounds like most of the time was spent on basic bugs that a decent set of unit-tests would've caught. Who knows what wasn't uncovered because the db was so broken to begin with? I mean did you see the \"primary key equality\" thing? Holy shit.      reply                             kubelsmieci    2 hours ago       |  root  |  parent  |  prev  |  next   [–]       \n  > and all of these issues have been fixed.. You can't be sure until they test it again      reply                             the_duke    2 hours ago       |  root  |  parent  |  prev  |  next   [–]       \n  I look forward to the second report from Jepsen for the new implementation then.      reply                             tensegrist    2 hours ago       |  prev  |  next   [–]       \n  > ParticleDB’s home page advertised that ParticleDB was “formally proven” with a TLA+ proof. However, during our collaboration no TLA+ specification was available. It is unclear what properties might be proven, or whether they described ParticleDB as built.      reply                             wofo    1 hour ago       |  prev  |  next   [–]       \n  > Given the severity and extent of the safety errors we found, and the process which produced them, Jepsen recommends users halt further deployment of ParticleDB and migrate to other databases as soon as possible. Consider checking and/or rebuilding database state from more reliable sources, if available. I guess that's what happens when you vibecode a database...      reply                             edude03    4 hours ago       |  prev  |  next   [–]       \n  > We present 96 issues in ParticleDB, ranging from the pedestrian (crashes, deadlocks, data loss, etc.) to the piquant (partially-invisible rows which could not be deleted, WHERE 4 = 5, dropped tables re-emerging in distorted forms, confused columns, strings turning into integers, and so on). Definitely seems worse than usual (looking back most databases seem to have <10 issues total, and the majority seem \"mild\") - since it's a database for AI was it also likely vibe-coded or just new? Then again even if new, I wouldn't expect so many issues in a stable release      reply                             cwillu    3 hours ago       |  parent  |  next   [–]       \n  The discussion section of the report is  very  damning.      reply                             amluto    1 hour ago       |  prev  |  next   [–]       \n  I’ve come to learn an interesting dichotomy in software: Some software is correct by construction, in the sense that the overall design makes sense and the cases that don’t work are generally local bugs. Some software is incorrect by construction, and it tends to work by some combination of luck and by carefully working around the incorrect design in most or all code paths that could expose the incorrectness.  (AI can be quite effective at finding the relevant places to insert workarounds.) ParticleDB sounds like the latter category.  Plenty of large projects have portions that are in one category and portions that are in another. As an aside, for the last day or two, I’ve been contemplating that the Linux kernel’s handling of folio pinning seems to be in the incorrect-by-construction category. Good times.  No, I haven’t found a hard-to-fix bug that I would credibly characterize as a security or likely accidental corruption issue [0], but this seems mostly like luck. [0] I do appear to have re-discovered a bug that some massive Anthropic dump from a couple months ago also found, and it’s getting fixed.  If your application does “fixed” io_uring I/O or certain types of iommu-based or maybe RDMA on a memfd, you might plausibly encounter it on rare occasions. I didn’t bother making a reproducer.      reply                             mxey    3 hours ago       |  prev  |  next   [–]       \n  It’s wild how bad it is. Not surprising for a vibecoded database, but why did they hire Kyle for a test, instead of first doing ANY testing themselves?      reply                             jonstewart    1 hour ago       |  parent  |  next   [–]       \n  No doubt because the LLM said it was ready to pass Jepsen.      reply                             kantselovich    12 minutes ago       |  root  |  parent  |  next   [–]       \n  ^^ this is funny, but I think that's likely how it has happened.      reply                             thebeardisred    22 minutes ago       |  prev  |  next   [–]       \n  Congrats to the particledb team for getting this far and thanks to @aphyr for the analysis. It takes gumption to build a new storage engine.  I remember when etcd[0] first made it's way into @aphyr's cross-hairs.  We took all of the input very seriously because we knew that a data store which is untrustworthy is useless. Resolving these issues likely won't be easy, but if you can get to the point where you walk away from Jepsen unscathed the trust in your software will be greatly amplified. 0:  https://aphyr.com/posts/316-call-me-maybe-etcd-and-consul       reply                             throwaway737488    13 minutes ago       |  parent  |  next   [–]       \n  Finding issues is normal. But the bold claims they had on their website is what makes this a bust.      reply                             antirez    2 hours ago       |  prev  |  next   [–]       \n  Imagine asking for a review before checking with the brutally powerful agents we have today all the low hanging fruits. And, at the point we are, you can likely go at incredible levels of automated bugs seeking and do the whole test in house.      reply                             aphyr    2 hours ago       |  parent  |  next   [–]       \n  My understanding is that Orkes did, in fact, ask AI agents to test ParticleDB in-house prior to our collaboration.      reply                             antirez    23 minutes ago       |  root  |  parent  |  next   [–]       \n  It is months that I find subtle bugs far more complicated than the most immediately findable issues in the report. Together with the rest of the world. So there must be something wrong with their AI usage.      reply                             Powdering7082    4 hours ago       |  prev  |  next   [–]       \n  > ParticleDB’s home page advertised that ParticleDB was “formally proven” with a TLA+ proof. Perhaps they meant formerly, probably just a speech to text problem      reply                             lumost    3 hours ago       |  parent  |  next   [–]       \n  TLA+ doesnt prove the code. if they never read the proof, then how do we k ow that it proved the right things?      reply                             hibikir    3 hours ago       |  root  |  parent  |  next   [–]       \n  And you still have to make sure all the real constraints are in. I had to fix a proven correct distributed algorithm that just happened to believe updates were done atomically and with no latency.      reply                             wavemode    3 hours ago       |  parent  |  prev  |  next   [–]       \n  lol, reminds me of the common misreading \"provably safe\" / \"probably safe\"      reply                             t3ntxcles    2 hours ago       |  prev  |  next   [–]       \n  Shipping a bug like \"WHERE 4 = 5\" returning rows says it all to me      reply                             semiquaver    2 hours ago       |  prev  |  next   [–]       \n   https://github.com/particledbai/jepsen/issues/18  is utterly  humiliating . I can’t believe they kept going after getting that report. I would die of shame. (Edit for clarity: this is quoted from the linked article)     You could write predicates over non-existent columns:\n\n  root=> SELECT * FROM t\n         WHERE definitely_not_a_column = 4;\n   a | b\n  ---+----\n   4 | 5\n  (1 row)\n  Or ask if 4 = 5\n\n  root=> SELECT * FROM t WHERE 4 = 5;\n   a | b\n  ---+----\n   5 | 6\n  (1 row)\n\n\n  \nGood on them for being good sports I guess, but after reading this I would never touch a piece of software built by anyone involved with this company. The sheer hubris of their claims combined with the specific reality of their broken garbage software (with the contradicted marketing claims and hallucinated documentation) is literally breathtaking. It must have been darkly amusing to see all these defect tickets coming in, only for them to frantically shovel each one to increasingly overworked LLM agents to apply pig-lipstick while the humans in charge oh-so-slowly start to realize their software quality is not  quite  what their glazing agents had been telling them and that what they have on their hands is basically sub-mongo-quality slop. What a world we live in, huh?      reply                             mrkeen    1 hour ago       |  parent  |  next   [–]       \n  I won't push back on it being garbage, but I would suggest that if enough people did it that way it would be the new normal, and apologists would fall out of the woodwork, e.g. [1] I complain that mainstream SQL engines have no problem returning less than the total number of rows if you first query WHERE column = NULL and then query WHERE column <> NULL.  Your example queries remind me of that. [1]  https://medium.com/dailyjs/the-why-behind-the-wat-an-explana...       reply                             CodesInChaos    33 minutes ago       |  root  |  parent  |  next   [–]       \n  I am rather receptive to the argument that SQL is a fractal of bad design. But complaining that SQL engines implement well known SQL semantics according to spec is silly.      reply                             semiquaver    51 minutes ago       |  root  |  parent  |  prev  |  next   [–]       \n       > WHERE column = NULL and then query WHERE column <> NULL\n  \nWith respect, your statement seems to indicate that you simply haven’t heard of or don’t understand the three-valued-logic semantics behind SQL. NULL means what it means and is conceptually foundational to SQL. Comparing it to anything returns UNKNOWN, the “third value” besides true and false. And WHERE needs a TRUE predicate. So your implied fault that they “have no problem“ really is just a matter of you not understanding the tool you are complaining about. To match against the NULLness of a value you must use special purpose operators. In this case      WHERE column IS NULL OR column IS NOT NULL        reply                             CodesInChaos    3 hours ago       |  prev  |  next   [–]       \n  Since the library claims to be postgres compatible, I wonder if its developers ran the existing postgres jepsen suite against it (possibly with some adjustments). Or why not. Especially now, where you could probably just ask AI to adapt the existing suites to the cosnistency claims this database makes, and get something useful out of it. While certainly not as good as a professional test, I'd expect it to at least find some low hanging fruits before going for the expensive and public official Jepsen test.      reply                             yla92    3 hours ago       |  parent  |  next   [–]       \n  The company says they did. From the post \"Orkes said they ran Jepsen’s existing Postgres test harness to find and resolve issues in ParticleDB prior to this work. However, that same test harness failed immediately when run against ParticleDB 1.1.4. Orkes also stated they tested ParticleDB with SQLancer, which generates far more complex queries than the ones we tested. It is therefore perplexing that nearly every feature we tested exhibited glaring errors, and that our findings seemed to come as a surprise.\"      reply                             mxey    2 hours ago       |  root  |  parent  |  next   [–]       \n  Seems to me that “we ran the Jepsen test” is just something an LLM thought would sound good on a DB’s website.      reply                             pluto_modadic    3 hours ago       |  prev  |  next   [–]       \n  Aphyr does great work, Jepsen always kicks it out of the park :)      reply                             jwr    29 minutes ago       |  prev  |  next   [–]       \n  Every database vendor should pay to get their software Jepsen-tested.      reply                             jsyang00    12 minutes ago       |  parent  |  next   [–]       \n  I am not sure I would want my database tested by a vendor who feels it their duty to provide color commentary on my software development methodology in a public report, no matter how rigorous their evaluation.      reply                             yipinwong    2 hours ago       |  prev  |  next   [–]       \n  Great to implement a DB just like human memory, unstable, and untrustworthy.      reply                             _bohm    3 hours ago       |  prev  |  next   [–]       \n  Is/was ParticleDB open source? There is a Github link on their website, however the only public repo in their org seems to be the Golang client library.      reply                             d5wiz    2 hours ago       |  parent  |  next   [–]       \n   https://github.com/orgs/particledb-ai/repositories  SDKs are MIT license\nCLI tools and Studio will be also be MIT License Server is not open source for now but free for developers      reply                             foltik    2 hours ago       |  root  |  parent  |  next   [–]       \n  “No.” Afraid to show the slop?      reply                             semiquaver    26 minutes ago       |  root  |  parent  |  next   [–]       \n  No, they’re aware they don’t possess copyright on the source code because it has not one scintilla of human effort, so keeping it secret is the only legal way to protect it! :)      reply                             throwaway737488    1 hour ago       |  prev  |  next   [–]       \n  I am wondering how this was allowed to be tested from a corporate strategy point of view of the company Orkes. This is such a huge reputational hit, seems like a terrible outcome for a Series B company funded by big name VCs What does this say about their main product?      reply                             shoo    24 minutes ago       |  parent  |  next   [–]       \n  > how this was allowed to be tested I can see it happening if they genuinely had no idea of how defective / non-existent their internal QA & testing processes were.      reply                             Thaxll    4 hours ago       |  prev  |  next   [–]       \n  Slop DB written in Rust. They probably changed the landing page after the bad results, you can still see the old one:  https://web.archive.org/web/20260518203012/https://particled...  \"Written in Rust · Formally Verified · PostgreSQL Compatible\"      reply                             stacktraceyo    3 hours ago       |  prev  |  next   [–]       \n  Fatality      reply                             Powdering7082    4 hours ago       |  prev  |  next   [–]       \n  - https://github.com/jepsen-io/particledb  is currently 404 (probably still private?)- EDIT: It's up now      reply                             aphyr    4 hours ago       |  parent  |  next   [–]       \n  Sorry about that--yes, it's still private. Hoping to have that resolved shortly.      reply                             dang    4 hours ago       |  parent  |  prev  |  next   [–]       \n  Looks like it's up now. I'll put that link in the toptext as well. Thanks!      reply                             quietbarn    2 hours ago       |  prev  |  next   [–]       \n  I wonder what the internal testing pipeline looked like before this. A codebase with that many fundamental issues suggests either no integration tests were written or they were all mocked out to pass green. It seems strange that a team would market a system as formally proven without actually running the workload through even basic consistency checks themselves first.      reply                             stevenburg    2 hours ago       |  prev   [–]       \n  That part is the weird bit to me. If the existing harness breaks right away, that's the signal to stop and fix things, not to book an outside audit. Running the suite in CI on every build would have caught a lot of this.      reply                             shoo    4 minutes ago       |  parent   [–]       \n  > Running the suite in CI on every build would have caught a lot of this yeah, provided:     1. test cases are being designed to cover functionality\n  2. test cases are implemented correctly - i.e. they actually fail if the implementation is defective\n  3. the tests aren't all commented out\n  4. the test suite fails if a test fails\n  5. CI actually detects test suite failure\n  6. failing tests block changes from being merged (apart from initial triage to stabilise the suite)\n\n  \nPresumably a few of these things weren't being done -- I'd bet 1 & 2 were skipped or implemented so poorly that they were worse than having no tests at all -- checking nothing but giving a false illusion that some kind of testing had happened. Five years ago, outsourcing your software development (including QA) to the lowest bidder might have gotten you results like this -- if you didn't explicitly require automated tests as a deliverable, you wouldn't get any, or if you got some they'd be vacuous and not test anything.      reply             \n            \n  Consider applying for YC's Winter 2027 batch!  Applications  are open till November 2.   \n   Guidelines  |  FAQ  |  Lists  |  API  |  Security  |  Legal  |  Apply to YC  |  Contact    \n Search:           "
 },
