esentation?](https://huggingface.co/papers/2610.22655) (2026)\\n* [JEPA Learns What the Mask Leaves Unrecoverable](https://huggingface.co/papers/2609.32481) (2026)\\n* [What Visual Generators Need from Teachers: Rethinking Representation Alignment](https://huggingface.co/papers/2609.34732) (2026)\\n* [Cross-Modal Forgetting and Transfer in Unified Multimodal Post-Training](https://huggingface.co/papers/2610.23918) (2026)\\n\\n\\n Please give a thumbs up to this comment if you found it helpful!\\n\\n If you want recommendations for any Paper on Hugging Face checkout [this](https://huggingface.co/spaces/librarian-bots/recommend_similar_papers) Space\\n\\n You can directly ask Librarian Bot for paper recommendations by tagging it in a comment: `@librarian-bot recommend`\",\"html\":\"<p>This is an automated message from the <a href=\\\"https://huggingface.co/librarian-bots\\\">Librarian Bot</a>. I found the following papers similar to this paper. </p>\\n<p>The following papers were recommended by the Semantic Scholar API </p>\\n<ul>\\n<li><a href=\\\"https://huggingface.co/papers/2610.30120\\\">ALPS: Visual State Encoders for World Models</a> (2026)</li>\\n<li><a href=\\\"https://huggingface.co/papers/2610.09700\\\">What Makes Synthetic Hard Negatives Work in Vision-Language Pretraining?</a> (2026)</li>\\n<li><a href=\\\"https://huggingface.co/papers/2609.23717\\\">BindCLIP: One Balanced Coupling For Compositional Vision Language Scoring</a> (2026)</li>\\n<li><a href=\\\"https://huggingface.co/papers/2610.22655\\\">Is a Linear Probe Evidence of a Linear Representation?</a> (2026)</li>\\n<li><a href=\\\"https://huggingface.co/papers/2609.32481\\\">JEPA Learns What the Mask Leaves Unrecoverable</a> (2026)</li>\\n<li><a href=\\\"https://huggingface.co/papers/2609.34732\\\">What Visual Generators Need from Teachers: Rethinking Representation Alignment</a> (2026)</li>\\n<li><a href=\\\"https://huggingface.co/papers/2610.23918\\\">Cross-Modal Forgetting and Transfer in Unified Multimodal Post-Training</a> (2026)</li>\\n</ul>\\n<p> Please give a thumbs up to this comment if you found it helpful!</p>\\n<p> If you want recommendations for any Paper on Hugging Face checkout <a href=\\\"https://huggingface.co/spaces/librarian-bots/recommend_similar_papers\\\">this</a> Space</p>\\n<p> You can directly ask Librarian Bot for paper recommendations by tagging it in a comment: <code>@librarian-bot recommend</code></p>\\n\",\"updatedAt\":\"2026-10-31T01:12:31.014Z\",\"author\":{\"_id\":\"63d3e0e8ff1384ce6c5dd17d\",\"avatarUrl\":\"https://cdn-avatars.huggingface.co/v1/production/uploads/1674830754237-63d3e0e8ff1384ce6c5dd17d.jpeg\",\"fullname\":\"Librarian Bot (Bot)\",\"name\":\"librarian-bot\",\"type\":\"user\",\"isPro\":false,\"isHf\":false,\"isHfAdmin\":false,\"isMod\":false,\"followerCount\":389,\"isUserFollowing\":false}},\"numEdits\":0,\"identifiedLanguage\":{\"language\":\"en\",\"probability\":0.7239620089530945},\"editors\":[\"librarian-bot\"],\"editorAvatarUrls\":[\"https://cdn-avatars.huggingface.co/v1/production/uploads/1674830754237-63d3e0e8ff1384ce6c5dd17d.jpeg\"],\"reactions\":[],\"isReport\":false}}],\"primaryEmailConfirmed\":false,\"paper\":{\"id\":\"2610.39302\",\"authors\":[{\"_id\":\"6ae4a81cac2aed10b8c1c16f\",\"user\":{\"_id\":\"6629cf0121e410e67c4de179\",\"avatarUrl\":\"/avatars/e5421133e9ab7cbf1b87cd640ca374dc.svg\",\"isPro\":false,\"fullname\":\"Randall Balestriero\",\"user\":\"RandallBalestriero\",\"type\":\"user\",\"name\":\"RandallBalestriero\"},\"name\":\"Randall Balestriero\",\"status\":\"claimed_verified\",\"statusLastChangedAt\":\"2026-10-30T16:08:42.066Z\",\"hidden\":false},{\"_id\":\"6ae4a81cac2aed10b8c1c170\",\"name\":\"Yann LeCun\",\"hidden\":false}],\"mediaUrls\":[\"https://cdn-uploads.huggingface.co/production/uploads/6629cf0121e410e67c4de179/4bn-J1bzdBosBvP6xm47N.mp4\"],\"publishedAt\":\"2026-10-29T00:00:00.000Z\",\"submittedOnDailyAt\":\"2026-10-30T00:00:00.000Z\",\"title\":\"Proving and Improving the Platonic Representation Hypothesis with JEPAs\",\"submittedOnDailyBy\":{\"_id\":\"6629cf0121e410e67c4de179\",\"avatarUrl\":\"/avatars/e5421133e9ab7cbf1b87cd640ca374dc.svg\",\"isPro\":false,\"fullname\":\"Randall Balestriero\",\"user\":\"RandallBalestriero\",\"type\":\"user\",\"name\":\"RandallBalestriero\"},\"summary\":\"Whether models trained on different modalities converge to the same representation of the world is central to AI. The Platonic Representation Hypothesis (PRH) conjectures that they do as they scale, but without a proof it offers neither a guarantee nor a recipe. We turn the hypothesis into a theorem. First, we prove a Platonic Representation Theorem: Joint-Embedding Predictive Architectures (JEPAs) trained separately on any number of modalities learn the same representation up to one rotation whenever their positive pairs are two observations of one scene--good views--and their misalignment is bounded otherwise. Second, we show that next-token prediction is a JEPA with bad views, parts of a single document, which explains why language models align with vision only through scale. Third, we validate the theory from 145k captions to the texts of 940M web images, and uncover a scaling law of alignment that predicts unseen runs within seed noise. Good views need 42 times fewer training samples than next-token prediction on the same corpus to reach the same alignment, and a 109M-parameter text JEPA aligns with DINOv2 and CLIP's image encoders better than every language model we evaluated, up to 72B parameters, and than CLIP's text encoder: next-token prediction is a remarkably inefficient route to platonic representations. As a showcase, one rotation turns separately trained encoders into an image captioner (CIDEr .838 on COCO). Even that rotation needs no pair: on the latest pair-free benchmark, our text JEPA beats every zero-shot accuracy reported with language models up to 8B parameters, e.g., 64\\\\% against at most 56\\\\% top-5 on ImageNet-100. Positive views, and not scale alone, are thus the lever to build platonic representations.\",\"upvotes\":58,\"discussionId\":\"6ae4a81cac2aed10b8c1c171\",\"projectPage\":\"https://github.com/galilai-group/prh-proof\",\"githubRepo\":\"https://github.com/galilai-group/prh-proof\",\"githubRepoAddedBy\":\"user\",\"ai_summary\":\"Joint-embedding predictive architectures trained on aligned multi-modal observations converge to shared representations with far greater efficiency than next-token prediction, demonstrating that positive view design—not scale alone—drives cross-modal alignment.\",\"ai_keywords\":[\"Platonic Representation Hypothesis\",\"Joint-Embedding Predictive Architectures\",\"JEPAs\",\"positive pairs\",\"good views\",\"next-token prediction\",\"scaling law of alignment\",\"DINOv2\",\"CLIP\",\"text JEPA\"],\"ai_summary_model\":\"thinkingmachines/Inkling-Small\",\"githubStars\":36,\"organization\":{\"_id\":\"66d46fac30d7ea0b28a1b98a\",\"name\":\"galilai-group\",\"fullname\":\"galilai-group\",\"avatar\":\"https://cdn-avatars.huggingface.co/v1/production/uploads/6629cf0121e410e67c4de179/Yf1a8X0mIW_TRVvGb0j6o.jpeg\"}},\"canReadDatabase\":false,\"canManagePapers\":false,\"canSubmit\":false,\"hasHfLevelAccess\":false,\"upvoted\":false,\"upvoters\":[{\"_id\":\"64b011856fa55c6ba9ab9df4\",\"avatarUrl\":\"/avatars/e9b2cbdbe4a2a80c11b6311447080926.svg\",\"isPro\":false,\"fullname\":\"Yin Baiqiao\",\"user\":\"yinbq\",\"type\":\"user\"},{\"_id\":\"672a1eadae82a4f934afca64\",\"avatarUrl\":\"/avatars/8a48dcca1fc0441c4f3e2abaf28ba326.svg\",\"isPro\":false,\"fullname\":\"Luan Marko Kujavski\",\"user\":\"LoanMaikon\",\"type\":\"user\"},{\"_id\":\"6267cdd3f91d1c1633c08bbf\",\"avatarUrl\":\"/avatars/e88095c31a4a0363bc6b6ed30877fc89.svg\",\"isPro\":false,\"fullname\":\"Artem Zholus\",\"user\":\"artemZholus\",\"type\":\"user\"},{\"_id\":\"62f2bd3bdb7cbd214b658c48\",\"avatarUrl\":\"https://cdn-avatars.huggingface.co/v1/production/uploads/62f2bd3bdb7cbd214b658c48/PyfUpBnNg4B3BWhgNQsYo.jpeg\",\"isPro\":true,\"fullname\":\"Logan Hallee\",\"user\":\"lhallee\",\"type\":\"user\"},{\"_id\":\"65b3d8dd791c5221280e43c4\",\"avatarUrl\":\"/avatars/f86803f1eb6d411c7e87d0520f728b1c.svg\",\"isPro\":false,\"fullname\":\"Agniv Sharma\",\"user\":\"agshar96\",\"type\":\"user\"},{\"_id\":\"68ab105a16e4098ee9599e07\",\"avatarUrl\":\"/avatars/b49efdb24178f91bd0270f7e5afb3ef5.svg\",\"isPro\":false,\"fullname\":\"Evani Lalitha\",\"user\":\"evanilalitha\",\"type\":\"user\"},{\"_id\":\"65e3f232f70c00af963b2179\",\"avatarUrl\":\"/avatars/5a6a1d27af6e5c08a3fcb93d008e7754.svg\",\"isPro\":false,\"fullname\":\"Tavis Siebert\",\"user\":\"tavi-sie\",\"type\":\"user\"},{\"_id\":\"64ece5e721ab42dfda145316\",\"avatarUrl\":\"/avatars/b3a95a180728e735397efad06685659e.svg\",\"isPro\":false,\"fullname\":\"dsfjklqmfdfjqk\",\"user\":\"sdmfjdkfqm\",\"type\":\"user\"},{\"_id\":\"6770909c6338f22c1c38e60a\",\"avatarUrl\":\"/avatars/d7ef44c85af979346e8745039c1d792a.svg\",\"isPro\":false,\"fullname\":\"Humzah Merchant\",\"user\":\"Humzahm\",\"type\":\"user\"},{\"_id\":\"6616ee229f00e704a660ab84\",\"avatarUrl\":\"/avatars/f034fccd124e900b7151dc83e12f1cde.svg\",\"isPro\":false,\"fullname\":\"Leyang Hu\",\"user\":\"leonleyang\",\"type\":\"user\"},{\"_id\":\"6678585635c15b509c850261\",\"avatarUrl\":\"/avatars/440738cd6457acee144d49741f023b9a.svg\",\"isPro\":true,\"fullname\":\"Minghao Fu\",\"user\":\"MinghaoFu\",\"type\":\"user\"},{\"_id\":\"63ff42bfcd242b2620a022fa\",\"avatarUrl\":\"/avatars/a73a86743d3ecf0ea6220e4c26035955.svg\",\"isPro\":false,\"fullname\":\"Wenquan Lu\",\"user\":\"daviddavidlu\",\"type\":\"user\"}],\"acceptLanguages\":[\"*\"],\"dailyPaperRank\":0,\"organization\":{\"_id\":\"66d46fac30d7ea0b28a1b98a\",\"name\":\"galilai-group\",\"fullname\":\"galilai-group\",\"avatar\":\"https://cdn-avatars.huggingface.co/v1/production/uploads/6629cf0121e410e67c4de179/Yf1a8X0mIW_TRVvGb0j6o.jpeg\"},\"markdownContentUrl\":\"https://huggingface.co/buckets/huggingchat/papers-content/resolve/2610/2610.39302.md\",\"query\":{}}\">      Papers     arxiv:2610.39302                                 Copy markdown                     Proving and Improving the Platonic Representation Hypothesis with JEPAs    Published on Oct 29     ·       Submitted by             Randall Balestriero            on Oct 30          ·        galilai-group                      Upvote  58                                                                               +50                           Authors:            Randall Balestriero            ,          Yann LeCun                       Abstract      Joint-embedding predictive architectures trained on aligned multi-modal observations converge to shared representations with far greater efficiency than next-token prediction, demonstrating that positive view design—not scale alone—drives cross-modal alignment.       Generated by  thinkingmachines/Inkling-Small         Whether models trained on different modalities converge to the same representation of the world is central to AI. The    Platonic Representation Hypothesis    (PRH) conjectures that they do as they scale, but without a proof it offers neither a guarantee nor a recipe. We turn the hypothesis into a theorem. First, we prove a Platonic Representation Theorem:    Joint-Embedding Predictive Architectures    (   JEPAs   ) trained separately on any number of modalities learn the same representation up to one rotation whenever their    positive pairs    are two observations of one scene--   good views   --and their misalignment is bounded otherwise. Second, we show that    next-token prediction    is a JEPA with bad views, parts of a single document, which explains why language models align with vision only through scale. Third, we validate the theory from 145k captions to the texts of 940M web images, and uncover a    scaling law of alignment    that predicts unseen runs within seed noise.    Good views    need 42 times fewer training samples than    next-token prediction    on the same corpus to reach the same alignment, and a 109M-parameter    text JEPA    aligns with    DINOv2    and    CLIP   's image encoders better than every language model we evaluated, up to 72B parameters, and than    CLIP   's text encoder:    next-token prediction    is a remarkably inefficient route to platonic representations. As a showcase, one rotation turns separately trained encoders into an image captioner (CIDEr .838 on COCO). Even that rotation needs no pair: on the latest pair-free benchmark, our    text JEPA    beats every zero-shot accuracy reported with language models up to 8B parameters, e.g., 64\\% against at most 56\\% top-5 on ImageNet-100. Positive views, and not scale alone, are thus the lever to build platonic representations.          View arXiv page      View PDF          Project page       GitHub      36                      Add to collection                                                        Community                                              RandallBalestriero             Paper author      Paper submitter                1 day ago                                                 Authors here 👋 We prove the Platonic Representation Hypothesis for JEPAs: with good views (two observations of one scene), encoders trained separately on any modalities learn the same representation, up to one rotation. \n Next-token prediction is a JEPA with bad views, so LLMs align with vision only through scale. Our 109M good-view text JEPA scores .184 on the PRH benchmark, above Qwen2.5-72B (.154) and Llama-3-70B (.151), and needs 42× fewer samples than next-token prediction on the same texts. \n 🤗 Demo, caption your own photos and videos (no network trained on image–caption pairs; one rotation fitted on pairs):  https://huggingface.co/spaces/galilai-group/prh_demo  💻 Code:  https://github.com/galilai-group/prh-proof  \n            See translation                     🔥   1    1                           +                    Reply                                        