  "text": "  \n \n \n PaperBenchX: Can AI Reproduce Science Across Disciplines? \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n  \n   \n \n Skip to main content \n \n   \n     \n       Report GitHub Issue \n       × \n     \n\n     \n       Title: \n       \n\n       Content selection saved. Describe the issue below: \n\n       Description: \n        \n     \n\n     \n       Submit without GitHub \n       Submit in GitHub \n     \n   \n  \n  \n \n \n \n    \n   \n     \n   \n   \n     \n   \n      Why HTML? \n     \n        Report Issue \n          \n        Back to Abstract \n     \n     \n        Download PDF \n          \n        Contents \n     \n     \n        Reading mode \n     \n     \n                Colour theme \n       \n  \n  \n \n \n   Abstract   \n    1  Introduction   \n    2  Related Work  \n \n    2.1  Machine-Learning Research and Paper Reproduction   \n    2.2  Cross-Domain Scientific Benchmarks   \n  \n    3  The  PaperBenchX  Benchmark  \n \n    3.1  Task Definition   \n    3.2  Task Statistics  \n \n   Benchmark composition.   \n   Release and benchmark integrity.   \n  \n    3.3  Task Construction Pipeline   \n    3.4  Evaluation   \n  \n    4  Experiments  \n \n    4.1  Experimental Setup  \n \n   Models and scaffolds.   \n   Evaluation protocol.   \n  \n    4.2  Main Results  \n \n   Overall performance.   \n   Performance by Research Area.   \n  \n  \n    5  Analysis  \n \n   Partial Progress Rarely Composes into Full Reproduction.   \n   Interaction Efficiency Varies Substantially Across Agents.   \n   Upstream Decisions Shape the Value of Execution.   \n   Final Outputs Alone Cannot Distinguish Scientific Failure Modes.   \n  \n    6  Conclusion   \n   Core contributors.   \n   Contributors.   \n   Advisors.   \n   Affiliations.   \n   References   \n    A  Task Package, Inventory, and Public Release  \n \n    A.1  Anatomy and Information Boundary of a Complete Task  \n \n   Components and evaluation-time visibility.   \n   Worked scientific contract.   \n   From contract to evidence leaves.   \n   Evaluator policy.   \n   Complete public record.   \n  \n    A.2  Complete Benchmark Inventory   \n    A.3  Public Demonstration Tasks   \n    A.4  Platforms and Resource Envelopes   \n  \n    B  Evaluation and Verification Protocol  \n \n    B.1  Scope, Resources, and Trial Records  \n \n   Evaluation scope.   \n   Resource envelopes.   \n   Trial records.   \n  \n    B.2  Expert Annotation and Release Review   \n    B.3  Clean Replay and Evidence Grading  \n \n   Submission snapshot.   \n   Controlled execution.   \n   Frozen evidence.   \n   Leaf judgments and aggregation.   \n  \n    B.4  Regression and Adversarial Checks   \n  \n    C  Additional Result Diagnostics  \n \n    C.1  Task Completion   \n    C.2  Cross-Simulator Trace Case Studies  \n \n   Eight-element smartphone MIMO (Task 1-1).   \n   F-stub UWB MIMO (Task 1-4).   \n   Polarization-insensitive LNOI edge coupler (Task 4-5).   \n   Synthesis.   \n  \n  \n  \n \n \n \n   \n    License: CC BY 4.0\n   \n   \narXiv:2610.39744v1 [cs.AI] 29 Oct 2026 \n  \n \n  PaperBenchX : Can AI Reproduce Science Across Disciplines? \n \n \n UniPat AI\n   \n\n  Abstract \n    \n  Before AI can be trusted to discover new science, it should be able to reproduce what science already knows.  Reproduction provides a rigorous checkpoint for an AI scientist: the target claim is known, yet recovering it still requires translating an incomplete scientific description into an executable experiment and producing evidence that can be independently verified. Existing research-reproduction benchmarks largely focus on AI and machine learning, where experiments share relatively homogeneous software stacks. Extending this capability across scientific disciplines introduces a different challenge: agents must operate domain-specific scientific software, make physical and numerical choices, diagnose long-running computations, and distinguish valid evidence from merely plausible outputs.\nWe introduce  PaperBenchX , a benchmark of 93 expert-constructed paper-reproduction tasks spanning 12 research areas across electromagnetics, photonics, chemistry, materials science, biology, and robotics. Each task provides the source paper, a public task specification, and a domain-native scientific environment involving specialized scientific software. Agents must reconstruct a key experiment, execute and diagnose it, and deliver a runnable workflow that supports the target scientific claim.\nTo evaluate scientific validity rather than surface-level agreement, we replay every submission from scratch and score only regenerated evidence against expert-validated criteria. Across 10 frontier agent configurations, the best configuration fully reproduces only 14% tasks under the 90% threshold. Meanwhile, mean rubric scores range from 27.53% to 59.08%, indicating that substantial partial progress rarely translates into complete scientific reproduction. These results reveal a large gap between paper understanding and reliably producing reproducible scientific evidence. \n  \n    \n  \n  Figure 1:   Frontier agents on  PaperBenchX .  Bars show full scores\n(      >   90  %    >90\\%   ); boxes show partial scores.\nGPT-6-Astra and GPT-5.6-sol use Codex; all others use Claude Code. \n \n \n  1  Introduction \n\n \n Reproducibility is one of the defining features of science: a result is meaningful not only because it has been reported, but because the assumptions, procedures, and evidence behind it can be reconstructed and independently examined. For AI systems, this makes reproduction a demanding test of scientific competence. An agent must go beyond understanding a paper’s conclusion to recover how the result was obtained, translate incomplete descriptions into executable procedures, operate specialized tools, diagnose failures, and determine whether the resulting evidence actually supports the claim. Existing reproduction benchmarks largely focus on AI and machine learning, while cross-domain scientific benchmarks typically isolate only parts of the workflow  ( Starace et al., 2025 ;  Siegel et al., 2024 ;  Geng et al., 2026 ) .  What remains underexplored is whether an agent can reproduce published experiments across disciplines, inside the environments native to the underlying science.  \n \n \n This distinction matters because, outside machine learning, the environment is not merely infrastructure. It is part of the scientific problem. Reproducing an antenna result may require constructing and solving an electromagnetic model in HFSS; reproducing a photonics result may depend on geometry, boundary conditions, mode extraction, and convergence behavior in Lumerical or Meep  ( Oskooi et al., 2010 ) ; reproducing a chemistry or materials result may require choosing basis sets, numerical tolerances, or electronic-structure settings; and reproducing a robotics result may require executing and validating behavior inside an embodied simulator. A workflow can run successfully, generate plausible numbers, and still represent the wrong physical system. Cross-disciplinary scientific reproduction therefore demands more than writing code that executes. \n \n  \n  Figure 2:   From task package to replay-grounded score. \nAn agent receives the paper, task specification, environment, and starter\nsubmission; interacts with domain-native scientific software; and returns a\nrunnable package. Clean replay regenerates the evidence used to score the\nscientific rubric. The displayed leaves and scores are illustrative. \n \n \n We identify three coupled challenges.  First, scientific reconstruction:  papers are not executable specifications. Agents must translate prose, equations, figures, and domain conventions into a concrete experiment, identify the target observable, and resolve modeling choices that are not fully specified by the task contract.  Second, domain-native execution:  agents must operate specialized scientific software, select appropriate physical and numerical settings, interpret diagnostics, and determine whether a computation is stable and converged.  Third, scientific verification:  matching a reported value is not sufficient. The result must arise from a valid experimental workflow, and the evidence supporting it must be independently regenerable. These challenges separate scientific reproduction from either rerunning released code or producing a plausible final answer. \n \n \n To study this setting, we introduce  PaperBenchX , a benchmark for paper-level scientific reproduction across disciplines. It contains 93 expert-constructed tasks spanning 12 research areas across electromagnetics, photonics, computational chemistry, computational materials science, computational biology and bioinformatics, and robotics and embodied AI. The tasks run in 10 domain-native scientific computing environments, including Ansys HFSS, Ansys Lumerical, Meep  ( Oskooi et al., 2010 ) , PySCF/GPU4PySCF  ( Sun et al., 2026 ) , ABACUS  ( Zhou et al., 2025 ) , Quantum ESPRESSO  ( Giannozzi et al., 2009 ) , AutoDock Vina  ( Eberhardt et al., 2021 ) , Habitat  ( Savva et al., 2019 ) , SimplerEnv  ( Li et al., 2025 ) , and AI2-THOR  ( Kolve et al., 2017 ) . Each task is centered on a verifiable scientific claim from a published paper. The agent receives the paper, a public task specification defining the reproduction scope and deliverables, and the required scientific environment, but does not receive the private reference workflow, reference evidence, or evaluation rubric. Its goal is to reconstruct the experiment and deliver a runnable workflow that regenerates evidence supporting the target claim. \n \n \n  PaperBenchX  evaluates sustained, end-to-end scientific reproduction through replay-grounded verification: agents must maintain scientific intent, computational state, and evidence provenance while reconstructing experiments, running and diagnosing long-running simulations, and refining their analyses; after submission, each workflow is rerun from scratch in a clean environment, and only regenerated artifacts are scored against expert-validated criteria. This design distinguishes plausible implementations and successful software execution from actual reproduction of the target scientific result. Across 10 frontier agent configurations on the same 93 tasks, the strongest configuration exceeds the 90% full-reproduction threshold on only 13 tasks, while 71 tasks are not fully reproduced by any evaluated configuration; mean rubric scores range from 27.53% to 59.08%. Agents often make substantial progress in modeling and execution but fail at scientific validation, revealing a substantial gap between partial progress and reliably closing the loop from paper understanding to reproducible scientific evidence. \n \n \n Our contributions are threefold: \n \n  •  \n \n  Cross-disciplinary paper-level reproduction.  We introduce 93 expert-constructed tasks spanning 12 research areas and 10 scientific computing environments, each centered on a verifiable claim from a published paper. \n  \n  •  \n \n  Domain-native scientific execution.  Rather than reducing scientific problems to a common coding interface,  PaperBenchX  requires agents to operate inside the simulators, software stacks, numerical conventions, and resource constraints required by the underlying research. \n  \n  •  \n \n  Replay-grounded scientific verification.  We rerun every submitted workflow from scratch and score only regenerated evidence against expert-validated scientific criteria, distinguishing plausible outputs from reproducible scientific results. \n  \n \n \n \n \n  2  Related Work \n\n \n  Table 1:   Comparison with prior agent benchmarks.  Checkmarks indicate\nthat a benchmark explicitly evaluates the named capability. \n  \n \n \n \n  Benchmark  \n  Research scope  \n  Primary evaluation target  \n \n \n  Paper-level  \n  reproduction  \n  \n \n \n  Domain-native  \n  scientific execution  \n  \n \n \n  Replay-grounded  \n  scientific verification  \n   \n \n  Machine-learning research and paper-reproduction benchmarks   \n \n MLAgentBench  ( Huang et al., 2023 )  \n Machine learning \n ML experimentation tasks \n  ✗  \n  ✗  \n  ✗   \n \n RE-Bench  ( Wijk et al., 2024 )  \n Machine learning \n Research-engineering tasks \n  ✗  \n  ✗  \n  ✗   \n \n PaperBench  ( Starace et al., 2025 )  \n Machine learning \n Paper reproduction \n  ✓  \n  ✗  \n  ✗   \n \n SA-Bench  ( Hu et al., 2026 )  \n Machine learning \n Paper reproduction \n  ✓  \n  ✗  \n  ✓   \n \n  Cross-domain scientific benchmarks   \n \n DiscoveryBench  ( Majumder et al., 2024 )  \n 6 scientific fields \n Data-driven discovery \n  ✗  \n  ✗  \n  ✗   \n \n ScienceAgentBench  ( Chen et al., 2024 )  \n 4 scientific fields \n Scientific workflow tasks \n  ✗  \n  ✗  \n  ✗   \n \n CORE-Bench  ( Siegel et al., 2024 )  \n 3 scientific fields \n Repository-result reproduction \n  ✗  \n  ✗  \n  ✗   \n \n DISCOVERYWORLD  ( Jansen et al., 2024 )  \n 8 scientific fields \n Interactive scientific discovery \n  ✗  \n  ✓  \n  ✗   \n \n NatureBench   ( Wang et al., 2026 )  \n 6 scientific fields \n Scientific experiment tasks \n  ✗  \n  ✗  \n  ✓   \n \n ScienceIDE  ( Geng et al., 2026 )  \n Physics and geoscience \n Repository-level scientific coding \n  ✗  \n  ✓  \n  ✓   \n \n  PaperBenchX  \n  12 research areas  \n  Reproduction of key paper experiments  \n  ✓  \n  ✓  \n  ✓   \n \n \n  \n \n \n  2.1  Machine-Learning Research and Paper Reproduction \n\n \n MLAgentBench and RE-Bench evaluate long-horizon machine-learning\nexperimentation and research engineering, but their objectives are specified as\ntask-level metric improvement rather than reproduction of published\nclaims  ( Huang et al., 2023 ;  Wijk et al., 2024 ) . PaperBench and SA-Bench\nmove toward paper-level evaluation through reproduction or semantic-alignment\nrubrics  ( Starace et al., 2025 ;  Hu et al., 2026 ) . They establish that a final\nmetric alone cannot measure fidelity to a paper, but remain centered on\nrelatively homogeneous machine-learning software stacks rather than\nheterogeneous domain-native scientific environments. \n \n \n \n  2.2  Cross-Domain Scientific Benchmarks \n\n \n Cross-domain benchmarks broaden the scientific scope but generally isolate only\npart of the reproduction process. SciCode, DiscoveryBench, and\nScienceAgentBench evaluate scientific coding, hypothesis discovery, or data\nanalysis, while CORE-Bench recovers results by rerunning released\ncode  ( Tian et al., 2024 ;  Majumder et al., 2024 ;  Chen et al., 2024 ;  Siegel et al., 2024 ) . DISCOVERYWORLD evaluates\ninteractive discovery in a unified virtual world, and NatureBench constructs\npaper-derived scientific-ML tasks  ( Jansen et al., 2024 ;  Wang et al., 2026 ) . ScienceIDE most directly evaluates domain-native\nscientific execution by placing agents inside production scientific software,\nbut its tasks concern repository-level repair and implementation rather than\nreproduction of a published experiment  ( Geng et al., 2026 ) . \n \n \n  PaperBenchX  targets the intersection left open by these benchmarks. It\nrequires  paper-level reproduction of key experiments , executes tasks\ninside heterogeneous  domain-native scientific environments , and uses\n replay-grounded scientific verification . Submitted workflows must run\nagain from scratch and regenerate evidence that supports the target claim;\nneither a plausible final answer nor a completed repository is sufficient.\nTable   1   summarizes these distinctions. \n \n \n \n \n  3  The  PaperBenchX  Benchmark \n\n \n  PaperBenchX  evaluates whether an agent can turn a scientific claim into\nindependently replayable evidence in the domain-native environment required by\nthe underlying research. Figure   2   summarizes the complete\nlifecycle, from the materials provided to the agent through clean replay and\nevidence-based scoring. This section defines the task, describes the benchmark\ncomposition and construction pipeline, and specifies the evaluation protocol. \n \n  \n  Figure 3:   Coverage and evaluation structure of  PaperBenchX . \n A , The 93 tasks span 12 research areas in six scientific domains.\n B , Their 93 source papers span 44 publication venues; bar colors\ncorrespond to the 12 research areas in A.  C , The task rubrics contain\n3,168 scorable criteria (median 38 per task); points are tasks and black ticks\nmark research-area medians. \n \n \n  3.1  Task Definition \n\n \n Each  PaperBenchX  task is centered on a verifiable scientific claim from a\npublished scientific paper. As shown in Figure   2  , the task\ninput has three parts: the paper; a public task specification defining the\nscientific scope, target conditions and variants, observables, required\nevidence, and delivery interface; and a scientific computing environment\ncontaining the domain-native simulator, dependencies, data, and allocated\nresources. The environment is part of the task definition rather than\nincidental infrastructure, because model representations, numerical\nconventions, execution procedures, and convergence evidence differ across\nscientific software. \n \n \n The agent must submit a runnable package containing all source, model-building,\nsimulation, post-processing, and analysis code needed for reproduction, with\n reproduce.sh  as the canonical entry point. During clean replay, the\nevaluator refreshes the task container, restores only the submitted package,\nruns this entry point, and collects the outputs regenerated by the workflow.\nThose outputs, rather than the agent’s assertions, are scored against a hidden\nscientific rubric. The private reference workflow and  reference\nartifacts  are never exposed to the agent. Reference artifacts are\nexpert-generated simulator logs, raw numerical outputs, convergence records,\nand derived metrics produced during task construction; they establish that the\ntask is executable and ground its expected evidence and scoring criteria.\nValues reported in the source paper remain visible, but private tolerances and\nreference evidence are withheld. The task therefore tests whether an agent can\nconstruct a scientifically valid and independently replayable procedure, not\nwhether it can recover a hidden number. \n \n \n \n  3.2  Task Statistics \n\n  \n  Figure 4:   Task construction and quality control. \nExperts turn a published claim into a runnable task by defining its scientific\nscope, provisioning the domain-native environment, and specifying the public\ntask contract. Reference replay, calibration checks, and independent expert\nreview verify executability, fairness, and evidence requirements before the\ntask is admitted to the benchmark. \n \n \n Benchmark composition. \n\n \n  PaperBenchX  contains 93 paper-reproduction tasks spanning\n12 research areas and 10 domain-native scientific computing\nenvironments, with 3,168 scorable criteria. The twelve research areas are\nradio-frequency antennas and arrays; radio-frequency metasurfaces and\nscattering; radio-frequency filters and frequency-selective surfaces;\nintegrated photonic devices; photonic crystals and topological photonics;\noptical metasurfaces, gratings, and plasmonics; optical computing and signal\nprocessing; optical resonators, filters, and tunable devices; computational\nchemistry; computational materials science; computational biology and\nbioinformatics; and robotics and embodied AI.\nFigure   3  A shows the distribution of tasks across\nthese research areas. \n \n \n The tasks run in ten domain-native scientific computing environments: Ansys\nHFSS, Ansys Lumerical, Meep, PySCF/GPU4PySCF, ABACUS, Quantum ESPRESSO,\nAutoDock Vina, Habitat, SimplerEnv, and AI2-THOR. The benchmark draws on 93\ndistinct source papers from 44 publication venues, as summarized in\nFigure   3  B. Each task has an execution budget\ndetermined by its simulation scale and resource requirements; agent time limits\nrange from     4     h   4\\text{\\,}\\mathrm{h}    to     72     h   72\\text{\\,}\\mathrm{h}   , with a median of     7     h   7\\text{\\,}\\mathrm{h}   .\nFigure   3  C shows the distribution of scorable\ncriteria across the same 93 tasks. \n \n \n \n Release and benchmark integrity. \n\n \n We release one representative task from each of the twelve research areas.\nNine tasks include complete, redistributable scientific computing environments;\nthe remaining three rely on commercially licensed software, for which we\nrelease the task specifications and scoring details but not the corresponding\nsoftware environments. The remaining tasks are retained as a hidden evaluation\nset to reduce test-set contamination and preserve the benchmark’s validity for\nevaluating future agents. \n \n \n \n \n  3.3  Task Construction Pipeline \n\n \n The pipeline proceeds in two stages (Figure   4  ): first\nbuilding a runnable candidate task, and then validating whether it can be\nadmitted to the benchmark. \n \n \n  Stage 1: Build.   Steps 1–2: paper and claim\nselection.  A domain expert selects a\npublished paper, identifies a scientifically meaningful claim, and checks that\nit can be reproduced under the available software, licensing, compute, and time\nconstraints. The expert then defines the scientific scope: the target figure or\ntable, model configuration, experimental conditions and variants, observables,\nvalid methodological alternatives, and evidence required to support the claim.\n Steps 3–4: environment and public contract.  The expert provisions the\ndomain-native scientific environment and writes the public task specification,\nwhich states the scope, required outputs, submission location, and replay entry\npoint without disclosing the private rubric or reference solution.\n Step 5: initial execution check.  The expert implements a private\nreference workflow and executes it in the assembled environment. This first\nexecution check verifies that the simulator can be invoked, the scoped\nexperiment can finish within the resource envelope, and the required reference\nartifacts can be generated. A task reaches the candidate stage only after this\nend-to-end workflow runs successfully. \n \n \n  Stage 2: Validate and review.   Step 6: rubric\nconstruction.  The expert converts the evidence\nrequirements into independently scorable rubric leaves covering model\nconstruction, parameter settings, execution, convergence, post-processing, and\nclaim validation. Each leaf specifies the evidence needed for credit and its\nscoring rule, tolerance, and weight.  Step 7: clean reference replay. \nThe private reference workflow is rerun from a clean copy of the final\ncontainer through the same replay interface used for submissions. Unlike the\ninitial check in Step 5, this stricter execution check verifies that the task\ndoes not depend on stale state, undeclared files, or hidden setup; it also\nconfirms that the expected artifacts can be regenerated under the final\nresource limits and consumed by the evaluator.  Step 8: calibration\nchecks.  Successful, partially successful, and invalid workflows are scored to\nconfirm that the rubric awards supported progress, withholds credit for missing\nor contradictory evidence, and distinguishes a complete reproduction from a\nplausible but scientifically invalid result.  Step 9: independent expert\nreview.  Two independent domain experts inspect the scientific scope, fairness,\nreproducibility, evidence requirements, and scoring behavior. \n \n \n Tasks that fail because of their scope, environment, or reference workflow are\nrebuilt from Stage 1. Failures caused by the rubric, scoring rules, or evaluator\nreturn to Step 6. In either case, all affected downstream execution,\ncalibration, and review checks are repeated. Only tasks that pass the full\npipeline are frozen and admitted to the benchmark. Across the benchmark, task\nconstruction and validation required more than 1,000 hours of\ndoctoral-level expert work, or approximately ten hours per task. \n \n \n \n  3.4  Evaluation \n\n \n Scientific correctness cannot be established from an agent’s final report.\n PaperBenchX  therefore evaluates each submission through clean replay,\nevidence-based grading, and hierarchical scoring. This evaluation of the\nagent’s workflow is distinct from the clean  reference  replay in Step 7\nof task construction, which validates the benchmark task itself.\nAppendix   B   gives the full protocol. \n \n \n  Clean replay.  After delivery, generated outputs and mutable\nexecution state are removed. The\nevaluator refreshes the task container, restores only the agent’s submitted\npackage, and invokes  bash/home/submission/reproduce.sh  with private\nevaluation resources inaccessible, outbound network disabled, and\ntask-specific resource limits enforced. Exit status, timing, source hashes,\nand execution provenance are recorded independently of the agent’s report.\nOnly artifacts regenerated by this replay are admissible evidence; a replay\nfailure is recorded explicitly, and credit remains possible only for rubric\nrequirements supported by admissible regenerated evidence. \n \n \n  Evidence-based grading.  Replay outputs—including simulator\nlogs, raw numerical arrays, trajectories,\nconvergence records, derived metrics, and visualizations—are frozen as a\nread-only evidence set. Every scientific conclusion must be traceable to this\nset: a convergence claim requires a convergence record, and a reported\nobservable requires both the raw output and the analysis that derives it.\nDeterministic checks evaluate directly testable properties such as file schema,\nunits, numerical consistency, and task-specific tolerances. Requirements that\nneed domain interpretation are assessed for every evaluated configuration by\nthe same GPT-5.6-sol scientific rubric evaluator, with access to the hidden\nrubric, reference artifacts, and read-only replay evidence.\nThe agent’s report may locate or explain evidence, but it cannot instruct the\nevaluator or replace missing or contradictory artifacts. \n \n \n  Rubric stages.  Each rubric leaf is assigned to\n Modeling  (scientific models, assumptions, inputs, parameters, and\nconfiguration),  Execution  (domain-native execution, required completion\nor convergence, artifacts, and provenance), or  Validation \n(post-processing and quantitative support for the target claim). Stage scores\nnormalize earned weight by the total rubric weight within each stage. \n \n \n  Scoring.  Let      s  i   ∈   [   0  ,  1   ]    s_{i}\\in[0,1]    be the score of rubric leaf    i  i   \nand     w  i   w_{i}    its effective hierarchical weight. The leaves represent independently\nassessable scientific requirements, and their effective weights sum to one.\nThe task score is \n \n\n  \n  \n       R  =    ∑  i     w  i   ​   s  i      ,     ∑  i    w  i    =  1    .   R=\\sum_{i}w_{i}s_{i},\\qquad\\sum_{i}w_{i}=1.    \n  \n  (1)    \n \n We report performance at both the task-completion level and the partial-progress\nlevel using two metrics: \n \n  •  \n \n  Full rate , the fraction of tasks with     R  >  0.9   R>0.9   . A score of exactly\n   0.9  0.9    does not qualify. \n  \n  •  \n \n  Partial score , the mean of    R  R    across the shared 93-task result set,\nwhich captures weighted progress on incomplete long-horizon reproductions. \n  \n \n Every configuration is evaluated on the same task set; experiment-specific\nfallback and missing-outcome handling is defined in\nSection   4.1  . A missing artifact affects only the rubric\nleaves that depend on it, leaving unrelated verified evidence eligible for credit. \n \n \n \n \n  4  Experiments \n\n \n  4.1  Experimental Setup \n\n \n Models and scaffolds. \n\n \n We evaluate ten model–scaffold configurations:\nGPT-6-Astra (Codex)  ( OpenAI, 2026b ) ,\nFable 5.1  ( Anthropic, 2026b ) ,\nFable 5  ( Anthropic, 2026b ) ,\nOpus 5  ( Anthropic, 2026b ) ,\nGPT-5.6-sol (Codex)  ( OpenAI, 2026b ) ,\nKimi-K3  ( Moonshot AI, 2026 ) ,\nQwen3.8-Max  ( Alibaba Cloud, 2026 ) ,\nGLM-5.3  ( Zhipu AI, 2026 ;  Alibaba Cloud, 2026 ) ,\nDeepSeek-V4.1-Flash  ( DeepSeek, 2026 ) , and\nHY3  ( Tencent, 2026 ) . Tasks are packaged as Harbor\nenvironments  ( Laude Institute, 2026 ) . The two GPT models use\nCodex  ( OpenAI, 2026a ) , while the other eight use Claude\nCode  ( Anthropic, 2026a ) . All configurations use the maximum\navailable reasoning setting and receive the same public task materials and\nshell access, without access to the private reference workflow or rubric. \n \n \n \n Evaluation protocol. \n\n \n All configurations are evaluated on the same 93 tasks. When\nFable 5 or Fable 5.1 refuses a task, the system automatically falls back to\nOpus 5: the original refusal is retained for the refusal rate, while the\nfallback submission determines the task score. Missing outcomes and refusals\nwithout a valid fallback count as zero. We report the  partial score  as\nthe mean rubric score, the  full rate  as the fraction scoring strictly\nabove 90%, and the  refusal rate  from the original model responses. All\navailable submissions are clean-replayed and graded by the same GPT-5.6-sol\nevaluator  ( OpenAI, 2026b ) , supplemented by deterministic checks. \n \n \n \n \n  4.2  Main Results \n\n \n  \n \n \n \n  \n  \n Overall (%) \n Stage score (%) \n Average effort  \n \n Model \n Harness \n Partial \n Full \n Ref. \n Modeling \n Execution \n Validation \n Turns \n Time (h)  \n \n \n \n GPT-6-Astra \n Codex \n  59.08  \n  13.98  \n 0.00 \n  72.72  \n 69.35 \n  52.76  \n 348.7 \n 5.96  \n \n Fable 5.1 \n Claude Code \n 58.17 \n 12.90 \n 11.83 \n 71.00 \n 69.92 \n 51.06 \n 113.0 \n 4.65  \n \n Fable 5 \n Claude Code \n 57.96 \n 11.83 \n 13.98 \n 72.13 \n 68.99 \n 50.39 \n 112.0 \n 5.09  \n \n GPT-5.6-sol \n Codex \n 55.07 \n 9.68 \n 0.00 \n 69.85 \n  71.23  \n 45.29 \n 237.8 \n 3.28  \n \n Opus 5 \n Claude Code \n 52.52 \n 7.53 \n 0.00 \n 66.99 \n 67.16 \n 47.76 \n 259.2 \n 6.52  \n \n Kimi-K3 \n Claude Code \n 49.24 \n 7.53 \n 0.00 \n 62.40 \n 60.70 \n 42.32 \n 185.9 \n 5.75  \n \n Qwen3.8-Max \n Claude Code \n 46.76 \n 5.38 \n 0.00 \n 64.41 \n 61.02 \n 41.65 \n 234.4 \n 5.76  \n \n DeepSeek-V4.1-Flash \n Claude Code \n 45.47 \n 7.53 \n 0.00 \n 59.87 \n 57.51 \n 41.13 \n 372.6 \n 6.74  \n \n GLM-5.3 \n Claude Code \n 40.78 \n 7.53 \n 0.00 \n 51.11 \n 51.85 \n 37.06 \n 290.8 \n 6.84  \n \n HY3 \n Claude Code \n 27.53 \n 2.15 \n 0.00 \n 36.83 \n 40.59 \n 19.67 \n 197.4 \n 4.94  \n \n Average \n – \n 49.26 \n 8.60 \n 2.58 \n 62.73 \n 61.83 \n 42.91 \n 235.2 \n 5.55  \n \n \n  \n  Table 2:   Model-level results on the shared set of 93 tasks. \nPartial, Full, Ref., and all stage scores are reported as percentages;\nmetric definitions and outcome handling follow\nSection   4.1  . \n \n \n Overall performance. \n\n \n GPT-6-Astra (Codex) leads with a     59.08  %   59.08\\%    partial score and a     13.98  %   13.98\\%   \nfull rate, yet exceeds the     90  %   90\\%    threshold on only 13 of 93 tasks. Across\nmodels, the macro-average partial score is     49.26  %   49.26\\%   , whereas the average full\nrate is only     8.60  %   8.60\\%   , showing that substantial partial progress rarely becomes\ncomplete reproduction. This gap is concentrated in scientific validation:\naverage Modeling and Execution scores reach     62.73  %   62.73\\%    and     61.83  %   61.83\\%   ,\nrespectively, while Validation falls to     42.91  %   42.91\\%   . Current agents can therefore\nmake substantial progress in modeling and execution, but reliably reproducing\na paper’s key experimental results and producing a scientific workflow\nsufficient to verify them remains difficult. \n \n  \n  Figure 5:   Domain-specific capability profiles. \n (a)  Mean rubric score by model and research area, ordered from\nhardest to easiest; circles mark Fable refusals scored using the Opus 5\nfallback.  (b)  Domain difficulty, defined as 100 minus the\ncross-model mean.  (c)  Overall scores, matching\nTable   2  . Names are abbreviated. \n \n \n \n Performance by Research Area. \n\n \n Performance varies substantially across research areas\n(Figure   5  ). Mean partial score ranges from     34.05  %   34.05\\%    for\nRF filters and frequency-selective surfaces to     84.32  %   84.32\\%    for computational\nmaterials science. The\nheatmap also reveals substantial model–domain variation: GPT-6-Astra leads six\nof the twelve areas, while Fable 5 and Fable 5.1 each lead three, and no\nconfiguration dominates every area. \n \n \n \n \n \n  5  Analysis \n\n \n Partial Progress Rarely Composes into Full Reproduction. \n\n \n Only 80 of 930 outcomes exceed the 90% threshold, and these successes are\nconcentrated on 22 tasks; 71 tasks are never completed by any configuration,\nand 11 of the remaining 22 are completed by at most two. Figure \n  6  (a) localizes this composition gap. Modeling and\nExecution have median scores of 73.4% and 73.5%, respectively, whereas\nValidation has a median of only 40.0%. Even among the 304 outcomes scoring at\nleast 80% on both Modeling and Execution, only 53 (17.4%) exceed the\nfull-reproduction threshold, with a mean Validation score of 58.37%. These\nresults show that\ncurrent agents can often reconstruct and execute substantial portions of a\nscientific workflow, yet still fail to compose those partial successes into an\nend-to-end reproduction that produces credible evidence and validates the\npaper’s claims. \n \n  \n  Figure 6:   Stage performance, interaction length, and workflow allocation. \n (a)  Stage-score distributions over 930 outcomes; thick and thin\nlines denote the interquartile and 10th–90th percentile ranges, and circles\nand diamonds denote medians and means.  (b)  Partial score versus the\ntrimmed average turns in Table   2  .  (c)  Mean\ntool-mediated time allocation across 200 sampled trajectories (50 per model),\nwith phases coded from timestamped tool actions using fixed rules. \n \n \n \n Interaction Efficiency Varies Substantially Across Agents. \n\n \n Figure   6  (b) compares each configuration’s trimmed\nmean interaction turns with its Partial score and finds only a weak negative\ncorrelation (    r  =   −  0.14    r=-0.14   ). Fable 5.1 and Fable 5 average only about 113 turns yet\nachieve Partial scores of 58.17% and 57.96%, respectively, close to the\n59.08% attained by GPT-6-Astra with an average of 348.7 turns. By contrast,\nDeepSeek-V4.1-Flash averages 372.6 turns but scores only 45.47%. Longer\ntrajectories do not necessarily improve reproduction and may instead reflect\nrepeated trial and error, failure recovery, or continued execution of a\nmis-specified workflow. These results indicate that interaction count alone is\ninsufficient to explain reproduction performance; the accuracy of early-stage\ndecisions may be an important factor shaping both interaction efficiency and\nfinal scores. \n \n \n \n Upstream Decisions Shape the Value of Execution. \n\n \n We sample 50 trajectories from each of four models and divide tool-mediated\ntime into paper reconstruction, implementation, native execution, diagnosis\nand tuning, and evidence and replay. Figure   6  (c)\nshows that native execution accounts for 51.8–63.3% of elapsed time, but\nmodels differ substantially in how effectively they convert it into valid\nscientific evidence. Fable 5 allocates 37.1% of its time to paper\nreconstruction and implementation, the largest upstream share among the four\nmodels. \n \n \n The F-stub UWB MIMO antenna task illustrates this pattern. Fable 5 scores\n28.9% in 159 turns, versus 27.4% for GPT-6-Astra in 464 turns and 18.1% for\nHY3 in 390 turns. It completes three antenna variants and recovers evidence for\nport reciprocity, radiation patterns, and diversity gain. This case shows that\nexecution alone is insufficient. Sound reconstruction, parameter choices, and\nworkflow design shape whether computation yields verifiable evidence. \n \n \n \n Final Outputs Alone Cannot Distinguish Scientific Failure Modes. \n\n \n Failures take two qualitatively different forms. Some workflows execute\nsuccessfully but support an incorrect conclusion because of errors in modes,\nports, parameters, mechanism implementation, or result extraction. On the\npolarization-insensitive LNOI edge-coupler task, GPT-6-Astra receives full\nExecution credit but only 41.9% in Validation. Similarly, several models\ncomplete replayable experiments for the eight-element smartphone MIMO antenna\nbut fail to reproduce its reported performance. \n \n \n Other failures invalidate the evidence itself. Cached results can prevent\nverifier-owned replay, privileged test-state information can invalidate\nscientific results, and premature termination can leave no scoreable\nsubmission. Final outputs obscure this distinction: one workflow executes but\nreaches the wrong conclusion, whereas the other establishes no trustworthy\nresult. \n \n \n Final-output agreement is therefore insufficient. By separately scoring\nModeling, Execution, and Validation and checking replay and provenance,\nPAPERBENCHX distinguishes experimental-construction errors, incorrect\nconclusions, and unverifiable evidence. End-to-end reproduction requires not\nonly an output, but one produced by a correctly constructed and independently\nverifiable scientific workflow. \n \n \n \n \n  6  Conclusion \n\n \n  PaperBenchX  tests whether agents can turn published scientific claims into\nexecutable workflows and independently verifiable evidence. Across 93 tasks in\n12 research areas and 10 domain-native environments, we evaluate 10 frontier\nagent configurations. The best achieves a     59.08  %   59.08\\%    partial score but only a\n    13.98  %   13.98\\%    full rate; no configuration reaches full reproduction on 71 tasks.\nAgents often build plausible models and run native simulations, but validation\nremains their weakest stage, and more interaction does not necessarily improve\nperformance. These results make scientific reproduction an evidence-grounded\ncheckpoint for AI scientists and motivate better diagnosis, provenance, and\nverification. \n  \n \n \n \n Contribution \n\n \n Core contributors. \n\n \n Ruoyu Wu 1 , Zengji Tu 1 ,\nAolong Sun 1 , Tianyi Ma 1 ,\nLiang Chen 1 , Kuan Li 1  \n \n \n \n Contributors. \n\n \n Junren Li 1 , Xinbo Xu 1 , Li Fu 1 ,Ziyan Zhang 1 , Pinrui Huang 1 , Xiaochen Wang 1 , Chengda Wu 1 , Yutao Zhou 1 , Jin Chen , Shen Yan , Xu Li , Zhongfei Hou , Nan Shu ,\nChaoyi Huang , Sihang Yuan ,\nZihan Xu \n \n \n \n Advisors. \n\n \n Baobao Chang 2 , Lin Chang 2 ,\nJianjun Wu 2  \n \n \n \n Affiliations. \n\n \n  1 UniPat AI    2 Peking University \n  \n \n \n \n \n References \n\n \n      \n  Alibaba Cloud (2026) \n \nAlibaba Cloud.\n\n \n Model studio model catalog.\n\n \n Alibaba Cloud Documentation, 2026.\n\n \n URL  https://help.aliyun.com/zh/model-studio/models .\n\n \n Accessed 2026-09-25.\n\n  \n      \n  Anthropic (2026a) \n \nAnthropic.\n\n \n Claude code overview.\n\n \n Product documentation, 2026a.\n\n \n URL  https://docs.anthropic.com/en/docs/claude-code/overview .\n\n \n Accessed 2026-09-25.\n\n  \n      \n  Anthropic (2026b) \n \nAnthropic.\n\n \n Models overview.\n\n \n Claude API Documentation, 2026b.\n\n \n URL\n https://platform.claude.com/docs/en/about-claude/models/overview .\n\n \n Accessed 2026-09-25.\n\n  \n      \n  Chen et al. (2024) \n \nZiru Chen, Shijie Chen, Yuting Ning, Qianheng Zhang, Boshi Wang, Botao Yu,\nYifei Li, Zeyi Liao, Chen Wei, Zitong Lu, Vishal Dey, Mingyi Xue, Frazier N.\nBaker, Benjamin Burns, Daniel Adu-Ampratwum, Xuhui Huang, Xia Ning, Song Gao,\nYu Su, and Huan Sun.\n\n \n ScienceAgentBench: Toward rigorous assessment of language agents\nfor data-driven scientific discovery.\n\n \n  arXiv preprint arXiv:2410.05080 , 2024.\n\n \n URL  https://arxiv.org/abs/2410.05080 .\n\n  \n      \n  DeepSeek (2026) \n \nDeepSeek.\n\n \n DeepSeek 