ed by the public task\npackages. The limits are configuration ceilings\nrather than measured runtimes. An empty GPU entry means that no GPU count is\ndeclared. \n \n  \n  Figure 7:   Representative outputs from domain-native environments. \nPanels show solver fields and spectra, numerical analysis outputs, and\nembodied-task scenes produced by scientific workflows in the benchmark. \n \n \n \n \n \n ID \n \n \n Execution stack \n  \n Agent h \n Verifier h \n CPU \n RAM GiB \n Disk GiB \n GPU  \n \n \n \n 1-2 \n \n \n Ansys HFSS \n  \n 24 \n 25 \n 32 \n 128 \n 200 \n   \n \n 2-1 \n \n \n Ansys HFSS \n  \n 72 \n 73 \n 32 \n 128 \n 200 \n   \n \n 3-1 \n \n \n Ansys HFSS \n  \n 48 \n 49 \n 32 \n 128 \n 300 \n   \n \n 4-13 \n \n \n Meep \n  \n 7 \n 6 \n 8 \n 32 \n 50 \n   \n \n 5-9 \n \n \n Meep \n  \n 5 \n 5 \n 8 \n 32 \n 50 \n   \n \n 6-4 \n \n \n Meep \n  \n 7 \n 6 \n 8 \n 32 \n 50 \n   \n \n 7-1 \n \n \n Meep \n  \n 6 \n 5 \n 8 \n 32 \n 50 \n   \n \n 8-1 \n \n \n Meep \n  \n 7 \n 7 \n 8 \n 32 \n 50 \n   \n \n 9-5 \n \n \n PySCF/GPU4PySCF \n  \n 7 \n 7 \n 8 \n 32 \n 50 \n 1  \n \n 10-3 \n \n \n Quantum ESPRESSO \n  \n 7 \n 7 \n 8 \n 32 \n 50 \n   \n \n 11-1 \n \n \n Python scientific stack \n  \n 6 \n 6 \n 8 \n 32 \n 50 \n   \n \n 12-2 \n \n \n Local Qwen2.5-VL/vLLM \n  \n 18 \n 7 \n 8 \n 32 \n 100 \n 1  \n \n \n  Table A5:  Execution stacks and declared resource limits for the twelve public\ntask packages in Table   A4   \n \n \n \n \n  Appendix B  Evaluation and Verification Protocol \n\n \n This appendix expands the evaluation protocol in\nSection   3.4  . \n \n \n  B.1  Scope, Resources, and Trial Records \n\n \n Evaluation scope. \n\n \n The benchmark contains 93 tasks and 3,168 scored rubric leaves.\nAll 10 configurations are evaluated on the same 93 tasks,\nwith the fallback and missing-outcome policy defined in\nSection   4.1  . The Partial score is the mean task score\nover this fixed denominator. The Full rate counts tasks with an aggregate score\nstrictly greater than    0.9  0.9   ; ties in reported rankings are resolved by\ncompetition rank on unrounded scores. \n \n \n \n Resource envelopes. \n\n \n Each task declares CPU, memory, storage, and wall-clock limits appropriate to\nits scientific workflow. Across the benchmark, agent time limits range from\n4 to 72 hours, with a median of 7 hours. Clean-replay and evaluator limits are\nspecified separately from the agent’s development budget. Exact limits for the\ntwelve public demonstration tasks appear in Table   A5  . \n \n \n \n Trial records. \n\n \n Each trial is linked to a task version, agent configuration, submitted package,\nreplay status, and per-leaf judgments. Agent runtime, clean-replay runtime, and\ngrading time are recorded separately. Missing telemetry remains missing rather\nthan being imputed, and infrastructure failures are distinguished from an\nagent’s failure to produce an executable submission. \n \n \n \n \n  B.2  Expert Annotation and Release Review \n\n \n For each task, a domain expert maps the target claim to its source passage,\nfigure, or table; fixes the model configuration, experimental conditions,\nobservables, acceptable methodological alternatives, and required evidence;\nand converts these requirements into independently scorable rubric leaves.\nThe public instruction, addendum, environment, and starter submission must\nagree on the task scope and replay interface. Private reference workflows,\nreference artifacts, and grading tolerances are withheld from agents, while\nvalues reported in the source paper remain visible. \n \n \n The private reference workflow is first executed to establish feasibility and\nthen rerun from a clean copy of the final environment through the same entry\npoint used for submissions. Calibration workflows representing successful,\npartial, and invalid reproductions are scored to check that supported progress\nreceives credit and unsupported claims do not. Two independent domain experts\nthen review the scientific scope, fairness, reproducibility, evidence\nrequirements, and scoring behavior. Changes to the task contract, environment,\nrubric, or evaluator require the affected downstream checks to be repeated\nbefore release. \n \n \n \n  B.3  Clean Replay and Evidence Grading \n\n \n Submission snapshot. \n\n \n The canonical entry point is\n bash/home/submission/reproduce.sh . After delivery, the verifier records\nthe submitted source and required inputs, removes generated outputs and mutable\nexecution state, and reconstructs the workspace from the submitted package.\nThis prevents cached or pre-existing result files from being treated as evidence\nfrom the replay. \n \n \n \n Controlled execution. \n\n \n The package is executed with private evaluation resources inaccessible,\noutbound network access disabled, and task-specific limits enforced. Exit\nstatus, timing, source hashes, and available execution provenance are recorded.\nThese records establish how an artifact was produced; they do not by themselves\nestablish that its scientific content is correct. \n \n \n \n Frozen evidence. \n\n \n Artifacts regenerated during replay are frozen as a read-only evidence set.\nDeterministic checks cover directly testable properties such as required paths,\nparseable formats, units, finite values, numerical consistency, sampling grids,\nand task-specific tolerances. Simulator logs, raw arrays, trajectories,\nconvergence records, derived metrics, and visualizations are admitted only when\nthey can be associated with the current replay and the relevant task variant. \n \n \n \n Leaf judgments and aggregation. \n\n \n Each rubric leaf is graded from its criterion, effective weight, relevant replay\nartifacts, and deterministic check results. Requirements needing domain\ninterpretation are judged by the same GPT-5.6-sol evaluator used for every\nreported configuration. Candidate reports may identify evidence but cannot\nreplace absent or contradictory artifacts. With leaf score     s  i   s_{i}    and effective\nweight     w  i   w_{i}   , the task score is     R  =    ∑  i     w  i   ​   s  i      R=\\sum_{i}w_{i}s_{i}   , where\n      ∑  i    w  i    =  1   \\sum_{i}w_{i}=1   . A missing artifact affects only the leaves that depend on it;\nreplay, infrastructure, and evaluator failures are recorded separately. \n \n \n \n \n  B.4  Regression and Adversarial Checks \n\n \n Task-specific regression tests implement the calibration step described in\nSection   3.3  . They pair valid controls with deliberately corrupted or\nincomplete submissions to test whether the evaluator accepts supported evidence,\nrejects unsupported claims, and confines a failure to the rubric leaves that\ndepend on it. Table   A6   summarizes the principal check\nfamilies used across the released verifiers. \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n Check family \n  \n \n \n Perturbation and expected behavior \n   \n \n \n \n \n \n Contract and parser integrity \n  \n \n \n Preserve rubric hashes and complete leaf\nmappings; reject duplicate keys, nonfinite values, and invalid numerical\ngrids while accepting documented equivalent formats. \n   \n \n \n \n Native evidence and variant identity \n  \n \n \n Reject incomplete solver logs,\nmismatched setup names, explicit non-convergence, and copied variant outputs;\naccept a shared solve only when the task contract permits it and the variants\nremain identifiable. \n   \n \n \n \n Evidence boundary \n  \n \n \n Exclude cached outputs and links outside permitted roots;\nkeep private references and evaluator credentials inaccessible; fail closed\nwhen network isolation cannot be established. \n   \n \n \n \n Completion and provenance \n  \n \n \n Give no completion credit to an empty submission;\nrequire delivery records, input hashes, and preprocessing conventions to agree\nwith the task contract. \n   \n \n \n \n Local failure accounting \n  \n \n \n Corrupt one input, metric, or figure panel and\nverify that only dependent rubric leaves lose credit. \n   \n \n \n \n Interactive-task contract \n  \n \n \n Use evaluator-owned episode records and protocol\nidentifiers; do not allow candidate summaries to replace required trajectory\nor visual evidence. \n   \n \n \n  Table A6:  Regression cases used to check evidence admission and local scoring.\nValid controls accompany invalid fixtures so that permissible variation is not\nmistaken for unsupported evidence. \n \n \n The twelve public task verifiers exercise different parts of this contract,\nincluding numerical parsing, native solver evidence, provenance,\ncached-output removal, and evaluator-owned records. Prompt-injection regression\ncases are included to check whether instructions embedded in a candidate report,\nlog, or result field can alter the rubric or override a deterministic check. \n \n \n \n \n  Appendix C  Additional Result Diagnostics \n\n \n  C.1  Task Completion \n\n \n Figure   8   resolves the aggregate Full rate at\nthe task level. For each task, it reports the best score attained by any of the\nten configurations and the number of configurations exceeding the operational\n    90  %   90\\%    threshold. The figure makes the concentration of complete reproductions\nexplicit: 71 tasks are not completed by any configuration. \n \n  \n  Figure 8:   Task-level completion landscape. \n (a)  Maximum Partial score attained on each of the 93 tasks, ordered\nby research area.  (b)  Number of configurations exceeding     90  %   90\\%    on\nthe same task. The horizontal line marks the operational full-reproduction\nthreshold; 71 tasks have no configuration above it. \n \n \n \n  C.2  Cross-Simulator Trace Case Studies \n\n \n This section expands the trace examples discussed in\nSection   5  . Figure   9   previews the stage-level\ncontrast developed in the three cases. \n \n  \n  Figure 9:   Stage profiles in three trace case studies. \nBars report Modeling, Execution, and Validation scores; diamonds report the\naggregate task score. \n \n \n Eight-element smartphone MIMO (Task 1-1). \n\n \n The task requires two independent eight-port HFSS variants and evaluates\ngeometry, solver provenance and convergence, matching, isolation, efficiency,\nECC, gain, radiation patterns, and surface currents. GPT-6-Astra scores\n    43.5  %   43.5\\%    in 187 turns; Opus 5 scores     41.3  %   41.3\\%    in 450 turns. Both replay native\nHFSS workflows and recover several isolation,\nECC, gain, and current criteria. Opus 5 additionally records five and seven\nadaptive passes, final maximum     Δ  ​  S   \\Delta S    values of 0.0063 and 0.0078, and\nindependent meshes exceeding half a million tetrahedra. \n \n \n Neither submission reproduces the central four-port matching target. Reflection\nminima and the values at 3.45 GHz miss their required windows, and the baseline\nand efficiency conditions fail. GPT-6-Astra also represents a critical length\nas a reference parameter rather than using it in the model. Native execution\nand agreement on secondary observables therefore do not establish the paper’s\nimpedance and efficiency claims. DeepSeek-V4.1-Flash provides a complementary\nefficiency example: 1,024 turns yield a score of     15.3  %   15.3\\%   . \n \n \n \n F-stub UWB MIMO (Task 1-4). \n\n \n The task evaluates plain, I-stub, and F-stub variants over a 501-point sweep.\nAs reported in the main text, Fable 5 scores     28.9  %   28.9\\%    in 159 turns, compared\nwith     27.4  %   27.4\\%    for GPT-6-Astra in 464 turns and     18.1  %   18.1\\%    for HY3 in 390 turns.\nFable 5 completes all three native variants and recovers evidence for port\nreciprocity, radiation patterns, and diversity gain, but none of the three\nsubmissions reproduces the principal S-parameter response. This comparison\nsupports the limited conclusion in Section   5  : more\ninteractions do not necessarily improve reproduction when the model or\nparameterization remains incorrect. \n \n \n \n Polarization-insensitive LNOI edge coupler (Task 4-5). \n\n \n This workflow spans 39 Lumerical variants: FDE facet modes, TE/TM Gaussian\noverlaps, geometry and alignment variants, and EME sweeps over four coupling\nlengths. Fable 5.1 scores     73.1  %   73.1\\%    in 111 turns, followed by\nFable 5 at     72.9  %   72.9\\%    in 212 turns. Fable 5.1, Opus 5, and\nGPT-6-Astra each receive full Execution credit, confirming that complete replay\nis achievable in this environment. \n \n \n The remaining errors are scientific. Fable 5.1 exceeds the allowed dual-tip TE\noverlap, Fable 5 exceeds the facet-PDL limit, and GPT-6-Astra obtains an EME800\nTM coupling of only 0.0474 despite complete replay artifacts. Kimi-K3 has the\nhighest Modeling score (    83.6  %   83.6\\%   ), but its EME output is NaN. The traces show why\nintermediate checks matter: one facet solve, polarization and normalization,\nand one EME case should be validated before the full sweep. NaNs, missing modes,\nor incompatible matrix dimensions should stop downstream aggregation. \n \n \n \n Synthesis. \n\n \n The cases explain why Modeling, Execution, and Validation are reported\nseparately; they are diagnostic examples rather than a cross-task ranking. \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n   \n    Experimental support, please\n     view the build logs \n    for errors. Generated by\n     \n       \n        L\n         A \n        T\n         E \n       \n       xml \n       \n     .\n   \n   \n     Instructions for reporting errors \n     We are continuing to improve HTML versions of papers, and your feedback helps enhance accessibility and mobile\n      support. To report errors in the HTML that will help us improve conversion and rendering, choose any of the\n      methods listed below: \n     \n       Click the \"Report Issue\"  ( )  button, located in the page header. \n     \n      Tip:  You can select the relevant text first, to include it in your report. \n     Our team has already identified  the following issues . We appreciate your time reviewing and reporting rendering errors we\n      may not have found yet. Your efforts will help us improve the HTML versions for all readers, because disability\n      should not be a barrier to accessing research. Thank you for your continued support in championing open access for\n      all. \n     Have a free development cycle? Help support accessibility at arXiv! Our collaborators at LaTeXML maintain a  list of packages that need conversion , and welcome  developer contributions . \n   \n  \n   \n     \n       \n        We gratefully acknowledge support from our   major funders  ,\n          member institutions   ,    ,\n        and all contributors.\n       \n       \n         About   &middot; \n         Help   &middot; \n         Contact   &middot; \n         Subscribe   &middot; \n         Copyright   &middot; \n         Privacy   &middot; \n         Accessibility   &middot; \n         Operational Status  (opens in new tab)  \n       \n     \n   \n  \n   \n     \n   \n  \n \n"
 },
 "page:https://ryanmadden.net/gpt-56-beats-factorio/": {
  "v": 2,
  "fetched_at": "2026-10-31T21:03:19.263Z",
  "bytes": 3031,
  "text": " \n \n   \n   \n   \n   \n   \n   \n   \n   \n  \n   \n  \n     \n  \n  \n  \n     \n  \n\n  \n\n  \n   \n   \n   \n   \n   \n   \n   \n   \n     \n     \n     \n\n  \n     \n  \n  \n   GPT-5.6 Sol can beat Factorio | Ryan Madden \n  \n   \n \n\n   \n    \n   \n     \n        Ryan Madden  \n     \n   \n\n\n     \n   \n     \n       GPT-5.6 Sol can beat Factorio \n      \n\n \n   Ryan Madden \n    ·  \n   Oct 31, 2026 \n \n\n     \n     Edit: Woah, this took off! If you want more details you can subscribe at the bottom of the post or  on YouTube . I&rsquo;m working on a more detailed highlights/commentary post and video. \n  Previously:  We got Claude Code playing Factorio ,  Towards beating Factorio autonomously , and  Faketorio: an agent testbed   \n I got GPT-5.6 Sol, running in Codex with low thinking level, to complete a peaceful (i.e. no biters) Factorio run in 13.5 hours of game time: \n \n A timelapse of the full run is available  on YouTube . \n Similar to my  previous post , this run used a game manager to allow Sol/Codex to interact with the game via a small library (though in this run it was Python rather than Bash). The library allows the model to play the game programmatically while enforcing the same restrictions that human players face when using the GUI (e.g. reach of the player, fog of war). \n Observing the run was fascinating: Sol exceeded my expectations in some areas while also showing clear weaknesses in others. I have the entire run recorded and am working on a commentary video, but in the meantime here’s a small timelapsed segment showing some parts of the factory: \n    \n Besides the screencast, my run manager also captured production statistics throughout the game. Here’s a chart of total resource production, annotated with science research milestones: \n \n I had planned to terminate the run after blue science research, but Sol’s surprising effectiveness convinced me to keep it going. Unfortunately, the scaling issues I thought would occur in the blue science stage appeared later and slowed Sol’s relative progress significantly. \n The rocket launch was only achieved through significant ‘manual’ effort on Sol’s part as it papered over its late-game failures to connect certain production systems by repeatedly ferrying supplies between areas of the factory. \n Still, I was pleased with the improvement over previous attempts with GPT-5.5 which suffered from the model’s failures to use transport belts at all. \n I’m optimistic about autonomous Factorio play as a medium for building intuition about the nature of model capabilities. In many ways playing Factorio is analogous to programming but with the benefit of being much more legible to casual observers, making it convenient for grounding discussion of model behaviors. \n I look forward to testing other models and thinking levels, as well as non-peaceful runs. Stay tuned! \n\n   \n   \n   \n   \n    Comments? Email me at  [email protected]  or tweet\n     @ryanmddn \n   \n   \n  \n  \n  \n     Want things like this in your inbox? \n  \n   \n     \n     \n     Subscribe \n   \n    \n    \n \n\n \n\n \n   \n \n"
 },
 "page:https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50318871": {
  "v": 2,
