  "text": "        GPT-5.6 Sol can beat Factorio on peaceful mode | Hacker News                  Hacker News   new  |  past  |  comments  |  ask  |  show  |  jobs  |  submit      login                           GPT-5.6 Sol can beat Factorio on peaceful mode   (  ryanmadden.net  )          104 points  by  ryanmadden    2 hours ago      |  hide  |  past  |  favorite  |  105 comments                          help    \n      \n                    mwt    1 hour ago       |  next   [–]       \n  > The rocket launch was only achieved through significant ‘manual’ effort on Sol’s part as it papered over its late-game failures to connect certain production systems by repeatedly ferrying supplies between areas of the factory. Robots: they're just like us!      reply                             root_axis    58 minutes ago       |  parent  |  next   [–]       \n  >  Robots: they're just like us!  Hah I dunno, I think most human players wouldn't find that very fun and would instead take that as an opportunity to refactor the factory for optimized performance (i.e. the core gameplay loop)      reply                             outworlder    32 minutes ago       |  root  |  parent  |  next   [–]       \n  Ish. When you just need a few parts to be made, it is sometimes very tempting to drop a chest, fill it up with parts, then go do something else, one of the 1000 tasks you have been neglecting.      reply                             root_axis    12 minutes ago       |  root  |  parent  |  next   [–]       \n  That's definitely true, but only to a certain point, after a while it becomes too monotonous to dig around in chests building and combining stuff in an ad hoc manner. LLMs have no problem with that kind of repetitive minutia though.      reply                             8note    1 hour ago       |  parent  |  prev  |  next   [–]       \n  i think its more of an effect of factorio's hidden information. youre not really sure how to set up some of the logistics/how much of what you will need where ahead of time      reply                             edaemon    52 minutes ago       |  root  |  parent  |  next   [–]       \n  What information is hidden in Factorio? I can't think of any stat that isn't shown to the player in some way.      reply                             pjerem    45 minutes ago       |  root  |  parent  |  next   [–]       \n  He is not talking about stats. When you start a new game in Factorio, if you never played it, you can’t know how much of each resource you’ll have to extract and neither how much of an item you’ll need to produce. I know it’s easy to forgot after thousands hours of playtime, but the game starts with your character having to manually mine coal one by one.      reply                             AngryData    15 minutes ago       |  root  |  parent  |  next   [–]       \n  Technically every item displays requirements and crafting time, but most human players will just eyeball production rates until they start trying to build larger scale factories that you can't easily jerry-rig fixes onto. Possibly there is a lack of item-throughput figures for belts and stuff though.      reply                             deepsun    26 minutes ago       |  root  |  parent  |  prev  |  next   [–]       \n  Not true, every item shows its raw materials right there in its menu. The rest is just arithmetic. Also the LLM probably has seen dozens of factorio-related websites and setups. And also, player (artificial or natural intelligence) don't need to setup everything upfront, it's actually the main gameplay feature is to run between production areas to expand/optimize them.      reply                             parineum    18 minutes ago       |  root  |  parent  |  prev  |  next   [–]       \n  That information is certainly in the training data.      reply                             jstummbillig    1 hour ago       |  root  |  parent  |  prev  |  next   [–]       \n  How do you mean? (I have played 1 hour of Factorio)      reply                             lifeisloving    49 minutes ago       |  root  |  parent  |  next   [–]       \n  I didnt think was possible to play for less than 1k hours.      reply                             sulam    40 minutes ago       |  root  |  parent  |  next   [–]       \n  We found the business guy.      reply                             scrollaway    1 hour ago       |  root  |  parent  |  prev  |  next   [–]       \n  What do you mean? Factorio is an extremely calculable game and tends to show most of its information (ratios aside, those are in the wikis)      reply                             Cpoll    47 minutes ago       |  root  |  parent  |  next   [–]       \n  Unless I misunderstand, ratios are second-order and can be calculated; production rates are presented in-game.      reply                             minimaxir    34 minutes ago       |  prev  |  next   [–]       \n  If you haven't been keeping an eye on LLMs playing games, GPT 6.1 Sol can new beat games 100% vision-only (without harnesses that give the agent extra information about the game). Pokemon is no longer a difficult challenge for it, even with additional difficulty restrictions such as hard mode and level caps. Recently, GPT 6.1 Sol beat Final Fantasy VII and its bonus bosses vision-only surprisingly quickly. I suspect Opus 5.5 can do even better but there aren't as many people testing it.      reply                             Uehreka    25 minutes ago       |  parent  |  next   [–]       \n  Where are the recordings of these games? I know this kind of stuff must be happening, but it seems oddly difficult to track down the actual recordings, which feel like the really fascinating thing people should be publishing if they’re putting in the time and tokens to do stuff like this. Like I want to see what Pokemon strategies it uses: Does it try to brute force with its starter? Does it try to load all of its HMs onto one or two Pokemon? Does it use Flash? If it needs to catch a new Pokemon to beat a gym leader, how much does it remember about which species it encountered where and what their levels were?      reply                             JimBlackwood    16 minutes ago       |  parent  |  prev  |  next   [–]       \n  How do they feed the game screen to the models? I imagine the models are too slow if you send 60FPS, but I feel like that’s also the case if you maybe feed it 1FPS? If you go any slower, you’ll miss too much info?      reply                             wmf    6 minutes ago       |  root  |  parent  |  next   [–]       \n  I would assume it's pull-based so the agent takes a screenshot whenever it finishes a turn.      reply                             walthamstow    31 minutes ago       |  parent  |  prev  |  next   [–]       \n  Your last sentence mirrors my model choices recently. Opus can do things very well but Sol can do them well for less than 1/4 the price.      reply                             Trung0246    17 minutes ago       |  parent  |  prev  |  next   [–]       \n  Do you have link for the VII bonus boss run?      reply                             mickeyp    1 hour ago       |  prev  |  next   [–]       \n  Watching AI play games is fun, but I can't wait for someone to crack having small LLMs in games to enrich them in useful and interesting ways.      reply                             KronisLV    37 minutes ago       |  parent  |  next   [–]       \n  Not sure about LLMs but I yearn for someone to train neural nets to play the NPCs in a shooter game well but without having aimbots or seeing through walls - basically a “true” version of F.E.A.R. AI where they play simulated squads that overwhelm the player with various tactics and adapt to their playstyle. Or even just make increasingly complex state machines and goal oriented systems which might be more performant but still encode the main rules.      reply                             deepsun    15 minutes ago       |  parent  |  prev  |  next   [–]       \n  I cannot wait for NPCs having a casual conversation. Doesn't seem too hard, as current chatbots are already role-playing.      reply                             jbm    1 hour ago       |  parent  |  prev  |  next   [–]       \n  I have been playing with this.  Having tiny models provide more contextual information when an NPC speaks is cool but the \"small enough\" models tend to go off the rails. It's fun if you have a handful of people playing, but it would quickly go towards negative reviews if you tried to sell it.      reply                             Jtarii    1 hour ago       |  root  |  parent  |  next   [–]       \n  I don't think an LLM should ever be having a two-way conversation with the player. It would be more suited to 2 npcs having conversations amongst themselves. All dialogue delivered to the player should be human written. At most it should be mildly tweaking existing dialogue, e.g updating a line to reflect game events. But as soon as you present a prompt window to the player the entire illusion that you are in a game world instantly dies.      reply                             outworlder    31 minutes ago       |  root  |  parent  |  next   [–]       \n  > I don't think an LLM should ever be having a two-way conversation with the player. It would be more suited to 2 npcs having conversations amongst themselves. All dialogue delivered to the player should be human written. I don't agree. All -important- dialogue yes. Background NPC chatter? Even when studios spend a lot of effort crafting multiple lines, pretty soon you have seen them all. They also don't change based on player actions.      reply                             8note    55 minutes ago       |  root  |  parent  |  prev  |  next   [–]       \n  i think it can work for procedurally generated situations, and the llm is acting more like a translator between data model and player rather than being a data storage layer. thats close to tweaking existing dialog, but with a bit more freedom. you still dont have to give the player arbitrary response      reply                             HDThoreaun    1 hour ago       |  root  |  parent  |  prev  |  next   [–]       \n  Most dialogue in games is crap. I would much rather have an LLM writing the dialogue so that it can include references to player choices over what currently exists.      reply                             CamperBob2    4 minutes ago       |  root  |  parent  |  prev  |  next   [–]       \n  Nothing great was ever built by someone who was sweating the reviews. Fuck 'em, do what feels right to  you.       reply                             jstummbillig    1 hour ago       |  parent  |  prev  |  next   [–]       \n  There has to be a AAAA studio building the new, greatest MMO, that also utilizes LLMs as part of the game loop, surely? (Not sure how prohibitive it would be to do anything interesting with local LLMs of any kind in a big game, given how many platforms/setups you have to make the stuff run smoothly on, and the sheer amount of RAM they would bind)      reply                             modeless    41 minutes ago       |  root  |  parent  |  next   [–]       \n  AAAA studios don't take risks. Financing a top game is way too expensive to gamble on. Only indies and small studios working on cheaper games can risk trying unproven ideas.      reply                             mixtureoftakes    23 minutes ago       |  root  |  parent  |  next   [–]       \n  not taking risks is the biggest risk in the times when the most anticipated game drop (gta 6) is probably going to be decompiled within days if not hours of release      reply                             SeanAnderson    47 minutes ago       |  root  |  parent  |  prev  |  next   [–]       \n   https://proxiai.com/  Will Wright (The Sims, etc) is dabbling with AI in his latest game. No idea if it'll be any good, but there's def companies out there trying.      reply                             scott01    1 hour ago       |  root  |  parent  |  prev  |  next   [–]       \n  In \"Where Winds Meet\" you can talk to NPCs      reply                             Vetch    1 hour ago       |  parent  |  prev  |  next   [–]       \n  With the aid of SOTA LLMs, you can encode specialist intelligence into computer programs without going through the roundabout route of training small LLMs. And even when you do go the route of ML, it doesn't necessarily have to be an LLM or even a Neural network.      reply                             Buttons840    1 hour ago       |  parent  |  prev  |  next   [–]       \n  I hope to experiment with using classic tabular reinforcement learning in game AI one day. Imagine a framework that makes setting up small Q-learning tables easy, and you can just sprinkle dozens of Q-learning algorithms throughout your traditional AI scripting. These old basic algorithms often have mathematical proofs that they will converge to optimal behavior, and they do so fairly quickly. Turning the complex world of the game into a Q-table would require some creativity and judgement, but is not that hard I think. In the end we would have a controllable AI that actually does adapt. That's my hope anyway.      reply                             damowangcy    40 minutes ago       |  parent  |  prev  |  next   [–]       \n  Fable seems to be a potential franchise to do all this; imagine a \"living fantasy world\" where everything truly unfolds based on your decisions, giving NPCs all the functions/actions that a player has.      reply                             8note    1 hour ago       |  parent  |  prev  |  next   [–]       \n  i think hooking in an llm to dwarf fortress for turning the very mechanistic descriptions into prose. same with the adventure mode talk. instead of a constant \"well weve got beasts\" an llm could read a bunch of the language tree to make a unique introduction for each. same with the df artworks. get kitfox to design some art styles for dwarves, elves, goblins, and humans, and then instead of just reading \"this is a fine engraving of a dwarf. it concerns the events of 102 when ulrist fastbuttons was elected mayor. ulrist fastbuttons is sobbing\"      reply                             root_axis    56 minutes ago       |  parent  |  prev  |  next   [–]       \n  SOTA LLMs would probably be fun opponents in turn based 4x.      reply                             jstummbillig    54 minutes ago       |  root  |  parent  |  next   [–]       \n  Will be interesting to find out what they can bring over simply having better SOTA LLM coded opponents (I am sure, we will).      reply                             root_axis    26 minutes ago       |  root  |  parent  |  next   [–]       \n  LLMs could be interesting geopolitical roleplayers if instructed to faithfully assume the leadership traits or cultural biases of the their intended role, especially in games with expressive diplomacy systems like Stellaris or Europa Universalis. It could also make the diplomacy experience a lot richer because the LLM is able to interpret ligusitic tone and other verbal cues. The modern diplomacy experience is basically just a dialog tree with stats and dice rolls attached to it.      reply                             ls612    34 minutes ago       |  root  |  parent  |  prev  |  next   [–]       \n  Unfortunately for it to be sustainable it would need to be an LLM with VRAM usage probably under a couple gigs max so it can run locally on the gaming PC. That said it may well be better than current AI in strategy games.      reply                             root_axis    21 minutes ago       |  root  |  parent  |  next   [–]       \n  Yes, the insane hardware requirements make it totally impractical - that's why I say \"turn based\" because turn based games would at least allow for a practical gameplay experience even with remote LLM APIs.      reply                             thi2    1 hour ago       |  parent  |  prev  |  next   [–]       \n  I would love the revival of the RTS genre with better computer enemies.      reply                             80fj0348i    59 minutes ago       |  root  |  parent  |  next   [–]       \n  Such AIs have existed in Brood War and Dota 2 for a very long time now (pre-LLM), problem is they aren't very fun for humans to play against.      reply                             vlyan    42 minutes ago       |  root  |  parent  |  next   [–]       \n  bah, such a pity we didn't have those in wc3 times. bots sucked badly. I could play online, but my dial-up costed $1 per hour for 250ms latency and near guarantee of at least one disconnect per hour.      reply                             8note    52 minutes ago       |  root  |  parent  |  prev  |  next   [–]       \n  with better being more convincing, rather than just stronger its the same for like a chess bot. i dont want a perfect stockfish that occasionally makes a serious blunder when a dice rolls a certain way. i want one that actually falls into traps      reply                             ItsYan    56 minutes ago       |  root  |  parent  |  prev  |  next   [–]       \n  You could play against an AI right now on the Brood War ladder right now. Granted, you have to make it to S rank first because that's where the bots end up.      reply                             direwolf20    1 hour ago       |  parent  |  prev  |  next   [–]       \n  SS13 but the ship's AI is actually an AI instead of a player in disguise      reply                             vlyan    46 minutes ago       |  parent  |  prev  |  next   [–]       \n  impossible, unfortunately, because LLMs suck at creative writing. without handholding, they are incapable of producing anything but cliche-ridden purple prose for young adults. you can feed it the first half of a book with lots of sex, violence, and profanity, ask it to continue the story, and it will degrade into safe slop within two chapters. both American and Chinese labs aim their models at coding and knowledge work, so they penalize adult themes in post-training.      reply                             mopsi    58 minutes ago       |  parent  |  prev  |  next   [–]       \n  There are already AI-based mods for games like Grand Theft Auto V that make NPCs talk back and follow your verbal commands:  https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=3_Eng-F4akI&t=106s  A little unpolished because it was bolted on by fans and not the original game developers, but otherwise very impressive.      reply                             program_whiz    30 minutes ago       |  prev  |  next   [–]       \n  One limitation of these type of demos (and similar with Minecraft, etc) is that:\n1. Putting into \"peaceful\" mode makes response time a non-factor \n2. These models have read and have access to every guide and video ever published on the subject.  Most humans, especially on first play, learn game systems and interactions from scratch, then develop novel strategies and plans and \"hypothesis test\" their way into beating the game. This is still impressive compared to where we were a few years ago, and being able to synthesize all existing information about a game and then play it to completion in a semi-realistic setting is great. At a quick glance someone might mistake this for \"brilliant AI solves Factorio\", when in reality, given a similar but different game where it wasn't gifted all the information or training data (e.g. massive videos and screenshots) it might fare terribly (as others have pointed out in comments).      reply                             js8    7 minutes ago       |  parent  |  next   [–]       \n  These are really two different challenges - playing the game for the first time with exploration, and beating the game with all the prior knowledge.      reply                             suddenlybananas    19 minutes ago       |  parent  |  prev  |  next   [–]       \n  Shhh you can't say anything anti-hype here.      reply                             VoidWhisperer    1 hour ago       |  prev  |  next   [–]       \n  Factorio is a much easier game without the bugs being a pain in the arse as you expand - i wonder how Sol would handle the bugs      reply                             chrisfosterelli    1 hour ago       |  parent  |  next   [–]       \n  The biggest thing the bugs introduce from an LLMs perspective is a necessity for realtime response. Without them, the LLM can take as long as it wants to decide and do its next operation on the factory. With them, not responding fast enough results in death (particularly early game where a lot of biter combat is player driven). I presume it doesn't do great at this.      reply                             blobbers    1 hour ago       |  root  |  parent  |  next   [–]       \n  Glad to hear we've got LLMs controlling military drones in real life :(      reply                             wmf    4 minutes ago       |  root  |  parent  |  next   [–]       \n  I don't think we do.      reply                             testaccount28    52 minutes ago       |  root  |  parent  |  prev  |  next   [–]       \n  you could pause the game between actions, while the model reasons about what to do next.      reply                             aeriose    12 minutes ago       |  parent  |  prev  |  next   [–]       \n  The issue is most of these play-throughs happen on “normal” response speed. Way too slow for these LLMs to send back and forth and process this many tokens in a quick enough reaction time. I’ve yet to see running these LLMs on Ultra-fast (8x speed on Sol). Wondering if that’s fast enough to do real time      reply                             numlock86    1 hour ago       |  parent  |  prev  |  next   [–]       \n  That was exactly my thought when reading the headline, before even looking into the article. Sure it's still somewhat complex to get to a rocket launch, but without the biters where is the challenge, other than complexity? It's like a big excel sheet with conveyor belts, pipes and electricity poles at that point only.      reply                             Vetch    1 hour ago       |  root  |  parent  |  next   [–]       \n  The more interesting challenge is the one you eliminated, complexity, in getting efficient logistics and arrangements. Dyson Sphere Program (until late in its existence) and Satisfactory don't have waves of enemies attacking your bases but remain challenging and a satisfying puzzle. I remember I used a constraint solver to help me optimize some late game recipes for Satisfactory.      reply                             ang_cire    1 hour ago       |  root  |  parent  |  prev  |  next   [–]       \n  > where is the challenge, other than complexity Patience, for one. I reach a point where I'm looking at a mess of coal-fed sorters that need a complete rip-and-replace with electric ones, and that's usually the point where I close the game and go back to something easier like Kenshi.      reply                             richwater    1 hour ago       |  root  |  parent  |  next   [–]       \n  I know it's a pipe dream, but these kinds of games (Factorio, Satisfactory, etc) would all benefit from sort of brain-game interface where I could just think about what I wanted to do. The tedium and repetition is really what kills the enjoyment for me. The problem solving, planning, forethought, etc are super fun but it's killed by the former.      reply                             fwlr    21 minutes ago       |  root  |  parent  |  next   [–]       \n  Blueprints and construction bots, plus the well-thought-out and extensively battle-tested set of keyboard commands for them, are basically a brain-game interface for Factorio on the level that Vim is for text. For the grandparent’s “rip and replace coal burners” I am hitting mod-D and dragging a selection box over the offending area, then hover-Q on a nearby element I want to use and shift-click to plan it, once I have planned one instance of a subpattern its mod-C select, click hold and walk in a direction to tile it in one dimension, mod-C select, click hold walk to extend this over the other direction. It is now done, the bots will handle everything else - because Wube are an incredible bunch of UX engineers, I can place the new building before the old buildings are even removed, leave, and trust that everything will be done correctly. Highly recommend getting a “Starter Bots” mod that feels appropriately balanced.      reply                             cjbgkagh    1 hour ago       |  root  |  parent  |  prev  |  next   [–]       \n  Factorio rewards the players progression through the game by taking away points of pain. The helper bots being a key transition. Without that pain there would be no reward. The fact that refactoring is expensive in time and complexity is what makes it realistic - it makes the earlier decisions important and consequential. You can always enter creative mode and make whatever changes you want in the game instantly, but in my experience that does make it less fun.      reply                             roncesvalles    1 hour ago       |  root  |  parent  |  prev  |  next   [–]       \n  It's basically programming with really bad ergonomics. I'm a programmer by profession and I was immediately frustrated by Factorio because I realized I'd rather just write code and build a project because it would scratch the exact same itch while actually being useful.      reply                             mh-    1 hour ago       |  root  |  parent  |  next   [–]       \n  I tried really hard to like Factorio, and I bounce off of it each time after playing a save for 4-8 hours for exactly this reason. I know a lot of programmers who love it, so I almost wish I could get into it. Now I'm tempted to try again, because it  feels  like the kind of game I should really enjoy.      reply                             sagarm    1 hour ago       |  root  |  parent  |  prev  |  next   [–]       \n  The biters are just a line item in the spreadsheet. Early players might build a wall with an ammo belt, for example. There's lots of options that don't involve responding in real-time. Though these days I just manually clear the pollution cloud with a pocket full of turrets. The forward deployed engineer model, if you will.      reply                             RHSeeger    1 hour ago       |  root  |  parent  |  prev  |  next   [–]       \n  Its puzzle solving. For a lot of people, that _is_ the fun part.      reply                             tyan-xiao-12    24 minutes ago       |  parent  |  prev  |  next   [–]       \n  The nests stop being a concern once you have significant production and a few hundred logistics bots. Then you just build walls beyond the pollution range and place a gun turret every other square (or lasers at level 10 or above). I only use nauvis for uranium now because my Aquino production is weak (fusion). Late game you rarely go back to nauvis, just send your fleet of platforms to resupply reactor fuel autonomously.      reply                             damowangcy    39 minutes ago       |  parent  |  prev  |  next   [–]       \n  \"AI found decompiling game to fix a bug after being instrcuted to beat it\" sounds like an IPO friendly headline.      reply                             fasterik    1 hour ago       |  prev  |  next   [–]       \n  It doesn't seem to understand the core mechanic of Factorio, which is putting outputs onto belts and routing them to other inputs. It does this in a few places, but for the most part everything is put into chests and moved around by the player.      reply                             tripleee    48 minutes ago       |  parent  |  next   [–]       \n  What if the player's inventory has a higher throughput than belts? Part of the reason players try to automate manual feeding is the annoyance factor, which AI doesn't care about.      reply                             teraflop    17 minutes ago       |  root  |  parent  |  next   [–]       \n  Back of the envelope math: If you measure by the product of throughput and distance traveled, for an item with a stack size of 100 (e.g. metal plates), the player's inventory adds up to roughly the same capacity as 2500 yellow belts. And that's assuming 100% utilization running back and forth in both directions, with no unused inventory space and no wasted time. A basic assembler can manufacture 2500 belts in about 21 minutes. So as a rule of thumb, if you're playing for longer than 21 minutes, belts are worth the investment (at least for bulk transport of raw materials). (I guess maybe add an additional factor of at most √2, since the player can run diagonally and belts are orthogonal.)      reply                             fasterik    43 minutes ago       |  root  |  parent  |  prev  |  next   [–]       \n  Handfeeding only has higher throughput for single trips/a single item type, definitely not for an entire factory. The player inventory and movement speed can't keep hundreds of assemblers working continuously. A high throughput starter factory looks something like this:  https://youtu.be/RI4ocNajf_w?si=O1aRi22riKwiuh_p&t=7493       reply                             Cpoll    37 minutes ago       |  root  |  parent  |  prev  |  next   [–]       \n  A T1 belt throughput is 15 items/s.\nPlayer inventory is 80 slots (2 cargo wagons, incidentally, though players load/unload instantly). Stack size varies, but let's take 75 as an average, which means a player can carry 5600 items. But carrying isn't throughput. If a round-trip takes 30s, player throughput will be 186 items/s. That's a measly 12 T1 belts.      reply                             CrimsonRain    1 hour ago       |  parent  |  prev  |  next   [–]       \n  If you don't have enemies, you have unlimited time. Doing it thru player is what it learned and wasn't pressured to do better (it has no fatigue as opposed to humans).      reply                             root_axis    1 hour ago       |  parent  |  prev  |  next   [–]       \n  It's just easier to do manual stuff when the complexity of scaling the automation becomes cognitively demanding. A new player does mostly everything manually while experienced players have a natural intuition for planning and scaling the automation.      reply                             gavmor    59 minutes ago       |  parent  |  prev  |  next   [–]       \n  Coding agents are bad at architecture, Exhibit no. 12057.      reply                             hartator    1 hour ago       |  parent  |  prev  |  next   [–]       \n  To be fair, I do do the same.      reply                             kanemcgrath    1 hour ago       |  prev  |  next   [–]       \n  It is weird and almost alien how AI designs factories.  When Claude announced Fable 5 they also did a Factorio demo that was the same kind of designs and a lot of hand feeding and direct insertion.  \nIn some sense it proves to me that llms are not just copying and are actually creating a bespoke solution.  But I honestly expected more from frontier models      reply                             Jtarii    1 hour ago       |  parent  |  next   [–]       \n  To them the player character is just as good as a belt for transporting materials. The player character actually does have an extremely high capacity so if you are constantly running between chests your factory will still be reasonably high throughput.      reply                             QuantumNomad_    1 hour ago       |  parent  |  prev  |  next   [–]       \n  > In some sense it proves to me that llms are not just copying and are actually creating a bespoke solution. Is the weirdness of the designs meaningfully beneficial in any way that the AI has figured out, or could it for example be that the training data the LLMs learned from are too different from the type of data it would need to learn from in order to interface properly and play the game better? And then because of that incompleteness, the LLM has to hallucinate a lot and it results in weird outcomes.      reply                             cjbgkagh    26 minutes ago       |  root  |  parent  |  next   [–]       \n  No their weird design are not better in any way - yet.      reply                             plorntus    1 hour ago       |  parent  |  prev  |  next   [–]       \n  I think one of the problems is that AI has no sense of laziness or time. Unless it's in their training data or prompted they're not going to bother automating something      reply                             js8    0 minutes ago       |  root  |  parent  |  next   [–]       \n  Actually if you estimate the time required it's plausible that player feeding comes out on top, especially if setting up automation is not that obvious. But human player would never play that way, they would get bored from the repetitiveness. So the game designers never really have to close that gameplay loophole (or it would make human experience worse).      reply                             gopher_space    48 minutes ago       |  root  |  parent  |  prev  |  next   [–]       \n  This is where human-directed looping revision fits in on a lot of my projects.      reply                             damowangcy    30 minutes ago       |  prev  |  next   [–]       \n  I don't have the budget to do so, but if I do, it would be interesting to put different models in the same game with the same prompt to observe the behaviors that different models have when told to problem solve.      reply                             CSMastermind    1 hour ago       |  prev  |  next   [–]       \n  I tried a few recently on Civ 6 Deity, and they all lose pretty quickly.      reply                             kyle_grove    1 hour ago       |  parent  |  next   [–]       \n  Sounds like an experiment I'd like to give a try--what integration layer did you use to get LLMs to control the civ?      reply                             Kotlopou    1 hour ago       |  parent  |  prev  |  next   [–]       \n  To be fair, Deity on Civ games is brutal. I'd be interested in where the cutoff is.      reply                             mh-    1 hour ago       |  parent  |  prev  |  next   [–]       \n  Doesn't the AI (the computer players, I mean) \"cheat\" at that high of difficulty level?      reply                             singingtoday    30 minutes ago       |  root  |  parent  |  next   [–]       \n  That's moot- it's a comparison vs humans who can beat it.      reply                             Olscore    27 minutes ago       |  prev  |  next   [–]       \n  Automating fun was not on the top of my priorities but sure.      reply                             blobbers    1 hour ago       |  prev  |  next   [–]       \n  This is a much better benchmark as a concept (without knowing the details of how the problem is framed to the agent) than some of these ridiculous things the frontier labs are reporting (vending machine simulator? come on). Can you report more in depth at stumbling blocks? It's a surprisingly linear curve. I'm going to try it on a local run. I wonder if there is room for \"recommendorio\" where it gives you AI advice on your current factory. Is the model pre-trained with any knowledge of the game?      reply                             sdwvit    1 hour ago       |  prev  |  next   [–]       \n  how much did it cost I wonder      reply                             ryanmadden    34 minutes ago       |  parent  |  next   [–]       \n  (Author) I did this on the 'Plus' plan over a series of 5h quota segments so $20 for me, but at API pricing the tokens would have been $52      reply                             bosshawg    1 hour ago       |  prev  |  next   [–]       \n  interesting artifact of over investment and subsidies of AI and datacenters      reply                             artninja1988    1 hour ago       |  parent  |  next   [–]       \n  Who determines \"over investment\"      reply                             bosshawg    1 hour ago       |  root  |  parent  |  next   [–]       \n  the market, and investors opinions on their returns.      reply                             ang_cire    1 hour ago       |  prev  |  next   [–]       \n  Another thing AI can do that I can't...      reply                             singingtoday    30 minutes ago       |  parent  |  next   [–]       \n  I'm sure you could if you put your mind to it. It's not so bad, on peaceful mode. Just takes time. Boring though without something to attack you imo      reply                             dfedbeef    1 hour ago       |  prev   [–]       \n  Good to know it's good at something, I suppose? Sol seems like it always just wastes my time and writes terrible code.      reply             \n            \n  Consider applying for YC's Winter 2027 batch!  Applications  are open till November 2.   \n   Guidelines  |  FAQ  |  Lists  |  API  |  Security  |  Legal  |  Apply to YC  |  Contact    \n Search:           "
